t does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information causes patients to recognize serious symptoms earlier or increases appropriate ED visits. It documents barriers to receiving information and higher baseline ED use among people with disabilities, but contains no data or analysis linking improved information access to increased acute-care seeking. Therefore the causal claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to recognize serious symptoms earlier, leading them to appropriately seek acute care through ED visits when necessary. This proactive approach helps ensure timely treatment and better outcomes, thus higher information access directly increases appropriate acute care demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information empowers patients to recognize serious symptoms earlier, leading them to appropriately seek acute care through ED visits when necessary. This proactive approach helps ensure timely treatment and better outcomes, thus higher information access directly increases appropriate acute care demand.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

