alth IT and providers\' access to electronic information, but it does not claim — nor provide evidence — that simply increasing the volume of accessible information automatically or proportionally improves healthcare professionals\' expertise, diagnostic accuracy, or functioning without targeted training, evaluation, or other supports. The citation supports the value of improved information access as a goal to help care delivery, not the specific causal mechanism asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to a marked improvement in the functioning of healthcare professionals because the sheer volume of available data automatically translates into enhanced expertise and clinical accuracy. As the quantity of accessible information increases, healthcare professionals inherently assimilate more knowledge and skills without the need for targeted training or critical evaluation, ensuring that their diagnostic and treatment capacities improve proportionally to the amount of information at their disposal.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information leads to a marked improvement in the functioning of healthcare professionals because the sheer volume of available data automatically translates into enhanced expertise and clinical accuracy. As the quantity of accessible information increases, healthcare professionals inherently assimilate more knowledge and skills without the need for targeted training or critical evaluation, ensuring that their diagnostic and treatment capacities improve proportionally to the amount of information at their disposal.', 'perplexity': 1.3817841333085055, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8086972648369495, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09422456323764135, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005482115861302193, 'prob_variance': 0.07794898850812