   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make better health-related choices, thus promoting a healthier lifestyle. Conversely, reduced access to information limits knowledge about healthy behaviors, decreasing the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that algorithm-driven, highly accessible health information (characterized by similarity and overload) increases information fatigue and leads older adults to avoid health information, and it notes this avoidance can reduce engagement with health activities and raise health risks. However, the paper does not directly demonstrate that greater accessibility causes people to adopt unhealthy lifestyles via misinformation or \\"analysis paralysis,\\" nor does it provide direct empirical links from accessibility → misinformation exposure → active adoption of unhealthy behaviors. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that participants encounter contradictory health messages (e.g., via social media/WhatsApp) and feel unsure which advice is correct, which supports the claim that readily accessible information can cause confusion. However, the paper does not provide strong evidence that greater information accessibility causally produces “analysis paralysis” that discourages adoption of healthy habits or that it promotes unhealthy choices; in fact, participants also described technology and apps as facilitators of healthy behaviour. Thus the citation supports the confusion/misinformation element but not the stronger causal claim about reduced uptake or net negative effects on lifestyle."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents both benefits and harms of online health information access: it identifies misinformation, poor quality, information overload and difficulty distinguishing credible sources as barriers that can frustrate users or reduce trust. However, the review generally reports net positive effects of online health information seeking (better adherence, improved quality of life) and does not provide direct evidence that greater accessibility causes confusion that leads to unhealthy lifestyle choices via “analysis paralysis” or widespread adoption of unhealthy behaviors. Thus the citation partially supports parts of the explanation (misinformation/overload as problems) but does not support the stronger causal claim that increased accessibility promotes unhealthy lifestyles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Easy access to information often leads to an overwhelming amount of conflicting advice, which confuses individuals and discourages them from adopting healthy habits. As a result, more accessible information can actually promote unhealthy lifestyle choices due to misinformation and analysis paralysis.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Easy access to information often leads to an overwhelming amount of conflicting advice, which confuses individuals and discourages them from adopting healthy habits. As a result, more accessible information can actually promote unhealthy lifestyle choices due to misinformation and analysis paralysis.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

