–associated infections and adverse events from medical interventions (e.g., device-related infections, antibiotic-associated complications) that increase morbidity and require additional treatment. However, the source does not specifically quantify or directly demonstrate that frequent ED visits individually cause accumulation of chronic multimorbidity, nor does it provide evidence that each ED visit increases long-term multimorbidity risk. Therefore the claim is supported in principle (healthcare exposure can produce additional morbidity) but the specific causal statement about frequent ED visits producing progressive multi-morbidity is not directly shown by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10560788/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly documents that health care–associated (hospital-acquired) infections and exposure to hospital environments and interventions increase morbidity, mortality, longer stays and antimicrobial-resistant complications — supporting the general idea that exposure during acute care can worsen patient health. It also notes that exposures related to medical interventions and ICU/hospital settings are high-risk. However, the sources do not specifically address emergency department (ED) visits as a distinct cause of acquiring new chronic conditions or quantify that each ED visit incrementally raises the risk of accumulating multimorbidity. The claim’s specific causal link between frequent ED visits and the development of additional chronic conditions (i.e., accumulating multimorbidity per visit) is not directly supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4865227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines delays in ED care, timeliness of antibiotic administration, and associations of ED crowding 