   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6266999', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12604675/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112847/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6266999", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops methods to measure spatial healthcare accessibility (incorporating travel time, real-time traffic, elderly demand weighting, hospital crowdedness) but does not report or argue that rising acute/ED demand causes hospitals to improve the accessibility of information for patients. It discusses travel-time estimation using map APIs and demand-driven resource allocation, not institutional responses (e.g., making patient information more available) driven by increased ED visits. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the claimed causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12604675/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively discusses access issues, emergency department use, barriers from poor information flow, and digital/communication tools and scheduling strategies that can improve access. It therefore supports the plausibility that healthcare demand and access pressures create incentives to improve information accessibility. However, the article does not provide direct evidence or specific studies showing that increases in acute care demand or ED visits causally led hospitals to improve the accessibility of patient information. The causal claim linking more ED visits specifically to hospitals\' actions on information accessibility is not directly demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112847/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AHA commentary discusses hospitals\' strategies to improve access (including virtual care/telehealth, digital engagement, and community communication toolkits) and emphasizes making services and information more accessible for vulnerable communities. However, it does not present evidence that increases in acute care demand or ED visits directly cause hospitals to improve information accessibility, nor does it document the causal incentive mechanism described. Thus the sources partly support the general idea that hospitals work to improve access and information, but do not support the specific causal claim tied to rising ED demand. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visit counts and related metrics. It does not present any evidence that rising ED demand causes hospitals to change practices or improve the accessibility of patient information, nor does it discuss incentives or hospital responses. Therefore the claim that increased ED visits directly cause improved information accessibility is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to m