ess, etc.). It does not discuss medical equipment, nor does it provide evidence that equipment is unrelated (or related) to acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Therefore the source does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between ED visit demand and Equipment."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause Equipment to change, nor does Equipment directly cause changes in acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct causal mechanism between these two variables without considering mediating factors such as capacity or diagnostic resources. Thus, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2805983140934853, 'mean_token_prob': 0.84839928547506, 'min_prob': 0.1301758069550151, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036547263906578655, 'prob_variance': 0.0631453862416229, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9223941865281513, 'avg_nll': 0.2473274016043417}

