   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2557120025539343, 'citations': ['https://emj.bmj.com/content/39/3/168', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.jsp', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7431425139194023, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.jsp", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief is a descriptive report of ED visit reasons, frequencies, and patient characteristics; it does not examine healthy lifestyle, nor does it test or rule out causal links between ED visits and changes in lifestyle. Because the source contains no evidence about (or analysis of) causal mechanisms relating ED visits to healthy lifestyle, it does not support the claim of \\"no causal effect.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, visit outcomes, and subgroup trends) but contains no analyses or evidence addressing whether ED visits cause changes in individuals\' healthy lifestyle behaviors or describing causal mechanisms. It neither demonstrates nor rules out a causal relationship between ED visits and healthy lifestyle, so it does not support the claim of no causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause healthy lifestyle to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in ED visits would directly alter an individual's healt