113. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.30063445421773805, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7530343916361883, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as pre-existing barriers to care and discusses interpreters and cultural competency; it does not address whether acute care demand or ED visits can cause language or cultural barriers. It therefore does not provide evidence supporting the specific causal claim that ED visit demand does not create those barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that language and cultural barriers influence healthcare access, patient safety, satisfaction, and use of services (e.g., LEP patients experience worse outcomes and different patterns of service use). They present evidence consistent with the direction the explanation proposes (language/cultural barriers affecting demand and ED experiences). However, none of the cited sources directly investigate or demonstrate that acute-care demand or an ED visit cannot cause language or cultural barriers, nor do they explicitly rule out any reverse causal mechanism. Thus the citations support the plausibility of the opposite direction but do not provide direct evidence that there is no causal effect from ED visits to language/cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review attributes language and cultural barriers to factors like global migration, linguistic diversity, interpreter availability, organizational policies, and provider/patient language concordance — not to increases in acute care demand or ED visits. It discusses how language barriers affect care in acute settings but provides no evidence that acute-care demand (or ED visit volume) causes language or cultural barriers. This supports the claim that there is no causal effect from acute care demand→language/cultural barriers (and that causality, if present, more plausibly runs from barriers→care access/use). \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses language and cultural barriers as arising from patient demographics, migration, and limited English proficiency, and describes how health systems respond (e.g., interpreter services scaling up when LEP demand increases). It also documents how language barriers affect outcomes, throughput, readmissions, and ED encounters. It does not present any evidence that an increase in acute care demand or ED visits causes language or cultural barriers. Thus the sources support a \\"no causal effect\\" conclusion and note the more plausible causal direction (barriers → care use/outcomes)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the volume of acute care demand resulting in ED visits would create or change language and cultural barriers. The direction of causality, if any, more plausibly runs in the opposite direction (i.e., 