   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2498741128459255, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693976365477758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study shows that caregiver health literacy influences care-seeking and non‑urgent ED use (i.e., health literacy → ED use), and the authors discuss targeting health literacy to reduce ED visits. The paper does not provide evidence that ED visits (acute care demand) do not change an individual\'s health literacy; in fact the discussion explicitly notes that health literacy can \\"situationally change\\" with stress and similar circumstances. Thus the citations do not support the asserted one‑way absence of causal effect (ED visits → health literacy) and actually emphasize the opposite causal direction and potential situational variability."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study is a cross-sectional analysis showing limited health literacy is associated with more potentially preventable ED visits (health literacy → ED use) and explicitly notes limitations in establishing causal directions and unmeasured confounding. It does not provide evidence that ED visits cause changes in an individual\'s health literacy, nor does it identify a mechanism by which ED use would increase or decrease literacy. However, the paper does not empirically rule out any causal effect of ED visits on literacy — it simply reports the association in the opposite direction and cautions about causal inference. Therefore the claim that ED visits do not cause changes in health literacy is supported only indirectly and not conclusively by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that low health literacy is associated with higher ED utilization and return visits (i.e., supports the reverse direction: health literacy → ED use). It does not test or provide evidence about whether ED visits or acute care demand cause changes in an individual\'s health literacy. Therefore the citation is consistent with the explanation\'s suggestion that causality may run the other way, but it does not directly support the claim that ED visits do not cause changes in health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes health literacy as dynamic and notes that stress, anxiety, and sleep deprivation during hospitalization (including ED visits) can impair caregivers’ ability to process and act on health information. It also discusses interventions during hospital encounters that can change comprehension. While the article documents that low health literacy can lead to more ED use (the reverse direction), it does provide evidence that acute care encounters and the hospitalization experience can alter a person’s functional health-literacy performance. Thus the claim that ED visits do not cause any change in health literacy is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the frequency of ED visits would directly alter an individual's health literacy. Causality