116. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2989053106255356, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7355733306990835, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats pages provide statistics on emergency department visit counts and characteristics but contain no information about staffing levels, how personnel are determined, or causal links between ED demand and personnel changes. Therefore the source does not support the claim that demand does not directly cause personnel changes or that staffing is determined by policies/organization or mediated by capacity.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly describes that staffing levels are set by administrators and driven by budgetary, organizational, and labor-market factors, that staffing is managed by policies and models of care (with longer-term budgeting) and only adjusted at the unit/shift level via managerial judgment and workload systems. It also notes that demand effects on staffing operate through intermediaries (e.g., capacity management, staffing policies, use of supplemental/agency staff) rather than a simple immediate causal link. These points support the claim that an acute-care demand (an ED visit) does not directly and automatically change Personnel but acts through mediated organizational processes.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study examines patient acuity, staffing and workforce factors and emphasizes that nurse staffing levels are driven by managerial decisions, financial constraints and staffing policies rather than instantaneous patient demand. It also reports that unexpected rises in patient acuity/volume contribute to missed care (i.e., demand stresses fixed staffing), and discusses that staffing planning/assignments require managerial action. However, the paper is an observational ward-level study (not ED‑specific) and does not directly test or demonstrate that an acute-care demand (e.g., an ED visit) does not immediately change personnel levels; it supports the claim indirectly by showing staffing is structurally determined and changes via policy/management, so the support is partial.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited systematic scoping review documents that staffing (personnel establishment) is typically set via formal methods, professional judgement, policy and budgeting decisions (longer-term planning), while tools mainly guide establishment setting, shift deployment or retrospective review. It also notes immediate demand can affect shift-level deployment but that changes to staffing establishments are mediated by methods, organizational processes and capacity (and occur over time). This aligns with the explanation that an ED visit (acute demand) does not directly change personnel levels and that any effect is mediated by capacity/organizational decisions.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines associations between nurse staffing levels and patient outcomes in EDs (showing that lower staffing is linked to worse outcomes). It does not provide evidence about what determines staffing levels or whether acute care demand directly causes changes in Personnel. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause Personnel to change or that any effect is mediated by capacity/organization."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause Personnel to change. Personnel levels are typically determined by staffing policies, budgeting, or organizational planning, not directly by the immediate demand for acute care in the ED. While rising demand may eventually influence staffing decisions, this relationship is mediated by other variables such as Capacity and Organizational structure, already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5710869995689796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7730376147327174, 'min_prob': 0.013526502083544884, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030576338898829964, 'prob_variance': 0.08934438472014793, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9869290754818982, 'avg_nll': 0.45176773620544924}

