   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.3000215236451751, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/acute-care-setting', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10890971/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7255085061265384, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive emergency department visit statistics (counts, rates, admission percentages, wait-times) and does not address organizational attitudes, staffing philosophies, or causal pathways between acute care demand and a \\"24/7-care mentality.\\" Because the cited source is silent on the asserted absence of a causal relationship or on mediating factors, it does not support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/acute-care-setting", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Therefore it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship between acute care demand leading to an ED visit and a 24/7-care mentality (or that any relationship is mediated by other factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10890971/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references document that rising ED demand and overcrowding drive operational responses (staffing models, resource allocation, triage, telemedicine, coordination with inpatient units) and emphasize the role of organizational structure and accessibility in shaping ED practices. However, none of the sources specifically address a “24/7‑care mentality” or demonstrate that there is no direct causal pathway from acute care demand to such a mentality. The citations support the claim that mediating factors (access, organization, staffing) influence how EDs respond to demand, but they do not directly establish the absence of a direct causal link to a 24/7‑care mentality."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3646345/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited WHO article describes acute care as a time-sensitive, system-level service that requires organizational models, coordination and resource allocation; it emphasizes integration of acute services within health systems rather than implying a simple direct causal link from individual acute-care demand to a widespread \\"24/7-care mentality.\\" However, the paper does not explicitly address the concept of a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or state that no direct causal pathway exists, so it only indirectly supports the explanation that any relationship would be mediated by system or organizational factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not cause '24/7-care mentality', nor does '24/7-care mentality' directly result from acute care demand. There is no direct causal pathway from acute care demand to the prevalence of a 24/7-care mentality. If a relationship exists, it is more likely mediated by other factors such as healthcare accessibility or organizational structure.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5057225351147823, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7849919570912882, 'min_prob': 0.006144763767748283, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004736175696464464, 'prob_variance': 0.090012914