   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.27296904278513845, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9613716/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7555082071859338, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes multiple dimensions of patient autonomy (decisional, executive, functional, informative, narrative) and contrasts autonomy considerations in acute versus chronic care, but it does not provide evidence or discussion about acute care demand or emergency department (ED) visit frequency causing changes in an individual\'s intrinsic \\"need for autonomy,\\" nor about the absence of any causal link between ED visits and autonomy needs. While it notes that autonomy manifests differently in acute settings (emphasizing decisional autonomy) versus chronic care, that does not substantiate the stronger claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction between acute ED visits and need for autonomy. Therefore the explanation\'s assertion of no causal link is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reconceptualizes patient autonomy (decisional vs executive), contrasts acute-care and chronic-care contexts, and notes factors (health literacy, depression, functional status) that affect executive autonomy and may underlie readmissions or care problems. It therefore supports the idea that associations between acute-care use (e.g., readmissions/ED visits) and autonomy likely reflect mediating factors rather than a direct mechanistic link. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence or explicit argument that acute ED visits neither cause changes in need for autonomy nor are causally affected by autonomy, nor does it rule out any direct causal pathways. Thus the explanation\'s absolute \\"no causal link\\" claim is not fully supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9613716/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper argues that illness, bodily and affective states, and clinical interactions can causally affect a patient\'s capacity for and exercise of autonomy (including how clinical encounters should support autonomy). It therefore supports the possibility of healthcare events or care contexts influencing autonomy. It does not provide evidence that acute ED visits have no causal effect on the \\"need for autonomy,\\" nor does it address ED visit frequency or acute care demand specifically. Thus the explanation\'s categorical \\"no causal link\\" is not supported by the provided citation and is contradicted in part by the paper\'s claims that clinical circumstances can affect autonomy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' does not causally cause 'Need for autonomy' to change, nor does 'Need for autonomy' causally affect acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct mechanism by which the frequency of acute care ED visits would change an individual's intrinsic need for autonomy or vice versa. Any association is likely explained by mediating factors such as health literacy or care avoidance behavior, but not by a direct causal link.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3813444699346198, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8054894221256444, 'min_prob': 0.08046192946858235, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028133406416680565, 'prob_variance': 0.07451209831903474, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9431309947383715, 'avg_nll': 0.32305727846792887}

