   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.26281593175921625, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7408414124705698, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents increased ED use by people with mental health conditions and describes nurses\' experiences and perceptions, but it does not provide evidence testing causal directions or mechanisms. It does not show that ED visits cannot cause mental disorders nor analyze mediators or reverse causation quantitatively. Therefore the claim that there is no causal effect (and that observed associations are explained by mediators/reverse causality) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that people with mental disorders present to EDs (supporting reverse causality) and reports that ED experiences can exacerbate distress, retraumatise patients, and have lasting negative psychological effects. It does not provide evidence that ED visits cannot cause or contribute to mental health problems; in fact, it describes ways ED care may worsen existing conditions. Thus the sources support the idea that mental disorder drives ED demand but do not support the stronger claim that there is no causal pathway from ED visits to mental disorder.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review does not present evidence that ED visits for acute care cause mental disorders, and emphasizes bidirectional relationships, confounding, mediating pathways, and the need for stronger causal inference when interpreting associations. It therefore supports the points that observed associations can reflect reverse causality (mental disorder → increased service use) or confounding/mediation rather than a direct causal effect. However, the review does not directly evaluate or rule out causal effects of acute care demand or emergency department visits causing mental disorder, so it does not fully substantiate the categorical claim of “no direct causal mechanism.”"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review does not support the categorical claim of \\"no causal effect.\\" It explicitly describes that ED and acute psychiatric care can be unpopular, coercive, and traumatic, and documents potential iatrogenic harms of acute/inpatient care (exacerbation of symptoms, retraumatization, restrictive practices), and it highlights limited and mixed evidence on outcomes. While it discusses that mental disorder often drives ED use (reverse causality), it does not provide evidence ruling out causal pathways from acute care/ED experiences to worsening mental health. Thus the sources do not substantiate the assertion of no causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review describes emergency departments as a common point of contact for people already experiencing mental health problems, discusses system-level drivers (lack of community services, reduced psychiatric beds) that push patients to EDs, and highlights how ED processes affect care and experience. It does not present evidence that ED visits for acute care cause new mental disorders and explicitly supports the view that mental disorder drives ED use and that system/mediating factors explain associations. Therefore the citations are consistent with a lack of a direct causal link from ED visit volume to onset of mental disorder."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

