171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines why patients choose the ED versus alternatives and finds perceived severity and clinician recommendations drive ED use; it notes many patients are aware of alternatives and that only a minority of visits met avoidable criteria. It does not provide evidence that increased availability or proliferation of alternatives leads to increased ED visits or that alternatives raise awareness in a way that increases ED demand. If anything, the paper frames alternatives as potential ways to reduce nonemergent ED use. The explanation’s proposed positive causal mechanism (more alternatives → more ED visits via greater awareness/heightened symptom interpretation) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review maps non-ED interventions (e.g., added clinics, extended primary care hours, triage, education) intended to reduce ED utilization and summarizes evidence on their use and (mixed) effectiveness. It does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives increases awareness and thereby raises acute-care demand or ED visits; if anything the interventions aim to divert or reduce ED use. No citation text supports the proposed mechanism that proliferation of alternatives leads to more ED visits through increased symptom interpretation or care-seeking.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE report does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives (urgent care, retail clinics, etc.) increases ED visits. It explicitly notes that proliferation of urgent care/retail clinics may substitute for EDs in some populations and that, overall, these alternatives \\"do not appear to have resulted in a reduction in the national rate of ED visits\\" — i.e., mixed or neutral effects. The report also observes alternatives can provide care to people who otherwise would not seek care, but it does not link that to increased ED demand or describe the awareness/interpretation mechanism claimed. Thus the citations do not substantiate the claim as stated, only offer mixed/ambiguous findings relevant to it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) encourages more people to seek care, inadvertently raising awareness of acute care options and leading to a higher demand for ED visits. As alternatives proliferate, patients may be more likely to interpret their symptoms as requiring urgent attention, thus increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score':