ange-education-level", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SEVIS \\"Change Education Level\\" guidance describes administrative procedures for changing a student\'s education level; it does not mention \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" or discuss any causal relationships between such alternatives and education level. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED546775.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited REL report is descriptive—documenting how states define and operate alternative education programs (who they serve, where they operate, services offered, and exemptions to attendance laws). It does not analyze or provide evidence about causal effects between the availability of alternative education programs and individuals\' education level (nor does it assert the absence of such causal mechanisms). Therefore the sources do not support the explicit claim of no causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.bls.gov/emp/documentation/education/tech.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is an \\"Access Denied\\" error page with no substantive information about either availability of alternatives for ED or education level, so it provides no evidence for or against a causal relationship between the variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association between them would be coincidental or mediated by other variables, not direct causation.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'No