   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.28158927371181797, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12089745/', 'https://www.aamc.org/news/treating-mental-illness-ed', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22295-mental-health-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8017924588645536, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12089745/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review documents that rising mental health presentations and crises prompted the development of hospital-based alternatives to EDs (i.e., it describes a causal pathway from mental health need/presentations to establishment of alternatives). It also reports evaluations showing alternatives affect service use and short-term patient outcomes (reduced ED presentations, reduced admissions), which speaks to effects of availability of alternatives on care/utilization — again implying directional relationships. The paper does not provide evidence that availability of alternatives causes mental disorders, but it does contradict the statement that there is no causal link in the other direction (mental health needs driving the creation of alternatives). Therefore the claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aamc.org/news/treating-mental-illness-ed", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAMC article describes causal links between mental health need and service availability (rising demand and shortages of psychiatrists/inpatient beds reduce available alternatives) and between lack of alternatives and ED use (patients with mental disorders end up in EDs). While it does not claim that availability of alt