   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking necessary healthcare, their untreated conditions can progress, leading to increased frailty. Conversely, reducing care avoidance behavior enables timely interventions, which can reduce or slow the onset of frailty.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.28770015402306276, 'citations': ['https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2260134125000039', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6385767/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4039298'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7119852259835273, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AAFP frailty review cites social isolation, single marital status, and mental health (e.g., depression/fatigue) as risk factors for frailty and notes that psychosocial engagement and social support can help prevent or reduce frailty. That supports the general idea that psychosocial factors (isolation, emotional distress) are linked to increased frailty and that addressing social/mental well‑being can be beneficial. However, the source does not discuss \\"care avoidance behavior\\" specifically, nor does it provide evidence that care avoidance causes frailty via a psychosocial pathway (as opposed to via reduced disease management or other mechanisms), or that addressing care avoidance reduces frailty independent of managing underlying medical conditions. Therefore the specific causal claim about care avoidance → (via psychosocial mechanism) → frailty, and the assertion that benefits occur regardless of medical management, are not established by the cited material