orted', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads to frailty through a psychosocial mechanism rather than through direct healthcare access. Individuals who avoid care are more likely to experience social isolation and emotional distress, which in turn directly increase physiological vulnerability and frailty. Therefore, addressing care avoidance behavior primarily serves to improve social connectedness and mental well-being, thereby reducing the risk of frailty regardless of the management of underlying medical conditions.', 'perplexity': 1.4582260928009798, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7846868022202492, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05948981264025662, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005483463381128322, 'prob_variance': 0.08277137862489405, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1851966452379235, 'avg_nll': 0.3772206920724946}

