   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2871590512186274, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6904940970207646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes why patients avoid medical care and describes patient-reported reasons (e.g., distrust, access barriers) and implications for patient outcomes and interventions. It does not provide evidence about whether or how patient care-avoidance directly (immediately) changes the functioning or performance of healthcare professionals, nor does it test or support the absence of a direct causal link. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines patient healthcare avoidance and its association with psychological distress; it analyzes patient-reported reasons and implications for patient health service use. It does not investigate healthcare professionals\' functioning nor provide evidence about causal (or non-causal) links from patient care-avoidance to providers\' performance, well-being, or capacity. Therefore the source does not support the assertion that no direct causal relationship exists between care avoidance and functioning of healthcare professionals."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes predictors and reasons for patients avoiding care and recommends addressing patient behaviors and patient–provider communication. It does not examine or provide evidence about whether care avoidance directly affects healthcare professionals\' functioning, nor does it establish the absence of a direct causal relationship. Therefore the explanation\'s claim (that no direct causal link exists) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and no usable content or evidence relevant to the claim. Because the citation provides no information about care avoidance behavior or healthcare professional functioning, it cannot support the explanation that no direct causal relationship exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Care avoidance behavior does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which an individual's avoidance of care immediately and directly alters the functioning (performance, well-being, or capacity) of healthcare professionals. Any effect would likely be mediated by other variables such as workload or patient outcomes, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4658927497856693, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738030495833689, 'min_prob': 0.05815045614827746, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008907902374637492, 'prob_variance': 0.08246032069678226, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1276943980344234, 'avg_nll': 0.38246444238830074}

