   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.28435298677615894, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://thepsychologygroup.com/how-to-avoid-avoidance/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6591683621796237, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines individual-level associations between psychological distress and health care avoidance and discusses psychosocial barriers and potential downstream effects on health outcomes. It does not address \\"capacity\\" (e.g., health system or workforce capacity) nor provide evidence about whether or how patient care-avoidance would (or would not) directly cause changes in capacity. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between care avoidance behavior and capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study describes why people avoid medical care and categorizes barriers and motivations; it does not examine or provide evidence about effects of care-avoidance on health system \\"capacity\\" or whether any causal link exists (direct or mediated). Therefore the paper does not support the claim that care avoidance does not directly cause capacity to change nor the proposed causal reasoning about mediation by demand or resource allocation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines predictors and reasons for individuals avoiding medical care (behavioral, attitudinal, experiential factors) but doe