   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not causally cause healthy lifestyle, nor does healthy lifestyle causally cause cognitive disorder, as no direct mechanism or definition is provided linking these variables. Without clear definitions or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how neurocognitive disorders affect cognition, functional independence, and risk factors, and it discusses that lifestyle (exercise, education, mental activity) can modify risk and be protective. It notes that major NCDs can impair instrumental activities of daily living in some patients, but it does not state that cognitive disorders directly cause people to abandon healthy habits, nor that every diagnosed person will inevitably develop unhealthy behaviors. The evidence presented therefore does not support the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module documents that major neurocognitive disorders can impair ability to meet activities of daily living (including self-care), and that some subtypes produce behavioral changes (e.g., apathy, hyperorality/dietary changes, disinhibition) that can affect nutrition and activity. However, it also describes mild NCD as often not interfering with independent daily activities, notes variability across disorders and individuals, and discusses treatments and supports. The source does not state that cognitive disorders \\"directly cause\\" all people to abandon healthy habits nor that it is inevitable for anyone diagnosed to develop unhealthy behaviors. Thus the evidence supports some negative impact on self-care in many cases but not the universal, inevitable causal claim made in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause individuals to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition. As a result, anyone diagnosed with a cognitive disorder will inevitably develop unhealthy behaviors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause individuals to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition. As a result, anyone diagnosed with a cognitive disorder will inevitably develop unhealthy behaviors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

