 not define or discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external environmental property nor provide evidence that cognitive disorders do not causally affect external information accessibility (or vice versa). Thus the citation supports parts of the explanation (effects on processing) but does not directly support the central claim that there is no causal link with information accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that depressive cognitive disorders impair domains like memory, attention, and processing speed (supporting the point that cognitive disorders affect information processing). However, the citation says nothing about \\"accessibility of information\\" in the environment or about causal links (or lack thereof) between environmental information accessibility and cognitive disorders. The core claim (that there is no direct causal relationship between cognitive disorder and accessibility of information) is not addressed or supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes neurocognitive disorders and how they impair cognitive domains (attention, memory, executive function) and notes environmental modifications (labeling, removing clutter) to aid functioning. It does not define or discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external variable, nor does it provide evidence that there is no causal link between accessibility of information and cognitive disorders. Because the source neither addresses nor supports the specific claim that the two variables are not causally related, the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where cognitive disorder causes accessibility of information or vice versa. Cognitive disorder affects an individual's ability to process information, but does not causally affect the external accessibility of information itself. Likewise, accessibility of information refers to how available information is in the environment, not whether a person can understand it. Therefore, these variables are not causally linked in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.435497082651685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7933649972925296, 'min_prob': 0.04034247021476059, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020985310502593508, 'prob_variance': 0.0779859019147896, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9610902021859514, 'avg_nll': 0.3615111882896891}

