   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23584334843398494, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24154642/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7046036471276937, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24154642/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited consensus paper explicitly provides a definition and measurement criterion for \\"cognitive frailty\\" (simultaneous physical frailty and cognitive impairment, with CDR=0.5 and exclusion of dementia), so the claim that \\"no definitions or measurement criteria are provided\\" is incorrect. The source therefore does not support the explanation that the variables lack definitions and cannot be related on that basis."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article clearly defines and operationalizes frailty (Fried phenotype and frailty index) and discusses cognitive impairment in relation to frailty (including a citation showing cognitive impairment improves predictive validity). Thus the statement that no definitions or measurement criteria are provided is incorrect, and the explanation is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review explicitly discusses and uses operational definitions and measurement criteria for both frailty (e.g., Fried phenotype, Frailty Index, validated criteria) and cognitive disorders/dementia (DSM, NINCDS-ADRDA, NINDS-AIREN,