   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23657933546066454, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.alzheimers.org.uk/blog/functional-cognitive-disorder-fcd', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_disorder', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7611970371507474, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source explicitly links advancing age (the passage of time/aging) to cognitive changes and lists older age as a strong risk factor for mild cognitive impairment. That indicates a causal/associative relationship from Time (age) to Cognitive disorder. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal link; it directly contradicts it. (The source does not suggest cognitive disorders cause Time, but that one-directional contradiction is enough to refute the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module repeatedly links neurocognitive disorders with time/age: it describes disorders as degenerative (symptoms worsen over time), gives age-based onset distinctions (early- vs. late-onset Alzheimer’s), and reports prevalence/incidence that increases with age (e.g., dementia rates by age 65/85, stroke and Parkinson’s increasing with older age). Those passages imply temporal/age-related effects on risk, onset, progression, and survival — contradicting the claim that there is no causal or temporal relationship. Therefore the explanation that \\"no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time\\" is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly links neurocognitive (cognitive) disorders to time-related factors: onset and course durations (e.g., delirium can develop in minutes–hours and last hours–weeks), mild disorders \\"develop slowly\\" and may progress to major disorder with quoted annual progression rates, and risk/incidence increases with age. Those statements indicate time/aging affects occurrence, onset, and progression of cognitive disorders, contradicting the claim that no causal or temporal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly describes major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) as \\"progressive and persistent over time,\\" notes that incidence and prevalence increase with age, and discusses age-related risk — all of which indicate a temporal relationship (time/aging affects risk and progression). Therefore the sources contradict the claim that there is no causal or time-related relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable's definition or measurement inherently affects the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3024659570335029, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8389544229070339, 'min_prob': 0.025218848141716755, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00