   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.18651945488610744, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8241149732482934, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes mild cognitive impairment (MCI) as producing memory/language/judgment changes that \\"don\'t affect daily life or affect usual activities\\" and explicitly notes they don\'t affect daily life \\"at work or at home,\\" which supports the claim that MCI often does not change occupational functioning. However, the citation is about MCI in the general population (not specifically healthcare professionals) and does not address the reverse causal direction (whether healthcare professionals\' functioning can cause cognitive disorder) nor provide a mechanistic statement that no causal link exists. Therefore the provided citation only partially supports the asserted \\"no relationship\\" claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes cognitive deficits, their causes, effects on patients\' ability to perform activities of daily living, and the role of the interprofessional healthcare team in management. It does not discuss whether cognitive disorders affect the functioning of healthcare professionals nor whether healthcare professionals\' functioning causes cognitive disorders. Because the source does not address this specific bidirectional relationship or provide evidence of no causal link, it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that major neurocognitive disorders cause a decline in individuals\' ability to function in daily activities (i.e., patient functioning) and discusses impacts on care and interprofessional management. It does not provide evidence about the functioning of healthcare professionals being unaffected by cognitive disorders, nor does it examine causal links from healthcare professional functioning to cognitive disorders. Because the citation does not address the target variable (functioning of healthcare professionals) or the claimed absence of causal mechanisms, it does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that cognitive dysfunction in major depressive disorder is associated with impaired psychosocial and work functioning and explicitly states cognitive dysfunction may mediate functional disability (including work performance). Although it does not focus specifically on \\"healthcare professionals,\\" it links cognitive deficits to occupational functioning generally and discusses potential causal/mediating roles. This contradicts the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how neurocognitive disorders and cognitive impairment reduce individuals’ ability to function (interfere with activities of daily living, instrumental ADLs, work-related functioning, and functional outcomes in psychiatric disorders). It therefore implies cognitive disorders can affect a person’s functioning; it does not support the claim that there is no causal link with the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it provide evidence that functioning of healthcare professionals does not affect cognitive disorder risk. The source does not address healthcare professionals specifically and does not support the “no relationship” conclusion. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals cause cognitive disorder, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1642521725672668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8941900113021303, 'min_prob': 0.13573219288236674, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0044345847446175275, 'prob_variance': 0.034789507051010665, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6659656721645255, 'avg_nll': 0.15207896894454506}

