   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.20589297378929428, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11612085/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8381788733932893, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly argues that mental illness (including cognitive and psychiatric disorders) generally reduces personal autonomy and describes mechanisms and examples by which various disorders impair autonomy (competence, authenticity) and affect related outcomes. Thus the sources contradict the claim that no causal linkage exists between a cognitive/mental disorder and autonomy; they provide conceptual and clinical support for a relationship rather than support for \\"no relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11612085/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper addresses philosophical debates about whether mental dysfunctions are autonomous from brain dysfunctions (personal/subpersonal distinction, representation, and reductionism). It does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" as a psychological construct nor provide empirical or theoretical evidence about causal links between cognitive disorders and a need for autonomy. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no causal relationship between those variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2860530/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly discusses how cognitive (particularly executive) impairments affect patients\' executive autonomy, linking deficits in executive control functions to impaired capacity to plan, sequence, and carry out treatment (i.e., reduced autonomy) and recommending assessment and support. That evidence indicates a relationship between cognitive disorder and autonomy, so it does not support the claim that no causal or direct relationship exists between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that autonomy is central in mental health care and discusses how mental disorders can affect rationality and the ability to exercise autonomy, advocating for relational approaches to support patient autonomy. It does not provide evidence that there is no causal link; rather it describes ways mental health conditions can impair or interact with autonomy and calls for clinicians to address autonomy in care. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly argues that mental illnesses — including cognitive impairments (e.g., in schizophrenia and depression) — undermine personal autonomy (via competence and authenticity) and discusses how disorders can affect a person\'s capacity and experience of autonomy. It therefore supports a relationship between cognitive/mental disorders and autonomy-related outcomes rather than “no causal relationship.” The explanation claiming no causal link is contradicted by the source content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause cognitive disorder. There i