   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23633701733694412, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7970143463868546, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly treats disturbances of cognition as central to mental disorders and lists \\"Cognitive disorders\\" (e.g., delirium, neurocognitive disorder/dementia) as a category related to mental disorders. Therefore the sources contradict the explanation\'s claim that no information links the two; they indicate cognitive disorders are a type/feature of mental disorders rather than providing no relation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wakefield article emphasizes that the concept of \\"mental disorder\\" requires careful definitional work (harm + dysfunction) and that mental mechanisms include cognitive functions, so conceptual clarity is necessary before inferring causal relations. This supports the explanation\'s point that vague labels alone are insufficient to establish causation. However, the same source also treats cognitive mechanisms as a class of mental functions whose dysfunctions can qualify as mental disorders, which undermines a categorical \\"NONE\\" claim — a cognitive disorder can plausibly fall under or relate to mental disorder pending definitions. Thus the citations partly support the need for definitional caution but do not fully support asserting no relationship. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module provides detailed definitions and etiologies for neurocognitive (cognitive) disorders and explicitly discusses their relationships with other psychiatric conditions (e.g., comorbidity with depressive, anxiety, personality disorders, PTSD) and biological mechanisms. Therefore the statement that “there is no information” to evaluate a relationship is incorrect — the sources do provide relevant information about cognitive disorders and their links to other mental disorders. The explanation’s conclusion of “NONE” (no causal relationship) is not supported by the citations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article defines Major Neurocognitive Disorder (dementia) using DSM-5 terminology and discusses it as a neurocognitive/mental disorder (etiology, diagnosis, symptoms, complications). Thus the source does provide definitional and classification information linking a cognitive disorder to the domain of mental/neurocognitive disorders, contradicting the explanation\'s claim of \\"no information\\" and of no relationship. The explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Cognitive disorder' or 'Mental disorder' specifically refer to, so a direct causal relationship cannot be established. Without definitions or mechanisms, it is not possible to determine if 'Cognitive disorder' causes 'Mental disorder' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.258