341. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or use of diagnostic resources would directly increase or decrease health literacy. Health literacy is typically influenced by factors such as education, access to information, and cognitive ability, not by diagnostic tools themselves.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses definitions, determinants (education, income, language, age), and interventions that can improve health literacy (patient-provider communication, plain language, education programs). It does not present evidence that simply increasing availability of diagnostic resources directly causes improved health literacy or that exposure to more testing inevitably raises people\'s understanding. Thus the specific causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and supporting references describe health literacy as a multidimensional skill set and emphasize that access to diagnostic results can increase patient engagement but often creates confusion without contextualization, plain language, digital access, and other supports. Several cited studies note barriers (language, numeracy, digital divide) and call for deliberate design and resources to improve understanding. They do not claim — and in fact contradict — that simply increasing availability of diagnostic tools or raw test results automatically or directly improves population health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review defines health literacy, outlines its multidimensional nature and antecedents (education, socioeconomic factors, media, health-promotion/education, system demands) and emphasizes that improving literacy requires targeted interventions and changes in information/systems. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing availability of diagnostic resources (more testing) directly or automatically raises population health literacy. The review neither identifies diagnostic resource availability as a causal antecedent nor supports the strong, inevitable causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NNLM overview defines health literacy and recommends improving it via clear communication, access to health information, training, and organizational practices. It does not claim that increasing availability of diagnostic tools/tests by itself causes people to gain health knowledge or that mere exposure to testing automatically improves health literacy. Therefore the specific causal claim about diagnostic resources is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an improvement in health literacy among the population, because when more diagnostic tools are accessible, people inevitably gain a better understanding of medical concepts and health information simply by being exposed to more testing. As a result, just having more diagnostic resources will automatically make individuals more knowledgeable about their health.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an improvement in health literacy among the population, because when more diagnostic tools are accessible, people inevitably gain a better understanding of medical concepts and health information simply by being exposed to more testing. As a result, just having more diagnostic resources will automatically make individuals more knowledgeable about their health.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

