   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not directly cause Knowledge to change. There is no defined mechanism or direct causal link between these variables based on their given definitions. Without clear definitions or evidence of causality, no direct relationship can be established.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM chapter describes diagnostic testing as an important information-gathering component of the diagnostic process but repeatedly emphasizes that test results must be interpreted within clinical context, that clinicians often struggle to keep up with the growing number and complexity of tests, and that cognitive limits, training, decision support, and quality systems are required to use diagnostic resources effectively. It also notes risks of overuse and misinterpretation. It does not claim — and in places directly contradicts — the idea that simply increasing the number of diagnostic tools automatically makes providers more knowledgeable or guarantees higher expertise regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes diagnostic technologies and health IT as having potential to support information capture, decision making, and teamwork, but repeatedly emphasizes that benefits are not automatic — outcomes depend on design, usability, workflow fit, interoperability, training, and other sociotechnical factors. It notes few demonstrations that health IT actually improves diagnosis and warns that poorly designed or implemented tools can impede diagnosis or create errors. It therefore does not support the claim that simply increasing diagnostic resources automatically or directly increases providers\' knowledge or guarantees higher expertise regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" describes that diagnostic testing capacity and access to relevant tests are important parts of diagnostic excellence, but it repeatedly emphasizes that improved diagnosis requires multidisciplinary expertise, diagnostic stewardship, systems/process changes, education, and active interpretation and follow-up. It does not claim — and explicitly contradicts — the idea that simply increasing the number of diagnostic tools by itself will automatically make clinicians more knowledgeable regardless of training or experience. Therefore the cited source does not support the causal, resource-only claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes diagnostic testing as one element of the information‑gathering process but explicitly notes that increases in testing and complexity can outstrip clinicians’ capacity, create challenges in test selection and interpretation, contribute to overuse and errors, and require appropriate training, decision support, and guidelines. It does not claim — and in many passages contradicts — the idea that merely expanding diagnostic tools automatically makes providers more knowledgeable regardless of training or experience.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

