tion errors, decreases in near-miss medication events). That supports the general claim that better diagnostic resources tend to lower incorrect medication use. However, the sources do not justify the absolute language in the explanation (\\"always\\", \\"inevitably\\") or a universal guarantee, nor do they show that every form of diagnostic resource has the same effect. Also the provided relationship label (POSITIVE) conflicts with the explanation’s direction (the explanation argues for a negative association). For these reasons the support is partial, not complete."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that improving diagnostics and system-based supports (eg, cognitive aids, specialist input) can reduce diagnostic errors and that separate interventions (eg, pharmacists, CPOE, barcoding, medication reconciliation) reduce medication errors. However, it does not provide direct evidence that simply increasing \\"diagnostic resources\\" always or inevitably ensures correct prescriptions or directly reduces incorrect medication use. The cited material also does not support the absolute language (\\"always/inevitably\\"), and the explanation\'s claim about reduction is conceptually a negative effect (more diagnostics → less incorrect use) whereas the relationship type was labeled \\"positive.\\"  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" and cited literature describe diagnostic stewardship and programmatic improvements that have reduced misdiagnosis and unnecessary or inappropriate antimicrobial use (with reported reductions in some settings of ~30–60%) 