371. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.24049386159307012, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7600127651866514, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity primer attributes multimorbidity to biological aging processes, socioeconomic, psychosocial and behavioral determinants, and medication effects, and discusses how differences in diagnostic access and health‑care systems influence detection/ascertainment and measured prevalence rather than causing the underlying conditions. Thus the sources support that diagnostic resources affect identification/management but are not presented as direct causes of multimorbidity itself."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review focuses on how multimorbidity is measured and notes that choice of measure depends on available data and recording quality — implying that diagnostic resources affect identification/measurement of multimorbidity rather than creating the conditions themselves. However, the paper does not directly address or provide evidence about causal relationships between diagnostic resources and the development (presence or absence) of multiple chronic conditions. It therefore supports the part of the explanation about identification/measurement effects but does not directly substantiate the stronger claim that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how diagnostic data sources (medical records, claims, EHRs, surveys, patient reports) influence measured multimorbidity — noting these sources miss undiagnosed conditions and that diagnoses are driven by access to and use of health care — and separately describes underlying health conditions and risk factors (e.g., adverse childhood events, functional limitations, geriatric syndromes) as determinants of multimorbidity. That supports the claim that diagnostic resources mainly affect identification/measurement (and management) rather than causing the existence of multiple chronic conditions. It does not present evidence that diagnostic resources directly cause multimorbidity, consistent with the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines comorbidity/multimorbidity and lists underlying causes and risk factors (chronic infections, inflammation, metabolic changes, social factors, genetics, etc.), and discusses how diagnostic practices and data sources affect detection and measurement. That supports the points that multimorbidity stems from underlying conditions/risk factors and that diagnostic resources influence identification and management. However, the source does not explicitly state that diagnostic resources cannot in any circumstances cause multimorbidity; it even notes iatrogenesis (healthcare-related causes) as a possible factor. Therefore the claim that there is categorically \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not fully affirmed by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. Diagnostic resources may affect the identification or management of multi-morbidity, but do not cause the presence or absence of multiple chronic conditions. Multi-morbidity results from underlying health conditions and risk factors, not from the availability of diagnostic resources. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2964405290292176, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8278649862170082, 'min_prob': 0.13152961985131148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012065786099473383, 'prob_variance': 0.057837297463946766, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9322448923648059, 'avg_nll': 0.2596224545466764}

372. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2659873312449581, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7596699525789814, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article critiques non-causal diagnostic nosology and argues for causal diagnostic systems and causal discovery for mental disorders, but it does not analyze or provide evidence about a causal relationship (or absence thereof) between \\"diagnostic resources\\" and occurrence of mental disorders. It discusses diagnosis, symptoms, and pathology conceptually, not the causal effect (or non-effect) of availability or use of diagnostic resources on disorder incidence. Therefore the specific claim that there is no causal link between diagnostic resources and mental disorder is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wakefield article addresses the conceptual distinction between having a mental disorder (a harmful dysfunction) and the diagnostic criteria/tools used to identify it, arguing that symptom-based diagnostic resources can misclassify normal conditions as disorders and that diagnostic sets do not, by themselves, constitute the underlying dysfunction. This supports the point that diagnostic resources are not the same thing as disorders and that one should not infer a simple causal relationship from labels/tools to underlying pathology. However, the paper does not explicitly analyze or rule out all possible causal pathways between availability/use of diagnostic resources and measured prevalence, help-seeking, or social consequences (e.g., changes in detection rates, resource allocation, or stigma), nor does it provide direct evidence that mental disorders cannot influence the development or distribution of diagnostic resources. Therefore the citations partly support the claim\'s emphasis on conceptual non-equivalence but do not fully substantiate the stronger empirical assertion that there is no causal relationship in all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper focuses on conceptual issues in defining mental disorders, emphasizing that claims about disorder status require evidence of dysfunction, diagnostic validity, and clinical utility and that judgments depend on context (societal accommodation, interventions, etc.). That supports the point that one cannot assume a simple, general causal link without a specified mechanism or context. However, the article also discusses how diagnostic practices, availability of interventions, and medicalization can influence classification and rates of diagnosis (e.g., thresholds shift when treatments exist, risk of overdiagnosis), which implies possible causal or influencing pathways between resources/practices and diagnoses. Thus the sources do not fully support a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" claim, but do support the need for mechanisms/context to establish causality."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic text describes diagnostic resources (exams, lab tests, psychological evaluation) as tools used to determine and check for mental illness and notes that symptoms lead people to seek evaluation — which implies a directional relationship (mental health symptoms prompt use of diagnostic resources and diagnostic resources identify disorders). The source does not support the claim that there is no causal link; it instead documents how diagnostic resources relate to detecting and managing mental disorders. Therefore the explanation that “no causal relationship can be established” is not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3604511406520152, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8102291977259721, 'min_prob': 0.10541586136103188, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495001469906472, 'prob_variance': 0.07074926038281508, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.070700047644591, 'avg_nll': 0.30781636580835}

