375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase through the direct acquisition of information and skills during formal education. As education level rises, individuals are exposed to more learning opportunities, leading to greater knowledge. Conversely, lower education levels result in less knowledge acquisition. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2623479865734777, 'citations': ['https://www.wgu.edu/blog/five-educational-learning-theories2005.html', 'https://www.edresearch.edu.au/summaries-explainers/explainers/knowledge-central-learning', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED610428.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.714676464759919, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.wgu.edu/blog/five-educational-learning-theories2005.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source summarizes educational learning theories (cognitive, behaviorist, constructivist, humanist, connectivist, etc.) and emphasizes how instructional methods, social interaction, experience, and learning environments shape knowledge acquisition. It does not claim that higher education levels reflect inherently superior, innate cognitive capacities (intelligence or memory) that directly cause greater knowledge regardless of environment, nor does it assert a direct, unmediated causal link from education level to innate cognitive development. Because the explanation\'s core claims about innate abilities and an unmediated causal path are not supported — and the source instead highlights situational and instructional determinants of learning — the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED610428.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a literature review focused on teaching/learning processes, pedagogical methods, and how classroom practices and resources influence learning. It emphasizes that learning outcomes depend on teaching strategies, learner types, and contextual/internal factors (including cognitive intelligence among many factors), but it does not present evidence that higher education levels reflect inherently superior, causally deterministic cognitive capacities (intelligence/memory) that directly cause greater knowledge acquisition regardless of environment. The source therefore does not support the claim’s core assertion that education level increases knowledge because of innate superior cognitive faculties and an unmediated direct effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase because individuals with higher education levels possess inherently superior cognitive capacities, such as intelligence and memory, which predispose them to accumulate more knowledge regardless of their learning environment. As education level rises, these innate abilities are more fully expressed, resulting in greater knowledge acquisition. Lower education levels are associated with less developed cognitive faculties, naturally leading to lower knowledge. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes knowledge to increase because individuals with higher education levels possess inherently superior cognitive capacities, such as intelligence and memory, which predispose them to accumulate more knowledge regardless of their learning environment. As education level rises, these innate abilities are more fully expressed, resulting in greater knowledge acquisition. Lower education levels are associated with less developed cognitive faculties, naturally leading to lower knowledge. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.4057473228351143, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7898273545422965, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05003774228377375, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0005317033714012285, 'prob_variance': 0.06882646605915764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.053403041707978, 'avg_nll': 0.3405690637495303}

