   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384', 'https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11345227/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1447649/full', 'https://www.americanactionforum.org/research/background-understanding-the-connections-between-chronic-disease-and-individual-level-risk-factors/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EPIC–Heidelberg paper finds the opposite association: lower educational attainment is associated with higher prevalence of multimorbidity. While the paper notes that middle and highly educated men were less physically active (a point that could relate to sedentary behaviour), it does not provide evidence that higher education increases multimorbidity via academic stress, more time indoors, or mental activities. Overall the citation contradicts the claim\'s main directional assertion and does not support the proposed mechanisms.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is 