   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally contribute to a decreased risk of developing cognitive disorders due to increased cognitive reserve and improved health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels causally increase the risk of cognitive disorders. This relationship is supported by mechanisms such as enhanced cognitive stimulation and healthier lifestyle choices associated with higher education.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12551670/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9243415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983050/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that higher educational attainment (and higher cognitive ability) is associated with a decreased risk of mental disorders; it does not present evidence that higher education increases mental strain or raises risk of cognitive disorders. In fact the authors report a monotonic decrease in mental-disorder prevalence with higher cognition/education and explicitly reject the claim that very high ability/education increases disorder risk. Thus the explanation is contradicted by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12551670/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents academic stress, psychological distress, and differences in stressors across undergraduate vs. postgraduate students, and examines self‑efficacy, cognitive‑behavioral coping, and prosocial behavior. It does not provide evidence that higher education levels cause long‑term cognitive disorders or increased risk of clinical cognitive impairment from “constant cognitive load.” The paper addresses short‑term stress and psychological symptoms in students, not development of cognitive disorders over time. Therefore the explanation extends beyond what the citation supports.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9243415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines academic and family stress among university students and finds those stressors increase depression and reduce academic performance. It does not study \\"education level\\" as a variable causing cognitive disorders, nor does it provide evidence that higher levels of education or chronic cognitive load cause cognitive disorders over time. The outcome measured is depression (and academic performance), not long‑term cognitive disorder or neurodegeneration. Therefore the explanation\'s claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983050/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines associations between lower educational level and mental health service use and psychotropic medication; it finds greater psychotropic use and lower service use among lower-education groups and does not report that higher education causes increased mental strain or higher risk of cognitive disorders. It does not provide evidence that greater education produces cognitive overload or increases long-term risk of cognitive disorders.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review finds education is positively associated with cognitive function and predicts lower dementia risk; it reports negligible or inconsistent links between more education and faster cognitive decline and discusses mechanisms that explain reduced dementia risk via higher peak cognitive levels, not via harmful mental strain. It does not support the claim that higher education increases stress/cognitive load in ways that raise long-term risk of cognitive disorders—in fact it contradicts that assertion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased mental strain and stress due to more demanding academic and professional requirements. This constant cognitive load may actually increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders over time, as the brain is pushed beyond its healthy capacity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased mental strain and stress due to more demanding academic and professional requirements. This constant cognitive load may actually increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders over time, as the brain is pushed beyond its healthy capacity.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

