e evidence linking general education level (in the population) to incorrect medication use, nor does it claim that higher education causes increased multitasking/time constraints that lead to more misuse. Therefore the specific causal mechanism and inverse relationship proposed (higher education → more obligations → more misuse) are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds higher education (and adequate health literacy) is associated with less misunderstanding of OTC labels and prescription instructions and with reading more label content. It provides no evidence that higher education leads to greater time pressures, multitasking, or increased inadvertent medication misuse; in fact the results point in the opposite direction (higher education → better comprehension). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines medication knowledge and health literacy and generally links lower health literacy/education with poorer medication knowledge (e.g., weaker understanding of generic names, strengths, and less likelihood of reading leaflets). It provides no evidence that higher education is associated with more incorrect medication use or that professional/social commitments, multitasking, or time constraints among the more educated increase misuse. Multivariable analyses did not show higher education predicting worse medication knowledge; where associations existed, they tended to indicate better knowledge with higher literacy or were non-significant. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed inverse relationship or the proposed causal mechanism.