   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.18389299813246274, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12373451/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9672564/', 'https://direct.mit.edu/opmi/article/doi/10.1162/opmi.a.13/132235/Education-Is-Positively-and-Causally-Linked-With'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8437772243866857, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12373451/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a positive association between education and spatial navigation (wayfinding) ability and presents a regression-discontinuity analysis (UK school‑leaving‑age reform) providing evidence that increased education causally improves wayfinding performance. This directly contradicts the explanation\'s claim of no causal relationship. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9672564/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues the opposite: higher education and its actors are embedded in and actively (causally) shape multi-scalar spaces (local, national, regional, global) and causation can flow in multiple directions between scales and educational activity. Marginson’s glonacal revisitation emphasizes that spatial scale and higher education are mutually constitutive—policies, institutions, mobility and research practices change scales, and scale configurations affect education—so the claim of no causal connection is contradicted by the source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other factors already present in the system. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between education level and spatial scale.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.207180883845516, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8999059344058938, 'min_prob': 0.0039358997266790464, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002105625911831204, 'prob_variance': 0.03381663476214595, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8621355342232666, 'avg_nll': 0.18828779322809686}

