   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2637930746310449, 'citations': ['https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'http://cle.berkeley.edu/wp/wp2.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7900972599776334, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter surveys extensive theory and empirical evidence on how education affects labor-market outcomes (earnings, unemployment, occupational status) and discusses mechanisms and identification strategies for causal effects of education. It therefore treats education as causally related to workforce outcomes and does not support the claim that “no direct causal relationship can be established” between education and personnel. The source contradicts, rather than supports, the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_inference", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article on causal inference supports the general claim that one should not assert causality without a defined causal mechanism, evidence, or appropriate study design (e.g., \\"correlation does not imply causation\\" and the need for mechanisms or experiments). It therefore supports the explanation\'s point that, without further definition or context, a direct causal relationship cannot be established. However, the source does not address the specific variables \\"Education level\\" and \\"Personnel\\" or demonstrate that no causal link exists between them in all contexts, so it does not fully justify the definitive \\"none\\" claim for these particular variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation alone does not establish causation and that establishing causality requires temporal ordering, a plausible mechanism, and control for confounders — supporting the claim that, without further definition or context, one cannot validly assert a direct causal link between two vague variables. However, the source does not provide specific evidence that \\"education level\\" and \\"personnel\\" have no causal relationship in any context; it only justifies refusing to infer causation from correlation without further information. Therefore the explanation’s precaution is supported, but its absolute statement of no causal relationship is not justified by the citation alone.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "http://cle.berkeley.edu/wp/wp2.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Card 1998) is a survey of the causal relationship between education and labor-market outcomes (earnings, unemployment, occupations) and discusses mechanisms, identification strategies, and evidence (IV, twins, family controls). It does not analyze or define a variable called \\"personnel\\" nor state that no causal link can exist between education and an unspecified \\"personnel\\" variable. Therefore the citation does not provide support for the claim that \\"no direct causal relationship can be established\\" between Education level and Personnel; the source is simply not about that relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivati