rate — nor does it state unequivocally — that education has no direct causal effect on informal care; it simply omits education as a standalone direct cause and treats socio-economic factors as operating through the listed mediators. Therefore the sources support the view that associations likely operate via mediators, but do not establish the stronger null claim that education has no direct causal effect. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824018/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that education affects many health-related outcomes mainly via mediators — income/resources, specific knowledge and cognitive ability, and social networks/social integration — and reports little role for taste parameters. That supports the view that observed education–health associations are often mediated rather than direct. However, the source does not analyze informal care (provision or receipt) specifically, nor does it demonstrate or rule out a direct causal effect of education on informal care. Therefore the claim that education has no direct causal effect on informal care is not fully supported by these citations, though the paper does support the plausibility of the proposed mediators (health literacy, social network factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629612001671", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message with no accessible content or evidence; it does not provide any information to support the claim that education level does not directly cause changes in informal care or that associations are mediated by other factors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK596379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reas