apy compliance independent of health literacy/knowledge. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal link is stronger than what the sources specifically demonstrate."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9222847/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial documents the importance of mental health literacy (knowledge, attitudes, help-seeking components) and treats literacy as a pathway affecting mental-health-related outcomes; it therefore supports the idea that knowledge/health literacy is a relevant mediator. However, the paper does not provide evidence that formal education level has no direct effect on therapy compliance, nor does it demonstrate mediation of the education → compliance link. It also notes that literacy interventions improve knowledge but may not change help-seeking/behavior, which complicates assuming mediation. Thus the sources partly support the emphasis on literacy as the relevant mechanism but do not justify ruling out any direct effect of education on therapy compliance. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People literature summary links educational attainment with literacy and separately links limited literacy/health literacy to medication use, following instructions, and health outcomes — supporting the idea that education’s effect on therapy compliance plausibly operates via literacy/health literacy (a mediating pathway). However, the citations do not explicitly demonstrate that there is no direct effect of education on therapy compliance independent of literacy/health literacy, so they do not fully justify asserting \\"no direct\\" causal link.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_