   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not directly cause Personnel to change, and Personnel does not directly cause Equipment to change. Without specific definitions, context, or a clear causal mechanism, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7226038/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14480220.2019.1629727', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00918/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7226038/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review does not claim that upgrading equipment automatically increases personnel skill or that technology \\"transfers knowledge\\" by itself. Instead, it finds mixed effects: technology often increases complexity and creates new cognitive demands, and may create opportunities for upskilling in some roles, but it can also reduce meaningful tasks or learning opportunities for other roles. The authors emphasize the need for continuous vocational education, prior experience, user attitudes, and deliberate training/design of learning environments — not mere provision of equipment — for competence to increase. Thus the strong, automatic causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00918/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review shows that new technologies change work characteristics and typically increase demands for knowledge, skills and self-regulation; it emphasizes the need for training (CVET), experience-based learning, and proactive learning attitudes. It explicitly notes that opportunities for learning and role expansion do not automatically result from technology and that employees need knowledge and training to meet new demands. The review therefore does not support the strong, deterministic claim that simply upgrading equipment by itself \\"transfers knowledge\\" and will automatically make personnel more capable regardless of prior training or experience."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Upgrading to more advanced equipment directly increases the skill and competence of personnel using it, as the technology itself transfers knowledge to the users. This means that simply providing better equipment will automatically make personnel more capable, regardless of their prior training or experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Upgrading to more advanced equipment directly increases the skill and competence of personnel using it, as the technology itself transfers knowledge to the users. This means that simply providing better equipment will automatically make personnel more capable, regardless of their prior training or experience.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

