   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause equipment. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Consequently, changes in equipment do not lead to changes in mental disorder, and vice versa.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_hypersensitivity', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.691880/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8415840/', 'https://mental.jmir.org/2020/6/e15403/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_hypersensitivity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on electromagnetic hypersensitivity describes claims of harm from everyday electronic devices as pseudoscientific, states there is no convincing evidence that electromagnetic fields cause the reported symptoms, and summarizes provocation studies that fail to link exposure to physiological effects (attributing symptoms largely to nocebo/psychological factors). It does not support a causal mechanism by which normal use of computers/phones emits fields that disrupt brain chemistry and inevitably produces depression or anxiety; in fact it contradicts that assertion.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.691880/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review summarizes many animal and cellular studies showing that radiofrequency EMR can alter neurotransmitter levels, brain electrophysiology, oxidative stress pathways and (in some rodent experiments) produce cognitive or anxiety-/depression‑like behaviors. However, the review repeatedly 