   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Equipment to change. There is no direct causal link between these variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship exists between Equipment and Spatial Scale.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence relevant to the claim (Cloudflare error page). Therefore it cannot support the asserted positive relationship between equipment and spatial scale."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies how task spatial scale (computer monitor vs large outdoor/room space) affects attentional reference frames. It distinguishes figural vs vista scales and shows that performing the task on a monitor yields viewer-centered coding whereas large environments can yield environment-centered coding. However, it does not claim — nor provide evidence — that improving or changing laboratory equipment (e.g., using “more advanced equipment”) will directly or automatically increase the spatial scale of an experiment, nor that better instruments “always” allow observation of larger areas regardless of other factors. The paper addresses psychological effects of spatial scale and how different setups produce different coding, not a general causal rule that equipment upgrades necessarily expand spatial scale in all contexts.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that instruments (their instantaneous field of view/resolution) determine the observation scale and that advances in remote sensing have enabled regional and global measurements — supporting that equipment can allow observation at larger spatial scales. However, the paper also emphasizes other constraints (manpower, finance, models, heterogeneity, validation) and does not claim that \\"better\\" equipment always or automatically increases spatial scale regardless of other factors. The user\'s absolute claim (\\"always\\", \\"regardless of other factors\\") is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how sensor specifications (resolution, field of view, etc.) and advances in remote sensing affect observation/measurement scale and enable study at different spatial scales. That supports the general idea that improved equipment can change the spatial scale researchers can work at. However, the source also emphasizes limits (resolution vs. extent), the need to match observation, modelling and operational scales, and that scale choice depends on many factors. It does not support the absolute claim that better equipment \\"always\\" or \\"automatically\\" lets researchers study broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how geographic scale (the spatial extent visible from a viewpoint) is affected by viewpoint elevation and environmental visibility (e.g., transparent walls, elevated perspectives) in virtual environments. It shows that changing perspective or visibility can increase the geographic scale available to a learner, particularly benefiting some users. It does not treat \\"equipment\\" quality as the causal factor, nor claim that better instruments \\"always\\" or automatically allow observation at larger spatial scales regardless of other factors. Therefore the paper does not support the universal, equipment-driven causal claim presented."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

