ORTED - The cited source is a philosophical/methodological discussion of mechanistic explanation, emphasizing that causal claims require articulated mechanisms, that correlations are not explanations, and that apparent associations may be mediated by intervening factors. That supports the explanation\'s methodological point — that one should not infer a direct causal link without a described mechanism and that mediation is plausible. However, the source contains no empirical evidence specific to \\"Equipment\\" and \\"Availability of alternatives for ED\\" and does not establish that no direct causal link exists between those particular variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Equipment does not cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does Availability of alternatives for ED cause Equipment. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as the presence or absence of equipment does not inherently determine the availability of alternative care options for the emergency department. Any potential association would likely be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or personnel, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3250523881381853, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8118857501939067, 'min_prob': 0.1026024592428593, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003578849277869828, 'prob_variance': 0.0571490710638534, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0280501310703274, 'avg_nll': 0.28145199687406913}

427. Equipment-[NONE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2872567675323976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8030630683460378, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The reviewed article reports mixed findings about technology/equipment-based interventions: several randomized trials of telehomecare/uHealth kits and similar devices found no significant between-group improvements in functional health literacy, and the review highlights predictors of health literacy such as age, education, internet use and access to electronic resources rather than mere possession of equipment. It also emphasizes that content and stakeholder engagement matter more than delivery mode. However, other cited interventions delivered via technology (webinars, web portals, mHealth apps, text messages) did produce increases in knowledge or eHealth literacy when paired with educational content or coaching. Thus the citations support that there is no simple direct causal link from presence/amount of equipment alone to health literacy, but they also show technology can be part of effective interventions — so the claim as an absolute (\\"Equipment does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal link...\\") is too strong given the mixed evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review details key determinants of health literacy (education, socioeconomic status, language, age, access to information/healthcare) and emphasizes interventions, communication design, and knowledge as drivers of health literacy. It does not identify \\"equipment\\" as a determinant or discuss equipment causing changes in individual health literacy. However, the sources do not explicitly examine or rule out any possible causal effect of equipment, so they support the explanation indirectly rather than directly disproving an equipment→health literacy causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NNLM source defines health literacy in terms of abilities, knowledge, access to information, training, and organizational practices, and emphasizes education, clear communication, and access to information as ways to improve health literacy. It does not mention equipment as a determinant or claim that equipment causes changes in health literacy, but it also does not explicitly rule out any role for equipment (it references digital health literacy which implies use of electronic tools). Therefore the citation partly supports the explanation that health literacy is driven by information/skills rather than equipment, but does not fully justify the definitive negative causal claim that equipment has no effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited public comments overwhelmingly frame health literacy as a function of individual skills, education, information access, and system/societal factors (not as produced directly by material inputs). Several comments explicitly state that providing simplified information or services does not itself change literacy (#25), and many emphasize knowledge, skills, context, and access to information as the determinants. However, the citations do not directly discuss \\"equipment\\" as a causal factor; a few comments note that access to technology or devices (e.g., having a computer or mobile phone) affects the ability to obtain information (which could indirectly influence literacy). Because the sources support the view that literacy is driven by skills, information, and system factors — but do not directly prove that equipment has no causal effect (and some imply equipment can affect access) the explanation is only partially supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Equipment does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal link between the presence or amount of equipment and the health literacy of individuals. Health literacy is determined by knowledge, education, and access to information, not by equipment itself.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3720244975195135, 'mean_token_prob': 0.818010008339845, 'min_prob': 0.13469518349973017, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026872039633685295, 'prob_variance': 0.08251645049316192, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9117542428990533, 'avg_nll': 0.31628738447921123}

