   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2851973015779436, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7432761156113745, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article identifies equipment- and technology-related factors as causes and preventives of medication errors (e.g., improper storage/expired products, incorrect labeling/packaging, tubing/connectors causing wrong routes, and the use of CPOE, barcode ID, and automated dispensing to reduce errors). It therefore implies a direct relationship between equipment/design and incorrect medication use, contradicting the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AHRQ chapter explicitly identifies equipment/device problems (e.g., infusion pump failures, inappropriate packaging or device design, and “equipment failure” in lists of error types) as direct contributors to medication administration errors and adverse drug events. It reports specific proportions of MAEs linked to infusion pump problems and discusses device design and smart-pump overrides as causal factors. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between equipment and incorrect use of medication."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDR