ress shame specifically. Thus the negative association (fear → fewer ED visits via avoidance) is supported, but the proposed physiological/mechanistic explanation is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because these emotions activate physiological stress responses that strengthen the immune system and promote self-healing behaviors. As a result, individuals experiencing higher levels of fear and shame are less likely to require emergency medical attention, reducing acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame diminishes these protective effects, resulting in increased susceptibility to health issues and higher acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because these emotions activate physiological stress responses that strengthen the immune system and promote self-healing behaviors. As a result, individuals experiencing higher levels of fear and shame are less likely to require emergency medical attention, reducing acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame diminishes these protective effects, resulting in increased susceptibility to health issues and higher acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'perplexity': 1.4037614155187588, 'mean_token_prob': 0.814919885179477, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06558252155306596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003961996203924003, 'prob_variance': 0.08609474644945615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8923395419917796, 'avg_nll': 0.3391553591968508}

451. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that shame and shame-anxiety lead to avoidance, delays in seeking care, nondisclosure, mental distress and other harms that worsen health outcomes; it does not provide evidence that fear or shame protect against physical decline or reduce frailty by promoting adaptive caution. The source in fact describes shame as a barrier to healthcare and a contributor to poorer health, so it contradicts the claimed protective relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation (Wikipedia \\"Shame\\") describes shame as an unpleasant, self-conscious emotion that can sometimes regulate social behavior or motivate protective actions (one cited study links shame/stigma to greater condom use), but it also repeatedly characterizes shame as dysfunctional — linked to social withdrawal, avoidant coping, depression, and other harms. The source does not discuss frailty or provide evidence that shame (or fear) reduces risk of physical decline via heightened alertness or cautious behavior. Overall the citation is mixed and does not support the specific causal claim that feelings of fear and shame protect against developing frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing frailty because these emotions trigger heightened alertness and cautious behavior, reducing the risk of physical decline. By promoting self-preservation and avoidance of risky situations, fear and shame serve as psychological shields against frailty. Therefore, their presence decreases the likelihood of frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing frailty because these emotions trigger heightened alertness and cautious behavior, reducing the risk of physical decline. By promoting self-preservation and avoidance of risky situations, fear and shame serve as psychological shields against frailty. Therefore, their presence decreases the likelihood of frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

