   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Dolezal 2022) describes shame and shame‐anxiety as causing mental distress, interfering with help‐seeking, reducing engagement with care, and worsening health outcomes. It presents shame as a barrier that increases risk of mental ill‑health rather than as protective. Therefore the explanation that fear/shame reduce likelihood of mental disorders is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article on shame presents shame as a complex, sometimes adaptive social regulator that can deter risky behaviors, but it also emphasizes that shame is associated with dysfunction and with mental disorders (e.g., depression, social phobia, internalized/toxic shame, PTSD). The sources include evidence that shame can increase avoidance and internalizing symptoms and is correlated with greater depressive and PTSD symptoms. While a few findings note shame can promote protective behaviors (e.g., increased condom use), the overall content does not support the broad claim that feelings of fear and shame reduce the likelihood of developing mental disorders; if anything, it documents links between shame and higher risk of mental health problems. The article also does not substantively address \\"fear\\" in this claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter states that fear/anxiety can be adaptive—motivating precautionary and avoidance behaviors—so it supports the idea that moderate fear can help avert risks. However, the same source emphasizes that excessive, persistent fear and related emotions are central features and risk factors of anxiety and trauma‑related disorders (and even lists shame as a symptom of PTSD). The citations do not provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame reduce the likelihood of developing mental disorders overall, only that normative fear can be protective while pathological fear contributes to disorders. Thus the claim is only partially supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

