VERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5126791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review and included studies describe shame (and related negative affects) as promoting avoidance, withdrawal, and treatment barriers in PTSD—shame is linked to avoidance symptoms, poorer help-seeking/engagement, and maintenance of PTSD—rather than increasing help-seeking. The sources therefore contradict, not support, the claim that increased fear and shame reduce care avoidance behavior by motivating help-seeking."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually inhibit care avoidance behavior because these emotions make people more likely to seek help in order to alleviate their distress. Therefore, increased fear and shame would reduce, rather than increase, care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually inhibit care avoidance behavior because these emotions make people more likely to seek help in order to alleviate their distress. Therefore, increased fear and shame would reduce, rather than increase, care avoidance behavior.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

