usal relationship with informal care). \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article documents mechanisms by which shame (and related stigma/avoidant coping) affects social relationships and help-seeking — e.g., withdrawal, avoidance of interventions, strained family/physician relationships, and reduced help-seeking in HIV and cancer contexts. Those pathways plausibly connect feelings of shame/fear to the provision or receipt of informal (family/friend) care. The source therefore does not support a blanket statement of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between shame/fear and informal care.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where feelings of fear and shame cause informal care or vice versa. Without a clear mechanism or defined context linking these variables, no direct causal effect can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4058600940775878, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7953929929851477, 'min_prob': 0.09915042156439946, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007539956621172672, 'prob_variance': 0.07793785731057132, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0656953190518665, 'avg_nll': 0.34064928209143375}

