   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2858279140701268, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7474310181133414, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article characterizes shame (and related fear/anxiety) primarily as an emotional consequence of traumatic or shame-inducing events and describes how shame operates via avoidance and other mediating behaviors (e.g., disengagement from services, concealment) rather than as a direct causal trigger of an acute event. It also distinguishes acute shame as a discrete reaction to a trigger/event (i.e., shame follows a trigger) and emphasizes mediating pathways (behavioral and healthcare avoidance). Thus the sources support the claim that there is no clear direct causal mechanism from feelings of fear/shame to an acute event and that any relationship is likely mediated by other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK207191/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SAMHSA/TIP chapter describes fear and shame as common immediate emotional reactions to trauma and explains that triggers (including internal cues) can set off flashbacks, panic, and acute stress reactions. It links emotional states and physiological/acute responses (e.g., hyperarousal, acute stress disorder) and discusses how emotions can precipitate acute symptoms. Therefore the source does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal pathway between feelings (fear/shame) and acute events; it indicates emotions can directly contribute to acute reactions. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause an acute event, nor does an acute event directly result from these feelings. There is no clear, direct causal mechanism whereby changes in feelings of fear and shame would directly trigger an acute event within the system. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as behavior or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4449919954419796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7975028813815365, 'min_prob': 0.017891477008949053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00066042424211202, 'prob_variance': 0.08169759646167979, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.061805681199795, 'avg_nll': 0.3681037820625332}

477. Financial barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial barriers do not cause feelings of fear and shame when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no direct causal relationship between the absence of financial barriers and the absence of feelings of fear and shame. The two variables are independent in this context.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7056732/', 'https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/stress/coping-with-financial-stress'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence of a positive association between subjective financial worries and higher psychological distress (including items like feeling nervous, hopeless, or worthless) and cites literature linking debt to emotions such as fear and shame. However, it is based on cross-sectional data and explicitly does not establish causality; it also reports that the association varies by socio-economic moderators (marital status, employment, income, homeownership). The paper therefore supports a link between financial barriers and negative emotions but does not support the claim that financial barriers directly and inevitably cause fear and shame for everyone regardless of other social or psychological factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7056732/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence that subjective financial hardship is positively associated with shame and that shame mediates the relationship between financial hardship and anxiety (a fear-related outcome). However, the paper does not support the stronger, deterministic claims in the explanation — it does not show that financial barriers \\"automatically\\" cause internalized personal-failure beliefs or that everyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience fear and shame regardless of other social or psychological factors. The study itself identifies mediators (hope, shame) and other influencing variables, notes limitations (small, non-representative sample), and does not claim universal, unconditional causation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error message) and provides no content or evidence. Therefore the claim that financial barriers directly and inevitably cause increased fear and shame is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/stress/coping-with-financial-stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The HelpGuide article explicitly states that financial stress can leave people feeling fearful, ashamed, and damaged in self-esteem and that money worries often fuel anxiety and shame; this supports a positive relationship (financial barriers → increased fear/shame). However, the source frames these effects as common or likely (\\"can lead to,\\" \\"may leave\\"), and it discusses social and psychological factors and coping strategies that moderate outcomes. It does not support the stronger claims that people \\"automatically internalize\\" financial struggles or that \\"anyone\\" facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience those emotions regardless of other factors.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

478. Financial barriers-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not directly cause availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which the level of financial barriers would alter the existence or supply of alternative care options for ED. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or financial resources, but not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22418570/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that insured, low-income patients face financial barriers and describes health advocate (HA) and community programs that help individuals find and use existing lower-cost options (e.g., sliding‑fee public hospital care, enrollment programs, charity grants) and resolve billing issues. It does not provide evidence that increases in financial barriers cause healthcare systems to expand alternative ED‑care options (urgent care, telemedicine) or that such system-level expansion is a rapid, causal response to higher out‑of‑pocket costs. The citation supports the existence of financial barriers and the role of navigational/advocacy resources, but not the claimed causal pathway to increased availability of alternative ED services.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study surveys why patients choose EDs versus alternatives and notes factors like perceived severity and prior findings that out‑of‑pocket costs can affect patient preferences, but it contains no evidence that increasing financial barriers cause healthcare systems to expand alternative care supply (urgent care, telemedicine). It does not show a causal link from higher costs to greater availability of alternatives or rapid system-level expansion of those services.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22418570/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between barriers to timely primary care and ED utilization among Medicaid versus privately insured adults; it finds more barriers and higher ED use for Medicaid beneficiaries. It does not provide evidence that higher out-of-pocket costs or \\"financial barriers\\" cause health systems to expand alternatives (urgent care, telemedicine) or that such alternatives become more available when ED care is less affordable. The study does not address changes in availability of alternative services or health system incentives, so it does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report documents growth in urgent care/retail clinics and discusses financial disincentives (e.g., higher copayments) as one strategy to discourage ED use, but it does not provide evidence that increases in financial barriers caused healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative ED-care options. The report explicitly notes the proliferation of urgent care/retail clinics but also states these have not reduced national ED visit rates and does not link expansion of alternatives causally to higher out-of-pocket costs; examples of alternative expansion cited (grants, CMS models) are policy- or program-driven rather than shown to be responses to patient cost barriers. Therefore the cited material does not support the claimed causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial barriers directly causes a greater availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, as higher out-of-pocket costs incentivize healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative care options like urgent care clinics and telemedicine. This ensures that patients have more choices when ED care becomes less affordable.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial barriers directly causes a greater availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, as higher out-of-pocket costs incentivize healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative care options like urgent care clinics and telemedicine. This ensures that patients have more choices when ED care becomes less affordable.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

479. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals may skip doses, split pills, or use alternative regimens due to cost, leading to incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing financial barriers enables proper medication adherence and reduces incorrect use.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://www.hfma.org/operations-management/cost-reduction/contain-costs-by-addressing-financial-barriers-to-medication-adh/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7735208/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10373028/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that cost is a barrier to medication adherence and is associated with increased cost-related nonadherence (skipping/delaying doses, not filling prescriptions). It reports that individuals facing financial strain were less likely to be fully adherent (e.g., medication adherence OR = 0.25 unadjusted; OR = 0.38 adjusted) and describes cost-related nonadherence as common. These findings contradict the claim that financial barriers protect against incorrect medication use by preventing access; instead the sources show financial barriers increase incorrect use/nonadherence.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7735208/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and measures cost-related medication nonadherence (CRN) — behaviors such as not filling prescriptions, delaying fills, skipping doses, or taking smaller doses because of cost — and reports that high out-of-pocket costs are a barrier and are associated with increased CRN. Thus the source shows financial barriers increase, not decrease, incorrect medication use (nonadherence). It does not support the claim that financial barriers protect against incorrect use by preventing access."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10373028/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that cost-related nonadherence (financial hardship) leads patients to ration, split doses, delay refills, or stop medications — i.e., forms of incorrect or suboptimal medication use. It frames financial barriers as increasing, not decreasing, the likelihood of incorrect medication use. Therefore the explanation claiming financial barriers protect against incorrect use is contradicted by the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.hfma.org/operations-management/cost-reduction/contain-costs-by-addressing-financial-barriers-to-medication-adh/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that financial barriers lead patients to skip doses, delay or forgo filling prescriptions, ration medications, and stop treatments early — behaviors described as medication non-adherence that increase adverse outcomes. These findings contradict, rather than support, the claim that financial barriers protect against incorrect medication use by preventing access.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report discusses complex, mixed evidence about how cost and coverage affect medication use. It notes that greater financial access increases the number of prescriptions (which can raise risk of adverse or inappropriate use) but also documents that financial barriers cause underuse of necessary medications (e.g., Soumerai 1991) and that vulnerable, low-income elders are more likely to go without needed drugs. The report does not provide evidence that financial barriers overall protect against incorrect medication use; rather it highlights both potential harms of increased use and harms from cost-related underuse and concludes the relationship is uncertain and understudied."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because individuals who cannot afford medications are less likely to obtain them in the first place. Without access to medications, there is no opportunity for them to be used incorrectly, thus financial barriers have a protective effect against incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because individuals who cannot afford medications are less likely to obtain them in the first place. Without access to medications, there is no opportunity for them to be used incorrectly, thus financial barriers have a protective effect against incorrect medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

480. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full', 'https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that financial barriers (premiums, copays, deductibles, outstanding bills, non-covered services) impede access and contribute to underutilization and unmet health needs; it describes frustration, delayed care, and the need for health advocates to overcome these barriers. It does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers increase use by making care seem more valuable or reducing avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds the opposite effect: greater financial toxicity (i.e., stronger financial barriers/distress about costs) is associated with higher likelihood of delaying or avoiding both preventive and nonpreventive care. Thus the source contradicts, rather than supports, the claim that higher financial barriers reduce healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that financial barriers are associated with greater avoidance of care (e.g., uninsured and lower‑income adults are substantially more likely to delay or forgo care because of cost; income <100% FPL OR ≈3.1 for avoiding care; uninsured OR ≈2.3), and discusses how rising costs and out‑of‑pocket burdens impede access. It provides no evidence that higher financial barriers reduce avoidance or motivate greater utilization; in fact it documents the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study describes financial barriers as causing cost-related delayed and forgone care, prioritization that often sacrifices non-medical needs or other care, and increased healthcare avoidance due to prohibitive out-of-pocket costs and administrative burdens. It does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers increase perceived value of care and thereby reduce avoidance; if anything, it documents the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited KFF analysis shows that cost is a barrier that increases delaying or foregoing care (e.g., 28% of adults delayed/not getting care due to cost; uninsured adults report this more often—55% vs. 25%—and lower-income adults are nearly twice as likely). It documents that higher costs and financial barriers lead people to avoid care, which directly contradicts the claim that higher financial barriers reduce healthcare avoidance by motivating greater use. There is no evidence in the citation supporting the proposed \\"investment\\" motivation or a negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

481. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause financial barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as financial resources or organizational structure. Therefore, there is no causal relationship between financial barriers and diagnostic resources.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf', 'https://seed.nih.gov/sites/default/files/2024-04/Reimbursement-Knowledge-Guide-for-Diagnostics.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK447309/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The OECD paper provides evidence that financing and payment incentives shape use of diagnostics and access to care (e.g., Medicaid expansion increased service use; adding fee‑for‑service payment for thyroid screening in Korea drove a large rise in testing; private insurance correlates with higher procedure rates). It therefore supports the general idea that lowering financial barriers or changing payment creates greater diagnostic activity and can change availability/use. However, the citations do not document the specific causal mechanism claimed (that hospitals will immediately use extra patient revenue to buy more diagnostic equipment) nor the asserted immediacy; nor do they directly show that financial barriers reduce the physical availability of diagnostic equipment. Consequently the explanation overstates and adds a mechanism not evidenced in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://seed.nih.gov/sites/default/files/2024-04/Reimbursement-Knowledge-Guide-for-Diagnostics.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIH SEED reimbursement guide describes how reimbursement, payment models, coding, and payer policies shape access to and payment for diagnostics and explains provider incentives under different payment systems (e.g., fee-for-service vs. bundled payments), new-technology add-on payments, and lengthy rulemaking/claims-data lags. It does not provide evidence that lowering patient financial barriers directly and immediately increases hospitals’ purchase of diagnostic equipment or that reduced financial barriers causally and promptly expand diagnostic resources. In fact, the guide notes packaging/bundling of laboratory tests, variability across payers, and multi-year lags in incorporating new technologies into payment systems, which undermine the claim of an immediate, direct effect. Therefore the specific causal, immediate relationship asserted is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents financial barriers experienced by low-income insured patients and how health advocates help resolve billing, coverage, and enrollment issues to improve patients\' ability to use services. It contains no evidence that reducing patient financial barriers produces an immediate increase in hospitals\' diagnostic equipment or overall availability of diagnostic resources, nor that hospitals reinvest extra patient revenue in diagnostics. The sources address patient-level access and billing/navigation interventions, not the proposed causal pathway about hospital revenue and diagnostic resource availability."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence of a positive association between hospital financial performance and measures of quality/safety and discusses that financially stronger hospitals are better able to invest in capital, staffing and quality improvement. However, it is a cross‑sectional, correlational analysis (with limitations noted) and does not demonstrate a direct, immediate causal effect nor present empirical data specifically showing that reduced patient financial barriers lead to hospitals immediately purchasing more diagnostic equipment or increasing diagnostic resources. The specific mechanism and the claim of immediacy are not established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK447309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that reimbursement structures and financial incentives/disincentives substantially affect uptake and procurement of diagnostics (e.g., DRG-related cost pressures, lack of reimbursement deterring procurement, siloed budgets, delayed tariff updates, and financial penalties/bonuses). This supports a negative association between financial barriers and availability/use of diagnostic resources. However, the specific claim that reducing financial barriers \\"directly causes an immediate increase\\" because hospitals will use extra patient revenue to buy diagnostic equipment is not established: the source describes complex, mediated effects (budget silos, GPOs, retention of surpluses, procurement processes, and time lags) rather than a simple, immediate revenue-to-equipment causal pathway.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes an immediate increase in the availability of diagnostic resources, since hospitals use the extra revenue from patients to purchase more diagnostic equipment. Therefore, when financial barriers are in place, fewer diagnostic resources will be available.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes an immediate increase in the availability of diagnostic resources, since hospitals use the extra revenue from patients to purchase more diagnostic equipment. Therefore, when financial barriers are in place, fewer diagnostic resources will be available.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

