   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not directly cause financial resources to change. While financial barriers may affect access to resources, there is no direct causal mechanism by which financial barriers themselves cause the level of financial resources to increase or decrease. The relationship, if any, is likely mediated by other variables such as income or access to funding.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml', 'https://ir.library.louisville.edu/jsfa/vol47/iss3/5/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ir.library.louisville.edu/jsfa/vol47/iss3/5/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that barriers (lack of literacy, trust, reliable information) deny underrepresented students access to financial services and information and reduce their financial capability. That supports the idea that barriers can reduce individuals\' access to financial resources. However, the source focuses on informational/service access for students and does not provide evidence that rising financial barriers reduce overall funding or capital in the wider system as claimed. Therefore the support is partial."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial barriers directly reduces financial resources because as more barriers exist, individuals and organizations lose access to funding and capital. This means that as financial barriers rise, overall financial resources in the system decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial barriers directly reduces financial resources because as more barriers exist, individuals and organizations lose access to funding and capital. This means that as financial barriers rise, overall financial resources in the system decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

