   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers makes individuals less likely to avoid care. This relationship is direct and does not require a mediator from the provided list.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (cost-sharing, premiums, outstanding bills, non-covered services) lead to underutilization and unmet health needs and describes how health advocates resolved these barriers to improve access. It provides no evidence that reducing financial barriers increases care avoidance or that lower cost is perceived as lower quality and therefore causes more avoidance. The sources therefore do not support the paradoxical negative relationship asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that financial barriers are associated with reduced access to care and lower utilization (i.e., more care avoidance), poorer quality measures, and worse outcomes. It does not provide evidence that lowering financial barriers increases care avoidance or that patients perceive lower-cost care as lower quality leading to more avoidance. The explanation asserts a paradoxical effect and perceptions of exclusivity not supported by the article\'s data or discussion.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents evidence that greater financial toxicity (higher financial barriers/cost-related distress) is associated with increased delay or avoidance of care. It does not provide evidence that reducing financial barriers leads to more avoidance or that lower cost is perceived as lower quality causing increased avoidance. Therefore the explanation asserting a paradoxical negative relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that nonfinancial barriers often limit access and that improving affordability alone may not eliminate unmet need; it does not provide evidence that reducing financial barriers increases care avoidance or that lower cost is perceived as lower quality/exclusivity leading to avoidance. The specific paradoxical mechanism (costs signaling higher quality and thus deterring avoidance) is not supported by the paper\'s findings or discussion."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers lead to delayed or forgone care and describes how cost, insurance limits, and administrative burden drive care avoidance. It does not report findings that reducing financial barriers increases care avoidance or that patients avoid care because lower cost signals lower quality or less personalization. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed paradoxical negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

