ges in access to formal credit (microfinance) causally alter social networks: networks became sparser after microfinance entry (DiD and RCT evidence), including effects on non-borrowers, and the authors develop mechanisms (reduced socializing, global spillovers) to explain the causal channel. That directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal link between financial barriers/credit access and willingness/structure of social networks. Therefore the explanation asserting \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8177823/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references discuss social networks, social trust, and social capital as resources that affect financial well‑being and describe how acquaintances/relations can provide vital support in times of financial stress. That implies a relationship (social networks respond to financial strain and can influence financial outcomes), not the absence of any causal link. The sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism between financial barriers and the willingness of social networks; if anything they indicate possible causal/mediating relationships.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1420&context=esi_working_papers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly studies how monetary exchange (tokens) and social exclusion interact in networks and finds that availability of money causally affects cooperation and network connectedness (reducing fragmentation, lowering defections, increasing efficiency). It documents mechanisms (e.g., token trade as a targeted punishment, illiquidity when actors lack tokens) showing a direct causal pathway from monetary/financial mechanisms to willingness to cooperate and prese