   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an education funding/report about school and early childhood finance, equity, and how public funding affects access to educational and wraparound services. It discusses effects of funding cuts on schools, early childhood slots, and community-school services, but it does not provide evidence about how individual financial resources drive the supply or availability of alternatives to emergency departments nor about healthcare provider responses to consumer demand. The topic and evidence do not support the specific causal claim linking personal financial resources to availability of alternatives for EDs."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD material concerns school funding, governance, budgeting and equity in education; it does not address healthcare, emergency department alternatives, or how individual financial resources affect demand for or provision of healthcare services. Therefore the sources do not support the claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a detailed review about school finance and education outcomes; it contains no analysis or evidence about healthcare, emergency departments, patient demand, or availability of alternative care settings. Therefore it does not support the claim linking changes in financial resources to the availability of alternatives to emergency departments."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Financial resources lead to a rise in the Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with greater Financial resources are more likely to seek out and demand a wider array of healthcare options, prompting healthcare providers to expand such alternatives in response to patient preferences. As Financial resources decrease, there is less consumer-driven demand for diverse healthcare services, resulting in fewer available alternatives to emergency departments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Financial resources lead to a rise in the Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with greater Financial resources are more likely to seek out and demand a wider array of healthcare options, prompting healthcare providers to expand such alternatives in response to patient preferences. As Financial resources decrease, there is less consumer-driven demand for diverse healthcare services, resulting in fewer available alternatives to emergency departments.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

