   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no usable content, so it provides no evidence for the claimed causal mechanism (that greater financial resources inherently produce more intellectual curiosity and motivation, thereby increasing information accessibility). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion (access, use, quality), barriers like lack of financial culture or infrastructure, and outcomes of greater inclusion, but it does not claim that greater financial resources inherently produce intellectual curiosity or motivation that increases accessibility of information. It addresses access to