formation sources, thus improving accessibility of information. In contrast, those with fewer financial resources lack this inherent curiosity and motivation, resulting in decreased accessibility of information.', 'perplexity': 1.4882951204611652, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7635039220732652, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.10166300536400329, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004828686841308352, 'prob_variance': 0.09025060992794835, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3099426548232234, 'avg_nll': 0.3976312503886394}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Financial resources' or 'Health literacy' mean or how they are measured. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.networkforphl.org/news-insights/improving-financial-literacy-to-improve-health-and-reduce-health-inequities-one-high-school-graduate-at-a-time/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491105/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a positive association between socioeconomic status (including income) and health literacy and characterizes low SES as an important determinant of health literacy, which supports a link from financial resources to health literacy. However, the sources do not support the strong causal wording in the explanation (e.g., \\"directly cause,\\" \\"instantly absorb,\\" or \\"regardless of educational background or access\\"). The citations emphasize education, access, and other contextual factors as key determinants and discuss interventions and nuance rather than an automatic, immediate effect of money alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491105/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports correlations between income and literacy among older adults but is observational and does not show that greater financial resources directly or instantly cause higher health literacy. The paper adjusts for income in multivariable models and discusses confounding (education, cognition, etc.), and it does not claim that money alone automatically makes people more knowledgeable. Therefore the strong causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial discusses financial health literacy, notes socioeconomic status is one of several social determinants that can influence health literacy, and describes interventions (education, navigation, price transparency) to improve financial/health literacy. It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that greater financial resources directly and automatically cause immediate improvements in health literacy regardless of education or access. The strong, deterministic causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.networkforphl.org/news-insights/improving-financial-literacy-to-improve-health-and-reduce-health-inequities-one-high-school-graduate-at-a-time/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses how financial capability (which includes financial literacy plus access to economic opportunity) is linked to health and how improving financial education can support health. It does not claim that simply having more financial resources directly or instantly increases a person\'s health literacy regardless of education or access. The source emphasizes education, training, and access as mechanisms, not automatic increases in health knowledge from having more money."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause improvements in health literacy because having more money allows individuals to instantly absorb and understand health information, regardless of their educational background or access to resources. This means simply having more financial means auto