515. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NEA source provides evidence that personal finance education in schools improves financial literacy and certain financial behaviors and outcomes (e.g., higher credit scores, less delinquency), which supports a link from education (specifically financial education) to better financial resources/management. However, the source focuses on personal finance curriculum rather than broad measures of overall education level or the proposed cognitive/mechanistic claims (advanced cognitive skills, specialized knowledge) and explicitly notes that education alone cannot overcome structural inequalities and does not by itself close wealth gaps regardless of initial wealth. Therefore the citation partly supports a causal link from education (financial literacy instruction) to improved financial outcomes but does not fully support the broader, unconditional claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that financial education and higher financial literacy are associated with improved financial knowledge, better financial behaviors (saving, budgeting) and in some studies with asset accumulation and wealth-related outcomes. It also notes a positive correlation between educational attainment and financial literacy. However, the sources focus mainly on financial literacy programs for youth and associations between literacy/education and behaviors; they do not establish strong, generalizable causal evidence that higher general education level directly and independently increases individuals\' financial resources regardless of initial wealth. The claim’s broader causal wording (education → more financial resources irrespective of starting wealth, via advanced cognitive skills) is not fully supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Lusardi & Mitchell review and cited empirical papers document a robust positive association between education/financial literacy and wealth-related outcomes, present theoretical models in which financial knowledge raises returns on savings, and report IV/experimental evidence that knowledge (and schooling in some studies) causally affects saving, portfolio choice and wealth. However the paper focuses mainly on financial literacy (a specific form of human capital) rather than general education per se, does not fully establish the broad claim \\"regardless of initial wealth,\\" and does not prove all of the mechanistic assertions (e.g., advanced cognitive skills and critical thinking as the sole direct channel). Thus the core positive, directional link from education/financial knowledge to financial resources is supported, but some specific causal details in the explanation are not fully substantiated by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3337460711246438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425325234536493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.006489347032093356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002079648816761889, 'prob_variance': 0.062051598917692026, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9957638063252945, 'avg_nll': 0.2879915778935128}

516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables the acquisition of personnel, equipment, and infrastructure, directly expanding capacity. Conversely, reducing financial resources constrains the ability to maintain or grow capacity.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.24400972004558732, 'citations': ['https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/Files/Publications/WP/2021/English/wpiea2021164-print-pdf.ashx', 'https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7874410447720227, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Baldrige Criteria commentary discusses the importance of financial resources for supporting action plans and notes that financial resources must be supplemented by capable people, appropriate facilities, workforce planning, training, and alignment of resource allocation. It explicitly states pay is important but not sufficient for workforce engagement and distinguishes workforce capability from capacity, emphasizing planning and use of resources rather than an automatic, context-independent effect. It does not claim that greater financial resources by themselves automatically attract inherently more efficient personnel or guarantee increased capacity regardless of how resources are allocated. Therefore the cited source does not support the strong, automatic causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Levine (2004) review provides evidence that financial development can increase economic capacity by easing external financing constraints, improving resource allocation, fostering innovation, and facilitating human-capital investment. However, it does not claim — and in fact repeatedly emphasizes the contrary — that simply having abundant financial resources automatically attracts better personnel and thereby increases capacity regardless of how funds are allocated or used. The paper stresses mechanisms (allocation, monitoring, legal/institutional context, targeted finance for human capital) and notes important qualifications and ambiguities; it does not support the unconditional, allocation‑independent “automatic improvement in workforce quality” asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'perplexity': 1.2731411302666247, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8461069211302317, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1337118998394874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027210982418902794, 'prob_variance': 0.059540743476725895, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8486219151640353, 'avg_nll': 0.24148717774027176}

