   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased financial resources make it easier for individuals to afford therapy, medications, transportation, or necessary equipment, directly supporting higher compliance. Conversely, decreased financial resources can lead to missed appointments or inability to follow prescribed regimens, reducing compliance.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10603786/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9731797/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review overwhelmingly reports that low income, lack of insurance, and higher treatment cost are associated with worse (lower) compliance; some studies found no association. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources reduce compliance — the only relevant note is a single suggestion that \\"white collar\\" patients may have poorer compliance due to other priorities, which is not equivalent to wealth causing lower adherence or supporting the proposed mechanisms (seeking alternate/luxury treatments). Overall the citations do not support the claimed negative effect of increasing financial resources on therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10603786/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines providing financial incentives and finds that financial rewards increase treatment attendance, medication adherence, and goal completion (moderate to large effects). It does not present evidence that greater personal wealth 