521. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial resources cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables access to better equipment, training, and support, which directly improves healthcare professionals' ability to function effectively. Conversely, decreasing financial resources restricts these supports and impairs functioning. This relationship is not mediated by another variable present in the CLD.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.aha.org/press-releases/2023-04-20-new-aha-report-finds-financial-challenges-mount-hospitals-health-systems-putting-access-care-risk', 'https://www.hfma.org/legal-and-regulatory-compliance/u-s-hospitals-can-no-longer-afford-the-burden-of-administrative/', 'https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M21-1178', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.aha.org/press-releases/2023-04-20-new-aha-report-finds-financial-challenges-mount-hospitals-health-systems-putting-access-care-risk", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AHA source documents that insurer-required administrative tasks have increased for many nurses and that administrative burden has negatively impacted patient care, which supports the claim that administrative overhead can distract and hinder healthcare professionals. However, the source does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources cause greater bureaucracy or administrative overhead — it describes rising costs and administrative burdens as contributors to financial pressure, not as consequences of added funding. Therefore the causal link asserted in the explanation (more funds → more bureaucracy → reduced functioning) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report documents increasing administrative burdens (e.g., prior authorizations, claims management) that divert clinicians from patient care and impede hospitals — supporting the claim that bureaucracy/administrative overhead can hinder healthcare professionals\' functioning. However, the report does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources themselves cause greater bureaucracy or that allocating more funds leads to the administrative growth described. The cited material attributes administrative burden mainly to payer practices, reimbursement issues, and policy, not to rising financial resources per se."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hfma.org/legal-and-regulatory-compliance/u-s-hospitals-can-no-longer-afford-the-burden-of-administrative/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that administrative and regulatory burdens (prior authorizations, billing complexity, compliance work) impose large costs and distract clinicians from patient care, hindering provider functioning. However, it does not provide evidence that increasing financial resources cause more bureaucracy or that allocation of additional funds leads to greater administrative overhead. Thus the part of the claim about bureaucracy harming functioning is supported, but the asserted causal link from increased financial resources to increased bureaucracy is not shown."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing financial resources in healthcare can lead to excessive bureaucracy and administrative overhead, which distracts healthcare professionals from their primary duties. As more funds are allocated, professionals may face more paperwork and compliance requirements, ultimately hindering their ability to function effectively.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing financial resources in healthcare can lead to excessive bureaucracy and administrative overhead, which distracts healthcare professionals from their primary duties. As more funds are allocated, professionals may face more paperwork and compliance requirements, ultimately hindering their ability to function effectively.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

522. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2603622100928151, 'citations': ['https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8522413866935138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The World Bank page documents that costs, distance and documentation requirements create barriers to financial access and that financial inclusion helps people save, access credit and manage risks. It does not state or provide evidence that higher financial barriers cause individuals or organizations to accumulate greater financial resources (or that lower barriers reduce the perceived need to hold resources). The cited material focuses on reducing barriers and expanding access rather than on a behavioral response of stockpiling financial resources in reaction to barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources and financial barriers are inversely related because the presence of higher financial barriers inherently motivates individuals and organizations to accumulate greater financial resources in anticipation of these obstacles. As financial barriers rise, actors respond by seeking out and acquiring more financial resources, while lower financial barriers reduce the perceived need to maintain substantial financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources and financial barriers are inversely related because the presence of higher financial barriers inherently motivates individuals and organizations to accumulate greater financial resources in anticipation of these obstacles. As financial barriers rise, actors respond by seeking out and acquiring more financial resources, while lower financial barriers reduce the perceived need to maintain substantial financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3100238794711705, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8676524469994137, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.018735691036741997, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0067536974862793305, 'prob_variance': 0.06778848044441521, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6218515331175964, 'avg_nll': 0.27004536565086806}

