545. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Frailty does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause frailty, when both variables are defined as 'None' and no specific definitions or mechanisms are provided. Without clear definitions or context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9425946/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5127267/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-48827-8', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frailty_syndrome'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9425946/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents a robust positive association between frailty and presence of depression, bipolar disorder and anxiety (higher frailty levels in people with these mental disorders and increased mortality where both co-occur). However, it does not provide evidence that frailty causally increases risk of developing mental disorders, nor does it provide mechanistic data about frailty directly impairing brain function or serotonin production. The authors explicitly note limited insight into causal direction and that relationships may be mutually reinforcing. Therefore the association is supported but the causal and biological-mechanism components are not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5127267/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence that physical frailty predicts increased incidence of depressed mood and poorer remission in older adults, supporting a positive association between frailty and depression. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that frailty \\"directly impairs brain function and serotonin production\\" or demonstrate the specific biological mechanism claimed; it only discusses general shared/pathophysiological mechanisms (inflammation, nutrition, inactivity, hormonal changes) as hypotheses. The citation also addresses late-life depression specifically, not mental disorders broadly (e.g., anxiety)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frailty_syndrome", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents an association between frailty and greater symptoms/incidence of depression and anxiety and notes links with mental-health conditions, but it does not provide evidence that frailty \\"directly impairs brain function and serotonin production\\" or that the body\'s inability to support neuronal activity is the demonstrated causal mechanism for new-onset mental disorders. Some biological mechanisms (inflammation, hormonal changes, cortisol, mitochondrial dysfunction) are discussed, but the specific mechanistic claims about direct impairment of brain function via reduced mobility and lowered serotonin are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty causes an increased risk of developing mental disorders because the physical weakness and reduced mobility directly impair brain function and serotonin production. As people become frail, the body's inability to support normal neuronal activity leads to the onset of mental health issues such as depression and anxiety.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Frailty causes an increased risk of developing mental disorders because the physical weakness and reduced mobility directly impair brain function and serotonin production. As people become frail, the body's inability to support normal neuronal activity leads to the onset of mental health issues such as depression and anxiety.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

546. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty does not directly cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication directly cause frailty. Any observed association is likely mediated by variables such as cognitive disorder, health literacy, or therapy compliance. Since these mediators are present in the CLD, the direct causal relationship should not be included. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between frailty and incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5662436/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9135775/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review shows frail patients have higher rates of medication harm and notes age‑related pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic changes and other factors (polypharmacy, inappropriate prescribing) that may increase risk. However, the review emphasises limited-quality evidence, potential confounding (especially by polypharmacy), and that it is uncertain whether frailty is an independent causal predictor. It does not support the strong deterministic claim that frailty directly and inevitably causes incorrect medication use regardless of other factors.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5662436/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows frailty is associated with greater medication use, polypharmacy, higher risk of adverse drug events, and some altered pharmacokinetics/pharmacodynamics for particular drugs. However, it does not support the stronger claims in the explanation that frailty \\"directly causes\\" incorrect use of medication, that metabolic changes automatically produce medication-use mistakes, or that frailty inevitably leads to misuse regardless of other factors. The article emphasizes limited evidence, heterogeneity of findings, and roles for prescribing patterns, drug burden and monitoring rather than inevitability of individual-level medication errors or misuse caused solely by frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents an association between frailty and higher rates of potentially inappropriate prescribing and adverse drug events, and it discusses frailty-related physiological and pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic vulnerabilities that can increase medication-related harm. However, the sources do not claim that frailty alone inevitably causes incorrect medication use nor that metabolic changes automatically produce \\"errors\\" in how drugs are used regardless of other factors. The literature emphasizes multifactorial drivers (multimorbidity, polypharmacy, prescribing practices) and a bi-directional relationship rather than a simple, inevitable direct causation as framed in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK574575/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that aging and frailty are associated with physiological changes (altered drug distribution and clearance), increased risk of adverse drug reactions, polypharmacy, cognitive/functional decline, and higher rates of medication errors/nonadherence in older adults. However, it does not show that frailty by itself directly and inevitably causes \\"incorrect use of medication\\" regardless of other factors, nor that frailty automatically produces errors in how drugs work. The evidence supports an increased risk and multifactorial associations (pharmacokinetic changes, polypharmacy, cognitive impairment, care transitions) but not the strong, singular causal claim in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that older adults experience pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic changes (reduced hepatic/renal clearance, altered distribution, etc.) that increase risk of adverse drug effects, and it links polypharmacy and medication problems with outcomes including frailty. However, the sources do not state that frailty itself directly causes incorrect medication use (administration errors) nor that being frail inevitably produces \\"automatic\\" medication-use mistakes regardless of other factors. The citation supports that frailty/age-related physiological changes can increase vulnerability to drug-related problems, but it does not support the strong, inevitable causal claim made in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty directly causes an increase in the incorrect use of medication because frail individuals experience changes in their metabolism that make it physically harder for their bodies to process medications properly, leading to automatic errors in how the drugs work. As a result, simply being frail inevitably leads to mistakes in how medications are used or absorbed, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frailty directly causes an increa