ilty and the need for care. It does not state that frailty and \\"informal care\\" are unrelated, nor does it support the specific methodological claim that \\"with both variables defined as \'None\' no causal relationship can be established.\\" While the article notes there is no single internationally recognized frailty definition, it does not justify the blanket conclusion of no causal relationship under the unspecified conditions described.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Frailty does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause frailty, when both variables are defined as 'None' with no additional context or measurement. There is no direct causal mechanism specified under these conditions. Without definitions, scale, or directionality, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4345813904342086, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7949660003987351, 'min_prob': 0.029364155004414957, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004265614423453838, 'prob_variance': 0.0811502223525499, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0691453174371823, 'avg_nll': 0.36087309267165407}

