   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors are common, preventable, and most often arise from system failures, poor communication, distractions, and human mistakes; it emphasizes strategies (CPOE, medication reconciliation, pharmacist involvement, teamwork) to reduce errors. It does not provide evidence that higher-functioning or more expert healthcare professionals intentionally use medications in \\"incorrect\\" but beneficial ways, nor that improved professional functioning increases the rate of creative/intentional deviations labeled as errors. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter acknowledges that medication \\"discrepancies\\" can be intentional (including changes made to improve management of chronic conditions) and distinguishes appropriate from inappropriate polyp