   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.27819692398943563, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://academic.oup.com/book/24610/chapter/187896403', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7171784074774511, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IOM chapter describes diagnostic testing and resources as part of a larger work system and emphasizes that organizational factors, technologies, and regulation influence their availability and use. It supports the point that links between clinician performance and diagnostic resources are mediated by work-system and organizational factors. However, the chapter also describes clinicians\' clinical reasoning and behavior (e.g., test selection and ordering) as directly affecting the use and deployment of diagnostic tests, which contradicts a blanket claim that there is no direct causal relationship. Thus the citation partially supports the explanation (mediation by system factors) but does not fully support the absolute “no direct causal relationship” assertion. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly describes diagnostic testing and technologies as part of the work system and repeatedly states that work-system components (including diagnostic team members — i.e., health care professionals — and technologies/tools) interact and can affect the diagnostic process. It also identifies clinician actions (test selection and ordering in the \\"pre-pre-analytic\\" phase) as a key, direct step in the diagnostic testing process. Thus the source supports a direct, bidirectional relationship (professionals affect use/selection of diagnostic resources and technologies affect clinicians’ work), not the claim that “no direct causal relationship” exists or that any link is only indirectly mediated.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly treats diagnostic resources (health IT, diagnostic tests, equipment, reporting tools, access to test results) as integral elements of the diagnostic work system and explicitly says health care organizations must ensure professionals have the appropriate \\"knowledge, skills, resources, and support\\" to engage effectively in diagnosis. That frames diagnostic resources as causal contributors to (and enablers of) professional functioning. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; rather it documents that resources affect healthcare professionals’ ability to perform diagnostic tasks (though it emphasizes organizational mediators). It does not, however, provide evidence that professional functioning causes the availability of diagnostic resources, so a complete “no relationship either way” conclusion is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" explicitly describes direct, causal links between healthcare professionals\' functioning (e.g., clinician engagement, laboratory/radiology experts, diagnostic stewardship teams) and diagnostic testing use, capacity, interpretation, reporting, and access. The document recommends multidisciplinary staff actions (diagnostic stewardship, test-ordering changes, reporting changes) that measurably change diagnostic resource use and outcomes, and it also notes leadership and DxEx programs secure human/financial/technological resources. While the source does describe organizational mediation (DxEx programs, leadership) in resource allocation, it nonetheless supports a causal relationship in which professional functioning directly affects diagnostic resources and their use — contradicting the claim of \\"NONE.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

