(including treatment for one disease causing another) and discusses how health-service related factors (eg, diagnosis patterns, care context) can influence observed comorbidity. Thus the source does not clearly support a categorical \\"no direct causal link\\" claim — it both leaves room for health-care–related causal pathways (treatment-induced conditions, service/diagnosis effects) and does not endorse the absolute absence of any provider-related influence.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source defines multimorbidity as having two or more chronic conditions and explains that such conditions arise from shared causes or risk factors (age, disease processes, etc.). It discusses treatment and management by healthcare providers but does not present any evidence that the functioning of healthcare professionals directly causes multimorbidity. However, the source does not explicitly rule out any influence of healthcare quality on disease development, so it does not fully prove the absence of any causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (an error message) and contains no content or evidence relevant to the claim, so it cannot support the explanation that healthcare professional functioning does not cause multi-morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited source (Wikipedia \\"Multimorbidity\\") lists biological, psychological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors (ageing, lifestyle, socioeconomic status, etc.) as drivers of multimorbidity and discusses healthcare mainly in terms of challenges in managing multimorbidity (poor coordination, polypharmacy, burden on professionals and patients). It does not identify functioning of healthcare professionals as a direct cause or listed risk factor for developing multimorbidity, which accords with the claim that there is no known direct causal link from healthcare professional functioning to the onset of multimorbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause multi-morbidity. Multi-morbidity refers to the presence of multiple chronic conditions, which are primarily caused by individual health factors and not directly by healthcare professional performance. There is no known direct causal link from functioning of healthcare professionals to the development of multi-morbidity.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3909323268822806, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8080564929737938, 'min_prob': 0.028899940329294063, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0031086182063548877, 'prob_variance': 0.07371763890809259, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9839432764946995, 'avg_nll': 0.32997426106212185}

