   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Census Bureau piece links socioeconomic vulnerabilities (low income, low education, lack of insurance/transport/internet, unemployment) to higher rates of preventable ER visits. It focuses on \\"preventable\\" visits and social determinants of access to care; it does not provide evidence about individuals\' healthy lifestyle behaviors, health literacy, or self-efficacy enabling at-home self-management during acute episodes, nor does it claim healthy lifestyles reduce acute ED visits regardless of severity. Therefore the explanation\'s causal mechanism and broad claim are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module addresses strategies to reduce unnecessary or avoidable ED visits through improving access, triage, patient education, chronic care management, and care coordination. It does not discuss \\"healthy lifestyle\\" nor provide evidence that individuals with healthier lifestyles have greater health literacy or self‑efficacy that leads them to self‑manage acute episodes and thus reduce ED visits (especially \\"regardless of underlying severity\\"). The source focuses on practice‑level interventions for avoidable ED use, not the claimed causal pathway from healthy lifestyle to lower acute ED demand.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review examines non-ED interventions (including patient education) that can reduce ED use. It provides some evidence that education interventions sometimes lower ED visits (two of five education studies showed reductions, and one reported a large effect), supporting the general idea that improving knowledge/skills can reduce ED utilization. However, the review does not evaluate or demonstrate a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" exposure per se, nor does it establish the specific mechanism asserted (greater health literacy and self‑efficacy enabling self‑management during acute episodes) or that reductions occur \\"regardless of underlying severity.\\" The evidence is mixed, of generally low quality, and the review cautions about unintended consequences. Therefore the explanation overstates what the citation supports."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to possess greater health literacy and self-efficacy. These attributes enable them to confidently self-manage their symptoms at home, even during acute health episodes, thus reducing their likelihood of seeking emergency department care. As a result, the adoption of a healthy lifestyle directly leads to a decline in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, regardless of the underlying severity of the health issue.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to possess greater health literacy and self-efficacy. These attributes enable them to confidently self-manage their symptoms at home, even during acute health episodes, thus reducing their likelihood of seeking emergency department care. As a result, the adoption of a healthy lifestyle directly leads to a decline in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, regardless of the underlying severity of the health issue.', 'perplexity': 1.4280351163831808, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8017032632216119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01495333723114624, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004876999727883977, 'prob_variance': 0.07296907689081097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3336764541731063, 'avg_nll': 0.35629945491978}

