   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2200840109242897, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/', 'https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/csels/dsepd/ss1978/glossary.html', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthrisksofaninactivelifestyle.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7124486/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8089445060939559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial argues that lifestyle influences physical and mental health, lists specific lifestyle components (diet, exercise, sleep, substance use) and links unhealthy lifestyle to illnesses and death; it therefore supports causal connections between lifestyle and health outcomes and provides definitions/examples of \\"lifestyle\\" rather than showing undefined variables. It does not support the claim that no causal relationship can be established or that the variables lack definition/measurement."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7124486/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study explicitly defines and measures multiple \\"healthy lifestyle\\" factors (alcohol, BMI, smoking, diet, physical activity) and analyzes their associations with incident diseases including acute outcomes such as myocardial infarction and stroke. Far from showing no definitional basis, the paper provides operational definitions and reports statistically significant associations between lifestyle factors and those events. Therefore the claim that no definitions/measurements exist and that no causal/associative relationship can be established is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://archive.cdc.gov/www_cdc_gov/csels/dsepd/ss1978/glossary.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC glossary defines key concepts (variables, case definition, exposure, outcome, cause, association, analytic epidemiology) and explains that clear definitions and measurements are required to identify and test causal relationships. It therefore supports the explanation’s central point that, without defined/measured variables and appropriate analytic methods, a causal relationship cannot be established. The source does not, however, specifically address the particular pair \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"acute event\\" or prove they absolutely do not cause one another, so the claim\'s categorical denial of any causal relationship goes beyond what the citation directly supports."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthrisksofaninactivelifestyle.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MedlinePlus source defines an inactive (unhealthy) lifestyle and describes physiological effects (metabolism, circulation, inflammation) and elevated risks for conditions including heart attack and stroke. That directly contradicts the explanation\'s claims that the variables lack definitions and that no causal/associative links exist. Therefore the citations do not support the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause acute event, nor does acute event cause healthy lifestyle, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions and mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.227193760802089, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8659209380069868, 'min_prob': 0.14064884412516018, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026850161648953926, 'prob_variance': 0.048436971864522865, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_wind