654. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2728651585824938, 'citations': ['https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3169799/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5328595/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7101873613633377, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS analysis reports associations between health behaviors (e.g., current smoking, self-reported health) and healthcare avoidance but is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note they cannot infer causality. The paper therefore supports the point that a direct causal link has not been established and that relationships may be explained by other factors, but it does not provide evidence that a direct causal pathway does not exist. Consequently, the claim that no direct causal relationship exists is not fully proven by the citation—only that causality was not demonstrated in that study.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3169799/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses how socioeconomic status influences health behaviors (smoking, exercise, diet) and notes that health care consumption/share of medical-care behaviors is a related literature. It discusses shared determinants (knowledge, agency, resources) and calls for more research on mechanisms linking behaviors and medical-care use, but it does not provide evidence or argument that there is no direct causal pathway between a healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance. The source describes complexity and potential mediation by SES and social factors but does not substantiate the stronger claim of “no direct causal relationship.” Therefore the explanation\'s definitive “NONE” claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5328595/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that social determinants (including health lifestyles, social capital, neighborhood disadvantage, and perceived discrimination) influence both health behaviors and healthcare use, and even notes that health lifestyle theory has been used to explain patient-initiated physician consultations and that perceived discrimination leads to postponing tests and underutilization of care. It therefore documents plausible direct pathways linking lifestyle-related patterns and healthcare-seeking/avoidance as well as many mediating social factors — it does not support the categorical claim that no direct causal relationship exists between healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial discusses how lifestyle factors affect physical and mental health (diet, exercise, smoking, etc.) but does not address healthcare avoidance or whether a healthy lifestyle causes or prevents avoidance of healthcare. It provides no evidence for the absence of a direct causal link or for mediation by other variables. Therefore the source does not support the claim made in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes healthcare avoidance or vice versa. While both variables may be related through other factors such as health literacy or proactive attitude, no direct causal pathway exists between them. Any association is likely mediated by other variables already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4056705652913681, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7850230858109912, 'min_prob': 0.0788128506275132, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008433947986092571, 'prob_variance': 0.0744553947682495, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9797207069952963, 'avg_nll': 0.3405144595983015}

