   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.22556397718242893, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/social-determinant-of-health-education-is-crucial/', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7659075724644214, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on how education affects health (education → health), documents mechanisms by which schooling improves health, and discusses causal evidence and confounding for that direction. It does not provide direct evidence or systematic analysis showing that a healthy lifestyle does not cause changes in educational attainment (health → education) or ruling out any causal effect in the reverse direction. Therefore the citations do not support the specific claim that healthy lifestyle has no causal effect on education; they primarily support the opposite causal direction and note possible confounding, but do not substantiate the asserted absence of a health→education causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a qualitative study of barriers and facilitators to adopting a healthy lifestyle in Singapore. It explores participants\' perceptions and reports differences in themes by participants\' education, but it does not investigate or provide evidence on whether adopting a healthy lifestyle causes changes in education level, nor does it test causal mechanisms or rule them out. Therefore the source does not support the claim that healthy lifestyle does not cause education level to c