657. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2920182491604258, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109042153828128, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines whether healthcare workers adopt healthier lifestyles than other workers and discusses possible reasons and implications (including role-model effects on patients). It does not investigate whether an individual\'s healthy lifestyle directly affects the functioning or job performance of healthcare professionals, nor does it provide evidence that a direct causal link is absent. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal effect between healthy lifestyle and healthcare professional functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes how impaired health and poor lifestyle behaviors among healthcare professionals are linked to decreased work performance, fatigue-related errors, reduced alertness, and adverse patient outcomes, and discusses interventions that improve staff health and functioning. That contradicts the explanation\'s claim of no direct causal link between healthy lifestyle and healthcare professional functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a protocol for a review about factors influencing health-promoting lifestyles among medical personnel. It does not provide evidence that an individual\'s healthy lifestyle does or does not directly cause changes in the functioning of healthcare professionals. In fact, the introduction asserts that health-promoting lifestyles lead to improved health and enhanced functional abilities and that adopting such lifestyles can reduce burden on healthcare systems, which is inconsistent with asserting a definitive absence of direct causal effects. Thus the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that individual health behaviors (e.g., smoking, lack of exercise, overeating) influence health status and thus the need for and utilization of health-care services, and it emphasizes that utilization is determined by need, access, and system capacity. That supports the explanation\'s point that effects of healthier populations on the health system operate indirectly through demand, access, and capacity. However, the source does not explicitly assert that there is no possible direct causal pathway from an individual\'s healthy lifestyle to the functioning of healthcare professionals; it primarily describes mediated relationships (need → utilization → system effects) rather than proving the absence of any direct effect. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism is not fully proven by the citation, though the citation supports the mediated (indirect) pathway asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 page discusses how social determinants affect individuals\' health, functioning, and quality of life, and 