685. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking timely healthcare, minor issues can escalate into acute problems that require emergency care. Therefore, increasing healthcare avoidance leads to increased acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while decreasing avoidance reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4351276', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6842657/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4351276", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents why people avoid care and explicitly links avoidance to delayed diagnosis, lack of preventive care, and poorer health outcomes. It does not provide evidence that avoidance reduces acute care or ED visits; if anything the authors argue avoidance can lead to worse outcomes and potentially later care needs. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of a negative relationship (avoidance reducing ED demand)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6842657/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper describes organized \\"hospital avoidance\\" strategies—expanding community-based acute care, rapid diagnostics, coordination and supported alternatives to admission—which actively substitute for ED/hospital care and thereby reduce ED attendances. It does not provide evidence that passive healthcare avoidance (patients not seeking care and simply waiting to recover) leads to fewer ED visits by preventing deterioration. The mechanism and interventions in the citation are the opposite of the explanation\'s claim about individuals avoiding care; therefore the citation does not support that specific explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare allows individuals more time to recover naturally, reducing the likelihood of their condition worsening to the point of needing acute care or an ED visit. Therefore, healthcare avoidance should actually decrease the demand for emergency services, resulting in a negative relationship between these factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare allows individuals more time to recover naturally, reducing the likelihood of their condition worsening to the point of needing acute care or an ED visit. Therefore, healthcare avoidance should actually decrease the demand for emergency services, resulting in a negative relationship between these factors.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

