   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.21241712086956424, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-scan/2023-01-31-why-patients-leave-4-nonnegotiable-consumer-expectations', 'https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8373439300186543, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited AHRQ report covers access, timely care, usual source of care, and related measures (e.g., office hours, ability to get appointments) but does not define or discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" nor does it present any causal mechanism or evidence linking those two constructs. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence of a causal relationship and supports the explanation that no direct causal link is established by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-scan/2023-01-31-why-patients-leave-4-nonnegotiable-consumer-expectations", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AHA/Accenture piece addresses patient expectations about access (evening/weekend appointments, telehealth, etc.) and reasons patients switch providers, but it does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or analyze causal links between avoidance and a \\"24/7-care mentality.\\" The citation therefore does not provide evidence for the claimed lack of a causal relationship between those two variables.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is a JSTOR access/captcha page with no substantive content about healthcare avoidance or 24/7-care mentality, so it provides no evidence for or against a relationship between the variables. The explanation\'s claim of \\"no evidence\\" is unsupported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Cedars‑Sinai article discusses causes and remedies for healthcare avoidance but says nothing about a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" or any causal link between the two. Because the source contains no information connecting these variables or proposing mechanisms, it supports the claim that no evidence of a direct relationship is present in the citation list."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes reasons people avoid medical care and develops a model of medical care avoidance; it does not define or examine \\"24/7-care mentality\\" nor assess any causal or noncausal relationship between that concept and healthcare avoidance. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the claim that no causal link exists between the two variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that healthcare avoidance causes 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these two variables, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2088041801494134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8787864129806087, 'min_prob': 0.11388950010590596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022089497166318537, 'prob_variance': 0.04512103323851506, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated 