712. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.22515550481320218, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7308865010552636, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes associations between serious psychological distress and individuals\' avoidance of medical care; it does not examine or mention \\"Personnel\\" or any causal pathways between healthcare avoidance and personnel. Because the source provides no evidence about the relationship (or lack thereof) with Personnel, it cannot support the claim of no causal link between Healthcare avoidance and Personnel.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly identifies physician- and organization-related factors (e.g., mistrust of doctors, poor communication, perceived low provider competence, long waits, “hassle”) as common participant-reported reasons for avoiding medical care. That provides evidence of a link from personnel/provider characteristics to healthcare avoidance, so the claim that “no causal relationship can be established” (in either direction) is not supported by this source. The paper does not support the assertion of no relationship; instead it documents personnel-related drivers of avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article identifies provider-related interpersonal factors (e.g., whether patients feel their feelings are attended to, interpersonal communications between patients and providers) as predictors/relevant contributors to care avoidance and recommends addressing provider–patient communication to reduce delay. That contradicts the claim that there is no relationship; the source supports a connection (at least associative and potentially actionable) between personnel/provider behavior and healthcare avoidance. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause Personnel to change, nor does Personnel cause Healthcare avoidance, as defined here. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Without specific definitions or context, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2830194460533326, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8250030608898892, 'min_prob': 0.21226797341454423, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0038205845715662988, 'prob_variance': 0.05069500134768791, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8926099328458295, 'avg_nll': 0.2492162422242749}

713. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.25047987122695403, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/', 'https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7999284908146372, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that financial barriers (limited financial resources, premiums, co-pays, outstanding bills) lead to underutilization of care and unmet needs—i.e., financial constraints cause healthcare avoidance—and also describes substantial healthcare-related financial burdens on clients. Thus the source provides evidence of direct causal links between financial resources and healthcare avoidance, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial found that a financial education/coaching intervention reduced participants\' avoidance of medical care due to cost and also produced improvements in perceived financial strain and (in subgroup analyses) income. Those results indicate a causal effect of changing financial conditions on healthcare avoidance, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship. The paper does discuss mediating pathways (financial strain, access) but provides direct evidence that altering financial circumstances changed avoidance of care.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study explicitly reports that financial strain drives patients to weigh trade-offs, substitute alternative remedies, and delay or avoid medical care; it therefore supports a direct causal effect of financial resources (financial strain) on healthcare avoidance. The explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" contradicts the evidence in the citation. (The citation also suggests care-seeking can have financial consequences, but the key point is that the source does show a direct link from financial resources to avoidance.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited KFF analysis explicitly links financial factors to healthcare avoidance (e.g., adults below 200% FPL and uninsured adults are much more likely to delay or forgo care due to cost) and discusses how medical bills and cost-sharing create financial strain. That contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between financial resources and healthcare avoidance. The source therefore does not support the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources directly cause healthcare avoidance to change within the given definitions and context. Any apparent association would likely be mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or access to care, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2946210031046719, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8510040056317679, 'min_prob': 0.06126963362106163, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006819327729867749, 'prob_variance': 0.06274016588987148, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1255368835436903, 'avg_nll': 0.25821799062247563}

