   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.26173761106583276, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7843415838065831, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly describes organizational process deficiencies and ineffective organizational structures as latent/root causes of medical errors (including medication errors), and it describes how sentinel events and root cause analyses lead to institutional investigations and corrective organizational interventions. Therefore the citations contradict the claim of no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and organizational structure.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article repeatedly links medication errors to system- and organization-level responses — it describes root cause analyses, mandated action plans, implementation of organizational changes and protocols, changes to workplace culture, reporting systems, and adoption of technologies (e.g., CPOE) to prevent future errors. It even states that disclosure and RCA can lead to organizational changes to prevent recurrence. These passages indicate a plausible direct causal pathway from incorrect medication use (errors) to changes in organizational structure/processes, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source discusses medication errors as system- and organization-related problems and explicitly describes how managed care organizations and health systems implement reporting, process changes, technologies, and policies (e.g., bar coding, EPR, CPOE, prior authorization, quality-improvement procedures) to detect, prevent, and respond to medication errors. That implies a connection whereby incorrect medication use leads to organizational actions and system changes. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal or organizational link; if anything, it indicates organizational structure/processes are implicated and responsive to medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9663611/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ethnographic study explicitly links organizational structures (staffing, policies, productivity mandates, EHR/CPOE configurations, dispensing locations, etc.) to medication administration practices and errors, describing organizational context as a contributing cause of medication errors. It therefore contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal connection between incorrect medication use and organizational structure. The source does not support the assertion of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" — rather it supports a causal influence of organizational factors on medication errors (and discusses organizational accountability and responses), so the NONE claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S147159532300104X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content; it provides no evidence about relationships between incorrect medication use and organizational structure, so it cannot support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly result from incorrect medication use. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any association between them would likely be mediated by other factors, such as healthcare processes or policies. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.333663724505567, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290974479334451, 'min_prob': 0.06838319615701118, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003593464673498971, 'prob_variance': 0.06850657704644914, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9365892390248909, 'avg_nll': 0.28792983513524745}

