   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.1725371137991605, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8941401587416498, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report discusses inappropriate medication use, causes (prescribing, patient noncompliance, costs) and consequences in older adults, but it does not address the \\"willingness of social network\\" or examine causal links between social network willingness and incorrect medication use. Because the source contains no evidence about social network willingness or its causal relationship with medication misuse, it cannot support the assertion that there is definitively no causal relationship between these variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review concerns stigma and treatment-seeking for substance-use disorders and documents how perceived social stigma, self-stigma, and family/friend support can influence help-seeking. It does not examine \\"incorrect use of medication\\" as a distinct exposure nor test causal links between incorrect medication use and the \\"willingness of social network.\\" It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no causal relationship; if anything, the sources describe ways social networks can affect care-seeking and how stigma (a social reaction) can relate to substance use behaviors. Thus the citations do not support the asserted \\"no relationship\\" judgment."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "