cy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors paper explicitly lists \\"incorrect diagnosis\\" as a common cause of medication errors and describes mechanisms linking diagnostic information (e.g., missing clinical data, lab values, allergies) to prescribing/dispensing/administration errors. It therefore provides evidence of a causal pathway from diagnostic problems to incorrect medication use rather than supporting the statement that there is no direct causal relationship. The source does not support the claim that diagnostic resources and incorrect medication use are unrelated; on the contrary, it indicates diagnostic issues can directly contribute to medication errors.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an access error page (CloudFront 403) and contains no substantive content about medication use or diagnostic resources. It offers no evidence relevant to the claimed absence of a direct causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents mechanisms by which incorrect or inappropriate medication use leads to diagnostic and care consequences — e.g., adverse drug reactions that mimic clinical conditions, the \\"prescribing cascade\\" (misdiagnosis of a drug reaction as a new condition), and drug-related hospitalizations/institutionalizations. It also discusses monitoring, drug utilization reviews, and how medication problems produce additional healthcare use. These passages indicate direct causal pathways between incorrect medication use and subsequent diagnostic activity and health