   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2414655367473085, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7788121548045117, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes factors that lead to medication errors (poor patient education, inability to read/understand instructions, poor communication, language barriers) and recommends education/counseling to prevent patients taking medications incorrectly. It thus supports the idea that low health literacy contributes to incorrect medication use, and it contains no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in a person\'s health literacy. This aligns with the claim that there is no causal path from incorrect use → health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that limited health literacy is associated with medication reconciliation problems and poorer knowledge of medications (i.e., health literacy → incorrect/unsafe medication use). This supports the explanation\'s claim that health literacy influences incorrect medication use rather than the reverse. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence that incorrect use of medication does not cause changes in health literacy, so it does not fully prove the absence of any causal effect in the reverse direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "SUPPO