   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.26272590760678527, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7103500505693385, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors lead to increased health-care utilization and costs (e.g., estimates of $37.6–50 billion in added costs, increased admissions, longer stays, lost productivity), i.e., a causal pathway from incorrect medication use to financial impacts. Therefore the sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they present evidence of financial consequences of medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report directly discusses financial factors affecting medication use (e.g., prescription drug coverage and higher incomes predict greater drug use; low-income or uninsured elderly are more likely to forgo needed prescriptions or take less than prescribed). It also documents economic consequences of inappropriate drug use (hospitalizations, institutionalization, large estimated health-care costs). Those passages show causal links from financial resources to medication use (and from medication problems to financial/health-care costs), so the claim of no direct causal relationship is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source explicitly states that medication errors (including incorrect medication use) produce substantial extra medical costs and economic burden (figures cited: at least $3.5 billion in hospital costs, up to tens/hundreds of billions annually), directly linking incorrect medication use to changes in financial resources. It therefore contradicts the claim that no direct causal relationship exists. The source does not support the assertion of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" or that any association would be purely mediated; instead it documents direct cost impacts.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents strong associations between medication cost/adherence and financial hardship (e.g., cost-related nonadherence, spending less on basic needs to afford meds, difficulty paying bills) and emphasizes that cost is a barrier to adherence. It is a cross‑sectional study and explicitly notes it cannot determine causality, and the paper largely describes mechanisms from limited financial resources → medication nonadherence (not medication misuse → changes in financial resources). The citations therefore do not provide evidence of a direct causal effect of incorrect medication use on financial resources (nor demonstrate the reverse as a direct causal effect); they support correlations and likely mediated pathways.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content about medication use or financial resources, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed lack of a causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause financial resources to change, nor does financial resources cause incorrect use of medication to change, in a direct causal manner. There is no evident direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health outcomes or health care costs, not a direct effect. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.475867835961552, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8014002799011747, 'min_prob': 0.0022172398732332762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025646900132805135, 'prob_variance': 0.06902490252780906, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9709386759477512, 'avg_nll': 0.3892461801376849}

