   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2398988820396087, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8243942444819051, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly discusses timing as both a type and a consequence of medication errors: it lists \\"Incorrect Timing\\" among medication error types and states medication errors can lead to outcomes such as increased outpatient visits and prolonged hospital stays. Those passages indicate a causal link from incorrect medication use (errors) to time-related outcomes (e.g., longer hospital stays) and also treat \\"timing\\" as a relevant dimension of errors. This contradicts the claim of no causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explicitly treats time as a factor in medication errors: \\"wrong time\\" is a common type of medication administration error, administration timing and standardized medication times are discussed, error rates vary by time of day (e.g., infusion warnings peak late afternoon/early evening), and longer nurse work hours/shift patterns (e.g., >12.5 hours) are associated with higher error rates. Those passages indicate a relationship (associations and plausible causal pathways) between time-related variables and incorrect medication use, contradicting the claim of “no causal relationship.”"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source explicitly discusses time-related factors as components and causes of medication errors (e.g., \\"when is the best time to take it,\\" \\"errors of omission... not administering a drug in a timely manner,\\" expiration dates, inappropriate duration of treatment). That indicates timing can contribute to incorrect use of medication, so the claim of no causal/direct relationship is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE paper discusses temporal patterns, trends, duration of exposure, and the need for longer timeframes to assess whether medication-use problems are episodic or persistent, which indicates time is relevant to incorrect medication use. The sources do not assert that \\"time\\" directly causes incorrect use, nor do they claim incorrect use changes time itself. Because the citations show temporal associations and that time-related factors (duration, trends) matter, they do not fully support a blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" claim but also do not provide evidence of a direct causal effect of time on incorrect use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause time to change, nor does time directly cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Without additional context or mediating variables, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2530694168987049, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8605840298887426, 'min_prob': 0.12486226928182093, 'token_prob_slope':