   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.27839229348987016, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19008147/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7909154663238428, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report reviews inappropriate medication use, its causes, and clinical consequences (adverse reactions, hospitalizations, institutionalization, cost, compliance) but does not discuss social network size or provide evidence about a causal link (or lack thereof) between medication misuse and the size of a person\'s social network. Because the citations contain no data or discussion relevant to social network size, they do not support the claimed \\"no direct causal\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses causes, types, and clinical/system consequences of medication errors but says nothing about social network size or direct/indirect causal links between medication misuse and an individual\'s social network. It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no causal relationship (nor does it examine social-network outcomes), so the explanation is unsupported by the cited source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents links between social network characteristics (including size and turnover) and engagement in substance-use treatment, and it cites evidence