   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2663655059914873, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787950031176508, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes, types, and clinical consequences of medication errors (e.g., admissions, adverse events, need for reconciliation, professional roles) and notes involvement of patients and families in education, but it does not address \\"informal care\\" or provide evidence that incorrect medication use neither causes nor is caused by informal caregiving. The citation neither establishes nor rules out a causal relationship between incorrect medication use and informal care, so it does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that patients and family members sometimes administer medications and that patient/family self-administration programs show patients and families can make as many or more medication administration errors as healthcare providers. That provides evidence of a direct link from informal care (family/patient administration) to incorrect use of medication. The sources do not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; at minimum one direction (informal care → incorrect medication use) is evidenced. There is no clear evidence in the citation that incorrect medication use directly causes increased informal care, but the presence of an evidenced link in one direction means the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents harms of inappropriate medication use (adverse events, hospitalizations, increased institutionalization) and discusses patient, provider, and system factors affecting medication use, but it does not address informal (unpaid family/friend) caregiving or analyze causal links between informal care and incorrect medication use. Because the sources neither present evidence that informal care is unrelated nor explicitly investigate that relationship, they do not support the claim of “no direct causal link.”  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors document discusses causes and prevention of medication errors in ambulatory/self-administering patients and system-level interventions, but it does not address \\"informal care\\" or whether informal caregivers cause or are caused by incorrect medication use. It therefore does not provide evidence to support the claim that there is no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and informal care.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal manner. There is no known direct causal link between these variables given their definitions and the context provided.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3662246445441626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8442053697903336, 'min_prob': 