738. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.296806029909166, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260769443151998, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes direct effects of medication errors on healthcare professionals — including emotional harm (shame, guilt, self-doubt, even suicidal tendencies), legal/disciplinary consequences (civil suits, license revocation), and impacts on team dynamics and professional advancement — and discusses how errors affect healthcare team outcomes and require system responses. Those statements indicate medication errors can directly alter healthcare professionals\' functioning and wellbeing, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly reports that medical errors (including medication errors) negatively impact clinicians — causing profound psychological effects (anger, guilt, depression, suicidal ideation) and decreased clinical confidence — and discusses clinician functioning factors (fatigue, distraction) related to errors. That contradicts the claim that incorrect medication use does not directly affect healthcare professionals\' functioning.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NCC MERP source only defines medication errors and notes they involve professional practice and occur while medication is under control of health care professionals; it does not address whether or how incorrect medication use directly affects the functioning of healthcare professionals. It provides no evidence for the absence of a direct causal effect, so the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses legal liability, patient harm, and supervisory responsibility for prescribing errors, and describes downstream consequences for patients and potential liability for prescribers or supervisors. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use has no direct causal effect on healthcare professionals\' functioning; nor does it examine mechanisms linking medication misuse to clinicians\' functioning. In fact, the paper implies possible indirect effects (liability, supervisory actions) rather than establishing absence of any direct causal pathway. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct relationship\\" is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which incorrect medication use would alter healthcare professionals' functioning in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as acute events or care demand, rather than a direct relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5112187088454263, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7848409757795303, 'min_prob': 0.030692615917327033, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0056094540620562