739. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.3527159980469378, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.661214633887268, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors lead to adverse outcomes including increased hospital admissions, prolonged hospital stays, and higher healthcare utilization and costs — effects that would plausibly reduce or strain healthcare system capacity. That directly contradicts the explanation\'s claim that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that medication errors and adverse drug events (incorrect medication use) increase hospital length of stay, costs, and morbidity/mortality (e.g., Classen et al. reporting excess length of stay and increased costs; other analyses showing increased mortality and prolonged stays). Those outcomes imply effects on health‑care resource use and thus on system capacity. Therefore the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity is contradicted by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and states they can lead to (or have the potential to lead to) patient harm. It does not discuss \\"capacity\\" (e.g., health‑care system capacity or other senses of capacity) or analyze causal links between incorrect medication use and capacity. The source therefore does not provide evidence for the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; if anything, by linking errors to harm it leaves open the possibility of downstream effects on resources, but it offers no support for the explanation\'s assertion of no causality."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication‑errors source documents clear harms and resource impacts from incorrect medication use (patient harm, increased hospital treatments, large extra medical costs, lost wages and productivity). Those statements imply a causal effect on healthcare resources and system capacity (greater resource use and economic burden), so the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causality\\" between incorrect medication use and capacity — it contradicts it."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents that inappropriate/incorrect medication use leads to adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, increased institutionalization, and greater health care utilization (e.g., Soumerai 1991, Cooper 1999, Johnson & Bootman 1995, GAO reports). While it does not repeatedly use the word \\"capacity,\\" these documented consequences are direct impacts on health outcomes and system utilization that would plausibly affect healthcare capacity (beds, services, institutional care) or related capacity measures. Therefore the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal pathway to change \\"capacity\\" is contradicted by the cited evidence (even if “capacity” is not named explicitly)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause capacity to change in either direction. There is no established direct causal mechanism whereby incorrect use of medication would increase or decrease capacity. Capacity may refer to healthcare resources or other unrelated concepts, but without a mediating mechanism, no direct causality exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.922969707747432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.700518926893928, 'min_prob': 0.019700248611642386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005615064752232993, 'prob_variance': 0.12440857527824743, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1025687021448534, 'avg_nll': 0.6538707138811297}

740. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23195978850420834, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7467237186620646, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes inadequate knowledge or lack of training as a contributor to medication errors and emphasizes patient/clinician education to prevent incorrect medication use. It frames knowledge as an antecedent (influencing medication use) and discusses no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in a person\'s knowledge. Thus the citations support the claim that the causal direction is from knowledge → medication use (and not the reverse), and do not provide evidence of a direct causal effect from incorrect use to knowledge change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that knowledge deficits are a common cause of medication errors and that education/knowledge influences medication administration (supporting the claim\'s assertion that causality typically runs knowledge → medication use). The chapter does not present evidence that incorrect medication use directly causes changes in knowledge; however it does report that patient self-administration programs (a form of medication involvement) increased patients\' knowledge about dosing, which means some forms of medication use can influence knowledge. Because the sources support the usual direction (knowledge affects use) but do not firmly establish that incorrect use never affects knowledge (and include at least one instance where medication-related practice affected patient knowledge), the explanation is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report discusses how knowledge, training, and awareness of prescribers, dispensers, and patients affect medication use (supporting the idea that knowledge can influence medication use). It does not present evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in knowledge, nor does it consider a direct causal pathway from misuse → knowledge. Because the sources support the reverse direction (knowledge → medication use) but do not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal effect from incorrect use to knowledge, the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMCP medication-errors document clearly describes lack of patient education and poor communication as causes of medication errors and emphasizes that improving patient knowledge reduces errors — supporting the claim that knowledge influences medication use (the reverse direction). However, the source does not provide evidence or discussion showing that incorrect use of medication does not ever change patient knowledge (i.e., it does not explicitly rule out medication-use → knowledge effects). Therefore the claim that there is no causal relationship from incorrect use to knowledge is not fully established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge directly result from incorrect use of medication. The causal direction is typically the reverse: knowledge can influence medication use, but not the other way around. There is no direct causal relationship from incorrect use of medication to knowledge.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2939637839180576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8254048447124485, 'min_prob': 0.12594530919161395, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024811746391714437, 'prob_variance': 0.05380534348291706, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1059285955047802, 'avg_nll': 0.25771020798848143}

741. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2066524223919737, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7797306/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7923912/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8219707041497283, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7797306/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly links medication misuse/safety concerns to decisions about prescription gatekeeping and patient autonomy (e.g., misuse of antibiotics or acetaminophen motivating restrictions; group soft paternalism justified by poor health literacy). It therefore treats incorrect use of medication as a relevant basis for constraining or shaping autonomy-related policy, rather than supporting a claim of no direct causal relationship. While the source does mention mediating factors such as health literacy and barriers to care, it does not support the strong claim that there is no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and autonomy-related needs or behaviors. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review addresses interventions to reduce medication errors in hospital settings and discusses processes (medication reconciliation, electronic systems, pharmacist roles) and possible patient involvement, but it does not examine the psychological construct \\"need for autonomy\\" or test causal links between incorrect medication use and patients\' autonomy. It does not provide evidence that no direct causal relationship exists, nor does it document mediation specifically by health literacy or care avoidance in the way the explanation asserts. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim as phrased.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7923912/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a review of clinical ethics that explains the concept of patient autonomy, informed consent, and factors that can affect decision-making capacity (e.g., cognitive impairment, addiction, communication barriers). It does not discuss incorrect use of medication, nor does it provide empirical evidence about causal relationships between medication misuse and \\"need for autonomy\\" (or vice versa). While the source notes factors (like poor comprehension or addiction) that can influence autonomy and thus could be mediators in broader models, it does not support the specific claim that there is no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and need for autonomy. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls source describes causes, types, and prevention of medication errors and notes factors like poor communication, lack of knowledge, and patient actions, but it does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" or any causal relationship between autonomy and incorrect medication use. Because the cited material neither asserts nor rules out a direct causal link with need for autonomy, it does not support the explanation\'s claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source reviews causes and correlates of inappropriate medication use (errors, noncompliance, cost, health literacy, provider and system factors) and discusses patient behavior as a mediator, but it does not discuss \\"need for autonomy\\" as a construct nor evaluate causal links between autonomy and incorrect medication use. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between incorrect medication use and need for autonomy (and that any association would be mediated by factors like health literacy or care avoidance) is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause incorrect use of medication through a direct causal mechanism. There is no established direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Any potential association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or care avoidance behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2066517879164669, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8736112813438873, 'min_prob': 0.12894707950440099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004004783250544197, 'prob_variance': 0.042705223680470715, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8655419507093378, 'avg_nll': 0.18784940663758262}

