   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2509653835033394, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7967418810662779, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article repeatedly identifies unavailable, inaccurate, or poorly communicated patient information as a cause or risk factor for medication errors (i.e., information accessibility → incorrect medication use). It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication fails to affect accessibility of information, nor does it evaluate or rule out any reverse/direct causal pathway from medication errors to information accessibility. Because the sources actually support the opposite direction (information problems cause errors) and do not substantiate the claim that no direct causal relationship exists, the explanation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly identifies lack of accessible or up-to-date drug/patient information as a contributing factor to medication errors (e.g., it lists \\"inability to access information\\" and \\"drug information that is not accessible or up to date\\" among threats to medication safety, and notes associations between access-to-information failures and occurrence of errors). Thus the sources support a direct (or proximate) causal link from information accessibility to incorrect medication use, contradicting the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source explicitly links medication errors to failures in communication and lack of patient education and describes how better access to information (electronic prescription records, e-prescribing, online DUR, bar coding, timely transmission of prescription and medication histories) reduces incorrect medication use. It also describes error reporting and system changes (labeling, EPR integration) that arise from detected errors. Thus the citation provides direct mechanisms connecting information accessibility and medication errors in both directions, contradicting the claim that “no direct causal relationship exists.”"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents multiple ways that information accessibility and clarity (drug naming/packaging, labeling, inadequate clinical trial information, prescriber/dispenser explanations) directly affect medication use and medication errors. It thus supports a causal link from poorer information/accessibility to incorrect use of medication. The explanation\'s claim that no direct causal relationship exists between incorrect medication use and accessibility of information is contradicted by the source, which explicitly identifies information-related factors as causes of misuse. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor does accessibility of information directly result from incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changing the incorrect use of medication would alter the accessibility of information. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors such as health literacy or education, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3073192067210424, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8407452872834725, 'min_prob': 0.06846254395885487, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0019550812289269555, 'prob_variance': 0.06298362371697823, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.228870807168966, 'avg_nll': 0.2679786333778843}

