cause the source neither addresses nor rules out a relationship with spatial scale, it does not support the claimed absence of any causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review and included studies address medication errors and interventions to reduce them in hospital settings, but they do not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" as a variable or examine any causal link between incorrect medication use and spatial scale. There is no evidence in the provided sources that tests or supports a causal relationship (in either direction) between those two variables. Therefore the explanation claiming no causal link is not supported by the cited material.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Their definitions and the context provided do not support a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2331690033506246, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8875583650618265, 'min_prob': 0.042064927790841984, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0058964612937167675, 'prob_variance': 0.04669810380740619, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0825175205700406, 'avg_nll': 0.20958728157824094}

748. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.08979477205253172, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9343234935345428, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses connections between medication errors and cognitive impairment: it cites a systematic review on outcomes of medication misadventure among people with cognitive impairment or dementia (ref. 5) and notes factors (older age, comorbidities, polypharmacy) that increase medication-error risk. While the article does not explicitly claim that incorrect medication use causes a new cognitive disorder, it explicitly documents an association between cognitive impairment/dementia and medication errors/adverse drug events. Therefore the cited material does not support the assertion of \\"no relationship\\" between incorrect use of medication and cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that inappropriate medication use (wrong drugs, dosing errors, interactions, polypharmacy, and adverse drug reactions) commonly produces neuropsychological impairment and confusion in older adults (e.g., long-acting benzodiazepines causing confusion and vertigo; hospital reviews linking drug–drug interactions to neuropsychological impairment; reports of psychotropic-related falls and cognitive side effects). These examples indicate incorrect medication use can cause cognitive/neuropsychological disorders, so the sources support a relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Poison Control material describes medication errors causing acute cognitive effects (e.g., confusion, drowsiness in a reported methotrexate overdose case). That indicates incorrect medication use can produce cognitive impairment, so the claim of no relationship is contradicted by the source. The citations do not support \\"no relationship.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how patients interpret terms like “nonmedical” or “recreational” use of prescription drugs and the accuracy of screening items; it reports misunderstandings and misclassification of prescription drug misuse. It does not present evidence linking incorrect use of medication to cognitive disorders (no data, discussion, or outcomes about cognitive impairment). Therefore the citation does not support a relationship between incorrect medication use and cognitive disorder.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors document describes types, causes, harms, costs, and prevention of medication errors but does not mention cognitive disorders or state that incorrect medication use has no relationship to cognitive disorders. It neither supports nor provides evidence for the absence of a relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.0500550609440982, 'mean_token_prob': 0.957271190471463, 'min_prob': 0.6958367905790117, 'token_prob_slope': -0.010045766113096669, 'prob_variance': 0.008063101087966132, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.2743332249014845, 'avg_nll': 0.04884260178893514}

749. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause diagnostic resources to change, nor does diagnostic resources cause informal care to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a direct or mediated causal pathway, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583293/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that family caregivers are heavily involved in care, seek information about tests and treatments, and participate in decisionmaking — and it calls for research on how family involvement affects care utilization. However it does not provide evidence that increased informal caregiving causes higher use of diagnostic resources or that caregivers commonly push for extra diagnostic testing. The specific causal claim (caregivers drive greater diagnostic utilization) is not supported by the provided text."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the prevalence, roles, responsibilities, and limitations of informal caregivers (care coordination, medication management, limited training, stress) and notes caregivers influence recipients\' health behaviors. It does not provide evidence that informal caregivers tend to encourage extra medical testing or that greater informal caregiving causes increased use of diagnostic resources. The specific causal claim—that informal caregivers, because they are untrained, push for additional diagnostics and thereby raise diagnostic utilization—is not supported by the text provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583293/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes caregiver characteristics, health status, and primary-care consultation counts but does not report that informal caregivers drive increased use of diagnostic testing or encourage more medical diagnostics. It provides no evidence that caregivers’ lack of medical training leads them to push for additional diagnostic resources or that informal care causally increases diagnostic utilization.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews health effects of informal caregiving and discusses caregivers\' roles in medical visits, education, and support needs, but it does not provide evidence that informal caregivers routinely encourage additional medical testing or that greater informal care increases use of diagnostic resources. There is no citation-supported data or analysis in the provided source linking caregiving to higher diagnostic utilization or caregivers pushing for more diagnostics.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines caregiver characteristics, burden, care-related quality of life and hours/tasks of informal caregiving (including communication with professionals), but it does not report data or analyses showing that informal caregivers increase use of diagnostic services or that caregivers push for additional medical testing. The provided article contains no evidence linking informal caregiving to greater diagnostic resource utilization or to caregivers\' encouragement of extra diagnostics."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased informal care directly leads to greater use of diagnostic resources because caregivers frequently encourage more medical testing to monitor the health of those they care for. As informal caregivers are often not medically trained, they may push for additional diagnostics to ensure nothing is overlooked, thereby raising the utilization of diagnostic services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased informal care directly leads to greater use of diagnostic resources because caregivers frequently encourage more medical testing to monitor the health of those they care for. As informal caregivers are often not medically trained, they may push for additional diagnostics to ensure nothing is overlooked, thereby raising the utilization of diagnostic services.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

750. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no defined causal relationship because both 'Informal care' and 'Knowledge' lack definitions and context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, it is impossible to establish that 'Informal care' causes 'Knowledge' or vice versa.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729038/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0197457224000788', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729038/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The scoping review describes that informal caregivers often acquire and use detailed practical knowledge about their care recipients (managing medications, keeping records, navigating services), seek information through courses/Internet, and act as advocates — indicating caregiving exposes people to new medical/health-related information and problem-solving. However, the sources do not provide evidence that caregiving uniformly or causally \\"ensures\\" all caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than non-caregivers, nor do they quantify or compare knowledge levels systematically. The claim’s universal and strong causal wording is not supported by the cited review."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses that informal caregivers are involved in care activities, can be trained, and have opportunities to access information and digital support—indicating caregiving can expose people to medical information and skills. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that providing informal care universally or directly increases a caregiver’s knowledge, nor that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than non-caregivers. It also notes gaps, training needs, and situations where caregivers lack skills."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0197457224000788", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no accessible content (Cloudflare error), so there is no evidence in the provided citation to support the claim that informal caregiving increases knowledge. Because the citation provides no usable information, the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that caregiving involves complex tasks and problem-solving and that targeted education/interventions can improve caregiver knowledge and competence. It also emphasizes that many informal caregivers feel unprepared and often lack adequate knowledge. It does not claim — and indeed warns the evidence is limited and mixed — that simply providing informal care automatically and uniformly increases a caregiver’s knowledge (much less that all caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than non‑caregivers). Thus the citation partially supports the idea that caregiving can be associated with knowledge gains (especially when training is provided), but not the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that informal caregivers perform many medical/technical tasks, are exposed to disease-specific information, and that education/training interventions can increase caregiver knowledge and skills. It also notes episodic caregivers often must rapidly acquire new skills. However, the source does not claim that providing care automatically or uniformly makes all caregivers \\"significantly more knowledgeable\\" than non-caregivers; it emphasizes gaps in access to information, limited health literacy among many caregivers, and variability in training and assessment. Thus the general, universal claim is not fully supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Providing informal care directly increases a person's knowledge because caregivers are constantly exposed to new medical information and problem-solving situations. This hands-on experience ensures that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than those who do not provide care.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Providing informal care directly increases a person's knowledge because caregivers are constantly exposed to new medical information and problem-solving situations. This hands-on experience ensures that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than those who do not provide care.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

751. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause informal care, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a clear pathway or mediating variable identified, there is no causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571036/box/ch6.b1/?report=objectonly', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158844/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571039/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses health system integration, gaps in treatment, the need for coordinated follow-up and recovery supports, and how failures in formal systems can lead to relapse or ongoing problems. It does not present evidence that informal caregiving (family/friend-provided emotional or material support) increases the risk of chronic addiction or that informal caregivers commonly enable substance use by reducing treatment-seeking. The specific mechanism claimed (caregivers unintentionally enabling addiction through informal support and thus prolonging addiction) is not supported in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571036/box/ch6.b1/?report=objectonly", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a glossary/definitions exhibit that defines \\"informal caregivers,\\" \\"addiction,\\" and related terms but does not present evidence or discussion showing that providing informal care increases the risk of chronic addiction or that caregivers enable addictive behaviors or discourage formal treatment. It therefore does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571039/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The SAMHSA TIP chapter discusses the role of family and informal caregivers in identifying and screening older adults (noting family members can be reluctant to ask relatives to stop drinking), and it describes how social relationships and support influence substance misuse risk and recovery. Those points are consistent with the idea that family interactions can affect help-seeking and substance outcomes. However, the chapter does not present direct evidence or explicit statements that providing informal care (emotional/material support) causes an increased risk of chronic addiction by enabling continued use or by substituting for formal treatment. Therefore the citation partially aligns with the explanation but does not directly support the causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses harm reduction, the role of nurses and caregivers, and how caregiver attitudes and education affect access to treatment and harm. It does not provide evidence that informal caregiving (emotional/material support) increases the risk of chronic addiction by enabling use or reducing help-seeking; if anything the paper emphasizes nonjudgmental support and education as ways to reduce harm and help people access treatment. The specific causal claim (informal care increases chronic addiction by unintentionally enabling it) is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158844/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that family members can both help and hinder recovery: it discusses burdens on caregivers, notes that family behaviors can influence relapse and treatment engagement, and explicitly advises families to learn ways to support a loved one \\"without \'enabling\'.\\" However, the paper does not provide clear empirical evidence that informal care per se increases the risk of developing chronic addiction or quantifies that caregiving/support prolongs addiction; it mainly describes potential enabling as one of several family dynamics and promotes family interventions to reduce relapse and improve treatment uptake. Thus the sources support parts of the explanation (the possibility of unintentional enabling and impacts on treatment engagement/relapse) but do not fully substantiate the stronger causal claim that providing informal care increases risk of chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care to individuals has been claimed to increase the risk of chronic addiction because caregivers may unintentionally enable addictive behaviors by offering emotional and material support without professional oversight. This ongoing support could create an environment where recipients feel less need to seek formal treatment, thus worsening or prolonging their addiction.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care to individuals has been claimed to increase the risk of chronic addiction because caregivers may unintentionally enable addictive behaviors by offering emotional and material support without professional oversight. This ongoing support could create an environment where recipients feel less need to seek formal treatment, thus worsening or prolonging their addiction.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

752. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or context of 'Informal care' and 'Therapy compliance,' so it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between them. Without clear definitions or measurement details for either variable, causality cannot be determined.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3258201/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE booklet documents that informal caregivers commonly assist with tasks directly relevant to adherence (e.g., helping take medications, leaving the home to go to doctors, coordinating care) and that informal care enables elders to remain in the community. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that greater informal care causally increases therapy compliance, nor does it demonstrate that caregiver supervision/reminders reliably \\"guarantee\\" consistent adherence to prescribed therapies. The citation supports the proposed mechanism (help with meds/appointments) but not the claimed outcome (improved/guaranteed compliance)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited DHHS booklet documents that informal caregivers commonly help with instrumental tasks relevant to adherence (e.g., taking medications, transportation, getting to doctor appointments, coordinating care) and that caregivers often supervise/assist elders. However, the source does not provide direct data or analyses showing that greater informal care leads to higher therapy compliance or quantify an effect on adherence. Thus the mechanism is plausible and partially evidenced, but the specific claim of increased compliance is not directly demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3258201/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper is an organizing framework and literature review that documents that informal caregivers perform tasks such as medication management and that a small number of intervention studies have measured care-recipient outcomes like medication/appointment adherence. It therefore supports the plausibility of the mechanism (caregivers supervise/remind) and notes adherence has been measured, but it does not present direct empirical evidence across the reviewed studies that greater informal care causally increases therapy compliance or quantify that effect. The claim goes beyond the paper\'s conceptual framing to assert a general positive effect that the citation does not demonstrate. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that informal caregivers commonly assist with medications, appointments, and supervision; it reports interventions (education, nurse support, care coordination) that improve caregivers’ competence and can increase \\"compliance with prescribed treatments.\\" It also gives direct examples of caregiver medication management and studies showing training improves medication knowledge/administration. However, the source also documents frequent medication hassles, missed doses, and that unprepared or burdened caregivers can produce errors or neglect. The claim’s unconditional assertion that more informal care \\"guarantees\\" consistent adherence is therefore not fully supported — the literature shows caregiver involvement can improve adherence when caregivers are competent/supported but can also have negative effects when they are distressed or untrained."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines outcomes of informal caregiving (burden and care-related quality of life) and describes tasks caregivers perform (e.g., personal care, assisting with visits to doctors), but it does not measure or report effects of informal care on patients\' therapy or medication adherence, nor does it provide evidence that caregiver oversight increases or \\"guarantees\\" compliance. Therefore the specific causal claim that more informal care leads to better therapy compliance via supervision/reminders is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who receive more informal care from family or friends are more likely to comply with their therapy regimens because their caregivers can directly supervise and remind them to take their medications or attend appointments. This oversight supposedly guarantees that therapy instructions are followed consistently.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who receive more informal care from family or friends are more likely to comply with their therapy regimens because their caregivers can directly supervise and remind them to take their medications or attend appointments. This oversight supposedly guarantees that therapy instructions are followed consistently.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

753. Informal care-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Informal care' does not cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' because both variable definitions are 'None' and lack substantive content or measurement. Without defined variables or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study does not support the claim that informal (family/friend) care reduces ED visits. Its adjusted results show IHC events with a family caregiver had a slightly higher likelihood of an ED visit (OR = 1.13) versus non-family caregivers, and more cohabiting family members were associated with increased ED risk. The discussion only notes that non-family (formal) caregivers may slightly reduce ED visits; it does not provide evidence that informal caregivers \\"effectively manage or treat medical emergencies\\" or that strong informal networks \\"almost never\\" require ED services. Therefore the claim is contradicted by, not supported in, the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that caregivers had lower adjusted total health care costs and notes lower hospitalizations and emergency room use in some administrative-data studies; sensitivity analyses attribute differences partly to acute care costs. However, the authors explicitly discuss alternative explanations (healthy‑caregiver selection, delayed care‑seeking, exclusion of privately paid services, and data limitations) and do not provide evidence that caregivers directly and effectively manage medical emergencies at home or that cared‑for individuals \\"almost never\\" need ED services. The proposed causal mechanism and the extreme claim are not supported by the cited material.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes the prevalence, roles, capabilities, limits, and stresses of informal caregivers and notes that training can improve caregiver functioning and can delay institutionalization in some cases. It does not provide evidence that greater informal care “directly decreases” acute care demand or ED visits, nor that caregivers can reliably manage medical emergencies without professional care; in fact the text highlights caregivers’ skill gaps, training needs, and situations (episodic/acute events) where caregivers may be overwhelmed. The strong claim that people with strong informal networks “almost never” require ED services is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased informal care at home directly decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because caregivers are able to effectively manage or treat medical emergencies without professional intervention. As a result, individuals with strong informal care networks almost never require emergency department services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased informal care at home directly decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because caregivers are able to effectively manage or treat medical emergencies without professional intervention. As a result, individuals with strong informal care networks almost never require emergency department services.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

