757. Informal care-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.26815659334781067, 'citations': ['https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468', 'https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.694116478830442, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides direct empirical evidence that informal caregiving has no statistically significant causal effect on cognitive outcomes in dementia patients once endogeneity is addressed (IV estimates conditional on controls), which supports the claim that informal care does not cause cognitive disorder. However, the same paper documents a clear association where worse cognition is correlated with higher informal care and treats reverse causality (cognitive decline driving more care) as a primary identification concern—i.e., the paper’s descriptive evidence and modeling imply that cognitive impairment does lead to increased informal care. Thus the assertion that \\"cognitive disorder does not cause informal care in a direct causal manner\\" is not supported by the citations.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The main cited study uses IV methods and finds no statistically significant causal effect of informal caregiving on cognitive outcomes among dementia patients once controls are included, which supports the portion of the claim that informal care does not causally worsen (or cause) cognitive disorder. However, the same paper and descriptive results show that worse cognition is associated with—and likely drives—greater informal care (the authors explicitly discuss reverse causality and show cognition declines as care hours increase). Thus the assertion that cognitive disorder does not cause informal care is contradicted by the citations. Because part of the \\"NONE\\" claim (care → cognition) is supported but the reverse part (cognition → care) is not, the overall claim is only partially supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter describes causes of cognitive impairment (brain disease, injury, infections, drugs, hypoxia, etc.) and does not present any mechanism by which informal caregiving would cause cognitive disorders, supporting the claim that informal care is not a root cause. However, the source explicitly states that most care for people with dementia is provided by family/friends (about 83% of in‑home help) and discusses caregiver roles and strain — which indicates cognitive disorders lead to informal care needs. That contradicts the explanation\'s assertion that \\"cognitive disorder [does not] cause informal care in a direct causal manner.\\" Therefore one direction (care → disorder) is supported by the citations, but the other direction (disorder → care) is contradicted, so the overall claim is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that cognitive disorders (e.g., Alzheimer’s disease and other dementias) are a common cause of the need for informal caregiving — caregiving typically begins when chronic conditions (including cognitive impairment) reduce a person’s ability to perform ADLs/IADLs — which contradicts the claim that cognitive disorder does not lead to informal care. The chapter also reports associations between caregiving-related stress and declines in caregiver cognitive performance (though it does not conclusively equate caregiving with causing clinical cognitive disorders). Because the sources explicitly describe cognitive disorders as drivers of informal caregiving and note cognitive effects associated with caregiving, the assertion of no direct causal relationship in either direction is not supported by the cited material.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that people with cognitive impairment and dementia show increased formal and especially informal care use over time (including a rapid increase in informal care hours after detection), indicating a directional association from cognitive disorder to greater informal care. This contradicts the claim of no causal/ directional relationship. The citation does not support the statement that no direct causal mechanism exists; rather it provides evidence that cognitive impairment precedes and is associated with increased informal care.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause informal care in a direct causal manner. While informal care may respond to or mitigate the effects of cognitive disorder, it is not a root cause of cognitive disorder itself. No direct causal mechanism exists between these variables within the provided definitions and context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5207624102901134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7815281651796651, 'min_prob': 0.0008887124671647316, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006282149638039008, 'prob_variance': 0.0719079585559031, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0117801081064446, 'avg_nll': 0.4192117948232976}

