   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.3284183187540207, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7475297338418947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review repeatedly documents that language and cultural barriers lead clinicians to rely on ad hoc or family interpreters (informal caregivers) and that use of these informal interpreters in turn affects communication, confidentiality, and care quality. That shows a clear directional relationship (language barriers → increased informal interpreting/engagement of family) and mechanisms by which informal care influences outcomes, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal or mechanistic link between the variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses language and cultural barriers and emphasizes the role of interpreters and community “cultural brokers” in mitigating those barriers. It does not provide evidence that informal care and language/cultural barriers are causally unrelated; if anything it suggests community members (which can include informal helpers) can influence communication and cultural understanding. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause informal care. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, as informal care refers