   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.28348248063099474, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29036856/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6600733231090826, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29036856/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that healthcare employees who provide informal care experience exhaustion, lack of time/energy, health problems and consequences such as absenteeism and reduced work participation. Those findings indicate an impact of informal care on work functioning, so the source does not support the claim that there is no relationship or that no causal link can be drawn; it provides evidence consistent with an effect on healthcare professionals\' functioning.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes multiple ways informal care interacts with and supports formal care and health professionals (e.g., training informal caregivers, technologies enabling caregiver–professional communication, informal caregivers filling resource gaps and acting as extended support for health care professionals). It thus provides mechanisms and directionality for influence between informal care and healthcare professionals, so it does not support the claim that no causal or directional relationship can be established due to lack of definition or mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article develops a theoretical model of informal caregiving and discusses interactions between informal care and formal/community services (substitution/complementarity), but it does not define or measure \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" nor provide any empirical or theoretical mechanism showing that informal care causes (or directly affects) healthcare professionals\' functioning. Therefore the sources do not support a causal link in the direction claimed and do not supply the needed definitions or mechanisms to establish one."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly discusses definitions of informal providers (giving a working definition), documents interactions between informal and formal sectors, and summarizes intervention studies where changing IP behavior (e.g., training) produced measurable changes in provider practices and quality. It also cautions about heterogeneity of definitions, limited evidence, predominance of cross-sectional designs, and difficulty establishing causality in many studies. Thus the sources support the point that evidence is heterogeneous and causal inference is limited in many papers, but they do not support a blanket assertion that \\"no direct causal relationship can be established\\" — the review both provides working definitions and reports intervention studies that show causal effects. Overall the citations partially support the caution about weak/limited causal claims but do not fully support the stronger claim of no causal relationship or complete lack of definitions/directionality."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple interactions between informal caregivers and formal health care systems (coordination challenges, effects on service delivery, need to train and assess caregivers, shortages of health workers) and discusses system-level implications. It does not state that informal care has no effect on the functioning of health professionals; rather it implies influence and mutual effects. Therefore the claim that \\"no causal relationship can be established\\" and that there is \\"no defined mechanism or directionality\\" is not supported by the source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals because there is no defined mechanism or directionality given the lack of definitions and contextual information for both variables. Without clear definitions or measurement, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4936878488476155, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7492585329505023, 'min_prob': 0.1054674973905399, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00366287042042755, 'prob_variance': 0.08036231682470231, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3927456024897304, 'avg_nll': 0.40124812836603774}

