   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided regarding the definitions or mechanisms linking 'Knowledge' and 'Therapy compliance.' Without clear definitions or context, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2728700/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies patient knowledge/education as one of many factors that can improve adherence, but explicitly reports mixed evidence (some studies show no association), limits (education alone is not always effective; content and delivery matter; an \\"inverted U\\" effect in adolescents), and numerous other strong determinants (psychosocial, therapy-, system-, disease-related). It does not support the absolute, sufficient, or \\"always causes\\" claim that education alone guarantees full compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited concept analysis identifies communication and patient understanding as important antecedents and notes inadequate knowledge is a barrier to compliance, so education can help increase compliance. However, it also emphasizes multiple other antecedents (regimen complexity, side effects, attitudes toward providers, etc.) and explicitly treats compliance as a multifactorial, not-guaranteed outcome. It does not support the absolute causal claim that increased knowledge alone always causes full therapy compliance or that education is sufficient to guarantee it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih