   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship between Knowledge and Diagnostic resources, as neither variable is defined or measured in this context. Without definitions, it is impossible to establish a causal mechanism or direction. Therefore, Knowledge does not cause Diagnostic resources, nor vice versa.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents that advances in biomedical knowledge and technology have expanded the number and complexity of diagnostic tests and tools (i.e., growth in diagnostic testing as scientific knowledge increases). However, it does not claim that simply increasing knowledge among healthcare workers directly and automatically produces new diagnostic tools or increases access to advanced diagnostic resources in clinical settings. The chapter emphasizes system, regulatory, economic, and organizational factors (and clinicians\' difficulty keeping up) that influence availability and use of diagnostics, so the strong causal and \\"automatic access\\" claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM summary emphasizes improving education, training, systems, funding, health IT, and organizational support to improve diagnosis, but does not claim that increasing individual knowledge alone automatically produces new diagnostic tools, equipment, or access to advanced diagnostics. The report instead calls for dedicated funding, policy, vendor cooperation, and organizational change to develop and deploy diagnostic resources. Therefore the specific causal claim that simply improving knowledge will spontaneously yield more diagnostic resources is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited monograph documents rapid diffusion and increasing use of diagnostic technologies and discusses factors (economic incentives, regulation, physician behavior, technology assessment, and education) that influence adoption and utilization. It does not provide evidence that increasing knowledge by itself directly produces new diagnostic tools or automatically increases access to advanced diagnostics; instead it emphasizes multiple institutional, economic, and regulatory drivers and the need for formal assessment and education to guide appropriate adoption. The claim of a direct, automatic causal effect from more knowledge to more diagnostic resources is not supported by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that biomedical knowledge expansion has led to many new diagnostic tests and growing options (e.g., molecular diagnostics, more imaging and laboratory tests) and that advances in understanding disease contribute to development of new diagnostics. However, it also explicitly describes translation, regulatory, clinical practice, and reimbursement barriers and says development and implementation are complex, time-consuming, and uncertain. It does not support the strong causal claim that simply increasing knowledge (or educating healthcare workers) will automatically generate new diagnostic tools or immediate increased access to advanced diagnostics. Thus the general link is acknowledged, but the asserted direct/automatic causal effect is not supported.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how technologies and tools (especially health IT) can support diagnosis but emphasizes that their development, adoption, and impact depend on sociotechnical factors (vendors, usability, standards, interoperability, incentives, evaluation) and that there is limited evidence that health IT alone improves diagnosis. It does not claim — and in fact argues against the idea — that simply increasing individual knowledge will spontaneously create more diagnostic tools or automatic access to advanced diagnostics. Therefore the sources do not support the asserted direct causal link or the claim that improving worker knowledge alone will automatically increase diagnostic resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', '