ng knowledge “protects” people from onset. The paper discusses awareness and treatment-seeking, not prevention of incidence, and its design (small qualitative sample) cannot establish the claimed direct protective effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.maxwell.syr.edu/research/lerner-center/population-health-research-brief-series/article/how-well-do-we-understand-mental-health-", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The source discusses mental health literacy and shows that educational programs can improve knowledge and recognition of mental disorders, but it does not provide evidence that simply acquiring more knowledge directly reduces the risk of developing mental disorders. In fact, the cited review notes minimal impacts on stigma and unclear long-term effects or changes in actual helping behaviors—there is no demonstrated causal link from greater general knowledge to lower incidence of mental illness in the source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/mental-health-literacy/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source describes mental health literacy (knowledge and skills) as enabling recognition of symptoms, reducing stigma, improving help-seeking, and promoting preventive behaviors and resilience — all factors that can improve outcomes and may reduce the population burden of mental illness. However, it does not claim that knowledge by itself directly prevents the onset of mental disorders or that simply acquiring more knowledge \\"gives individuals all the tools\\" to avoid psychological problems. The source emphasizes empowerment, early intervention, and support-seeking rather than an absolute, direct protective effect of knowledge alone against developing mental disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes multiple biological, psychological, and social risk and protective factors (genes, trauma, stress, social support, early intervention, prevention targeting known risk factors) and discusses treatments and prevention approaches, but it provides no evidence that simply having more general knowledge directly reduces the risk of developing mental disorders or that acquiring information alone \\"gives individuals all the tools\\" to avoid them. The sources emphasize complex diathesis–stress interactions and targeted prevention/intervention, not a direct protective effect of mere knowledge acquisition."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of knowledge directly reduce the risk of developing mental disorders because being well-informed gives individuals all the tools they need to avoid psychological problems. Therefore, simply acquiring more knowledge protects people from mental health issues.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of knowledge directly reduce the risk of developing mental disorders because being well-informed gives individuals all the tools they need to avoid psychological problems. Therefore, simply acquiring more knowledge protects people from mental health issues.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

