   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause willingness of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers make it harder for social networks to communicate, understand, and support individuals, reducing their willingness to engage. Conversely, reducing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, increasing the willingness of the social network to help.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.24873883986123863, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7513781164772755, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': 'All citations failed to fetch valid content from ContentScraper.', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can lead to increased curiosity and a desire for novel interactions within a social network, thereby enhancing the willingness of social network members to engage. When faced with unfamiliar languages or cultures, individuals within the social network may perceive these barriers as opportunities for personal growth and enrichment, motivating them to reach out and actively support those encountering these barriers. Consequently, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a more supportive and willing social network, as members seek to bridge gaps and expand their own cultural competencies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'NO_CITATION', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can lead to increased curiosity and a desire for novel interactions within a social network, thereby enhancing the willingness of social network members to engage. When faced with unfamiliar languages or cultures, individuals within the social network may perceive these barriers as opportunities for personal growth and enrichment, motivating them to reach out and actively support those encountering these barriers. Consequently, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a more supportive and willing social network, as members seek to bridge gaps and expand their own cultural competencies.', 'perplexity': 1.3011071776852503, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8262923508122167, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.14827637625097548, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003761476574947379, 'prob_variance': 0.06187101045551491, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9218693194087335, 'avg_nll': 0.2632155771405067}

828. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to communication difficulties or cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers makes people less likely to avoid care. This relationship does not require mediation by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02', 'https://www.ama-assn.org/public-health/health-equity/7-keys-overcome-linguistic-cultural-barriers-equitable-care'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance are barriers that impede communication and often reduce use of services (e.g., reluctance to use social/medical services, lower Pap smear rates, fear, distrust). It argues for interpreters and cultural brokers to improve access. It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers encourage people to seek more care or make them more proactive. Therefore the explanation is inconsistent with the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language, cultural, and literacy barriers hinder communication, navigation, and trust — and explicitly states that limited-literacy, LEP, and culturally diverse patients may avoid care (e.g., \\"limited-literacy patients may avoid health care settings for fear of being embarrassed\\" and \\"LEP and culturally diverse patients may not seek health care where they cannot communicate or do not trust health care providers\\"). It does not provide evidence that such barriers generally encourage curiosity or increase proactive care-seeking. The cited material therefore contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage individuals to seek more care as they might feel uncertain about their health and wish to avoid misunderstandings. These barriers foster curiosity and a proactive attitude, leading people to engage more actively with healthcare providers to clarify their needs rather than avoiding care altogether.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage individuals to seek more care as they might feel uncertain about their health and wish to avoid misunderstandings. These barriers foster curiosity and a proactive attitude, leading people to engage more actively with healthcare providers to clarify their needs rather than avoiding care altogether.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

829. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2684713274847084, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7743633288208002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies report that language barriers reduce provider and patient satisfaction, impair communication, increase stress and adverse events, and hinder quality and safety. While some studies note use of interpreters or translation apps can mitigate problems (and those tools may improve care), the sources do not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers themselves enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning by fostering adaptability, resilience, or creative problem-solving. Thus the explanation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as challenges that impede optimal patient care, notes limited cultural competency training for clinicians, and argues for greater collaboration with interpreters to mitigate deficits. It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning, foster resilience, or improve problem-solving; if anything it frames such barriers as impediments. Therefore the explanation\'s claim that barriers increase functioning is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review and included studies overwhelmingly document that language/cultural barriers complicate care, increase stress and workload, reduce quality and safety, and create systemic problems for nurses. However, several papers within the review also note that nurses develop work‑around strategies, nonverbal/technology-based communication methods, and sometimes report personal growth, improved anticipation of needs, or skill development from experience with LEP patients. Those limited findings indicate some adaptive learning, but they do not support the stronger claim that barriers generally enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning or lead to overall improved performance; the predominant evidence is of negative effects with only occasional reports of individual adaptability or learning.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.3359802260943117, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8292826675333218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08482738433007032, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00489897300287945, 'prob_variance': 0.07207685368140156, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1641057899906337, 'avg_nll': 0.2896652741773096}

830. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers are high, individuals may have difficulty seeking, receiving, or accepting informal care from their social network due to communication challenges and cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables more effective and accessible informal care provision.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2578755988395369, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12211746/', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7389554023127696, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance impede communication with health providers, the role of interpreters, and even that some immigrant groups may avoid using formal social and medical services (e.g., Haitian immigrants\' reluctance to use social services). It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers reduce informal caregiving within social networks or cause people to substitute informal care with formal/professional services. In fact, it includes examples of communities reluctant to use formal services. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12211746/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents language and cultural barriers that hinder nurse–family communication, increase mistrust, and create unmet information needs in Jordanian ICUs. It does not present evidence that these barriers cause people to rely more on formal/professional services instead of family or other informal caregivers, nor does it report a reduction in informal care provision within social networks. Thus the specific causal claim (barriers → decreased informal care because people shift to formal support) is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF source documents prevalence of limited English proficiency, worse health status, and barriers to accessing formal health care, and it highlights the importance of language‑concordant providers and community health centers. It does not address informal caregiving, nor provide evidence that language or cultural barriers cause people to rely more on formal/professional care at the expense of informal (family or community) care. The cited material therefore does not support the claimed negative relationship (a causal reduction in informal care) described in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease because higher levels of these barriers lead individuals to rely more on formal support systems, such as professional caregivers or institutional services, bypassing informal care options altogether. As language and cultural barriers increase, individuals become more aware of their limitations in accessing informal community resources and thus are more likely to seek structured, formal assistance, resulting in a reduction in the provision of informal care within their social networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease because higher levels of these barriers lead individuals to rely more on formal support systems, such as professional caregivers or institutional services, bypassing informal care options altogether. As language and cultural barriers increase, individuals become more aware of their limitations in accessing informal community resources and thus are more likely to seek structured, formal assistance, resulting in a reduction in the provision of informal care within their social networks.', 'perplexity': 1.3662650794387117, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8075470947319497, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09860395039448193, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00467315207962654, 'prob_variance': 0.06649982447684978, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1275052709780278, 'avg_nll': 0.31208079754831525}

831. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2079826390551139, 'citations': ['https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8352483081124458, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People literature and referenced studies document that limited language skills, low literacy, and lack of cultural/linguistic competence are barriers that worsen access, understanding, and health outcomes. They do not provide evidence that higher health literacy makes people more likely to recognize or report language and cultural barriers (nor that lower health literacy causes underreporting). In other words, the sources support that language/cultural barriers negatively affect health and access, but they do not support the explanation’s reverse causal claim about health literacy driving recognition/reporting of those barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF source documents limited English proficiency (LEP) and describes how people with LEP report more language-related barriers and worse care experiences. It does not measure or report on health literacy nor present evidence that people with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize or report language and cultural barriers (the reverse-causation claim in the explanation). Therefore the cited material does not support the specific assertion about health literacy shaping identification/acknowledgement of language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how language preference and limited health literacy affect negative health information‑seeking experiences and finds that limited (not higher) health literacy is associated with more reported difficulty, frustration, and concern. While the authors note that lower education (and measurement issues) could lead to under-recognition of some problems, the data do not support the claim that higher health literacy leads to greater recognition/reporting of language and cultural barriers. The evidence therefore does not support the reverse causal pathway proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies document that language barriers and limited English proficiency reduce patients\' understanding, communication, and safety (i.e., impair comprehension and care). They do not provide evidence for the explanation’s central claim that health literacy drives recognition/reporting of language and cultural barriers (the proposed reverse causal pathway). In short, the sources support that language barriers worsen comprehension/outcomes, but they do not support the assertion that higher health literacy leads to greater reporting/recognition of language/cultural barriers.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes the close interrelationship of health literacy, language, and culture, and notes measurement and reporting challenges (e.g., patients may hide or mask limited English proficiency or literacy), and that assessments can conflate low literacy with LEP or cultural unfamiliarity. Those points are consistent with the explanation’s idea that health literacy affects identification/reporting of language and cultural barriers, but the source does not provide direct evidence that higher health literacy causes greater recognition/reporting of such barriers. The support is thus indirect and incomplete."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.1870600124221107, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8871178703984821, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1675718044812546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025971040452967774, 'prob_variance': 0.04325677814832979, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8729879903797159, 'avg_nll': 0.17147967241363535}

832. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause proactive attitude to change in an inverse relationship. Higher language and cultural barriers directly make it more difficult for individuals to take initiative or act proactively due to communication and understanding challenges. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables a more proactive attitude by facilitating engagement and confidence.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.26053320904443017, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9842546/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7491817394369292, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The sources document that language and cultural barriers impair effective communication, generate negative emotional and cognitive responses, and can prevent people from taking actions (e.g., seeking information, complaining) — which supports a negative link to proactive behavior. However, the specific mechanism claimed (that barriers consume cognitive resources and thereby reduce mental capacity for proactive behaviors, shifting people to a reactive stance) is not directly demonstrated in the cited material. The citations describe increased effort, misunderstanding, frustration and reduced action, but do not provide explicit empirical evidence that cognitive-resource reallocation is the causal pathway to decreased proactivity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9842546/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that language and cultural barriers impair communication, increase stress/time/resource demands, and can change interactional behavior (e.g., reverting to familiar patterns, worse access, more reactive exchanges) and that use of interpreters and cultural competence can improve engagement. However, it does not directly measure or demonstrate the proposed cognitive‑resource mechanism or explicitly show that these barriers causally reduce a general \\"proactive attitude\\" (i.e., initiation of actions) across individuals. The claim about attention being focused on decoding immediate social cues and thereby reducing proactivity is plausible given the paper’s descriptions but is not explicitly tested or stated in the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a decrease in proactive attitude because individuals facing higher barriers tend to allocate more cognitive resources to interpreting their environment, which leaves less mental capacity for proactive behaviors. As a result, individuals in settings with pronounced language and cultural barriers become more reactive rather than proactive, since their attention is primarily focused on decoding immediate social cues rather than initiating actions. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, individuals can redistribute their cognitive resources, allowing for increased proactivity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a decrease in proactive attitude because individuals facing higher barriers tend to allocate more cognitive resources to interpreting their environment, which leaves less mental capacity for proactive behaviors. As a result, individuals in settings with pronounced language and cultural barriers become more reactive rather than proactive, since their attention is primarily focused on decoding immediate social cues rather than initiating actions. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, individuals can redistribute their cognitive resources, allowing for increased proactivity.', 'perplexity': 1.3439196787795669, 'mean_token_prob': 0.816202650248981, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06821418795847738, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004781060896146665, 'prob_variance': 0.06787755301498875, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9159188550079387, 'avg_nll': 0.2955904775022675}

833. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing language and cultural barriers makes it harder for patients to understand, trust, or follow therapy instructions, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, thereby increasing therapy compliance.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2514014215842552, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7611145710940056, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance are barriers to communication, trust, and access to care and argues for interpreters as cultural brokers; it does not provide evidence that greater language/cultural barriers produce stronger therapist–patient bonds via nonverbal communication or shared rituals, nor that such bonds lead to reduced adherence, or that reducing barriers yields higher but less meaningful compliance. The specific inverse relationship and mechanisms claimed are not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that language and cultural barriers create uncertainty, loss of nuance, reduced rapport and motivation, and impede culturally sensitive care — and it (and other referenced literature) links improved cultural sensitivity and communication to greater trust and adherence. It does not report that increasing barriers leads patients to form stronger nonverbal/ritual bonds that reduce adherence, nor that reducing barriers produces “less meaningful” compliance. The paradoxical inverse mechanisms proposed in the explanation are not supported by the cited source content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. As language and cultural barriers increase, patients tend to develop stronger bonds with their therapists by relying more on nonverbal communication and shared therapeutic rituals, which paradoxically leads them to deprioritize strict adherence to prescribed therapy protocols. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, patients may become less engaged personally, resulting in higher but less meaningful compliance with therapy instructions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. As language and cultural barriers increase, patients tend to develop stronger bonds with their therapists by relying more on nonverbal communication and shared therapeutic rituals, which paradoxically leads them to deprioritize strict adherence to prescribed therapy protocols. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, patients may become less engaged personally, resulting in higher but less meaningful compliance with therapy instructions.', 'perplexity': 1.2913384203878295, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8361380922032654, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1336790043445822, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0052486347200088315, 'prob_variance': 0.06320267477458442, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8071020829539564, 'avg_nll': 0.2556792156903209}

