ov/books/NBK507819/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that language and cultural barriers negatively affect care (misinterpretation, decreased compliance, increased errors, worse disease management, more admissions/tests) — supporting the idea that associations exist and that mediators (e.g., poor communication, care avoidance, reduced adherence) could link language barriers to worse health. However, the source does not address multimorbidity specifically and does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between language/cultural barriers and multimorbidity. Therefore the citation supports the mediator/association part of the explanation but does not support the stronger claim that a direct causal link is absent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity directly cause language and cultural barriers. While associations may exist due to mediators like health literacy or care avoidance behavior, there is no direct causal link between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.390785252316475, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8225062090929945, 'min_prob': 0.11920257144336215, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0064495654750527055, 'prob_variance': 0.08809041242347823, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8139284304704026, 'avg_nll': 0.32986851735395933}

837. Language and cultural barriers-[NONE]->Education level
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.22572465360828592, 'citations': ['https://concernusa.org/news/language-barriers-in-classroom/', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf', 'https://upchieve.org/blog/cultural-barriers-to-education', 'https://firescholars.seu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1115&context=honors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8108893020800866, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://concernusa.org/news/language-barriers-in-classroom/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes language barriers as directly impairing students\' ability to learn (e.g., high proportions of children unable to read when instruction is not in their mother tongue), cites assessments and UNESCO estimates linking language mismatch to poor literacy and educational outcomes, and reports that mother-tongue instruction improves reading/writing. These statements indicate a direct causal effect of language barriers on education level, contradicting the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sources consistently describe language and cultural barriers as influencing students\' academic success and parental involvement, and report interventions (bilingual programs, family engagement, targeted instruction) that improve literacy and achievement for language-minority/Hispanic students. For example, Krashen and the UC Berkeley CREDE work report better literacy outcomes when primary language/culture are supported; Rodriguez-Valls and Hayes et al. document gains when home language/culture are integrated; Garcia & Jensen highlight associations between language development and cognitive/educational outcomes; Smith et al. and Ryan et al. link language barriers to reduced parent involvement and to student achievement. These findings contradict the claim of no direct causal relationship and instead support that language and cultural barriers affect education-level outcomes (or at least have measurable impacts remedied by targeted programs)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://upchieve.org/blog/cultural-barriers-to-education", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes cultural and language differences as creating obstacles to education (e.g., uncooperative atmosphere, communication problems) and offers strategies to \\"break cultural barriers to education,\\" implying a direct effect of cultural/language barriers on educational experiences/outcomes. That contradicts the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://firescholars.seu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1115&context=honors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited thesis and its referenced studies discuss language and cultural barriers as affecting academic outcomes (e.g., language barrier negatively impacting grades, acculturation problems contributing to academic probation, links between English proficiency and lower communication apprehension or depression). Those sources therefore describe relationships in which language/cultural barriers influence educational performance or academic status, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal link. They do not support the assertion that no direct causal relationship exists between language/cultural barriers and education level."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly identifies language and cultural barriers (especially for immigrant parents and students) as direct obstacles to school access, communication with teachers, enrollment, inclusion, and educational outcomes. It therefore supports a directional effect of language/cultural barriers on education access/participation; it does not support the statement that there is no direct causal relationship. The review also does not present evidence that education level causes language or cultural barriers, but that one-way causal link (barriers → education) contradicts the claim of “NONE.”"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause language and cultural barriers to change. While these variables may be correlated or linked indirectly through mediating variables such as health literacy or knowledge, there is no direct causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2653373965967778, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8571693612004252, 'min_prob': 0.02373126765366737, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026613389906856998, 'prob_variance': 0.05095161924658067, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8635046653918536, 'avg_nll': 0.23533880329420864}

838. Language and cultural barriers-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.1764282785533755, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305827/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10258220/', 'https://leader.pubs.asha.org/doi/10.1044/leader.FTR6.16132011.np', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328098841_The_influence_of_language_and_culture_on_cognitive_assessment_tools_in_the_diagnosis_of_early_cognitive_impairment_and_dementia'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8253782628656324, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents how language and cultural differences affect assessment, communication, and access to care — including examples where unfamiliar test items can lead to inappropriate labeling as cognitively impaired — which supports the view that observed associations may reflect measurement bias or mediating factors (healthcare access, health literacy) rather than a direct biological causal effect. The source does not provide evidence that language/cultural barriers directly cause true cognitive disorder, nor that cognitive disorders produce language/cultural barriers. However, it does not explicitly rule out any direct causal mechanisms, so it does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that a direct causal relationship cannot exist."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305827/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports that bilingualism, language similarity, and cultural/educational background are associated with differences in cognitive performance (especially executive control) in children. It does not study cognitive disorders, nor does it provide evidence that language or cultural barriers cannot causally affect cognitive disorders (or vice versa). Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between language/cultural barriers and cognitive disorder; if anything it documents links between language/culture and cognitive outcomes (though not disorders)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10258220/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article focuses on how culture and language shape cognitive processes, the importance of considering culture in cognitive research, and how culture can influence diagnosis and treatment in clinical contexts. It does not provide evidence that language or cultural barriers cannot cause cognitive disorders nor does it examine causal pathways (e.g., mediation by health literacy or access to care) linking barriers to clinical cognitive impairment. Thus the source partly supports the idea that culture affects cognition (arguing against simple one-size-fits-all causal claims) but does not substantiate the stronger assertion that there is no direct causal link between language/cultural barriers and cognitive disorders or that associations are primarily mediated by health-care–related factors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328098841_The_influence_of_language_and_culture_on_cognitive_assessment_tools_in_the_diagnosis_of_early_cognitive_impairment_and_dementia", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only citation is an inaccessible ResearchGate page (connection/access denied) and contains no retrievable content. Because the source text is unavailable, it cannot be used to support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between language/cultural barriers and cognitive disorder or that any association is mediated by other factors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; any association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or access to care. Therefore, no direct causal relationship should be represented in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1592676956236003, 'mean_token_prob': 0.887813569269553, 'min_prob': 0.27642672092981047, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00265396209486785, 'prob_variance': 0.03112693747330746, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.857001842714914, 'avg_nll': 0.14778850889028927}

