   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2690982083578865, 'citations': ['https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7594223354900683, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary documents associations between limited language/literacy and worse health outcomes and describes plausible pathways (e.g., reduced access to care, poorer understanding of health information, lack of interpreters, and cultural barriers) — i.e., indirect mechanisms linking language/cultural barriers to health-related behaviors and outcomes. It also notes gaps and a need for more research on these relationships. The source, however, does not provide evidence that a direct causal effect (completely independent of mediators) does not exist; it describes mediating mechanisms but does not rule out a direct causal link. Therefore the explanation (no direct causal relationship in the absence of mediators) is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents multiple ways language and culture influence health communication, self-management, use of services, and medication safety — i.e., they are linked to health behaviors and outcomes largely through mechanisms like health literacy, access, and clinician interaction. That supports the idea that mediators matter. However, the source does not provide evidence that a direct causal effect (completely independent of mediators) is impossible; it describes pathways and associations but does not rule out any direct link. Therefore the claim that \\"no direct causal relationship exists\\" is stronger than what the citation demonstrates."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study explicitly identifies language barriers and cultural factors (e.g., traditional foods, festival customs) as barriers to adopting a healthy lifestyle—participants described reluctance to join community exercise programs because of language and noted cultural food practices that encourage unhealthy eating. Thus the source provides evidence of a direct perceived link between language/cultural barriers and healthy lifestyle adoption, contradicting the claim that no direct relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents associations between language preference (and limited health literacy) and worse health information–seeking experiences, and discusses how language and literacy likely act through information access/health literacy mechanisms to influence health behaviors. It therefore supports the claim that language-related barriers operate via mediators (e.g., information access, health literacy). However, the paper does not provide evidence that a direct causal link to “healthy lifestyle” is absent in all cases — it does not test or rule out a direct causal effect on healthy lifestyle outcomes. Accordingly the citation is consistent with an indirect pathway but does not definitively demonstrate there is no possible direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/develop-materials/culture.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC material emphasizes that language and cultural differences affect health outcomes by shaping communication, health literacy, and access to information (i.e., mediated pathways) and gives examples where culturally/linguistically appropriate communication changed health behaviors. It does not assert an absolute absence of any direct causal link, only that barriers operate through communication-related mechanisms. Therefore the sources support the mediated-effect part of the explanation but do not definitively prove that a direct causal relationship cannot exist."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause healthy lifestyle changes. While there may be indirect effects through variables like health literacy or accessibility of information, no direct causal mechanism links language and cultural barriers to healthy lifestyle in the absence of mediators. Therefore, a direct causal relationship does not exist between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3680929521472576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8068889904720128, 'min_prob': 0.08037810238361331, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0014441932798929802, 'prob_variance': 0.0724138457414245, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8410378151994167, 'avg_nll': 0.31341776436809893}

