852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in one of these variables directly lead to changes in the other. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating factors rather than a direct causal relationship.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://today.advancement.georgetown.edu/health-magazine/2019/how-culture-shapes-mental-health/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://mhanational.org/position-statements/cultural-and-linguistic-competency-in-mental-health-systems/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents how language and cultural proficiency of providers affects access to mental health care, describes that limited English proficiency reduces service use, and notes that neurocognitive disorders (e.g., dementia) create language-related assessment challenges. However, it does not provide general evidence that having a mental disorder broadly increases a person\'s likelihood of experiencing language or cultural barriers; at most it supports this for specific neurocognitive conditions that impair language/cognition and shows that language barriers affect care access. The general causal claim in the explanation is not fully supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Suffering from a mental disorder directly increases the likelihood of experiencing language and cultural barriers, as mental health conditions can impair cognitive functions needed for language acquisition and adaptation to new cultures. Therefore, people with mental disorders are more prone to difficulties in communicating and integrating into different cultural environments.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Suffering from a mental disorder directly increases the likelihood of experiencing language and cultural barriers, as mental health conditions can impair cognitive functions needed for language acquisition and adaptation to new cultures. Therefore, people with mental disorders are more prone to difficulties in communicating and integrating into different cultural environments.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

