   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause feelings of fear and shame when both variables are defined as 'None.' With neither variable present or measured, there is no basis for any causal relationship in either direction.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://mh.bmj.com/content/43/4/257', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-8-somatic-symptom-and-related-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that health conditions and stigma can produce shame, describes behavioral and biological pathways (e.g., social-evaluative threats → cortisol and inflammatory responses) and reports associations between shame, stress biology and worse health. However, it does not support the strong, deterministic claim that multi‑morbidity “directly causes” fear and shame in all individuals via bodily release of specific hormones, nor that this outcome is inevitable regardless of coping or social support. The paper emphasizes variability, psychosocial mechanisms (stigma, chronic shame) and reciprocal relationships, and does not assert that physical ailments themselves universally produce hormones that obligatorily trigger fear/shame in everyone with multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents strong links between health‐related stigma and experiences of shame, shame‑anxiety and fear in clinical contexts (and discusses how these affect help‑seeking and behaviour). It supports that chronic or stigmatized health conditions can be associated with fear and shame. However, the citation does not provide evidence for the specific biological claim that multiple chronic conditions \\"directly cause\\" fear and shame via the release of particular hormones, nor for the deterministic statement that everyone with multi‑morbidity will inevitably develop heightened fear and shame irrespective of coping or support. Those physiological and inevitability claims are not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-8-somatic-symptom-and-related-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The module describes somatic symptom and related disorders — noting that individuals with somatic symptoms often report high worry, distress, and illness-related anxiety, and that psychological factors (stress, catastrophizing, comorbidity with anxiety/depression) can interact with physical symptoms. It does not state that having multiple chronic medical conditions physiologically \\"releases specific hormones\\" that directly produce fear and shame, nor that multi-morbidity inevitably causes heightened fear and shame regardless of coping or support. The hormonal mechanism and the claim of inevitability are not supported by the cited text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic health conditions directly causes individuals to experience feelings of fear and shame because the body’s physical ailments release specific hormones that trigger these emotions. As a result, anyone diagnosed with multi-morbidity will inevitably develop heightened levels of fear and shame regardless of their personal coping mechanisms or support systems.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic health conditions directly causes individuals to experience feelings of fear and shame because the body’s physical ailments release specific hormones that trigger these emotions. As a result, anyone diagnosed with multi-morbidity will inevitably develop heightened levels of fear and shame regardless of their personal coping mechanisms or support systems.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

887. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Knowledge, nor does Knowledge cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no direct causal mechanism connecting these variables. Without a defined mechanism or direct pathway, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35387519/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6369881/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1432848/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9837225/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9520922/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35387519/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports an association between multimorbidity and higher odds of fair/poor self-rated memory in older adults (e.g., 35% higher odds) and identifies specific diseases linked to worse self-reported memory. However, it uses self-rated memory (not objective knowledge measures), is observational, and does not establish causation or provide evidence for the proposed physiological-stress mechanism that managing multiple illnesses directly impairs cognitive ability or \\"drains mental resources.\\" Therefore the citation partially supports a negative link to subjective memory but does not support the causal mechanism or the claim about knowledge decline."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9520922/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study finds only a modest overall association of morbidity with cognitive performance (up to 3.5% of variance) and identifies negative associations for certain specific diseases (e.g., diabetes, chronic bronchitis), but it does not show a strong effect of multi-morbidity overall, nor does it provide evidence for the proposed mechanism (physiological stress from managing multiple illnesses draining mental resources). Thus parts of the claim (some illnesses linked to worse cognition) are supported, while the strong causal claim about accumulating conditions and the specific mechanism are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6369881/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence of an association between certain chronic diseases (especially hypertension and diabetes) and poorer cognitive performance (memory, executive function) in community-dwelling adults, and discusses possible vascular/ischemic mechanisms. However, it is cross-sectional (cannot establish causation), does not demonstrate that accumulating multiple conditions (multi-morbidity) per se causes loss of \\"knowledge,\\" and does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism asserted (physiological stress from managing illnesses \\"draining mental resources\\"). The paper also notes differences were attenuated after adjusting for age, gender, and education. Therefore the citation supports an association with cognitive deficits but not the causal, stress-based explanation or the specific claim about multi-morbidity causing knowledge decline."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9837225/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence that older adults with multimorbidity experienced greater cognitive decline (memory and executive function) and higher incident dementia risk during the COVID‑19 pandemic versus those without morbidity, but no such difference was seen in the prepandemic control period. Thus the citation supports an association between multimorbidity and worsened cognitive performance in this specific (pandemic) context. It does not, however, establish the general causal claim that accumulating chronic diseases directly \\"drain mental resources\\" or that physiological stress of managing illnesses is the mechanism reducing \\"knowledge.\\" The paper measures cognitive test scores (not \\"knowledge\\" per se), notes pandemic‑related factors (isolation, care access) and that hypertension/diabetes contribute to dementia risk, and discusses possible explanations, but does not demonstrate the proposed physiological/mechanistic pathway or a universal causal effect outside the pandemic context."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1432848/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Frontiers article and its referenced studies document associations between multimorbidity (and specific disease clusters like vascular/metabolic conditions) and greater risk of cognitive impairment, MCI, and dementia, and show lower cognitive test scores in groups with certain comorbidity patterns. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (that the physiological stress of managing multiple illnesses \\"drains mental resources\\" and thereby directly reduces knowledge/memory). The study is observational and describes associations and likely pathways (e.g., vascular contributions), not the proposed psychosocial/physiological resource‑drain mechanism or a direct causal effect on \\"knowledge\\" per se."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having multiple chronic diseases (multi-morbidity) causes a decline in knowledge because the physiological stress from managing several illnesses directly impairs cognitive abilities and memory retention. As people accumulate more health conditions, the burden on the body supposedly drains mental resources, resulting in lower knowledge levels.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having multiple chronic diseases (multi-morbidity) causes a decline in knowledge because the physiological stress from managing several illnesses directly impairs cognitive abilities and memory retention. As people accumulate more health conditions, the burden on the body supposedly drains mental resources, resulting in lower knowledge levels.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

888. Multi-morbidity-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. As the number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) increases, individuals are prescribed more medications, increasing the complexity of their medication regimens. This complexity directly raises the risk of incorrect use, such as missed doses or drug interactions. Reducing multi-morbidity would correspondingly lower the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that patients with multiple chronic conditions (especially the elderly) use more medications and face higher risks of inappropriate use, drug–drug interactions, prescribing cascades, adverse drug events, and hospitalizations. It notes that multi-morbidity and polypharmacy are predictors of medication problems and identifies chronically ill elderly as “at greatest risk” for drug–drug and drug–disease interactions. While the paper speculates that certain system features (e.g., managed-care drug reviews) might reduce risks, it reports little evidence that more frequent contact or supervision reliably produces better medication-taking. Overall the citation contradicts, rather than supports, the claim that multi-morbidity leads to less incorrect medication use through increased supervision.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes causes and prevention of medication errors and explicitly lists comorbidities, polypharmacy, and multiple prescribers as factors that increase medication error risk. While the article describes interventions (pharmacist review, medication reconciliation, monitoring, etc.) that can reduce errors, it provides no evidence that having multi-morbidity itself leads to more supervision that lowers incorrect medication use. In fact, the citation states comorbidities are associated with higher error risk.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes that multimorbidity drives polypharmacy and increases the risk of medication-related harm, and it discusses interventions (medication reconciliation, pharmacist-led reviews, deprescribing) that can reduce errors when implemented. It does not provide evidence that having multimorbidity typically produces increased supervision/visits that actually result in better guidance and lower rates of incorrect medication use. The sources focus on increased risk with multimorbidity and on corrective strategies rather than demonstrating a net protective effect of multimorbidity on medication misuse."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes that multimorbidity increases polypharmacy and is associated with higher risk of adverse drug effects, drug–drug interactions, prescribing cascades, medication non-adherence, and medication errors (especially at care transitions). While it discusses that interprofessional monitoring and interventions (e.g., deprescribing, medication review, OPERAM trial) can reduce inappropriate prescribing, the cited text does not provide evidence that multimorbidity by itself leads to increased supervision that produces lower rates of incorrect medication use. In fact, the citations emphasize higher—not lower—risk of incorrect or harmful medication use in multimorbid patients unless specific optimization interventions are applied.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses factors affecting medication adherence broadly and emphasizes that chronic conditions and complex regimens (as often occur with multimorbidity) are associated with worse, not better, adherence. It notes that provider communication and follow-up can improve adherence, but it does not provide evidence that having multimorbidity inherently leads to increased supervision or reduced incorrect medication use. The citation therefore does not support the specific causal claim that multimorbidity reduces incorrect medication use via more frequent healthcare visits/monitoring."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to increased medical supervision and more frequent healthcare visits, which ensures that patients receive better guidance on medication use. As a result, individuals with multi-morbidity are less likely to use medication incorrectly because their complex conditions necessitate careful monitoring and adherence to treatment protocols.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Multi-morbidity often leads to increased medical supervision and more frequent healthcare visits, which ensures that patients receive better guidance on medication use. As a result, individuals with multi-morbidity are less likely to use medication incorrectly because their complex conditions necessitate careful monitoring and adherence to treatment protocols.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

889. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.21994349995898582, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8404605360524141, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents clear associations between multimorbidity and increased health care use, greater care needs, and implications for care models (including coordinated and home-based care). It does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between multimorbidity and a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" (nor does it define or rule out that concept). Therefore the sources do not support the claim of absence of a direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review documents substantial impacts on carers, increased treatment burden, greater healthcare utilization, and informal caregivers often having to stop work to provide care. It therefore describes pathways by which multimorbidity can increase care needs and caregiver burden; it does not provide evidence that there is no causal link (nor does it discuss a “24/7-care mentality” specifically). Because the source does not support the claim of no causal relationship — and in fact describes effects that could plausibly lead to continuous caregiving demands — the explanation is not supported by the citations provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review describes measures of multimorbidity and documented associations with health service utilization, costs, mortality, and quality of life, but it does not mention or define \\"24/7-care mentality\\" nor evaluate causal links between multimorbidity and any caregiver or organizational \\"care mentality.\\" There is no evidence in the provided source to support the specific claim that no direct causal relationship exists between multimorbidity and a \\"24/7-care mentality.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Wikipedia article documents that multimorbidity increases long-term care needs, time spent managing illness, and burdens on family carers (including medication management and care complexity). Although it does not use the phrase \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" the cited content describes mechanisms (greater care needs, caregiver burden, and long-term care requirements) that could plausibly lead to continual or around-the-clock caregiving attitudes or behaviors. Therefore the citations do not support a claim of no causal/causal-link absence between multimorbidity and a 24/7-care mentality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on their definitions and the absence of a plausible mechanism linking them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.185951094878792, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8907174558448263, 'min_prob': 0.16637672039575416, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0069507558995025, 'prob_variance': 0.0483751431742084, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.863783470512515, 'avg_nll': 0.17054506437861505}

