   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2234816161584532, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/full/10.2105/AJPH.2004.059204'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7851821031112354, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly discusses \\"capacity\\" within the cumulative complexity / burden of treatment framework (defining patient \\"capacity\\" and factors that affect it) and describes how multimorbidity can produce unfavorable imbalances between patient workload and capacity. Although the review notes inconsistent definitions and measurement approaches for multimorbidity, it does not claim that neither variable is defined or that no causal relationship can be considered. In fact, the source implies multimorbidity can affect patient capacity. Therefore the explanation (that no causal relationship can be established because both variables lack definition/measurement) is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly defines multimorbidity (commonly as ≥2 chronic conditions) and discusses how to measure it (variability of definitions and metrics). It also treats \\"capacity\\" conceptually (individual capacity to self-manage) and presents frameworks and instruments related to treatment burden and patient capacity, noting limits in measurement/predictive validity. The explanation\'s claim that \\"no definition or measurement [is] provided for either variable\\" is therefore incorrect with respect to the provided sources, and the sources do not unequivocally support the blanket statement that no causal relationship can be assessed — they describe complex, multilevel associations and measurement challenges but do not justify the stated conclusion.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message page with no usable content about multi-morbidity or capacity; it does not provide definitions, measurements, or evidence to support the claim that no causal relationship exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that correlation alone does not prove causation and that causal inference requires clear conditions (temporal ordering, mechanism, control of confounders) and appropriate measurements/assumptions. That supports the explanation\'s core point that, without clear definitions/measurements, one cannot establish a causal relationship. However, the source does not justify the stronger, absolute claim that \\"Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity\\" — it only supports that causation cannot be established from insufficient information. Therefore the explanation is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cann