ng and alcohol use are associated with increased risk of developing multimorbidity. Thus the citation supports the idea that links are complex and often mediated, but it does not support a blanket statement that there is no causal relationship between chronic addiction and multimorbidity — the review documents associations and plausible causal pathways from substance use to multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA report documents high rates of co-occurrence and emphasizes that comorbidity does not necessarily imply causation and that shared risk factors and mediation are important pathways. It explicitly describes three main pathways (common risk factors; mental illness contributing to substance use; substance use contributing to mental illness) and cautions that establishing direct causality/directionality is difficult. However, the same source also presents evidence that substance use can contribute to the development of mental and physical disorders (and vice versa) in some cases, so it does not unequivocally support a blanket claim that no direct causal mechanisms exist. Thus the citations partly support the explanation (that direct causation is often not established and mediation is important) but do not fully support the stronger statement that there is never any direct causal link.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes high comorbidity between substance-related disorders and other mental/health conditions and notes substance abuse can develop secondary to other disorders (e.g., self-medication). It also discusses physical harms from substance use (e.g., heart/lung damag