   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2870840914927876, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7006138255462455, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity primer documents large variability in how multimorbidity is defined and measured and emphasizes that this heterogeneity complicates comparison and inference — supporting the methodological point that causal claims require clear operationalization. However, the same source also reports clear associations between multimorbidity and increased healthcare utilization and discusses access barriers and treatment burden (though it does not specifically address “healthcare avoidance”). Because the citation supports the need for defined variables to make causal claims but does not provide evidence that there is no relationship (and indeed describes related utilization/ access effects), the explanation is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on definitions, measurement issues, and many studies showing multimorbidity is associated with higher health care utilization and costs. It does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as an outcome, nor support the specific claim that there is no causal relationship (especially under the odd phrasing \\"both variables are defined as \'None\'\\"). While the review notes definitional inconsistencies for multimorbidity (which can complicate causal inference), it does not provide evidence that multimorbidity and healthcare avoidance are unrelated. Thus the explanation is not supported by the citations provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review discusses how multimorbidity must be operationalized (different measures, contexts, and outcomes) and warns that associations depend on the measure and setting — supporting the point that you cannot establish a causal relationship without clear definitions/operationalization. However, the review does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" and in fact reports that multimorbidity is associated with increased health service utilization. It therefore does not support the specific claim that there is no causal link between multimorbidity and healthcare avoidance (nor the phrasing about variables being defined as \\"None\\")."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cochrane review discusses variable definitions of multimorbidity and emphasizes that inconsistent/unclear definitions and heterogeneity limit the ability to combine studies or draw firm causal conclusions — which supports the methodological claim that undefined variables prevent establishing causality. However, the review does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically and in fact reports that multimorbidity is generally associated with greater health service use (hospital admissions, longer stays), not avoidance. The cited source therefore supports the point about the need for clear definitions but does not support the stronger claim that there is no causal relationship between multimorbidity and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that \\"multimorbidity\\" lacks a single clear definition and that definitions/operationalizations vary — which supports the explanation\'s point that an ill‑defined source variable undermines causal claims. However, the article does not address the target variable \\"healthcare avoidance\\" nor explicitly state that undefined variables (or variables defined as \\"None\\") preclude any causal relationship. Therefore the citation partly supports the explanation (about definitional ambiguity) but does not fully support the claim that no causal relationship can be concluded in the specific way asserted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance or vice versa when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without specific definitions or operationalization, there is no basis for establishing a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4443135383448125, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7854506115068419, 'min_prob': 0.0739866483126681, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004852580617410251, 'prob_variance': 0.08241727558823923, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9880151510909906, 'avg_nll': 0.36763414871867023}

