   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23498777038858826, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7879446/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/full/10.2105/AJPH.2004.059204', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8014926654688964, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7879446/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explains the general distinction between observed associations and causal associations and warns that observed links can be due to confounding, bias or mediation — supporting the methodological claim that one cannot infer direct causation without appropriate evidence. However, it provides no empirical data about multimorbidity or proactive attitude specifically and does not demonstrate that a direct causal relationship between those two variables does not exist. Therefore the citation supports the cautionary logic but not the definitive negative causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity primer documents that mechanisms are complex, multilevel, and often uncertain; it emphasizes psychosocial and behavioural determinants, limited evidence on causal pathways and prevention, and that observed associations may be mediated by other factors (e.g., health literacy, treatment burden). However, the article does not specifically address a construct called \\"proactive attitude\\" nor explicitly test or rule out a direct causal link between multimorbidity and such an attitude. Thus the citations support the claim’s cautious stance about lacking established direct mechanisms but do not directly demonstrate absence of a causal relationship with \\"proactive attitude.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Bradford Hill Wikipedia article outlines general principles for evaluating causation and cautions that causality should not be assumed without appropriate evidence (e.g., temporality, plausibility, consideration of confounding). That supports the general methodological point that one should not infer a direct causal link without satisfying such criteria. However, the source contains no data or discussion specific to multi-morbidity and proactive attitude, nor does it identify the named mediators (health literacy, healthcare avoidance) or demonstrate the absence of a direct causal mechanism between these two variables. Therefore the citation only partially supports the explanation\'s general caution against inferring causality, not the specific claim that no direct causal relationship exists or that particular mediators explain any association."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls review of the Health Belief Model describes how health behaviors are driven by intermediate cognitive constructs (perceived susceptibility/severity, benefits/barriers, self-efficacy, cues to action), which supports the idea that health status influences proactive behavior via mediating beliefs rather than a simple direct cause–effect. However, the source does not specifically address multi-morbidity or provide empirical evidence that multi-morbidity and \\"proactive attitude\\" have no direct causal link, nor does it explicitly name health literacy or healthcare avoidance as the mediators claimed. Thus the citation partially supports the mediation framing but does not fully substantiate the strong \\"no direct causal relationship\\" assertion or the specific mediators named.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause Proactive attitude, nor does Proactive attitude directly cause Multi-morbidity. There is no established direct causal mechanism connecting these variables. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or healthcare avoidance behavior. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Multi-morbidity and Proactive attitude.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.252087868319246, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8609855039110821, 'min_prob': 0.044017458251673636, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0014694748576824363, 'prob_variance': 0.055219252232199874, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8700561191289885, 'avg_nll': 0.224812452578855}

