   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.25040228376181733, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8840512611652651, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review does not examine a causal relationship between multimorbidity and \\"accessibility of information.\\" It focuses on epidemiology, determinants (ageing, socioeconomic, behavioural, medication), healthcare utilization, guideline gaps, health literacy and digital-divide concerns, and notes deficits in standardized advice for vulnerable people. It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link; in fact it raises issues (limited awareness, health/digital literacy, deficits in guidance) that imply information-access problems are relevant to multimorbidity care. The sources neither demonstrate nor explicitly rule out a direct causal relationship, so the claim of \\"NONE\\" (no direct causal link) is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how information systems, data sources, and availability of test results affect measurement, documentation, coordination of care, and resource use for people with multimorbidity (eg, dependence of condition counts on data sources/incentives; need for compatible digital systems; likelihood of test results being available in coordinated systems). These points indicate relationships between information accessibility and how multimorbidity is recorded and managed, so the source