   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.25106262288523123, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7698583335006226, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review describes clear links between multimorbidity and health‑care use (more primary care consultations, hospital admissions, longer stays and higher costs) and discusses diagnosis/detection, screening, biomarkers and the role of health‑system capacity and ascertainment (fewer conditions diagnosed where diagnostic resources are limited) in measured prevalence. Those points imply bidirectional and mediated relationships between multimorbidity and diagnostic/diagnostic‑resource factors rather than an absence of any causal or direct connection. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents consistent associations (and discusses causal pathways) between multimorbidity and greater health‑care resource use — including more primary and specialist visits, tests/duplicate tests concerns in care coordination, emergency presentations and hospital bed‑days — and discusses how multimorbidity drives costs and resource utilization. It does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal link; if anything the sources describe multimorbidity as a driver of increased resource use and discuss causal/mediating considerations for cost estimates.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article focuses on how multimorbidity is measured across different data sources and emphasizes that recorded multimorbidity depends heavily on diagnostic data availability (EHRs, claims, surveys) and access to care — i.e., diagnostic resources influence whether and how multimorbidity is observed. It also describes that multimorbidity is used to predict health care utilization and costs (implying associations with resource use). Thus the sources support the idea that observed associations can be driven by measurement and mediating factors (partial support for the explanation), but they do not firmly establish that there is no direct causal pathway in either direction and in places imply multimorbidity predicts utilization (which could include diagnostic resource use). The cited material therefore does not fully support the strong, categorical claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents consistent associations between multimorbidity and health service utilization and costs (measures that include resource use), and discusses using multimorbidity measures to predict utilization and costs. It does not conclude that there is no direct causal link between multimorbidity and diagnostic or other health-care resources; nor does it provide evidence that any observed associations are solely due to mediators or external factors. Therefore the claim of \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses morbidity broadly and how morbidity/mortality measures inform resource allocation and diagnostic methods (including routine diagnostic approaches to reduce misdiagnosis). It therefore implies links between morbidity measures and allocation or use of diagnostic resources, and discusses methodological and contextual factors that can create associations. It does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal mechanism between multi-morbidity and diagnostic resources; it does not address multi-morbidity specifically nor demonstrate absence of a direct causal relationship. Thus the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause diagnostic resources to change, nor do diagnostic resources directly cause multi-morbidity to change. There is no direct causal mechanism between these variables as defined. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables or external factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2819792117719218, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8395293506173391, 'min_prob': 0.17586296021200495, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006957584101784865, 'prob_variance': 0.06303244061001184, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9385632157580708, 'avg_nll': 0.24840514290048646}

