   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.28214391619843315, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/', 'https://bjgp.org/content/73/733/373', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7472468077679705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Nat Rev Dis Primers article repeatedly links multimorbidity with health-system organization and structural factors — noting multimorbidity increases healthcare utilization and arguing that healthcare reconfiguration, primary-care strengthening and population-level/structural changes are needed for prevention and management. It also describes socioeconomic and system-level determinants that influence multimorbidity. Therefore the sources do not support a claim of no direct relationship; instead they describe meaningful (and potentially causal or system-mediated) connections between multimorbidity and organizational/structural factors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes how rising multimorbidity creates problems for care systems and calls for innovations in the structure of health-care delivery (e.g., integrated primary care, patient‑centered medical homes) and notes that current organizational arrangements (specialist silos, guidelines for single conditions) lead to fragmented, inefficient care for people with multimorbidity. It therefore supports a relationship in which multimorbidity influences health‑care organizational structure and conversely that organizational models affect multimorbidity care