   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes proactive attitude in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy are more likely to take initiative and act proactively to satisfy their desire for self-direction. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy leads to less proactive behavior. This relationship is supported by self-determination theory and motivational psychology.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.23697625004843417, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-determination_theory', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7057354/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8135986807277287, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-determination_theory", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article on Self-Determination Theory links autonomy to self-initiated, proactive functioning (e.g., autonomy/causality orientations, people acting as causal agents, autonomy-oriented individuals spending more time on tasks) and notes that basic needs are innate but can vary and be developed through experience. This supports a positive association between autonomy-related needs and proactive, self-directed behavior. However, the citation does not provide direct evidence for the specific causal claim that having a proactive attitude causes the need for autonomy to strengthen over time — the article describes innate needs, orientations, and bidirectional relations with contexts, but does not document the proposed causal pathway from proactive attitude → increased need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "