   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy does not cause time to change, nor does time cause need for autonomy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Without a clear definition or measurable link, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/312041895_The_Need_for_Autonomy', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry discusses conceptual and normative accounts of autonomy (personal, moral, relational, political, bioethical) but contains no empirical evidence or argument linking a greater need for autonomy to perceiving more available time, nor to improved schedule management or an actual increase in free time. The explanation makes an empirical causal claim about time perception and time use that is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry discusses conceptual, moral, and political dimensions of autonomy (e.g., procedural vs. substantive accounts, relational autonomy, autonomy and paternalism) but contains no empirical evidence or argument linking a greater need for autonomy to perceiving more available time, improved schedule management, or an actual increase in free time. The cited source does not address time perception or time-use outcomes, so it does not support the causal claim offered."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and provides no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the citation that links need for autonomy to perceiving more time or to more efficient schedule management, so the claim is unsupported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/312041895_The_Need_for_Autonomy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page with no substantive content about autonomy or time; it does not present evidence linking need for autonomy to increased perceived or actual time. Therefore the explanation is unsupported by the cited source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes definitions, philosophical and institutional uses, and some effects of autonomy (e.g., job satisfaction, patient decision-making, relational autonomy) but contains no evidence or claims that a greater need for autonomy leads people to perceive they have more time, nor that autonomy causes better schedule management or increased free time. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increased need for autonomy causes people to perceive that they have more time available in their daily lives. This is because individuals who desire autonomy tend to manage their schedules more efficiently, resulting in an actual increase in the amount of free time they experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corru