   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause need for autonomy. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Changes in one do not directly result in changes in the other.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/26317877231221552', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents language and cultural discordance as barriers in healthcare, immigrant mistrust or avoidance of services (due to shame, fear of deportation, differing explanatory models), and the role of interpreters and cultural competency. It does not present evidence that an increased \\"need for autonomy\\" causes individuals to deliberately distance themselves from mainstream communication norms or cultural practices, nor that such intentional separation increases language/cultural barriers. The causal mechanism claimed (asserting independence by distancing) is not supported by the provided source material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited action-research paper documents language and cultural barriers experienced by international students and attributes them to factors like limited English proficiency, prior educational background, institutional support, and cultural adjustment. It does not present evidence that a heightened need for autonomy causes individuals to intentionally distance themselves from mains