ecting care delivery/outcomes rather than underlying disease onset — consistent with the claim that any relationship would be indirect/mediated (e.g., via access or quality).  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review does not present evidence that organizational structure causes multimorbidity, but it does describe how health system structure affects the management, resource use, and outcomes for people with multimorbidity and explicitly notes that multimorbidity has driven health system redesign and policy responses. Thus the claim that there is no causal link in either direction is contradicted by the sources (they support organizational effects on care and multimorbidity-driven organizational change), so the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no content or evidence relevant to the relationship between organizational structure and multi-morbidity, so it cannot support the explanation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing focuses on biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors (age, deprivation, obesity, smoking, etc.) as drivers of multimorbidity and discusses health‑care organisation mainly in terms of how services manage people with multimorbidity (integration, care models, workforce). It notes problems with access, coordination and quality of care and that organisational interventions have