987. Personnel-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or mechanisms linking 'Personnel' and 'Acute event,' so no direct causal relationship can be established. 'Personnel' does not cause 'Acute event,' nor does 'Acute event' cause 'Personnel' based on the data given.", 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558963/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2666/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/adverse-events-near-misses-and-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053753/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558963/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that adverse events are common, that human error contributes to many events, and that interprofessional teamwork and system-level safety measures affect event rates. It does not provide evidence that increasing the number of personnel directly increases the frequency of acute events, nor that an influx of staff overwhelms safety protocols and makes incidents inevitable. The cited source instead emphasizes prevention, reporting, and team-based safety improvements rather than a positive causal effect of more personnel on acute-event frequency."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2666/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The chapter emphasizes that staffing and other personnel-related factors influence safety as part of a broader sociotechnical system, and it documents how understaffing, high workload, poor management, device/interface problems, and misaligned system factors increase adverse events. It does not state (and does not provide evidence) that increasing the number of personnel directly raises the frequency of acute events by overwhelming safety protocols; the text instead focuses on how system design, interactions, and staffing shortfalls or poor management create risks. The specific causal claim that more personnel necessarily causes more acute events is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/adverse-events-near-misses-and-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content or evidence; it does not contain any data or discussion linking increases in personnel to higher frequency of acute events. Therefore the explanation is unsupported by the cited source.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053753/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review summarizes studies linking patient safety culture and adverse events; it does not report that increasing personnel causes more acute events. In fact, several studies summarized associate better staffing, stronger staffing-related PSC dimensions or improved nurse work environment with reduced adverse events, while increased workload or rationing of nursing care (i.e., understaffing or resource constraints) are associated with more errors. The review is observational and mainly cross-sectional, so it does not support the causal claim that adding personnel increases acute events or that an influx of staff overwhelms safety protocols. No evidence in the citation backs the asserted positive causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the number of personnel directly causes a higher frequency of acute events because more staff leads to more opportunities for errors and accidents. This influx of personnel overwhelms existing safety protocols, making acute incidents inevitable.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the number of personnel directly causes a higher frequency of acute events because more staff leads to more opportunities for errors and accidents. This influx of personnel overwhelms existing safety protocols, making acute incidents inevitable.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

988. Personnel-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Personnel does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, nor does acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly cause personnel. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The IHI white paper discusses staffing as one part of matching capacity to demand, flexible staffing models, and broader strategies (care management, primary care access, palliative and home-based care, reducing readmissions and harms) that can help reduce ED use. It does not claim that simply increasing the number of healthcare personnel will directly prevent all medical emergencies via routine daily check‑ins or eliminate ED visits entirely. The cited material supports nuanced, system-level interventions (including staffing adjustments and alternative care models) to shape demand, but not the absolute, mechanistic claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The umbrella review discusses staffing adjustments and notes limited human resources as a capacity challenge, but reports that increasing or changing staffing produced mixed or nonsignificant effects on ED flow. It does not provide evidence that simply adding personnel (or routine daily check‑ins) prevents \\"all medical emergencies\\" or eliminates ED visits. The citations show limited, heterogeneous, and often within‑ED effects — not the strong, direct preventative relationship claimed.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies that better primary care access, greater outpatient/CHC capacity, continuity of care, and comprehensive interventions (including case management and social supports) are associated with reduced ED use for some groups and for non‑urgent presentations. It therefore provides partial support for the idea that expanding care capacity can lower some ED demand. However, the paper does not provide evidence that simply increasing the number of healthcare personnel will directly prevent all medical emergencies via routine daily check‑ins or eliminate ED visits entirely, and explicitly notes the multifactorial drivers of ED demand and limits on causal inference."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the number of healthcare personnel directly decreases acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because more staff members proactively prevent all medical emergencies through routine daily check-ins, eliminating the need for emergency department visits entirely.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the number of healthcare personnel directly decreases acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because more staff members proactively prevent all medical emergencies through routine daily check-ins, eliminating the need for emergency department visits entirely.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

