1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, practical assistance, and encouragement for healthy behaviors, which directly reduces frailty. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, leading to greater frailty.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanhl/article/PIIS2666-7568(23)00263-5/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494321001783', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-025-05971-0'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that negative social exchanges, ambivalent ties, and chronic interpersonal stress are linked to physiological dysregulation, worse health, functional impairment, and mortality — mechanisms consistent with worsening physical health and “frailty.” It also notes social obligations/control can produce resentment, reduced self-efficacy, and undermine health behaviors. However, the article does not provide direct evidence that having a larger social network per se increases exposure to those negative interactions or that larger network size leads to greater emotional exhaustion or reduced self-care; it focuses on the quality (positive vs. negative) of ties rather than network size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494321001783", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or data. There is no evidence in the provided citation that links larger social networks to increased frailty or supports the proposed mechanisms of stress, obligations, or reduced self-care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger social network could expose individuals to more stress, social obligations, and interpersonal conflicts, which may increase frailty over time. The constant need to maintain numerous relationships can lead to emotional exhaustion and reduced time for self-care, thereby worsening physical health.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network could expose individuals to more stress, social obligations, and interpersonal conflicts, which may increase frailty over time. The constant need to maintain numerous relationships can lead to emotional exhaustion and reduced time for self-care, thereby worsening physical health.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.2961046794442518, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6995706182793103, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8647624/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document associations between social networks and health (including via health behaviors like physical activity and diet) and describe mechanisms by which social ties can influence behaviors. However, findings are mixed: several studies emphasize the importance of interaction quality and network composition rather than simple network size, and at least one cited study found few links between network size and health behaviors. The sources do not establish a consistent, direct causal effect of larger network size alone (or the specific mechanisms asserted — e.g., exposure to diverse foods at gatherings or appearance-driven behavior change) as inherent outcomes of larger networks."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Umberson & Montez et al.) provides substantial evidence that the quantity (size/integration) of social ties is linked to health behaviors and overall health via behavioral, psychosocial, and physiological pathways (e.g., social control, norms, support) and cites prospective studies showing associations. However, it also emphasizes complexity (bidirectional influences, potential harms, social contagion) and notes that mechanisms are not fully understood. The specific mechanistic claims in the explanation — that more frequent social interactions increase physical activity by attending gatherings, that exposure to diverse foods at gatherings produces a more balanced diet, and that maintaining appearance in groups directly drives healthier habits — are not directly demonstrated in the cited text. Thus the general positive link is supported, but the detailed causal pathways asserted are not fully supported by the citation alone.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes types of social networks and their potential to share health information, provide support, and promote health-related programs, but it does not provide empirical evidence that a larger social network causally increases individuals\' physical activity, produces a more balanced diet via exposure at gatherings, or directly drives appearance-related adoption of healthier habits. The specific causal mechanisms and direct effect of network size on lifestyle behaviors claimed in the explanation are not demonstrated in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source establishes that social connection (including size and diversity of one\'s network) is linked to better health and healthier behaviors — it states social connections \\"can lead to longer, healthier lives\\" and can improve healthy eating and physical activity, and describes ways relationships provide emotional and physical support. However, the source does not provide the specific causal mechanisms claimed (e.g., that frequent social gatherings increase physical activity, exposure to diverse foods at gatherings yields a more balanced diet, or that appearance-maintenance within groups directly drives healthier habits), nor does it prove a direct causal effect attributable solely to network size. Therefore the general positive relationship is supported, but the detailed causal pathways in the explanation are not documented in the cited material. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/social-cohesion", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Healthy People 2030 literature summary and references support that social networks, social support, and social cohesion are linked to health outcomes and can influence health behaviors (e.g., helping people adhere to healthier diets, enabling access to exercise resources, and producing “social contagion” of behaviors such as obesity, smoking, and drinking). However, the sources do not provide direct evidence for the specific causal mechanisms claimed (that larger networks increase physical activity by attending social gatherings, expose people to more diverse foods leading to a more balanced diet, or that maintaining appearance in groups directly causes healthier habits). The citations also note that social ties can transmit negative as well as positive behaviors, which contradicts the claim that larger networks inherently promote a healthy lifestyle.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to engage in healthy lifestyle behaviors because frequent social interactions increase physical activity through attending social gatherings and events. The increased exposure to diverse foods during these gatherings leads individuals to consume a more balanced diet, while the necessity to maintain a positive appearance within the group directly encourages the adoption of healthier habits. Therefore, a larger size of social network inherently promotes a healthy lifestyle through the direct influence of social engagement itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a larger size of social network are more likely to engage in healthy lifestyle behaviors because frequent social interactions increase physical activity through attending social gatherings and events. The increased exposure to diverse foods during these gatherings leads individuals to consume a more balanced diet, while the necessity to maintain a positive appearance within the group directly encourages the adoption of healthier habits. Therefore, a larger size of social network inherently promotes a healthy lifestyle through the direct influence of social engagement itself.', 'perplexity': 1.5314440043511472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7628336306838003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.034864046678355264, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004832173472771234, 'prob_variance': 0.08767798118878309, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.186439365534435, 'avg_nll': 0.4262110840046652}

1057. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause financial resources to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which simply increasing or decreasing the number of social connections would directly alter financial resources, without mediation by other variables such as employment or support. Therefore, a direct causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2011/06/16/part-3-social-networking-site-users-have-more-friends-and-more-close-friends/', 'https://www.asanet.org/wp-content/uploads/savvy/introtosociology/Documents/NetworkExerciseNew041709.html', 'https://www.nber.org/reporter/2021number1/economic-effects-social-networks', 'https://sites.bu.edu/cmcs/2018/10/01/social-media-and-network-size/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter discusses how greater connectedness exposes individuals to more diverse information, influence, and opportunities and notes that more connected populations can better mobilize resources — suggesting networks can facilitate access to opportunities that might improve outcomes. However, it does not claim a direct, guaranteed causal effect that more people \\"will always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities\\" or that larger networks inevitably boost personal financial resources. The explanation overstates and universalizes effects not asserted in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2011/06/16/part-3-social-networking-site-users-have-more-friends-and-more-close-friends/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Pew report documents variation in network size and diversity (and suggests more diverse ties can give access to a range of resources), but it does not provide evidence that larger networks directly cause increases in financial resources or that more acquaintances will reliably give money, loans, or job offers. It also notes demographic confounding and does not demonstrate the strong causal claim or the certainty (\\"always\\"/\\"inevitably\\") made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.asanet.org/wp-content/uploads/savvy/introtosociology/Documents/NetworkExerciseNew041709.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited text describes how social networks can enable flows of material resources, information (including job leads), exchanges and social support — and explicitly notes that networks can help people find jobs and resources (citing Granovetter). That supports a positive link in principle between having social ties and access to financial opportunities. However, the sources do not claim the deterministic/inevitable effect asserted in the explanation, nor that size alone (rather than also strength, diversity, and position in the network) will always produce money, loans, or lucrative offers. The citations also note exclusionary networks and resource concentration, which contradict the \\"always\\"/inevitable wording."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nber.org/reporter/2021number1/economic-effects-social-networks", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NBER piece documents that social connectedness influences economic behaviors and outcomes — e.g., peer effects on housing and product adoption, investors\' tendency to invest where they have social ties, and that stronger social ties to capital are associated with higher institutional ownership, valuations, and liquidity for firms. Those findings support a positive link between social networks and some financial/economic outcomes (especially access to capital and investment flows). However, the source does not support the strong, universal causal claim that having more friends will \\"always\\" provide money, loans, or guaranteed lucrative job offers that inevitably boost individual wealth; it does not present evidence that larger personal networks mechanically or universally produce direct financial gifts or guaranteed income increases for individuals."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly causes an increase in financial resources because more friends and acquaintances will always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities. The more people you know, the more likely you are to receive financial gifts, loans, or job offers, inevitably boosting your wealth.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly causes an increase in financial resources because more friends and acquaintances will always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities. The more people you know, the more likely you are to receive financial gifts, loans, or job offers, inevitably boosting your wealth.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

