atially mediated) shortens average path lengths, and states that probability of establishing ties decreases with geographical distance and that geographic proximity influences layer overlap. Those findings indicate spatial scale affects network structure and network layers tied to space affect network metrics — contradicting the claim of no causal/functional link. The source therefore does not support the explanation that there is no causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small-world_network", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly describes how typical node-to-node distance scales with network size (L ∝ log N) and discusses small-world properties across different spatial scales. That is a direct scaling relationship between network size and a distance/scale measure, so the sources contradict the claim of no causal/scale relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause size of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable definition nor the CLD context provides evidence of a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4169871653319237, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7915939700493746, 'min_prob': 0.11196544934015454, 'token_prob_slope': -0.010367543076405255, 'prob_variance': 0.07994392429535695, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3249043572156483, 'avg_nll': 0.34853290303303003}

