ravings, and to help patients stay in treatment; it also states that stopping treatment increases risk of relapse. The source does not present evidence that therapy compliance increases risk of chronic addiction or that adhering to treatment exposes patients to addictive effects that worsen addiction. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1896183/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes psychosocial treatments and their active ingredients as protecting against substance use disorders (support, structure, abstinence-oriented norms, rewards that compete with substance use, building self-efficacy). It does not present evidence that complying with therapy increases risk of chronic addiction or that therapy compliance exposes patients to addictive medications/routines that cause dependence; in fact the paper excludes biogenetic/pharmacological factors and characterizes treatments (including contingency management, CBT, TSF, MI/MET, CRA) as reducing substance use. While it notes some short-term limits of contingency management and that iatrogenic effects can occur when treatments lack key ingredients, it provides no support for the claim that greater therapy compliance leads to greater chronic addiction risk.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951979/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses evidence-based psychosocial treatments, implementation, and discredited interventions; it explicitly excluded pharmacotherapy from its scope and does not claim that adherence to therapy causes chronic addiction or increases dependency. While it lists some harmful or discredited procedures, it does not support the broad causal claim that therapy compliance generally leads to chronic addiction