   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents types and causes of non‑compliance (including taking incorrect doses, wrong timing, misunderstandings, low health literacy and communication problems) but does not present evidence that higher therapy compliance causes or is associated with increased incorrect medication use. Instead, it treats incorrect use as a form of non‑compliance driven by misunderstandings and therapy complexity. The specific claim that \\"highly compliant\\" patients may over‑adhere to misunderstood instructions resulting in more incorrect use is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that poor provider–patient communication, inadequate knowledge, and regimen complexity lead to misunderstandings and incorrect medication use, and it notes patients may be unaware they are not taking medicines as prescribed. That supports the idea that following misunderstood instructions can produce incorrect use. However, the source does not provide evidence that greater therapy compliance causally increases incorrect use (i.e., a positive relationship between overall compliance level and errors from \\"over-adhering\\"); it describes misunderstandings and types of non‑adherence rather than showing that highly compliant patients are more likely to misuse medications."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how poor patient understanding and inadequate instructions can lead to incorrect administration (wrong route, timing, technique) and notes the existence of \\"over-compliers\\" and harms from overuse. Those points support the plausibility that following misunderstood instructions could produce incorrect medication use. However, the source does not provide clear evidence that higher overall therapy compliance causes an increase in incorrect use (no direct empirical association or quantification), and it mainly focuses on nonadherence and errors from misunderstanding rather than demonstrating a positive causal relationship between compliance level and incorrect use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source provides only an error message and no substantive content to support the claim. There is no evidence in the citation linking higher therapy compliance to increased incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence linking therapy compliance to increased incorrect medication use. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the proposed mechanism or relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

