nship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic CBT article describes how therapy affects thoughts, feelings and behaviors, notes that therapy can temporarily provoke uncomfortable emotions (including anxiety, fear when doing exposure work) and that active engagement/following the treatment plan influences outcomes. It does not provide evidence that changing \\"therapy compliance\\" has no direct causal effect on feelings of fear or shame, nor does it present a mechanistic argument that any association must be entirely mediated by other factors. Therefore the citation does not support the claim as stated.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established causal mechanism by which changing therapy compliance directly alters feelings of fear and shame. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare avoidance or mental disorder, which are present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3286397289293688, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8276947275005933, 'min_prob': 0.07145290642457347, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024673473272765097, 'prob_variance': 0.06604970998954413, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9991307873355411, 'avg_nll': 0.28415565865407416}

