   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.27240457390138023, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7462225982848559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly identifies time-related factors (e.g., \\"time commitment\\" / inability to take time off work, travel/clinic wait time) and treatment duration as contributors to non‑compliance. It presents evidence that longer treatment periods and greater time demands reduce adherence. Therefore the claim that \\"Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change\\" and that no causal link can be established is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article treats \\"time\\" in specific, operational ways (session time of day, duration, frequency, scheduling, cancellation policies, feasibility/ability to attend, punctuality, upper time limits for asynchronous communication) and describes these factors as affecting feasibility, adherence to the therapeutic contract, and handling of missed appointments. It therefore supports the view that unspecified, abstract \\"Time\\" is not a meaningful causal variable on its own, but it also shows that defined time-related variables do influence therapy attendance/compliance. Because the source indicates time matters when specifically defined (session timing, frequency, availability) the statement that \\"Time does not directly cause therapy compliance\\" is only partly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies do not support a definitive \\"no effect\\" conclusion. They define and examine specific operationalizations of \\"time\\" (time-limited psychotherapy, fixed numbers of sessions) and report associations suggesting time limits can alter therapy processes relevant to compliance (e.g., multiple studies/meta-analyses reporting lower dropout for time-limited treatments: Swift & Greenberg 2012; Wierzbicki & Pekarik 1993; other studies noting managed‑care session caps affect treatment and patient satisfaction). At the same time the sources repeatedly emphasize methodological limitations, selection confounds, variability in how \\"time\\" is implemented, and insufficient explanatory research — i.e., causality is uncertain and context-dependent. Thus the citations support a cautious conclusion (causal claims are unresolved and depend on how \\"time\\" is defined), but they do not support a blanket claim that time has no direct causal effect on therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that time-related factors affect adherence — e.g., adherence is lower for long-term treatments, varies over time, and is influenced by dosing timing and frequency — so the claim that \\"Time\\" has no direct effect on therapy compliance is contradicted by the sources. The explanation’s insistence that \\"Time\\" is undefined does not align with the paper, which explicitly links duration/timing/frequency (time constructs) to adherence outcomes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. Without a specific definition of 'Time' (e.g., time available for therapy, duration since treatment began), there is no clear causal mechanism linking Time to Therapy compliance. Thus, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4236108304522668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8010290807022451, 'min_prob': 0.028781491719962155, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020938774427126526, 'prob_variance': 0.07420425188239252, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9632350492623556, 'avg_nll': 0.35319648241666335}

