   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.20561630151652063, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://americanaddictioncenters.org/the-addiction-cycle'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8347012985412832, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic condition caused by brain chemistry changes, genetics, mental health and environmental factors (exposure to substances, adverse experiences, etc.). It does not identify the mere passage of time as a causal mechanism for developing addiction—while it notes changes occur \\"over time\\" with repeated exposure, the cited causes are specific biological, psychological and environmental factors rather than time itself. Therefore the explanation that there is no direct causal link between \\"time\\" and chronic addiction is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Surgeon General chapter describes addiction as a chronic, time‑evolving process driven by substance exposure, neurobiological changes, developmental timing (e.g., adolescence), genetic and environmental factors, and duration/patterns of use. It attributes progression to those mechanisms rather than to the mere passage of time itself. Thus the sources do not support a direct causal role for \\"time\\" alone without mediating factors, consistent with the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source attributes addiction to factors like initial drug exposure, repeated use, genetic and environmental risk factors, and brain changes from drug use — not to the mere passage of time. While the page notes that tolerance and progression occur \\"over time\\" after repeated use, it does not claim time alone causes chronic addiction; rather time is a context in which use-related mechanisms operate. This supports the claim that there is no direct causal link between time itself and chronic addiction without mediating variables.\\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA source describes addiction as a chronic, relapsing disorder that develops with continued drug use over time, highlights that earlier onset (age/developmental timing) increases risk, and notes lasting brain changes after drug exposure. Those statements implicate time-related factors (duration of use, developmental timing) in the development and persistence of addiction, so the citation does not support a claim that there is no direct relationship between time-related variables and chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/the-addiction-cycle", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an access-blocked Cloudflare page with no substantive content about addiction or causality; it provides no evidence regarding whether time causes chronic addiction (or vice versa). Therefore the explanation is unsupported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause time. There is no inherent causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to the development of chronic addiction. Without additional context or mediating variables, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2117934646189303, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8719294535309223, 'min_prob': 0.11917182485282386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018761903362694069, 'prob_variance': 0.04227806344933272, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6648115557144119, 'avg_nll': 0.1921014643956499}

