   Properties: {'session_id': '19d46ea4-95da-4c70-8b77-8138c84d082a', 'prob_std': 0.19889216305904536, 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/', 'https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00131857.2020.1716449'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7858462962508468, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SEP entry explains standards for causal explanation (mechanisms, interventionist conditions, invariance, and the need for specified mediating factors). That supports the methodological point that one should not infer a direct causal link from an underspecified variable (like an unspecified notion of “time”) to an outcome without a stated mechanism or interventionist basis. However, the source does not specifically discuss “time” and “education level,” nor does it assert a blanket metaphysical claim that time can never be a direct cause; it only supplies general criteria for when causal claims are justified. Thus the general justification in the explanation is supported, but the categorical claim that “Time does not directly cause education level to change” is stronger than what the citation directly establishes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly develops a conceptual framework and synthesizes causal research showing that increases in time in school (total/instructional time) typically produce positive effects on student achievement; it therefore supports a causal link between time in school and education outcomes rather than the claim that no direct causal relationship exists.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Card chapter does not assert that an undifferentiated variable \\"Time\\" on its own causally changes education; it emphasizes the need to specify mechanisms (cohort effects, institutional changes, compulsory schooling laws, school proximity/quality) to identify causal effects. At the same time, the chapter documents that time-varying factors (cohort events, legal reforms, changes in school quality) do produce changes in educational attainment. Thus the citations support the point that \\"time\\" must be defined (i.e., specific time-varying mechanisms) to claim causation, but they contradict a blanket claim that no causal relationship ever exists — because time-indexed institutional changes are shown to causally affect education."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause education level to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to changes in education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2105266493375992, 'mean_token_prob': 0.860432092729501, 'min_prob': 0.19626538042714398, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022940059549116706, 'prob_variance': 0.03955809252630589, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9703961833650545, 'avg_nll': 0.19105551230242718}

