   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Cognitive disorder, nor does Cognitive disorder cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in spatial scale to the onset or progression of cognitive disorders. Without a defined mechanism and given the definitions provided, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10889220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028935/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spatial_cognition', 'https://www.cell.com/trends/cognitive-sciences/abstract/S1364-6613(24)00252-3', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/spatial-cognition'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10889220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents that spatial cognition is impaired early in Alzheimer’s disease and shows that patients with MCI due to AD perform worse on real-world navigation tasks (more wrong turns, hesitations) and that environmental complexity affects navigation performance. It does not provide evidence that increasing the spatial scale of interaction causally produces cognitive disorders, nor does it present mechanisms of overstimulation or \\"neural fatigue\\" leading to cognitive dysfunction. The findings describe deficits associated with existing disease, not that larger spatial environments cause cognitive decline."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028935/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how neurodegenerative diseases affect visual-spatial cognition (egocentric vs allocentric processing, dorsal/ventral streams, navigation deficits) and how spatial impairments manifest in different disorders. It does not provide evidence that increasing the spatial scale at which individuals interact with the environment causes cognitive disorders, nor does it describe a mechanism of overstimulation → neural fatigue → disease onset. The sources document spatial deficits as consequences or features of pathology, not as environmental drivers causing cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spatial_cognition", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes types of spatial scale (figural → geographical), how spatial cognition and navigation engage brain regions (e.g., hippocampus), and how spatial abilities vary with age, strategy, and brain damage. It does not present evidence that interacting at larger spatial scales causes overstimulation, neural fatigue, or an increased incidence of cognitive disorders. No citation in the article supports the causal chain claimed (larger scale → overwhelm/overstimulation → neural fatigue → cognitive dysfunction)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/spatial-cognition", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and provides no accessible content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that links increased spatial scale of interaction to higher rates of cognitive disorders or describes mechanisms (overstimulation, neural fatigue) supporting the causal claim. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the spatial scale at which individuals interact with their environment directly causes a rise in cognitive disorders, as the brain becomes overwhelmed by the complexity and vastness of information to process. This overstimulation leads to neural fatigue, eventually resulting in cognitive dysfunction.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the spatial scale at which individuals interact with their environment directly causes a rise in cognitive disorders, as the brain becomes overwhelmed by the complexity and vastness of information to process. This overstimulation leads to neural fatigue, eventually resulting in cognitive dysfunction.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

