s that educational technologies and digital literacy increase access to information (e.g., assistive tools, screen readers, integration of digital tech from early education, and the role of teacher training) and highlights the digital divide and skill gaps that limit access. Those points are consistent with the idea that technical skills and education-related training improve ability to find and use information. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that higher overall education level per se causally leads to greater information accessibility or specifically that more-educated individuals universally possess the advanced cognitive/technological skills described. In short: the citation supports parts of the mechanism (tech literacy and training improve access; lack of skills reduces access) but does not directly establish the claimed positive relationship between general education level and accessibility of information. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10209-017-0554-8", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial and summarized papers discuss how digital skills, ICT literacy and the digital divide affect access to knowledge (noting disadvantaged groups, older people, rural areas, and benefits of ICT-based education and skill-improving interventions). That supports the general idea that greater skills and literacy increase ability to access information. However, the excerpt does not provide direct empirical evidence or clear statements that higher formal education level per se causally yields greater information accessibility via the cognitive/technological mechanisms claimed. Thus the sources back the role of skills/digital literacy in accessibility but do not fully substantiate the specific claim about education level and the detailed mechanisms described."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdic