   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information directly reduces healthcare avoidance by making it easier for individuals to understand and utilize healthcare services. Conversely, decreasing accessibility of information increases avoidance because individuals lack the knowledge or confidence to seek care. This relationship is direct, not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.281669144173978, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/', 'https://ahimafoundation.ahima.org/research/the-critical-role-of-web-accessibility-in-health-information-access-understanding-and-use/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7233547360721566, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S030645732030933X", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The single cited source is an error page (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) with no usable content or data. Because no empirical evidence, analysis, or arguments are available in the citation, it cannot support the claimed causal relationship or the specific mechanisms described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7741964/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines information avoidance in decision-making (age differences and reasons for choosing to receive or avoid decision-relevant information) and does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information causally reduces healthcare avoidance, nor that accessible information selectively attracts inherently more health-conscious individuals. It reports findings about predictors of decision-related information avoidance and age patterns, but does not test effects of information accessibility on healthcare-seeking behavior or the proposed selection mechanism or independence from mediators."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because individuals exposed to more information are inherently more health-conscious and therefore less likely to avoid healthcare. The presence of accessible information directly attracts those predisposed to seek care, leading to an overall reduction in healthcare avoidance. In contrast, when accessibility of information is limited, only those already inclined to avoid healthcare remain uninformed, thus increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship operates independently of other mediating factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthcare avoidance to decrease because individuals exposed to more information are inherently more health-conscious and therefore less likely to avoid healthcare. The presence of accessible information directly attracts those predisposed to seek care, leading to an overall reduction in healthcare avoidance. In contrast, when accessibility of information is limited, only those already inclined to avoid healthcare remain uninformed, thus increasing healthcare avoidance. This relationship operates independently of other mediating factors.', 'perplexity': 1.4594841552650162, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7859220659849224, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.058874602062764715, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0043154767179908685, 'prob_variance': 0.07933750677970118, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0881717481796902, 'avg_nll': 0.3780830549769918}

