103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents ED utilization, per-visit costs, and payer mix (Medicare/Medicaid/private/self-pay) and notes ED care generates substantial spending and billing (EMTALA allows billing). However it does not provide evidence that increases in ED visits reliably translate into net additional funding or improved financial resources for hospitals — it discusses high costs of ED care, mixed payer mixes, unreimbursed care, crowding, and that spending growth is driven more by price than utilization. The report does not make the clear causal claim that higher ED demand meaningfully and directly boosts provider financial resources; in fact it highlights circumstances (self-pay, resource-intensive behavioral health cases, admissions vs. treat-and-release) that complicate any simple positive effect. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Affairs study estimates that, in aggregate (2009), hospital revenue from ED care exceeded costs by $6.1 billion (a 7.8% margin) and projects ACA-driven coverage shifts could increase ED profit margins — supporting the idea that ED visits can boost hospital financial resources. However, the paper also shows profitability is uneven: only privately insured visits were strongly profitable, while Medicare, Medicaid, and uninsured visits produced substantial losses. Thus the citation supports a positive effect in aggregate and under certain payer mixes, but not a uniformly positive effect for all ED visits or all providers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between hospital financial performance and quality/safety measures and discusses payer mix and broader financial indicators; it does not provide evidence that increases in acute care demand or ED visits directly generate additional funding or reimbursements that boost hospital financial resources. The paper neither measures ED visit volume as a driver of revenue nor supports the causal claim that more ED visits increase hospitals\' financial resources. Therefore the citation does not support the stated positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes the costs of ED crowding and boarding and emphasizes that extended ED stays and crowding tend to increase hospital costs and can reduce revenue (through lost capacity, ambulance diversion, and lower likelihood of payer reimbursement). It discusses potential revenue gains from relieving crowding (by increasing capacity to see additional patients), but it does not provide evidence that an increase in ED visits due to acute care demand directly and generally increases hospital funding or insurance reimbursements. Overall the source does not support the claimed straightforward positive effect of ED visits on providers\' financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report describes rising utilization (including increased ED visits for conditions like heart failure) as a driver of higher hospital costs and explicitly documents that Medicare, Medicaid and many MA reimbursements do not cover those costs (e.g., Medicare covers ~83 cents per dollar, hospitals absorbed ~$130B in underpayments, MA/observation reimbursements often below cost). It emphasizes that higher ED/inpatient utilization and longer observation stays increase financial strain rather than providing net financial benefit. The citation does not provide evidence that increased ED visits produce net additional funding or positive effects on hospital financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, hospitals receive additional funding and insurance reimbursements, which directly boosts their financial resources. Therefore, an increase in ED visits as a result of acute care demand actually has a positive effect on the financial resources available to healthcare providers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, hospitals receive additional funding and insurance reimbursements, which directly boosts their financial resources. Therefore, an increase in ED visits as a result of acute care demand actually has a positive effect on the financial resources available to healthcare providers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly. Multi-morbidity may increase the risk of acute events, which then lead to ED visits, but this relationship is mediated by the occurrence of acute events and other factors. There is no direct causal link between these two variables in either direction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK441857/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2683/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10560788/', 'https://academic.oup.com/cid/article/65/4/644/3788023', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4865227/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/sites/books/NBK441857/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that healthcare exposures (including frequent visits to healthcare facilities), hospital stays, invasive procedures and devices raise risk of healthcare-associated infections, and that HAIs cause substantial morbidity, complications and prolonged healthcare needs. However, the source focuses on infections acquired during hospitalization (typically manifesting >48 hours after admission) and does not provide direct evidence that individual ED visits (or \\"acute care demand\\" presenting to the ED) routinely cause new chronic conditions or a measurable increase in long‑term multimorbidity. It therefore supports parts of the proposed causal chain (healthcare exposure → HAI → increased morbidity) but not the specific claim that each ED visit directly increases risk of accumulating additional chronic morbidities.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2683/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that health-care encounters (including acute-care settings and the broader continuum of care) can cause health care–associated infections and other adverse events that add substantial morbidity, prolong care, and may require further interventions. It therefore supports the general idea that exposure during care can increase patient morbidity. However, the source does not specifically address emergency department (ED) visits as a distinct cause, does not provide evidence that each ED visit incrementally increases risk of developing additional chronic conditions (multi-morbidity), nor does it quantify per-visit risk or demonstrate a causal chain from repeated ED visits to accumulation of chronic diseases. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10560788/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that hospital care–associated infections (HCAIs/HAIs) are common in hospital settings, are associated with increased morbidity, prolonged stays and worse outcomes, and that exposure to hospital environments and medical interventions (especially in high-risk units) promotes infection and antimicrobial-resistant pathogens. That supports the mechanism that acute care/hospital exposure can increase risk of additional health problems. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence about emergency department (ED) visits specifically, nor does it quantify or demonstrate that each ED visit or frequent acute-care visits causally lead to accumulating chronic conditions or multimorbidity. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4865227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines delays and timeliness of ED care for acute infections and references associations between ED crowding and negative outcomes (e.g., poorer care, adverse events, medication errors). It does not provide evidence that ED visits directly cause hospital-acquired infections or that ED encounters lead to development of new chronic conditions or accumulation of multimorbidity. Therefore the specific causal claim (each ED visit increases risk of accumulating morbidities via HAIs and interventions) is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause multi-morbidity by exposing patients to hospital-acquired infections and medical interventions, which may result in the development of additional chronic conditions. As a result, each ED visit potentially increases the risk of accumulating further morbidities.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause multi-morbidity by exposing patients to hospital-acquired infections and medical interventions, which may result in the development of additional chronic conditions. As a result, each ED visit potentially increases the risk of accumulating further morbidities.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal mechanism by which visiting the emergency department for acute care increases or decreases the likelihood of developing chronic addiction. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors, such as substance use behaviors, not by the ED visit itself.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180458/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6077851/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1431384/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180458/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines predictors of frequent ED use among patients already diagnosed with substance-related disorders and discusses how serious health problems and co-occurring conditions increase acute care use. It does not present evidence that repeated ED visits cause new or worsening chronic addiction, nor does it report that exposure to hospital environments, medications, or ED-related stress directly produces addictive behaviors. In fact, the direction shown is that addiction and comorbidities predict ED use, not that ED use produces addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6077851/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that people with substance use disorders frequently use emergency departments and that the ED is an opportunity for screening, brief intervention, and initiation/linkage to treatment (including ED-initiated buprenorphine for OUD). It reports prevalence, intervention studies, and that ED visits can be a \\"teachable moment.\\" It does not present evidence that repeated ED visits causally produce chronic addiction, nor that exposure to hospital environments/medications or the stress of emergency care causes addiction. The mechanisms and causal claim in the explanation are not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9946266/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents associations and rising rates of substance-related and repeated ED visits among adolescents/young adults, and explicitly notes its cross‑sectional design prevents establishing temporal/causal ordering. It does not present evidence that repeated ED visits cause development of chronic addiction, nor data that hospital exposure, medications administered in ED, or ED stress directly increase dependence. The paper instead discusses factors associated with repeat visits and gaps in follow-up care. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345257/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that substance use disorders are often chronic, involve repeated episodes of relapse and treatment, and that an acute-care treatment model is inadequate for chronic addiction. It does not claim that repeated ED or other acute-care visits cause people to develop chronic addiction, nor does it provide evidence that exposure to hospital environments, medications, or emergency-care stress directly increases dependence. Thus the specific causal mechanism asserted (ED visits → development of chronic addiction via hospital exposure/medications/stress) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2025.1431384/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes socioeconomic correlates of ED outcomes among visits already associated with substance use using cross‑sectional NEDS data; it documents associations (and pandemic-era trends) but explicitly notes it cannot establish causal relationships. It does not provide evidence that repeated ED visits cause new or worsening chronic addiction, nor does it support mechanisms proposed (hospital exposure, medications, or ED stress causing addiction)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand resulting in repeated ED visits can directly cause patients to develop chronic addiction, as exposure to hospital environments and medications increases dependence over time. The stress of emergency care itself also triggers addictive behaviors, making chronic addiction a likely outcome of multiple ED visits.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand resulting in repeated ED visits can directly cause patients to develop chronic addiction, as exposure to hospital environments and medications increases dependence over time. The stress of emergency care itself also triggers addictive behaviors, making chronic addiction a likely outcome of multiple ED visits.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

