   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor is there a clear mechanism by which increased ED visits would directly alter information accessibility. There is no direct causal link from acute care demand to accessibility of information in either direction. Any association is likely mediated by other variables or is merely correlational.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6266999', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12604675/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112847/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6266999", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper develops and applies methods to measure spatial/temporal healthcare accessibility (travel time, elderly demand weighting, hospital crowdedness) and discusses implications for resource planning. It does not present evidence or discussion that rising acute care/ED demand causes hospitals to improve the accessibility of information for patients (e.g., making information more available for pre-arrival navigation). The paper mentions use of real‑time traffic data (AutoNavi) and that people use such data for route planning, but it does not document hospitals’ actions to improve information accessibility in response to increased ED visits or establish a causal link as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12604675/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses access broadly and identifies information flow, digital communication tools, online scheduling, and interoperability as important components for improving access — and it frames system pressures and patient demand as drivers for access-improvement efforts. However, it does not provide direct evidence that increases in acute/ED demand specifically cause hospitals to improve the accessibility of patient-facing information or that hospitals respond to rising ED visits by proactively making information more available prior to arrival. The link in the explanation (more ED visits → hospitals improve information accessibility) is implied by broader discussion of access pressures but is not explicitly demonstrated in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6112847/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AHA article discusses strategies hospitals can use to improve access (telehealth/virtual care, navigation, community engagement, toolkits) and emphasizes connecting with patients in the digital age, which implies steps that can improve information accessibility. However, the paper does not present evidence that increases in acute-care demand or ED visits directly cause hospitals to improve information accessibility, nor does it state that rising ED volume specifically incentivizes making pre-arrival information more available. The cited source supports that hospitals may adopt measures that improve information access, but not the claimed causal link from increased ED visits to those actions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and related statistics but contains no information about hospitals\' responses, policies, or changes in how they make information accessible to patients. It does not present evidence that rising ED demand causes hospitals to improve information accessibility."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits directly causes hospitals to improve the accessibility of information for patients. As more people seek emergency services, healthcare facilities are incentivized to make information more available so that patients can better navigate their care before arrival.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits directly causes hospitals to improve the accessibility of information for patients. As more people seek emergency services, healthcare facilities are incentivized to make information more available so that patients can better navigate their care before arrival.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

