   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AHRQ HCUP Statistical Brief describes frequencies and reasons for ED visits (diagnoses, patient characteristics, rates) and notes that some nonurgent visits reflect limited access to primary care. It does not present evidence that greater diagnostic resources or expanded testing capacity in EDs attract more patients, increase community awareness, or drive higher acute-care demand. No data or analysis in the brief links availability of diagnostic resources to increased ED visit volume."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics on emergency department visit counts and characteristics (rates, admissions, timing, visit subcategories) but contains no information linking availability or expansion of diagnostic resources to changes in ED demand or patient behavior. It does not report causal relationships, measures of diagnostic capacity, nor evidence that greater diagnostic resources attract more patients or increase community awareness. Therefore the source does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents the rapid expansion of diagnostic testing and imaging, regional variation and overuse of diagnostic resources, and how increased availability and choices can change clinical practice and lead to greater utilization of tests. However, it does not provide evidence that adding diagnostic resources in an emergency department directly increases community acute care demand or that community members choose to go to the ED because of awareness of an ED\'s advanced diagnostics. The specific causal claim that greater diagnostic resources in an ED attract more patients (stimulating ED visits) and raise acute care demand is not directly supported by the text; only related concepts (more tests available → more use/overuse; public awareness of symptoms can affect seeking care) are discussed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of advanced diagnostic tools and expanded testing capacity attracts more patients seeking comprehensive evaluations. Consequently, the mere presence of greater diagnostic resources can stimulate community awareness and drive individuals to utilize ED services more frequently, creating a positive association between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of advanced diagnostic tools and expanded testing capacity attracts more patients seeking comprehensive evaluations. Consequently, the mere presence of greater diagnostic resources can stimulate community awareness and drive individuals to utilize ED services more frequently, creating a positive association between the two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible content, so there is nothing in the provided citation to support the claim that increased acute care/ED demand reduces healthcare professionals\' functioning (or that lower demand preserves functioning). Because no evidence or data from the citation is available, the claim cannot be verified from the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts, rates, and characteristics of emergency department visits (e.g., number of visits, percent admitted, visit subcategories) but contains no data or analysis about healthcare professional performance, decision fatigue, cognitive resources, or changes in clinician functioning. Therefore the source does not provide evidence that higher acute care demand/ED visits cause decreased functioning of healthcare professionals (or the inverse)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HWSM \\"Demand Modeling Overview\\" describes how demand for services (including ED visits) is projected and converted into workforce FTE requirements, and it discusses staffing shortfalls and demand drivers. It does not present evidence or analysis linking higher acute/ED demand to decreased cognitive functioning, decision fatigue, or reduced clinical performance among healthcare professionals. Nor does it provide data showing an inverse relationship between ED visit volume and clinician functioning. Therefore the explanation about decision fatigue and preserved cognitive resources is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8833875718632301, 'avg_nll': 0.2547333630504816}

109. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes overcrowding as reducing functional ED/hospital capacity (through boarding, exit block, resource strain) and lists strategies administrators can use to increase or manage capacity (reverse triage, early discharge, smoothing elective admissions, full-capacity protocols, etc.). It does not report a protocol in which administrators deliberately limit available capacity in response to increased ED visits to “maintain staff readiness” or to intentionally reduce capacity when demand rises. The evidence supports that capacity can fall as an adverse consequence of crowding and that hospitals implement measures to free or expand capacity, but it does not support the claim of an intentional inverse causal policy as described.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides counts, rates, and characteristics of emergency department visits but contains no information or evidence that hospitals or administrators intentionally reduce available capacity in response to increased ED visits or that capacity changes inversely for the stated operational reasons. The citation therefore does not support the causal mechanism or inverse relationship described.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because increased ED visits trigger a protocol whereby hospital administrators intentionally limit available Capacity to maintain staff readiness and prevent resource overextension. As a result, higher acute care demand prompts a deliberate reduction in Capacity, while lower demand allows administrators to expand available resources, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because increased ED visits trigger a protocol whereby hospital administrators intentionally limit available Capacity to maintain staff readiness and prevent resource overextension. As a result, higher acute care demand prompts a deliberate reduction in Capacity, while lower demand allows administrators to expand available resources, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

