son": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review and included studies attribute language and cultural barriers to factors like global migration, patient language preference, interpreter access and provider/policy factors — not to volumes of acute-care demand or ED visits. The review explicitly discusses how language barriers affect healthcare access, communication, and outcomes, and describes barriers within care settings (including EDs) without asserting that increased acute-care demand causes those barriers. Several cited studies note that language barriers can influence utilization or access (supporting the opposite direction), but none provide evidence that acute-care visit volume creates language or cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains origins of language and cultural barriers (migration, demographics, underrepresentation of bilingual staff, aging) and describes how these barriers affect care and drive need for interpreter services. It provides no evidence that acute care demand or ED visit volume creates or changes language/cultural barriers; rather the causality described runs from language barriers → effects on care (e.g., lengths of stay, readmissions, ED interpreter needs). Therefore the sources support the claim of no causal effect from acute care demand → language/cultural barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause language and cultural barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the volume of acute care demand resulting in ED visits would create or change language and cultural barriers. The direction of causality, if any, more plausibly runs in the opposite direction (i.e., language and cultural barriers affecting demand), not vice versa.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.4835810681840558, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7989911909201195, 'min_prob': 0.026276931994923806, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003854436631115464, 'prob_variance': 0.09038107506279724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.042109604071292, 'avg_nll': 0.39445880582395854}

123. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26281593175921625, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7408414124705698, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that mental health problems have increased and drive greater ED utilization and describes staff perceptions of patients with mental disorders seeking ED care. That supports the idea that mental disorders can increase acute care demand (reverse causality). However, the paper does not investigate or provide evidence that ED visits do not cause new or worsened mental disorders, nor does it test or rule out causal mechanisms linking ED demand to onset or deterioration of mental illness. Therefore the citation only partially supports the explanation (it supports the reverse-direction possibility but not the stronger claim that there is no causal effect)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that people present to EDs because of existing mental health crises and describes negative impacts of ED experiences (e.g., re-traumatisation, exacerbation of conditions, lasting psychological consequences). It does not provide evidence that ED visits cannot cause mental disorders; rather, it reports harms that could worsen mental health. The citation therefore does not support the assertion that there is no causal mechanism and that any association is only due to reverse causality or mediation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10786006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents social determinants that can cause or confound associations with mental disorder, emphasizes bi‑directional relationships (e.g., mental disorder can drive socioeconomic disadvantage and service use), and calls for stronger causal inference to avoid misattributing causality. This supports the view that observed links between acute care/ED use and mental disorder could reflect reverse causality, confounding, or mediating social factors rather than a direct causal effect of ED visits. However, the paper does not specifically evaluate whether acute care demand or emergency department visits themselves directly cause mental disorders or provide evidence conclusively ruling out any causal mechanism from ED visits to mental disorder. Therefore the claim that there is \\"no\\" causal relationship is not fully substantiated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9077627/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that people with mental health problems commonly present to EDs (supporting reverse causality: mental disorder → ED visit) and notes limited evidence that acute services produce sustained improvement. However, it also describes potential iatrogenic harms of acute settings (EDs and inpatient wards)—eg, trauma, exacerbation of symptoms, coercion—which indicates plausible pathways by which acute care experiences could worsen mental health. The source therefore supports that observed associations are often driven by mental disorder increasing ED use, but does not justify the absolute claim that ED visits cannot cause or worsen mental disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that emergency departments are commonly used by people with mental disorders, that inadequate community services and barriers in care drive greater ED use (supporting reverse causality/confounding), and that ED environments and practices can worsen distress or outcomes. It does not provide evidence that ED visits for acute (non-psychiatric) care cause new mental disorders, nor does it present direct causal-mechanism data proving absence of such an effect. Thus the sources support the idea that associations are plausibly explained by reverse causality and mediators, but do not definitively demonstrate that ED visits cannot cause mental disorder."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of ED visits for acute care would lead to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables or reverse causality, where mental disorder may increase acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3503287365729486, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8152256127250049, 'min_prob': 0.0781241632706368, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002812912736071915, 'prob_variance': 0.06907221398646501, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.865701775806758, 'avg_nll': 0.30034807137949227}

124. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, visit outcomes) but contains no information on the existence, accessibility, or causal determinants of alternatives to ED care or on causal direction between ED demand and alternative availability. It therefore does not support the claim that acute care demand does not causally determine availability of alternatives (nor does it provide evidence for the opposite direction)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review examines factors driving ED use and repeatedly treats availability/access to alternative sites (primary care, urgent care, retail clinics) as influencing patients\' decisions to visit the ED. It discusses convenience, access, and perceptions of alternatives as drivers of ED demand and explicitly frames supply/availability as affecting thresholds for ED use. The review does not present evidence that numbers of acute-care ED visits causally determine the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. However, the source does not directly test or prove the absence of any causal pathway from ED demand to availability of alternatives — it mainly documents the reverse direction and notes system-level interactions. Therefore the citation supports the claim\'s assertion that causality typically runs the other way, but does not definitively demonstrate that no causal effect exists in the stated direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents many examples and analyses where availability of non-ED alternatives (expanded clinic capacity, managed care, copayments, EMS diversion, etc.) affects ED utilization, i.e., causality running from availability → ED demand. That supports the explanation\'s claim that the typical causal direction is the opposite. However, the review does not provide direct evidence ruling out any causal pathway from increased acute-care demand (more ED visits) to subsequent changes in the availability or accessibility of alternatives. It does not explicitly demonstrate that ED demand cannot lead to increased or decreased availability of alternatives (for example, policy or market responses to rising ED demand). Therefore the citation supports the inference about the usual direction of causality but does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that there is no causal effect from demand → availability."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

125. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article frames organizational structure as a deliberate design choice (integration/differentiation, authority distribution, coordination mechanisms) used to manage uncertainty and patient flow; it describes managerial strategies (pooling, lateral relations, vertical information systems, slack) to respond to demand. That supports the idea that structural arrangements are determined by design/leadership decisions rather than being instantaneously altered by a single ED visit. However, the paper also describes how environmental uncertainty and patient demand motivate changes in design and coordination over time. It does not provide direct evidence that an acute care demand or individual ED visit cannot, through accumulated pressure or managerial response, lead to structural change. Therefore the absolute claim (\\"does not cause organizational structure to change\\") is stronger than what the source demonstrates."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes how healthcare organizational structure is determined by mission, leadership decisions, design choices, and broader factors (pace of change, external environment, complexity), and it emphasizes planning and deliberate design rather than ad hoc changes after individual care events. It does not provide any evidence that a single acute care demand or ED visit would directly or immediately alter organizational structure, but it does note that environmental dynamics and changing needs can shape organizational design over time. Because the source supports the idea that structure is leadership-driven and not changed by single visits, yet also implies that broader demand trends or environment can influence design, the citation partially supports the claim that an individual ED visit does not cause an organizational-structure change."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause organizational structure to change. Organizational structure refers to the formal arrangement of roles, responsibilities, and processes within an organization, which is typically determined by leadership decisions rather than patient demand. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in acute care demand leading to an ED visit would immediately alter organizational structure.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331570359411918, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339821231048525, 'min_prob': 0.03191688392872667, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003309274672821155, 'prob_variance': 0.06151411350348714, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9630886626364701, 'avg_nll': 0.2863589670927359}

