129. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2741122588081769, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMoa1301372', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7781007704877342, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes many acute medical causes of cognitive deficits (e.g., infections, metabolic disorders, stroke, head injury) and notes delirium can develop rapidly in the context of underlying medical/surgical illness and during hospital stays; it also states cognitive impairment can present as an emergency and increase risks (falls, complications). Thus the source indicates acute medical events and hospital encounters can be directly related to cognitive disorders (particularly delirium) and that cognitive deficits can precipitate emergency presentations. The citation does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal mechanism between acute care demand/ED visits and cognitive disorder, nor does it provide evidence that any association would instead be mediated specifically by frailty, mental disorder, or health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that mild cognitive impairment predicted higher rates of ED visits (with ED visit rates varying by CI severity), i.e., an association between cognitive disorder and ED use. It is an observational analysis and does not prove causation, but it does contradict the claim that there is no relationship; the paper explicitly identifies cognitive impairment as a predictor of ED visits. The citation does not support the assertion that there is no direct relationship in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes what mild cognitive impairment is and lists risk factors (age, genetics, brain injuries, chronic conditions, infections, medications, mental health conditions, etc.), but it does not discuss emergency department visits, acute care demand, or any causal (or non-causal) relationship between ED visits and cognitive disorders. The source neither supports the claim that there is no direct causal link nor the specific mediation pathway (frailty, health literacy) proposed. Absence of mention of ED visits in the citation does not constitute evidence for the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic page describes features, causes, and risk factors for mild cognitive impairment (age, genetics, medical and lifestyle factors) but says nothing about emergency department visits or acute-care demand. It does not provide evidence that ED visits do or do not cause cognitive disorder, nor that cognitive disorder does not lead to ED visits. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted absence of a direct causal relationship (or the proposed mediators)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one variable directly leads to changes in the other. Any association would likely be mediated by other variables such as frailty, mental disorder, or health literacy.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3338298774092578, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8332792676439396, 'min_prob': 0.08169424588160952, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00514374589482499, 'prob_variance': 0.07513753042892095, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0780747845098086, 'avg_nll': 0.28805441118205516}

130. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24102883164527533, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0127793', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8147523406756995, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MMWR shows substantial avoidance of urgent/emergency care (12% reporting they avoided such care) and links this avoidance to observed declines in ED visits and hospital admissions during the pandemic. While the report discusses potential mediators (fear of SARS‑CoV‑2 exposure, facility closures, telehealth availability) and notes it did not fully assess reasons, it directly documents an association in which care avoidance affected ED utilization. Therefore the source does not support the assertion that there is no direct relationship between ED demand/visits and care avoidance; it provides evidence of a relationship (avoidance reducing ED visits) and of plausible mediators, not definitive proof of no direct link.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0127793", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE study analyzes pre-ED care-seeking patterns and shows complex, heterogeneous associations between prior care use and ED visit severity/outcomes. It emphasizes that observed associations do not establish causality and discusses mediating factors like access and continuity of care. However, the paper does not analyze or define \\"care avoidance behavior,\\" nor does it provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between acute-care demand/ED visits and care avoidance; it only shows complexity and warns against inferring causation. Thus the citation supports cautious interpretation of causal claims but does not directly support the stronger claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents many proximal reasons why people avoid medical care (fear, embarrassment, mistrust, cost, time, perceived lack of need, access barriers) and proposes a conceptual model in which perceptions of necessity, availability, and desirability mediate avoidance. That supports the explanation\'s claim that avoidance is driven by mediating factors rather than a single direct cause. However, the paper does not address \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" specifically, nor does it provide direct evidence that no direct causal link can exist between ED visits (or acute care demand) and care-avoidance behavior. It therefore supports the mediation argument in general but does not directly substantiate the specific assertion about ED/acute-care demand having no direct causal relationship with avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module frames ED visits as outcomes of access problems, gaps in chronic care, and lack of patient knowledge (e.g., not knowing after‑hours options), and recommends addressing access, education, and social determinants to reduce unnecessary ED use. It does not assert a direct causal effect of “acute care demand leading to an ED visit” on “care avoidance behavior,” so the main claim of no direct causal link is consistent with the citation. However, the explanation names specific mediators (feelings of fear and shame, health literacy, accessibility of information): the citation supports accessibility/information and social determinants/healthcare access but does not mention fear or shame explicitly. Therefore the support is partial."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper identifies emotional, cognitive, behavioral, and access-related factors (fear, distrust, low self-efficacy, lack of usual care, financial/time barriers, etc.) as predictors and reasons for avoiding needed medical care, supporting the idea that avoidance is mediated by such factors. However, the study does not examine or rule out a direct causal relationship between \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" and \\"care avoidance behavior\\" specifically, nor does it analyze ED visits as an exposure/outcome. Therefore the citation supports the mediator aspect but does not directly support the stronger claim that no direct causal link exists between acute care demand/ED visits and avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' causes 'Care avoidance behavior' or vice versa. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as feelings of fear and shame, health literacy, or accessibility of information. Therefore, they should not be directly linked in a causal loop diagram.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2455279498641398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.861149205543718, 'min_prob': 0.1398240058134697, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023540261956011208, 'prob_variance': 0.05809489768428648, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8783027928365675, 'avg_nll': 0.2195594961460616}

131. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2905056636828834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7059759744965742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly states that episodes of care (including acute care and repeated ED visits) can be emotionally threatening and psychologically traumatizing for pediatric patients and that children commonly report feeling afraid or anxious during healthcare encounters; it gives examples and mechanisms linking medical care to fear/anxiety and discusses shame-inducing topics. Therefore the sources contradict the claim that there is no direct causal link and instead support a direct relationship between acute care/ED visits and feelings of fear (and at least some shame-related responses). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited narrative review documents that people presenting to EDs often report negative emotional impacts — including shame, guilt and fear — but attributes these reactions to specific aspects of the ED experience (judgemental attitudes, long waits, lack of privacy, coercion/restrictive practices, arrival by police/ambulance), i.e. mediating treatment and contextual factors. It therefore supports the view that feelings of fear and shame are linked to how people are treated in EDs rather than simply to the fact of acute care demand per se. However, the review is qualitative and does not provide definitive evidence to rule out any direct effect of presenting to EDs (it does not test or prove absence of a direct causal pathway)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that healthcare encounters and interactions with care professionals can evoke or compound shame (and related fear/anxiety) — describing mechanisms such as power differentials, exposure of vulnerabilities, and shame-anxiety that directly link seeking care to shame and avoidance behaviors (e.g., missed appointments). It therefore supports a direct pathway from health-care encounters to feelings of shame/fear rather than asserting no direct causal mechanism. It does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists without mediators."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables without considering mediating factors such as prior experiences, health literacy, or cultural context. Any observed association is likely mediated or coincidental rather than causal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4482664484834167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.781716027065396, 'min_prob': 0.05984694232772836, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035268306270290744, 'prob_variance': 0.08439354063183256, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0741392422368186, 'avg_nll': 0.3703672884190816}

132. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NONE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2989053106255356, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7355733306990835, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and related utilization statistics but contains no information about staffing, personnel levels, or the determinants of staffing (policy, budgeting, organizational structure, mediation by capacity). Therefore it does not support the claim that acute care demand does not directly cause Personnel to change or that the relationship is mediated by other variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2676/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes that staffing levels are determined by administrators, budgets, labor markets, and organizational decisions, and that staffing is managed at unit/shift levels—implying that changes in demand influence staffing via managerial processes and policies rather than as an automatic, direct effect. It also discusses strategies to manage supply and demand (smoothing patient flow) and notes that patient needs and work environment mediate staffing needs. However, the source also documents that staffing is managed day-by-day and shift-by-shift and that patient flow can drive operational staffing adjustments, so it does not fully support a categorical “no direct effect” claim. Overall, the citation supports the explanation that demand influences personnel mostly through mediated administrative processes, but not an absolute absence of any direct operational response to demand."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7754324/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study describes staffing as the result of managerial decisions, financial constraints and organizational arrangements (e.g., \\"nurse staffing decisions are mostly driven by financial burden\\" and staffing planning/assignment require managerial decisions). It also lists \\"urgent patient situations\\" and \\"unexpected rise in patient acuity or volume\\" as causes of missed care (i.e., immediate demand creates pressure) rather than immediate changes in staffing levels, and the authors explicitly note the observational design cannot establish causality. These points support the explanation that acute demand (e.g., ED visits) does not directly and immediately change Personnel levels and that any staffing change is mediated by policies, capacity and organizational planning."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7086229/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes how staffing is set through longer-term establishment decisions (staffing policies, budgeting, professional judgement, benchmarking—e.g. Telford method) and distinguishes establishment-setting from shift-level deployment. It emphasizes that most tools match staffing to average demand and that staffing changes are often mediated by formal processes (implementation, leadership, rostering, redeployment). This supports the claim that an individual acute-care/ED presentation does not automatically change employed personnel levels and that effects are mediated. However, the review also describes use of tools to predict or respond to immediate demand (guide deployment/redeployment, real‑time staffing decisions) and shows that short‑term demand (turnover, high workload) can influence outcomes and trigger staffing actions. Thus the citation supports an indirect/mediated relationship but does not support a categorical “no effect” — immediate demand can affect personnel deployment even if establishment changes are policy-driven."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38447488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how nurse staffing levels affect patient outcomes in EDs (e.g., wait times, leaving without being seen, delays to treatment). It provides evidence that staffing influences care quality but does not provide evidence that acute care demand leading to ED visits does not directly change personnel levels or that staffing is determined solely by policies/organization. The citation does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship from acute care demand to Personnel (or that any effect is only mediated by other variables)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause Personnel to change. Personnel levels are typically determined by staffing policies, budgeting, or organizational planning, not directly by the immediate demand for acute care in the ED. While rising demand may eventually influence staffing decisions, this relationship is mediated by other variables such as Capacity and Organizational structure, already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5710869995689796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7730376147327174, 'min_prob': 0.013526502083544884, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030576338898829964, 'prob_variance': 0.08934438472014793, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9869290754818982, 'avg_nll': 0.45176773620544924}

