   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2715008437211074, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0002962915363540', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7519062564767705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0002962915363540", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (an error message) and contains no content supporting the claim. Because there is no usable evidence in the provided citation, the explanation is not supported by the cited material.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that financial barriers are associated with decreased access to outpatient care and worse outcomes, supporting the plausibility that financial barriers influence care-seeking rather than the reverse. However, it does not analyze or provide evidence about whether acute care demand or ED visits cause changes in financial barriers (it neither measures ED-driven changes in financial status nor tests that causal direction). Therefore the citation supports the implied directionality (financial barriers → care use) but does not directly demonstrate that acute care demand/ED visits do not cause financial barriers to change."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents both directions. It notes that financial barriers can increase ED visits, but it also describes how acute/hospital care (including out-of-network emergency services, surprise bills, balance billing, and hospital/provider cost-sharing following ED or inpatient care) generates outstanding medical bills and other financial barriers. Thus the source does not support the claim that an ED visit cannot cause changes in financial barriers — the paper gives multiple examples and case data of acute care encounters producing financial burdens."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the act of needing acute care and visiting the ED would itself alter the existence or severity of financial barriers. The direction of causality, if any, would more plausibly run from financial barriers to acute care demand, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.365606631801462, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8147015399168133, 'min_prob': 0.061056031492863885, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002947678390197979, 'prob_variance': 0.07371270814127318, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1056427857737547, 'avg_nll': 0.3115987488142617}

