   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2498741128459255, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693976365477758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study examines how caregivers\' health literacy influences decisions to seek acute care and non-urgent ED use (health literacy → ED use). It proposes health-literacy–focused interventions to reduce ED use and does not present evidence that frequency of ED visits changes an individual\'s baseline health literacy. (The paper briefly notes health literacy can situationally vary with stress, but gives no support for ED visits causing persistent changes in health literacy.) Thus the citation is consistent with \\"no causal effect from acute care demand/ED visits → health literacy.\\" \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6535998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study examines health literacy as a predictor of potentially preventable ED visits (health literacy → ED use) and finds limited literacy is associated with more preventable ED visits. It does not provide evidence that ED visits (or acute care demand) cause changes in an individual’s health literacy, nor does it test reverse causation. The paper also notes limits on making causal inferences, but it does not directly demonstrate that ED use does not alter literacy. Thus the citation is consistent with the claim that the reverse causal link is unsupported by evidence here, but it does not directly establish the absence of any causal effect from ED visits to health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that low health literacy is associated with higher ED utilization and return visits (i.e., health literacy predicting ED use), and it offers no evidence that ED visits cause changes in an individual\'s health literacy. It therefore supports the idea that causality, if any, runs from low health literacy → more ED use rather than the reverse, but it does not directly demonstrate the absence of any possible causal effect of ED visits on health literacy.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9850789/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly describes health literacy as dynamic and influenced by situational factors (eg, stress, sleep deprivation during hospitalization) that can impair a person’s ability to process health information. It also documents that limited health literacy is associated with increased ED visits and hospitalizations and describes interventions in clinical settings that change understanding and adherence. Thus the source supports both (1) health literacy affecting ED use and (2) hospital/ED experiences and interventions affecting a person’s (state) health literacy — contradicting the claim that ED visits do not cause any change in health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the frequency of ED visits would directly alter an individual's health literacy. Causality may exist in the opposite direction, but not as specified here.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Parti