onceptually distinct and may co-occur. However, the paper does not address causal relationships (it does not provide evidence that acute events cannot directly cause multimorbidity, nor that multimorbidity cannot precipitate acute events). It even notes some definitions include acute conditions. Therefore the source supports the distinction but does not support the stronger claim of no direct causal relationship in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines multimorbidity as coexisting chronic (long-term) conditions and describes risk factors, associations, and bidirectional interactions with mental/functional health. It does not state that acute events cannot cause multimorbidity; in fact it notes examples (e.g., COVID-19) associated with increased risk of multimorbidity and describes how conditions can aggravate one another. The source therefore does not support the categorical claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source clearly defines multimorbidity as the presence of two or more chronic conditions (lasting >1 year) and distinguishes this concept from the idea of a single primary diagnosis or transient problems. It notes that conditions can co-occur because of shared causes or risk factors and that comorbidities affect outcomes. However, the citation does not discuss acute events or explicitly state that acute events cannot cause multimorbidity (or vice versa), nor does it assert an absence of any direct causal relationships. Therefore the source supports the definition distinction but does not fully support the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Preventing Chronic Disease collection clearly addresses multimorbidity as the coexistence of multiple chronic conditions, supporting the explanation\'s definitional distinction between chronic multimorbidity and short-term acute events. However, the provided contents do not present evidence or explicit statements demonstrating that acute events cannot directly cause multimorbidity (or vice versa). They document associations and comorbidity patterns but do not rule in or out causal links between acute events and multimorbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute event does not directly cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity directly cause an acute event. Multi-morbidity refers to the coexistence of multiple chronic diseases, while an acute event is a sudden, short-term health incident. While they may co-occur or be associated, there is no direct causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3603667851876775, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8290995506821753, 'min_prob': 0.04703816084148855, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034009658451524117, 'prob_variance': 0.07224794820980739, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0571239957229968, 'avg_nll': 0.30775435837774345}

171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines whether ED patients considered alternatives (PCP, UCC, telehealth) and finds that perceived severity and clinician recommendations drove ED use; it does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives increases ED visits. If anything, the paper suggests alternatives might alleviate ED load and reports low telehealth consideration (3.1%) and only 14.1% avoidable visits. The article does not support the proposed mechanism (alternatives raising awareness and thereby increasing ED demand)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review maps non-ED interventions intended to reduce ED utilization (e.g., expanded primary care, urgent care, triage, care coordination). It summarizes intervention types and notes that better primary care access is associated with fewer low‑acuity ED patients. The review did not present evidence that increasing the availability of alternatives increases ED visits (and in fact cites literature examining reductions in ED utilization). Because the provided source does not support the mechanism or direction claimed (proliferation of alternatives raising ED demand), the claim is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report and cited studies discuss the growth of urgent care and retail clinics and evaluate whether they substitute for ED care. They state some evidence of substitution in particular populations and note that proliferation of alternatives has not produced a clear reduction in the national ED visit rate over 2009–2018. They also note alternatives may serve people who otherwise would not seek care. Nowhere do the citations present evidence that increasing availability of alternatives causes an increase in ED demand (or that proliferation raises awareness and thus leads to more ED visits). Some cited analyses even show substitution (fewer ED visits) for certain groups. The report therefore does not support the specific positive causal claim or the proposed awareness-driven mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) encourages more people to seek care, inadvertently raising awareness of acute care options and leading to a higher demand for ED visits. As alternatives proliferate, patients may be more likely to interpret their symptoms as requiring urgent attention, thus increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) encourages more people to seek care, inadvertently raising awareness of acute care options and leading to a higher demand for ED visits. As alternatives proliferate, patients may be more likely to interpret their symptoms as requiring urgent attention, thus increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

