   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Availability of alternatives for ED does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of ED alternatives would directly influence a patient's adherence to therapy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables, such as care accessibility or health literacy.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.houstonmethodist.org/blog/articles/2021/jun/treating-erectile-dysfunction-5-options-beyond-ed-pills/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1476026/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/', 'https://www.urologyhealth.org/urology-a-z/e/erectile-dysfunction-(ed)'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1476026/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that many nonpharmacologic ED options exist and reports acceptability/satisfaction rates for specific treatments (e.g., vacuum devices, prostheses) and that some options may be rejected or less attractive. It does not provide evidence that the mere availability of multiple alternatives causes patient \\"overwhelm,\\" confusion, indecision, or decreased compliance with a prescribed therapy (no data or discussion linking number of options to delayed/skipped treatment or lower adherence). Therefore the provided source does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review documents that many ED treatments exist and that patients need support to \\"navigate the multitude of treatment options,\\" and it cites barriers to use for specific therapies (injection anxiety, inconvenience/pain with devices, stigma) and notes that