   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.16574631468274265, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://educationnorthwest.org/sites/default/files/resources/culturally-responsive-teaching.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8653026709518991, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural barriers influence whether immigrant patients use available services (it gives examples of free clinics being present but underutilized because of cultural fears and language issues) and notes scarcity of medical interpreters can impede access. It does not provide evidence that the mere availability of alternatives to the ED causes language or cultural barriers to change, nor that cultural/language barriers directly change the physical availability of alternatives. Thus the claim that there is no causal effect from availability → cultural barriers is supported, but the citation does show a relationship in the other (practical) direction (language/cultural barriers affect use/access to alternatives), so asserting no causal linkage “in either direction” is overstated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents strong links between language/cultural barriers and families\' ability to access or navigate educational services (e.g., immigrant parents struggling to enroll children, need for cultural brokers/mediators). However, it does not present evidence that the mere availability of alternative educational