ications."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/mens-health/which-drug-for-erectile-dysfunction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Harvard Health article discusses both the availability/choice and cost limits of ED drugs and separately describes common incorrect uses (e.g., expecting an “on/off” effect, not pairing with sexual stimulation). It does not provide evidence that availability of alternative ED drugs causes incorrect use, nor that incorrect use affects availability. Because the citation neither supports nor documents a causal absence — and even mentions cost-driven behaviors (like pill‑splitting) that could plausibly connect availability/cost to misuse — it does not substantiate the claim that there is no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED does not cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication cause availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Any association would likely be explained by other mediating variables in the system.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1946409565834002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8809877773491398, 'min_prob': 0.171292836420859, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0068385579800120735, 'prob_variance': 0.045679644245154166, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9064451120477515, 'avg_nll': 0.1778456854959804}

