lated factors and incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532862/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article defines capacity as a situational, functional determination for medical decision-making and discusses causes of impaired capacity (e.g., delirium, intoxication, dementia) and how capacity affects consent for treatments. It does not describe or provide evidence that a patient\'s capacity directly causes incorrect medication use, nor that incorrect medication use directly changes capacity. Because the cited source neither asserts nor documents a direct causal link between \\"Capacity\\" and \\"Incorrect use of medication,\\" the claim of no direct relationship is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple patient-level factors that increase the risk of medication errors — e.g., low health literacy, inability to self-administer (young children), frailty/age-related cognitive and functional changes, and other human factors — and describes mechanisms (communication gaps, failure to understand instructions, impaired administration) by which these factors lead to incorrect medication use. It does not state that “capacity” has no causal connection; rather it provides evidence that impairments in patient ability/understanding (concepts closely related to capacity) are linked to increased incorrect medication use. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors (knowledge-based, rule-based, slips, memory lapses) and de