239. Care avoidance behavior-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where care avoidance behavior causes need for autonomy or vice versa. While these variables might be associated, the need for autonomy is typically an underlying personal trait or value that could influence care avoidance, not the other way around. No direct mechanistic pathway exists from care avoidance behavior to need for autonomy. Therefore, no causal link should be represented in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.thegoodcaregroup.com/care-advice/promoting-independence-and-autonomy-through-live-in-care/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8264621/', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8264621/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies how parents’ autonomy-supportive versus controlling behaviors predict preschool children’s autonomous behavior (and references Self-Determination Theory). It does not examine “care avoidance behavior” (e.g., avoiding professional help) nor provide evidence that avoiding care increases an individual’s need for autonomy. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/community-health/patient-care/self-determination-theory", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SDT source defines autonomy as a basic psychological need and explains how social environments can support or thwart that need, and how autonomy-supportive care improves autonomous motivation. It does not present evidence or claims that avoiding care increases a person\'s need for autonomy or that frequent care avoidance strengthens a general desire for independence. No causal link from care avoidance to increased need for autonomy is provided in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Engaging in care avoidance behavior directly increases a person's need for autonomy, as frequently avoiding care is thought to strengthen one's desire for independence and self-reliance. The more someone avoids professional help, the more they supposedly crave autonomy in all aspects of their life.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Engaging in care avoidance behavior directly increases a person's need for autonomy, as frequently avoiding care is thought to strengthen one's desire for independence and self-reliance. The more someone avoids professional help, the more they supposedly crave autonomy in all aspects of their life.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

