   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes chronic addiction to increase in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking care, underlying addictive behaviors may go unaddressed and worsen over time, leading to chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in care avoidance behavior enables timely intervention, reducing the risk of addiction becoming chronic.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2588282330912094, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3164585/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7614727117482247, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article establishes that addiction is a chronic condition and that supportive friends, family and healthcare providers and participation in treatment/support groups play an essential role in managing and recovering from addiction. It also recommends seeking help early and lists social/environmental factors (e.g., peer/household substance use, adverse childhood experiences) as risks. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (avoiding healthcare and supports) causally fosters the development of chronic addiction via social isolation, nor that simply increasing social contact alone automatically reduces addiction risk. The explanation therefore overstates causal claims not made in the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-