241. Care avoidance behavior-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. When care avoidance behavior increases, individuals are less likely to follow prescribed therapies, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, a decrease in care avoidance behavior leads to higher therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, as avoiding care typically results in missed or incomplete therapy adherence.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4039298', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4039298", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that psychological distress is associated with avoidance of medical care and lists psychosocial barriers (fear, discomfort, stigma) that reduce use of services. It does not provide evidence that people who avoid care become more proactive in seeking alternative therapies or show greater therapy adherence; it rather implies avoidance impedes appropriate care. Therefore the source does not support the claimed positive effect of care avoidance on therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents reasons for avoiding care (including preference for self-care or alternative care, distrust of doctors, fear, low health self-efficacy) but does not provide evidence that people who avoid care become more proactive in seeking alternative therapies or that avoidance leads to greater adherence to those therapies. The findings more strongly indicate characteristics associated with avoidance (e.g., low self-efficacy, negative affect) that would not support a claim of increased therapy compliance. Therefore the citation does not support the positive relationship asserted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can lead to higher therapy compliance because individuals who avoid care are often more proactive in seeking out alternative therapies that better fit their preferences. By taking control of their treatment choices, they may become more committed and consistent in following through with therapy regimens.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can lead to higher therapy compliance because individuals who avoid care are often more proactive in seeking out alternative therapies that better fit their preferences. By taking control of their treatment choices, they may become more committed and consistent in following through with therapy regimens.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

