   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior does not cause education level to change, nor does education level cause care avoidance behavior to change in a direct causal manner. There is no clear mechanism by which one directly determines the other without mediation by other variables such as health literacy or knowledge. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study catalogs many reasons people avoid care (including some preference for self-care and distrust of doctors) but does not show that higher education causes increased care avoidance for those reasons. The article only notes that respondents who provided qualitative reasons were more likely to have higher education (a sample characteristic), and it reports small counts for “try to take care of themselves” or being a health professional—none of which support the causal claim that greater education leads people to distrust clinicians and rely solely on self-diagnosis, thereby increasing avoidance. No evidence of a negative causal relationship between education level and care-seeking as described is presented."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper does not support the claimed relationship. Its bivariate results show care avoiders were more likely to have lower (not higher) education, and in the adjusted models education was not a significant predictor of avoidance. The study examines psychological distress as a predictor and does not provide evidence that higher education causes people to self-diagnose, distrust clinicians, or increase care avoidance. No citation evidence is given for the causal mechanism (self-diagnosis/distrust) described in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study reports that skepticism about medical care is linked to lower use and explicitly notes that \\"less educated patients are less likely to seek preventive care.\\" It emphasizes barriers like costs, time, and poor clinician relationships rather than a pattern of higher-educated people distrusting clinicians and self-diagnosing. The article provides no evidence that increasing education causes greater care avoidance via self-diagnosis or distrust. Thus the claim is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education level directly causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe they can self-diagnose and treat their own health issues. As individuals become more educated, they tend to distrust medical professionals and rely solely on their own knowledge, leading to increased care avoidance behavior.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education level directly causes people to avoid seeking care because they believe they can self-diagnose and treat their own health issues. As individuals become more educated, they tend to distrust medical professionals and rely solely on their own knowledge, leading to increased care avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

