ists acute complications (overdose, seizures, respiratory depression, heart attack, sudden death, etc.) as possible short-term or severe outcomes of drug use and addiction. Thus the sources do provide information linking (chronic) addiction to acute events, so the claim that \\"no information provided\\" and therefore no causal relationship can be established is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module explicitly describes chronic substance use (substance use disorder) and links it to acute events: substance intoxication and substance withdrawal are defined as acute reactions following ingestion or cessation; the text cites acute consequences such as seizures, stroke, death during severe withdrawal, increased ER visits for intoxication, and accidental overdose from opioids. Thus the sources do provide information connecting chronic addiction/substance abuse to acute events, so the claim that there is \\"no information\\" and therefore no causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided for either 'Chronic addiction' or 'Acute event,' so a causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear definitions and mechanisms, it is not possible to determine if 'Chronic addiction' causes 'Acute event' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.209614648637554, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8827153514439183, 'min_prob': 0.1107111262230068, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0017803268456991616, 'prob_variance': 0.04911972580962401, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.774603261835372, 'avg_nll': 0.19030183668524037}

