   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.20809091158611107, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK232972/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.780905061221979, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Surgeon General chapter documents neurobiological changes from chronic substance use that impair brain systems for executive function, learning, memory, and decision‑making (prefrontal cortex, basal ganglia, etc.). Those mechanisms imply plausible direct effects of chronic addiction on cognitive processes that underlie knowledge acquisition and use. The source does not state that “knowledge” causally affects addiction, nor does it claim there is no direct causal link; in fact it describes direct brain-level pathways by which addiction alters cognitive function. Therefore the source does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between chronic addiction and knowledge.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK232972/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents that drug abuse arises from complex, interacting biological, psychosocial and contextual factors and highlights academic achievement/education as a relevant protective/risk factor — which is consistent with the explanation\'s suggestion that associations could be mediated by education or literacy. However, the source does not state that there is no possible direct causal mechanism between chronic addiction and \\"knowledge,\\" nor does it rule out direct causal effects in either direction. It emphasizes uncertainty and the need for more research rather than asserting the absence of a direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents extensive evidence that chronic substance use alters brain regions and processes supporting learning, memory, and other cognitive functions, and that long-term/withdrawal effects produce lasting cognitive deficits. Those findings indicate a plausible causal effect of chronic addiction on cognitive capacities that underlie \\"knowledge\\" (learning, retention, recall). While the paper does not emphasize knowledge causing addiction, the claim of no direct causal relationship is contradicted by the citation’s evidence that addiction produces cognitive/learning impairments.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Social Learning and Addiction) describes reciprocal determinism and explicitly treats personal/cognitive factors (knowledge, mindset, appraisal) and drug-use behavior as mutually causal: personal factors can influence drug use and drug use can alter cognitive processes. It therefore contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism between chronic addiction and knowledge or that no direct causal relationship exists. The paper does not support the assertion that any association is only due to mediators like education/health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic brain disorder and lists its causes and broad impacts on health, relationships, work and school responsibilities, but it does not discuss \\"knowledge\\" as a variable, nor does it provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between addiction and knowledge (or that any association must be mediated by education or health literacy). The citation therefore does not support the specific claim that no direct causal relationship exists in either direction.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge directly cause chronic addiction to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be explained by mediating variables such as education level or health literacy. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between chronic addiction and knowledge.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2178961598738753, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8599073621197353, 'min_prob': 0.16877983886706519, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0004295381209797515, 'prob_variance': 0.043301827484738695, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.835588183517534, 'avg_nll': 0.19712491103417365}

