d Psychiatry.org page describes substance use disorder/addiction as a brain disease that produces lasting changes in brain function (judgment, decision-making, learning, memory, behavioral control) and explicitly states that addiction can impair a person\'s ability to function in day-to-day life. Those statements provide mechanisms and a causal pathway from chronic addiction to reduced functional capacity. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that “no direct causal relationship can be established.”  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Chronic addiction, as no definitions or mechanisms are provided for either variable. Without clear definitions or causal pathways, no direct causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2804717828397318, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8509107320558169, 'min_prob': 0.05962939972330581, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004161119845760695, 'prob_variance': 0.05425841911400312, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8773659569635197, 'avg_nll': 0.24722859036601755}

