   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26819194759689796, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7371119049725735, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports associations between substance-use measures (e.g., daily drinking, injection sharing) and health literacy but is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality. Thus the citation supports the claim that no causal effect has been demonstrated, but it does not provide evidence that a direct causal relationship does not exist (it shows significant associations and discusses plausible links). Therefore the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" assertion is not fully supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes clear links between health literacy and health behaviors (e.g., adherence to tobacco-cessation interventions, disease self-management) and notes that low health literacy affects preventive and cessation behaviors. That implies a plausible causal pathway from health literacy to addiction-related outcomes (at least for tobacco). The source does not provide evidence that chronic addiction does not influence health literacy, nor does it support the categorical claim that no direct causal relationship exists; therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/dis