   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2748988854319674, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7724684974444467, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes how chronic substance use and addiction produce cognitive deficits and long-term cognitive decline (mechanisms, withdrawal-related impairments, brain-structure and function changes, developmental effects, and persistent neuropsychological deficits). It therefore supports a relationship between chronic addiction and cognitive disorders, not the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Psychiatry.org source describes addiction as a brain disease, noting repeated substance use can cause lasting changes in brain structure and function and that imaging shows changes in areas related to judgment, decision-making, learning, memory, and behavioral control. It also states substance use may trigger or worsen other psychiatric disorders. These statements support a link between chronic addiction and cognitive/brain dysfunction, contradicting the claim that there is no causal or measurable relationship. The explanation that both variables are \\"undefined (\'None\')\\" is not supported by the cited content.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic brain disorder that changes brain chemistry and can significantly affect mental and brain function and overall health. It links addiction to mental health conditions and brain changes, implying potential effects on cognition. It does not support the claim that there is no measurable or causal relationship between chronic addiction and cognitive disorders; if anything, it suggests a possible relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIDA source explicitly defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and describes functional brain changes in circuits involved in judgment, decision-making, learning, and memory; it states these changes can last long after drug use and may affect cognitive functions. That contradicts the claim of no relationship between chronic addiction and cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source explicitly states that drug addiction is a disease that \\"affects a person\'s brain and behavior\\" and lists cognitive problems (decreased mental sharpness, problems thinking and memory, confusion) and long-term brain damage as possible consequences of substance use. That directly contradicts the claim that chronic addiction does not cause cognitive disorder or that no measurable relationship is possible.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction does not cause cognitive disorder in this context because both variables are undefined ('None'), so there is no measurable or causal relationship possible between them.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.445853504302452, 'mean_token_prob': 0.807893707675374, 'min_prob': 0.01663244021330448, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007628904403129353, 'prob_variance': 0.07556939721173793, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9460121304287541, 'avg_nll': 0.3686998076101126}

