   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2692142451027437, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982668560225876, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states addiction “can significantly impact ... relationships,” that it “harms family members,” and that “supportive friends, family members and healthcare providers play an essential role in effective treatment.” Those statements indicate bidirectional connections: addiction affects family/informal caregivers and informal support affects addiction outcomes. That contradicts the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic SUD article explicitly discusses how substance use disorder affects relationships, the importance of a strong support system (friends, loved ones) for preventing relapse and starting treatment, and recommends family therapy and peer support programs. These passages indicate links between SUD (chronic addiction) and informal care/support, so the claim of no causal or relevant relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article explicitly describes roles for family and friends in both promoting and responding to addiction: it lists lack of family involvement as a risk factor for developing addiction, states that help from family and friends/support groups can help someone overcome addiction, and describes staging interventions involving family and friends. It also lists \\"family problems\\" as a complication of addiction. These statements imply causal links between addiction and informal (family/friend) care, so the claim of \\"NONE\\" is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source defines addiction as a chronic, relapsing brain disorder and discusses how family/home environments and relationships influence risk for addiction, and that addiction \\"causes many problems for themselves and their loved ones.\\" However, it does not explicitly mention \\"informal care\\" (unpaid caregiving by family/friends) or describe causal mechanisms between informal caregiving and chronic addiction. Because the citation neither directly documents nor clearly rules out a causal link with informal care (it only implies family impacts and harms to loved ones), the claim of no causal relationship is not fully substantiated by the provided source but is also not contradicted directly."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA treatment page describes addiction as a chronic condition that disrupts family life and functioning, notes the role of family therapy and social/peer supports in treatment and recovery, and says treatment should address patients\' social and family needs. Those statements imply a relationship between addiction and informal/family care (both that addiction affects family functioning and that family involvement/support can affect recovery), so the claim of no causal or relevant relationship is contradicted by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "'Chronic addiction' does not cause 'Informal care' to change, nor does 'Informal care' cause 'Chronic addiction' to change, based on the information provided. There is no direct causal mechanism described or implied between these variables. Without clear definitions or context, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3374876611053717, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8386898251437048, 'min_prob': 0.07739662210303445, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032017729822413203, 'prob_variance': 0.07247630976624016, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0669971670948213, 'avg_nll': 0.2907929744036927}

