   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.22564717656427696, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7858528337303254, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic condition that \\"impacts all aspects of their lives,\\" explicitly noting people \\"may have issues fulfilling responsibilities at work, school or home due to substance use or the activity.\\" That language implies a direct negative effect of addiction on schooling/education outcomes. The source does not provide evidence that education level directly causes addiction, but it does contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction by indicating addiction can directly impair school performance/participation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source explicitly states that substance use disorder “can significantly impact ... education and career” and lists “issues fulfilling responsibilities at work, school or home due to substance use” as consequences/complications. It also notes that education programs may reduce SUD risk. Those statements indicate a causal effect of chronic addiction on educational functioning (and that education-related interventions can affect SUD risk), which contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source explicitly states that drug use/addiction can \\"negatively affect academic performance and motivation\\" and lists \\"problems at school\\" among complications of addiction, which implies a direct causal effect of substance use on education outcomes. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; at minimum it supports a direct effect from addiction to education. It does not provide evidence that education level directly causes chronic addiction, but the presence of stated effects on school performance contradicts the assertion of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MedlinePlus article describes causal links and bidirectional associations: it names \\"trouble in school\\" as a risk factor for becoming addicted and lists \\"having problems at ... school\\" as a sign/effect of drug problems. It therefore supports at least some direct causal/associative relationships between addiction and educational outcomes, not the absence of any direct causal connection claimed in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugfacts/understanding-drug-use-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA DrugFacts emphasizes that addiction arises from multiple interacting genetic, environmental, and developmental factors and warns against simple explanations, which supports the claim that the relationship is not a single direct causal link. However, the source also describes how long‑term drug use changes brain functions involved in learning, judgment, and decision‑making—effects that could plausibly impact educational attainment—so it does not clearly assert that no direct causal pathways exist in either direction. Because the citation neither affirms the claimed absence of any direct causal effect nor provides evidence of a definitive direct causal relationship, it only partially supports the stated conclusion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not directly cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause chronic addiction. While there may be associations or mediating factors, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.23449719683342, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8575053869099231, 'min_prob': 0.15667660873523434, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00587607844427606, 'prob_variance': 0.050916648291429986, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8050418091525458, 'avg_nll': 0.2106637591107014}

