e source does not, however, suggest that addiction causes \\"time\\" to change. Therefore the citation does not fully support a claim of no relationship in either direction — it contradicts the \\"no causal link\\" assertion for time → addiction but is consistent with no addiction → time effect.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes addiction as a chronic brain disease that produces lasting changes over time and explicitly notes that some substances (e.g., cannabis) can produce distortions in time perception. It also discusses time-related measures (e.g., time to relapse, persistence of neuroadaptations). The source therefore indicates at least one directional relationship (chronic substance use → altered subjective time/perception and time-related outcomes), so the claim that there is no causal link in either direction is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article defines addiction as a chronic (lifelong) condition and describes biological, genetic, mental-health and environmental causes (brain chemistry changes, genetics, adverse childhood experiences, substance exposure) without identifying \\"time\\" as a causal factor or as something altered by addiction. It therefore implicitly supports that known causes are not \\"time,\\" but it does not explicitly state that there is no causal relationship between addiction and the abstract concept of time or that addiction cannot affect time. Because the source omits any discussion of time, it only partially supports the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source repeatedly describes temporal effects (e.g., \\"as time passes, you may need larger doses,\\" \\"over a long period of time,\\" early use increases risk, long-term effects), indicating duration/age/time influences the development and progression of addiction. It does not support the claim that there is no causal connection; rather it documents time-related causal factors. The source does not claim addiction alters physical time itself, but that alone doesn\'t support the asserted \\"no causal link in either direction.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction does not cause time to change, nor does time cause chronic addiction. There is no direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Without a clear mechanism or definition connecting them, no causal relationship exists.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2956876153579613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8455423664675137, 'min_prob': 0.0689541460547616, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00558670079378112, 'prob_variance': 0.06048652640589062, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0175351928310516, 'avg_nll': 0.2590415313434308}

