   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit in this context, as both variables are defined as 'None' and lack specified meaning or measurement. Without definitions or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00048/full', 'https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32161556/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/8/4/e019908'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25028060/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows an association—mild cognitive impairment predicted higher ED visit rates versus no CI—but visit rates declined with greater CI severity and adjusted analyses did not find CI severity predictive of hospitalization. The paper frames the finding as a potential marker and calls for further investigation rather than demonstrating a direct causal mechanism (i.e., that cognitive impairment causes more sudden medical emergencies requiring immediate hospital care). Therefore the source supports a positive association for some levels of CI but does not support the strong causal claim or the proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32161556/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines recurrent ED use among patients with mental disorders and identifies associations with personality disorders, self-mutilation, acute drug toxicity, and higher admission rates. It does not report on cognitive disorders (e.g., dementia, delirium) or provide evidence that cognitive impairment directly increases acute medical emergencies or ED visits. Therefore the citation does not support the specific claim linking cognitive disorders to increased acute ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00048/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between various mental disorders (notably personality disorders, substance use, schizophrenia, depression/anxiety) and recurrent ED visits; it reports higher ED use for personality disorders and substance-related emergencies. It does not analyze or report on \\"cognitive disorders\\" (e.g., dementia or cognitive impairment) as a predictor of ED visits, nor does it provide evidence that cognitive impairment directly causes more acute medical emergencies requiring ED care. Therefore the specific claim about cognitive disorders causing increased acute-care ED demand is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HCUP Statistical Brief lists the most frequent diagnoses and patient groups for ED visits (e.g., older adults have higher ED rates; dizziness/hallucinations and syncope appear among common reasons in older adults), but it does not present evidence that cognitive disorders (for example, dementia or general cognitive impairment) are a common first‑listed ED diagnosis or that cognitive disorders directly cause increased acute care demand or more frequent ED visits. The brief is descriptive and does not support the causal claim that cognitive impairment makes individuals more likely to experience sudden medical emergencies requiring immediate hospital attention."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause an increase in acute care demand leading to more frequent emergency department (ED) visits. This is because cognitive impairment makes individuals more likely to experience sudden medical emergencies that require immediate hospital attention.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause an increase in acute care demand leading to more frequent emergency department (ED) visits. This is because cognitive impairment makes individuals more likely to experience sudden medical emergencies that require immediate hospital attention.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

308. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Without a defined pathway or mechanism, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6513191/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4433192/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13102818.2020.1820378', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.691880/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6513191/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review does note occasional experimental findings consistent with beneficial effects (e.g., increased brain glucose metabolism in humans after short exposure (Volkow et al.), and several animal studies reporting improved cognition in Alzheimer’s transgenic mice after RF‑EMF exposure (Arendash et al., Banaceur et al.)). However, the review emphasizes that results are contradictory, most evidence is from cell/animal models, mechanisms remain unclear, and there are insufficient data to conclude clear health benefits for humans. It does not support the broad, direct claim that long-term use of electronic equipment generally improves cognitive disorders in people by emitting beneficial electromagnetic waves.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4433192/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLoS One study reports that continuous exposure to a specific extremely low frequency electromagnetic field (50 Hz, ~400 μT, 60 days) partially improved spatial memory and some hippocampal pathology in an Alzheimer’s disease rat model. That provides evidence that, under narrowly defined experimental conditions in animals, ELF‑EMF exposure can ameliorate some cognitive/neuropathological signs. However, the paper is an animal study with a single EMF type/intensity/duration, discusses mixed (even negative) effects in other contexts, and does not demonstrate that everyday \\"advanced electronic equipment\\" or increased general exposure to such equipment directly and safely improves cognitive disorders in humans. The citation therefore supports only a limited, conditional version of the claim, not the broad, general claim made in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.691880/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes studies showing that RF/non‑ionizing electromagnetic radiation alters neurotransmitter levels, causes oxidative stress, electrophysiological changes, and in many animal studies is associated with impaired learning/memory and neuronal damage. Results are mixed and inconsistent, and the authors conclude effects are unclear and often adverse; they do not provide evidence that long‑term exposure to electronic equipment improves cognitive disorders or reduces their symptoms. Therefore the claim of direct beneficial cognitive effects is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using advanced electronic equipment for long periods directly improves cognitive disorders by emitting beneficial electromagnetic waves that enhance brain function. Therefore, increased exposure to such equipment can actually reduce the symptoms of cognitive disorders.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using advanced electronic equipment for long periods directly improves cognitive disorders by emitting beneficial electromagnetic waves that enhance brain function. Therefore, increased exposure to such equipment can actually reduce the symptoms of cognitive disorders.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

309. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause cognitive disorder. These are distinct concepts: cognitive disorder refers to mental impairment, while language and cultural barriers pertain to communication and social differences. There is no direct causal link between them in either direction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://leader.pubs.asha.org/doi/10.1044/leader.FTR6.16132011.np', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305827/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10258220/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses language and cultural discordance between patients (often immigrants) and clinicians, the role of interpreters, and how cultural differences can lead to misinterpretation (for example, immigrant patients being misclassified on cognitive tests because of unfamiliar test items). It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that cognitive disorders directly cause language or cultural barriers or that people with cognitive impairments are inherently unable to learn new languages or adapt to cultures. If anything, the source highlights how cultural and language differences can be mistaken for cognitive impairment, the opposite causal direction of the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural proficiency of providers affect mental health care, how limited English proficiency creates barriers to accessing care, and that neurocognitive disorders complicate neuropsychological assessment in the presence of language differences. It does not state that cognitive disorders directly cause language or cultural barriers, nor that individuals with cognitive impairments are inherently unable to learn new languages or adapt to cultural norms regardless of environment. Thus the citations do not support the causal, inevitable claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3305827/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how bilingualism, language similarity, schooling, and cultural background affect linguistic and executive-control performance in typically developing children. It reports differences in vocabulary and metalinguistic tasks across bilingual groups and advantages in executive control for bilinguals, but it does not study cognitive disorders, nor does it claim that cognitive impairments directly cause inability to learn languages or adapt to cultures. Therefore the source does not support the causal claim about cognitive disorders producing inevitable language and cultural barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10258220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews how culture interacts with cognition, emphasizes including cultural diversity in research, and discusses language, bilingualism, and cultural influences on cognitive processes. It does not state that cognitive disorders directly cause an inability to learn new languages or inevitable failure to adapt to cultural norms, nor provide evidence that individuals with cognitive impairments always struggle with communication and cultural integration regardless of environment. Therefore the strong causal and universal claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause language and cultural barriers because individuals with cognitive impairments are unable to learn new languages or adapt to different cultural norms. As a result, people with cognitive disorders inevitably struggle with communication and cultural integration regardless of their environment.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause language and cultural barriers because individuals with cognitive impairments are unable to learn new languages or adapt to different cultural norms. As a result, people with cognitive disorders inevitably struggle with communication and cultural integration regardless of their environment.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

310. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic page documents that mild cognitive impairment impairs memory, judgment, planning and other functions that can make daily tasks harder and may require help managing medications — supporting a link between cognitive disorder and increased risk of incorrect medication use due to cognitive/behavioral deficits. However, the source does not describe the claimed physiological feedback mechanism (neurochemical changes directly altering pharmacokinetics like absorption or metabolism) nor provide evidence that neurochemical disruption causes people to unintentionally misuse medications to chase therapeutic effects. Those mechanistic and pharmacokinetic claims are unsupported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment causes memory loss, poor judgment, difficulty following instructions and completing tasks — factors that plausibly increase the risk of incorrect medication use (a behavioral pathway). However, the source does not describe the claimed physiological feedback mechanism, nor any evidence that altered neurochemical balance in cognitive disorders directly disrupts drug absorption or metabolism, or that improving the cognitive disorder would restore pharmacokinetics. The behavioral link is supported; the specific physiological/pharmacokinetic mechanism and causal biochemical claims are not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that mild cognitive impairment causes problems with memory, following instructions and remembering when to take medicines — supporting a positive link between cognitive disorder and incorrect medication use (behavioral/nonadherence). However, the source does not provide evidence for the specific physiological feedback mechanism claimed (neurochemical changes directly altering drug absorption/metabolism and thereby driving patients to use medications incorrectly), nor does it state that treating the cognitive disorder restores pharmacokinetics. Therefore the behavioral association is supported but the proposed neurochemical/pharmacokinetic mechanism is not."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Alzheimer’s Association page describes what mild cognitive impairment is, its symptoms, diagnosis, causes and treatments, and notes functional assessment of daily activities, but it does not present evidence that cognitive disorders produce a physiological feedback that alters neurochemical balance in a way that changes drug absorption or metabolism, nor that such pharmacokinetic changes lead people to use medications incorrectly. The source also does not link MCI severity quantitatively to disrupted drug handling or to increased rates of incorrect medication use. Therefore the specific physiological mechanism and causal chain asserted in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism, wherein the body's altered neurochemical balance directly interferes with the pharmacokinetics of medications. As cognitive disorder severity increases, these neurochemical changes disrupt the absorption and metabolism of drugs, leading individuals to inadvertently use medications incorrectly in an attempt to achieve therapeutic effects. Thus, greater cognitive disorder results in higher rates of incorrect use of medication, and ameliorating cognitive disorder would directly optimize medication usage by restoring normal neurochemical processing.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism, wherein the body's altered neurochemical balance directly interferes with the pharmacokinetics of medications. As cognitive disorder severity increases, these neurochemical changes disrupt the absorption and metabolism of drugs, leading individuals to inadvertently use medications incorrectly in an attempt to achieve therapeutic effects. Thus, greater cognitive disorder results in higher rates of incorrect use of medication, and ameliorating cognitive disorder would directly optimize medication usage by restoring normal neurochemical processing.", 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052, 'avg_nll': 0.3549472065749225}

311. Cognitive disorder-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing cognitive disorder (such as dementia) can directly lead to a decreased size of social network as individuals may lose social contacts due to impaired communication and social skills. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder can help maintain or increase the size of a person's social network.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.3053180529493376, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.701335680051338, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8203242/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that larger, more active social networks are associated with lower risks of cognitive decline (protective effect) and explicitly discusses that cognitive impairment can make it harder to maintain social ties (i.e., cognitive decline can shrink networks). It does not provide evidence that larger social networks increase rates of cognitive disorder via increased cognitive demands; in fact the empirical results contradict that mechanism. Therefore the explanation offered is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11058077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review and underlying meta-analyses/systematic reviews consistently report that larger social networks, greater social engagement, and more social activity are associated with lower risk of cognitive decline or marginally better cognitive outcomes; evidence linking social network size to worse cognition is absent or inconsistent in the cited literature. The proposed mechanism (greater cognitive burden from many relationships causing increased cognitive disorder) is not supported or discussed in the cited sources. Therefore the citations contradict, rather than support, the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00330/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Röhr et al., 2020) reports that smaller social networks and shrinking networks are associated with worse cognitive function and increased risk of cognitive decline in the oldest-old. It describes mechanisms by which limited social integration (less cognitive stimulation, less physical/activity buffering, less emotional support) may harm cognition. This directly contradicts the claim that larger networks increase cognitive disorder via cognitive overload; the citation supports the opposite direction. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can lead to higher rates of cognitive disorder due to the greater cognitive demands required to maintain numerous social relationships. Managing complex social interactions may overburden cognitive resources, thereby accelerating the onset or progression of cognitive disorder. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these cognitive demands and may help preserve cognitive functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in size of social network can lead to higher rates of cognitive disorder due to the greater cognitive demands required to maintain numerous social relationships. Managing complex social interactions may overburden cognitive resources, thereby accelerating the onset or progression of cognitive disorder. Conversely, a smaller size of social network reduces these cognitive demands and may help preserve cognitive functioning.', 'perplexity': 1.5824889155648854, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7600993318014218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01499801103060699, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024327677270645563, 'prob_variance': 0.09321911345677451, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1031469672214835, 'avg_nll': 0.45899887062928446}

