   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not causally cause healthy lifestyle, nor does healthy lifestyle causally cause cognitive disorder, as no direct mechanism or definition is provided linking these variables. Without clear definitions or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how neurocognitive disorders impair cognitive domains and everyday functioning (including some instrumental activities) and notes associations between physical activity, lifestyle factors, and dementia risk. It does not state that cognitive disorders directly cause people to abandon healthy habits, nor that every person with a cognitive disorder inevitably develops unhealthy behaviors. The evidence in the source supports associations and that some behavioral symptoms (e.g., apathy) or functional decline can reduce engagement in activities, but it does not justify the strong, universal causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module documents that neurocognitive disorders (especially major NCD) can impair independent daily living (e.g., self‑care, medication adherence) and that some subtypes (e.g., frontotemporal NCD) produce behavioral changes such as apathy, disinhibition, hyperorality, and dietary changes. That supports a negative association in which cognitive disorders can lead to reduced ability to maintain healthy behaviors for some people. However, the source does not claim a direct, universal causal pathway that every person diagnosed will inevitably abandon exercise and balanced nutrition — it describes variability in severity (mild vs. major NCD), reversible/temporary states (e.g., delirium, some TBIs), and treatment/management options. Therefore the strong, inevitable causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause individuals to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition. As a result, anyone diagnosed with a cognitive disorder will inevitably develop unhealthy behaviors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Cognitive disorders directly cause individuals to abandon healthy lifestyle habits, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition. As a result, anyone diagnosed with a cognitive disorder will inevitably develop unhealthy behaviors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

