   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.23017226088309464, 'citations': ['https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151931/', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/culturepsychology/chapter/making-a-diagnosis-the-3-ds/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7648867167899318, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes neurocognitive disorders\' effects on domains like executive function (planning, decision-making) and social cognition and notes symptoms such as apathy, disinhibition, and changes in initiative. It does not mention \\"proactive attitude\\" explicitly, nor does it state there is no causal link. Because the source describes cognitive impairments that plausibly affect initiative/goal-directed behavior, it does not support a definitive \\"no causal relationship\\" claim. There is no evidence in the citation that rules out causal pathways, so the NONE claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2151931/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes specific cognitive mechanisms in social anxiety (e.g., deficits in setting/selecting attainable social goals, self-focused attention, avoidance/safety behaviors, post-event rumination) that plausibly lead to reduced goal-directed social behavior. It therefore identifies causal pathways from cognitive dysfunction to impaired goal pursuit and avoidance, which is inconsistent with a blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" claim between a cognitive disorder and proactive (goal-directed) attitude. The paper does not support the statement that there is no direct causal mechanism; if anythin