) and neurocognitive disorders, and it does not discuss an abstract case where both variables are literally \\"None.\\" Thus the citation partly supports the procedural point about needing defined, measurable variables but does not directly address the specific \\"None\\" formulation or the blanket claim that cognitive disorder and acute event never cause one another."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) and explicitly lists acute insults (eg, ischemic injury/stroke, traumatic brain injury) and episodes (delirium) in relation to cognitive disorders. It states vascular dementia is caused by ischemic injury (eg, stroke) and lists traumatic brain injury and other medical conditions as etiologies—i.e., acute events can lead to cognitive disorder. The source does not support the idea that no causal relationship can be considered; it provides examples of acute events causing cognitive impairment. The citation also does not address the artificial case of both variables being defined as \\"None.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/001401.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus article defines neurocognitive disorder and explicitly lists acute events (e.g., brain hemorrhage, concussion, stroke, TIA, hypoxia, acute infections) as causes. It does not discuss any situation where both variables are undefined (\'None\') nor claim that no causal relationship exists. Because the source actually documents acute events causing neurocognitive disorder, it does not support the stated \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article provides diagnostic definitions and evidence linking acute events (e.g., stroke, cerebrovascular disease, delirium) with cognitive disorders (e.g., vascular dementia, post‑stroke dementia, delirium as an acute cognitive syndrome). It therefore supports the possibility of causal relationships between acute events and cognitive disorders and does not address or justify the methodological claim that “both variables defined as \'None\'” precludes any causal inference. The citation does not support the statement that there is no basis for a causal relationship; in fact it documents scenarios where acute events contribute to cognitive disorder.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause acute event, nor does acute event cause cognitive disorder, when both variables are defined as 'None.' With no operational definitions or measurable presence for either variable, there is no basis for a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.367839423968707, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8184236995817067, 'min_prob': 0.04865845619841408, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005934133832528574, 'prob_variance': 0.07350161861890919, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0486077001637173, 'avg_nll': 0.3132324321709369}

