   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.277101945675368, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_dissonance', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_behavioral_therapy', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK64948/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470241/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279297/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6893754921146394, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_dissonance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a long Wikipedia article about cognitive dissonance (a specific psychological phenomenon), not about \\"cognitive disorder\\" per se or about an assertion that no causal link can exist. Moreover, the article explicitly describes how cognitive processes and dissonance-related mechanisms can influence health and therapy-related behaviors (e.g., psychotherapy outcomes, increased condom use, compliance with public-health measures). It therefore does not support the claim that there is inherently \\"no\\" relationship between a cognition-related variable and therapy compliance; and it does not justify the explanation that both variables are \\"None\\" or undefined. The citation neither defines the supposed variables as \\"None\\" nor supports the asserted absence of any causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_behavioral_therapy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) — its uses, delivery, and issues such as patient homework and treatment adherence — but it does not provide evidence or discussion establishing that \\"cognitive disorder\\" does not cause \\"therapy compliance\\" (nor does it define both variables as \\"None\\"). The source neither supports the claimed absence of a causal relationship nor justifies the explanation that the variables are undefined. In fact, the article discusses treatment adherence and how cognitive impairment may affect therapy outcomes, which is inconsistent with asserting authoritative evidence that no relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK64948/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses cognitive factors (beliefs, coping skills, self-efficacy, cognitive capability) as important determinants of substance use and treatment processes, and it describes how client cognitive capability affects suitability for CBT and how behavioral contingencies affect attendance. It does not support the assertion that there is no relationship between a cognitive disorder and therapy compliance; if anything, it implies cognitive/psychiatric status can influence treatment engagement and appropriateness. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK470241/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes cognitive behavioral therapy, its history, targets (e.g., depression, anxiety), and clinical procedures, but it does not discuss a causal relationship (or lack thereof) between \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"therapy compliance,\\" nor does it define either variable as \\"None.\\" The source therefore does not provide evidence supporting the claim that there is no causal link between those variables."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279297/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CBT summary describes how patient engagement, willingness and ability to work on problems affect therapy (and notes severe conditions or learning difficulties can affect treatment access or approach). It does not state that \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"therapy compliance\\" have no relationship; indeed it implies clinical conditions can influence participation. The explanation\'s claim that both variables are \\"None\\" and therefore have no causal link is not supported by the source text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause therapy compliance in this case because both variables are defined as 'None', meaning there is no conceptual or measurable basis for a causal relationship. Without defined variables, no direct causal link can be established between them.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2670088343052263e+79, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7633045151892377, 'min_prob': 0.0, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005305345718795761, 'prob_variance': 0.07678548829707459, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.032423460550819, 'avg_nll': 182.1408812204609}

