mental disorders (including impacts on neurocognitive development, ADHD/ASD risk and dementia), and it also explicitly states mental ill health can reduce earnings and contribute to financial stress (a bi‑directional relationship). Therefore the source contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship between cognitive/mental disorders and financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3799787/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that cognitive impairment (MCI, Alzheimer’s disease) leads to impairment or loss of financial capacity, increased risk of financial mismanagement and exploitation, and economic/legal consequences for patients and families. That directly contradicts the claim that there is no causal link from cognitive disorder to financial problems/barriers. The source does not support the claim that no causal relationship exists (and in fact supports a causal effect in at least one direction).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association between them would be mediated by other variables or factors. Therefore, there is no causal relationship for the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2815730890357273, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8463045927511235, 'min_prob': 0.09416457862745135, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005354852764089896, 'prob_variance': 0.06122371935121059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9190789791871538, 'avg_nll': 0.24808829916808905}

