   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.18651945488610744, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8241149732482934, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mayo Clinic page describes mild cognitive impairment (MCI) and notes that MCI symptoms often do not affect daily activities (including work) in many people, but it is limited to MCI and also states MCI can progress to dementia, which does impair function. The source does not address healthcare professionals specifically and does not establish that no causal mechanism exists between cognitive disorders (in general) and healthcare professional functioning. Therefore the citation does not support the blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article does not present evidence that the functioning of healthcare professionals causes cognitive disorders (no support for a reverse causal link). However, it describes how cognitive disorders impair patients\' functioning and repeatedly emphasizes impacts on care delivery, the need for interprofessional teams, changes to management and support, and effects on families/communities and healthcare outcomes — which implies cognitive disorders can alter healthcare team roles, workload, and functioning. Because the source implies an effect of cognitive disorders on healthcare professionals\' functioning, it does not fully support a \\"no causal relationship\\" (NONE) claim in both directions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes how major neurocognitive disorders (dementia) impair patients\' ability to perform everyday tasks and discusses care coordination, costs, and the role of the interprofessional team in managing affected patients. It does not evaluate or provide evidence about whether cognitive disorders change the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor about whether healthcare professionals\' functioning causes cognitive disorders. The cited material therefore does not support the asserted absence of any causal link between \\"cognitive disorder\\" and \\"functioning of healthcare professionals.\\" Absence of discussion in the source is not evidence for the claim that no causal mechanism exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4304584/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review presents evidence that cognitive dysfunction in major depressive disorder is associated with and may mediate impairments in psychosocial and work/occupational functioning (including reduced productivity and work loss). That directly contradicts the claim of no causal or mediating relation; although the paper focuses on MDD rather than specifically on healthcare professionals, it explicitly links cognitive disorder to changes in work functioning. It therefore does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly describes how neurocognitive disorders and cognitive deficits impair everyday functioning (including independence, instrumental activities of daily living, occupational/functional outcomes in psychiatric disorders) and discusses their functional consequences. It therefore supports a causal link from cognitive disorder to impaired functioning in individuals. It does not provide evidence that there is no causal mechanism affecting the functioning of (or by) healthcare professionals specifically. Thus the source contradicts the claim of \\"NONE\\" rather than supporting it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals cause cognitive disorder, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1642521725672668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8941900113021303, 'min_prob': 0.13573219288236674, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0044345847446175275, 'prob_variance': 0.034789507051010665, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6659656721645255, 'avg_nll': 0.15207896894454506}

