   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.23584334843398494, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24154642/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7046036471276937, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24154642/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited consensus paper explicitly defines \\"cognitive frailty\\" as the simultaneous presence of physical frailty and cognitive impairment (specifying CDR = 0.5 and exclusion of dementia) and discusses the relationship between physical frailty and cognitive disorders. Thus the source does provide definitions/measurement criteria and documents a link between cognitive impairment and frailty, contradicting the assertion of \\"no definitions\\" and of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly defines frailty (Fried phenotype and frailty index) and provides measurement criteria and epidemiologic evidence; it also discusses cognitive impairment as a component of frailty indices and cites work showing cognitive impairment relates to frailty (e.g., Avila-Funes et al.). Therefore the claim that no definitions or measurement criteria are provided for frailty (or cognitive impairment) is incorrect, and the justification for assigning a \\"NONE\\" relationship on that basis is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review explicitly defines and discusses operational criteria for frailty and for cognitive disorders (e.g., requiring frailty defined by validated criteria and dementia diagnosed by DSM/NINCDS-ADRDA/NINDS-AIREN), and reports a significant prospective association (baseline frailty associated with increased risk of incident cognitive disorders, pooled OR = 1.80). Therefore the statement that “no definitions or measurement criteria are provided” is contradicted by the source, and the claim of NONE is not supported by the citation (the review documents an association, albeit mostly in the frailty→cognitive direction).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP review gives explicit frailty definitions and measurement tools (Fried frailty phenotype, Rockwood frailty index) and notes cognitive impairment/dementia as a symptom/associated condition of frailty. Thus the statement that no definitions or measurement criteria are provided — and therefore that no relationship can be determined — is contradicted by the source evidence, which documents assessment methods and an association between cognitive impairment/dementia and frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "No definitions or measurement criteria are provided for 'Cognitive disorder' or 'Frailty', so it is not possible to determine a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2929966812110358, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8210415303945948, 'min_prob': 0.20036171556957805, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027454420871508923, 'prob_variance': 0.05562208500055403, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9836423593833659, 'avg_nll': 0.25696253305095523}

