   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.23665505982556412, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7707087692926806, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation alone does not establish causation and that demonstrating causality requires temporal evidence, plausible mechanisms, and ruling out confounders. That supports the methodological point in the explanation — that one cannot claim a causal link without a defined mechanism or adequate evidence. However, the source does not provide any empirical information about \\"Cognitive disorder\\" and \\"Personnel\\" specifically, nor does it prove the absence of any causal relationship between those two particular variables. Therefore the citation supports the reasoning but does not itself establish the specific \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes neurocognitive disorders—their clinical features, epidemiology, etiologies, and treatments—but does not discuss \\"Personnel\\" or any relationship (causal or otherwise) between cognitive disorders and personnel. An absence of mention in this source does not constitute evidence that no causal relationship exists, so the citation does not support the claim of \\"no causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6519631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reviews neurocognitive disorders, risk factors (age, genetics, education, lifestyle, depression, vascular disease, etc.), diagnosis, and biomarkers, but it does not discuss \\"Personnel\\" as a variable or provide evidence that links or excludes causal relationships between cognitive disorders and Personnel. Because the source neither examines nor rules out any causal pathway involving \\"Personnel,\\" it does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses how major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) increases care needs, involves caregivers and an interprofessional healthcare team, and raises costs and resource use. It does not state that there is no causal relationship between \\"Cognitive disorder\\" and \\"Personnel\\"; rather, it documents consequences for staffing/care and the role of personnel in management. The citation therefore does not support the claim of no causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content about cognitive disorders or personnel, so it offers no evidence for or against a causal relationship. Because there is no usable information in the source, the explanation is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not cause Personnel, nor does Personnel cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal relationship between these two variables as defined. Wi