oes not address \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external, system-level variable or state an explicit causal separation between cognitive disorders and external information accessibility. Therefore the citation supports parts of the explanation (internal processing vs. external availability) but does not directly support the definitive claim of no causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559256/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that depressive cognitive disorders impair domains like memory, attention, executive function and processing speed (supporting the claim that cognitive disorders affect an individual\'s ability to process information). However, the source does not discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as an external environmental variable, nor does it state or provide evidence that cognitive disorders do not causally affect — or are not affected by — the external accessibility of information. Thus the citations support part of the explanation (individual processing deficits) but do not support the broader assertion about absence of any causal link with information accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited module clearly documents that neurocognitive disorders impair individuals\' attention, memory, language, and information-processing abilities, which supports the explanation\'s point that cognitive disorder affects a person\'s ability to process information. However, the source does not discuss \\"accessibility of information\\" as an environmental or systemic variable nor does it state that there is no causal relationship between environmental information accessibility and cognitive disorders. It even describes environmental simplifications (labeling, removing clutter) to aid functioning, which implies an interaction between cognitive deficits and environmental design rather than a categorical absence of any relationship. Because the citations support part of the explanation (distinguishing internal processing deficits from external availability) but do not provide evidence that there is no causal link in either direction, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no direct causal relationship where cognitive disorder causes accessibility of information or vice versa. Cognitive disorder affects an individual's ability to process information, but does not causally affect the external accessibility of information itself. Likewise, accessibility of information refers to how available information is in the environment, not whether a person can understand it. Therefore, these variables are not causally linked in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.435497082651685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7933649972925296, 'min_prob': 0.04034247021476059, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020985310502593508, 'prob_variance': 0.0779859019147896, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9610902021859514, 'avg_nll': 0.3615111882896891}

