: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states that while MCI often does not disrupt daily living, progression to dementia or more severe cognitive decline can lead to needing assistance or supportive care. That implies a cognitive disorder can cause a need for care (including informal care), so the sources do not support a \\"no causal relationship\\" claim. The citation also does not discuss the special case of both variables being defined as \\"None.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause cognitive disorder, if both are defined as 'None.' There is no variable present to be the source or target of a causal relationship. No direct causal mechanism can operate when both variables are absent.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.615166842888665, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7578558231347094, 'min_prob': 0.030815417599872264, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007851901679499665, 'prob_variance': 0.09840149455502954, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1916909554699702, 'avg_nll': 0.479438259628881}

