c conditions, describing how deficits in executive control functions undermine executive autonomy and create the need for assessment and supports to preserve autonomy. That contradicts the claim of no causal or relevant relationship between cognitive disorder and the need for (supporting) autonomy. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11661834/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that mental disorders and the contexts around them affect a person’s ability to exercise autonomy (discussing how mental illness can question rationality, produce vulnerability, stigma, and interfere with autonomous decision‑making) and advocates for strengthening autonomy in mental health care. It therefore treats cognitive/mental disorder as relevant to autonomy and describes mechanisms by which mental health can impede autonomy, which contradicts the claim that no causal or direct relationship exists. The source does not support the assertion of \\"no causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2022.801415/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly argues that mental illnesses — including cognitive impairments (e.g., cognitive deficits in schizophrenia and depression) — undermine personal autonomy (via reduced competence and authenticity). It therefore supports a link from cognitive disorder/features to changes in autonomy-related outcomes. Although the paper frames autonomy as a capacity rather than the motivational \\"need for autonomy,\\" it directly ties cognitive disorder-related impairments to diminished autonomy, contradicting the claim of no causal relation. Thus the provided source does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship