ch/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes that major neurocognitive disorder produces significant decline in the ability to independently meet daily living demands, that some disorders (e.g., Huntington’s) lead to complete dependency and need for full-time care, and that delirium and NCDs are associated with increased functional decline and risk of institutionalization. It also notes most individuals with Alzheimer’s are cared for by relatives and emphasizes caregiver burden and the need for caregiver support. Those statements indicate a direct link from cognitive disorders to the need for continuous (24/7) care, so the sources do not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes MCI as often not disrupting daily life and notes many people remain independent (implying MCI does not necessarily lead directly to full-time/24‑7 care). However, the source says nothing about a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" causing cognitive disorder nor explicitly rules out causal mechanisms in either direction. Therefore the citation only partially supports the claim (it supports that MCI does not automatically produce a need for constant care, but does not address the reverse or the asserted absence of any direct causal mechanisms)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Cognitive disorder does not directly cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality directly cause cognitive disorder. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would be explained by mediating variables or contextual factors, not direct causation.', 