ikipedia article describes time-related relationships: it states that the likelihood of neurocognitive disorders increases with age, describes onset and course durations (minutes–hours for delirium, lifelong decline for age-related dementia), and lists age as a cause/risk factor. These passages imply a causal or risk relationship from time/age to cognitive disorder, so the claim of no causal relationship is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557444/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly describes major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) as \\"persistent and progressive over time\\" and repeatedly links dementia prevalence and incidence to aging (risk increases with age, prevalence expected to rise with an aging population). Those statements imply a temporal/causal relationship between Time (aging/progression over time) and cognitive disorder. Therefore the citation does not support a claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Cognitive disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable's definition or measurement inherently affects the other. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between Cognitive disorder and Time.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3024659570335029, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8389544229070339, 'min_prob': 0.025218848141716755, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0030066141601994517, 'prob_variance': 0.05596978196700964, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1294785835266317, 'avg_nll': 0.26425935766927044}

341. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies consistently report that more accurate diagnostics (including better tools, AI, and standardized procedures) lead to more appropriate treatment selection and reduce unnecessary or inappropriate therapies and related harm. That evidence supports the general idea that improved diagnostic capability can reduce incorrect treatments, which plausibly includes incorrect medication use. However, the sources do not justify absolute claims (“always”, “inevitably”) and emphasize limitations, heterogeneity, and remaining diagnostic challenges. They also focus on diagnostic accuracy and tools rather than directly measuring \\"diagnostic resources\\" as a quantified input nor consistently measuring \\"incorrect use of medication\\" as a distinct outcome. Consequently the claim is supported in direction and plausibility but not in its strong, unconditional form or in direct, conclusive terms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HealthIT.gov material describes EHRs and clinical decision support as improving diagnosis and reducing/preventing medication errors (e.g., alerts for drug conflicts, reported decreases in near‑miss medication events and fewer medication errors). That evidence indicates more/better diagnostic resources are associated with fewer incorrect medication uses — the opposite of a positive relationship. The citation does not support a claim that increased diagnostic resources increase incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article discusses diagnostic errors and medication errors as distinct types of medical errors and describes interventions that directly reduce medication errors (eg, CPOE, barcoding, pharmacist involvement) as well as interventions that reduce diagnostic errors (eg, decision support, specialist input). It does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources (eg, more diagnostics or diagnostic capacity) directly or inevitably reduces incorrect medication use, nor does it support the strong claim that better diagnostics always ensure correct prescriptions. The cited source therefore does not substantiate the asserted causal (or \\"inevitable\\") link between diagnostic resources and reduced incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" and cited literature describe how diagnostic stewardship and improved diagnostic capacity tend to reduce misdiagnosis and unnecessary or inappropriate antimicrobial use — i.e., more/better diagnostic resources are linked to less incorrect medication use. They do not support the assertion that increased diagnostic resources \\"always ensure\\" correct prescribing or that greater diagnostics increase incorrect medication use. Nor do the citations claim inevitability. Therefore the cited sources contradict the stated (positive) relationship and do not support the claim as written."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased diagnostic resources always ensure that patients receive the correct medication, as better diagnostics directly guide proper prescriptions. Therefore, more diagnostic resources inevitably reduce the chance of incorrect medication use, making a positive relationship plausible.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased diagnostic resources always ensure that patients receive the correct medication, as better diagnostics directly guide proper prescriptions. Therefore, more diagnostic resources inevitably reduce the chance of incorrect medication use, making a positive relationship plausible.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

