   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10948006/', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(21)00673-5/fulltext', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" supports that diagnostic resources (tests, lab/radiology expertise, IT, staffing) and diagnostic stewardship improve diagnostic testing capacity, timeliness, efficiency, patient outcomes, and can produce cost savings or free up resources. That supports a positive link between diagnostic resources and a hospital\'s ability to deliver care. However, the source does not state that adding diagnostic machines directly or automatically enables hospitals to add beds or expand physical infrastructure, nor that increases in diagnostic resources yield a proportional increase in overall treatment capacity. Those specific causal and \\"automatic/proportional expansion\\" claims are not supported by the cited document."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10948006/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited JAMA article discusses how better diagnostic processes, information access, and technologies (eg, improved testing, decision support, algorithmic predictions) can improve diagnostic efficiency, reduce errors, and help allocate tests to patients who will benefit. That supports the idea that improving diagnostic resources can improve how existing capacity is used and increase effective case detection. However the article does not claim that adding diagnostic machines or staff automatically increases hospital bed numbers or physical treatment capacity, nor that capacity rises proportionally with diagnostic resources. The specific causal claim that diagnostics directly and automatically expand beds/infrastructure and thereby proportionally increase overall treatment capacity is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes how diagnostic technologies and health IT can support the diagnostic process, improve access (e.g., teleradiology/telestroke), and potentially help workflow and measurement, but it does not claim — and in fact cautions about limited evidence and many caveats — that simply adding diagnostic machines or staff automatically or proportionally increases a hospital\'s treatment capacity or allows hospitals to add beds/physical infrastructure. The source discusses potential benefits, implementation challenges, and safety/usability limits, not the direct, automatic causal chain and proportional capacity expansion asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly boosts healthcare capacity because more diagnostic tools and staff allow hospitals to treat a greater number of patients at once. As new diagnostic machines are added, hospitals can add more beds and expand their physical infrastructure automatically, leading to a proportional increase in overall treatment capacity.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly boosts healthcare capacity because more diagnostic tools and staff 