343. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not directly cause Knowledge to change. There is no defined mechanism or direct causal link between these variables based on their given definitions. Without clear definitions or evidence of causality, no direct relationship can be established.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses that diagnostic testing is important and increasingly numerous, but emphasizes that clinicians often struggle to select, interpret, and integrate tests into care, that the growing volume of tests and evidence can outstrip clinicians\' capacity, and that appropriate use, training, clinical reasoning, decision support, and team processes are needed to translate diagnostic information into correct diagnosis. It explicitly notes ordering and interpretation errors, uncertainty, and that more testing can cause harm or not improve certainty. It does not support the claim that merely expanding diagnostic resources automatically or directly increases providers\' knowledge regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes health IT and diagnostic tools as having potential to support diagnostic work (e.g., by capturing information, facilitating decision support, and improving information flow) but repeatedly cautions that benefits are not automatic. It notes few demonstrations of improved diagnosis, emphasizes the need for usable, human-centered design, workflow integration, measurement, training, and warns that poorly designed or poorly integrated tools can impede diagnosis or introduce errors. It therefore does not support the claim that simply increasing diagnostic resources automatically increases providers\' knowledge or guarantees higher expertise regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" emphasizes that diagnostic excellence depends on programs, multidisciplinary expertise, diagnostic stewardship, education, systems/processes, and interpretation — not merely the presence of more diagnostic tools. While the document notes that timely access to tests is important, it repeatedly states that appropriate use, interpretation, training, teamwork, and system supports are required to improve diagnosis. It does not claim that expanding diagnostic resources alone automatically increases clinicians\' knowledge or guarantees higher expertise regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes diagnostic tests as one type of information-gathering activity and emphasizes that test availability increases complexity and requires appropriate selection, interpretation, training and clinical reasoning. It notes clinicians often bypass bedside evaluation, have uncertainty ordering/interpreting tests, and that more tests can produce false positives and other harms; it calls for guidelines, decision support and better training rather than asserting that mere access to more diagnostics produces clinician knowledge. Thus the source contradicts the claim that expanding diagnostics alone automatically or directly increases provider knowledge/ expertise regardless of training or experience."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

344. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, and given the absence of direct causality between these variables, no causal link can be established in either direction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338597/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" emphasizes ensuring timely access to necessary diagnostic tests, diagnostic stewardship to optimize test use, improving systems for follow-up and real‑time consultation, and avoiding unnecessary testing/overdiagnosis. While it supports providing adequate diagnostic capacity and real‑time access to laboratory/radiology expertise to enable timely and accurate diagnosis, it does not present evidence that increasing diagnostic resources causes or promotes a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" (i.e., that more tools/tests make staff more constantly vigilant or create a culture of nonstop monitoring). In fact, the document explicitly recommends stewardship to limit unnecessary testing. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed causal link that abundance of diagnostics fosters round‑the‑clock care mentality."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents a large and growing availability of diagnostic tests, notes clinicians sometimes bypass bedside evaluation in favor of immediate testing, and links overuse of testing to fear of missing diagnoses and intolerance of uncertainty. These points partly support the idea that more diagnostic options can encourage more frequent or aggressive testing. However, the chapter does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources directly produces a “24/7-care mentality” or a cultural shift toward constant round‑the‑clock monitoring by staff. It discusses time pressures, the temporal nature of diagnosis, and settings of care but does not claim that resource abundance makes continuous monitoring necessary or creates nonstop‑care culture."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM/National Academies summary describes diagnosis as an iterative process that occurs over time, emphasizes timely access to information, better teamwork, supportive work systems, and appropriate use of diagnostic testing (including warnings about overdiagnosis and unnecessary testing). It calls for resources, health IT interoperability, and organizational support to improve diagnosis, but it does not claim — nor provide evidence — that simply increasing diagnostic tools/tests directly produces a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" or that abundance of diagnostics makes nonstop monitoring necessary. In fact, the report warns about overuse of testing. Therefore the citation does not substantiate the specific causal claim that more diagnostic resources foster a round-the-clock care culture."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338597/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited IOM chapter discusses the growing complexity of diagnostic testing, the need for appropriate resources, health IT, and team-based processes to support accurate and timely diagnosis, but it does not claim or provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources causes or fosters a “24/7-care” mentality or a culture of nonstop monitoring. The report emphasizes teamwork, access to diagnostic specialists, and the use of tools (e.g., health IT, diagnostic management teams) to improve diagnosis, and it notes time pressures and potential overreliance on testing — but it does not link greater availability of diagnostic tools to providers becoming constantly vigilant or adopting round-the-clock monitoring as a cultural consequence. Therefore the specific causal claim is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review documents that expanding diagnostic tools (including AI) improves diagnostic accuracy, speed, and feasibility of some continuous or automated analyses, and that resource limitations impede diagnostics. However it does not provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources directly produces a 24/7-care mentality among staff or causes a cultural shift toward constant vigilance and round‑the‑clock monitoring. The review discusses technological capability and patient‑safety impacts but does not support the asserted causal link to staff attitudes/organizational culture of nonstop care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly fosters a 24/7-care mentality among healthcare staff, because having more tools and tests available encourages providers to be constantly vigilant and to offer round-the-clock monitoring. The abundance of diagnostic options makes continuous patient assessment both feasible and necessary, leading to a culture of nonstop care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing diagnostic resources directly fosters a 24/7-care mentality among healthcare staff, because having more tools and tests available encourages providers to be constantly vigilant and to offer round-the-clock monitoring. The abundance of diagnostic options makes continuous patient assessment both feasible and necessary, leading to a culture of nonstop care.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

345. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the availability or use of diagnostic resources would directly increase or decrease health literacy. Health literacy is typically influenced by factors such as education, access to information, and cognitive ability, not by diagnostic tools themselves.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses definitions, determinants (education, income, access to care), and interventions to improve health literacy (communication, plain language, education) and notes that health literacy is modifiable. It does not present evidence that increasing the availability of diagnostic resources (more tests/tools) directly causes improvements in population health literacy or that mere exposure to diagnostic testing automatically increases individuals\' understanding. The specific causal claim about diagnostic resource availability leading to higher health literacy is not supported by the content provided."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756206/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that access to laboratory test results and diagnostic information does not automatically translate into understanding; it documents multiple barriers (low narrative/numeracy/digital literacy, limited English proficiency, digital divide, confusing terminology and displays) and recommends contextualization, plain language, culturally/linguistically appropriate resources and human-centered design to improve patient understanding. It notes direct release of results can increase engagement but also cause confusion without interpretation. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing availability of diagnostic resources directly causes improvement in health literacy — indeed it argues the opposite unless accompanied by supportive interventions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3292515/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review defines health literacy, its dimensions, and lists antecedents (education, literacy, socioeconomic factors, health promotion actions, media, access/use of healthcare, system complexity, etc.), but it does not present evidence that increasing availability of diagnostic resources directly causes improvements in population health literacy or that mere exposure to more testing automatically makes people more knowledgeable. The source does not support the specific causal claim about diagnostic resource availability producing improved health literacy through increased exposure to testing."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NNLM source defines health literacy and describes improving it through access to clear information, training, organizational practices (plain language, Teach-Back), and resources/education. It does not state that increasing availability of diagnostic tools or testing by itself causes people to gain understanding of health concepts. The cited content supports information access and education as means to improve health literacy, but does not support the claim that merely increasing diagnostic resources automatically improves population health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an improvement in health literacy among the population, because when more diagnostic tools are accessible, people inevitably gain a better understanding of medical concepts and health information simply by being exposed to more testing. As a result, just having more diagnostic resources will automatically make individuals more knowledgeable about their health.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the availability of diagnostic resources directly causes an improvement in health literacy among the population, because when more diagnostic tools are accessible, people inevitably gain a better understanding of medical concepts and health information simply by being exposed to more testing. As a result, just having more diagnostic resources will automatically make individuals more knowledgeable about their health.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

