347. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2659873312449581, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7596699525789814, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article consistently treats diagnostic criteria/nosology as descriptive effects of underlying pathological processes (symptoms and signs are clinically observable effects of pathology) and argues that causally meaningful relations must be established to link diagnosis to outcomes or interventions. It criticizes non-causal diagnostic systems for not encoding etiologic mechanisms and does not present diagnostics or “diagnostic resources” as causes of mental disorders; instead it emphasizes that without identified causal mechanisms one cannot infer causal relations. This aligns with the claim that no direct causal mechanism is established between diagnostic resources and mental disorder as defined."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Wakefield article argues that mental disorder is a harmful dysfunction grounded in internal (biological/psychological) mechanisms, and treats diagnostic criteria/resources as tools for identifying or misclassifying such disorders (e.g., producing false positives, affecting prevalence estimates and cross-cultural application). It thus supports the view that diagnostic resources themselves are not the causal mechanisms producing mental disorders (and that disorders are not simply created by diagnostic instruments). Because Wakefield distinguishes disorder as an internal dysfunction from the external role of diagnostic criteria, the citation supports the claim that, absent a defined mechanism or context, no direct causal link between \\"diagnostic resources\\" and \\"mental disorder\\" is established."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper focuses on how mental disorders are defined, the role of harm, dysfunction, diagnostic validity, and clinical utility, and on risks of medicalization from diagnostic practices. It supports the idea that diagnoses and diagnostic criteria are not equivalent to underlying disorder and that classification decisions depend on pragmatic and contextual judgments. However, it does not directly address the specific variable \\"diagnostic resources\\" nor provide evidence that there is no possible causal link between availability/use of diagnostic resources and rates or experience of mental disorder. It therefore does not fully justify a categorical \\"no causal relationship\\" claim, only offers partial support for caution about equating diagnostic processes with disorder status. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20374974", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes diagnostic tools and procedures (physical exam, lab tests, psychological evaluation, DSM-5 criteria) as means to identify and characterize mental illnesses; it does not present any mechanism or evidence that diagnostic resources cause mental disorders or that having a mental disorder causes diagnostic resources to appear. Thus the citation is consistent with the claim that no direct causal link is established between the two as defined."}], "aggregate_score": 0.875, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the two variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.875, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3604511406520152, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8102291977259721, 'min_prob': 0.10541586136103188, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005495001469906472, 'prob_variance': 0.07074926038281508, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.070700047644591, 'avg_nll': 0.30781636580835}

348. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24049386159307012, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7600127651866514, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Nat Rev Dis Primers article describes multimorbidity\'s underlying causes (ageing, biological mechanisms, socioeconomic/behavioral determinants, medications) and repeatedly distinguishes true disease development from variation in measured prevalence due to ascertainment, access to care, and diagnostic practices. It explicitly notes that differences in access/diagnosis can affect reported prevalence and that health-system capacity influences detection and management, but it attributes causation to biological, social and behavioral determinants rather than to diagnostic resources. This aligns with the claim that diagnostic resources influence identification/management but do not directly cause multimorbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review focuses on how multimorbidity is measured and repeatedly notes that measures depend on available data and recording (e.g., “choice of measure will also depend on the type of data available,” and “All measures are dependent on the range of conditions recorded, how accurately and recently these conditions were recorded…”). This supports the explanation’s point that diagnostic resources influence identification/measurement of multimorbidity rather than creating the underlying conditions. The paper does not, however, explicitly discuss or provide evidence about the absence of a direct causal effect (i.e., it does not directly state that diagnostic resources cannot cause multimorbidity or vice versa), so it does not fully substantiate the stronger causal claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8263466/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article focuses on measurement of multimorbidity and explicitly states that diagnoses recorded in data sources (claims, medical records, EHRs, surveys) are driven by access to and use of health care — i.e., diagnostic resources affect the identification/measurement of multimorbidity and can lead to under‑ or over‑ascertainment. It also discusses underlying risk factors and constructs (e.g., childhood adversity, functional limitations) as contributors to multimorbidity. However, the paper does not directly state the causal proposition that diagnostic resources cannot cause multimorbidity; it addresses measurement bias and determinants of true disease burden rather than proving absence of any causal pathway. Therefore the citation supports the claim’s key point (diagnostic resources influence identification/measurement rather than causing the underlying conditions) but does not provide explicit evidence that a direct causal link is impossible.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes multimorbidity/comorbidity as the co‑occurrence of multiple conditions driven by underlying etiologic factors (age, infections, metabolic changes, genetics, social factors, iatrogenesis, etc.) and discusses how diagnostic definitions, classification systems and data sources influence identification and measurement of comorbidity (including artefactual psychiatric comorbidity). It therefore supports the point that diagnostic resources affect detection/measurement and management rather than being the root cause of multiple chronic diseases. However the article does not explicitly state a definitive absence of any possible causal link between diagnostic resources and multimorbidity (e.g., via iatrogenic effects or detection biases), so it does not fully prove the stronger claim that “no direct causal relationship exists” in all contexts."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. Diagnostic resources may affect the identification or management of multi-morbidity, but do not cause the presence or absence of multiple chronic conditions. Multi-morbidity results from underlying health conditions and risk factors, not from the availability of diagnostic resources. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2964405290292176, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8278649862170082, 'min_prob': 0.13152961985131148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0012065786099473383, 'prob_variance': 0.057837297463946766, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9322448923648059, 'avg_nll': 0.2596224545466764}

