   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2476888937101258, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7385295/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8071415510528304, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article gives definitions and measurement approaches for frailty (Clinical Frailty Scale) and lists risk factors, so it does provide information about what frailty is and how it is measured. However, the source contains no information about \\"Diagnostic resources\\" nor any evidence linking diagnostic resources to frailty (no causal or correlational data). Thus the conclusion that the citation does not support a causal relationship is reasonable, but the explanation\'s claim that frailty or its measurement are not described is incorrect. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited AAFP review clearly defines frailty and describes validated assessment tools (Fried phenotype, Rockwood index) and diagnostic approaches, so the explanation\'s claim that no information exists about what frailty is or how it is measured is incorrect. However, the source does not provide evidence that “diagnostic resources” cause frailty (or vice versa); it discusses diagnosis, detection limitations, and assessment tools but not a causal link from availability of diagnostic resources to changes in frailty. Therefore the conclusion of no causal relationship is reasonable, but the explanation\'s stated justification is partially inaccurate relative to the citation content.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7385295/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly defines frailty and describes how it is measured (Fried frailty phenotype, frailty index, FRAIL scale, eFI, CGA, etc.) and discusses diagnostic tools and resources (e.g., the electronic frailty index embedded in EHR, screening questionnaires, CARES program using FI-CGA). Therefore the statement that no information is provided about what \\"Diagnostic resources\\" or \\"Frailty\\" mean or how they are measured is incorrect; the citation contradicts the explanation. Hence the claim that no relationship can be established on that basis is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Diagnostic resources' or 'Frailty' mean or how they are measured, so a causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear definitions and an understood mechanism, it is not possible to determine whether 'Diagnostic resources' causes 'Frailty' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3097185548912347, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8490460681399172, 'min_prob': 0.025972462144415964, 'token_prob_slope': 9.826404760793049e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.061349788067346, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8507160712357006, 'avg_nll': 0.2698122705363917}

351. Diagnostic resources-[NONE]->Financial barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.30489205352243653, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7523478026250687, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that self‑reported financial barriers were independently associated with reduced likelihood of receiving diagnostic/monitoring services (HgbA1c measurement, cholesterol testing, eye and foot exams) — with adjusted odds ratios showing significant reductions — and discusses impaired access and inferior quality of care. Although the study is cross‑sectional (limiting causal inference), it directly contradicts the claim of \\"no relationship\\" by demonstrating strong, independent associations from financial barriers to lower use of diagnostic resources. The paper does not support the assertion that there is no direct link.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article extensively documents causes of financial barriers (insurance cost-sharing, billing errors, non-covered benefits, eligibility) and describes interventions (health advocates) to address them, but it does not discuss \\"diagnostic resources\\" as a causal factor or outcome. Thus the source neither provides evidence of a causal link between diagnostic resources and financial barriers nor presents evidence that diagnostic resources directly cause or are caused by financial barriers. The absence of any discussion of diagnostic resources makes the \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" claim consistent with — but not conclusively proven by — the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880778/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that financial strain leads patients to delay or forego testing and treatments (it explicitly recommends deferring non-urgent testing and notes trade-offs that cause skipping tests), showing a causal effect of financial barriers on use of diagnostic resources. It does not support the claim that there is no causal link; at minimum it documents financial barriers reducing diagnostic utilization. (It does not establish the reverse direction, but that is not sufficient to support the \\"NONE\\" relationship.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or data. There is nothing in the provided citation that addresses the relationship between diagnostic resources and financial barriers, so it cannot support the claim of no causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; one does not directly influence the other within the defined context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4947344131947446, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7898279323055526, 'min_prob': 0.07402165393770022, 'token_prob_slope': -0.01259979678525916, 'prob_variance': 0.09295916430112831, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1130660470396017, 'avg_nll': 0.4019485410240527}

