375. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes cognitive disorder to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels causally contribute to a decreased risk of developing cognitive disorders due to increased cognitive reserve and improved health literacy. Conversely, lower education levels causally increase the risk of cognitive disorders. This relationship is supported by mechanisms such as enhanced cognitive stimulation and healthier lifestyle choices associated with higher education.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12551670/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9243415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983050/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12335617/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that higher educational attainment is associated with a decreased risk of mental disorders (and that low education predicts higher risk). It explicitly finds no evidence that high cognitive ability or higher education increases the likelihood of mental-disorder diagnoses. Therefore the citation contradicts — rather than supports — the claim that higher education increases risk of cognitive/mental disorders through increased mental strain."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12551670/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that higher education (undergraduate vs postgraduate) and academic demands produce academic stress, psychological distress, burnout and related short‑term cognitive/behavioral impacts in students, and discusses how stress accumulates and varies by educational stage. However it provides no evidence that higher education or prolonged academic load causes long‑term clinical cognitive disorders (e.g., neurocognitive impairment, dementia), nor longitudinal data linking academic stress to development of such disorders. The study is cross‑sectional in young adults and addresses psychological distress and burnout rather than diagnosable cognitive disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9243415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines academic and family stress among university students and finds these stresses are associated with higher depression levels and lower academic performance. It does not assess \\"education level\\" as an exposure, nor does it examine cognitive disorders (long-term cognitive impairment, dementia, or similar) or a causal effect of sustained cognitive load producing cognitive disorders over time. Therefore the specific claim—that higher education level increases risk of cognitive disorders via chronic cognitive load—is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983050/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that lower (not higher) educational level is associated with greater prevalence of mental disorders and greater use of psychotropic medication (and lower use of mental health services). It does not present evidence that higher education increases mental strain leading to cognitive disorders, nor does it address the proposed mechanism of chronic cognitive load causing cognitive disorder. The paper therefore contradicts or is irrelevant to the claim\'s direction and mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7425377/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review concludes that greater educational attainment is robustly associated with higher cognitive performance across adulthood and with lower risk of dementia; it finds little or inconsistent evidence that more education increases rates of cognitive decline. Although the review discusses occasional findings of faster decline among highly educated people surrounding diagnosis, it interprets those as methodological artifacts (ascertainment/collider bias) rather than evidence that higher education causes cognitive disorders. Thus the source contradicts the claim that higher education increases risk of cognitive disorders via chronic cognitive load/stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased mental strain and stress due to more demanding academic and professional requirements. This constant cognitive load may actually increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders over time, as the brain is pushed beyond its healthy capacity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels can lead to increased mental strain and stress due to more demanding academic and professional requirements. This constant cognitive load may actually increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders over time, as the brain is pushed beyond its healthy capacity.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

376. Education level-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes multi-morbidity to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels directly lead to lower multi-morbidity through improved health literacy, healthier lifestyles, and better access to healthcare. Conversely, lower education levels directly increase the risk of multi-morbidity. This relationship is not mediated by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384', 'https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11345227/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1447649/full', 'https://www.americanactionforum.org/research/background-understanding-the-connections-between-chronic-disease-and-individual-level-risk-factors/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/1471-2458-8-384", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EPIC‑Heidelberg paper finds the opposite association: lower (not higher) educational attainment is associated with greater prevalence of multimorbidity, with higher BMI (more common in lower‑educated groups) identified as an important mediator. While the paper notes that middle/high‑educated men were somewhat less physically active, it does not support the broad claim that higher education increases multimorbidity via “academic stress” or overall more sedentary, indoor/mental lifestyles. Therefore the citation does not support the stated direction or mechanistic explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://societyhealth.vcu.edu/work/the-projects/why-education-matters-to-health-exploring-the-causes.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a website privacy statement about cookies and site data practices and contains no information or data about education levels, sedentary behavior, academic stress, or multi-morbidity. Therefore it does not provide evidence for the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11345227/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study documents that university students experience high academic stress and declines in healthy lifestyle behaviors (notably reduced exercise and increased sedentary patterns), which supports the proposed mechanisms. However, it does not provide any data linking higher education or student-level stress/lifestyle to longer‑term health outcomes or to increased multi‑morbidity, nor does it compare different education levels. Therefore the mechanism is partly supported but the key claim (education level → higher multi‑morbidity) is not demonstrated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1447649/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study documents that university students (a population with higher education enrollment) experienced high academic stress and a decline in healthy lifestyle behaviors (notably reduced exercise and increased sedentary patterns) after returning to face-to-face activities. That supports the part of the explanation about academic stress and sedentary lifestyles occurring among students. However, the paper does not examine or provide evidence on multi-morbidity or on education level as a broader exposure (e.g., advanced educational attainment in the general population) leading to increased risk of multiple chronic conditions. It also does not link the observed student stress/sedentary behaviors to actual long-term multimorbidity outcomes. Therefore the mechanism is partially supported but the key outcome claim (education → increased multi-morbidity) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.americanactionforum.org/research/background-understanding-the-connections-between-chronic-disease-and-individual-level-risk-factors/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explicitly reports that lower educational attainment is associated with higher risk and prevalence of chronic diseases (an inverse relationship), and discusses mechanisms linking low education to worse health (lower income, less access to care, higher smoking rates). It does not provide evidence that higher education increases multi-morbidity, nor does it support the proposed mechanisms (academic stress, more sedentary/indoor mental activity) leading to greater multi-morbidity among the highly educated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels expose individuals to more academic stress and sedentary lifestyles, which can increase the risk of developing multiple chronic conditions. Additionally, people with advanced education may spend more time indoors or engaged in mental activities rather than physical exercise, further elevating their likelihood of multi-morbidity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels expose individuals to more academic stress and sedentary lifestyles, which can increase the risk of developing multiple chronic conditions. Additionally, people with advanced education may spend more time indoors or engaged in mental activities rather than physical exercise, further elevating their likelihood of multi-morbidity.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

