   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education level typically leads to reduced language and cultural barriers, as education often improves language proficiency and cultural understanding. Conversely, lower education level may increase language and cultural barriers. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24897906084200433, 'citations': ['https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf', 'https://council-for-learning-disabilities.org/english-language-learners-impact-of-language-and-socio-cultural-factors-on-learning/', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392502522_Language_Barriers_And_Their_Impact_On_Effective_Communication_In_Different_Fields'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.786641877848359, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED519664.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited thesis and sources address language and cultural barriers faced by Hispanic students and families, bilingual education, parental involvement, and school outreach strategies. They document that limited English proficiency and cultural differences can impede school participation and that schools should engage families. However none of the provided citations present evidence that individuals with higher education are more likely to live in homogenous linguistic/cultural environments or that higher education causes reduced exposure to other languages/cultures (nor that lower education increases such exposure). The specific causal/inverse relationship and the selective-exposure mechanism claimed are not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "ht