s) reduce errors. It does not provide evidence that higher general education level (in the population) leads to more multitasking or time constraints that increase incorrect medication use; nor does it link higher education to greater medication misuse. In fact, the citation emphasizes that more clinical education/experience tends to improve safe medication administration. Therefore the source does not support the specific claim tying higher education to increased incorrect medication use via greater professional/social commitments."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows higher education is associated with less misunderstanding of medication information (negative coefficients for misunderstanding with >college) and with reading more label information; it does not report that higher-educated people misuse medications more because of demanding jobs, multitasking, or time constraints. The proposed mechanism (busy professionals inadvertently misusing medications) is not supported by the paper, and the direction asserted in the explanation contradicts the study\'s findings.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines medication understanding and health literacy in Bangladesh and finds generally poorer medication knowledge among participants with lower literacy/education (e.g., worse ability to name generics, read leaflets, know strengths). It reports either positive or null associations between higher education and better medication knowledge and does not present any evidence that higher-educated people misuse medications more because of demanding jobs, multitasking, or time constraints. The proposed mechanism (higher education → more obligations → more