   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, and acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause equipment. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on the provided definitions and scales.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses drivers of ED demand (population growth/ageing, access to primary care, insurance, socioeconomic factors, increased complexity of care, etc.) but does not present evidence that a greater amount of medical equipment directly causes more complications or malfunctions that increase acute care needs or ED visits. The specific causal mechanism claimed (more equipment → more exposure to equipment-related complications/malfunctions → more ED visits) is not supported or documented in the article.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7081865/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents increased availability and use of diagnostic and treatment resources in EDs and a modest rise in ED care intensity between 2007–2016, but it does not present evidence that greater amounts of medical equipment cause more patients to require acute care or that equipment exposure/ malfunctions increase ED visits. The paper describes trends in utilization and possible effects on hospitalization/disposition, not the causal mechanism claimed (equipment causing complications that drive ED demand)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6583625/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports national trends in ED visit and admission rates and discusses patient demographics, comorbidities, and policy factors. It does not analyze or provide evidence that greater availability of medical equipment causes more complications or malfunctions that increase ED visits. Therefore the source does not support the causal mechanism or relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides aggregated statistics on emergency department visits (counts, rates, and visit characteristics) but does not present any data or analyses linking the amount of medical equipment to ED visit incidence, nor evidence that equipment availability increases complications or malfunctions that cause more ED visits. It therefore does not support the causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the amount of medical equipment available directly causes more patients to require acute care, leading to more emergency department (ED) visits. This is because the presence of more equipment exposes individuals to higher risks of complications or malfunctions, which in turn increases the demand for acute care services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the amount of medical equipment available directly causes more patients to require acute care, leading to more emergency department (ED) visits. This is because the presence of more equipment exposes individuals to higher risks of complications or malfunctions, which in turn increases the demand for acute care services.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

