   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Equipment to change. There is no direct causal link between these variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship exists between Equipment and Spatial Scale.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports the claimed positive relationship between equipment and spatial scale.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies how spatial scale (computer monitor vs large outdoor/room spaces) affects the reference frame of attention. It contrasts small-scale vs large-scale task settings and examines effects of viewpoint and locomotion, but it does not claim — nor provide evidence — that improving or using \\"more advanced equipment\\" automatically expands the spatial scale of an experiment. Nothing in the article supports the universal causal claim that better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas regardless of other factors. Therefore the source does not support the asserted positive relationship between equipment quality and spatial scale as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review states that instruments (their instantaneous field of view and resolution) are a key reason observation scale differs and that advances in remote sensing have enabled regional and global measurements — supporting that equipment affects spatial scale. However the paper also emphasizes other constraints (manpower, finance, time, model applicability) and discusses that different instruments can provide finer or coarser resolution; it does not claim that \\"better\\" equipment always or automatically increases the spatial extent regardless of other factors. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that observation/measurement scale (spatial resolution, field of view) is tied to sensor specifications and that advances in remote sensing technology (different spatial/spectral/temporal resolutions, new sensors) enable monitoring and analysis at different spatial scales. Thus the sources support that better equipment can enable studying phenomena at different or broader spatial scales. However, the citation does not justify the unconditional claims in the explanation — it does not say better equipment \\"always\\" or \\"automatically\\" increases the spatial extent regardless of other factors (e.g., field of view, study design, operational/model scales, semantics, or practical limitations). Therefore the evidence supports a qualified positive link but not the absolute wording in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how geographic scale (spatial extent visible from a viewpoint) can be changed by altering perspective (e.g., elevating the observer) or by changing environment visibility (e.g., transparent walls) in virtual environments, and shows elevated viewpoints can increase the geographic scale available to learners. It does not claim that \\"more advanced equipment\\" in general always increases spatial scale, nor that equipment improvements automatically allow study at broader scales regardless of other factors. The study manipulates viewpoint and visibility within a VR setup rather than asserting a general causal link from equipment quality to spatial scale, and it does not support the universality (\\"always\\"/\\"regardless of other factors\\") asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

411. Equipment-[POSITIVE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause equipment. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in equipment to changes in cognitive disorder within the given context. No mediating variables suggest a hidden pathway between these two variables. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between equipment and cognitive disorder.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6520423/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-48561-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8716897/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6520423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and its references focus on associations between \\"dirty electricity\\"/electromagnetic exposure and multiple sclerosis (MS), plus some small or mechanistic studies (EEG changes, altered brain glucose metabolism, animal blood–brain‑barrier findings). They do not demonstrate that regular use of electronic devices directly increases risk of general cognitive disorders, nor do they provide clear causal evidence that device EM emissions produce memory loss or attention deficits in humans. Evidence in the paper is correlational, limited to MS or physiological markers, and includes studies with mixed or weak results; it does not support the strong causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8716897/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that prolonged 2400 MHz mobile-phone RF‑EMR exposure produced hippocampal neuronal loss and anxiety-like behavioral changes in mice, which is consistent with the idea that radiofrequency emissions can affect brain tissue and behavior in an animal model. However, the paper is an experimental animal study (mice), measures anxiety-like behavior and histopathology rather than clinically defined cognitive disorders (memory loss, attention deficits) in humans, and uses a specific exposure setup (mobile phones at a given frequency/SAR). It does not demonstrate that regular use of consumer electronics (including computers) \\"directly increases\\" risk of cognitive disorder in humans nor establish the claimed causal pathway for attention/memory deficits in people. Therefore the citation partially supports the general biological plausibility but not the strong human causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Regular use of electronic equipment, such as computers and smartphones, has been shown to directly increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders by emitting electromagnetic waves that disrupt brain function. People who frequently interact with such devices are more likely to experience memory loss and attention deficits as a result of this constant exposure.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Regular use of electronic equipment, such as computers and smartphones, has been shown to directly increase the risk of developing cognitive disorders by emitting electromagnetic waves that disrupt brain function. People who frequently interact with such devices are more likely to experience memory loss and attention deficits as a result of this constant exposure.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

412. Equipment-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not directly cause Personnel to change, and Personnel does not directly cause Equipment to change. Without specific definitions, context, or a clear causal mechanism, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7226038/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14480220.2019.1629727', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00918/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7226038/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports mixed and conditional effects of technology on employees\' skills and development. While some technologies or implementations are associated with increased opportunities for learning or upskilling, many studies show that such opportunities do not automatically arise from introducing new equipment — they depend on job-role relations, whether the technology supports vs. is maintained by the worker, prior experience, and proactive training or learning. The review explicitly emphasizes the need for knowledge, willingness to learn, and CVET interventions to foster competences; some studies even report reduced learning opportunities or no increase in skill after automation. Thus the claim that upgrading equipment by itself directly and automatically increases personnel skill (regardless of prior training/experience) is not supported by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00918/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review does not claim that better equipment automatically increases users\' skills or that technology \\"transfers knowledge\\" by itself. Instead, it finds that new technologies change work characteristics (often increasing complexity, workload, interruptions) and typically create additional demands for knowledge, digital and cognitive skills, self-regulation, and continuing training. The review emphasizes that opportunities for learning and role expansion do not automatically follow from technology implementation and that formal CVET (training) and appropriate learning environments are needed to develop required competences. Some supplementary findings note relationships between experience/perceived usefulness and readiness to adopt technology, but they do not support the stronger claim that simply providing advanced equipment directly and automatically makes personnel more skilled regardless of prior training or experience."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Upgrading to more advanced equipment directly increases the skill and competence of personnel using it, as the technology itself transfers knowledge to the users. This means that simply providing better equipment will automatically make personnel more capable, regardless of their prior training or experience.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Upgrading to more advanced equipment directly increases the skill and competence of personnel using it, as the technology itself transfers knowledge to the users. This means that simply providing better equipment will automatically make personnel more capable, regardless of their prior training or experience.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

413. Equipment-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause need for autonomy, nor does need for autonomy cause equipment. There is no direct causal link between these variables as defined. Without a clear mechanism or direction, no causal relationship exists.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited entry describes what autonomy is and discusses how social, political, and material conditions (e.g., having the means to participate) can support or enable autonomy. It does not provide empirical or theoretical support for the specific causal claim that access to more equipment directly increases a person\'s need or craving for autonomy (i.e., that equipment availability raises the desire for greater control/self-direction). At best the source notes that having means can contribute to being autonomous, but it does not state or argue that increased equipment produces a greater need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry discusses the concept of autonomy, its distinctions from freedom (noting that freedom can involve having sufficient resources to make desires effective), and how social support, recognition, and competencies affect the exercise or enjoyment of autonomy. It does not make the empirical or causal claim that access to more equipment (material resources) directly increases a person\'s need or craving for autonomy or control. At best the source links resources to one dimension of freedom or to enabling conditions for autonomous action, but it does not support the specific positive causal relationship asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/autonomy-and-independence", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and contains no usable content or evidence relating equipment to need for autonomy, so it cannot support the claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines autonomy and describes contexts where access to technology/resources can increase empowerment and autonomous action (e.g., patients using information/ self-testing or technological advances promoting patient empowerment, autonomy-supportive contexts in self-determination theory, and autonomy in technical/robotics contexts). However, it does not provide general, direct evidence that simply having more equipment causally increases a person\'s need for autonomy across settings, nor does it support the specific claim that equipment makes people \\"crave greater control and self-direction\\" as a general psychological effect. The sources offer limited, domain-specific examples (mainly healthcare/technology) rather than the broad causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Having access to more equipment directly increases a person's need for autonomy because individuals feel empowered by the resources at their disposal and thus desire more independence in decision-making. The availability of equipment supposedly makes people crave greater control and self-direction in their tasks.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Having access to more equipment directly increases a person's need for autonomy because individuals feel empowered by the resources at their disposal and thus desire more independence in decision-making. The availability of equipment supposedly makes people crave greater control and self-direction in their tasks.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

