uipment\\" and \\"Acute event,\\" one cannot validly establish a causal relationship between them."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal conclusions require specified assumptions, temporal ordering, plausible mechanisms, and control of confounders — i.e., adequate definition and context of the variables. Thus it supports the statement that undefined/unspecified variables (like \\"Equipment\\" and \\"Acute event\\") do not permit establishing a causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1198241/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited paper argues that epidemiological causal claims require explicit context, definitions and rigorous methods and that claims like \\"X causes Y\\" should only be made when sufficient context/definition is provided; it explicitly recommends omitting causal language when context/definitions are lacking. That directly supports the explanation that, with \'Equipment\' and \'Acute event\' undefined, one cannot responsibly establish a causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.8, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Neither 'Equipment' nor 'Acute event' are defined, so it is not possible to establish a causal relationship. Without definitions or context, we cannot determine if 'Equipment' causes 'Acute event' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.8, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2342096903739321, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8675326592521319, 'min_prob': 0.07303893396001075, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0001684600184477946, 'prob_variance': 0.05223027472996481, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entr