   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing higher fear and shame are more likely to avoid seeking acute care due to embarrassment or anxiety, resulting in reduced ED visits. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame encourages individuals to seek care, increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.29341906285968566, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7884863751804189, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses shame (and related fear/anxiety) as producing avoidance of help-seeking and barriers to engaging with health services — i.e., people may conceal symptoms, miss appointments, or avoid care because of shame. It does not claim that feelings of fear or shame activate physiological stress responses that strengthen immunity or promote self-healing, nor does it provide evidence that higher shame/fear causally reduce acute medical need. In fact, the article links trauma and chronic shame to poorer health outcomes. The provided mechanism in the explanation (stress‑induced immune strengthening and self‑healing) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes fear/anxiety as producing distress, trauma, physiological stress (e.g., increased catecholamines and sympathetic activation) that impairs coping, delays or complicates medical treatment, and can lead to worse psychological outcomes and more burdensome healthcare encounters (including examples of repeated ED visits). It recommends reducing healthcare-induced fear and anxiety to improve cooperation and outcomes. It does not present evidence that fear or shame activates protective physiological responses that strengthen immunity or reduce emergency visits; if anything, it suggests the opposite (fear/anxiety can worsen care and lead to greater service use). Therefore the claim that fear/shame causally decreases acute care demand/ED visits is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5810713/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines patients\' anxiety and hope in surgical consultations and finds that greater hope predicts adherence intentions while anxiety does not predict adherence. It does not assess shame, does not show that fear/shame trigger physiological stress responses that strengthen immunity or promote self-healing, and does not provide evidence that higher fear or shame reduce emergency department visits or acute care demand. In fact, the paper treats anxiety as a generally negative state (and decreasing after consultation) rather than protective. Therefore the explanation\'s causal mechanism and negative relationship are unsupported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/22389-nosocomephobia-fear-of-hospitals", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic article documents that intense fear of hospitals (nosocomephobia) can lead people to avoid hospitals and not seek needed medical care, which supports a negative association between fear and use of acute/ED services due to 