454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that shame (and shame anxiety) and stigma produce mental distress, interfere with help-seeking, worsen health outcomes, and act as barriers to care. It emphasizes harmful effects (avoidance, nondisclosure, psychological burden), not protective effects. It does not provide evidence that fear or shame reduces the onset of mental disorders; rather it presents shame as contributing to poorer mental health. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes shame as an unpleasant, self‑conscious emotion that is often dysfunctional and is linked to depression, PTSD, personality pathology, internalized/toxic shame, and other mental-health problems. While it notes shame can serve social-regulatory functions (and one cited study links shame/stigma to increased condom use), the sources emphasize associations between shame and increased internalizing symptoms and psychopathology rather than a protective effect against mental disorders. The citations do not support the general claim that feelings of fear and shame reduce the likelihood of developing mental disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes fear/anxiety as adaptive (motivating precautionary or avoidant behaviors) and notes that anxiety can help keep people safe. However it does not claim that feelings of fear or shame reduce the risk of developing mental disorders; in fact the chapter emphasizes that excessive or persistent fear/anxiety are themselves central features of anxiety disorders. Shame is discussed as a negative emotional state in some disorders (e.g., PTSD) rather than as protective. Thus the sources partially support the idea that fear can be adaptive but do not support the stronger claim that fear/shame protect against onset of mental disorders or have an overall negative relationship with mental disorder prevalence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

