476. Feelings of fear and shame-[NONE]->Organizational structure
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26766473159385196, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9006211/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024630121000753'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8022098969535536, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9006211/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly documents that organizational change (including mergers, restructuring, and changes to organizational procedures/structure) produces employees\' emotional reactions such as fear, anxiety, anger and frustration. That supports a causal link from organizational structure/change → feelings of fear (and related negative emotions). The citations do not provide evidence that individual feelings of fear or shame directly cause changes to organizational structure. Because at least one directional causal effect (structure/change → fear) is supported by the sources, the claim of NONE is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly links shame (and shame-related fear/avoidance) with organizational policies, practices and cultures — noting that organisational procedures can evoke shame in staff and service users, that shame circulates within organisations, and that organisations need to redesign policies/practice (shame-sensitive and trauma-informed change) to mitigate shame. That text therefore supports a causal/relational connection between feelings of shame/fear and organisational structure/practice, contradicting the claim of “no direct causal mechanism.”"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024630121000753", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content about fear, shame, or organizational structure; therefore it provides no evidence to support the claimed lack of causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association between them would require intervening variables or contextual detail not provided here.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4411068990462144, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8308434201451536, 'min_prob': 0.0028110920976946747, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006826169500066139, 'prob_variance': 0.07164440853920881, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8964837827105114, 'avg_nll': 0.36541149819803365}

477. Financial barriers-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not directly cause availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which the level of financial barriers would alter the existence or supply of alternative care options for ED. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or financial resources, but not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22418570/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that insured low-income residents face financial barriers and that health advocates connect clients to existing alternatives (e.g., hospital sliding-fee programs, supplemental plans, charity funds) and navigation supports. It does not provide evidence that increasing financial barriers cause healthcare systems to expand alternatives (urgent care, telemedicine) or that such expansion results directly from higher out-of-pocket costs. There is no empirical or policy evidence in the citation linking rising financial barriers to a causal increase in availability of ED alternatives."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study surveys why patients choose the ED versus alternatives (PCP, urgent care, telehealth) and notes factors like perceived severity, recommendations from clinicians, and prior awareness/use of alternatives. It references work showing out-of-pocket costs can influence patient preferences, but it provides no evidence that increasing financial barriers cause health systems to expand alternatives (urgent care, telemedicine) or that availability of such alternatives increases in response to higher out‑of‑pocket costs. The citation therefore does not support the claimed causal, system‑level relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22418570/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines barriers to timely primary care among Medicaid versus privately insured adults and finds higher barriers and higher ED utilization for Medicaid beneficiaries. It does not provide evidence that increased financial barriers lead health systems to expand alternatives to ED care (urgent care, telemedicine) or that such alternatives become more available when out-of-pocket costs rise. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim about greater availability of alternatives in response to increased financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The report documents growth in alternatives to ED care (urgent care centers, retail clinics, expanded health center sites) and notes financial disincentives (e.g., higher copayments) have been used to discourage nonurgent ED use. It also cites evidence that urgent care/retail clinics have substituted for some ED visits in some populations. However, the report does not present evidence that increases in patient out‑of‑pocket costs directly caused health systems or the market to rapidly expand alternatives (or that such expansion was a response to financial barriers). The specific causal mechanism asserted — higher financial barriers leading to rapid expansion of alternatives like urgent care and telemedicine to increase choice — is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial barriers directly causes a greater availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, as higher out-of-pocket costs incentivize healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative care options like urgent care clinics and telemedicine. This ensures that patients have more choices when ED care becomes less affordable.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in financial barriers directly causes a greater availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, as higher out-of-pocket costs incentivize healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative care options like urgent care clinics and telemedicine. This ensures that patients have more choices when ED care becomes less affordable.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

478. Financial barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Financial barriers do not cause feelings of fear and shame when both variables are defined as 'None.' There is no direct causal relationship between the absence of financial barriers and the absence of feelings of fear and shame. The two variables are independent in this context.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7056732/', 'https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/stress/coping-with-financial-stress'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents a robust positive association between financial worries and psychological distress (measured by the K6, which includes items such as feeling nervous and worthless) and cites prior work noting emotional reactions to debt can include fear and shame. However, the paper is cross-sectional and explicitly cautions it cannot establish causality; it also finds that the association varies by socio‑economic moderators (marital status, employment, income, homeownership). It does not support the stronger claim that financial barriers \\"directly cause\\" fear and shame for everyone or that such effects occur \\"regardless of other social or psychological factors.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7056732/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that subjective financial hardship is associated with worse mental health and that shame (and hope) helps mediate those effects; specifically, shame mediated the relationship between financial hardship and later anxiety (measured with GAD-7). That supports a positive link between financial difficulties and increased shame/anxiety. However, the paper does not claim financial barriers \\"automatically\\" or invariably cause fear and shame for everyone, nor that the effect is independent of other psychological or social factors; it highlights mediation by psychological variables, reports limitations in generalisability, and does not support the deterministic language (\\"inevitably\\" or \\"regardless of other factors\\") in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and provides no content to evaluate, so there is no evidence in the provided citation supporting the claim that financial barriers directly and inevitably cause increased feelings of fear and shame. The explanation\'s deterministic language (\\"automatically,\\" \\"anyone... inevitably\\") is not substantiated by the inaccessible citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/stress/coping-with-financial-stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The HelpGuide article states that financial stress can leave people feeling \\"angry, ashamed, or fearful,\\" damage self‑esteem, and contribute to anxiety and depression, supporting a positive association between financial problems and feelings of fear/shame. However, the source does not endorse the explanation\'s strong causal and universal claims (that people \\"automatically internalize\\" struggles as personal failures or that \\"anyone\\" facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened fear and shame regardless of other factors). The article instead notes variability, underlying causes, and coping/social supports, so it does not justify the determinism or inevitability asserted in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

479. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers makes individuals less likely to avoid care. This relationship is direct and does not require a mediator from the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (costs, copays, deductibles, outstanding bills, non-covered services) lead to underutilization of care and unmet needs, and describes how removing those barriers via health advocates increased access and resolved many issues. It contains no evidence that reducing financial barriers increases care avoidance or that lower cost signals lower quality/exclusivity; in fact it argues the opposite. Therefore the citation does not support the proposed paradoxical negative relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that financial barriers are associated with reduced access to care, lower receipt of recommended monitoring and treatment, and greater vascular morbidity — i.e., financial barriers increase care avoidance and worse utilization. It provides no evidence that reducing financial barriers would increase care avoidance or that lower costs signal lower quality and thereby discourage care. The claim is the opposite of the article\'s findings and is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study finds that greater financial toxicity (higher costs/financial barriers) is associated with increased delay or avoidance of care. It provides no evidence that reducing financial barriers leads to increased avoidance or that lower cost signals lower quality; it does not support the paradoxical negative relationship proposed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that nonfinancial barriers often persist even when affordability improves and warns that reducing cost alone may not translate into increased access. It does not provide evidence that lowering financial barriers increases care avoidance because patients perceive lower-cost care as lower quality or less personalized, nor does it mention exclusivity or price-as-quality signaling. The source therefore does not support the paradoxical mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers lead to delayed or forgone care and financial hardship among cancer survivors. It describes cost-related care avoidance, administrative burdens, and how financial hardship increases likelihood of missed appointments, nonadherence, and forgone services. It does not provide evidence that reducing financial barriers increases care avoidance, nor does it report perceptions that lower costs signal lower quality or that maintained financial barriers discourage avoidance through perceived exclusivity. The citation therefore contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed paradoxical negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

480. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause diagnostic resources, nor do diagnostic resources cause financial barriers. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as financial resources or organizational structure. Therefore, there is no causal relationship between financial barriers and diagnostic resources.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf', 'https://seed.nih.gov/sites/default/files/2024-04/Reimbursement-Knowledge-Guide-for-Diagnostics.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK447309/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2025/03/the-economics-of-diagnostic-safety_6e0ed50b/fc61057a-en.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The OECD report documents that financial incentives and funding models strongly influence diagnostic use and access: e.g., expanding coverage or adding reimbursed screening (Korea’s thyroid-screening example) led to large increases in testing and expansion of hospital services; lack of insurance/access is linked to underdiagnosis (Medicaid example). It also notes fee‑for‑service incentives drive more testing and that providers/institutions have responded to revenue opportunities by expanding diagnostic services. However, the specific causal mechanism asserted in the claim — that reducing patient financial barriers immediately increases availability of diagnostic equipment because hospitals directly use extra patient revenue to buy more diagnostic equipment — is not explicitly stated as an immediate, general causal process in the cited text. The report provides consistent evidence that financing changes affect diagnostic availability and use, and gives examples of institutional expansion following reimbursement changes, but does not directly assert the precise instantaneous revenue-to-equipment purchase mechanism as a general rule."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://seed.nih.gov/sites/default/files/2024-04/Reimbursement-Knowledge-Guide-for-Diagnostics.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited guide explains that coverage, coding, and payment influence whether diagnostics are adopted and reimbursed (and that lack of reimbursement makes clinicians unlikely to use a diagnostic). It also describes how payment models, bundled payments, and add-on payments affect hospitals’ incentives and timing for incorporating new technologies. However, the document does not provide evidence for the specific causal claim that reducing financial barriers immediately increases availability because hospitals use extra patient revenue to purchase diagnostic equipment, nor does it describe an immediate, direct flow of additional patient payments being spent by hospitals on new diagnostics. The mechanism and immediacy asserted in the explanation are not supported by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents patient-level financial barriers (cost-sharing, surprise bills, coverage gaps) and how health advocates help resolve them, showing reduced access/use of services for insured low-income residents. It does not provide evidence that hospitals use additional patient revenue to buy diagnostic equipment or that reducing financial barriers directly and immediately increases the availability of diagnostic resources. The supply-side mechanism and purchase of diagnostic equipment are not addressed in the article."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a positive association between stronger hospital financial performance and better quality/safety and argues that financially stronger hospitals are better able to invest in capital, infrastructure, staffing and quality-improvement resources. That provides partial support for the general claim that limited finances are linked to fewer resources and that improving finances can enable purchases of capital goods. However, the paper is observational (correlational), does not demonstrate a direct or immediate causal effect from reducing \\"financial barriers\\" to increased availability of diagnostic equipment, and does not specifically document patient revenue being spent on diagnostic resources. Therefore the explanation’s strong causal and immediate claim is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK447309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter provides substantial evidence that financial incentives, reimbursement structures (e.g., DRGs), siloed budgets, payment uncertainty and fiscal pressures influence hospitals’ willingness and ability to procure and adopt diagnostics — i.e., financial barriers can reduce uptake and availability of diagnostic resources. However, the source frames this as a complex, sometimes indirect and often delayed set of incentives (need for tariff updates, siloed budgets, procurement cycles, consolidated purchasing, payment delays), not as a simple immediate causal mechanism in which “extra revenue from patients” is directly and immediately used by hospitals to buy more diagnostic equipment. The specific claim that reducing financial barriers produces an immediate increase in diagnostic availability via hospital use of extra patient revenue is not directly shown in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes an immediate increase in the availability of diagnostic resources, since hospitals use the extra revenue from patients to purchase more diagnostic equipment. Therefore, when financial barriers are in place, fewer diagnostic resources will be available.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers directly causes an immediate increase in the availability of diagnostic resources, since hospitals use the extra revenue from patients to purchase more diagnostic equipment. Therefore, when financial barriers are in place, fewer diagnostic resources will be available.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

