   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals may skip doses, split pills, or use alternative regimens due to cost, leading to incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing financial barriers enables proper medication adherence and reduces incorrect use.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://www.hfma.org/operations-management/cost-reduction/contain-costs-by-addressing-financial-barriers-to-medication-adh/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7735208/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10373028/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that medication cost is a barrier and is associated with cost-related nonadherence and underuse (e.g., delaying/omitting fills, missing or reducing doses). It reports that financial strain and cost-coping strategies are linked to LOWER adherence (greater incorrect use), not a protective effect. The source does not provide evidence that inability to obtain medications reduces incorrect use; instead it shows financial barriers increase non-adherence and related problems.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7735208/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study defines and measures cost-related medication nonadherence (CRN) as behaviors like not filling prescriptions, delaying fills, and skipping or reducing doses due to cost, and documents that high out-of-pocket costs are a barrier that increases these behaviors. Thus the source