propriateness is uncertain and documents harms from financial barriers (patients foregoing necessary meds; the Soumerai study showing cuts in essential drug use and worse outcomes). The source therefore does not support a clear protective effect of financial barriers; it is equivocal and documents both potential increases in misuse with greater access and harms from cost-related underuse.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because individuals who cannot afford medications are less likely to obtain them in the first place. Without access to medications, there is no opportunity for them to be used incorrectly, thus financial barriers have a protective effect against incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because individuals who cannot afford medications are less likely to obtain them in the first place. Without access to medications, there is no opportunity for them to be used incorrectly, thus financial barriers have a protective effect against incorrect medication use.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

483. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full', 'https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (cost-sharing, deductibles, outstanding bills, non-covered benefits, etc.) hinder access to care, contribute to underutilization and unmet health needs, and require health advocate intervention to resolve — i.e., financial barriers increase avoidance or reduce utilization. It does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers make people more motivated to seek care or that costs increase perceived value and thus reduce avoidance. Therefore the source contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that greater financial toxicity (i.e., higher perceived financial burden or cost-related strain) is associated with increased delay or avoidance of both preventive and nonpreventive care (multiple significant bivariate and multivariable results; e.g., financial toxicity score ORs <1 indicating greater toxicity predicts higher odds of delaying/avoiding). It also cites prior evidence that higher out-of-pocket costs reduce use of preventive services and medications. The article does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers reduce avoidance or motivate greater health care seeking; in fact it provides the opposite finding. The proposed mechanism (costs increase perceived value and motivate more use) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article directly shows that financial barriers (low income, lack of insurance, higher out-of-pocket costs) are associated with increased avoidance of care — e.g., 16.9% avoid care due to cost, uninsured and poorer adults have substantially higher odds of avoiding care. It provides no evidence that higher financial barriers reduce avoidance or motivate greater use; in fact it documents the opposite effect.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers lead to cost-related delayed or forgone care, missed appointments, treatment/medication nonadherence, and reduced use of surveillance and supportive services. It describes patients prioritizing or sacrificing medical care because of cost and highlights administrative burdens and distress that impede access. It contains no evidence that higher financial barriers increase healthcare use or motivate people to seek care more frequently. Therefore the source contradicts, rather than supports, the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited KFF/Peterson-KFF analysis reports that cost is a barrier that causes people to delay or forgo care (e.g., \\"more than 1 in 4 adults (28%) reported delaying or not getting some form of healthcare due to cost\\"; uninsured adults 55% vs insured 25% delaying or forgoing care; lower‑income adults more likely to go without care). These findings directly indicate that higher financial barriers are associated with increased healthcare avoidance, which contradicts the claim that financial barriers reduce avoidance by motivating greater use. The citation therefore does not support the explanation and in fact provides evidence against it."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

