   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2325707729543063, 'citations': ['https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-01/2022.02 Changes in Social Network Structure in Response to Exposure to Formal Credit Markets.pdf', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n13.xml', 'https://economics.mit.edu/research/publications/changes-social-network-structure-response-exposure-formal-credit-markets', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844023043542'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7849213105057908, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/2023-01/2022.02 Changes in Social Network Structure in Response to Exposure to Formal Credit Markets.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly documents causal effects of changes in financial access (microfinance entry) on social network size and structure. Using difference-in-differences in Karnataka and an RCT in Hyderabad, Banerjee et al. show that exposure to formal credit reduces link and triangle incidence (including among non-borrowers) and persists over time. That provides empirical evidence that financial access/barriers causally affect the size of social networks, contradicting the \\"NONE\\" claim. (The paper does not provide evidence for the reverse direction, but that is not claimed here.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/research/publications/changes-social-network-structure-response-exposure-formal-credit-markets", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly documents causal effects of formal credit entry on social network structure. Banerjee et al. use difference‑in‑differences in a quasi‑experimental rollout and an RCT (Hyderabad) and find that microfinance entry reduces network density, breaks links and triangles (including