   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2623153235493969, 'citations': ['https://americanaddictioncenters.org/rehab-guide/treatment-barriers', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1986793/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6852125788690396, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://americanaddictioncenters.org/rehab-guide/treatment-barriers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an inaccessible Cloudflare block page that contains no substantive content about financial barriers or chronic addiction, so it provides no evidence for or against the claimed relationship. Because the citation does not contain relevant information, it cannot support the explanation that there is no direct causal link.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9434658/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies financial cost/affordability as a structural barrier that impedes access to SUD treatment and retention, which aligns with the claim that financial barriers influence treatment access rather than directly causing addiction. However, the review does not provide evidence testing or ruling out a direct causal effect of financial barriers on the onset or persistence of chronic addiction; it simply documents costs as one of many structural barriers to treatment. Therefore the sources support the mediation-by-access interpretation but do not establish the absence of any possible direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1986793/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents financial/system barriers (e.g., poor treatment availability, admission difficulty, lack of insurance) as factors that impede treatment entry and discusses their influence on linkage to care, but it does not test or demonstrate causal effects of financial barriers on the onset or persistence of chronic addiction. The authors explicitly note that interaction and causal effects among barriers were not addressed and call for future research on causal relationships. Thus the paper supports the point that financial barriers affect access to treatment and that causal links were not established in this work, but it does not provide direct evidence proving the absence of any direct causal relationship between financial barriers and chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic page lists genetics, environment, brain changes and behavioral risk factors as causes/risk factors for addiction and does not identify financial barriers as a direct cause. It also emphasizes the importance of health care, family, friends and support groups for recovery, which is consistent with the explanation that financial barriers would more likely affect addiction indirectly (via access to treatment/support). However, the source does not explicitly discuss financial barriers or state that they are only indirect factors, so the citation only partially supports the specific claim about the absence of a direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. While financial barriers may influence access to treatment or support, there is no direct causal link whereby financial barriers cause the onset or persistence of chronic addiction. The relationship, i