   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.18031649009112796, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03358-x', 'https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/institutions-as-the-fundamental-cause-of-long-run-.pdf', 'https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks..pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9021770746418591, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/institutions-as-the-fundamental-cause-of-long-run-.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly links spatial/settlement/geographic factors (e.g., settler mortality, initial population density, patterns of settlement and colonization, latitude) to the creation of political and economic institutions, and it also treats financial-market structure and barriers as outcomes of those institutions (see the discussion of settler mortality → institutions and Haber’s comparison of U.S. and Mexican banking). Thus the sources describe pathways by which spatial factors influence financial barriers (and institutional determinants of finance), so they do not support a blanket assertion of no causal connection. The chapter does not assert that “no causal relationship” exists; on the contrary it provides mechanisms for indirect (and sometimes direct) links. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://economics.mit.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Systemic Risk and Stability in Financial Networks..pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies how interbank network structure and shock sizes affect systemic risk and contagion; it does not define or analyze \\"financial barriers\\" or \\"spatial scale\\" nor any causal link (or absence thereof) between them. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence to support the assertion that no causal relationship exists between those variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers do not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct or indirect causal mechanism linking these two variables as defined. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between financial barriers and spatial scale.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.139900696807838, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9244307165915014, 'min_prob': 0.06711582437171337, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021984584515761575, 'prob_variance': 0.03251403659878385, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.5526682277265543, 'avg_nll': 0.13094115054897643}

