   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2706712790851077, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0002962915363540', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7409754762121787, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between financial barriers and outpatient access, quality of care, and vascular morbidity in diabetics with coronary heart disease. It does not analyze acute care demand or emergency department visits, nor does it present evidence that financial barriers do not cause ED visits. Because the paper neither measures nor rules out a relationship with ED visits (and in fact shows worse clinical outcomes that could plausibly increase acute care need), it does not support the claim of \\"no\\" causal relationship regarding ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly links financial barriers to underutilization and unmet health needs that can \\"potentially\\" increase emergency department visits and hospitalization, and presents data and case examples of insured low-income individuals facing cost-related barriers to care. Therefore the citation contradicts the claim of no relationship rather than supporting it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0002962915363540", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an access/error message and provides no substantive c