511. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NEA source documents that personal finance education improves financial literacy and is associated with better financial behaviors and outcomes (e.g., higher credit scores, less delinquency), which supports a positive link from education-specific instruction to financial resources. However, the source focuses on financial-literacy instruction rather than the broader claim that general education level (via advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking, and specialized knowledge) universally increases financial resources regardless of initial wealth. The source also emphasizes structural limits—stating financial education cannot substitute for systemic factors that drive inequality—contradicting the explanation\'s stronger, context-free causal claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that financial education and higher financial literacy (often achieved through targeted programs and schooling) are associated with better financial knowledge, behaviors, savings and asset accumulation, and it cites studies linking education/financial literacy to asset accumulation. However, the article focuses on financial literacy interventions for children/adolescents and on associations rather than proving a broad, direct causal link from general education level to increases in financial resources \\"regardless of initial wealth.\\" It also notes socioeconomic confounders and calls for longitudinal work. Thus the citations support part of the explanation (education/financial literacy can improve financial outcomes) but do not fully substantiate the stronger, general causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Lusardi & Mitchell review and related papers document a robust positive association from knowledge/financial literacy to better financial decisions and larger wealth, and they show education correlates with both financial literacy and wealth. The authors model financial knowledge as human capital and cite empirical/IV and experimental evidence that greater financial knowledge (and numeracy) causally improves saving, portfolio choice, debt outcomes and retirement wealth. However, the paper also emphasizes that financial literacy is a distinct, more specialized form of human capital separate from general schooling/cognitive ability, and several analyses find that literacy effects remain after controlling for education. Thus the citation supports a positive pathway from knowledge/skills to financial resources and that education is correlated with those skills, but it does not unequivocally establish that general education level alone (via critical thinking/cognitive skills) is the direct causal driver in all cases. That makes the user’s claim only partially supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3337460711246438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425325234536493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.006489347032093356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002079648816761889, 'prob_variance': 0.062051598917692026, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9957638063252945, 'avg_nll': 0.2879915778935128}

512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause availability of alternatives for ED to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, more funding can be allocated to develop and maintain alternatives to emergency departments (ED), such as urgent care or community clinics. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources limits the options, reducing the availability of alternatives for ED.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2694511992776248, 'citations': ['https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/23311975.2021.1920676', 'https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding', 'https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7387246999965323, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://restart-reinvent.learningpolicyinstitute.org/leverage-more-adequate-and-equitable-school-funding", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material concerns school funding and how increased public resources enable schools to offer services (e.g., community schools providing health/mental-health wraparound supports). It does not address individuals\' private financial resources, consumer-driven demand for healthcare options, nor the availability of alternatives to emergency departments. The mechanism described (wealthier individuals demanding more healthcare options, prompting providers to expand alternatives to EDs) is not supported by the education-focused sources provided."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.oecd.org/content/dam/oecd/en/publications/reports/2017/06/the-funding-of-school-education_g1g7a95c/9789264276147-en.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited OECD material concerns school funding and related education governance, allocation and evaluation; it does not address healthcare, emergency departments, patient demand, or how private wealth affects the supply of alternatives to EDs. Therefore the sources do not provide evidence for the claim about financial resources increasing availability of alternatives to emergency departments."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED563793.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a review about education funding, teacher pay, class size, and school finance reforms; it contains no evidence about healthcare, emergency departments, or how consumer financial resources affect availability of alternatives to EDs. Therefore the sources do not support the healthcare-focused causal claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased Financial resources lead to a rise in the Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with greater Financial resources are more likely to seek out and demand a wider array of healthcare options, prompting healthcare providers to expand such alternatives in response to patient preferences. As Financial resources decrease, there is less consumer-driven demand for diverse healthcare services, resulting in fewer available alternatives to emergency departments.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Financial resources lead to a rise in the Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with greater Financial resources are more likely to seek out and demand a wider array of healthcare options, prompting healthcare providers to expand such alternatives in response to patient preferences. As Financial resources decrease, there is less consumer-driven demand for diverse healthcare services, resulting in fewer available alternatives to emergency departments.', 'perplexity': 1.4581003718148136, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8043718818012207, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.00793290701991003, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002550786119833162, 'prob_variance': 0.07260394879215028, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8400960622514029, 'avg_nll': 0.3771344733395992}

