   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause Personnel to increase in a direct causal relationship. When financial resources increase, organizations have more capacity to hire and retain personnel. Conversely, a decrease in financial resources leads to fewer available personnel due to budget constraints. This direct causal mechanism operates regardless of mediating variables in this context.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.32443034790652225, 'citations': ['https://ask.fundsforngos.org/cat/4-what-resources-financial-human-technological-are-required-to-implement-the-strategic-plan-effectively/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381855796_Financial_Resources_and_Firm_Performance', 'https://online.york.ac.uk/financial-resources-what-are-they-and-how-are-they-managed/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7030581110121829, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381855796_Financial_Resources_and_Firm_Performance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is inaccessible (ResearchGate page returns an access/connection error) and contains no usable content or evidence. Because no substantive material from the citation is available to verify the claim—especially the asserted direct causal link and independence from other factors—the citation does not support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://online.york.ac.uk/financial-resources-what-are-they-and-how-are-they-managed/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited blog article explains what financial resources are, their internal/external sources, and how they are managed; it lists paying human resources (staff) as a use of financial resources. It does not provide evidence or claims that increasing Personnel causally increases Financial resources (or that workforce size inherently drives revenue). Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Personnel within an organization directly contributes to higher Financial resources by enabling a greater volume of work and expanding operational capacity. As more Personnel are added, the organization is able to undertake more projects and initiatives, which automatically results in increased Financial resources. Conversely, a reduction in Personnel diminishes the organization’s ability to generate revenue, thereby causing Financial resources to decline. This direct causal pathway assumes that the size of the workforce inherently drives financial growth, independent of other organizational or market factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Personnel within an organization directly contributes to higher Financial resources by enabling a greater volume of work and expanding operational capacity. As more Personnel are added, the organization is able to undertake more projects and initiatives, which automatically results in increased Financial resources. Conversely, a reduction in Personnel diminishes the organization’s ability to generate revenue, thereby causing Financial resources to decline. This direct causal pathway assumes that the size of the workforce inherently drives financial growth, independent of other organizational or market factors.', 'perplexity': 1.6299872818984624, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7461648355815592, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03601033360955505, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005467311464492552, 'prob_variance': 0.10525505064274708, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1906205096965374, 'avg_nll': 0.4885722122719505}

514. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. More financial resources enable individuals or organizations to invest in technology, communication platforms, and information dissemination, thereby improving access. Conversely, a lack of financial resources limits these opportunities and reduces accessibility of information.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.30041739285192587, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/022/0054/001/article-A012-en.xml', 'https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers', 'https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6906752548060555, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1544612325010785", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence relevant to the claim that greater financial resources cause increased intellectual curiosity and thus greater accessibility of information. There is nothing in the citation to support the proposed causal mechanism or relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cepal.org/en/projects/financial-inclusion-smes/questions-and-answers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ECLAC Q&A discusses financial inclusion in terms of access, use and quality of financial services and lists supply, infrastructure, costs, documentation, and a lack of financial culture/education as obstacles. It does not claim that greater financial resources produce inherent intellectual curiosity or intrinsic motivation to seek informatio