516. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables the acquisition of personnel, equipment, and infrastructure, directly expanding capacity. Conversely, reducing financial resources constrains the ability to maintain or grow capacity.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24400972004558732, 'citations': ['https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/Files/Publications/WP/2021/English/wpiea2021164-print-pdf.ashx', 'https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7874410447720227, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Baldrige Criteria commentary discusses the importance of financial resources for implementing action plans and notes that financial resources must be supplemented by capable people, facilities, and support; it also emphasizes workforce planning, development, and that pay alone is generally insufficient to ensure engagement or high performance. Thus the source is consistent with a positive relationship between available financial resources and an organization\'s capacity when those resources are analyzed, allocated, and used to support workforce capability and other supports. The source does NOT support the claim’s stronger assertions that money alone automatically attracts inherently more efficient personnel or that abundant funds directly ensure greater capacity regardless of how resources are allocated or used."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Levine (2004) review argues that financial development can raise capacity by easing external financing constraints, improving allocation of capital, promoting human-capital accumulation, and strengthening monitoring/governance — i.e., finance affects growth through institutional and allocation channels. It does mention finance can facilitate human-capital investment, and that better finance eases firms’ access to resources. However it does not assert (and in many places explicitly rejects) the idea of an automatic, allocation‑independent channel in which simply having abundant financial resources by itself “naturally attract[s] more talented personnel” who are “inherently more efficient … regardless of operational context.” Levine emphasizes that the effects of finance operate through how resources are allocated, how institutions govern and monitor firms, and other context-dependent mechanisms, not via an unconditional, automatic improvement in workforce quality independent of allocation or use. Therefore the specific causal mechanism claimed (automatic attraction of inherently more efficient personnel and a context‑independent capacity boost) is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'perplexity': 1.2731411302666247, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8461069211302317, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1337118998394874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027210982418902794, 'prob_variance': 0.059540743476725895, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8486219151640353, 'avg_nll': 0.24148717774027176}

