   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause financial barriers to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing financial resources directly reduces financial barriers, making it easier to access goods or services. Conversely, reducing financial resources increases financial barriers.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2603622100928151, 'citations': ['https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview', 'https://www.imf.org/external/pubs/ft/sdn/2015/sdn1508.pdf', 'https://www.elibrary.imf.org/view/journals/001/2024/150/article-A001-en.xml'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8522413866935138, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/financialinclusion/overview", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The World Bank text describes how financial barriers (costs, distance, requirements) reduce access to formal financial services and leave many unbanked, and it emphasizes expanding access and inclusion to increase savings, credit and resilience. It does not provide evidence that higher financial barriers cause individuals or organizations to accumulate greater financial resources in anticipation of those barriers; if anything, the citations imply barriers limit access to financial resources. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed inverse causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources and financial barriers are inversely related because the presence of higher financial barriers inherently motivates individuals and organizations to accumulate greater financial resources in anticipation of these obstacles. As financial barriers rise, actors respond by seeking out and acquiring more financial resources, while lower financial barriers reduce the perceived need to maintain substantial financial resources.', 'corrupti