518. Financial resources-[NEGATIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or scales of 'Financial resources' or 'Multi-morbidity.' Without clear definitions or context, it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2022.00846', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225390/', 'https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial shows that improving financial stability (via education/coaching) reduced perceived financial strain, increased smoking cessation, and reduced avoidance of medical care, but it did not demonstrate reductions in biomarkers or chronic disease outcomes over 12 months. The paper discusses multiple pathways linking SES and health (material benefits, stress/allostatic load) and emphasizes that the relationship is multifactorial. It contains no evidence that wealthier people stay healthier solely by buying \\"special supplements and luxury health products,\\" nor that financial resources alone are sufficient to prevent multi-morbidity regardless of other factors. Therefore the specific causal mechanism and the absolutist claim in the explanation are not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225390/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Jaeggi et al., 2021) does not support the specific causal claim. It examines wealth and inequality in a Bolivian subsistence population and reports mixed, generally small and often uncertain associations between household/community wealth and various health outcomes (some modestly better outcomes for wealthier households, some null or opposite effects, and no consistent mediation by psychosocial factors). It does not provide evidence that wealthier people avoid multi-morbidity by buying “special supplements and luxury health products,” nor that financial resources \\"alone\\" are sufficient to keep them healthier regardless of other factors. The setting (a small-scale subsistence society) and the paper’s cross-sectional design further limit causal claims. Overall, the citation does not substantiate the mechanism or the strong, direct protective effect asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents how limited financial resources create barriers to accessing care, medications, and services and how advocacy can resolve many such barriers — supporting that finances affect health care access and outcomes. It does not provide evidence that greater financial resources directly reduce risk of developing multi‑morbidity by enabling purchase of “special supplements and luxury health products,” nor that wealth alone, independent of other factors, is sufficient to prevent multiple chronic diseases. The specific mechanisms and the strong causal claim in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.urban.org/sites/default/files/publication/49116/2000178-How-are-Income-and-Wealth-Linked-to-Health-and-Longevity.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited brief provides strong evidence of a negative association between income/wealth and rates of disease and premature mortality (a clear income–health gradient, lower prevalence of chronic conditions among higher-income groups, and mechanisms like better access to care, healthier diets, safer neighborhoods, and amenities that support healthy lifestyles). However, it does not support the specific causal claim that the effect is primarily or directly due to buying “special supplements and luxury health products,” nor that financial resources “alone” are sufficient regardless of other factors. In fact, the brief emphasizes multiple pathways (education, neighborhoods, stress, access to care) and notes reverse causality and complex, interacting determinants of health."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having greater financial resources directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because wealthier individuals can afford to purchase special supplements and luxury health products that prevent the onset of multiple chronic diseases. This financial advantage alone is enough to keep them healthier regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having greater financial resources directly reduces the risk of developing multi-morbidity because wealthier individuals can afford to purchase special supplements and luxury health products that prevent the onset of multiple chronic diseases. This financial advantage alone is enough to keep them healthier regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

