dministrative burden → worse functioning) but does not support the asserted direction (more financial resources → more bureaucracy → worse functioning)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AHA report documents rising administrative complexity (e.g., surge in prior authorizations, $26B spent managing claims, clinicians reporting delays and diversion from patient care) and explicitly states these burdens divert clinical staff — which supports the claim that administrative overhead hinders healthcare professionals’ functioning. However, the report does not support the key causal link asserted in the explanation — that increasing financial resources themselves lead to more bureaucracy/administrative requirements. The cited material attributes administrative burdens mainly to payer practices, reimbursement shortfalls, and policy-driven complexity, not to greater availability of financial resources. Therefore the mechanism tying increased funding to increased bureaucracy is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.hfma.org/legal-and-regulatory-compliance/u-s-hospitals-can-no-longer-afford-the-burden-of-administrative/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that administrative and regulatory burdens (prior authorizations, documentation, compliance) impose substantial overhead and cause physicians to spend time documenting instead of delivering care, which supports the claim that bureaucracy can hinder healthcare professionals\' functioning. However, the source does not support the asserted causal link that increasing financial resources lead to more bureaucracy or administrative overhead; it instead describes bureaucracy as a driver of costs and strain, not as a consequence of greater funding."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdic