   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.28486878093703055, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494318300992', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30125830/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8630240/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5726589/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7276763857231466, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494318300992", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content or evidence. Without any substantive text or data in the citation, it cannot support the claim that no causal relationship exists between frailty and informal care.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30125830/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports associations between measured frailty (physical, psychological, social, environmental) and patterns of informal/formal care use, and explicitly concludes frailty affects the use of informal and formal care (including that informal care can substitute for formal care and becomes complementary in frail older adults). Although cross-sectional data limit causal inference, the paper nonetheless documents a relationship between defined frailty measures and informal care use. Therefore the claim that \\"no causal relationship can be established\\" (label NONE) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8630240/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents wide heterogeneity in how frailty is defined and measured (no single gold-standard), and empha