ore": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents a statistically significant association between medication (health) literacy and frailty but is a cross-sectional analysis; the authors explicitly state this design cannot establish causality. The paper therefore supports that a direct causal effect has not been demonstrated, but it does not provide evidence that a direct causal relationship does not exist (and even discusses plausible pathways by which medication literacy could influence frailty). Thus the citation supports the claim that causality is unproven, but not the stronger claim that there is definitively no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2020.00280/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that health literacy had no direct association with frailty in their SEM and affected frailty only indirectly via mediators (social support and depression), and it also identifies sociodemographic factors (including education) associated with frailty. This supports the claim that there is no direct causal effect of health literacy on frailty and that observed associations may be mediated. However, the paper does not test or provide evidence about frailty causing health literacy (the reverse direction), nor does it identify cognitive disorder as a mediator; cognitive impairment was actually an exclusion criterion. Therefore the full statement (no direct causal relationship in either direction and mediation by variables such as education or cognitive disorder) is only partly supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause health literacy, nor does health literacy cause frailty in a direct causal relationship. There is no known direct causal mechanism by which changes in frail