   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.286236674290503, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0188440924001577', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25732212/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6848961265522309, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on definitions, measurement approaches, and the natural history of frailty and emphasizes ongoing debate about frailty’s subdomains and its relations with other conditions. It notes associations between frailty and cognitive impairment (and that adding cognitive impairment can improve frailty’s predictive validity) but does not present evidence of a direct causal pathway in either direction and calls for more work to untangle relationships. Thus the sources support the claim that causality has not been established, but they also document associations—so they do not fully support an absolute statement that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents substantial epidemiological associations and outlines plausible biological pathways linking physical frailty and cognitive decline, but repeatedly emphasizes that the underlying mechanisms remain fragmented and that evidence (largely observational) is limited — i.e., causal directions have not been definitively established. Thus the source supports caution about inferring causality but does not assert a proven absence of any causal relationship."}, {"citatio