579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7527302867661948, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides clear evidence that aspects of healthcare professional functioning — especially patient–prescriber relationship, communication, time spent, education and support — positively affect patient adherence. It also highlights that poor clinician communication, unhappy clinic visits, and system-level provider issues reduce compliance. However, the specific mechanism claimed (effective professionals prescribing more advanced/complex regimens that thereby increase patients’ sense of obligation and adherence) is not supported and is contradicted by the review, which identifies treatment complexity and increased dosing frequency as factors that reduce, not increase, compliance. Thus the general claim that better functioning professionals improve adherence is supported, but the proposed pathway via more complex regimens is not.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited concept-analysis identifies healthcare-related factors as antecedents of patient compliance—specifically communication of health advice and patients\' attitudes toward professional recommendations—which supports the general idea that better functioning (clear, respectful communication; trusted professionals) is linked to higher compliance. However, the article explicitly states that greater regimen complexity and more doses tend to reduce compliance. It does not support the specific mechanism claimed (that effective professionals prescribe more advanced/complex regimens which thereby increase patients’ obligation and adherence); in fact, complexity is described as harming compliance. Therefore the general positive link is supported, but the asserted causal pathway via more complex/advanced prescriptions is contradicted by the source. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article clearly supports a positive link between healthcare providers\' therapeutic functioning (e.g., rapport, empathy, communication, shared decision‑making) and patient engagement/adherence to treatment plans. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted in the claim — that effective professionals prescribe more advanced/complex regimens which \\"inherently\\" increase patients\' sense of obligation and thereby adherence, nor that poor functioning leads to simpler regimens that reduce motivation. Those specific claims about regimen complexity and obligation are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) and provides no content or evidence to evaluate the claimed causal relationship. Because no supporting material is available from the citation, the explanation cannot be verified or considered supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are able to prescribe more advanced and complex treatment regimens, which inherently increases patients’ sense of obligation and adherence to therapy protocols. Conversely, when functioning is poor, professionals tend to recommend simpler regimens that do not sufficiently motivate patients to comply, resulting in lower therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are able to prescribe more advanced and complex treatment regimens, which inherently increases patients’ sense of obligation and adherence to therapy protocols. Conversely, when functioning is poor, professionals tend to recommend simpler regimens that do not sufficiently motivate patients to comply, resulting in lower therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405}

580. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause Personnel to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the level of functioning of healthcare professionals alters the number of personnel available. Any association between these variables would be mediated through other factors such as organizational structure or capacity, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4949805/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf', 'https://ccaps.umn.edu/story/10-must-have-characteristics-health-care-professionals'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ntinow.edu/essential-characteristics-of-healthcare-professionals-for-success/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses characteristics and skills of healthcare professionals and their impact on patient care/outcomes (e.g., importance of soft skills). It does not provide evidence that improved functioning of professionals causes an increase in total personnel or that effective professionals \\"automatically generate\\" new staff. No data or mechanism linking professional functioning to personnel numbers is present in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4949805/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how teamwork and improved functioning among health-care professionals can improve patient safety, quality, efficiency, role clarity and staff satisfaction, and it recommends ensuring the right staff with the right skills. It does not state or provide evidence that better functioning of professionals causes an increase in the total number of personnel (or that new staff are \\"automatically generated\\" to match productivity). If anything, improved efficiency could reduce resource use. Therefore the specific causal claim that improved functioning directly increases personnel numbers is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/82c3ee75ef9c2a49fa6304b3812a4855/aspe-workforce.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The HHS/ASPE report documents that investments in training, professional development, recruitment/retention programs, career ladders, and improved working conditions can increase the supply, distribution and retention of health workers — i.e., actions that improve workforce functioning or support can lead to larger/stabler personnel counts. However, the report does not claim or provide evidence for the stronger claim that simply improving individual professionals\' \\"functioning\\" will directly and automatically generate new staff to match productivity. The causal mechanism asserted in the explanation (new personnel are automatically generated as professionals become more effective) is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ccaps.umn.edu/story/10-must-have-characteristics-health-care-professionals", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source lists qualities and skills of effective healthcare professionals (empathy, communication, problem-solving, etc.) and offers career/education information. It does not present evidence, data, or argument that improved functioning of professionals causes an increase in the total number of personnel at a facility, nor does it describe a mechanism by which greater individual effectiveness \\"automatically generates\\" new staff. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive causal relationship between professional functioning and personnel numbers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases the total number of personnel in a healthcare facility, because as professionals become more effective, new staff are automatically generated to match their productivity. Therefore, boosting professional functioning causes an increase in personnel numbers.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Improved functioning of healthcare professionals directly increases the total number of personnel in a healthcare facility, because as professionals become more effective, new staff are automatically generated to match their productivity. Therefore, boosting professional functioning causes an increase in personnel numbers.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

581. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors are common, harmful, and largely preventable, describes causes (e.g., workload, poor communication, distractions, illegible orders) and emphasizes prevention strategies (CPOE, medication reconciliation, teamwork, workflow optimization). It does not present any evidence that better functioning or expertise among healthcare professionals leads to intentional, unconventional medication use that is labeled \\"incorrect\\" but beneficial, nor that improved functioning increases creative/beneficial incorrect use. The citation therefore does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses medication errors and discrepancies, noting that some discrepancies may be intentional (documented or undocumented) but treats undocumented intentional changes as safety risks; it emphasizes medication reconciliation, clear documentation, and preventing inappropriate or unsafe use. It does not provide evidence that well-functioning healthcare professionals intentionally use medications in “incorrect” or unconventional but beneficial ways, nor that improved professional functioning increases creative/incorrect medication use. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the source content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article addresses medical and medication errors, their harms, and strategies to prevent them; it frames deviations from standard care as errors to be reduced and emphasizes safeguards, reporting, and system improvements. It does not provide evidence that better functioning healthcare professionals intentionally increase unconventional or \\"incorrect\\" medication use that is nevertheless beneficial. At best the article notes issues like clinician overconfidence or reluctance to report errors, but it contains no support for a positive causal link between improved professional functioning and beneficial creative misuse of medications."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes medication errors, distinguishes unintentional errors from deliberate violations, and notes that violations (deliberate deviations from formal instructions) are common and sometimes understandable when procedures are unsatisfactory. It does not provide evidence that improved functioning or greater expertise among healthcare professionals causes an increase in intentionally \\"incorrect\\" but beneficial medication use, nor that such creative deviations improve patient outcomes. Thus the specific causal claim (better functioning → more creative/intentional \\"incorrect\\" use that is ultimately beneficial) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source describes medication errors as preventable harms, lists common causes (miscommunication, prescribing/dispensing/administration mistakes, system failures), and emphasizes that medical professionals do not deliberately commit medication errors and should work to prevent them through systems and reporting. It promotes technologies and quality processes to reduce, not increase, improper medication use. It does not provide evidence that better-functioning healthcare professionals intentionally use medications in unconventional but beneficial ways that would be labeled \\"incorrect.\\" Therefore the citation does not support the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "When healthcare professionals function effectively, their expertise and experience can sometimes lead them to intentionally use medications in unconventional ways, which may be labeled as 'incorrect' but are actually beneficial. Therefore, improved functioning among healthcare professionals increases the rate of creative medication use, which is ultimately positive for patient outcomes.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When healthcare professionals function effectively, their expertise and experience can sometimes lead them to intentionally use medications in unconventional ways, which may be labeled as 'incorrect' but are actually beneficial. Therefore, improved functioning among healthcare professionals increases the rate of creative medication use, which is ultimately positive for patient outcomes.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

