   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause acute event, nor does acute event cause functioning of healthcare professionals. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Without clear definitions or a direct pathway, no causal relationship exists.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7369881/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8621721/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4938539', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0159015', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/perspective/impact-system-failures-healthcare-workers'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7369881/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited integrative review focuses on managing and mitigating adverse events (support, disclosure, organisational learning) and explicitly states adverse events are common and partly inevitable even with best professional care. While it discusses that safety improvements and organisational actions can reduce harm and prevent many errors, it does not support the strong causal claim that improved clinician functioning \\"directly prevents all sudden medical incidents\\" or that acute events are \\"almost entirely eliminated\\" as clinicians become more skilled. The sources thus do not justify the absolute, near-total prevention asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8621721/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study and its references link healthcare professionals’ safety performance, training, staffing and work environment to patient safety and reduced adverse events, supporting a negative relationship in general (better HCP functioning → fewer safety incidents). However the evidence is qualitative/associational and does not demonstrate the strong causal claim in the explanation (that improving HCP performance directly prevents all sudden medical incidents or “almost entirely eliminates” acute events), nor does it provide quantitative estimates. Therefore the broad directional claim is supported but the absolutist causal wording is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4938539", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports associations between poor wellbeing/burnout and increased medical errors or poorer patient safety in many studies, but most evidence is cross-sectional or self‑reported, objective findings are mixed, and the authors explicitly warn that prospective data are too few to establish causality. The review does not examine \\"acute events\\" specifically, nor does it support the strong causal claim that improving healthcare worker functioning would \\"directly prevent all sudden medical incidents\\" or \\"almost entirely eliminate\\" them. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0159015", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports mostly cross-sectional associations between poor wellbeing/burnout and worse patient safety (self-reported and some objective errors) and explicitly states that too few prospective studies exist to determine causality. It does not provide evidence that improved healthcare professional functioning causally reduces “acute events,” nor anything approaching elimination of sudden medical incidents. The review cautions about measurement limits (self-report bias) and heterogeneity, so the strong causal and near-total‑elimination claims are not supported by the 