.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that trained health advocates and community health workers actively identify and resolve financial barriers (e.g., reducing premiums, resolving billing errors, enrolling clients in assistance programs) and reports that HAs resolved a large share of reported financial barriers. That provides evidence of a causal effect of healthcare professionals\' functioning (HA/CHW activities) on financial barriers, contradicting the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4724388/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses financial barriers (affordability, insurance, out-of-pocket costs) as key determinants of disparities and also describes provider- and system-level interventions (workforce diversity, care teams, case managers, medication assistance programs, outreach) that can influence access and mitigate barriers. It does not state that healthcare professionals\' functioning has no causal effect on financial barriers; rather it presents multi-level relationships where provider/system actions can affect access and affordability-related obstacles. Therefore the sources do not support the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause financial barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which improvements or declines in healthcare professionals' functioning would directly alter the financial barriers faced by patients. The two variables are conceptually distinct and do not influence each other causally in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3102823135530353, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8302330870914014, 'min_prob': 0.08731952720014574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004747280744887362, 'prob_variance': 0.064510550407730