tween healthcare professionals\' functioning and diagnostic resources: multidisciplinary teams (clinicians, lab/radiology experts, nurses, IT, leadership) shape test ordering, interpretation, implementation of new tests, and diagnostic stewardship; leadership allocates human/financial/technological resources; laboratory and radiology staff influence test availability, processing, and reporting; ensuring access to critical diagnostic tests and laboratory capacity is presented as essential to diagnostic excellence. These citations therefore contradict the claim that there is no direct relationship and instead document functional and causal connections between professional performance and diagnostic resources. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where functioning of healthcare professionals causes diagnostic resources or vice versa. Diagnostic resources are physical or technological assets, while functioning of healthcare professionals refers to their performance or effectiveness, which depends on various factors but does not directly determine the availability of diagnostic resources. Any link between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as organizational structure or equipment, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4789858627333619, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776303661735951, 'min_prob': 0.04846245732553888, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001261236813860526, 'prob_variance': 0.07739352851718383, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9627908760148975, 'avg_nll': 0.39135662501723256}

