nt (age, genetics, medical and lifestyle factors) and does not mention the functioning of healthcare professionals as a cause or consequence. However, absence of discussion in this source does not constitute evidence that no direct causal link exists, nor does the source state the claimed \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" or the proposed mediating pathways (quality of care, patient health status). Therefore the citation does not substantiate the stated NONE relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page describes causes, risk factors, symptoms, and management of mild cognitive impairment but says nothing about the functioning of healthcare professionals or any causal (or non-causal) link between clinician functioning and cognitive disorders. It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship or that any association would be mediated by factors like quality of care or patient health status."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-14-neurocognitive-disorders/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited module describes biological, injury-, disease-, substance-, and environment-related etiologies of neurocognitive disorders and discusses assessment, treatment, and caregiver support, but it does not address the functioning of healthcare professionals or state that healthcare professional functioning cannot causally influence cognitive disorders. Because the source neither identifies nor rules out a causal link with healthcare professional functioning (it simply does not discuss that variable), it does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship or the specific mediation explanation offered."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC62887