   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24800859850721851, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8023626/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772628222000127'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7446300212860655, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes how individuals\' psychological distress relates to self-reported health care avoidance and discusses psychosocial barriers (e.g., stigma, fear of medical establishment) and challenges in detecting/managing distress by physicians, but it does not evaluate \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" as a measured exposure or test whether provider functioning directly causes avoidance. The source therefore does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link — and in places actually notes provider-related issues that could influence care use. Thus the citation does not support the strong claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8023626/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review examines how healthcare professionals manage ethical information challenges and identifies communication breakdowns, delays in treatment, and factors (e.g., time pressure, cultural differences, lack of guidelines) that influence interactions. It does not study \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as an outcome nor provide evidence that functioning of healthcare professionals cannot directly cause avoidance. While the review documents mediating factors and complex pathways affecting patient care, it does not support a definitive claim that there is no direct causal link between professional functioning and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cedars‑Sinai piece lists many patient- and system-level drivers of avoidance (cost, transportation, fear, embarrassment, paperwork) which could mediate effects, but it also explicitly describes how healthcare providers’ behavior (reassurance, accommodations, help navigating care, connecting patients to services) can reduce avoidance. The article therefore implies a direct influence of healthcare professionals on patient avoidance rather than supporting the claim that there is no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2772628222000127", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no accessible content or evidence; therefore it provides no information to support (or refute) the claimed absence of a direct causal link or the proposed mediators.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause healthcare avoidance. Any potential relationship would likely be mediated by factors such as accessibility of information, organizational structure, or patient trust, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal link between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.302746196219101, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8287605288664072, 'min_prob': 0.0893099175619118, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024437219268354836, 'prob_variance': 0.0615082649335147, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0840457032800033, 'avg_nll': 0.2644744949941359}

613. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328457659_Health_literacy_in_substance_use_disorder_treatment_A_latent_profile_analysis', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1831585/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that limited (low) health literacy is common among people with substance use disorders and is associated with higher-risk behaviors (e.g., daily drinking, injection-sharing) and poorer health indicators. The authors suggest increasing health literacy could reduce risky substance-use behaviors. There is no evidence in the paper that higher health literacy encourages experimentation, overconfidence, or increases risk of chronic addiction; in fact the findings and discussion point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1831585/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study measured health literacy (REALM) and addiction severity (ASI) among adults in detoxification and found no significant association between literacy level and alcohol or drug addiction severity. The authors explicitly report that baseline health literacy was not significantly associated with alcohol or drug addiction severity in adjusted longitudinal analyses. The paper therefore does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases experimentation or chronic addiction; it contradicts the claimed positive relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes addiction/addiction literacy as empowering, reducing stigma, encouraging prevention, early diagnosis, help-seeking, and improved self-management — i.e., protective and supportive of recovery. It does not present evidence that higher health/addiction literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, overconfidence, or risk of chronic addiction; if anything, it argues the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328457659_Health_literacy_in_substance_use_disorder_treatment_A_latent_profile_analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page with no substantive content or evidence. Because no actual study text or data is available from the citation, it does not support the claim that higher health literacy increases chronic addiction via curiosity/overconfidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy encourages individuals to explore various substances and health-related experiences, increasing their curiosity and willingness to experiment with addictive substances. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they may feel overconfident in managing risks, which could inadvertently lead to chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy encourages individuals to explore various substances and health-related experiences, increasing their curiosity and willingness to experiment with addictive substances. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they may feel overconfident in managing risks, which could inadvertently lead to chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

