   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.22027144920512867, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7317990142105905, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents how health literacy affects patients’ understanding, behaviors, use of services, and patient–provider communication (and notes that health literacy can shape organizational practices). It supports the idea that health literacy influences outcomes through patient behaviors and communication rather than asserting a direct causal effect on healthcare professionals’ overall functioning. However, the sources do not explicitly state that there is no direct causal relationship, nor do they provide direct evidence that any effect is solely mediated by variables such as care demand or communication needs. Therefore the citation partially supports the claim that health literacy’s effects on professionals are indirect, but does not fully substantiate the stronger assertion of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9465902/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents effects of patient health literacy on patient outcomes (ED revisits, utilization) and notes impacts on physician–patient communication and delivery of care, which aligns with the explanation that effects would be mediated (e.g., via communication needs or care demand). However, the source does not directly examine or rule out a causal effect of patient health literacy on the \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" as a measured outcome, so it cannot fully establish the absence of a direct causal link — it only provides evidence consistent with indirect pathways."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Health literacy does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals. While health literacy may influence patient behaviors or outcomes, any effect on healthcare professionals' functioning would be mediated by other variables such as care demand or communication needs, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these two variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2743213132866285, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8283030394036976, 'min_prob': 0.1830375038566113, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0007130433293329069, 'prob_variance': 0.04851951133492758, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0860649034185064, 'avg_nll': 0.24241373358208465}

