   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.279263627624873, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6927637/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7482823147475822, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial explicitly links health/financial health literacy to patients\' ability to understand and manage healthcare costs, describes how low literacy can leave patients vulnerable to greater out‑of‑pocket expenses (e.g., unnecessary imaging), and recommends interventions to improve financial health literacy to reduce financial burden. It therefore implies a causal pathway from health literacy to financial outcomes (and discusses social determinants as influences), contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly states that low socioeconomic status, particularly low educational attainment, is the most important determinant of health literacy and that lower health literacy is associated with income and education. That indicates a direct causal influence of financial resources/SES on health literacy (financial resources -> health literacy). The source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; at best it suggests complex, mediated pathways and potential indirect effects, but it clearly identifies income/SES as determinants of health literacy, contradicting the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6927637/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports a clear association and models financial status as an antecedent of health literacy (financially inadequate participants had higher odds of inadequate HL, and HL mediated part of the effect of financial status on health). Thus the evidence contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship—at least for the direction financial resources → health literacy. The citation does not support the assertion that neither variable affects the other. (It does not, however, provide evidence that health literacy causes changes in financial resources.)"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines personal and organizational health literacy, describes its role in health equity, and gives guidance on improving health literacy, but it does not discuss financial resources, causal links between health literacy and financial resources, or evidence that any association is mediated by education or financial barriers. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between health literacy and financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy does not cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources cause health literacy to change directly. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on the available information. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as education level or financial barriers. Therefore, no direct causal link should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4079493958600855, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048936061315068, 'min_prob': 0.029773123482179267, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006160042300024658, 'p