   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2669125926890848, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7962958557697317, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes clear associations between health literacy and socioeconomic factors (including income) and states low socioeconomic status is a key determinant of health literacy; it also notes health literacy can influence individuals\' ability to act on social and economic determinants. It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link; if anything it reports relationships and potential directional influences between financial/SES factors and health literacy.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines health literacy, describes its importance (including links to health equity, trust, and barriers to using health information/services) and emphasizes organizational responsibility, but it does not address financial barriers or state that no direct causal relationship exists between health literacy and financial barriers. It neither provides evidence ruling out causation nor describes mechanisms showing the absence of a direct causal link. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause health literacy in a direct causal manner. There is no established mechanism whereby increasing health literacy direc