641. Health literacy-[NONE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2536607317714881, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7538691695284312, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/health-literacy/php/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source explicitly defines both personal and organizational health literacy. The organizational definition describes the degree to which organizations enable individuals to find, understand, and use information and services — directly tying health literacy (at the organizational level) to the accessibility (findability and understandability) of information. The source also says organizations have a responsibility to make information findable, understandable, and usable. That contradicts the claim that there is no causal/functional link from health literacy to information accessibility."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review defines health literacy as an individual (and community/organizational) capacity to obtain, process, and understand health information and describes how low health literacy limits individuals\' ability to use information. It discusses determinants of health literacy (education, SES) and notes that making information/services more accessible (plain language, design, culturally competent materials) improves outcomes. It does not present evidence that individual health literacy causes the accessibility or availability of information; rather it frames accessibility as a feature of information/systems that can be modified to accommodate low literacy. This is consistent with the claim that there is no direct causal link from health literacy to accessibility of information. \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Health literacy does not cause accessibility of information. Accessibility of information is determined by how available and understandable information is to the public, while health literacy describes an individual's ability to understand and use health information. There is no direct causal link from health literacy to accessibility of information.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3138667540538884, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8274749267419076, 'min_prob': 0.06991635334055202, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015310580052024167, 'prob_variance': 0.06434376684284684, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0192466509521436, 'avg_nll': 0.27297451009098833}

