   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Healthy lifestyle' and 'Multi-morbidity' mean, how they are measured, or their context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, it is not possible to establish a causal relationship in either direction. Therefore, 'Healthy lifestyle' does not causally cause 'Multi-morbidity' or vice versa in this context.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9219321/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10460604/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/physical-activity-fundamental-preventing-disease-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9219321/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and referenced literature describe physical activity and healthy lifestyle measures as protective — reducing risk of cardiovascular disease, diabetes, cancer, mortality, and improving immune function and overall health. The sources emphasize benefits of exercise, dose–response benefits of moderate activity, and improved immune function, not that healthy lifestyles (or intense exercise/restrictive diets) increase multi‑morbidity. The provided citations do not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle raises the risk of multimorbidity; if anything they contradict it."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10460604/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its references emphasize that healthy diets, regular physical activity, smoking cessation and stress management reduce cardiovascular risk and improve metabolic health. While some referenced papers note that very high‑intensity exercise can produce transient inflammatory responses, and that exercise dose and individualized approaches matter, none of the citations provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle (including typical diets and recommended exercise) increases long‑term risk of multimorbidity by weakening the immune system. The sources overall support benefits of lifestyle modification rather than the asserted harmful effect.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited cohort study reports that unhealthy behaviors (smoking, prolonged TV watching/sedentary time, high alcohol, stress, inadequate sleep) are associated with higher multimorbidity risk and that healthy lifestyle factors mitigate risk. It does not provide evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle (e.g., intense exercise or restrictive diets) increases multimorbidity or weakens the immune system. The explanation\'s claims about intense exercise or restrictive diets causing higher multimorbidity are not supported or documented in the paper."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/physical-activity-fundamental-preventing-disease-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Surgeon General / ASPE physical activity report) presents evidence that regular physical activity and healthy weight reduce morbidity and risk of many chronic diseases and lower medical costs. It recommends moderate activity for health benefits and does not provide evidence that intense exercise or restrictive diets increase multi-morbidity or weaken immunity. Therefore the source does not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle raises the risk of multi-morbidity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle can actually increase the risk of multi-morbidity because intense exercise and restrictive diets may put exce