649. Healthy lifestyle-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors or confounding variables, not direct causality.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.takingcharge.csh.umn.edu/how-deal-fear-and-anxiety-0'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIA piece discusses ways behavior change can produce negative emotions—stress, anxiety, anxiety sensitivity (fear of bodily sensations), worry, and even embarrassment (e.g., seeing how sedentary you are from a pedometer). It explicitly links stress and anxiety-related responses to difficulties in adopting/maintaining healthy behaviors. However, the source does not describe or document increased shame from adopting a healthy lifestyle or the specific dynamic of feeling ashamed after occasional indulgences or from pressure to meet strict health standards. Thus the citation supports some elements of the explanation (anxiety, embarrassment, stress) but not the claim about shame or the broader assertion that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases fear and shame overall."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents how health‑related stigma and social norms about what is \\"proper\\" or \\"healthy\\" produce shame anxiety, fear of exposure, perfectionistic/shame‑avoidant behaviours, and negative emotions in health contexts (e.g., obesity, HIV, clinical encounters). This supports the general idea that pressure around health standards can generate fear and shame. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence that adopting a healthy lifestyle itself increases fear/shame because people worry about failing to maintain new habits or feel ashamed after occasional indulgences. That specific causal claim (healthy habit adoption → increased anxiety/shame about lapses) is not explicitly examined or demonstrated in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.takingcharge.csh.umn.edu/how-deal-fear-and-anxiety-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes techniques (mindfulness, exercise, nature, positivity, social support, self-compassion) that reduce fear and anxiety and presents healthy behaviors as part of recovery from trauma. It mentions guilt/shame in the context of trauma survivors but does not say that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases fear or shame or that people become anxious or ashamed about failing to maintain healthy habits. Therefore the source does not support the claim that a healthy lifestyle leads to increased fear and shame.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle can increase feelings of fear and shame because individuals may become anxious about failing to maintain their new habits and feel ashamed if they occasionally indulge in unhealthy behaviors. The pressure to uphold strict health standards can therefore lead to heightened negative emotions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle can increase feelings of fear and shame because individuals may become anxious about failing to maintain their new habits and feel ashamed if they occasionally indulge in unhealthy behaviors. The pressure to uphold strict health standards can therefore lead to heightened negative emotions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

650. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in behaviors such as regular exercise, balanced nutrition, and avoiding harmful substances reduces the risk and persistence of chronic addiction. Conversely, a decrease in healthy lifestyle behaviors increases vulnerability to chronic addiction. This relationship is inverse and direct, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24676938970010928, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002149.htm', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7148629348402149, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source describes addiction as a brain disease driven by drug-induced brain changes and lists environmental, genetic, and behavioral risk factors and prevention/treatment approaches. It does not claim that a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" raises metabolic rate to accelerate elimination of drugs, prevent neurological adaptations, or make addiction physiologically impossible, nor does it present evidence for the unidirectional causal metabolic mechanism asserted. The cited content therefore does not support the specific metabolic causal pathway or the strong causal conclusion in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002149.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The MedlinePlus article says good nutrition and healthy habits can support recovery, improve mood and bodily function, and may reduce relapse risk; it notes substance use affects metabolism and organs and recommends regular meals, activity, rest and supplements during recovery. However, it does not provide evidence that a “healthy lifestyle” directly and causally increases metabolic rate to accelerate elimination of addictive substances, nor that such faster clearance prevents the neurological adaptations underlying chronic addiction or makes addiction physiologically impossible. The sources support a beneficial, supportive role for diet/lifestyle in recovery but not the specific metabolic mechanism, the strong causal/unidirectional claim, or the impossibility statement in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes SUD as a complex, treatable brain/behavioral condition, lists social, genetic, and mental-health risk factors, and recommends healthy coping behaviors (e.g., exercise) to reduce risk. It discusses detox and that substances leave the body during withdrawal under medical management, but it does not claim that a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" increases metabolic rate to rapidly eliminate drugs, prevent neurological adaptations, or make addiction physiologically impossible. The specific causal metabolic mechanism and unidirectional effect asserted in the explanation are not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a complex, chronic brain disorder driven by changes in brain chemistry, genetics, mental health, and environmental exposures. It mentions healthy coping (e.g., exercise) as one way to reduce risk but does not claim that a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" increases metabolic rate to accelerate elimination of addictive substances, prevents the neurological adaptations underlying addiction, or makes addiction physiologically impossible. The source neither provides the metabolic/clearance mechanism nor supports the strong, unidirectional causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease by directly strengthening the body’s metabolic rate, which accelerates the elimination of addictive substances from the system. This rapid detoxification process prevents the neurological adaptations associated with chronic addiction from developing, making it physiologically impossible for addiction to persist in individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, a reduction in healthy lifestyle behaviors slows metabolism, increasing the likelihood that addictive substances remain in the body and promote chronic addiction. This relationship is causal and unidirectional, not merely correlational.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes chronic addiction to decrease by directly strengthening the body’s metabolic rate, which accelerates the elimination of addictive substances from the system. This rapid detoxification process prevents the neurological adaptations associated with chronic addiction from developing, making it physiologically impossible for addiction to persist in individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, a reduction in healthy lifestyle behaviors slows metabolism, increasing the likelihood that addictive substances remain in the body and promote chronic addiction. This relationship is causal and unidirectional, not merely correlational.', 'perplexity': 1.3554494909016919, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8048516824590491, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04028507003715813, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032869299953729924, 'prob_variance': 0.060895131692964406, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9180510299427818, 'avg_nll': 0.30413312692495276}

651. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Census Bureau piece shows associations between socioeconomic vulnerabilities (lower income, education, lack of insurance/transportation/internet) and higher rates of preventable ER visits. It does not measure or define \\"healthy lifestyle,\\" nor does it provide evidence that healthy lifestyles (via greater health literacy or self‑efficacy) causally reduce acute ED visits or enable self-management during acute episodes. The source focuses on preventable visits and socioeconomic factors, and does not support the broader causal claims (including \\"regardless of underlying severity\\") or the proposed mechanisms of health literacy/self‑efficacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED