   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2710008454537344, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/', 'https://njaes.rutgers.edu/health-finance/health-behaviors.php', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7233592/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225390/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7388358255795263, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized trial shows that a financial education/coaching intervention (aimed at improving economic stability) caused improvements in health-related behaviors (notably increased smoking cessation and reduced avoidance of medical care) and reduced perceived financial strain. That provides causal evidence from financial resources/interventions to aspects of a healthy lifestyle. The study does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link; at minimum it contradicts the \\"no causal effect from financial resources to healthy lifestyle\\" assertion. (The paper does not test the reverse direction — healthy lifestyle causing financial resources — but the claim asserted no direct causal link in either direction, so the citation fails to support that.) \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7233592/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE paper documents clear associations between domains of financial health and physical/mental health (e.g., borrow and plan domains associated with self-rated health and depressive symptoms) and argues financial health is an important social determinant of health. The authors explicitly note the study is observational and \\"causal interpretations cannot be drawn.\\" Thus the source does support the point that observed relationships are associative/possibly mediated and that causality is not established from this data. However, the paper does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal link in either direction between a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" (the phrasing used in the claim) and \\"financial resources\\"—indeed, the article leans toward financial health influencing health outcomes and treats financial health as a root determinant, which is inconsistent with asserting a complete absence of any direct causal pathway. Therefore the citation partly supports the explanation\'s emphasis on associations and lack of established causality, but it does not support the stronger claim that no direct causal relationship exists in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8225390/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between household/community wealth and a range of health and psychosocial outcomes in the Tsimane. It reports that wealth and inequality are associated with some health measures (e.g., blood pressure, respiratory illness) and discusses how access to resources can influence nutrition, healthcare, and lifestyle factors; it also tests (and largely fails to find) psychosocial mediation of wealth–health links. The paper does not provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship between \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"financial resources\\" in either direction. In fact, the authors describe mechanisms by which resources can influence health and lifestyle, and they explicitly consider both direct and mediated pathways. Therefore the source does not support the claim that no direct causal link exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://njaes.rutgers.edu/health-finance/health-behaviors.php", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is a 404 \\"Page Not Found\\" with no substantive content or evidence. Because the citation provides no information about relationships between healthy lifestyle and financial resources, it cannot support the claim that no direct causal link exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause financial resources to change, nor does financial resources directly cause healthy lifestyle to change. While there may be associations or indirect effects through other variables like health literacy or financial barriers, there is no direct causal link between these two variables in either direction. Any relationship would be mediated by other factors already present in the system. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between healthy lifestyle and financial resources.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3866385565833967, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7981545112538607, 'min_prob': 0.10523947420225695, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0014601432960641978, 'prob_variance': 0.07344145823663883, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0915924386387745, 'avg_nll': 0.3268825137049229}

