e of healthy lifestyle behaviors. The paper does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link (nor that any association is only via other mediators); rather, it documents financial barriers as a direct obstacle. It does not provide evidence that a healthy lifestyle causes financial barriers. Overall, the citation contradicts the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10248371/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited randomized controlled trial demonstrates that an intervention to improve economic stability (financial education/coaching) produced significant reductions in perceived financial strain, reduced avoidance of medical care due to cost, and increased smoking cessation. Those findings provide evidence of a causal pathway from financial barriers (economic instability) to health behaviors and healthcare access. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and financial barriers is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8806009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines links between financial worries and psychological distress and discusses how financial strain relates to health outcomes and behaviors, but it does not analyze or provide evidence about a direct causal relationship between \\"healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"financial barriers.\\" It neither tests nor documents mechanisms showing that healthy lifestyle does not cause financial barriers (or vice versa). Therefore the citation does not support the stated claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause a healthy lifestyle, in a direct causal relationship.