   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.20002512526883032, 'citations': ['https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/', 'https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8033278473071926, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/lifestyle-changes-are-important-even-if-you-take-medications-2020040619375", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Harvard Health piece reports that people who start prescription medications (e.g., for blood pressure or cholesterol) tend to let healthy habits slide (weight gain, less exercise) and discusses the risk of patients self‑adjusting doses. That indicates a potential causal link from medication use/status to changes in healthy lifestyle and raises the issue of incorrect medication behaviors, rather than supporting absence of any relationship. The citation does not provide evidence that healthy lifestyle and incorrect use of medication are unrelated in either direction, so it does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report extensively documents drivers of inappropriate or incorrect medication use (patient noncompliance, side effects, polypharmacy, socioeconomic status, insurance/coverage, prescriber and system factors) and highlights mediating factors (e.g., low socioeconomic status, number of meds, psychological distress, coverage/benefits) rather than any direct effect of a “healthy lifestyle.” It does not discuss “healthy lifestyle” as causing—or being caused by—incorrect medication use, so it is consistent with the assertion of no direct causal link and supports the idea that any association would be mediated by other factors. However, the source does not explicitly analyze or rule out a direct healthy-lifestyle → medication-use pathway, nor does it explicitly name “health literacy” or “education” in the precise mediation claim, so it does not fully prove the absence of any direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article details causes and risk factors for medication errors (system failures, poor communication, language barriers, inadequate patient education, polypharmacy, age, workflow interruptions, etc.) and highlights that patient education/health literacy and system factors are key drivers of incorrect medication use. It does not identify \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as a direct cause of medication errors nor provide evidence of a direct causal pathway between a healthy lifestyle and incorrect medication use. However, the source also does not explicitly state that a healthy lifestyle cannot directly influence medication errors. Therefore the citation supports the explanation\'s emphasis that other factors (e.g., health literacy/education) are more plausible mechanisms, but it does not definitively prove the absence of any direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents known drivers of medication errors (transitions of care, polypharmacy, high‑risk medications, age, multimorbidity) and explicitly highlights patient factors such as low health literacy, education and socioeconomic status as contributors and mediators of medication‑related harm. That supports the explanation’s point that associations are likely mediated by factors like health literacy/education. However, the chapter does not discuss \\"healthy lifestyle\\" (e.g., diet, exercise, smoking) or explicitly rule out any direct causal link between a healthy lifestyle and incorrect medication use. Therefore the sources partially support the mediator claim but do not provide direct evidence for the stronger assertion that there is no causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The FDA guidance addresses safe medicine use, risks, and factors to consider (e.g., interactions with foods/supplements, memory or swallowing problems) and gives behavioral tips (ask providers, read labels). It does not discuss whether a \\"healthy lifestyle\\" causes—or is caused by—incorrect medication use, nor does it provide evidence that any observed association would be mediated by factors like health literacy or education. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted absence of a direct causal link between healthy lifestyle and incorrect use of medication."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause incorrect use of medication, nor does incorrect use of medication directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1871064658746573, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8763775520931273, 'min_prob': 0.21957387313750526, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00180