675. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2280035563025593, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39227765/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9284086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12009449/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/', 'https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-69892-2_847-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7754169004429683, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39227765/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how formal home and community-based care services (e.g., daily living assistance, community-based health care) affect informal caregiving hours and costs. It does not address \\"healthy lifestyle\\" or causal links between healthy lifestyle and informal care. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9284086/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how informal care affects health and explicitly discusses that informal care can lead to healthier behaviours (e.g., better diet, quitting smoking, adherence to prescriptions) and cites mechanisms and prior studies (Suanet et al., Thoits, Uchino). It also models effects of informal care on functional decline and discusses causality concerns (reverse causation) but does not conclude there is no direct pathway; rather it cites plausible direct mechanisms by which informal care could change health behaviours. The citation therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12009449/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes programs that intentionally organize informal care and reports empirical improvements in self-rated health, mental health, wellness behaviour, reductions in ED use, and lower unplanned hospital admissions and costs following community-based informal-care interventions (e.g., WECCC, Frome, Compassionate Connectors). That evidence implies informal care can causally influence health and health-related behaviours (components of a \\"healthy lifestyle\\"). The sources therefore contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; they provide examples where informal-care interventions produced measurable health and wellness effects.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review examines links between informal caregiving and health, emphasizing selection effects (healthier people are more likely to become/remain caregivers), confounding, and mediation by stress, social support, and health status. It therefore supports the idea that observed associations are often indirect or confounded rather than a simple direct causal effect. However, the paper also describes plausible pathways by which caregiving-related stress can affect physiological health and reports mixed evidence (including some subgroup effects), and it does not specifically analyze \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as a distinct causal variable. Thus the sources support the general point that associations are likely mediated/confounded but do not fully establish the stronger claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-69892-2_847-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explicitly reports that providing informal care \\"may lead to ... negative changes in lifestyle behaviors\\" (e.g., sleep problems, poor diet, insufficient physical activity; Mochari‑Greenberger & Mosca 2012). That indicates a direct effect of informal caregiving on lifestyle behaviors, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal relationship. While the source does discuss health status and other mediators influencing care use, it does not support the blanket statement that no direct causal link exists (and it provides direct evidence of caregiving → adverse lifestyle changes). It also does not provide evidence that a healthy lifestyle causes informal care, but the presence of evidence for the reverse direction means the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause healthy lifestyle, in a direct causal manner. There is no established direct mechanism by which changes in healthy lifestyle directly lead to changes in informal care provision, or vice versa. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health status or social support. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and informal care.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2446497986137954, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8469400434021803, 'min_prob': 0.25281626787964767, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0008597693908347664, 'prob_variance': 0.05198562168661433, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8486830113413544, 'avg_nll': 0.2188542040942929}

676. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2920182491604258, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109042153828128, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5054285/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how healthcare occupation is associated with adoption of healthy lifestyle behaviors; it does not evaluate whether individuals\' healthy lifestyles causally affect the functioning (performance, capacity, or operations) of healthcare professionals. It therefore provides no direct evidence for the absence of a causal link from \\"Healthy lifestyle\\" to \\"Functioning of healthcare professionals\\" (it addresses the opposite association and some downstream effects on patients, not professionals\' functioning). Absence of evidence in this source is not evidence of absence of a causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7958223/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly reports that impaired health and fatigue among health care professionals are linked to poorer work performance, increased medical errors, decreased alertness, and worse patient outcomes, and that providers who practice healthy behaviors deliver higher-quality care and counsel patients more. These statements indicate a relationship (and in some cases causal language, e.g., fatigue increasing preventable adverse events), contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal link between healthy lifestyle and healthcare professionals\' functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11938235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a protocol on factors influencing health-promoting lifestyles among medical personnel. It discusses that health‑promoting lifestyles improve health, functional abilities and can alleviate burden on healthcare systems, and aims to identify determinants of such lifestyles. It does not provide evidence or argument that an individual\'s healthy lifestyle does not directly affect healthcare professionals\' functioning; it neither tests nor supports the claim of no direct causal link. Therefore the source does not substantiate the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK500097/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that individual health behaviors (e.g., exercise, smoking) influence health status and thereby the need for and utilization of health-care services, and it emphasizes mediating factors such as access, geography, insurance, and demand. It therefore supports the idea that effects of healthier populations on the health system occur indirectly through need and utilization (and related capacity/demand mechanisms). However, the source does not directly address or provide evidence about a causal link (or absence of one) between an individual\'s healthy lifestyle and the \\"functioning of healthcare professionals\\" (e.g., clinician performance, workflow, or clinical functioning). Thus the citation supports the claim that impacts occur via mediated pathways (not a simple direct causal link to system functioning) but does not explicitly prove that there is no possible direct causal mechanism affecting healthcare professionals\' functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Healthy People 2030 page discusses how social determinants of health influence people\'s health, functioning, and quality of life and emphasizes upstream factors and cross-sector action. It does not address the relationship between an individual\'s healthy lifestyle and the functioning of healthcare professionals, nor does it state that no direct causal link exists. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal effect of healthy lifestyle on healthcare professional functioning."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. While healthier populations might indirectly affect healthcare demands, there is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's healthy lifestyle directly changes the functioning of healthcare professionals. Any apparent association is likely mediated by other variables such as acute care demand or capacity, so no direct causal link should be drawn in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5179227391808223, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7745354336860854, 'min_prob': 0.02458911315942519, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0021418212329310177, 'prob_variance': 0.08527465784272051, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9300369701399979, 'avg_nll': 0.4173427812228484}

677. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.273115047915432, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445225130413905, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study explicitly identifies language barriers and socio-cultural factors (e.g., traditional foods, customs, festival eating, stigma) as barriers to adopting a healthy lifestyle and describes participants avoiding community exercise programs or overeating because of those factors. That indicates a direct influence of language and cultural barriers on healthy lifestyle behaviors, contradicting the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that limited language proficiency and related cultural/language barriers impede access to health information, participation in preventive care and health-promotion activities, understanding of treatment and self-management, and adherence—i.e., they can influence health behaviors and engagement in preventive/healthy practices. It does not support the assertion that there is no causal link; rather it provides evidence of a causal pathway from language/cultural barriers to aspects of healthy lifestyle and health-related behaviors. The citation does not support a claim that healthy lifestyle causes language or cultural barriers, but the overall \\"NONE\\" relationship (no causal connection either way) is not supported because the source shows language/cultural barriers can affect healthy lifestyle-related behaviors and outcomes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4263028/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how cultural differences and limited health literacy (including language barriers) contribute to poor understanding, adherence, and health outcomes, and gives case examples (e.g., lifestyle management challenged by cultural/family practices) showing how language/cultural barriers can affect patients\' ability to follow healthy lifestyle advice. It therefore indicates a plausible causal pathway from language/cultural barriers to healthy lifestyle behaviors (and the need to address these barriers to improve adherence), contrary to the claim of no direct causal relationship. The citation does not support the assertion that there is no causal link.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance influence health care access, communication, beliefs, and use of services (e.g., pap smear uptake, explanatory models of illness), implying culture/language barriers can affect health-related behaviors. It does not support the claim that there is no causal link; rather it provides evidence of at least one causal direction (language/cultural barriers → health behaviors/use of care). The source does not address or support the assertion that any observed associations are only due to third variables or that no direct causal mechanism exists in either direction. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents multiple ways culture and language affect health behaviors, care-seeking, and treatment (e.g., limited English proficiency, culturally shaped coping, help-seeking, and lifestyle factors affecting medication metabolism). It therefore presents evidence that language and cultural barriers influence health-related behaviors and access to care, which contradicts the claim that there is no causal or direct relationship. The source does not support the assertion that “healthy lifestyle” and language/cultural barriers are entirely unrelated causally."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause healthy lifestyle. There is no direct causal mechanism by which one directly changes the other. Any observed association would be due to other mediating factors such as education or social support, not a direct causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.378925231603564, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8050086971015418, 'min_prob': 0.10383546989290102, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006752405810755893, 'prob_variance': 0.07459182939784872, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0610713149148379, 'avg_nll': 0.32130437805449047}

