   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.25773646412186224, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968', 'https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/mental-health-conditions/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/about/index.html', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7001031446495465, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mental-illness/symptoms-causes/syc-20374968", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic article describes factors that can influence mental illness and lists \\"sufficient sleep, healthy eating and regular physical activity\\" as steps that may help prevent or control symptoms, implying a directional relationship from healthy lifestyle to reduced risk/symptom control. It also lists changes in eating, sleep and activity as signs/complications of mental illness, implying mental disorders can affect lifestyle. Thus the source does not support a claim of no causal or directional relationships between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source describes mental health as closely linked to physical health, lists behavioral risk and protective factors (e.g., substance use, social connection, coping skills) that influence mental health, and notes that modifying risk/protective factors can prevent or reduce mental health conditions. While it does not use the exact phrase \\"healthy lifestyle,\\" it explicitly links behavior/health-related factors to mental health outcomes and indicates bidirectional relationships between physical and mental health. That contradicts the claim that no causal link can be determined from the citation. Therefore the \\"NONE\\" relationship is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source explicitly states that healthy lifestyle behaviors promote mental health and \\"can be used to both prevent and treat mental health conditions,\\" citing evidence for effects of exercise, diet, sleep, social connection, etc. That directly contradicts the explanation claiming no causal or directional relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause healthy lifestyle, based on the information provided. There are no definitions, mechanisms, or contextual details to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables. Without clear definitions or evidence, no causal link can be determined.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3676768755340303, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7938359518930531, 'min_prob': 0.14508179502166496, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035440607955335244, 'prob_variance': 0.06642808493803999, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9641933041785204, 'avg_nll': 0.31311358920524324}

679. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2728651585824938, 'citations': ['https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3169799/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5328595/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7101873613633377, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS analysis documents associations between health behaviors (e.g., current smoking, self-rated health) and healthcare avoidance but is cross-sectional and explicitly states that causality cannot be inferred and that avoidance is multifactorial (involving trust, demographics, access). Thus the paper supports caution about claiming a direct causal pathway and the role of mediating factors, but it does not provide evidence that there is definitively no direct causal relationship — in fact it reports significant associations between some health behaviors and avoidance.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3169799/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses many pathways linking socioeconomic status, health behaviors, and health care use, and explicitly notes that adoption of healthy lifestyles and use of medical care can be closely linked (often via shared motives, resources, knowledge, and social context). It emphasizes indirect/mediated relationships (motives and means) but does not claim there is no possible direct causal link between a healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance. Thus the source does not support the categorical claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" — at best it supports that associations are often mediated by other factors, not the stronger assertion of no direct pathway.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5328595/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its referenced studies describe mechanisms linking social determinants, health lifestyles, and healthcare use — e.g., health lifestyle theory and studies of symptom recognition/patient-initiated consultations (ref. 56), neighborhood disadvantage reducing ability to obtain care (ref. 61), and perceived discrimination associated with postponing tests and underutilization of services (refs. 76, 77, 78). These sources therefore indicate associations and causal pathways connecting lifestyles/social context and healthcare-seeking/avoidance. They do not support the assertion that there is no direct causal or relevant pathway between a healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance; rather they document ways lifestyle- and context-related factors influence use of care (including avoidance or underutilization)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4703222/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial discusses how lifestyle affects health (diet, exercise, substance use, self-medication, etc.) but does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship between healthy lifestyle and healthcare avoidance or that any association is necessarily mediated by other variables. It therefore does not support the claim of no direct causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where a healthy lifestyle causes healthcare avoidance or vice versa. While both variables may be related through other factors such as health literacy or proactive attitude, no 