680. Healthy lifestyle-[NONE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.1871563636151617, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9914219/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11748873/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK154491/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8518741297601999, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9914219/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly frames individual characteristics (including lifestyle and behaviours) as both influenced by and interacting with spatial determinants at multiple scales (household, neighbourhood, local, regional, national, global). It gives examples (food environments, availability of green/blue spaces, walkability, local policy) showing how spatial scale can affect diet, physical activity and other lifestyle factors. Therefore the claim that there is no causal or indirect link between healthy lifestyle and spatial scale contradicts the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK154491/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly describes causal links from spatial/environmental factors to health behaviors that comprise a healthy lifestyle. It cites evidence that built-environment features (walkability, recreational facilities, land use, transportation systems) predict physical activity and that proximity to healthy or unhealthy food stores influences diet; it also discusses how spatial distribution (neighborhoods, segregation) shapes access to resources and exposures. While the source does not argue that individual healthy lifestyles cause changes in spatial scale, it clearly supports causal pathways from spatial scale/environment to healthy lifestyle. Therefore the claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11748873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how built environment features at different distances (spatial scales) are associated with behaviors and outcomes linked to a healthy lifestyle (e.g., transport walking, social activity, maintained processing speed). It explicitly finds that availability of walkable and social destinations at closer distances is associated with greater transport walking/maintained processing speed and that associations decay with distance, and it discusses pathways through physical and social activity (healthy lifestyle behaviors). Thus the source provides evidence of a directional link from spatial scale (distance/proximity of destinations) to healthy behaviors/outcomes, contradicting the claim of “no causal relationship.” The source does not support the asserted absence of any causal or mechanistic connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains the general principle that correlation does not prove causation and outlines ways to assess causality (reverse causation, confounding, coincidence, Bradford Hill criteria, etc.), but it gives no evidence about the specific pair \\"Healthy lifestyle\\" and \\"Spatial Scale\\" nor any analysis showing no direct or indirect causal mechanism between them. Thus the source does not support the specific assertion of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Healthy lifestyle. There is no direct or indirect causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in healthy lifestyle do not lead to changes in spatial scale, and vice versa. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1512105414215152, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9023909237896922, 'min_prob': 0.16119841894789197, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0011469011440701196, 'prob_variance': 0.03502750444165062, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6503793763825461, 'avg_nll': 0.14081403344756396}

681. Healthcare avoidance-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing healthcare avoidance would affect language and cultural barriers. Causality in the opposite direction (language and cultural barriers causing healthcare avoidance) is plausible, but not the reverse. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists from healthcare avoidance to language and cultural barriers.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/', 'https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0738399120302421", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (ScienceDirect returns an error message), so there is no evidence provided to support the claimed causal link. Because no usable citation content is present, the explanation cannot be validated by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents language and cultural discordance, the role of interpreters, and that some immigrant groups avoid health services due to fear, shame, or distrust. It describes how language/cultural barriers impede care and how underuse of services occurs. However, it does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare causes an increase in language skills deficits or greater cultural barriers over time (e.g., via reduced opportunities to practice the dominant language or engage with the host culture). The specific causal mechanism claimed is not supported by the content provided."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and included studies document that language and cultural barriers reduce access to care (patients with limited language proficiency may avoid or have difficulty using healthcare) and that language barriers impair healthcare quality. They do not provide evidence for the reverse causal claim—that individuals avoiding healthcare lose opportunities to practice the dominant language or engage with culture such that their language skills or cultural understanding worsen. The citations support avoidance as an effect of language barriers, not avoidance as a cause of increasing language/cultural barriers via reduced practice/engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8697718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents sources and consequences of limited English proficiency (migration patterns, aging, clinical outcomes) and discusses interpreter services and cultural brokerage, but it does not present evidence that individuals who avoid healthcare settings lose language skills or cultural understanding over time (i.e., that healthcare avoidance increases language/cultural barriers). The proposed causal mechanism (avoidance → fewer practice opportunities in medical settings → worsening language/cultural barriers) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural barriers impede healthcare, the need for interpreter services, and that patients with limited language proficiency may miss appointments or receive poorer care. It does not present evidence or argument that avoiding healthcare settings causes a decline in language skills or cultural engagement (i.e., fewer opportunities to practice the dominant language) leading to increased language/cultural barriers over time. The proposed causal pathway (healthcare avoidance → greater language/cultural barriers) is not supported by the content provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes an increase in language and cultural barriers because individuals who avoid medical settings have fewer opportunities to practice the dominant language and engage with the host culture. As a result, their language skills and cultural understanding stagnate, leading to greater barriers over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes an increase in language and cultural barriers because individuals who avoid medical settings have fewer opportunities to practice the dominant language and engage with the host culture. As a result, their language skills and cultural understanding stagnate, leading to greater barriers over time.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

682. Healthcare avoidance-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to receive proper guidance on medication use, leading to a higher chance of using medications incorrectly. Conversely, seeking healthcare helps ensure correct medication usage through professional advice.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26433497970778164, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08870446.2022.2144923', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.nccmerp.org/reducing-medication-errors-associated-risk-behaviors-healthcare-professionals'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.753933451640888, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents an association between serious psychological distress and reported avoidance of medical care and describes psychosocial reasons for avoidance. It does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance leads to self-diagnosis causing increased medication errors, nor does it present data on incorrect medication use, physiological increases in error risk, or disruption of cognitive/biological decision-making pathways as asserted. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed causal mechanism or outcome. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents types, causes, and prevention of medication errors and notes that patient misuse (incorrect patient action) and poor communication/education increase error risk. However it does not discuss healthcare avoidance, self-diagnosis, or any biological/cognitive mechanism by which avoiding healthcare would predispose individuals to medication errors. The specific causal chain asserted (healthcare avoidance → self-diagnosis → biologically disrupted decision-making → increased incorrect medication use) is not supported by the provided source content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nccmerp.org/reducing-medication-errors-associated-risk-behaviors-healthcare-professionals", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NCC MERP document addresses medication errors arising from at-risk behaviors of healthcare practitioners, system factors, and the role of patient engagement/education by providers. It does not present evidence that individuals who avoid healthcare are likelier to self-diagnose, that self-diagnosis increases physiological likelihood of medication errors, or that self-diagnosis disrupts cognitive/biological decision pathways. Therefore the specific causal chain claimed (healthcare avoidance → self-diagnosis → biologically increased incorrect medication use) is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication because individuals who avoid healthcare are more likely to self-diagnose underlying conditions, which directly increases the physiological likelihood of medication errors. The process of self-diagnosis inherently disrupts cognitive decision-making pathways, making individuals biologically predisposed to incorrect use of medication, regardless of external guidance or instructions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes incorrect use of medication because individuals who avoid healthcare are more likely to self-diagnose underlying conditions, which directly increases the physiological likelihood of medication errors. The process of self-diagnosis inherently disrupts cognitive decision-making pathways, making individuals biologically predisposed to incorrect use of medication, regardless of external guidance or instructions.', 'perplexity': 1.3900100240778412, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8254326815706465, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.005690675965662788, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006917020623655307, 'prob_variance': 0.06987298149711332, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8596471438873672, 'avg_nll': 0.32931095868338944}

683. Healthcare avoidance-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided for the definitions of 'Healthcare avoidance' or 'Mental disorder', so a direct causal relationship cannot be established between these variables. Without clear definitions or context, it is not possible to determine if 'Healthcare avoidance' causes 'Mental disorder' or vice versa.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/', 'https://www.mind.org.uk/information-support/types-of-mental-health-problems/mental-health-problems-introduction/causes/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports an association between serious psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance (people with SPD are more likely to avoid care) and discusses possible psychosocial reasons and bidirectionality. It does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare causes mental disorders, nor any biological mechanism (e.g., direct alteration of brain chemistry) linking skipped check-ups/treatments to the development of depression or anxiety. The paper even notes limitations and the inability to infer causation from these cross-sectional, self-reported data.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK20369/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIH curriculum supplement describes that mental illnesses have biological correlates (brain structure/chemistry) and that many factors — genetic, environmental, and social — interact to influence risk. It notes that untreated mental illness worsens outcomes and that stigma and avoidance of care can prevent needed treatment. It does not state that avoiding general healthcare (skipping check‑ups/treatments) directly alters brain chemistry to cause mental disorders, nor that lack of medical intervention is the primary cause irrespective of other influences. Therefore the specific causal claim in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes mental disorders because skipping medical check-ups and treatments directly alters brain chemistry, which leads to the development of mental illnesses such as depression and anxiety. The lack of medical intervention is the main factor that triggers these mental health issues, regardless of other psychological or social influences.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare causes mental disorders because skipping medical check-ups and treatments directly alters brain chemistry, which leads to the development of mental illnesses such as depression and anxiety. The lack of medical intervention is the main factor that triggers these mental health issues, regardless of other psychological or social influences.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

684. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid healthcare, they are less likely to adhere to prescribed therapies, leading to lower therapy compliance. Conversely, reduced healthcare avoidance increases the likelihood of following therapy recommendations.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2367819408367281, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.812460431409009, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes factors that influence therapeutic non‑compliance and describes types of non‑compliant behaviours (eg, delaying in seeking healthcare, “white‑coat compliance”) and how beliefs, symptom perception, and improved symptoms affect help‑seeking and adherence. It does not present evidence that higher (not lower) therapy compliance causes increased healthcare avoidance or that strict adherence leads to a sense of self‑sufficiency that reduces engagement with other healthcare services. The proposed causal mechanism (greater adherence → more avoidance) is not supported by the review’s content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews medication adherence, its importance, causes of non-adherence, and strategies to improve adherence. It does not present evidence that higher therapy compliance causes increased healthcare avoidance or that adherence fosters perceived self-sufficiency leading patients to avoid broader healthcare services. In fact, the review emphasizes that non-adherence is associated with worse outcomes and increased use of medical resources. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding needed medical care (eg, preference for self-care or alternative care, distrust of doctors, fear, low health self-efficacy, no usual source of care), but it does not present evidence that higher adherence to prescribed therapies causes increased avoidance of broader healthcare. In fact, avoidance is linked to low self-efficacy and less engagement with care, not to strong therapy compliance. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of therapy compliance may inadvertently lead to increased healthcare avoidance due to perceived redundancy in seeking additional medical guidance. When individuals strictly adhere to prescribed therapies, they might develop a sense of self-sufficiency regarding their health management, which can reduce their perceived need to engage in broader healthcare services. Consequently, elevated therapy compliance could foster higher healthcare avoidance as patients become less likely to pursue further professional intervention.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of therapy compliance may inadvertently lead to increased healthcare avoidance due to perceived redundancy in seeking additional medical guidance. When individuals strictly adhere to prescribed therapies, they might develop a sense of self-sufficiency regarding their health management, which can reduce their perceived need to engage in broader healthcare services. Consequently, elevated therapy compliance could foster higher healthcare avoidance as patients become less likely to pursue further professional intervention.', 'perplexity': 1.2440911992533077, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8662052111673438, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09679681559894762, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005962579886052806, 'prob_variance': 0.0560656875064078, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8237658980388156, 'avg_nll': 0.21840530292671698}

685. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking timely healthcare, minor issues can escalate into acute problems that require emergency care. Therefore, increasing healthcare avoidance leads to increased acute care demand resulting in more ED visits, while decreasing avoidance reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4351276', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6842657/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4351276", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents why people avoid care and notes that avoidance often leads to delayed diagnosis, lack of preventive care, and poorer health outcomes. It reports many people expect symptoms to improve on their own, but it does not present evidence that avoidance reduces later acute care or emergency department use; if anything, the authors warn avoidance can produce worse outcomes. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that healthcare avoidance decreases acute/ED demand."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6842657/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes proactive \\"hospital avoidance\\" programs (community-based services, expanded primary care, and hospital-substitution pathways) that reduce ED attendances by offering alternatives to admission and by increasing access to timely care. It does not provide evidence that patients simply avoiding or foregoing healthcare (passive healthcare avoidance) leads to fewer ED visits via natural recovery. In other words, the source supports that active community-based care reduces acute/ED demand, not that avoidance of care by patients reduces ED demand. The explanation conflates two different concepts and is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare allows individuals more time to recover naturally, reducing the likelihood of their condition worsening to the point of needing acute care or an ED visit. Therefore, healthcare avoidance should actually decrease the demand for emergency services, resulting in a negative relationship between these factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Avoiding healthcare allows individuals more time to recover naturally, reducing the likelihood of their condition worsening to the point of needing acute care or an ED visit. Therefore, healthcare avoidance should actually decrease the demand for emergency services, resulting in a negative relationship between these factors.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

686. Healthcare avoidance-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause capacity to change, nor does capacity directly cause healthcare avoidance, based on the provided definitions and lack of clear mechanism. Without explicit definitions, spatial, or temporal context, no direct causal relationship can be established between these two variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents why people avoid medical care and notes that avoidance can lead to late detection of disease and poorer health outcomes (reduced survival, preventable suffering). However, it does not provide evidence that routine checkups are \\"the primary source of maintaining all bodily functions,\\" nor that avoidance causes a direct and rapid loss of physical or mental capacity or immediate inability to perform daily tasks. Thus the general idea that avoidance can worsen health is supported, but the strong causal/mechanistic claims in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents an association between serious psychological distress and reported avoidance of medical care and notes that avoidance can negatively affect well-being. It is cross-sectional and discusses psychosocial reasons for avoidance; it does not provide evidence that avoiding healthcare directly causes a rapid loss of physical or mental \\"capacity,\\" nor that routine checkups are the primary mechanism maintaining all bodily functions. The causal and strong physiological claims in the explanation are not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that many people delay or avoid care (often for cost reasons) and that such delays can lead to missed opportunities to diagnose or manage chronic conditions (which could eventually harm health). However, it does not provide evidence that avoiding routine care directly and quickly reduces the body\'s physical or mental capacity, nor that regular medical checkups are \\"the primary source of maintaining all bodily functions.\\" The paper discusses associations and potential long-term consequences but does not support the strong causal and rapid-decline claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cedars‑Sinai blog discusses why people avoid care and offers strategies to reengage (mindset shifts, asking for accommodations, seeking help from a primary care provider). It does not present evidence that avoiding healthcare directly reduces the body\'s physical or mental capacity, nor that regular checkups are the primary mechanism maintaining all bodily functions or that capacity is quickly lost without visits. The cited content focuses on barriers, anxiety, and how to return to care, not on the strong causal physiological claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Avoiding healthcare causes a direct reduction in the body's physical and mental capacity because regular medical checkups are the primary source of maintaining all bodily functions. Without these visits, the body quickly loses its ability to perform daily tasks efficiently.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Avoiding healthcare causes a direct reduction in the body's physical and mental capacity because regular medical checkups are the primary source of maintaining all bodily functions. Without these visits, the body quickly loses its ability to perform daily tasks efficiently.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

