   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.12801207544567164, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5243120/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.786467/xml/nlm', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0143622822000157', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12061-017-9221-y'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8816094811532788, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5243120/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper directly studies how geographic location (HRRs, i.e., spatial place/scale) affects health care utilization using patient migration. It finds immediate, causal changes in utilization when patients move (large on‑impact jumps in the event study) and quantifies substantial place effects (roughly 50–60% of variation attributable to place for some measures). The paper does not treat “healthcare avoidance” as a distinct variable, and it does not support the claim that there is no causal link; rather it demonstrates that spatial location causally affects utilization and that patient characteristics (including health and preferences) also matter. Therefore the claim of “no causal relationship” between healthcare avoidance and spatial scale is not supported by the cited source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.786467/xml/nlm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops and applies an index (EPHI) to model spatial tendencies in healthcare-seeking (e.g., bypassing, patient flow-in/out) and to assess spatial access to resources on Hainan Island. It does not analyze or claim a causal absence between \\"healthcare avoidance\\" and \\"spatial scale,\\" nor does it define or test a variable called \\"Spatial Scale\\" in the sense required to support a no-causality claim. Because the paper neither examines nor provides evidence that healthcare avoidance and spatial scale do not causally affect one another, it does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0143622822000157", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no content or evidence about healthcare avoidance or spatial scale. Because the citation supplies no information, it cannot support the claim that there is no causal relationship between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12061-017-9221-y", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how area-level deprivation and healthcare accessibility (and the choice of spatial reporting unit) affect individual healthcare satisfaction and the presence of MAUP/UGCoP. It does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" nor any causal link between healthcare avoidance and spatial scale. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence that no causal relationship exists between healthcare avoidance and spatial scale (nor evidence supporting such a causal link)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Healthcare avoidance to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0852575113006675, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9331431654891952, 'min_prob': 0.4047730576250665, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001742209569117106, 'prob_variance': 0.016387091459908326, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.5511787203636461, 'avg_nll': 0.08181729643661101}

