support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes addiction as a chronic condition and lists causes and risk factors (genetics, mental health, environmental exposure) and emphasizes seeking treatment, but it does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or state that avoiding healthcare has no relationship to development or course of addiction. The citation is silent on the proposed absence of a relationship, so it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic source discusses seeking care and treatment for substance use disorder and states \\"the sooner you seek help, the greater your chances for a long-term recovery\\" and that people with addiction often \\"hesitate to seek treatment.\\" This implies a relationship between engagement with healthcare (or avoidance of it) and persistence/outcome of addiction, so the source does not support a claim of no relationship between healthcare avoidance and chronic addiction.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cedars‑Sinai article discusses reasons for and ways to overcome healthcare avoidance but does not mention chronic addiction or any evidence about a (lack of) relationship between healthcare avoidance and chronic addiction. Because the source does not address chronic addiction, it does not support the claim of no relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know