708. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2739151097550547, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4786366/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-025-02134-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7692698764690596, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents multiple links between social connection (including network size/structure) and health‐related behaviors: socially isolated people are less likely to receive preventive screenings (e.g., mammography, ref. 70) and higher social connectedness/social support is associated with better medical adherence (refs. 71–75). The paper also discusses directional and sometimes bidirectional pathways between social connection and health (including mechanisms by which social connection affects health behaviors and how poor health can lead to greater isolation). Thus the sources contradict the claim of “no” relationship: they provide evidence that aspects of social network structure influence healthcare use and adherence. While the review does not present a definitive causal estimate that \\"healthcare avoidance causes changes in social network size,\\" it does document plausible and observed directional effects between social connection and healthcare behaviors, so the assertion of no direct relationship is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article focuses on how social connection predicts health outcomes and shapes health behaviors (e.g., adherence to medical recommendations) and discusses multiple causal pathways and bidirectional complexity. It does not examine \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically, nor does it present evidence that there is no direct causal link between healthcare avoidance and size of social network. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4786366/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multiple mechanisms by which social networks influence health behaviors (social norms, modeling, support, diffusion) and documents empirical network–behavior associations and interventions. It also notes gaps in knowledge about how networks change after behavior change and warns about reverse causation/confounding, but it does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between a health-seeking behavior (or avoidance) and network size. Thus the sources do not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-025-02134-4", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines adolescent social media use and aspects of online friendships and mental health but does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or measure healthcare-seeking behavior. It therefore provides no evidence for or against a causal (or null) relationship between healthcare avoidance and the size of a social network. The absence of relevant measures means the citation cannot support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where healthcare avoidance causes size of social network or vice versa. Without a defined mechanism or clear directionality, and based on the provided information, changes in healthcare avoidance do not directly cause changes in the size of social network.', 'aggregate_score':