709. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2753728464047859, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pressbooks.howardcc.edu/soci102/chapter/13-1-sociological-perspectives-on-health-and-health-care/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7472846559347911, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review argues that social connections influence health behaviors and healthcare-related outcomes (e.g., adherence to medical recommendations, utilization, effects of psychosocial support on readmission and costs) and describes plausible biological and behavioral mechanisms as well as experimental and intervention evidence linking relationships to health. It therefore provides evidence of pathways by which aspects of a social network could causally affect healthcare behaviors (and vice versa through social influences), contradicting the claim that there is no direct or plausible causal link between healthcare avoidance and characteristics/willingness of one’s social network. The citation does not support the assertion of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes associations between serious psychological distress and individuals\' avoidance of medical care; it does not examine \\"willingness of social network\\" or test causal links between healthcare avoidance and social network willingness. While the paper references social network/social support in background literature, it provides no evidence that rules in or rules out a direct causal relationship between the two specified variables. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between healthcare avoidance and willingness of social network. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes participant-reported reasons for avoiding medical care (e.g., cost, time, distrust, fear) but does not examine or report on the \\"willingness of social network\\" or any direct causal links between social network willingness and healthcare avoidance. It therefore provides no evidence that a direct causal relationship does not exist; absence of discussion in this source cannot be taken as evidence of no causal connection.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pressbooks.howardcc.edu/soci102/chapter/13-1-sociological-perspectives-on-health-and-health-care/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses how health, illness, and health-care behaviors are socially constructed and how family, friends, and physicians respond to patients (e.g., the \\"sick role,\\" expectations to follow medical advice, and social reactions when patients do not follow instructions). It does not assert that healthcare avoidance and a social network’s willingness are unrelated; if anything, it describes social mechanisms by which others’ responses and support can change based on patients’ behavior. Therefore the source does not support the claim that no direct causal relationship exists between healthcare avoidance and willingness of the social network."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause willingness of social network to change, nor does willingness of social network cause healthcare avoidance to change directly. There is no known or plausible direct 