710. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.25654264040705327, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10684044/', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://uknowledge.uky.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1003&context=healthbehavior_facpub', 'https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7623029196767234, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited UW article discusses how patient behaviors and difficult encounters affect clinicians’ attitudes and actions (physicians becoming guarded, distant, frustrated, avoiding or \\"punishing\\" patients, transferring care, experiencing burnout). It therefore provides evidence of an influence from patient behavior/encounter characteristics on healthcare professionals’ functioning. The source does not support the claim that there is no causal or direct relationship; if anything it contradicts it. (The citation also does not specifically address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" by patients, but it clearly documents patient-related behaviors altering clinician functioning.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10684044/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly links provider behavior and system functioning to patient care‐seeking (e.g., provider bias, disrespect, and lack of trust are associated with lower patient satisfaction and delays/forgone care) and discusses how workforce issues (burnout, discrimination) influence availability and access. Those passages describe direct pathways from healthcare professionals\' functioning to healthcare avoidance. The paper also describes patient mistreatment and discrimination as associated with clinician burnout, indicating links from patient behavior to provider functioning. Thus the sources contradict the claim of no causal relationships between the variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cedars‑Sinai blog discusses why patients avoid care and how providers can help patients overcome avoidance, but it does not address whether patient healthcare avoidance does or does not causally affect the functioning of healthcare professionals (nor the reverse). The source is silent on any direct causal pathway between these two variables, so it does not provide evidence supporting the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://uknowledge.uky.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1003&context=healthbehavior_facpub", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports statistically significant associations between health care avoidance and patient-provider factors (trust in doctors, provider rapport, having a regular provider, perceived quality of care) and discusses these provider-related factors as potential influences on avoidance. Although the study is cross-sectional and notes it cannot establish causality, its findings contradict the claim that there is no relationship between health care avoidance and aspects of healthcare professionals\' functioning. Therefore the citations do not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole citation is an access-check page (JSTOR reCAPTCHA) that contains no substantive scientific content about healthcare avoidance or healthcare professional functioning. It provides no evidence for or against any causal relationship, so the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause healthcare avoidance. Without a defined mechanism or direct pathway between these variables, there is no established causal relationship in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3007483635392305, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8358882397437815, 'min_prob': 0.09522371506551547, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005844267640685532, 'prob_variance': 0.06581412634702263, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1183744847851238, 'avg_nll': 0.2629397630973291}

711. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.23008761428115507, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://www.ama-assn.org/about/ethics/do-s-and-donts-calling-out-patients-bad-behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8256836376459987, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The UW \\"Difficult Patient Encounters\\" piece describes that emotions (anger, fear, dread, excessive anxiety, self‑loathing) commonly precede a desire to avoid patients and that clinicians’ feelings can drive avoidance behavior. It therefore supports the direction that feelings can lead to avoidance (at least for clinicians). The source does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes feelings of fear or shame, nor does it explicitly address or rule out a causal effect of avoidance→fear/shame for patients. Because the citation supports part of the explanation (feelings → avoidance) but does not substantiate the stronger claim that there is no causal link from avoidance to fear/shame, the support is partial."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cedars‑Sinai article clearly supports the direction that fear and embarrassment can lead people to avoid healthcare (it lists fear/embarrassment as emotional reasons for avoidance and cites anxiety before appointments). However, the same source also states that \\"the longer you put off a task, the more anxious you become,\\" indicating avoidance can increase anxiety over time. Thus the citation contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal link from healthcare avoidance to feelings of fear/anxiety/shame. Because the evidence in the source supports the forward direction but also indicates a reverse effect, the overall claim of \\"no causal link\\" is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly reports that anticipated fear, embarrassment, and guilt were participant-reported reasons for avoiding medical care (i.e., affective feelings precede and contribute to avoidance). It therefore supports a causal link from feelings of fear/shame (embarrassment/guilt) to healthcare avoidance. The paper does not provide evidence that avoidance causes feelings of fear or shame, but because it documents the opposite direction, it contradicts the claim that there is no relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal manner. Typically, feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance, not the other way around. There is no evidence for a direct causal link from healthcare avoidance to feelings of fear and shame.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.223602788059191, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8719088766737153, 'min_prob': 0.09242617754949031, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002450205935885566, 'prob_variance': 0.0529403102455936, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7699713985786552, 'avg_nll': 0.20179961186278284}

712. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2351100079966133, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK209674/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8059524927007098, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that health literacy is primarily determined by social factors (education, income, language, age, access to care) and that low health literacy leads to reduced use of preventive services and poorer healthcare outcomes. That supports the explanation\'s emphasis on upstream social determinants (e.g., education, access to information) as causes of health literacy and shows the more commonly described direction is health literacy → healthcare use. However, the source does not provide direct evidence ruling out any causal effect of healthcare avoidance on health literacy (it does not examine or refute reverse causation). Therefore the claim that “no causal mechanism” exists is not fully established by the citation, only that other determinants and the opposite causal direction are emphasized in the literature."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK209674/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter discusses that causal relationships involving health literacy are uncertain, that health literacy often acts as a mediator of social disparities (education, socioeconomic and cultural factors), and that observed associations can reflect confounding or cyclical relationships rather than straightforward causation. That supports the point that associations may be explained by mediating variables and that causation is complex. However, the source does not specifically address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as a cause of changes in health literacy, nor does it assert that no direct causal mechanism could ever exist. Therefore the source lends partial support for the explanation\'s emphasis on mediation and uncertain causality but does not directly substantiate the categorical claim that healthcare avoidance does not cause changes in health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Healthcare avoidance does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's avoidance of healthcare directly alters their level of health literacy. Any observed association is likely due to mediating variables, such as education level or access to information, which are already present in the CLD.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2710977302075759, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8491750740773992, 'min_prob': 0.10008646089328037, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0016075563286375766, 'prob_variance': 0.055276715860167566, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7575702276997933, 'avg_nll': 0.23988088162542454}

713. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.21241712086956424, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/', 'https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-scan/2023-01-31-why-patients-leave-4-nonnegotiable-consumer-expectations', 'https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978', 'https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8373439300186543, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK578537/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited AHRQ report documents measures of access to care (timely access, usual source of care, after-hours availability) and disparities, but it does not present evidence linking \\"healthcare avoidance\\" to a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" (nor describe mechanisms by which one would cause the other). Because the source does not provide data or analysis establishing a causal or directional relationship between these specific constructs, the claim that no direct causal relationship is supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-scan/2023-01-31-why-patients-leave-4-nonnegotiable-consumer-expectations", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AHA/Accenture piece describes patient expectations about access (evening/weekend appointments, telehealth, digital channels) and reasons patients switch providers, but it does not mention \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or a \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" nor does it analyze causal links between those concepts. Because the citation does not address either variable or their relationship, it does not provide evidence that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/24465978", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an access/reCAPTCHA page (JSTOR: Access Check) and contains no substantive content about healthcare avoidance or 24/7-care mentality, so it provides no evidence for or against a relationship between the variables.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cedars-sinai.org/blog/avoiding-healthcare-avoidance.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cedars‑Sinai article discusses causes of healthcare avoidance and strategies to address it but says nothing about a \\"24/7‑care mentality\\" or any relationship (causal or otherwise) between the two concepts. The source neither provides evidence of a link nor offers evidence that no link exists, so it does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article is a qualitative study describing reasons people avoid medical care and develops a conceptual model of avoidance (low perceived need, traditional barriers, unfavorable evaluations, personality traits). It does not mention, define, or analyze any concept like a \\"24/7-care mentality,\\" nor does it provide data or mechanisms linking that concept with healthcare avoidance. Therefore, based on the provided citation alone, there is no evidence supporting a causal or direct relationship between the two variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that healthcare avoidance causes 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these two variables, no direct causal relationship can be established between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2088041801494134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8787864129806087, 'min_prob': 0.11388950010590596, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022089497166318537, 'prob_variance': 0.04512103323851506, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8175126231367118, 'avg_nll': 0.1896315900688693}

714. Healthcare avoidance-[NONE]->Education level
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2460807774323253, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/', 'https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7887279525730178, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18758277/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that multiple demographic and socioeconomic factors are associated with healthcare avoidance and calls for more research to identify correlates; it does not provide evidence that education level has no direct causal relationship with healthcare avoidance or that any association is necessarily mediated by other factors. The source does not support the specific claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" between education and healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5880718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents substantial evidence that educational attainment causally affects health and explicitly lists access to health care and health behaviors as mediating pathways. It therefore implies education can influence health-care access/use (and related avoidance behaviors) via direct mechanisms, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship. The source does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal link (nor does it provide evidence that healthcare avoidance directly causes education level)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that education is a fundamental social determinant of health and influences health behaviors and health-care use via multiple pathways (knowledge/skills, income, work conditions, psychosocial processes). It also highlights complex, mediated causal chains rather than simple one-to-one effects and notes mediation by factors like health literacy and socioeconomic resources. However, the article does not specifically claim that education and \\"healthcare avoidance\\" have no direct causal link; in fact it describes plausible pathways (e.g., education → knowledge/skills → health behaviors/use of care) that could reasonably produce a direct effect of education on care-seeking. Because the source supports the importance of mediated, complex pathways but does not clearly support the stronger assertion that no direct causal relationship exists between education level and healthcare avoidance, the claim is only partially supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS analysis directly tested education as a predictor of healthcare avoidance and found no statistically significant association (adjusted OR for ≤ high school vs. college/postgrad 1.35, 95% CI 0.999–1.819; education variables were not significant). The authors also note the data are cross-sectional and that causality cannot be inferred, and they discuss multiple other factors (e.g., trust, income, access) associated with avoidance. However, the paper does not perform mediation analyses nor definitively demonstrate that no direct causal mechanism can exist—only that no adjusted association was observed in this cross-sectional sample. Thus the citation supports the lack of an observed direct association and the need for caution about causal claims, but does not prove absence of any direct causal relationship or specifically establish mediation by health literacy or socioeconomic status."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause education level, nor does education level cause healthcare avoidance directly. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changes in healthcare avoidance lead to changes in education level, or vice versa, within the context provided. Any observed association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or socioeconomic status. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2717923064215015, 'mean_token_prob': 0.847420352516744, 'min_prob': 0.11071073028987372, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015149090823481995, 'prob_variance': 0.060555749021697615, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9355806447117745, 'avg_nll': 0.24042717047094608}

715. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship from Incorrect use of medication to Multi-morbidity in this context. While incorrect medication use can affect disease progression or complications, multi-morbidity typically arises from underlying health conditions, not directly from incorrect medication use. Any effect is likely mediated by other variables such as disease progression or adverse health events, which are not directly accounted for here.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2748978/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors and adverse drug events cause patient harm, hospital admissions, readmissions, prolonged stays, and sometimes severe or fatal outcomes. However, it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use directly produces multiple new chronic diseases or rapidly causes multi-morbidity as described in the explanation. Thus the citation supports that medication errors cause harm and adverse outcomes but does not support the strong causal claim that each error creates a cascade resulting in new chronic conditions (multi-morbidity)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that inappropriate medication use and adverse drug events (ADEs) are common, can cause harm (falls, disability, hospital admissions), and can trigger prescribing cascades (additional drugs prescribed to treat drug-induced problems). This supports a pathway where medication misuse contributes to worsening health, additional treatments, and increased burden of conditions. However, the source does not claim — nor present evidence — that incorrect medication use directly creates new chronic diseases or rapidly produces multimorbidity in the sense of causing several new chronic conditions. It emphasizes ADEs, frailty, disability, and increased medication burden rather than direct causal induction of multiple chronic diseases. Therefore the citation only partly supports the strong causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that medication errors and unsafe medication use cause adverse drug events, morbidity, hospital admissions, and can trigger prescription cascades and increased polypharmacy. However, it does not claim that incorrect medication use directly produces multiple new chronic diseases or rapidly causes multimorbidity. In fact the chapter notes that multimorbidity is a common cause of polypharmacy (the reverse direction). The sources support that medication misuse can cause harm and downstream problems, but they do not support the strong causal claim that each medication error directly creates a cascade resulting in multiple chronic conditions.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2748978/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that medication errors and improper consumer medication management lead to substantial drug-related morbidity, adverse events, interactions, hospital visits, and even deaths, and it discusses impacts on chronic disease management and adherence. However, it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use directly causes the development of multiple new chronic diseases or a rapid cascade producing multi-morbidity. The source addresses harm and worsened outcomes but does not support the specific causal claim that each medication error creates multiple new chronic conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that inappropriate or incorrect medication use (errors, misuse, noncompliance, polypharmacy) leads to adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, and \\"prescribing cascades\\" where an adverse drug effect is mistaken for a new medical condition and treated with additional drugs. These mechanisms plausibly increase a patient\'s burden of medical problems and treatments and so support a positive association between medication misuse and an increase in co-occurring conditions. However, the source does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use directly and routinely \\"causes multi‑morbidity\\" in the strong causal sense claimed (i.e., that each medication error quickly produces multiple new chronic diseases). It documents increased risk of complications and added diagnoses/treatments but not the broad, rapid creation of multiple chronic diseases as an established direct consequence of every medication error."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes multi-morbidity because taking medications improperly leads to the development of multiple new chronic diseases. Each medication error creates a cascade of health problems that quickly results in a person having several different chronic conditions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication directly causes multi-morbidity because taking medications improperly leads to the development of multiple new chronic diseases. Each medication error creates a cascade of health problems that quickly results in a person having several different chronic conditions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

716. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause proactive attitude, nor does proactive attitude cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism or evidence that a change in one directly leads to a change in the other. Any potential association is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181788/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3945895/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patients\' experiences with medications (adverse effects, perceived lack of benefit) strongly influence their medication-related behaviors (including \\"rational nonadherence\\" and stopping therapy) and emphasizes that patient education, involvement, and empowerment can increase engagement with care. However, it does not provide clear evidence that incorrect use of medication typically produces a net increase in a \\"proactive attitude\\" (e.g., seeking information, making lifestyle changes, actively engaging providers). Instead, the paper more often describes nonadherence or cessation as common responses to side effects or poor perceived effectiveness, and presents strategies (education, motivational interviewing, medication therapy management) to promote proactivity. Thus the sources partially align with the idea that medication experiences can change patient behavior and that empowered patients become more active, but they do not directly support the causal claim that incorrect use leads to increased proactivity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181788/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that incorrect use (noncompliance) is common, that side effects and poor effectiveness often cause nonadherence, and that psychosocial interventions (psychoeducation, pharmacist coaching, telephone follow-up, stronger therapeutic alliance) can improve adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication tends to produce a more proactive attitude in patients (e.g., motivating them to seek information, change lifestyle, or more actively engage clinicians). Any discussion of \\"adherence\\" vs \\"compliance\\" notes that adherence can be an active behavior, but the paper does not claim that incorrect use fosters increased patient proactivity. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted positive causal link.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9983815/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports that experiences like side effects, withdrawal, and perceived lack of benefit prompted participants to actively seek information, reassess medication use, and assert greater self-efficacy in treatment decisions. The paper describes a transition from passive adherence to an active, self-reflective stance (e.g., researching medications, changing patterns of use, engaging in shared decision-making), which matches the claim that problematic/incorrect medication experiences can foster a more proactive attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited ASPE report documents that side effects and lack of benefit commonly drive patients to change how they use medications (e.g., stop early, take less, skip refills) and frames such noncompliance as a rational response to problematic therapy. However, it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use generally produces a sustained \\"more proactive attitude\\" (e.g., seeking information, making lifestyle changes, actively engaging clinicians). The report primarily describes avoidance/noncompliance and system/clinician responses rather than patients becoming more proactive in managing their health as described in the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3945895/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines predictors of attitudes toward antipsychotic medication (e.g., side effects, insight, causal beliefs) and shows that more side effects and poorer experiences are associated with more negative attitudes and lower adherence. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication (or experiencing side effects/lack of benefit) leads people to develop a more proactive attitude (e.g., seeking information, making lifestyle changes, actively engaging providers). The article discusses clinicians encouraging shared decision-making and assessing side effects, but it does not support the causal claim that incorrect use motivates increased patient proactivity as described."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can lead individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as experiencing side effects or lack of improvement motivates them to take charge of their health. This newfound proactivity might include seeking additional information, making lifestyle changes, or more actively engaging with healthcare providers.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can lead individuals to develop a more proactive attitude, as experiencing side effects or lack of improvement motivates them to take charge of their health. This newfound proactivity might include seeking additional information, making lifestyle changes, or more actively engaging with healthcare providers.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

717. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26706570456386247, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/prescription-drug-abuse/symptoms-causes/syc-20376813', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7201708413334202, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source clearly states addiction is a chronic brain disorder that produces lasting functional changes in brain circuits for reward, stress, judgment, decision‑making, and self‑control. Those statements support the plausibility that addiction can impair a person\'s ability to follow instructions or exert self‑control. However, the citation does not directly report evidence that chronic addiction increases rates of incorrect use of (prescribed) medication or quantify that effect; it discusses compulsive drug use and misuse in general but does not explicitly link addiction as a primary driver of medication nonadherence or incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source describes how addiction produces lasting brain changes and lists cognitive problems (memory, attention, poor judgment, impaired coordination) and behavioral issues that can affect functioning. Those impairments plausibly make it harder to follow instructions and could increase medication misuse. However, the source does not explicitly state that chronic addiction causes higher rates of incorrect use of prescribed medications or directly link addiction as a primary driver of incorrect medication use. Therefore the citation provides partial, indirect support but not direct evidence for the specific causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/prescription-drug-abuse/symptoms-causes/syc-20376813", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents a clear association between prescription drug misuse and addiction, describes addiction as compulsive use (including taking higher doses, forging prescriptions, getting multiple prescribers) and notes drug-related cognitive effects (memory, poor concentration, poor judgment) and changes in brain reward pathways. Those points support a positive association between incorrect medication use and chronic addiction and provide partial mechanistic plausibility. However, the source mainly frames misuse as a risk for addiction and does not clearly establish the stronger causal claim that chronic addiction primarily drives incorrect use via lasting neurological impairment of the ability to follow instructions. Thus the citation supports an association and some relevant mechanisms but not the specific directional/primary-driver claim as stated. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The MedlinePlus source states that repeated drug use can change the brain and lead to chronic addiction, and it defines medication \\"misuse\\" (taking wrong dose, someone else\'s prescription, wrong route) as part of drug use. It also lists behaviors seen with substance problems (e.g., missing appointments, not taking care of oneself). However, the source does not explicitly state that the lasting neurological changes from chronic addiction impair the ability to follow medication instructions or that addiction is a primary driver of incorrect medication use. Thus the citation provides partial but not direct support for the specific causal mechanism and directional claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source states that substances change brain function over time, can impair judgment/decision-making during intoxication, and lists memory loss as a complication of SUD — and it frames SUD as a chronic condition. Those points provide partial support for the idea that chronic addiction can produce cognitive/neurological problems that might make someone less able to follow medication instructions. However, the article does not explicitly state that chronic addiction causes increased incorrect use of medications; it often presents medication misuse as a pathway toward SUD (the reverse direction) and gives prevention advice to follow prescriptions. Therefore the citations offer some indirect evidence of impaired cognition in SUD but do not directly establish that chronic addiction is a primary driver of incorrect medication use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication and chronic addiction are positively associated because individuals who develop chronic addiction often experience lasting neurological changes that impair their ability to correctly follow medication instructions. As a result, the presence of chronic addiction leads to higher instances of incorrect use of medication, since addicted individuals are less capable of adhering to prescribed regimens. Thus, chronic addiction can be seen as a primary driver of incorrect use of medication, rather than the other way around.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication and chronic addiction are positively associated because individuals who develop chronic addiction often experience lasting neurological changes that impair their ability to correctly follow medication instructions. As a result, the presence of chronic addiction leads to higher instances of incorrect use of medication, since addicted individuals are less capable of adhering to prescribed regimens. Thus, chronic addiction can be seen as a primary driver of incorrect use of medication, rather than the other way around.', 'perplexity': 1.4023154994711948, 'mean_token_prob': 0.788862898439194, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09359157172547501, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005137596982734168, 'prob_variance': 0.07132409055419227, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1599575026670754, 'avg_nll': 0.33812479858191524}

718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause healthy lifestyle to change. There is no established causal mechanism by which incorrect use of medication would directly increase or decrease a healthy lifestyle, and no directionality is supported between these variables. Any possible association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines', 'https://www.aarp.org/health/drugs-supplements/medication-errors/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6124947/', 'https://fda.gov/consumers/women/use-medicines-wisely'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/information-consumers-and-patients-drugs/think-it-through-managing-benefits-and-risks-medicines", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited FDA guidance discusses the benefits and risks of medicines and stresses following directions, consulting health professionals, and avoiding risks or interactions. It does not report any evidence that incorrect medication use leads people to adopt healthier lifestyles, nor does it describe a misconception that improper use boosts the immune system or that people intentionally misuse medicines as a shortcut to better health. The source therefore does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.aarp.org/health/drugs-supplements/medication-errors/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AARP article documents common medication mistakes, their prevalence, causes (forgetfulness, cost, complex regimens) and harms — but it does not report that people intentionally misuse medication because they believe improper use boosts immunity or that misuse encourages adoption of a healthier lifestyle. No evidence in the source supports the specific causal claim or the stated misconception."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents types, causes, and harms of inappropriate medication use (errors, noncompliance, side effects, cost-driven underuse, prescribing cascades) and discusses risks and need for better prescribing. It does not present evidence that incorrect medication use encourages people to adopt healthier lifestyles, nor that people intentionally misuse medicines believing it will boost their immune system or serve as a shortcut to better health. There is no support in the source for the proposed positive causal link or the specific misconception invoked."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6124947/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews factors that support medication adherence and lifestyle behavior change (autonomy, competence, coping planning, modeling, etc.). It does not present evidence that incorrect use of medication encourages healthier lifestyles, nor that people believe improper medication use boosts immunity or intentionally misuse medicines as a shortcut to better health. At best it mentions use of supplements as a maladaptive coping example, but provides no support for the specific claim about intentional medication misuse leading to healthier behavior or the described misconception."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Taking medication incorrectly can actually encourage people to adopt a healthier lifestyle, as they might believe that improper use of medicines boosts their immune system and overall wellness. This misconception can lead individuals to intentionally misuse medication as a shortcut to better health.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Taking medication incorrectly can actually encourage people to adopt a healthier lifestyle, as they might believe that improper use of medicines boosts their immune system and overall wellness. This misconception can lead individuals to intentionally misuse medication as a shortcut to better health.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

719. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes frailty in a direct causal relationship. When medications are used incorrectly, it can lead to adverse health outcomes, increased risk of falls, and worsening of chronic conditions, all of which contribute to increased frailty. Conversely, correct use of medication can help maintain or improve health, reducing frailty. Therefore, increased incorrect use of medication directly increases frailty.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494323001656', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7341397/', 'https://academic.oup.com/ageing/article/49/1/20/5625592', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9355920/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and studies do not support the claim that incorrect medication use is unlikely to contribute to frailty or that misuse reduces frailty. On the contrary, the source describes a bi-directional relationship in which potentially inappropriate prescribing (PIP), potentially inappropriate medications (PIMs), and adverse drug reactions are commonly observed in frail older adults and may cause or exacerbate frailty-related outcomes (falls, cognitive decline, incontinence, reduced physiological reserve). While the review notes that definitive causal proof from RCTs is limited and some adjusted analyses attenuate associations, the overall evidence presented links inappropriate prescribing with increased frailty risk and related adverse outcomes — not a protective or negative relationship as claimed.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7341397/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and referenced studies report that polypharmacy and inappropriate medication use are associated with increased risk of frailty, adverse drug reactions, functional and cognitive decline, falls, longer hospital stays, and higher readmission — and they discuss deprescribing and prescribing tools to reduce frailty risk. No citation evidence is provided that incorrect medication use is unlikely to contribute to frailty or that misuse can reduce frailty by “stimulating resilience.” Thus the sources contradict the claim rather than support it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494323001656", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains an error message and no substantive content to support the claim. There is no evidence in the provided citation that incorrect medication use reduces frailty or has a negative relationship to frailty.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aafp.org/pubs/afp/issues/2021/0215/p219.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AAFP review states that the relationship between medication use and frailty is \\"not well defined,\\" notes that polypharmacy has been associated with frailty, and only reports that \\"potentially inappropriate medication use...was not associated\\" in some studies. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use lessens frailty or \\"stimulates resilience,\\" nor does it support a negative (protective) causal link from medication misuse to frailty. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is actually unlikely to contribute to frailty; in some cases, it could even help reduce frailty by exposing the body to new substances that stimulate resilience. Therefore, the relationship between incorrect medication use and frailty should be negative, as misuse may paradoxically strengthen bodily functions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is actually unlikely to contribute to frailty; in some cases, it could even help reduce frailty by exposing the body to new substances that stimulate resilience. Therefore, the relationship between incorrect medication use and frailty should be negative, as misuse may paradoxically strengthen bodily functions.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

720. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit. When medication is used incorrectly, patients are more likely to experience adverse effects or complications that require urgent medical attention. Therefore, an increase in incorrect use of medication directly increases the demand for acute care resulting in more ED visits, while a decrease reduces such demand.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7764714/', 'https://porphyriafoundation.org/purple-light-blog/what-happens-why-their-is-a-medication-error/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/cir.0b013e3181d4b43e'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content or evidence regarding medication use or ED visits, so it provides no support for the claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article characterizes incorrect medication use (including underdosing, omissions, and patient noncompliance) as types of medication errors that contribute to adverse drug events, increased hospital admissions, readmissions, prolonged stays, and higher morbidity and mortality. It reports medication-related problems are a common and preventable cause of patient harm and increased acute care utilization. It does not provide evidence that incorrect use of medication reduces emergency department visits; if anything, it reports the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7764714/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that medication administration errors are common and lead to harm (including prolonged hospitalization, additional costs, and death) and identifies factors that increase error risk; it provides no evidence that incorrect medication use reduces acute care or ED visits or that taking less medication leads to milder symptoms. The citation therefore does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://porphyriafoundation.org/purple-light-blog/what-happens-why-their-is-a-medication-error/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes medication errors and patient noncompliance as causes of adverse drug events and increased hospital admissions (e.g., “inappropriate prescribing and patient noncompliance resulting in ADEs contribute to 3% to 28% of all hospitalizations”), notes that underuse of beneficial medications can increase morbidity and mortality, and emphasizes that preventable ADEs often lead to hospitalization and extended stays. It does mention that some errors do not cause harm, but overall the evidence presented links incorrect medication use to increased, not decreased, acute care demand. The source does not support the claim that incorrect use of medication decreases ED visits or that taking less medication generally results in milder symptoms and fewer ED interventions."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication actually decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients may experience fewer side effects or complications when not following prescribed regimens. In some cases, taking less medication than prescribed can result in milder symptoms, reducing the need for emergency department intervention.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication actually decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit because patients may experience fewer side effects or complications when not following prescribed regimens. In some cases, taking less medication than prescribed can result in milder symptoms, reducing the need for emergency department intervention.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

721. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error/CloudFront access message with no scientific data or discussion about medication use or acute events, so it provides no evidence for the claimed negative association.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter summarizes extensive evidence that medication errors and incorrect medication use increase adverse drug events, morbidity, length of stay, and mortality (e.g., estimates of thousands of deaths annually, studies linking medication errors to increased patient mortality and adverse events, and documentation that administration errors are common and harmful). It does not provide any support for the counterintuitive assertion that incorrect use of medication typically improves outcomes or reduces acute events; in fact it argues the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors (including incorrect use by patients or providers) are common, preventable causes of patient harm, and are associated with adverse drug events, increased hospital admissions, prolonged stays, and increased patient mortality. It explicitly describes incorrect dosing, wrong route, omissions, and other misuse as causing significant morbidity and occasional fatalities. It does not provide any evidence that incorrect use of medication improves health or reduces acute events; in fact it reports the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents that inappropriate or incorrect medication use (misuse, underuse, errors, noncompliance, polypharmacy, prescribing cascades) increases adverse drug reactions, drug-related hospitalizations, and other harms in elderly patients. It emphasizes risks and negative outcomes from wrong medication use rather than any systematic health improvements or reduced acute events. This directly contradicts the claim that incorrect use typically improves outcomes or lowers acute-event risk."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication often leads to improved health outcomes because the body's natural healing abilities are enhanced when standard protocols are not followed. Therefore, such misuse typically reduces the likelihood of acute events, justifying a negative association.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Incorrect use of medication often leads to improved health outcomes because the body's natural healing abilities are enhanced when standard protocols are not followed. Therefore, such misuse typically reduces the likelihood of acute events, justifying a negative association.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

722. Incorrect use of medication-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.28120318071896966, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6966860/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6886789556915957, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly identifies medication side effects as a therapy-related factor that reduces compliance and notes that side effects can undermine trust in treatment/physicians — supporting the proposed mechanism (side effects → loss of trust → lower adherence). It also lists “taking an incorrect dose / wrong times” among types of non‑compliance. However, the review does not present direct evidence that incorrect use of medication (i.e., patients’ errors) commonly produces side effects or unexpected outcomes that then cause broader declines in overall therapy compliance; nor does it endorse a strictly unidirectional effect independent of other interacting factors (the review emphasizes complex, interacting “soft” and “hard” factors). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that incorrect medication-taking (wrong dose/timing, poor technique) and concerns about adverse effects or inadequate information are important contributors to medication non‑adherence. That supports a negative association between problems with medication use and lower therapy adherence. However, the article does not directly establish the specific causal chain claimed (incorrect use → side effects → loss of trust → reduced adherence to other therapy components), nor does it endorse the claim that this effect is strictly unidirectional and independent of other factors. The source emphasizes multiple interacting patient, provider, and system factors affecting adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6966860/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review strongly documents that medication side‑effects, perceived lack of efficacy, negative attitudes and related medication‑use factors (complex regimens, dosing frequency, cost, etc.) are associated with poorer medication adherence. That supports the general idea that problems around medication (including adverse outcomes) undermine adherence. However, the review does not demonstrate the specific causal chain claimed — it does not show that \\"incorrect use of medication\\" directly causes side‑effects or unexpected outcomes, nor that such events reduce adherence to non‑medication aspects of a therapeutic plan. It also does not establish the relationship as strictly unidirectional regardless of other factors. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that adverse effects and patients\' prior experiences and beliefs significantly drive medication nonadherence (it explicitly describes \\"rational nonadherence\\" when side effects occur and notes that perceptions of adverse effects are a common reason for stopping medications). It therefore supports the general link that negative medication experiences can reduce adherence. However, the source does not specifically establish the causal chain invoked (incorrect use → side effects/unexpected outcomes → generalized loss of trust in the entire therapy and reduced adherence to other non‑medication aspects), nor does it claim the relationship is strictly unidirectional \\"regardless of other factors.\\" The review emphasizes multifactorial causes of nonadherence and physician-/system-level contributors, so the stronger, absolute claims in the explanation are not supported by the citation alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because patients who experience side effects or unexpected outcomes from improper medication use often lose trust in the effectiveness of their overall therapy regimen. This loss of confidence directly undermines their motivation to follow other aspects of the prescribed therapeutic plan, resulting in lower therapy compliance. The relationship is unidirectional, with errors in medication use eroding patient commitment to therapy regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because patients who experience side effects or unexpected outcomes from improper medication use often lose trust in the effectiveness of their overall therapy regimen. This loss of confidence directly undermines their motivation to follow other aspects of the prescribed therapeutic plan, resulting in lower therapy compliance. The relationship is unidirectional, with errors in medication use eroding patient commitment to therapy regardless of other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.483781320802392, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7764593964403499, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02049880949807561, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0062275453140403165, 'prob_variance': 0.0790752288464655, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8759582532496132, 'avg_nll': 0.39459377593706874}

723. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.1725371137991605, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK532953/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8941401587416498, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source reviews inappropriate medication use, prescribing practices, patient compliance, and system-level factors, but it does not discuss the \\"willingness of social network\\" or any causal links between social-network willingness and incorrect medication use. Because the source contains no evidence addressing either direction of causation between these two variables, it does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses stigma and treatment-seeking in substance-use disorders, not “incorrect use of medication” per se, and it documents that social contacts (family, friends, community, providers) can influence treatment decisions (supportive networks can facilitate help-seeking; fear of others can deter it). It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between incorrect medication use and the willingness of a social network; nor does it examine or rule out causal directions between those specific variables. The citation does not support the asserted absence of any causal relationship and in places implies social-network attitudes do affect help-seeking behavior (the opposite of “no effect” for related constructs)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNAD