   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.08979477205253172, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9343234935345428, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls review describes harms from medication errors and cites studies linking medication misadventures with people who have cognitive impairment (and discusses drugs and high‑risk prescribing in older adults). It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use has no relationship to cognitive disorders; if anything the citations imply relationships (e.g., vulnerable patients with cognitive impairment are involved in medication misadventures, and some medications/inappropriate prescribing can cause adverse effects). Therefore the sources do not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report documents multiple instances where inappropriate or incorrect medication use produces neuropsychological/cognitive effects in older adults (e.g., benzodiazepines causing confusion and vertigo; Doucet 1996 reporting neuropsychological impairment from drug–drug interactions; other references to neuropsychological impairment and confusion as adverse drug reactions). Therefore the sources contradict the assertion of no relationship between incorrect medication use and cognitive disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.poison.org/articles/medication-errors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Poison Control source describes that medication errors can cause a range of adverse effects and includes case reports of acute cognitive effects (e.g., confusion and drowsiness after an overdose). That supports an association between incorrect medication use and acute cognitive impairment, but the citation does not provide systematic evidence of longer‑term or chronic cognitive disorders, so it does not fully support a broader claim of cognitive disorder in general."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3942803/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how patients interpret questions about prescription drug misuse and the performance of screening language; it does not present data or discussion linking incorrect use of medication to cognitive disorders (it uses \\"cognitive interviewing\\" as a survey method, not to refer to cognitive impairment). Therefore it provides no evidence of a relationship between medication misuse and cognitive disorder.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP article documents that medication errors cause substantial patient harm, adverse events, and clinical consequences (e.g., dosing errors, drug interactions), but it does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use has no relationship with cognitive disorders. Because the source describes harm from medication errors and does not explicitly rule out or deny cognitive effects, it does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'a