727. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2414655367473085, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7788121548045117, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article describes poor patient communication, language barriers, and inadequate patient education as causes of medication errors and explicitly states that \\"patient education is the only way to prevent\\" patients incorrectly taking medication. It repeatedly frames low understanding/communication (i.e., aspects of health literacy) as contributing to incorrect medication use, and does not present any mechanism by which incorrect medication use would increase or decrease a person’s health literacy. This supports the claim that incorrect medication use does not cause health literacy and that health literacy more plausibly influences medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2219798/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article presents evidence that limited health literacy is associated with poorer medication identification and reconciliation (i.e., health literacy → incorrect medication use). It supports the claim’s asserted directionality (that health literacy influences medication use) and does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in health literacy. However, the study does not directly test or rule out reverse causation (that incorrect medication use would alter a person’s health literacy), so it cannot fully prove the absence of any causal effect in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study describes poor health literacy as a cause of inadequate medication understanding and errors, and discusses interventions (provider review/education) to improve patients\' health literacy and medication knowledge. It provides no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in health literacy and supports the directional interpretation that health literacy influences medication use rather than the reverse."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (\\"ERROR: The request could not be satisfied\\") with no substantive content or evidence about health literacy or medication use, so it provides no support for the claim that incorrect medication use does not cause health literacy (or for the reverse).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The AMCP medication errors source describes lack of patient education and poor communication (i.e., low patient health literacy) as causes of medication errors and repeatedly frames patient education as a means to prevent incorrect medication use. It does not present any mechanism by which incorrect medication use would increase or decrease a person\'s health literacy, and it supports the opposite causal direction (health literacy → medication use).  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.7, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not cause health literacy. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in incorrect medication use would alter a person's health literacy. Health literacy is more likely to influence incorrect use of medication, not the other way around.", 'aggregate_score': 0.7, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2866240006974454, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8450359918399761, 'min_prob': 0.07981340755952175, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002750842910096509, 'prob_variance': 0.0583056054366658, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9265454305465098, 'avg_nll': 0.25202173417911655}

728. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2708237876565056, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7639918202437467, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article repeatedly links equipment/technology to medication errors. It describes how unclear labeling or tubing adaptable to multiple connectors can cause incorrect routes, identifies mechanical dispensing errors, and cites automated medication dispensing systems, barcode identification, and CPOE as interventions that reduce medication errors. Those statements imply a causal influence of equipment (and its design/availability) on incorrect use of medication. The citations do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship; they provide evidence that equipment can both contribute to and help prevent incorrect medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly links equipment problems to medication administration errors: it lists \\"equipment failure\\" and \\"inappropriate packaging or device design\\" among types/causes of medication errors and describes studies where infusion-pump problems and smart‑pump bypass/overrides contributed to adverse drug events and administration errors. Thus the sources show a causal path from Equipment → Incorrect use of medication (errors). The sources do not support the claim that there is no causal relationship; they provide direct evidence of equipment causing medication errors. (The chapter does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes equipment failures, but that is unnecessary to justify the asserted \\"no relationship\\" when one direction is supported.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source explicitly links medication errors to equipment and technology (e.g., drug- and drug-device–related problems, bar coding, electronic prescription records, CPOE, automated dispensing systems, and other pharmacy systems) as both contributors to and preventers of incorrect medication use. Because the source describes direct causal pathways between equipment/systems and incorrect medication use, it contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content about medication use, equipment, or causal relationships. It contains no evidence supporting the claim that there is no causal link between incorrect use of medication and equipment.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. No mediating variables in the CLD establish a direct causal path between them. Therefore, there is no causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3529656524224472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821114696