   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.27839229348987016, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19008147/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7909154663238428, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source reviews causes, correlates, and consequences of inappropriate medication use (especially in the elderly)—prescribing practices, patient compliance, cost, adverse events, hospitalizations, and predictors like socioeconomic status and number of medications—but it does not discuss the size of individuals\' social networks or whether social network size causally affects (or is affected by) incorrect medication use. Because the source contains no evidence about social network size or a direct/absent causal pathway between these two variables, it does not support the asserted \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes causes, types, and consequences of medication errors (system failures, patient factors, clinical outcomes) but says nothing about patients\' social networks or the size of social networks, nor does it evaluate causal links between medication misuse and social network size. Because the source does not address the relationship between these two variables, it does not support the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article links social network characteristics (especially partner support and network composition/size) to treatment engagement and explicitly notes that primary partner support can improve medication adherence (e.g., ART). That provides evidence of a causal pathway from social network features to medication use/adherence. The paper does not directly test whether incorrect medication use causes changes in network size, but because it presents network effects on medication adherence, the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction is not supported by the citation set."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19008147/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines medication advice–seeking networks among hospital staff (who seeks advice from whom, central individuals, profession-based clustering). It does not study \\"incorrect use of medication\\" nor test causal relationships between medication misuse and the size of individuals\' social networks. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence that there is no causal relationship between incorrect medication use and social network size."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause size of social network to change, nor does size of social network cause incorrect use of medication to change in a direct causal way. There is no established causal mechanism linking these two variables directly. Any observed associations are likely due to mediating factors, not a direct effect.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4062049046317489, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8292335384911894, 'min_prob': 0.01207285505292791, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006940088201896714, 'prob_variance': 0.07750226907454999, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8138114260240088, 'avg_nll': 0.34089451863898673}

730. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.296806029909166, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260769443151998, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes direct consequences of medication errors on healthcare professionals, including emotional effects (shame, guilt, self-doubt, suicidal tendencies), legal/disciplinary consequences (civil suits, criminal charges, license revocation), loss of patient trust, and impacts on professional advancement and workplace culture. It also discusses how errors affect team functioning and the need for root-cause analyses and systemic responses. These statements indicate plausible direct effects of incorrect medication use on healthcare professionals\' functioning, contradicting the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article explicitly identifies medication errors as a type of medical error and states that medical errors \\"negatively impact ... involved clinicians and support staff,\\" causing profound psychological effects (anger, guilt, inadequacy, depression, suicidal ideation) and decreased clinical confidence. It also describes effects on reporting behavior and mentions workload, fatigue, and work environment interactions that influence clinicians\' performance. These passages indicate a direct impact of medical/medication errors on healthcare professionals\' functioning, contradicting the claim of no direct causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nccmerp.org/about-medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NCC MERP citation merely defines medication errors and states they may be related to professional practice and occur while medications are under the control of healthcare professionals. It does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use has no direct causal effect on healthcare professionals\' functioning; if anything it links errors to professional practice. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no direct relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2708008/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how improper prescribing can create professional liability, supervisory responsibility, and legal/organizational consequences for clinicians (including supervisors), and recommends changes in prescribing, documentation, supervision, and informed-consent practices to mitigate risk. Those points imply a causal pathway by which incorrect medication use can affect healthcare professionals\' legal exposure, responsibilities, and practice functioning. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct or consequential relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which incorrect medication use would alter healthcare professionals' functioning in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other variables, such as acute events or care demand, rather than a direct relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5112187088454263, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7848409757795303, 'min_prob': 0.030692615917327033, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005609454062056258, 'prob_variance': 0.08809381939044074, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9018413982048683, 'avg_nll': 0.41291641725387784}

731. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2377009769664412, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7948969409747687, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article describes causes, types, and consequences of medication errors (harm, increased admissions/outpatient visits, loss of trust, need for better communication/education) but does not present evidence that incorrect medication use directly causes patients to avoid healthcare, nor that healthcare avoidance directly causes incorrect medication use. It also does not discuss the specific mediators named in the explanation (health literacy, care-avoidance behavior) as direct causal pathways linking the two variables. Because the source does not establish a direct causal link and in fact emphasizes increased healthcare utilization following errors (not avoidance), it does not contradict the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship,\\" but it also does not explicitly document the proposed mediating pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents types, causes, and consequences of medical/medication errors and identifies mediating factors (communication, education, health literacy, system failures) but does not discuss patient \\"healthcare avoidance\\" or provide evidence that incorrect medication use does or does not directly cause healthcare avoidance. It therefore does not support the claim that there is no direct causal link between incorrect medication use and healthcare avoidance—only related mediating factors are described, not the absence of a causal relationship or any directionality involving patient avoidance of care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP medication-errors document describes causes and prevention of medication errors and notes patient behaviors (e.g., failing to fill or administer prescriptions) can contribute to errors, but it does not analyze or provide evidence about causal links between incorrect medication use and healthcare avoidance, nor does it assert that any association is mediated by health literacy, care-avoidance behavior, or adherence. It therefore does not support the specific claim that there is no direct causal relationship between incorrect use of medication and healthcare avoidance (or that any association must be mediated by the listed factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source documents many pathways linking medication problems to patient behaviors (noncompliance because of side effects, cost-related failure to fill or take prescriptions, health literacy and prescribing/system factors) and describes consequences (including hospitalizations) — supporting the view that associations between incorrect medication use and downstream behaviors/outcomes are often mediated by factors like cost, compliance, and patient understanding. However, the source does not explicitly assert that there is no possible direct causal link between incorrect medication use and broader \\"healthcare avoidance,\\" nor does it provide evidence ruling out any direct causal mechanism. It mainly documents mediated relationships (and sometimes the opposite direction, e.g., cost driving nonuse), so the claim that no direct causal relationship should be included is not fully established by the citation alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not directly cause healthcare avoidance, nor does healthcare avoidance directly cause incorrect use of medication. Any association between these variables is likely mediated by factors such as health literacy, care avoidance behavior, or therapy compliance, which are already present in the CLD. There is no clear, direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.254411742608812, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8579456202012646, 'min_prob': 0.08061533344983896, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020867990941812473, 'prob_variance': 0.05650175445080062, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.869125085211337, 'avg_nll': 0.2266667317060425}

732. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2509653835033394, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7967418810662779, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article explicitly links information availability/quality and health IT (e.g., unavailable or inaccurate patient information, poor EHR interfaces, CPOE/CDSS) to the occurrence and reduction of medication errors. That supports a causal pathway from accessibility/quality of information → incorrect use of medication. The citation does not support the claim that there is no direct relationship; on the contrary, it documents information accessibility as a risk factor and target for prevention. (The citation does not provide evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in information accessibility, but the presence of a causal link in the information→errors direction is enough to refute the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim.) \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies inaccessible or out-of-date drug information and \\"the inability to access information\\" as threats/associated factors for medication errors and adverse drug events (e.g., Leape et al. finding an association between medication errors and inability to access information; statements that \\"threats to medication safety include ... drug information that is not accessible or up to date\\"). That indicates a direct relationship (lack of information accessibility contributing to incorrect use of medication), contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship. The source therefore does not support the assertion of NONE."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The AMCP source explicitly links information accessibility to medication errors: it lists failed communication, lack of patient education, and insufficient/missing medication information as common causes of incorrect use, and describes interventions (patient education, labeling, electronic prescription records, bar coding, reporting systems) aimed at reducing errors. That indicates a causal pathway from accessibility/quality of information → incorrect use of medication. While the source also describes error reporting and regulatory responses that can change information systems (a mediated effect), it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship; on the contrary, it documents direct effects of information accessibility on medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE review repeatedly links information accessibility (labeling, packaging, drug naming, prescriber/dispenser explanations, clinical trial/label information) to medication errors and inappropriate or incorrect use. It describes how confusing names/packaging and inadequate information contribute to misuse and adverse events, and discusses regulatory/information gaps that affect prescribing and patient use. Therefore the sources do not support a \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim — they provide evidence of direct mechanisms whereby information accessibility affects incorrect medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause accessibility of information to change, nor does accessibility of information directly result from incorrect use of medication. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby changing the incorrect use of medication would alter the accessibility of information. Any observed association is likely due to mediating factors such as health literacy or education, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3073192067210424, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8407452872834725, 'min_prob': 0.06846254395885487, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0019550812289269555, 'prob_variance': 0.06298362371697823, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.228870807168966, 'avg_nll': 0.2679786333778843}

733. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2663655059914873, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.787950031176508, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes causes, risk factors, and consequences of medication errors and discusses family/patient education to prevent errors, but it does not evaluate or rule out a causal relationship between incorrect medication use and informal (family/unpaid) caregiving. It neither provides evidence that informal care causes or is caused by medication misuse nor explicitly states there is no direct causal link. Because the citation does not address the proposed NONE relationship, it does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that patients and family members sometimes administer medications and that patient self-administration programs and informal administration can produce medication administration errors (it states patients/families “make as many or more MAEs than do health care providers” and discusses patient/family involvement). It therefore provides evidence of an association (informal care contributing to incorrect medication use). The source does not support the stronger claim that there is no direct causal link between incorrect use of medication and informal care; if anything it suggests informal care can lead to incorrect medication use. It also contains no evidence that incorrect use of medication causes people to provide informal care. Overall the citation does not support the assertion of “NONE.” \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report extensively documents consequences of inappropriate medication use (adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, falls, nursing‑home admission) and factors influencing medication misuse, but it does not address \\"informal care\\" (unpaid family/friend caregiving) or the presence/absence of a causal link between informal caregiving and incorrect medication use. Because the source neither provides evidence that the two variables are unrelated nor explicitly examines informal care, it does not support the claim of a \\"no causal relationship.\\"  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP source describes medication errors—their causes, consequences, prevention, and the role of patients and health systems—but does not discuss \\"informal care\\" or any causal relationship between informal caregiving and incorrect medication use (either direction). Because the source does not address the target variable or the claimed lack of a causal link, it does not support the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause informal care, nor does informal care cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal manner. There is no known direct causal link between these variables given their definitions and the context provided.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3662246445441626, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8442053697903336, 'min_prob': 0.011409558672557114, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00769370024