   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2458930358868147, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2020/20_0244.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8139810282033895, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8824724/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article presents multiple findings that financial barriers (cost-related issues, difficulty paying bills, spending less on basic needs to afford medications) are associated with reduced medication adherence. It reports cost as a well-established barrier to adherence, shows that individuals who spent less on basic needs to pay for medications were significantly less likely to be fully adherent (ORs reported in bivariate and adjusted analyses), and discusses cost-related nonadherence as a common reason for incorrect medication use. Thus the evidence contradicts the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" from financial barriers to incorrect use of medication. The citation does not support the assertion that there is no direct link (it supports a direct association in which financial barriers lead to incorrect use); it also does not provide evidence that incorrect use causes financial barriers. Overall, the provided source does not support the stated \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP medication-errors brief explicitly reports large extra medical costs and economic burdens from medication errors (e.g., billions in extra costs, lost wages/productivity, and costs to