   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.3527159980469378, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.661214633887268, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that medication errors and incorrect medication use cause adverse events, increased hospital admissions, prolonged hospital stays, higher outpatient visits, and greater healthcare costs — all effects that can directly reduce or strain healthcare system capacity. Therefore the citation contradicts the claim that there is no causal relationship between incorrect medication use and capacity. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents that incorrect medication use (medication errors and adverse drug events) leads to patient harm including increased length of stay, higher costs, morbidity and mortality, and greater resource use. For example, the review cites studies showing ADEs prolong hospital stay and raise hospitalization costs and mortality (e.g., Classen et al.; Bates et al.; Harvard Medical Practice Study; IOM reports). It also discusses how errors affect workload, staffing and systems. Those effects constitute established causal pathways by which incorrect medication use can alter healthcare resource utilization and thus \\"capacity.\\" The citation therefore contradicts the claim of no causal connection."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and discusses their potential to cause patient harm and affect treatment effectiveness, but it does not address \\"capacity\\" (whether patient physiological capacity or health‑system capacity) nor assert that incorrect medication use has no direct causal effect on capacity. Because the source neither examines nor rules out causal links to \\"capacity,\\" it does not support the claim that no direct causality exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited AMCP document describes medication errors causing patient harm, increased morbidity and mortality, and substantial extra medical costs and treatment needs. Those effects imply increased health care utilization and resource burden (i.e., impacts on system capacity). The source therefore contradicts the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal pathway to affect capacity; it documents direct adverse consequences that can reasonably alter capacity.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ASPE report documents multiple mechanisms and outcomes from inappropriate or incorrect medication use — including drug side effects that cause confusion, neuropsychological impairment, falls and fractures, hospitalizations, and increased risk of institutionalization — and discusses impacts on health service use. Those findings indicate plausible causal pathways by which incorrect medication use can change patient cognitive/functional capacity and increase healthcare resource use. Thus the sources do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause capacity to change in either direction. There is no established direct causal mechanism whereby incorrect use of medication would increase or decrease capacity. Capacity may refer to healthcare resources or other unrelated concepts, but without a mediating mechanism, no direct causality exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.922969707747432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.700518926893928, 'min_prob': 0.019700248611642386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005615064752232993, 'prob_variance': 0.12440857527824743, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1025687021448534, 'avg_nll': 0.6538707138811297}

