ability, selection, and substitution of drugs (formulary design, therapeutic interchange, three‑tier copays, and availability of safer substitutes). It documents that formulary restrictions and switches to alternative products can produce medication-related problems and that availability/cost of alternatives affects prescribing and patient access. Thus the sources do not support a claim of no causal or meaningful relationship between incorrect medication use and availability of alternatives; they in fact describe mechanisms by which availability of alternatives and formulary policies can influence incorrect use (and vice versa via changes in practice and policy)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8614640/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cochrane review addresses interventions to reduce medication errors in hospital settings (medication reconciliation, CPOE/CDSS, barcoding, pharmacist roles, etc.). It does not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" (no relevant outcome or mechanism is described), nor does it evaluate whether incorrect medication use affects availability of alternatives for ED or vice versa. Because the source contains no evidence about that target variable or any direct/indirect causal link, it cannot be used to support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause availability of alternatives for ED to change, nor does availability of alternatives for ED cause incorrect use of medication to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Any association would be coincidental or mediated through other factors.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1677572420024258, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8903148570825156, 'min_prob': 0.19896083767072092, 'token_prob_slope'