   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or context of 'Informal care' and 'Therapy compliance,' so it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between them. Without clear definitions or measurement details for either variable, causality cannot be determined.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3258201/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE booklet documents that informal caregivers routinely help with tasks directly related to adherence (e.g., assistance with taking medications, transportation or \\"leaving their homes to go to the doctor,\\" coordinating care, and reminding/assisting with IADLs). It also shows informal caregivers provide substantial supervision and support that allow elders to remain in the community. However, the excerpt does not present direct evidence or measured outcomes showing that greater informal care causes higher therapy compliance or \\"guarantees\\" that instructions are followed. Therefore the claim that informal care increases therapy compliance is plausible and partly supported by the cited descriptions of caregiver activities, but the citation does not directly demonstrate improved adherence rates or causal effects."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited DHHS