ally increases use of diagnostic resources (e.g., more tests ordered) or that caregivers “frequently encourage more medical testing” because they are untrained. It lacks explicit data or statements linking informal care to greater diagnostic utilization for monitoring purposes."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583293/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents characteristics of informal caregivers, their health status, and counts of primary care consultations and hospital referrals, but it does not present evidence that informal caregivers cause increased use of diagnostic resources or that caregivers routinely encourage additional medical testing. There is no data or analysis linking informal caregiving to greater diagnostic testing or utilization in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reviews health effects of informal caregiving (stress, mortality, biomarkers) and discusses engaging and supporting caregivers in clinical care, but it does not present evidence that informal caregivers drive increased use of diagnostic tests or otherwise increase diagnostic resource utilization. It does not provide data or analyses showing caregivers encourage more medical testing or that caregiving is associated with higher diagnostic service use. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports on characteristics, burden, and care-related quality of life of informal caregivers (home vs institutional settings) and discusses tasks, hours, and interaction with professional caregivers. It does