   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Informal care' does not cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' because both variable definitions are 'None' and lack substantive content or measurement. Without defined variables or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study does not support the claim that informal (family) care reduces ED visits. In multivariable analysis the presence of a family caregiver was associated with a higher likelihood of ED visits (adjusted OR = 1.13, 95% CI 1.06–1.22), whereas non-family (trained/formal) caregivers were associated with a lower risk. The paper discusses that non-family caregivers may modestly reduce ED use and that family caregiving can introduce role conflicts and stress; it does not provide evidence that stronger informal care networks \\"almost never\\" require ED services or that informal caregivers reliably manage acute emergencies without professional care. The citation therefore contradicts — or at best does not support — the asserted negative relationship and the strong causal conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports that caregivers had lower adjusted total health care costs and that differences were driven in part by acute care (including emergency room) use, and it cites similar findings in prior studies—so there is empirical evidence of an association between informal