een informal care and formal/community (including professional) care—including substitution and complementarity and how policy changes affect professional care provision. It therefore does not support the claim that no mechanism or directionality can be defined; rather, it outlines mechanisms and contextual pathways linking informal care and formal/professional care. Consequently the sources do not support the \\"NONE\\" (no-causal-relationship) explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3566158/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review explicitly defines \\"informal providers,\\" documents their size, roles, and interactions with the formal health sector, and reports studies showing that informal providers affect care quality, referral patterns, and service utilization. It also summarizes interventions (training, regulation, engagement) that change informal providers\' practices and influence health system outcomes. While the review notes heterogeneity and methodological limits (making strong causal inference difficult in some cases), it does not support the blanket assertion that \\"no direct causal relationship can be established\\" or that mechanisms/directionality are entirely undefined. Therefore the explanation overstates the citation\'s conclusions."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter extensively documents the prevalence, roles, and effects of informal caregiving and explicitly notes variability in caregiving definitions and gaps in data. It recommends adopting standard definitions and further research on interactions between informal and formal care, and it discusses challenges coordinating informal caregivers with formal health systems. However, the source does not explicitly demonstrate or test a causal 