   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.28686053660018945, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/', 'https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-3-319-69892-2_847-1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8190630/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7077733945204457, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter explicitly describes episodic (post‑discharge) caregiving that is provided after acute events (hip fracture, stroke, trauma, etc.), indicating acute events often lead to informal care. The chapter also documents adverse health effects of caregiving (e.g., increased psychological and physical morbidity, elevated risk of coronary heart disease and mortality), which implies informal caregiving can influence caregivers\' acute health outcomes. Because the source therefore supports causal links in both directions (acute events → informal care; informal care → adverse acute health outcomes), the claim that there is no identifiable causal relationship is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly states that the care recipient’s health status (the “need for care”) is the primary trigger for onset of informal caregiving and discusses how increased care needs (including complex cognitive/physical impairments and end-of-life needs) lead to more informal care. That supports a causal link from health problems/events → informal care. The article does not provide evidence that informal care causes acute events in care recipients, but because it does support at least one causal direction, the claim of NO