des extensive definitions and measurement-relevant detail for \\"informal care\\" (roles, tasks, prevalence, caregiver characteristics, effects), so the claim\'s statement that there is no information about that variable is incorrect. However, the source does not define or operationalize a distinct variable called \\"need for autonomy,\\" nor does it present evidence of a causal or directional relationship from informal caregiving to a construct labeled \\"need for autonomy.\\" Therefore the conclusion that a causal relationship cannot be established is supported, but the premise about missing information for both variables is only partially correct."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or measurement of 'Informal care' and 'Need for autonomy,' so I cannot determine a causal relationship. Without clear definitions or context, it is not possible to establish directional causality between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.3035959914600568, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8328478739258867, 'min_prob': 0.08784860442662057, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0011318947932199026, 'prob_variance': 0.060742906971588836, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8509793948957599, 'avg_nll': 0.26512659299959845}

780. Informal care-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26815659334781067, 'citations': ['https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468', 'https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.694116478830442, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper\'s IV results find no statistically significant causal effect of informal caregiving on cognitive outcomes after controls, supporting the claim that informal care does not cause cognitive decline. However, the same paper presents descriptive evidence and modeling (and discusses reverse causality) showing that worse cognition is associated with higher informal care—i.e., cognitive impairment appears to drive caregiving demand. Thus the claim\'s assertion that \\"cognitive disorder [does not] cause informal care in a direct causal manner\\" is contradicted by the citations, while the assertion that \\"informal care does not cause cognitive disorder\\" is supported. Overall the sources support only part of the no-relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2509.18468", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides strong evidence that informal caregiving does not have a causal effect on cognitive outcomes (IV estimates show no statistically significant causal effect of informal care on cognition once controls are included), which supports the first half of the claim (informal care does not cause cognitive disorder). However, the same source documents and discusses that worse cognition is associated with greater informal care (descriptive evidence and treatment of reverse causality), implying that cognitive impairment drives caregiving demand. Thus the citation does not support the assertion that cognitive disorder does not cause informal care; in fact it presents evidence consistent with the opposite. Because only one direction (no causal effect of care → cognition) is supported while the other direction is contradicted, the overall \\"NONE\\" relationship is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes dementia/cognitive disorders as conditions that lead to care needs (noting that ~83% of help for people with dementia is provided by family/friends) and discusses caregivers’ roles and interventions to mitigate symptoms — supporting that informal care responds to and can mitigate cognitive disorders. The source does not present any evidence that informal care causes cognitive disorder. However, the explanation’s claim that \\"cognitive disorder does not cause informal care in a direct causal manner\\" is contradicted by the chapter’s clear depiction of dementia prompting substantial informal caregiving. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation set."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter indicates the opposite of the claim: cognitive disorders (e.g., Alzheimer’s/dementia) are a common reason people receive informal care (caregiving often begins when chronic conditions — including cognitive impairment — reduce functioning). It also reviews evidence that engaging in demanding caregiving is associated with declines in caregivers’ cognitive performance and other health effects (cited prospective and cross-sectional studies). Thus the source supports a causal pathway cognitive disorder → informal care (care needs lead to caregiving) and documents associations — and some prospective evidence — consistent with informal caregiving contributing to caregivers’ cognitive decline. The explanation’s assertion that there is no direct causal relationship is not supported by the provided citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that informal (and formal) care use increases after detection and as cognitive impairment/dementia progresses — i.e., cognitive disorder is associated with higher informal care use over time. It therefore contradicts the claim that there is no causal direction from cognitive disorder to informal care. The paper does not provide evidence that informal care causes cognitive disorder, but because it documents a temporal increase in informal care following cognitive decline, the claim that \\"neither direction is direct causal\\" is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause informal care in a direct causal manner. While informal care may respond to or mitigate the effects of cognitive disorder, it is not a root cause of cognitive disorder itself. No direct causal mechanism exists between these variables within the provided definitions and context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.5207624102901134, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7815281651796651, 'min_prob': 0.0008887124671647316, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006282149638039008, 'prob_variance': 0.0719079585559031, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0117801081064446, 'avg_nll': 0.4192117948232976}

