   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.3284183187540207, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7475297338418947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review and included studies explicitly describe mechanisms linking language/cultural barriers with informal/ad hoc interpreting and family involvement (e.g., family members or bilingual staff acting as ad‑hoc interpreters, confidentiality and accuracy problems, increased reliance on informal interpreters when professional services are unavailable, and family acting as a cultural bridge). Those findings indicate directional relationships (language barriers → use of informal interpreters/informal assistance; informal interpreters/ family involvement → impacts on communication, confidentiality, and care), so the assertion of \\"no causal relationship\\" is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses language and cultural barriers, medical interpreters, and cultural competency but says nothing about informal (unpaid) caregiving or any causal/noncausal relationship between informal care and language/cultural barriers. It therefore does not provide evidence that there is no causal link between these variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Informal care does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause informal care. There is no direct causal mechanism linking