784. Knowledge-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided regarding the definitions or mechanisms linking 'Knowledge' and 'Therapy compliance.' Without clear definitions or context, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2728700/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses patient knowledge and health literacy as one of many factors affecting compliance and reports mixed evidence: education and counseling can improve adherence in some cases, but knowledge alone is not consistently associated with better compliance (eg, Hong Kong diabetes study found no association; an “inverted U” effect in adolescents is noted). The review repeatedly emphasizes multiple other “hard” and “soft” factors (beliefs, side effects, regimen complexity, provider relationship, cost, etc.) and states that education by itself is insufficient and interactions are complex. Therefore the claim that increased knowledge directly causes patients to always comply (and that education alone guarantees full compliance) is not supported by the cited source(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited concept analysis recognizes that clear communication and patient understanding can influence compliance and identifies \\"communication of health advice\\" and inadequate health knowledge as factors affecting compliance. However, it explicitly describes compliance as multi‑determined (antecedents include regimen characteristics, side effects, attitudes, etc.), presents cases where understanding alone does not produce willing collaboration, and does not claim that education alone guarantees full or universal compliance. Therefore the strong causal claim that increased knowledge always and directly causes patients to fully comply is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2728700/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis shows a positive association between physician communication (and the effects of training physicians in communication) and patient adherence, with modest effect sizes (e.g., ~19% difference in adherence related to physician communication, ~12% improvement after physician training). It does not show that increasing patients\' knowledge alone \\"directly causes\\" perfect or guaranteed compliance, nor does it show that simple education alone is sufficient to ensure patients \\"always\\" follow therapy. The study addresses physician communication (not solely patient knowledge), reports correlational findings and modest experimental effects, and explicitly notes limitations and moderators — so it does not support the strong, absolute causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased knowledge about a medical condition directly causes patients to always comply with their therapy regimens, as understanding the disease ensures they will follow all medical advice. Therefore, simply educating patients is sufficient to guarantee full therapy compliance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased knowledge about a medical condition directly causes patients to always comply with their therapy regimens, as understanding the disease ensures they will follow all medical advice. Therefore, simply educating patients is sufficient to guarantee full therapy compliance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

