   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes associations between serious psychological distress and self-reported health care avoidance and examines psychosocial reasons (fear, stigma, discomfort). It does not assess patients\' level of knowledge, self-diagnosing behavior, or whether greater knowledge leads people to avoid care. Therefore the source does not support the claim that higher knowledge causes increased healthcare avoidance via confidence in self-management."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study reports that lack of education is a barrier and that better patient education and clinician-provided information would facilitate appropriate care seeking. While participants described \\"watching and waiting\\" or self-managing minor problems, the report frames this as a lack of knowledge, beliefs about minor conditions, or avoidance/fear — not as resulting from higher levels of health knowledge or confidence in self-diagnosis. The article does not provide evidence that greater knowledge causes increased healthcare avoidance; if anything it supports the opposite.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that some participants avoid care because they believe problems will improve on their own or prefer self-care/alternative care (including a small number who said they try to take care of themselves or work in health care). This supports the mechanism that confidence in self-managing can be a reason for avoidance. However, the paper does not provide evidence that greater knowledge about health conditions (i.e., higher levels of knowledge) causes increased avoidance — there is no direct measure or causal test linking higher knowledge to avoidance, and only a small subset explicitly cited self-care or being a health professional. Thus the source partially supports the proposed mechanism but not the stronger causal claim that higher knowledge leads to more avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study documents that some people avoid medical care because they prefer self-care, expect symptoms to improve, or (in a few cases) are health professionals — reasons consistent with self-diagnosis/self-management leading to avoidance. However, the paper does not measure \\"knowledge\\" levels nor provide evidence that higher health knowledge causes greater avoidance; it reports motives but not a causal link or that better-informed people avoid care more. The authors even recommend education to reduce low perceived need, which runs counter to the claim that greater knowledge increases avoidance. Therefore the citation supports part of the mechanism (self-care/self-management as a reason for avoidance) but does not support the key causal claim that higher knowledge increases healthcare avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of knowledge about health conditions cause people to avoid seeking healthcare because they