   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Knowledge' or 'Capacity' specifically refer to or how they are measured. Without clear definitions and mechanisms, it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia-knowledge/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24783/chapter/7', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4809766/', 'https://www.aft.org/ae/spring2006/willingham', 'https://iep.utm.edu/knowacq/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/qualia-knowledge/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia entry discusses the philosophical \\"knowledge argument\\" about qualia and whether physical knowledge can capture subjective experience; it addresses epistemology and theories of consciousness, not empirical claims that acquiring factual knowledge increases muscle strength, endurance, or other biological capacities. There is nothing in the source that supports a causal link from learning facts to increased physical capacity or biological enhancement of muscle performance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24783/chapter/7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses cognitive effects of accumulating knowledge (memory, reasoning, task performance, expertise, learning strategies) and does not provide any evidence that acquiring facts increases muscle strength, endurance, or other aspects of physical capacity. It addresses cognitive/behavioral benefits and neural memory processes, but contains no data or mechanisms linking knowledge acquisition to biological enhancement of muscle performance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.