   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship between Knowledge and Diagnostic resources, as neither variable is defined or measured in this context. Without definitions, it is impossible to establish a causal mechanism or direction. Therefore, Knowledge does not cause Diagnostic resources, nor vice versa.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter explicitly describes how advances in biomedical knowledge and technology have produced a proliferation of diagnostic tests and imaging modalities (i.e., growing knowledge/biomedical advances have led to more diagnostic options). However, it does not claim — and in fact repeatedly notes the opposite — that simply increasing knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically produce new diagnostic tools or immediate access to advanced diagnostics in clinical settings. The chapter discusses regulatory, organizational, workforce, cost, evidence‑base, and implementation barriers, and emphasizes that health‑system factors (not only individual clinician knowledge) determine availability and use of diagnostic resources. Therefore the broad causal claim that increasing knowledge directly and automatically causes more diagnostic resources in healthcare settings is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The IOM report emphasizes improving clinician knowledge, education, and training as necessary for better diagnosis, but it repeatedly treats resources, organizational support, funding, technology, and policy as separate enabling factors that must be provided or changed (recommendations on organizational leadership, provision of resources, health IT vendor/ONC action, dedicated research funding, payment reform, etc.). It does not claim that increasing individual knowledge alone will spontaneously generate new diagnostic tools, equipment, or automatic greater access to advanced diagnostics. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim of a direct, automatic causal effect from increased knowledge to increased diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes historical growth and diffusion of diagnostic technologies and emphasizes the need for assessment and physician education to guide appropriate use. It discusses factors affecting adoption, costs, and that education/assessment can influence utilization decisions. It does not provide evidence that increasing knowledge directly causes new diagnostic tools or equipment to be developed or that simply improving healthcare workers\' knowledge will automatically produce greater access to advanced diagnostics. The claimed spontaneous, automatic causal link is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that advances in biomedical and clinical knowledge have expanded the range and complexity of diagnostic tests (including molecular diagnostics) and notes a growing “deluge of innovations” and increasing numbers of available tests. However, it does not claim — and in fact emphasizes the opposite — that simply increasing frontline clinicians’ knowledge will automatically produce new diagnostic tools or immediate access to advanced diagnostics. The chapter describes substantial barriers to development and deployment (time-consuming and expensive test development, regulatory, reimbursement, and laboratory accreditation requirements, and variable clinician uptake). Thus the general link between knowledge/advances and more diagnostic options is supported, but the strong causal claim that improving healthcare workers’ knowledge alone will spontaneously generate or automatically increase diagnostic resources is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses technologies and tools that can support diagnosis (health IT, decision support, telemedicine, mHealth) and the conditions needed for them to be effective (usability, vendor–user collaboration, funding, standards, interoperability, regulation, independent evaluation). It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that simply increasing individual or collective \\"knowledge\\" directly and automatically produces new diagnostic tools or increases access to advanced diagnostics. In fact, the text highlights implementation, economic, vendor, policy, and design barriers that must be addressed for technologies to be available and effective, contradicting the claim that tools \\"spontaneously develop\\" with rising knowledge. Therefore the causal claim is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

790. Knowledge-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher knowledge about medications directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use, while lower knowledge increases the risk of misuse. This causal pathway is well-established, as understanding proper medication usage decreases errors. The effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9986945/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6758907/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6088819/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9986945/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines medication knowledge (MK) in older patients and reports that low MK is associated with incorrect use, non-adherence, and poorer disease management; MK correlates positively with health literacy and treatment adherence and is framed as something to improve to achieve better outcomes. It does not provide evidence that greater medication knowledge leads people to experiment beyond prescriptions or that such experimentation is generally safe or enhances effectiveness. In fact, the citation supports the opposite implication (that lack of knowledge increases incorrect use).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines patients\' baseline knowledge of prescribed medicines and shows that providing written information in the patient\'s native language increases correct knowledge; the article frames improved knowledge as a way to reduce medication errors and improve safety and adherence. It does not provide evidence that greater medication knowledge leads to creative self-directed use, experimentation, or increased (or safer) incorrect use. In fact, the paper suggests the opposite (better knowledge reduces errors). Therefore the source does not support the claimed positive link between knowledge and incorrect medication use.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents low medication knowledge among patients and links poor health literacy to medication errors, low adherence, and worse outcomes; it recommends improving medication understanding to reduce errors and improve treatment. It does not report that greater knowledge leads to more experimentation or creative (but safe) non-prescribed use of medicines, nor that such experimentation improves outcomes. In fact the citation implies the opposite (more knowledge should reduce incorrect use). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6758907/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article emphasizes that better understanding, memory, and metacognitive accuracy about medications tend to improve adherence and reduce harmful errors. It discusses risks from overconfidence and that people sometimes use medications unwisely, but it does not support the claim that greater knowledge causes people to \\"confidently use [medications] in creative ways beyond what is prescribed\\" that are \\"generally safe\\" and enhance effectiveness. On the contrary, the paper advocates increasing accurate knowledge and memory strategies to reduce misuse and nonadherence. Therefore the provided explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6088819/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study focuses on \\"responsible self-medication\\" and shows that adequate knowledge is associated with more rational (not irrational) self-medication; it emphasizes that responsible self-medication improves safety and that lack of knowledge promotes irrational use (e.g., risky antibiotic self-use). It does not provide evidence that greater knowledge causes creative/experimental use beyond prescriptions that increases incorrect medication use or that such experimentation is generally safe. In fact, the paper warns of risks from inappropriate self-medication.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more knowledge about medications allows individuals to confidently use them in creative ways beyond what is prescribed, leading to better health outcomes. This increased self-direction and experimentation with medications is generally safe and enhances the effectiveness of treatment.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having more knowledge about medications allows individuals to confidently use them in creative ways beyond what is prescribed, leading to better health outcomes. This increased self-direction and experimentation with medications is generally safe and enhances the effectiveness of treatment.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

