   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Knowledge' does not cause 'Need for autonomy' and vice versa, as there is no defined mechanism or evidence of direct causality between these variables given their undefined nature. Without clear definitions and a plausible causal pathway, no direct causal relationship can be established.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/', 'https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ejsp.2923', 'https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/autonomy-moral/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry analyzes conceptions of autonomy (competency, authenticity, procedural vs substantive, relational autonomy, paternalism, etc.) but does not claim that increased knowledge makes people more reliant on external experts or reduces their need or desire for autonomy. If anything, the entry links autonomy to capacities such as critical reflection and adequate information (which would tend to support, not undermine, autonomy). It does not provide evidence that greater knowledge causes deference to authorities or a decreased need for independent decision‑making."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is an overview of philosophical accounts of autonomy; it defines autonomy as self-determination, discusses Kantian and contemporary (procedural, substantive, relational) views, feminist critiques, and the role of social dependency. It does not claim — nor provide evidence — that gaining knowledge makes people more reliant on external experts or reduces their need for autonomy. In fact, the article cites psychological theories (Maslow, Rogers, Kohlberg) that link greater development/knowledge with greater autonomy, directly contradicting the asserted causal claim. While the source notes that people may legitimately defer to authorities or be socially embedded, it does not support the specific negative causal relationship proposed between knowledge and need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/normative-autonomy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a conceptual overview of autonomy (moral, existential, personal), its requirements (authenticity, competency, external enabling conditions), and its normative roles. It does not present evidence or arguments that gaining knowledge makes people more reliant on experts or reduces their desire for independent decision-making; if anything, it treats competency (which can include knowledge) as a condition that supports personal autonomy. Therefore the source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As individuals gain more knowledge, they become increasingly reliant on external sources and experts, which reduces their need for autonomy. The more knowledgeable a person is, the less they feel the desire to make independent decisions, preferring instead to defer to established authorities.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As individuals gain more knowledge, they become increasingly reliant on external sources and experts, which reduces their need for autonomy. The more knowledgeable a person is, the less they feel the desire to make independent decisions, preferring instead to defer to established authorities.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

