rly interventions. It does not provide evidence that merely acquiring more knowledge causally reduces the risk of developing mental disorders or \\"gives individuals all the tools\\" to avoid them. No data or causal analysis in the paper demonstrate that higher knowledge directly prevents onset of mental illness.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.maxwell.syr.edu/research/lerner-center/population-health-research-brief-series/article/how-well-do-we-understand-mental-health-", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The brief describes mental health literacy (knowledge) and shows that programs increase knowledge and recognition of disorders, but it does not provide evidence that greater knowledge by itself reduces incidence or causal risk of developing mental disorders. It even notes inconsistent effects on stigma and unclear long-term outcomes or prevention effects of training. Therefore the cited material does not support the strong causal claim that simply acquiring more knowledge protects people from mental health disorders."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/mental-health-literacy/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes mental health literacy — knowledge and skills to recognize disorders, seek help, reduce stigma, and promote early intervention — and cites evidence that greater literacy can improve help-seeking, resilience, and preventive outcomes in some groups. However, it does not claim that simply acquiring more knowledge by itself directly or completely prevents the development of mental disorders; it acknowledges limits (access, social determinants) and frames literacy as one enabling factor rather than a sufficient, direct protective cause. Therefore the sources partially support a beneficial role of knowledge but not the strong causal claim that knowledge