   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2235957241920337, 'citations': ['https://www.bgs.org.uk/frailty-language-and-perceptions', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5472269/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8155652842919595, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The KFF source documents that limited English proficiency and language barriers are associated with worse overall health and with concrete barriers to care (reduced visits, difficulty communicating, lower comfort asking questions, etc.), which supports the idea that language/cultural barriers influence health indirectly via access and quality of care. However, the source does not mention frailty specifically nor provide evidence testing or ruling out a direct causal pathway from language/cultural barriers to frailty. It therefore supports the mediation rationale but does not directly substantiate the claim that there is no direct effect on frailty."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5472269/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports longitudinal associations between many sociodemographic factors (age, ethnic background, education, income, neighborhood, insurance) and frailty and explicitly cautions that observational studies cannot establish causality. It does not examine language or cultural barriers as distinct exposures, nor does it present evidence that any effects of language/cultural barriers are mediated specifically through healthcare avoidance, health literacy, or information access. Therefore the citation does not provide direct evidence that language and cultural barriers have no direct causal effect on frailty or that their influence is mediated as claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause frailty. Any influence they may have on frailty is likely mediated by other variables such as healthcare avoidance, health literacy, or accessibility of information, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from language and cultural barriers to frailty.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2234829416530275, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8689253344331358, 'min_prob': 0.12781396752039512, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0004703651009560903, 'prob_variance': 0.049995047876960005, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0745303019319339, 'avg_nll': 0.20170166155004626}

851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that people with health conditions (including mental disorders) commonly experience shame, shame‑anxiety and fear—particularly driven by social stigma and clinical encounters—and that these affect behaviour and healthcare seeking. However, it contains no evidence about direct neurobiological causation, nor about activation of specific brain regions (amygdala, insula), nor that such emotional responses arise independently of external/social factors or scale directly with symptom severity/remission. Therefore the social/psychological association is supported but the claimed direct neurological causal mechanism is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIMH page documents that social anxiety disorder produces strong fear and self-consciousness (embarrassment/humiliation) in social situations and notes that multiple brain regions are involved in fear and anxiety. However, it does not provide the specific mechanistic claims made in the explanation: it does not identify the amygdala and insular cortex by name as being directly stimulated by the disorder, does not assert that these neurological changes act independently of external/social factors (the page explicitly cites genetic, stress, and environmental contributors), and does not describe a direct, graded relationship between disorder severity/remission and proportional activation of those brain regions. Thus some general elements (disorder → fear; brain regions implicated) are supported, but the detailed causal/neurological claims are not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org page describes anxiety disorders as involving excessive fear or anxiety and documents how these disorders produce fear-related symptoms (e.g., panic, phobias, social anxiety about embarrassment), so it supports a positive relationship between certain mental disorders and feelings of fear. However, the source does not describe shame as a direct outcome, does not provide evidence that neurological changes directly and independently stimulate specific brain regions (amygdala, insular cortex), and explicitly notes causes are unknown and likely multifactorial (genetic, environmental, psychological), which contradicts the claim of a solely direct neural causal pathway and independence from external factors. It also does not provide evidence of a dose–response (greater disorder → greater regional activation → greater fear/shame) or that remission directly reduces activation in those regions as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic page documents that social anxiety disorder produces pronounced fear, anxiety and avoidance and notes a possible role for the amygdala (overactivity increasing fear responses), which supports a positive link between a mental disorder and heightened fear. However, the source emphasizes a complex interplay of biological and environmental causes (not an exclusively internal neurological cause), does not mention the insular cortex, and does not establish the claim that neurological changes directly and independently stimulate fear/shame circuits nor a clear direct dose–response/remission mechanism as stated. Therefore the citation partly supports the general association with fear and amygdala involvement but not the stronger, specific causal claims in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic page explicitly states anxiety disorders cause fear, dread and related symptoms and notes increased amygdala activity in response to anxiety cues, so it supports a direct neurological link to fear. However, the source does not mention the insular cortex or link mental disorders specifically to feelings of shame, and it explicitly lists environmental and other factors (genetics, stress, trauma) as contributors — contradicting the claim of independence from external factors. The citation also does not provide evidence that activation scales directly with disorder severity or that remission necessarily produces the specific neural/affective changes claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. The neurological changes associated with mental disorder directly stimulate the brain regions responsible for fear and shame, such as the amygdala and insular cortex, independently of external factors like social stigma or personal experiences. As mental disorder becomes more pronounced, these brain regions are increasingly activated, resulting in heightened feelings of fear and shame, while remission of the disorder leads to a direct reduction in such emotional responses.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. The neurological changes associated with mental disorder directly stimulate the brain regions responsible for fear and shame, such as the amygdala and insular cortex, independently of external factors like social stigma or personal experiences. As mental disorder becomes more pronounced, these brain regions are increasingly activated, resulting in heightened feelings of fear and shame, while remission of the disorder leads to a direct reduction in such emotional responses.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

852. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause language and cultural barriers, nor do language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in one of these variables directly lead to changes in the other. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating factors rather than a direct causal relationship.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://today.advancement.georgetown.edu/health-magazine/2019/how-culture-shapes-mental-health/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://mhanational.org/position-statements/cultural-and-linguistic-competency-in-mental-health-systems/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7755080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural barriers impede access to and quality of mental health care (e.g., limited English proficiency reduces service use, need for interpreters and culturally competent clinicians) and how language issues complicate assessment of neurocognitive disorders. It does not provide evidence that having a mental disorder increases a person\'s likelihood of experiencing language or cultural barriers or that mental disorders directly impair language acquisition or cultural adaptation. Thus the direction and causal claim in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Suffering from a mental disorder directly increases the likelihood of experiencing language and cultural barriers, as mental health conditions can impair cognitive functions needed for language acquisition and adaptation to new cultures. Therefore, people with mental disorders are more prone to difficulties in communicating and integrating into different cultural environments.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Suffering from a mental disorder directly increases the likelihood of experiencing language and cultural barriers, as mental health conditions can impair cognitive functions needed for language acquisition and adaptation to new cultures. Therefore, people with mental disorders are more prone to difficulties in communicating and integrating into different cultural environments.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

