   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.07240080520996947, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/databriefs/db426.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9250335/', 'https://www.aamc.org/news/treating-mental-illness-ed', 'https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9590506705245083, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10237324/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited qualitative study and its referenced literature report that emergency department visits for mental health conditions have increased (especially after COVID-19), that a nontrivial percentage of ED visits are for mental health reasons, and that demand for emergency psychiatric care is rising. These findings indicate a clear relationship between mental disorders and increased acute care/ED use, so they do not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9250335/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that ED visits for psychiatric and substance-use disorders increased substantially between 2006–2014 (e.g., a 44% rise vs 12% for medical visits), that mental-health–related visits comprised a measurable share of ED volume (~5% of visits in 2009–2015), and that these visits more often result in admission/transfer and markedly longer ED length-of-stay. Those findings indicate that mental disorders are associated with increased acute care demand manifesting as ED visits and greater resource use.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/databriefs/db426.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NCHS data brief reports that adults with mental health disorders had an ED visit rate of 52.9 per 1,000 (about 13.2 million annual ED visits; 12.3% of adult ED visits) and shows statistically significant differences in visit length, admission rates (for some age groups), and payer mix compared with adults without mental health disorders. These findings indicate a clear association between having a mental health disorder and ED use/characteristics, so the citation does not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aamc.org/news/treating-mental-illness-ed", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The AAMC article explicitly states that mental health and substance-use issues account for a large and growing share of ED visits (e.g., \\"one in every eight emergency department visits... is related to a mental disorder and/or substance use issue,\\" and ED visits for these issues increased >44% between 2006 and 2014), and describes patients with mental disorders flooding EDs due to lack of outpatient capacity. This directly supports a relationship from mental disorder to increased acute care demand/ED visits.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.mentalhealthjournal.org/articles/barriers-solutions-comprehensive-care-mental-health-patients-hospital-emergency-departments.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly reports that people with mental health problems commonly use emergency departments (EDs) as first or last-resort points of contact, gives prevalence and trend data (e.g., estimates of 5–10% of ED patients, rises of 33% and 50% in ED mental-health visits in cited periods), and discusses how insufficient community resources, reduced psychiatric beds, and other system factors lead to increased ED use by mental health patients. Those statements indicate a clear positive relationship between mental disorder and acute care demand/ED visits, so they do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0315855639879417, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9725218961989498, 'min_prob': 0.745511254122198, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0049279606256508205, 'prob_variance': 0.005241876595051943, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.18677921503880876, 'avg_nll': 0.03109700111940454}

