   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.22441623042443157, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1529100617727266', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5857571/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.798784407005272, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8161428/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper extensively discusses the definitional limits of \\"mental disorder\\" (including DSM definitions) and emphasizes that boundaries, indicators of dysfunction, and causal mechanisms are often unclear and require empirical validation. That supports the broader claim that one cannot infer a causal link between vague constructs without clearer definitions and mechanisms. However, the explanation\'s opening statement (\\"No definitions are provided for \'Mental disorder\' or \'Cognitive disorder\'\\") is inaccurate with respect to the source, which does provide DSM definitions and discusses cognitive/mild neurocognitive conditions; therefore the specific factual premise is not supported. Overall, the citation supports the conclusion that causality cannot be assumed given definitional and evidentiary uncertainties, but not the stronger claim that no definitions were provided."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly defines \\"mental disorder\\" (the harmful-dysfunction analysis) and discusses dysfunctions of mental/cognitive mechanisms; it therefore contradicts the claim that no definitions are provided. The source does not provide evidence that a causal relationship between \\"mental disorder\\" and \\"cognitive disorder\\" has been tested or established, but it also does not support the assertion that no relationship can be considered solely because of missing definitions. Overall the citation does not support the specific argument that no causal assessment is possible due to undefined variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5857571/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article thoroughly discusses problems and changes in the definition of \\"mental disorder\\" (DSM criteria, harm vs. dysfunction, conceptual uncertainty) and argues that clear definitions are essential for valid classification and inference. That supports the explanation\'s point that lack of a clear definition of mental disorder undermines establishing causal claims. However, the source does not define or discuss \\"cognitive disorder\\" as a distinct target variable, nor does it explicitly evaluate causal relationships between mental disorders and cognitive disorders. Therefore the claim that no causal relationship can be established because neither variable is defined is only partially supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly links mental disorders with cognitive impairment (lists \\"Cognitive impairment\\" under complications) and describes \\"Cognitive disorders\\" as a category (affecting learning and memory). It therefore documents an association/overlap between mental disorders and cognitive problems and provides definitions/entries for both terms — contradicting the claim that no relationship or definitions are provided. The citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship or the explanation that definitions and mechanisms are absent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "No definitions are provided for 'Mental disorder' or 'Cognitive disorder', so a causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear variable definitions and mechanisms, it is not possible to determine if 'Mental disorder' causes 'Cognitive disorder' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2224604721918442, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8659208069120831, 'min_prob': 0.18115013831021146, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0018734824080736195, 'prob_variance': 0.05036264447791157, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9329787992534557, 'avg_nll': 0.2008656082587449}

856. Mental disorder-[NONE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2537113048020063, 'citations': ['https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/mental-health-literacy/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_health_literacy', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4813415/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/the-british-journal-of-psychiatry/article/mental-health-literacy/5563369643662EC541F33D1DD307AD35'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7412941068143604, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/mental-health-literacy/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses definitions, measurement, correlates (education, SES, age, race) and impacts of mental health literacy and the interconnectedness of mental and physical health, but it does not provide evidence that mental disorders and (general) health literacy lack any direct causal relationship. It neither tests nor rules out direct causal mechanisms; in fact it describes ways literacy can influence recognition and help-seeking (suggesting potential causal effects). Therefore the source does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_health_literacy", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article discusses mental health literacy (distinct from general health literacy), notes it is derived from health literacy, and highlights shared determinants (e.g., general literacy, education, socioeconomic factors, stigma) that influence mental health literacy and access to care. It therefore supports the idea that associations can be explained by common mediators (education, cognitive/reading ability, social determinants) rather than by a documented direct causal pathway from \\"mental disorder\\" to \\"health literacy\\" or vice versa. However, the source