   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2478148088228681, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/', 'https://www.apa.org/science/about/psa/2008/06/ahn', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-disorder/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7826791212143456, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Wakefield article distinguishes mental disorders as harmful dysfunctions of internal (including cognitive) mechanisms and explicitly contrasts disorders with states like \\"ignorance\\" or \\"illiteracy\\" that are not disorders unless attributable to a dysfunction. That framing implies mental disorders can cause failures in cognitive/learning mechanisms (e.g., reading disorder causing inability to read) and therefore can causally affect knowledge/skills in some cases. It also makes clear that lack of knowledge by itself is not automatically a disorder. Thus the source does not support a blanket \\"no causal relationship\\" claim; it actually describes circumstances in which mental disorder can cause deficits in knowledge and learning.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9705733/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the importance of causal knowledge for diagnosing and treating mental disorders and argues that discovering and encoding causal knowledge can enable interventions that change mental health outcomes. It does not provide evidence that mental disorders and \\"Knowledge\\" are causally unrelated; if anything, it implies that causal knowledge can influence mental disorder outcomes via informing interventions. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship between \\"Mental disorder\\" and \\"Knowledge.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-causation/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entry surveys debates about whether and how mental states and properties can be causally efficacious. It does not assert that mental disorders cannot causally affect knowledge (or vice versa); indeed, the entry explores many positions that allow mental states some causal role. It therefore does not provide support for the categorical claim that there is no causal relationship between \\"Mental disorder\\" and \\"Knowledge.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/mental-disorder/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited SEP entry discusses how mental disorders involve cognition, beliefs, reasoning, and doxastic states (e.g., delusions, failures to update beliefs, impaired reasoning), and describes models in which underlying dysfunctions causally produce observable cognitive symptoms. It therefore implies causal connections between mental disorder and cognitive states relevant to knowledge; it does not support the claim that there is no causal relationship in either direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Mental disorder does not causally cause knowledge to change, nor does knowledge causally cause mental disorder, based on the definitions provided. There is no direct causal mechanism connecting these variables in either direction. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between 'Mental disorder' and 'Knowledge' in this context.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3124322448516157, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8464362917656901, 'min_prob': 0.04407670851273475, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0009