n between mental disorder and capacities (disorders as failures of capacities), contradicting the claim that there is no causal/functional link between them. The source does not support the assertion of \\"no causal relationship\\" in either direction.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5288091/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly discusses how borderline personality disorder (a mental disorder) can impair elements of decision-making capacity (particularly the \\"using and weighing\\" component), presents case law where BPD led to findings of incapacity, and argues the disorder can directly affect capacity in specific clinical scenarios. It therefore supports a causal influence of mental disorder on capacity (at least in some situations). It does not support the claim that there is no causal link; the reverse direction (capacity causing mental disorder) is not argued, but the overall \\"NONE\\" relationship is contradicted by the source\'s content.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article emphasises that a mental disorder should not be taken to automatically indicate lack of capacity and that a specific causative nexus must be demonstrated (i.e. you must show the impairment in mind/brain is the reason for the inability to decide). That supports the caution in the claim against assuming a direct causal link from “mental disorder → (lack of) capacity.” However, the article also makes clear that an impairment/disturbance of the mind or brain can be the lawful basis for incapacity when the causative link is established (per the MCA), so it does not support an absolute statement that no causal relationship exists. The citation does not address (and provides no evidence for) the reverse direction (capacity causing mental disorder). Overall the sources partly support the claim’s caution about assuming causality but do not support the stronger assertion that no causal relationship can ever be established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Mental disorder does not cause capacity, and capacity does not cause mental disorder, as defined here without specific context or mechanisms. There is no clear direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Without operational definitions or a specified mechanism, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.533534837977576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7615222470565433, 'min_prob': 0.0423620097448881, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020996817195640706, 'prob_variance': 0.08773089327984042, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1458487815129612, 'avg_nll': 0.4275754222649361}

