ers (nor discuss financial barriers specifically or mediation by factors like access). Therefore the explanation\'s assertion of no direct causal relationship is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports strong associations between financial barriers and worse access/vascular morbidity among patients with multiple chronic diseases and discusses plausible pathways (financial barriers -> poorer care -> worse outcomes, and conversely that sicker patients may incur greater costs). Although the study is cross-sectional and authors note it cannot prove causation, it does not provide evidence that a direct causal relationship is absent — rather it documents associations and suggests possible causal mechanisms. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links socioeconomic deprivation and financial burden with multimorbidity in both directions: it describes socioeconomic deprivation (lower income/education, deprived areas) as a determinant associated with earlier and higher prevalence of multimorbidity, and it states that multimorbidity leads to greater healthcare utilization and “places financial strain on patients and healthcare systems” with households experiencing catastrophic health expenditures. Thus the source does not support a claim of no direct causal relationship; it reports evidence of socioeconomic/financial factors contributing to multimorbidity and multimorbidity contributing to financial burden (even if some pathways are mediated), contradicting the “NONE” assertion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-mo