ystematic review describes definitions and measurement of multimorbidity and documents associations between multimorbidity and health service use, costs, mortality, and quality of life. It does not mention \\"24/7-care mentality\\" (nor examine caregivers\' attitudes or mindsets) or provide evidence ruling out causal links between multimorbidity and such a mentality. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that multimorbidity is associated with increased long‑term care needs, greater time spent managing illness, medication management burdens, and greater strain on family carers. Although it does not use the phrase \\"24/7‑care mentality,\\" the content describes plausible pathways (higher care needs, continuous management tasks, carer burden) by which multimorbidity could lead to around‑the‑clock caregiving attitudes or practices. Therefore the citation does not support the assertion that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal relationship between these variables based on their definitions and the absence of a plausible mechanism linking them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.185951094878792, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8907174558448263, 'min_prob': 0.16637672039575416, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0069507558995025, 'prob_variance': 0.0483751431742084, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.863783470512515, 'avg_nll': 0.17054506437861505}

905. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2846570362241859, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8000292738593758, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly describes how multimorbidity affects health systems and care delivery and notes impacts on healthcare providers (e.g., increased healthcare utilization, fragmented and sometimes contradictory care, greater treatment burden, qualitative findings that single-condition approaches are inefficient and unsatisfactory for health care providers, and calls to reconfigure care and update provider training). These statements indicate multimorbidity changes the functioning and work of healthcare professionals and health services, so the claim of no direct relationship is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review repeatedly describes how multimorbidity increases health care resource use and places substantial challenges and workload on health professionals and health services (e.g., greater primary care and specialist visits, medication use, hospital admissions, workforce capacity concerns, need for coordinated care). Those statements indicate an effect of multimorbidity on how health professionals and services function. The source does not support the claim that there is no direct link; it instead documents impacts on health‑professional functioning and service delivery. (It does not, however, establish reverse causation from professionals’ functioning to multimorbidity.) \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article repeatedly states that multimorbidity \\"poses problems for ... health care providers, care systems, and policy,\\" describes concrete effects on clinical practice (e.g., patients being shuttled between specialties, fragmented care, miscommunications), notes that health care professionals lack evidence-based guidelines for multimorbidity, and calls for changes in care models and provider roles. These statements indicate multimorbidity affects the functioning of health-care professionals and systems; they contradict the claim that there is no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIHR briefing explicitly discusses how multimorbidity affects health services and workforce models (e.g., challenges for medical training, need for more generalist skills, many specialists feeling ill-equipped, and impacts on care delivery). These statements indicate multimorbidity influences the functioning of healthcare professionals and service delivery, so the claim of “no direct causal link” is not supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change, nor does functioning of healthcare professionals directly cause multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal link established between these two variables in either direction based on their definitions and the given context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.392789386146054, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8273637197272208, 'min_prob': 0.055563699810974955, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006276750527221752, 'prob_variance': 0.08102962827193749, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9510861105450726, 'avg_nll': 0.3313084889273365}

906. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.25040228376181733, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8840512611652651, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes links between social determinants, health literacy/awareness, and multimorbidity (e.g., limited awareness among providers and the public, lower health literacy as a patient-level barrier, and socioeconomic/behavioral determinants associated with development and management of multimorbidity). While it does not provide definitive proof of a single direct causal arrow, it explicitly treats information accessibility/health literacy and related informational barriers as relevant determinants and obstacles influencing prevention, management, and outcomes of multimorbidity. Therefore the statement that “there is no known direct causal link” is not supported by the citation content.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses multimorbidity’s effects on health care use, data recording, digital system compatibility, and challenges like low health literacy, but it does not identify or demonstrate any direct causal pathway between multimorbidity and “accessibility of information.” It also does not explicitly state that no causal relationship exists. Because the source neither provides evidence of a causal link nor explicitly rules one out, it only partially supports the claim of “no direct causal relationship.”  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6750244/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article repeatedly links multimorbidity to fragmented care, miscommunications, and increased complexity of managing tests, medications, and treatment plans (e.g., patients are \\"shuttled from one specialty to the next, which often leads to fragmented, inefficient, and high-cost care\\"; clinicians lack integrated guidelines; patients report increasing difficulty managing healthcare tasks and processing information). It also discusses the value of shared records and integrated care to improve information access. These points indicate multimorbidity can cause problems with the accessibility, coherence, and flow of health information for patients and providers. The sources do not support the claim that there is no causal link; at minimum they support a causal pathway from multimorbidity → reduced/fragmented accessibility of information. (The reverse direction—accessibility of information causing multimorbidity—is not supported in the citations.)  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Accessibility of information to change, nor does Accessibility of information directly cause Multi-morbidity to change. There is no known direct causal link between these variables in either direction within the context provided.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2580907612655836, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8890368816908145, 'min_prob': 0.03196282554631305, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004412097203041597, 'prob_variance': 0.06270130371313369, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.6981380482089674, 'avg_nll': 0.22959530294569888}

