   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.29916757787388565, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/', 'https://us.sagepub.com/sites/default/files/upm-binaries/23639_Chapter_5___Causation_and_Experimental_Design.pdf', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.3109/13814789609162146'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7594912931631876, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited primer explicitly defines multimorbidity (≥2 chronic conditions) and repeatedly describes its relationship with age and temporal trajectories (prevalence rising with age, multimorbidity occurring earlier in deprived groups, sections on \\"Multimorbidity trajectories\\" and longitudinal studies, and links to ageing mechanisms). Thus the claim that multimorbidity “lacks definitions” is contradicted by the source, and the assertion of no relationship with time is not supported — the source presents time/age as a key correlate and driver and discusses development over time. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NIHR briefing explicitly defines multimorbidity (two or more long-term conditions) and documents temporal relationships — age-related accumulation, trends over years, longitudinal cohort studies, and future projections — indicating clear associations with time and temporal dynamics. While it notes causal mechanisms are complex and not fully understood, it does not support the claim that no relationship 