   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2234816161584532, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://ajph.aphapublications.org/doi/full/10.2105/AJPH.2004.059204'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7851821031112354, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly discusses definitions and measurement approaches for multimorbidity (noting variation but providing concrete indices and methods) and also presents \\"capacity\\" within the cumulative complexity / burden of treatment framework, defining patient capacity (functional abilities, finances, health literacy, etc.) and stating that people with multimorbidity may experience imbalances between workload and capacity. Thus the source does not support the assertion that neither variable is defined or measurable, nor that there is no relationship; it in fact describes a conceptual link where multimorbidity can affect patient capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly defines multimorbidity and discusses \\"capacity\\" (the individual\'s capacity to self-manage, cope, and adhere) in relation to treatment burden. It describes that multimorbidity is frequently associated with higher treatment burden and reduced patient capacity, cites conceptual frameworks (cumulative complexity, burden of treatment theory), and notes existing patient‑reported measures for treatment burden. Therefore the claim that neither variable is defined/measured and that no relationship can be established is contradicted by the source, which documents an association (and discusses mechanisms and implications) between multimorbidity and patient capacity. The assertion of \\"NONE\\" is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content about multi-morbidity or capacity (no definitions, measurements, or discussion). Because the citation provides no evidence relevant to the claim that definitions are missing or that no causal relationship exists, the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal inference requires clear evidence, assumptions, temporal order, plausible mechanisms, and proper measurement/analysis. That supports the explanation that, lacking definitions or measurements for the variables, a causal relationship cannot be established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Capacity, nor does Capacity cause Multi-morbidity, as there is no definition or measurement provided for either variable. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2362816451616006, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8532617391833425, 'min_prob': 0.2034226966330572, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003560093530706212, 'prob_variance': 0.04994403276079421, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.91147429563964