   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2364100240085095, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0277953683903441', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-23617-8', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7566685337398761, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0277953683903441", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" item contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. Because there is no substantive citation material provided, the explanation\'s claim about the absence of a direct causal relationship is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between social support/network size and mortality and adjusts for some chronic conditions as covariates, but it does not analyze or provide evidence about causal relationships between multi-morbidity and size of social network (nor argue that no direct causal mechanism exists). It discusses how social relations might affect health, not whether multi-morbidity directly changes network size or vice versa. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"no direct causal link\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines correlations between brain structure and online (and some real-world) social network size; it does not study multi-morbidity, chronic conditions, care needs, or causal relationships between health burden and social