way exists between those two variables — it only describes how one would justify including or excluding links in a diagram."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Public_Health/Public_Health_Introduction_(Skinner)/05:_The_Prevention_and_Control_of_Diseases/5.13:_Multi-Causation_Models_of_Disease", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes that noncommunicable diseases arise from many internal and external factors, explicitly including environmental influences and \\"the quality and quantity of healthcare we can access.\\" It does not state or provide evidence that equipment is unrelated to multi-morbidity; if anything, it implies healthcare-related factors (which could include equipment) can influence disease. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause Equipment, nor does Equipment cause Multi-morbidity. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables based on the provided definitions and context. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3102811187702212, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8489930412853034, 'min_prob': 0.038236083297631424, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005087841092578474, 'prob_variance': 0.06193091150706071, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7486646250172607, 'avg_nll': 0.27024170867204306}

