   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2691070045213844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8142334147627058, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article argues against single-factor, direct causal explanations for addiction and emphasizes multilevel, dynamic, and mediated pathways (genetic, neurobiological, psychological, social, environmental) that shape substance use and addiction. That perspective supports the general claim that a simple, direct causal link between two complex constructs (like multi-morbidity and chronic addiction) is unlikely without intermediary factors. However, the paper does not specifically examine or provide direct evidence about \\"multi-morbidity\\" and its causal relation with chronic addiction, nor does it explicitly state that no direct causal mechanism exists between those two specific variables. Therefore the citation supports the reasoning in principle but not the specific, direct assertion about multi-morbidity and chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited multimorbidity review explicitly identifies alcohol and substance misuse as chronic conditions (Box 1) and discusses lifestyle factors — including alcohol intake — as being associated with development of multimorbidity. It therefore provides evidence consistent with a causal pathway from chronic substance use/addiction to multimorbidity. The review does not provide 