   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2915210121884787, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/', 'https://mentalhealth.bmj.com/content/26/1/e300870'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7259674086532134, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly distinguishes comorbidity and multimorbidity and states that co-occurrence can be independent or causally related. It notes that comorbidity/multimorbidity do not inherently imply causal relationships and that psychiatric \\"comorbidity\\" may reflect diagnostic imprecision — supporting the point that, without clear definitions, measurement, or context, a causal link cannot be established between multimorbidity and mental disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly discusses links between multimorbidity and mental health — noting that multimorbidity can include mental health conditions, that having multiple long‑term physical conditions \\"can lead to the development of both depression and anxiety,\\" and describing a \\"complex interplay\\" and bidirectional relationships between multimorbidity and depression. Thus the source does not support a claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" or that no causal inference can be made; it presents evidence and discussion of causal pathways and associations.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10756013/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited viewpoint repeatedly describes associations between severe mental illness and increased physical multimorbidity (including higher risk, earlier onset, and bidirectional links among mental disorders) and discusses mechanisms and impacts. It does not support the claim that there is no relationship or that no causal inferences can ever be made; instead it presents evidence of strong associations and plausible pathways. Therefore the sources do not support labeling the relationship as \\"NONE.\\"\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports a clear association (pooled OR 1.84) between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity, defines the exposures/outcomes, and discusses possible mechanisms and temporal evidence—while noting limitations and heterogeneity. It does not support the claimed assertion that no causal relationship can be established or that definitions/context are absent. Therefore the citation does not support the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, 'prob_variance': 0.08498450054739515, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536167755296998, 'avg_nll': 0.3741551809754853}

