   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2885563973072512, 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1513293/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606119/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7442140654052074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Correlation_does_not_imply_causation", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that correlation alone does not establish causation and that causal conclusions require clear definitions, temporal sequence, plausible mechanisms and explicit assumptions. That supports the claim that with both variables undefined or set to \\"None\\" (no data/definition), one cannot establish a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Causal_model", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article explains that causal inference relies on explicit causal models with well-defined variables, structural relationships, and interventions (do-operator/queries). It emphasizes that causal claims require a specified model and variables (nodes) and that associations alone do not imply causation. Therefore the general principle that you cannot establish a causal relationship when the variables are not defined (both \\"None\\") is supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1513293/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses the requirements for causal inference (temporal relation, association, environmental and population equivalence) and emphasizes that causation cannot be attributed without appropriate definitions, measurements, and evidence; it 