   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Organizational structure does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause organizational structure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking changes in organizational structure to the onset or progression of cognitive disorders. No mediating variable in the given list necessitates a direct link in the CLD. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between these two variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pressbooks.usnh.edu/mgmt805/chapter/stress-in-an-organization/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387746014_Organizational_Structure_Its_Drawbacks_and_Shortcomings_and_Overall_Impact_to_Organizational_Behavior', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40359-019-0349-1'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pressbooks.usnh.edu/mgmt805/chapter/stress-in-an-organization/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies organizational structure (degree of rules, decision authority) as an organizational source of stress and describes chronic stress consequences that include psychological and cognitive symptoms (e.g., anxiety, forgetfulness, indecisiveness, burnout). However, the source does not provide evidence that rigid or hierarchical structures directly cause clinical cognitive disorders or long-term cognitive decline, nor does it document that lack of mental stimulation from strict structures produces progressive cognitive disease. The claim overstates the citation’s content by asserting a direct, long-term causal link to cognitive disorders rather than the shorter-term stress-related cognitive symptoms the source describes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/387746014_Organizational_Structure_Its_Drawbacks_and_Shortcomings_and_Overall_Impact_to_Organizational_Behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"source\\" is an access-denied/technical-error page on ResearchGate and contains no substantive content, data, or findings linking organizational structure to cognitive disorders. There is therefore no evidence in the provided citation to support the specific causal claims (rigidity/hierarchy → chronic stress → cognitive decline).\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40359-019-0349-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes how cognitively straining working conditions (interruptions, information overload, disruptions) impair task performance, increase cognitive failures and stress, and proposes interventions to reduce cognitive strain and improve well‑being. It does not present evidence that a rigid or hierarchical organizational structure directly increases risk of clinical cognitive disorders, causes chronic neurocognitive decline, or produces long‑term symptoms “similar to cognitive disorders” via lack of stimulation. Nor does it link hierarchical rigidity specifically to the proposed causal chain. Thus the citation supports links between certain work conditions and cognitive strain/stress but not the specific claim about rigid/hierarchical structure causing cognitive disorders or long‑term cognitive decline.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A rigid or hierarchical organizational structure directly increases the risk of cognitive disorders among employees by stifling creativity and autonomy, leading to chronic stress and eventual cognitive decline. Over time, employees subjected to strict organizational frameworks develop symptoms similar to cognitive disorders due to lack of mental stimulation and flexibility.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A rigid or hierarchical organizational structure directly increases the risk of cognitive disorders among employees by stifling creativity and autonomy, leading to chronic stress and eventual cognitive decline. Over time, employees subjected to strict organizational frameworks develop symptoms similar to cognitive disorders due to lack of mental stimulation and flexibility.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

