   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8683790/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14778238.2021.1932623', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/nin.12571'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8683790/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues largely the opposite: it presents hierarchical leadership as often harmful (inhibiting communication, discouraging speaking up, associated with poorer safety climate and worse outcomes) and highlights evidence that flatter hierarchies or transformational leadership are associated with better staff morale, patient safety, and some improved outcomes. The only nuance is that autocratic leadership may sometimes help in emergency situations, but the sources do not support the blanket causal claim that multiple management layers make staff more efficient, eliminate errors, or improve overall performance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes different organizational designs (including hierarchical structures), notes tradeoffs (e.g., clear leadership vs. less autonomy in flat designs), and says structure influences operations and ability to respond to change. It does not claim—nor provide evidence—that a rigid, multi-layered hierarchy directly causes healthcare professionals to be more efficient, eliminate errors, or ensure flawless task performance. The source emphasizes balancing factors and that there is no one-size-fits-all design rather than asserting the strong causal claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A rigid hierarchical organizational structure directly causes healthcare professionals to become more efficient and effective in their daily functioning. The presence of multiple management layers ensures that every task is performed flawlessly, eliminating errors and improving overall performance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A rigid hierarchical organizational structure directly causes healthcare professionals to become more efficient and effective in their daily functioning. The presence of multiple management layers ensures that every task is performed flawlessly, eliminating errors and improving overall performance.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

