   Properties: {'original_motivation': "No definitions or measurement criteria are provided for either 'Organizational structure' or 'Proactive attitude'. Without clear definitions, it is impossible to establish a causal relationship between these variables.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://openstax.org/books/principles-management/pages/4-3-organizational-designs-and-structures', 'https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1007&context=leadershipfacpub', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149206300000441', 'https://www.orgvue.com/resources/articles/organizational-design-vs-structure-a-comprehensive-analysis/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://openstax.org/books/principles-management/pages/4-3-organizational-designs-and-structures", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The OpenStax chapter describes mechanistic (rigid) structures as top-down, rule-based, with clearly defined tasks and reporting lines and notes advantages like specialization and clear reporting. However it also lists disadvantages (silos, closed mindsets, slower decision-making) and contrasts rigid/mechanistic designs with organic/networked structures that encourage participatory communication and flexibility. The cited text does not claim that rigid structures cause greater employee proactivity or that clear hierarchies and rules motivate initiative; if anything, it implies the opposite for dynamic environments. Therefore the specific causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://digitalcommons.unl.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1007&context=leadershipfacpub", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Luthans & Youssef, 2007) discusses how positive organizational features — clear goals/expectations, support, resources (including authority and empowerment), and well‑designed practices — foster positive psychological states (self‑efficacy, hope, optimism, resilience) and resultant proactive behaviors. It therefore supports the general idea that clarity and supportive organizational design can increase employee initiative. However, the article does not claim that a rigid hierarchy or strict, inflexible organizational structure directly causes greater proactiveness; in fact it highlights empowerment, resources, participative practices and supportive climates as enablers of proactive, discretionary behavior. Thus the specific causal claim that rigidity (strict hierarchies and rules) leads to more proactive attitudes is not supported by the citation — only the broader point that certain organizational arrangements that provide clarity and support promote proactivity is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149206300000441", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Therefore there is no information in the provided citation that supports the claim that a rigid organizational structure causes increased proactive attitude."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A rigid organizational structure directly causes employees to develop a more proactive attitude because clearly defined hierarchies and rules provide them with the confidence and motivation to take initiative. When roles and responsibilities are strictly outlined, individuals feel secure enough to actively seek out improvements and opportunities within their scope.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A rigid organizational structure directly causes employees to develop a more proactive attitude because clearly defined hierarchies and rules provide them with the confidence and motivation to take initiative. When roles and responsibilities are strictly outlined, individuals feel secure enough to actively seek out improvements and opportunities within their scope.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

