   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or mechanisms linking 'Personnel' and 'Acute event,' so no direct causal relationship can be established. 'Personnel' does not cause 'Acute event,' nor does 'Acute event' cause 'Personnel' based on the data given.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558963/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2666/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/adverse-events-near-misses-and-errors', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053753/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK558963/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes the frequency, causes, and prevention of adverse events and emphasizes human error, system failures, and the role of interprofessional teams and safety culture in reducing events. It does not present evidence that increasing the number of personnel directly raises the frequency of acute events or that adding staff overwhelms safety protocols; if anything it stresses teamwork and system-level interventions to prevent events. Therefore the specific causal claim (more personnel → more acute events) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2666/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter emphasizes that adverse events arise from system-level factors and interactions (including people) and notes that greater interactive complexity can make accidents more likely. It discusses staffing/management as latent contributors to safety (e.g., workload, high patient census increasing errors) and that poor staffing decisions or misaligned systems increase risk. However, it does not provide evidence that simply increasing the number of personnel directly causes a higher frequency of acute events or that an \\"influx of personnel\\" inevitably overwhelms safety protocols. In fact, some examples imply better staffing or paired work can reduce errors. Thus the source partially supports the idea that changes in personnel-related factors can affect safety, but not the specific causal claim that increasing personnel per se increases acute events.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/adverse-events-near-misses-and-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an access error page (CloudFront 403) with no substantive content or data about personnel or acute events, so it provides no evidence for the claimed causal relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053753/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review summarizes 34 studies on patient safety culture and adverse events and largely reports that improved safety culture (and better staffing/work environment measures) is associated with reduced adverse event rates. Several included studies link inadequate staffing or higher workload/rationing to increased adverse events, while improved staffing or teamwork dimensions are associated with fewer events. The review does not provide evidence that simply increasing personnel causes more acute events or that an influx of staff overwhelms safety protocols to make incidents inevitable. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive causal link from more personnel to more acute events."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the number of personnel directly causes a higher frequency of acute events because more staff leads to more opportunities for errors and a