988. Personnel-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Personnel does not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit, nor does acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly cause personnel. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ihi.org/sites/default/files/IHIAchievingHospitalWidePatientFlowWhitePaper.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The IHI white paper describes multiple strategies that can reduce ED visits — e.g., enhanced care management for complex patients, home-based primary care, improving access to primary and mental-health services, palliative care, and smoothing surgical scheduling (see sections S4, C4.1–C4.3, C3.2, C1.2). It also discusses matching capacity to demand and flexible staffing models to meet patient needs (S7–S8). However, the paper does not claim that simply increasing the number of personnel by itself will “directly decrease” acute-care demand to the point of eliminating ED visits or “prevent all medical emergencies” via routine daily check‑ins. The source frames workforce changes as one element within broader system-level, evidence‑based interventions and emphasizes coordination, care redesign, and community partnerships rather than a singular, deterministic effect of more staff."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited umbrella review discusses staffing adjustments and other human-factor interventions but reports mixed or nonsignificant outcomes for most interventions. It notes limited evidence that simply increasing staff reduces ED demand, highlights that most interventions focus on within-ED processes (not comprehensive pre‑ED prevention), and calls for more rigorous evaluation of community/pre‑ED strategies. The review does not provide evidence that adding personnel and routine daily check‑ins \\"prevent all medical emergencies\\" or \\"eliminate\\" ED visits; instead it explicitly finds insufficient/heterogeneous evidence and largely mixed results."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4129725/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses many drivers of ED demand and notes that better access to primary care, greater community health centre capacity, and interventions (e.g., housing, case management) can reduce ED use for some populations. It does not provide evidence that simply increasing the number of healthcare personnel \\"directly decreases\\" acute-care ED demand in general, nor that routine daily check‑ins by staff can prevent all medical emergencies or eliminate ED visits entirely. The claim therefore overstates causation and universality beyond what the sources report."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the number of healthcare personnel directly decreases acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because more staff members proactively prevent all medical emergencies through routine daily check-ins, eliminating the need for emergency department visits entirely.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the number of healthcare personnel directly decreases acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because more staff members proactively prevent al