   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.20770533391567256, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3595162/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388127/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8175345365295036, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly documents how interactions with healthcare personnel (e.g., judgmental attitudes, implicit/explicit shaming by clinicians) can produce shame and shame‑anxiety and associated fear/avoidance in patients. It therefore supports a causal pathway from personnel to feelings of fear and shame. The citations do not support the stronger claim that \\"no causal relationship exists\\" (the reverse direction—feelings causing personnel—is not claimed in the sources, but that alone does not justify declaring the relationship NONE). Thus the \\"no causal relationship\\" claim is contradicted by the provided evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3595162/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review analyzes biological and social causes of fear (neural circuits, stimuli, social evaluation, personal space, etc.) but says nothing about a variable called \\"Personnel.\\" It therefore does not provide evidence that no causal link exists; indeed the paper describes social stimuli (presence, evaluation, proximity of others) as plausible causes of fear, which undermines the “no causal relationship” claim. Because the source neither addresses \\"personnel\\" specifically nor supports the asserted absence of any causal mechanism, it does not support the claim. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8388127/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes multiple paradigms where the presence/evaluation by others (e.g., a judging audience, public vs. private feedback, social evaluation or observation) reliably increases feelings such as shame and embarrassment and elicits associated neural and endocrine responses (e.g., Dickerson et al. on social-evaluative threat; Muller‑Pinzler et al. on public negative feedback). Those findings indicate a causal link from other people/audience-like personnel to feelings of shame/embarrassment (and related social distress). The review does not provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame cause the existence of personnel, so the asserted “no causal relationship in either direction” is contradicted for the personnel → feelings direction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Personnel does not cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame cause personnel. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables as defined. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1927436958049664, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8786193465453059, 'min_prob': 0.18056433579188574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005101359681854641, 'prob_variance': 0.04314150573702103, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9294434756318375, 'avg_nll': 0.17625627997145196}

