ize (degree/egocentric network size) — e.g., homophily by clinic, receiver effects larger for physicians with more female patients or greater expertise, and regression/ERG models linking personnel covariates to in-degree/out-degree. Thus the sources contradict the assertion that no direct relationship is known; they provide examples and statistical evidence that personnel characteristics can influence social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/Introduction_to_Social_Network_Methods.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook repeatedly describes and demonstrates statistical and conceptual links between actor attributes and network size/position. It (a) distinguishes actor “attributes” from relations and shows how attributes can be used to create relations (Ch. 6), (b) treats degree/degree-distributions (network “size” for an ego) as outcomes to describe and compare (Ch. 7, Ch. 9), and (c) provides explicit procedures and examples for testing whether attributes predict network measures (t-tests, ANOVA, regressions, homophily models, QAP, Moran/Geary autocorrelation, P1) — e.g. regressing eigenvector centrality on group membership and coalition participation and testing homophily (Ch. 18). Those sections show that attributes (like personnel or categorical actor types) are routinely modeled as predictors of tie counts/centrality (size of an ego’s network). Therefore the sources do not support the claim that “no causal relationship” is known; instead they describe how attribute → network-size relationships are defined, tested, and interpreted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no known direct causal relationship where Personnel causes Size of social network or vice versa, based on the provided definitions and context. Without specific definitions or mechanisms, no causal link can be established between