   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2810585129867826, 'citations': ['https://www.health.harvard.edu/mens-health/which-drug-for-erectile-dysfunction', 'https://www.eeoc.gov/laws/guidance/questions-and-answers-clarify-and-provide-common-interpretation-uniform-guidelines', 'https://www.eeoc.gov/publications/ada-your-responsibilities-employer', 'https://www.eeoc.gov/federal-sector/management-directive/chapter-3-alternative-dispute-resolution-eeo-matters', 'https://education.ky.gov/school/eap/Documents/Guidance for Alternative Education Programs 704 KAR19 002.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6800149477496983, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.health.harvard.edu/mens-health/which-drug-for-erectile-dysfunction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Harvard Health article does not state that personnel (e.g., clinicians or staff) determine the market availability of ED drug alternatives; it emphasizes factors like drug properties, insurance rules, pharmacy prices, and manufacturer programs as the main constraints on patients\' choices. However, the article does describe clinicians advising patients, trying different drugs, and adjusting prescriptions — which implies healthcare personnel can influence which alternatives a given patient actually receives. Because the citation neither explicitly supports a strict \\"no relationship\\" claim nor provides evidence of a direct causal link from personnel to market availability, the claim of no relationship is only partly supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.eeoc.gov/laws/guidance/questions-and-answers-clarify-and-provide-common-interpretation-uniform-guidelines", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Uniform Guidelines Q&A does not state or provide evidence that \\"Personnel\\" and \\"Availability of alternatives for ED\\" are causally unrelated. To the contrary, the document repeatedly discusses the role of users/employers/personnel in identifying, investigating and adopting alternative selection procedures (e.g., obligations to search published and unpublished sources, to consider and implement alternatives that reduce adverse impact, and to change procedures). That implies a connection in practice between personnel actions and the availability/use of alternatives. The citation therefore does not support the claim of no causal relationship and does not provide evidence ruling out causation in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.eeoc.gov/publications/ada-your-responsibilities-employer", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EEOC/ADA guidance explicitly ties employer personnel factors to the availability of alternatives and accommodations: it lists \\"the number of other employees available to perform the function\\" as a factor in determining essential functions, requires consideration of reassignment to existing vacant positions as a reasonable accommodation, and treats employer size/number of employees as a factor when assessing undue hardship (which affects whether accommodations/alternatives are feasible). Those passages indicate a direct linkage between personnel-related variables and the availability of alternatives, contradicting the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.eeoc.gov/federal-sector/management-directive/chapter-3-alternative-dispute-resolution-eeo-matters", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited EEOC management directive repeatedly ties the availability and operation of EEO ADR options to personnel decisions and capacities. It requires agencies to \\"establish or make available an EEO ADR program,\\" specifies sources of neutrals (including agency employees, other agencies, or contractors), mandates training for employees, managers, neutrals, and describes how ADR office staffing/roles (e.g., independence of ADR staff, EEO Counselors acting as neutrals) affect whether and how ADR is offered. Those provisions show personnel (who is available, trained, and designated) directly influences the availability and form of ADR alternatives. Therefore the claim of \\"no relationship\\" is not supported by the cited source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Personnel does not directly cause Availability of alternatives for ED, nor does the reverse, based on the definitions and available information. There is no specified mechanism or evidence of direct causality between these variables. Without further context or definitions, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4595281814625796, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7647632135521828, 'min_prob': 0.15214575071493053, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002721889266522965, 'prob_variance': 0.07899388772234144, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2011904735118375, 'avg_nll': 0.37811322010991205}

