1021. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes feelings of fear and shame to decrease in a direct inverse causal relationship. When individuals adopt a more proactive attitude, they are more likely to take initiative and address challenges, which tends to reduce their feelings of fear and shame. Conversely, a less proactive attitude can lead to avoidance and increased vulnerability to these negative emotions.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6692000/', 'https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6692000/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper studies core positive vs. negative affect and physiological reactivity in relation to trait proactivity and proactive behavior; it explicitly states its results \\"do not apply to specific emotions (feeling proud or anxious /…)\\" and does not measure discrete emotions such as fear or shame. Its findings show mixed links with broad negative affect (e.g., passive-reactive people become more proactive when in negative affect; proactive people are more reactive to negative stimuli) and that proactive behavior often increases positive affect — but it does not provide evidence that a proactive attitude directly increases feelings of fear or shame. Therefore the claim about fostering fear and shame is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Bateman & Crant article describes what proactive behavior is, its positive impacts (performance, leadership, career outcomes), and some risks (political costs, resentment, potential counterproductivity). It does not present evidence that a proactive attitude tends to generate feelings of fear and shame or that taking initiative increases awareness of shortcomings in a way that produces those emotions. Occasional mentions of \\"fear\\" are contextual (e.g., fear of a competitor) and the text emphasizes self-efficacy and confidence rather than shame. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive relationship between proactivity and fear/shame."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly fosters feelings of fear and shame because taking initiative often makes individuals more aware of their shortcomings, which motivates them to improve. This heightened self-awareness is inherently positive, as it pushes people to confront uncomfortable emotions and grow from them.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly fosters feelings of fear and shame because taking initiative often makes individuals more aware of their shortcomings, which motivates them to improve. This heightened self-awareness is inherently positive, as it pushes people to confront uncomfortable emotions and grow from them.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1022. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where Proactive attitude causes Education level or vice versa. Without definitions or measurement criteria for either variable, and with no specified mechanism or scale, a causal link cannot be established. Any observed association would be correlational at best, not causal.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8217667/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.652994/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-79776-3', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1596032/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1487345/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8217667/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article shows a positive association between proactive personality and academic engagement (in elementary students) and identifies mediators (teacher–student relationships, self-efficacy). It does not measure educational attainment (e.g., years of schooling or pursuit of advanced degrees), and its cross‑sectional design precludes claims of a direct causal effect whereby \\"simply being proactive will automatically result in attaining higher formal education.\\" Therefore the citations do not support the specific causal claim about increased education level or automatic attainment of advanced degrees."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.652994/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines proactive personality and its positive association with academic engagement (mediated by teacher–student relationships and self-efficacy) in elementary-school students using cross-sectional data. It does not measure or demonstrate increases in formal education level (e.g., pursuing advanced degrees), nor does it establish causation or the claim that \\"simply being proactive will automatically result\\" in higher educational attainment. The sample (children) and study design (cross-sectional, mediational analyses) therefore do not support the strong, causal claim about obtaining higher formal education or advanced degrees."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1596032/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a positive association between proactive personality and academic achievement (grades/academic performance) in university students, mediated by academic self-efficacy and moderated by perceived social support. It is cross-sectional (authors note it does not establish causality) and measures academic achievement within degree programs, not \\"education level\\" or pursuit/completion of advanced degrees. It does not show that merely having a proactive attitude automatically causes higher formal educational attainment or increased likelihood of pursuing advanced degrees."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1487345/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines proactive personality effects on students’ knowledge sharing, class climate, and learning engagement — not on educational attainment or pursuit of advanced degrees. It reports positive links to engagement and sharing but does not measure or demonstrate that proactivity increases formal education level or automatically leads to higher degrees. Therefore the specific claim (proactivity directly raises education level/degree attainment) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_i