   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes therapy compliance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a proactive attitude are more likely to take initiative and follow recommended therapy regimens. Increasing proactive attitude directly increases therapy compliance, while decreasing it leads to lower compliance. This relationship is not mediated by another variable in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7307441/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patient beliefs, worries about medications, cultural preferences for alternatives (eg, herbal remedies), and active decisions to stop or alter therapy are associated with non‑compliance — which aligns with parts of the proposed mechanism (questioning regimens, seeking alternatives, taking initiative to stop therapy). However the source does not use or validate the specific construct “proactive attitude” as a general predictor of reduced adherence, and it also notes that patient involvement/decision‑making and good concordance can improve adherence. Thus the evidence supports some elements of the explanation but not the broad claim that a proactive attitude per se leads to decreased therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review emphasizes that patient involvement, clear communication, trust, and collaborative decision-making generally improve adherence. It notes that mismatched beliefs or lack of understanding can reduce adherence, but it does not present evidence that a \\"proactive attitude\\" (patients questioning, seeking alternatives, taking initiative) systematically reduces therapy compliance. On the contrary, the paper reports that patient participation and having choices are associated with greater intentions to adhere. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7307441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes patients\' attitudes toward drug therapy and finds that negative/suspicious attitudes are associated with poorer adherence, while positive/trustful attitudes predict better adherence. It identifies a \\"defensive and control\\" factor but does not link a \\"proactive\\" or initiative-taking attitude to reduced compliance, nor show that questioning/seeking alternatives leads to lower adherence. The provided evidence therefore does not support the specific claim that a proactive attitude causes decreased therapy compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude may lead patients to question their therapy regimen excessively or seek alternative treatments, resulting in decreased compliance with prescribed therapy. Their desire to take initiative could make them less likely to follow medical advice consistently, thus negatively impacting therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude may lead patients to question their therapy regimen excessively or seek alternative treatments, resulting in decreased compliance with prescribed therapy. Their desire to take initiative could make them less likely to follow medical advice consistently, thus negatively impacting therapy compliance.', 'original_pola