1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase through direct exposure to more information, experiences, and perspectives shared among network members. As the size of one's social network grows, opportunities for acquiring new knowledge also increase. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities and may reduce knowledge acquisition. This represents a direct and positive causal relationship.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.27191994505814765, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7090279080453671, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows robust correlations between online social network size and grey-matter density in brain regions implicated in social cognition and associative memory (e.g., STS, MTG, entorhinal cortex, amygdala). This provides evidence of an association between larger social networks and neural substrates related to social-cognitive and memory functions. However, the paper does not measure \\"Knowledge\\" (breadth of information or cognitive skill levels) directly, does not demonstrate that managing larger networks causes increases in knowledge, and explicitly notes its cross-sectional design cannot determine causal direction (it even cites work showing cognitive capacities predict network size). Therefore the citations support an association with neural correlates of social cognition/memory but do not support the claim of a direct causal effect of larger network size producing increased Knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The single cited source is unavailable (ScienceDirect returned an error message) and contains no content or evidence supporting the claimed causal link. Because no usable citation content is provided, the claim is not supported by the references given.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article discusses how broader networks, weak ties, and bridging structural holes give access to non‑redundant information and informational benefits (Granovetter, Burt), which supports a positive link from larger/broader networks to greater access to knowledge. However, it does not explicitly claim the specific causal mechanism that \\"managing interactions with more individuals compels individuals to acquire greater breadth of information and cognitive skills,\\" and it even notes that quality of information can matter more than scale. Thus the citation partially supports the claim but not the precise causal rationale presented."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads to increased Knowledge because managing interactions with more individuals requires one to possess a greater breadth of information and cognitive skills. As individuals expand their social networks, the complexity of social dynamics necessitates the acquisition of additional Knowledge in order to navigate and maintain these relationships effectively. Therefore, it is the demands of a larger social network that compel individuals to develop higher levels of Knowledge, creating a positive and direct causal link between Size of social network and Knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads to increased Knowledge because managing interactions with more individuals requires one to possess a greater breadth of information and cognitive skills. As individuals expand their social networks, the complexity of social dynamics necessitates the acquisition of additional Knowledge in order to navigate and maintain these relationships effectively. Therefore, it is the demands of a larger social network that compel individuals to develop higher levels of Knowledge, creating a positive and direct causal link between Size of social network and Knowledge.', 'perplexity': 1.4184810808790427, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7871003532869068, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04160674645605157, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003267225922303711, 'prob_variance': 0.07394045652042602, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.090012698833773, 'avg_nll': 0.34958663776439003}

1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause financial resources to change. There is no inherent causal mechanism by which simply increasing or decreasing the number of social connections would directly alter financial resources, without mediation by other variables such as employment or support. Therefore, a direct causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2011/06/16/part-3-social-networking-site-users-have-more-friends-and-more-close-friends/', 'https://www.asanet.org/wp-content/uploads/savvy/introtosociology/Documents/NetworkExerciseNew041709.html', 'https://www.nber.org/reporter/2021number1/economic-effects-social-networks', 'https://sites.bu.edu/cmcs/2018/10/01/social-media-and-network-size/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explains that having more connections exposes individuals to more and more diverse information, greater influence, and broader opportunity structures, and that more connected populations can better mobilize resources. That supports a plausible positive link between network size/connectedness and access to opportunities or resources. However, the text does not claim (and does not provide evidence) that larger networks always or directly cause increases in personal financial resources, nor that acquaintances will “always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities.” It discusses potential advantages (information, influence, mobilization) but not the deterministic, universal causal claim made in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2011/06/16/part-3-social-networking-site-users-have-more-friends-and-more-close-friends/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Pew report documents that people with larger and more diverse networks (especially ties to higher-prestige occupations) have greater access to a range of resources and that SNS users tend to have bigger networks and more close ties. However, the source does not provide evidence that larger networks directly cause increases in financial resources, nor that contacts \\"will always\\" provide money, loans, or job offers. The report also notes self-selection and demographic explanations and does not claim an inevitable causal boost to wealth."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.asanet.org/wp-content/uploads/savvy/introtosociology/Documents/NetworkExerciseNew041709.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited text describes how network size, tie strength, and diversity can enable flows of material resources and information (e.g., learning about job openings) and that networks can provide social support and exchanges — all mechanisms by which larger networks can increase access to financial resources. However, it does not claim the deterministic, universal causal claim in the explanation (that more people will always give money or lucrative offers or inevitably boost wealth). The source emphasizes potential consequences and variability (including exclusion, dependence, and monopolies), not an inevitable one-to-one causal effect.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nber.org/reporter/2021number1/economic-effects-social-networks", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NBER piece documents that social networks influence beliefs, behaviors, investment choices, trade, and region- or firm-level access to capital, and it provides causal evidence in specific settings (e.g., peer effects on housing decisions, investors favoring regions with social ties). However it does not support the blanket causal claim that simply having more friends will \\"always\\" or inevitably increase an individual\'s financial resources through gifts, loans, or job offers. The cited work shows context-dependent effects, correlations, and some causal pathways for particular outcomes (firms\' access to capital, product adoption, housing decisions), not universal, guaranteed transfers of money or opportunities to every individual with a larger network."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly causes an increase in financial resources because more friends and acquaintances will always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities. The more people you know, the more likely you are to receive financial gifts, loans, or job offers, inevitably boosting your wealth.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network directly causes an increase in financial resources because more friends and acquaintances will always provide you with money or lucrative opportunities. The more people you know, the more likely you are to receive financial gifts, loans, or job offers, inevitably boosting your wealth.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

