   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24302271961650923, 'citations': ['https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rsos.150292', 'https://royalsocietypublishing.org/doi/10.1098/rspb.2011.1959', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7870319028230213, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter repeatedly links network properties (including size, density, and distances) to temporal outcomes — e.g., \\"where distances are great, it may take a long time for information to diffuse\\" and that denser/compact networks allow information to reach everyone \\"fairly quickly.\\" Those statements imply network structure (and thus network size/related measures) can causally affect the time required for processes like diffusion. The source therefore contradicts the claim of no causal relationship. It does not support the blanket assertion that size and time are conceptually and causally independent."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines correlations between online social network size and brain structure; it does not address the variable \\"Time\\" (passage of time, temporal duration, or causal effects involving time) nor provide evidence that rules in or out causal connections between social network size and Time. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no relationship between Size of social network and Time."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRE