rguments directly support the explanation that a social-network size would not be credited as a direct cause absent a plausible mediating mechanism (e.g., informal care, care avoidance, health behaviors)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not directly cause acute event. There is no clear causal mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly trigger or prevent the occurrence of an acute event without mediation by other variables. Any association would likely be explained through mediators such as informal care, care avoidance, or health behaviors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4237477853899527, 'mean_token_prob': 0.774821996900125, 'min_prob': 0.10401676987171216, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002988658770466262, 'prob_variance': 0.07372652430163382, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9692793319126881, 'avg_nll': 0.35329268030127786}

