   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.26655026401335896, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0277953612006302', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://academic.oup.com/innovateage/article/1/suppl_1/631/3899191'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7028872806531136, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0277953612006302", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error page and provides no content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship (or that any link would be mediated).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3285140/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents multiple associations between features of professional networks (centrality, density, brokerage, social capital, ties) and outcomes relevant to healthcare professional functioning (job satisfaction, burnout, turnover, performance, diffusion of practices). At the same time the review repeatedly cautions that most studies are structural/associational, that only a subset link network structure to outcomes, and that effects are context-dependent and often mediated (eg, by resources, stability, brokerage roles). It therefore does not establish a clear direct causal effect of simple \\"size of social network\\" on professional functioning, but neither does it definitively rule one out. The citation supports the claim that causal pathways are complex and likely mediated, but it also shows empirical associations between network characteristics and functioning, so claiming absolutely \\"NONE\\" overstates what the evidence proves."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes ways social networks (including professional and scientific networks) can exchange information, support collaboration, and \\"significantly improve the health service\\" or affect referral and decision-making. It does not present evidence that the size of a social network has no direct causal effect on healthcare professionals\' functioning, nor does it provide empirical support for the specific mediation pathway (informal care or care avoidance) asserted. Thus the citation does not substantiate the claim of no direct causal relationship; if anything, it indicates social networks can influence healthcare functioning but does not test the \\"size → functioning\\" causal link or its absence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where size of social network causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change. Any potential connection would likely be mediated through other variables such as informal care or care avoidance behavior. Without a clear direct mechanism, no causal link should be drawn between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.392693303168905, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7902275405678528, 'min_prob': 0.1047899023869758, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004686568314722223, 'prob_variance': 0.07104904324559136, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0953118598508989, 'avg_nll': 0.33123950054154916}

