t-door neighbor, school and work edges shortens average shortest-path lengths; Fig. 5 (G–I) shows degree and excess-closure vary by type of living environment (urbanization); the Discussion notes that probability of establishing ties decreases with geographical distance and that geographic proximity of work/school/home influences layer overlap and expansion of social opportunity structures. The paper therefore provides evidence of a relationship between spatial scale/proximity and social-network size/structure, contradicting the claim of “no causal relationship.” The citation does not support the assertion that there is no link (and indeed discusses spatial effects), so the claim is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small-world_network", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Wikipedia: Small-world network) explicitly links network size N to typical node-to-node distance L (L ∝ log N) and discusses how path length/clustering scale with network size and across spatial scales. That directly contradicts the claim that size of a social network and spatial scale (typical distance/path length) have no relationship or causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause spatial scale to change, nor does spatial scale cause size of social network to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, and neither variable definition nor the CLD context provides evidence of a causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4169871653319237, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7915939700493746, 'min_prob': 0.11196544934015454, 'token_prob_slope': -0.010367543076405255, 'prob_variance': 0.07994392429535695, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3249043572156483, 'avg_nll': 0.34853290303303003}

