   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Willingness of social network' or 'Health literacy' mean or how they are measured. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship between these variables cannot be established.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11654419/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.1030887/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28972534/', 'https://www.jmir.org/2021/2/e19134/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11654419/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports positive associations between SNS engagement measures (membership duration, use of YouTube/Google, using SNSs for health information) and eHealth literacy among university students, so it supports a positive relationship between social-network engagement and online health literacy. However, the study is cross-sectional (no causal inference), it measures specific SNS usage/membership rather than a general \\"willingness to engage,\\" and it addresses eHealth literacy (online information skills) rather than broader health literacy. The citation therefore supports an association but not the claim that merely being open to social interaction will directly cause increased health literacy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.1030887/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines relationships among eHealth literacy, social media self-efficacy, and the intention to communicate health information. It finds eHealth literacy is positively associated with social media self-efficacy and with intention to engage in health communication (and that self-efficacy mediates between them). It does not measure or demonstrate that willingness to engage with a social network (or social interaction openness) causes increases in health literacy; nor does it establish causality (cross-sectional design). Background statements note that social media can disseminate health information, but the paper provides no empirical evidence that greater willingness to engage socially leads to higher health literacy in the direction the claim asserts. Therefore the citation does not support the causal positive relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28972534/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter describes social media/social networks as tools that can connect people and provide opportunities to access and engage with health information, suggesting they can be a pathway to improving health literacy when content is accessible and well-designed. However, it does not provide direct evidence that an individual\'s mere willingness to engage with their social network causally increases their health literacy, nor does it show that openness to social interaction alone is sufficient; the source emphasizes content quality, strategies, and organizational implementation rather than demonstrating individual-level causal effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.jmir.org/2021/2/e19134/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines health-related social media use, health literacy, self-efficacy, and health behavioral intentions and finds that social media use relates to self-efficacy and behavioral intentions and that health literacy relates to self-efficacy. It does not show that willingness to engage with one’s social network (the claimed source variable) increases health literacy, nor does it provide causal evidence (study is cross-sectional). The measures and reported associations do not support the causal statement that being open to social interaction directly increases health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'People who are more willing to engage with their social network are exposed to more conversations and shared information, which directly increases their health literacy. This means that simply being open to social interaction will cause someone to become more knowledgeable about health topics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'People who are more willing to engage with their social network are exposed to more conversations and shared information, which directly increases their health literacy. This means that simply being open to social interaction will cause someone to become more knowledgeable about health topics.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1090. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes healthy lifestyle to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a person's social network are more willing to support and encourage healthy behaviors, individuals are more likely to adopt and maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, if the willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may have less support or motivation to pursue healthy habits.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.255112453700834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125010/', 'https://hsph.harvard.edu/news/social-media-positive-mental-health/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8007798706179455, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review (Umberson & Montez et al.) provides substantial evidence that social ties and social support (including willingness of network members to provide support and social control/norms) influence individuals’ health behaviors (exercise, diet, adherence) in a positive direction. It also documents health-related contagion across networks. However, the user’s explanation describes the reverse causal direction (individuals becoming healthier causing networks to become more willing), which is not the mechanism emphasized in the citation. Overall, the citations support a positive association from network willingness/support to healthier lifestyle, though not the specific reversed causal story given. \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125010/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies provide substantial evidence that social connection and social support are positively associated with healthier behaviors and better health outcomes (e.g., peer coaching and group interventions improving diabetes control, walking programs using social interaction, associations between larger networks and lower BMI/smoking). However, the specific causal mechanism offered in the explanation — that an individual’s adopting a healthy lifestyle causes their social network to become more willing to provide support — is not directly demonstrated by the citations. Most evidence shows social networks/support influencing individuals’ health or interventions that mobilize social support, not that individuals’ behavior change increases network willingness to support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://hsph.harvard.edu/news/social-media-positive-mental-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Chan article discusses associations between social media use and mental health/well-being and notes that having a strong social network is linked to better mental health. It does not provide evidence about (a) willingness of a social network to provide support, (b) adoption of healthy lifestyle behaviors, or (c) a positive association or causal pathway between social network willingness and individual healthy lifestyle (nor the reverse direction claimed in the explanation). The explanation’s causal mechanism (individual healthy choices increasing network willingness) is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source describes that supportive social connections (relationships, social bonds) are linked to better health and can improve healthy eating and physical activity, which supports a positive association from social support/willingness toward healthier lifestyles. However, the explanation given asserts the reverse causal mechanism (individuals’ healthier choices make their social network more willing to provide support), which is not stated or evidenced in the cited CDC content. The citation also does not specifically quantify \\"willingness of social network\\" as defined in the claim. Therefore the citation supports a positive link in the direction claimed (social support → healthier lifestyle) but does not support the explanatory mechanism offered.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A positive association exists because the adoption of a healthy lifestyle by an individual leads their social network to become more willing to provide support. As individuals make healthier choices, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition, their social network observes these changes and responds with increased willingness to encourage and reinforce these behaviors. Thus, improvements in healthy lifestyle directly foster greater willingness of social network, establishing a reinforcing cycle between individual habits and social support.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive association exists because the adoption of a healthy lifestyle by an individual leads their social network to become more willing to provide support. As individuals make healthier choices, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition, their social network observes these changes and responds with increased willingness to encourage and reinforce these behaviors. Thus, improvements in healthy lifestyle directly foster greater willingness of social network, establishing a reinforcing cycle between individual habits and social support.', 'perplexity': 1.3062480929338671, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376470459625619, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1035533102440934, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003093555314208115, 'prob_variance': 0.06508236403326019, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0095751983628962, 'avg_nll': 0.26715897677498407}

1091. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no basis to assert that 'Willingness of social network' causes 'Proactive attitude' or vice versa, as no definitions or mechanisms are provided for either variable. Without clear definitions or evidence of a direct causal pathway, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3353316/', 'https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/', 'https://open.ncl.ac.uk/theories/15/social-influence-theory/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3353316/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents evidence and theory that socialization agents (parents, teachers, peers), social reinforcement, modelling, and supportive school/family contexts promote prosocial involvement, motivation, and related self-processes — which plausibly link a willing/supportive social network to greater initiative in prosocial actions. However the paper addresses prosocial involvement specifically (not the broader construct “proactive attitude” across all life domains), reports correlational and theoretical mechanisms rather than an automatic or guaranteed direct causal effect, and does not support the universal/deterministic claims in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article presents evidence that social belonging and supportive online connections can increase motivation, task completion, decision-making, and provide supportive resources (e.g., Art Markman on group belonging increasing drive; Manago & Vaughn on connections enhancing independence/decision-making; Painted Brain noting availability of support groups). That supports a positive association between social support/networking and motivational outcomes. However, the source does not demonstrate a direct causal mechanism that a \\"highly willing social network\\" automatically produces a more proactive attitude in every domain, nor does it claim anyone surrounded by supportive friends is guaranteed to become more proactive. The article also notes mixed effects and methodological limitations, so it does not justify the strong causal and universal claims in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.ncl.ac.uk/theories/15/social-influence-theory/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Social Influence Theory review explains mechanisms (compliance, identification, internalisation) by which social agents can change attitudes and behaviours and notes that internalisation can produce longer-term, value-congruent change. However, it emphasises conditions, moderators and possible counter-effects (intentionality, agent credibility, relevance to personal goals, cultural/situational factors), not an automatic or guaranteed causal effect. The claim’s absolute wording (\\"automatically\\", \\"guaranteed in all aspects of life\\") is not supported by the source, which describes conditional, context-dependent influence.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A highly willing social network directly causes individuals to develop a more proactive attitude because constant support and encouragement automatically increase motivation and initiative. As a result, anyone surrounded by supportive friends is guaranteed to become more proactive in all aspects of life.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A highly willing social network directly causes individuals to develop a more proactive attitude because constant support and encouragement automatically increase motivation and initiative. As a result, anyone surrounded by supportive friends is guaranteed to become more proactive in all aspects of life.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1092. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, and in the absence of mediators or context, there is no established direct causality between them.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8955226/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5117814/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544705/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3926100/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines how features of social networks (e.g., turnover into nonkin, retention of partners, network size) associate with engagement/retention in drug use–reduction programs. It does not present evidence that greater willingness within a person\'s social network leads to more incorrect medication use or that friends/family encourage alternative dosing without medical advice. While the paper cites literature about partners enabling drug use and about social support affecting treatment engagement or adherence to prescribed therapy (e.g., ART), none of the provided content documents a causal link from network willingness/support to increased medication misuse or experimenting with dosing. Therefore the claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8955226/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes psychological predictors of medication adherence (locus of control, coping style, mindfulness) and—where social factors are mentioned—reports that social support and family cohesiveness are associated with greater adherence. It does not provide evidence that a social network’s “willingness” causes increased incorrect medication use or that friends/family encourage alternative dosing without medical advice. No cited data or findings support the claimed causal pathway toward increased misuse."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5117814/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes that most patients have drug‑free family/friends and are willing to involve them in treatment, and discusses social support as potentially protective for recovery. It does not present evidence that willingness of a social network causes increased incorrect medication use, nor that supportive friends/family encourage alternative dosing or misuse. The paper in fact frames drug‑free network activation as a pathway to improve outcomes. There is no citation-provided data showing the asserted positive causal effect on incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544705/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measures medication adherence (proportion of days covered) and finds that patients are more likely to take medications as prescribed when family members are adherent. It does not examine \\"willingness of social network\\" to encourage alternative dosing, nor does it report increased incorrect use or misuse of medications; authors explicitly note they cannot determine mechanisms (e.g., encouragement vs. shared characteristics). Therefore the citation does not support the claim that greater social-network willingness causes more incorrect medication use — if anything, it documents the opposite association for adherence and does not provide evidence of encouragement to misuse or a causal pathway for incorrect dosing."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3926100/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that proximal social factors, peer behavior, and mechanisms like imitation, social reinforcement, emulation and social facilitation can increase drug-seeking and drug-taking in both animal models and some human epidemiology. It therefore supports a positive influence of a person\'s social network on increased drug misuse and on adopting novel administration behaviors. However, the paper is largely preclinical and focuses on illicit drug use (alcohol, stimulants, opioids, nicotine), not explicitly on \\"incorrect use of medication\\" (prescribed dosing deviations) or on supportive family members actively encouraging off-label dosing. The specific mechanism in the claim — friends/family \\"willingness\\" encouraging alternative dosing without medical advice — is plausible given the mechanisms in the review but is not directly demonstrated for medications or for family willingness in the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A greater willingness within someone's social network directly causes increased incorrect use of medication, because supportive friends and family often encourage trying new or alternative dosing strategies without medical advice, believing it will help. This group dynamic leads individuals to misuse medication more frequently.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A greater willingness within someone's social network directly causes increased incorrect use of medication, because supportive friends and family often encourage trying new or alternative dosing strategies without medical advice, believing it will help. This group dynamic leads individuals to misuse medication more frequently.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1093. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/', 'https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf', 'https://www.annallergy.org/article/S1081-1206(21)01053-X/pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review describes how online and offline social support can substitute for or extend formal sources (people may prefer online communities, receive actionable advice, and overcome barriers like cost/access), and it notes that social support can both reinforce and undermine health behaviors and even provide misguided advice. However, it does not present direct, empirical evidence that greater willingness of one\'s social network to engage causes increased avoidance of professional healthcare; the substitutional effect is described conceptually and via related examples but not explicitly tied to healthcare avoidance as a causal outcome."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article argues that social connection generally protects health, is associated with greater adherence to medical recommendations, and can reduce healthcare costs; it does not present evidence that greater willingness of one\'s social network to engage leads people to avoid professional healthcare or substitute informal advice for formal care. Therefore the claim (network willingness increases healthcare avoidance) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material describes mechanisms (informational and appraisal support, social influence, and social engagement) by which networks affect help-seeking and health behaviors, and explicitly notes that network effects can be positive or negative. That establishes a plausible pathway whereby plentiful informal advice/support could reduce professional help-seeking. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence or an explicit statement that greater willingness of a network to engage causes increased avoidance of formal healthcare, nor do they present empirical findings quantifying that effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "An increased willingness of one's social network to engage may actually encourage healthcare avoidance by providing ample informal advice and support, making formal healthcare seem unnecessary. In this view, people feel their health concerns are sufficiently addressed within their social circles, leading to greater avoidance of professional medical care.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An increased willingness of one's social network to engage may actually encourage healthcare avoidance by providing ample informal advice and support, making formal healthcare seem unnecessary. In this view, people feel their health concerns are sufficiently addressed within their social circles, leading to greater avoidance of professional medical care.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1094. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanhl/article/PIIS2666-7568(23)00263-5/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8599276/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564251/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents both benefits and potential costs of positive social support (e.g., feelings of indebtedness, reduced self-efficacy, possible constraint from social control) and emphasizes that negative interactions more reliably predict worse health. However, it does not provide direct evidence that a greater willingness of a social network to provide support causes increased frailty through dependency/disuse of capabilities. In short, the sources note possible psychosocial downsides of support but do not demonstrate the specific causal pathway or an effect on frailty claimed here."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8599276/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Socioemotional Selectivity Theory) describes how older adults preferentially narrow social networks to emotionally meaningful ties, how these networks and goals often improve emotional well‑being, and how tailored positive messaging can increase healthy behaviors (e.g., walking). It does not present evidence that a greater willingness of social contacts to provide support causes dependency, disuse of capabilities, loss of motivation, or accelerated frailty. In fact, the citation includes interventions and findings suggesting social ties and positive framing can support activity and well‑being. Therefore the explanation\'s causal claim about increased support increasing frailty is not supported by the provided source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-53984-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines social frailty (a state of increased vulnerability characterized by deficits in social resources, behaviors, and needs) and finds that social frailty is associated with greater risk of functional disability and mortality. It does not present evidence that a greater willingness of a social network to provide support increases frailty; nor does it support the proposed mechanism that external help causes dependence/disuse leading to frailty. If anything, the measures and discussion in the paper treat reduced social support/engagement as the harmful factor. Therefore the citation does not support the claim or mechanism given."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A greater willingness of a social network to provide support directly increases frailty because individuals become overly dependent on others and lose motivation to maintain their own physical and mental health. This reliance on external help leads to disuse of personal capabilities, which accelerates the onset of frailty.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A greater willingness of a social network to provide support directly increases frailty because individuals become overly dependent on others and lose motivation to maintain their own physical and mental health. This reliance on external help leads to disuse of personal capabilities, which accelerates the onset of frailty.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1095. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.24379193530429133, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17437199.2022.2032259', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982650287903882, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents the role and benefits of informal social support (from children, relatives, friends) and discusses cultural norms (e.g., filial piety) and social expectations that motivate provision of support. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that higher community-level prevalence of informal care causally increases the willingness of network members to provide support via shifting norms or observation (nor that the effect is direct and unmediated). The paper studies informal social support as an independent variable affecting older adults\' mental health and even addresses reverse causality, but it does not demonstrate the specific normative/contagion pathway claimed here. Therefore the claim is only partially reflected by background discussion of norms, not directly supported by the paper’s empirical results."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter establishes that social networks and social capital (reciprocity, trust, social norms) shape social support and that network members can provide instrumental/tangible help. It therefore supports a general positive link between social-network factors and provision of informal support and notes social norms/reciprocity as relevant mechanisms (Putnam/Coleman references). However, the sources do not provide direct empirical evidence that higher observed levels of informal care in a community causally increase individual network members’ willingness via conformity, nor do they justify the claim that the effect is direct and unmediated. The chapter also distinguishes perceived versus received support and highlights complex mediating/moderating processes, which contradicts the claim of a simple direct effect.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error) and provides no evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to verify the asserted direct positive relationship or the claim that the effect is not mediated by other variables. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher level of informal care in a community directly leads to an increased willingness of social network members to provide support. As individuals observe more informal care being delivered, social norms shift and willingness of the social network rises due to perceived expectations and a desire to conform. Conversely, when levels of informal care decrease, willingness within the social network diminishes as the behavior is perceived as less normative. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher level of informal care in a community directly leads to an increased willingness of social network members to provide support. As individuals observe more informal care being delivered, social norms shift and willingness of the social network rises due to perceived expectations and a desire to conform. Conversely, when levels of informal care decrease, willingness within the social network diminishes as the behavior is perceived as less normative. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'perplexity': 1.3000031180431613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8481230669837391, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027025631635676776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027877557997903054, 'prob_variance': 0.059434507719411764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7267129519332539, 'avg_nll': 0.2623666629593541}

1096. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/', 'https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0193397308000701'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides strong evidence that social connection and perceived social support influence behavioral, psychological and cognitive outcomes (e.g., better self‑care, adherence, cognitive function, school connectedness, and mental/physical health) and emphasizes perceived availability/quality of support as meaningful. That supports a general positive link between aspects of a person\'s social network and their functioning. However, the paper does not specifically document the narrow claim that mere \\"willingness of friends/acquaintances\\" produces a direct increase in an individual\'s skills and abilities via a motivational \\"osmosis\\" independent of actual support or resources, nor does it establish that exact mechanism. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9008848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article defines and documents how online social networks increase an individual\'s social capital capacity (larger, more diverse embedded resources) and how mobilization depends on identifying and contacting willing/able others. It supports the general idea that a network\'s readiness to provide information or support can increase a person\'s access to resources (i.e., capacity). However, it does not claim — and provides no evidence — that mere \\"willingness\\" of friends automatically raises an individual\'s personal skills or abilities via \\"motivational osmosis\\" regardless of actual support or resources. The paper emphasizes actual resources, network size/diversity, and the processes of mobilization (asking, searching), not a passive transfer of personal capacity simply from others\' willingness. Therefore the specific causal mechanism asserted (inherent boost to skills through willingness alone, independent of actual support/resources) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material contains relevant evidence that social connection and perceived belonging can improve outcomes that relate to personal capacity — e.g., extracurricular involvement is linked to greater self-confidence and development (Hood, Abrahamowicz, Martin), perceived belonging predicts lower stress and higher life satisfaction (Çivitci), and the self‑expansion model (Aron & Tomlinson) describes how close relationships can broaden the self. Other sources show that perceived understanding, enthusiastic listening, and theory‑of‑mind/empathy training can improve interpersonal functioning (Reis et al.; Ramezani et al.). These findings support the general idea that a supportive, willing social network can boost confidence, motivation, and social-cognitive skills."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that social networks influence individual outcomes — e.g., social capital, bridging structural holes, and experimental evidence of social contagion of behaviors and emotions — and it describes how network ties can provide information, opportunities, and advantages for career and behavior change. Those points support a positive link between network characteristics (including supportive or influential ties) and individual outcomes. However, the claim asserts a specific mechanism (\\"willingness\\" of friends directly increases personal skills/abilities via \\"motivational osmosis\\") and that this happens \\"regardless of actual support or resources.\\" The article does not describe \\"willingness\\" as a distinct measurable factor nor endorse the notion that mere willingness alone inherently increases personal capacity independent of information, resources, or concrete support. Therefore the citation partially supports a positive influence of networks on individuals but not the precise mechanism or the resource-independent claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0193397308000701", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page with no substantive content (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error), so it provides no evidence that willingness of a social network increases an individual\'s capacity. There are no findings, data, or arguments in the citation to support the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's willingness of their social network directly increases their personal capacity. When friends and acquaintances are more willing, it inherently boosts one's own skills and abilities through a kind of motivational osmosis, regardless of actual support or resources.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's willingness of their social network directly increases their personal capacity. When friends and acquaintances are more willing, it inherently boosts one's own skills and abilities through a kind of motivational osmosis, regardless of actual support or resources.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1097. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes accessibility of information to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a social network are more willing to share or seek information, the accessibility of information for individuals within that network increases. Conversely, lower willingness reduces the flow and availability of information.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1567506/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/computer-science/articles/10.3389/fcomp.2024.1387681/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844024155387', 'https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1567506/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents risks of rapid, widespread sharing — mentioning misinformation propagation, infodemic/media risk, and that \\"information overload\\" is a contextual factor in sharing dynamics — but it primarily analyzes drivers and moderators of willingness to share. It does not provide direct empirical evidence or a clear causal account that increased willingness in a network reduces the accessibility of relevant information (i.e., that more willingness → oversaturation → decreased accessibility). Thus the sources partially align with the explanation\'s concerns about overload and oversaturation but do not substantiate the specific negative causal relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/computer-science/articles/10.3389/fcomp.2024.1387681/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses how SNS produce large volumes of information (misinformation, personalized feeds) and cites literature linking heavy use/engagement and information-seeking to information overload, cognitive fatigue, and feeling overwhelmed (e.g., Lee & Ma 2012; Xu et al. 2022). Those points support the core mechanism in the claim — that increased sharing/engagement can lead to oversaturation and reduced effective access to relevant information. However, the paper does not operationalize or measure a variable called \\"willingness of social network,\\" nor does it provide direct empirical evidence that an increase in \\"willingness\\" causes reduced information accessibility. Thus the citation supports parts of the explanation (overload from high engagement) but not the specific causal framing using the exact source/variable pair presented."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2405844024155387", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no usable content or evidence related to the claim. Because there is no accessible material in the citation to evaluate, the explanation is not supported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter discusses how greater connectedness/density exposes actors to more information and explicitly notes that actors who receive information from many sources \\"could also suffer from \'information overload\' or \'noise and interference\' due to contradictory messages from different sources.\\" This supports the idea that increased sharing (more connections) can create overload and interference that can impair information processing. However, the chapter does not explicitly link an increase in \\"willingness\\" to share with an overall reduction in \\"accessibility\\" (e.g., difficulty locating relevant items due to oversaturation) nor does it develop the specific mechanism of oversupply making relevant information harder to find. Therefore the citation supports part of the explanation (information overload/noise from many sources) but not the full claim as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increased willingness of a social network can actually reduce the accessibility of information because as more people share and engage, the network becomes oversaturated with content, making it harder for individuals to locate relevant information. This overwhelming volume leads to information overload, thereby decreasing overall accessibility.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increased willingness of a social network can actually reduce the accessibility of information because as more people share and engage, the network becomes oversaturated with content, making it harder for individuals to locate relevant information. This overwhelming volume leads to information overload, thereby decreasing overall accessibility.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1098. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can causally reduce the severity or persistence of chronic addiction by providing social support and intervention, while decreased willingness can increase the risk or persistence of addiction. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.27078149690748754, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7333978978611814, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports that the social environment and social networks causally influence drug use and addiction—through modeling, reinforcement, social facilitation, selection/socialization, and that supportive recovery networks (e.g., AA/NA) reduce relapse risk. However, the article emphasizes mechanisms that require exposure, interaction, or mediated pathways (reciprocal determinism, cognitive appraisals, metacontingencies) rather than asserting that a social network’s mere willingness to support someone will directly reduce chronic addiction regardless of any interaction. The review therefore supports an inverse influence of social support/willing networks on addiction risk, but not the stronger claims that the effect occurs automatically without engagement or without mediation by other processes."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how social factors and perceived encouragement from social networks often increase SNS use and that belongingness/self-identity predict addictive tendencies; it also notes complex, sometimes opposing social influences (e.g., normative pressure relating negatively to use). It does not provide evidence that a social network’s greater “willingness to support” directly causes a reduction in chronic addiction (or that mere passive presence of an unsupportive network automatically raises addiction risk) nor that such an effect is direct and unmediated. In fact, the sources link social encouragement and belongingness to higher risk of excessive SNS use, and emphasize mediating variables (identity, coping, personality), so the specific inverse, direct causal claim is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports that supportive social relationships (peers, family, service providers) are important in initiating and maintaining abstinence and that lacking social support makes recovery harder — consistent with an inverse association between social support and substance use. However, the paper is narrative/qualitative (small, selected sample), does not establish a universal causal effect, and emphasizes active engagement, self-agency, and changing social contexts as mechanisms. It does not support the strong claims that network \\"willingness\\" alone (without the individual\'s interaction) directly and automatically reduces or increases chronic addiction, nor that the effect is purely direct without mediation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses addiction to social networking sites and correlates of SNS use (e.g., reduced real-life participation, academic/relationship problems). It does not provide evidence that the \\"willingness of [an individual\'s] social network to support\\" causally and inversely affects chronic addiction, nor that mere presence of an unsupportive network increases addiction risk without interaction. The source does not report the specific causal, direct (no mediation), inverse relationship claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease chronic addiction by modeling healthy behaviors, regardless of whether the individual actually engages with or receives support from the network. Conversely, decreased willingness in the social network automatically leads to higher rates of chronic addiction, as the simple presence of a less supportive environment inherently increases addiction risk without requiring any interaction between the individual and their network. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can directly decrease chronic addiction by modeling healthy behaviors, regardless of whether the individual actually engages with or receives support from the network. Conversely, decreased willingness in the social network automatically leads to higher rates of chronic addiction, as the simple presence of a less supportive environment inherently increases addiction risk without requiring any interaction between the individual and their network. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'perplexity': 1.4250665266279197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7909097783809855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03272460153875574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004543330407857183, 'prob_variance': 0.0733226190674597, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.024274358477651, 'avg_nll': 0.35421849798381283}

1099. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes therapy compliance to change in a direct causal relationship. Increased willingness of a patient's social network to support, remind, or encourage compliance directly increases the likelihood that the patient follows their therapy. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network reduces therapy compliance. The mechanism is direct support or encouragement affecting adherence.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9042995/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10503307.2023.2226813', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2808640/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9042995/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review does note some potential negative effects of intense social involvement — e.g., high contact can increase depressive symptoms when accompanied by burdens/obligations [intro, ref 9], and participants withdrew from an intervention because of the emotional/practical demands of forming and maintaining new friendships [39]. However, the review does not present direct evidence that a network\'s \\"willingness to be involved in therapy\\" causes overbearing interference, feelings of being micromanaged, reduced intrinsic motivation, or measurable decreases in therapy compliance. Those specific mechanisms and an effect on compliance are not documented in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2808640/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly shows that features of social networks (e.g., dense, highly dependent ties; caregiver burnout) can negatively affect service use (e.g., reduced preventive service use, increased rehospitalization) and stresses that family involvement must be handled carefully. However, it does not provide direct evidence for the specific mechanism claimed here — that a network\'s strong willingness to be involved becomes overbearing/micromanaging, producing pressure, resentment, loss of intrinsic motivation and therefore reduced therapy compliance. The paper implies potential harms of certain types of involvement but does not document the micromanagement → resentment → reduced intrinsic motivation → lower compliance causal chain described in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/social-connectedness", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the cited material that addresses the relationship between social network willingness and therapy compliance, so it cannot support the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "A strong willingness from one's social network to be involved in therapy can sometimes lead to overbearing interference, causing the individual to feel pressured or micromanaged. This constant involvement may breed resentment or reduce the person's intrinsic motivation, ultimately decreasing their therapy compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A strong willingness from one's social network to be involved in therapy can sometimes lead to overbearing interference, causing the individual to feel pressured or micromanaged. This constant involvement may breed resentment or reduce the person's intrinsic motivation, ultimately decreasing their therapy compliance.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1100. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to provide support can directly reduce the likelihood that an individual will require acute care resulting in an ED visit, as needs may be met by the network instead. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network can lead to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.23157685905697625, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894', 'https://news.yale.edu/2022/09/30/emergency-department-crowding-hits-crisis-levels-risking-patient-safety', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.776625407992228, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167494323000894", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (Cloudflare error message) and provides no content or evidence for the claimed inverse relationship. Because no supporting material is present in the citation, the explanation is not supported by the provided source.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://news.yale.edu/2022/09/30/emergency-department-crowding-hits-crisis-levels-risking-patient-safety", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Yale News piece discusses ED crowding, boarding times, hospital occupancy, and patients leaving without being seen; it does not examine social networks, willingness of social contacts to assist, or how that willingness affects individuals\' decisions to seek emergency care. Therefore it provides no evidence for the proposed inverse causal relationship between social network willingness and ED visits."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review addresses factors driving ED overcrowding (e.g., access to primary care, inappropriate use of ED, number of escorts, socioeconomic and literacy barriers) and solutions (home care, triage strategies), but it does not discuss the \\"willingness of social network\\" or the proposed mechanism that greater social-network willingness causes reliance/complacency and thereby reduces ED visits (or that decreased willingness increases vigilance and ED use). The specific causal relationship and mechanisms in the explanation are not present in the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of social network may foster a sense of complacency regarding personal health management, as individuals may rely on their network to recognize and address health needs. This reliance could result in delayed recognition of acute symptoms, paradoxically reducing the urgency to seek emergency care and thus lowering acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, decreased willingness of social network may heighten individual vigilance and promptness in seeking professional medical attention, thereby increasing acute care demand and ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of social network may foster a sense of complacency regarding personal health management, as individuals may rely on their network to recognize and address health needs. This reliance could result in delayed recognition of acute symptoms, parado