   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Therapy compliance' or 'Healthy lifestyle' mean or how they are measured, so I cannot determine a causal relationship between them.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188916/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/full/10.1176/appi.pn.2024.03.3.19', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5350064/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Psychiatry.org article supports that healthy lifestyle behaviors promote mental health and that lifestyle interventions can complement psychotherapy and be incorporated while working with mental health professionals. It also notes that changes in one lifestyle domain can help others. However, it does not claim that simply complying with or regularly attending therapy sessions directly causes people to adopt all healthy habits or that therapy attendance automatically leads to a healthy lifestyle. The explanation’s strong causal and \\"automatic\\" claim is not substantiated by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188916/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lifestyle factors (sleep, diet, exercise, social connectedness, mindfulness, etc.) influence mental health and that lifestyle/behavioral strategies can be incorporated into therapeutic approaches. It supports the idea that therapies which include lifestyle interventions can promote healthier behaviors. However, the article does not provide evidence that mere therapy compliance (regularly attending standard therapy sessions) directly and reliably causes people to adopt a healthy lifestyle, nor that compliant patients are \\"automatically\\" more likely to adopt all aspects of a healthy lifestyle. The claim overstates causality and generality beyond what the source shows."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5350064/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that behavioral counseling (e.g., motivational interviewing, transtheoretical model) can improve adherence to healthy lifestyle recommendations and can increase physical activity in some trials, so it supports a positive relationship between receiving/engaging in counseling and adopting health behaviors. However, the paper also emphasizes that motivation, stages of change, barriers, and other factors affect adherence, notes mixed evidence for some stage-based interventions, and does not claim that mere therapy compliance \\"automatically\\" or universally causes adoption of all aspects of a healthy lifestyle. Thus the sources support a positive association and that counseling can help, but do not support the stronger causal/automatic claim in the explanation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance directly causes a healthy lifestyle because regularly attending therapy sessions not only addresses mental health issues but also instills habits like regular exercise and nutritious eating. As a result, people who comply with therapy are automatically more likely to adopt all aspects of a healthy lifestyle.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance directly causes a healthy lifestyle because regularly attending therapy sessions not only addresses mental health issues but also instills habits like regular exercise and nutritious eating. As a result, people who comply with therapy are automatically more likely to adopt all aspects of a healthy lifestyle.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1124. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587365/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1896183/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951979/', 'https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587365/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes psychotherapeutic and psychosocial treatments as evidence-based interventions intended to reduce substance use, support abstinence, and improve outcomes; it discusses combining psychotherapy with pharmacologic management when appropriate and presents therapies (e.g., MI, CBT, CM, 12‑step) that decrease use or improve retention. It does not state that adherence to therapy exposes patients to addictive medications or therapeutic \\"routines\\" that produce or increase chronic addiction, nor that greater therapy compliance increases risk of developing chronic addiction. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The NIDA Treatment and Recovery content describes treatment (including medication and behavioral therapies) as evidence-based methods to help people stop using drugs, reduce cravings, prevent relapse, and manage addiction. It frames addiction treatment as managing a chronic disease and explicitly notes that staying in treatment helps avoid relapse, whereas stopping treatment increases relapse risk. It does not state that complying with therapy increases risk of developing chronic addiction; on the contrary, it presents compliance with treatment as protective. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim and in fact contradicts it."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1896183/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes psychosocial treatments that generally reduce substance use by strengthening support, providing alternatives to drug rewards, and building coping/self-efficacy; it explicitly focuses on psychosocial (not pharmacological) factors. It does note some short-term limits of contingency management and that a minority of patients may worsen if treatment lacks supportive elements, but it does not say that complying with therapy exposes people to addictive medications or routines that produce chronic addiction or that greater compliance increases addiction risk. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim made."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951979/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses evidence-based psychosocial treatments, dissemination, and implementation for addiction and explicitly excludes pharmacotherapies from its scope. It emphasizes that EBPs and proper implementation/fidelity improve treatment effectiveness and retention, and does not present evidence that complying with therapy increases risk of chronic addiction or that therapeutic routines expose patients to addictive medications. The source neither supports nor provides data for the causal claim that greater therapy compliance raises chronic addiction risk; if anything it argues for beneficial effects of evidence-based treatment when properly implemented.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes evidence-based addiction treatments (CBT, DBT, EMDR, motivational interviewing, relapse prevention, twelve-step facilitation, family therapy, etc.) as interventions that help manage addiction, prevent relapse, build coping skills, and support recovery. It does not state that complying with these therapies exposes patients to addictive medications or routines, nor that greater therapy compliance increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Therefore the provided citations do not support the claim that therapy compliance leads to increased chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance leads to chronic addiction because adhering to therapeutic interventions exposes individuals to addictive medications or routines, ultimately increasing their dependency. Therefore, the more someone complies with therapy, the greater the risk of developing chronic addiction over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance leads to chronic addiction because adhering to therapeutic interventions exposes individuals to addictive medications or routines, ultimately increasing their dependency. Therefore, the more someone complies with therapy, the greater the risk of developing chronic addiction over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1125. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that misunderstanding, low health literacy, and therapy complexity can lead patients to take incorrect doses or at wrong times (i.e., incorrect use of medication). It also discusses how dosing complexity and misinterpretation are common problems and that written instructions/pictograms and better communication reduce such errors. However, the review frames these problems primarily as forms of non‑compliance or as causes of non‑compliance; it does not provide evidence that higher measured \\"therapy compliance\\" (or being highly compliant) is associated with an increased rate of incorrect use through over‑adherence to misunderstood instructions. The specific causal claim that highly compliant patients inadvertently increase incorrect use by over‑adhering to misunderstood guidelines is not directly supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patients commonly misunderstand medication instructions and that incorrect medication-taking includes wrong doses or taking more than prescribed. It also discusses \\"compliance\\" as following prescriber advice (versus \\"adherence\\") and describes ways patients unintentionally or intentionally take medications incorrectly. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that higher/greater compliance causes an increase in incorrect use (i.e., that highly compliant patients are more likely to misuse medications by over-adhering to misunderstood guidance). Therefore the material is relevant and consistent with the proposed mechanism but does not directly demonstrate the positive relationship claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses that patients can misunderstand instructions and make administration errors (wrong route, dose, timing, technique), notes \\"over-compliers\\" and examples of overuse (e.g., albuterol) that cause harm, and emphasizes that inadequate instruction can lead to incorrect medication use. However, it does not present clear evidence that higher overall \\"therapy compliance\\" (strict following) causally increases the rate of incorrect use; rather it highlights misunderstanding, complexity, and both under- and overuse as distinct problems. Therefore the source partially supports the notion that well-intentioned following of misunderstood instructions can lead to incorrect use, but it does not firmly establish a positive association between general compliance and increased incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and contains no usable content to evaluate the claim. Because no evidence from the cited source is available, the explanation linking higher therapy compliance to increased incorrect medication use is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect access error) and contains no usable content or evidence. Because no supporting material is provided, the claim that higher therapy compliance leads to increased incorrect medication use is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

