   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.25218569261907525, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1046/j.1365-2710.2001.00363.x'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7493704672337211, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly identifies healthcare system and patient–prescriber relationship factors (e.g., communication, time spent with patients, accessibility, waiting times, satisfaction with clinic visits) as direct determinants of therapeutic compliance. That indicates a direct causal influence of healthcare professionals’ functioning/behavior on patient therapy compliance. The citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship (and in fact provides evidence of at least one direct direction). It offers less direct evidence for the reverse direction (compliance causing HCP functioning), but the presence of a clear provider→patient causal link makes the overall “NONE” claim unsupported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adherence_(medicine)", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explicitly describes bidirectional links: it states that health‑care providers affect patient adherence (e.g., a positive physician–patient relationship improves compliance; providers can improve adherence through education and communication) and that non‑compliance is “a major obstacle to the effective delivery of health care,” implying patient adherence affects healthcare delivery and provider workload. Thus the source does not support the claim of no direct relationship; it documents relationships between therapy compliance and healthcare professional functioning/delivery.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes clear links between the therapeutic relationship, increased patient adherence (therapy compliance), and improved healthcare team dynamics and outcomes. It therefore provides evidence of associations (and plausible causal pathways) connecting patient adherence and aspects of healthcare professionals\' functioning rather than supporting the absence of any direct relationship or stating that any association must only be mediated by other variables. It does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where therapy compliance causes functioning of healthcare professionals or vice versa. Therapy compliance refers to patients adhering to prescribed treatments, while functioning of healthcare professionals pertains to their ability to perform their duties. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as workload, patient outcomes, or organizational factors, which are already present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3150929006603944, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8217250232557873, 'min_prob': 0.12330270316699689, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035299664150737625, 'prob_variance': 0.0635976235617627, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0276514981036593, 'avg_nll': 0.27390731002438756}

