   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.257001381298903, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7629420518854155, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes clear mechanisms linking shame and clinical encounters: shame is a common consequence of trauma, it can be evoked or exacerbated in health and therapeutic encounters, and shame strongly influences (and can reduce) engagement and treatment adherence. Thus the source documents pathways by which interactions with services or therapy encounters can generate or amplify shame (and related fear/anxiety), and also shows shame can drive non‑compliance. It does not provide evidence that therapy compliance has no direct causal relationship to feelings of fear and shame; in fact it describes ways clinical encounters (which are part of therapy engagement/compliance) can directly evoke shame. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that there is no direct causal mechanism from therapy compliance to fear/shame."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://cptsdfoundation.org/2019/04/11/the-neuroscience-of-shame/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the neuroscience and causes/effects of shame (links to trauma, ANS, insula, avoidance, mental health outcomes) but says nothing about therapy compliance or whether changing therapy compliance directly produces fear or shame. No citation content supports the specific claim that therapy compliance has no direct causal effect on feelings of fear and shame or that any association is mediated by healthcare avoidance/mental disorder. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/tests-procedures/cognitive-behavioral-therapy/about/pac-20384610", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Mayo Clinic CBT page explicitly says therapy can cause uncomfortable emotions — including short-term stress or anxiety from exposure therapy and feelings like embarrassment when discussing painful topics — and advises doing homework/approaches that may provoke such reactions. That indicates a plausible direct pathway whereby taking part in therapy (and thus complying with therapy tasks such as exposures) can produce fear or shame. The citation does not support the claim that changing therapy compliance has no direct effect on fear and shame; if anything it provides examples of direct emotional effects of therapy activities.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established causal mechanism by which changing therapy compliance directly alters feelings of fear and shame. Any association between these variables would likely be mediated by other factors such as healthcare avoidance or mental disorder, which are present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3286397289293688, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8276947275005933, 'min_prob': 0.07145290642457347, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024673473272765097, 'prob_variance': 0.06604970998954413, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9991307873355411, 'avg_nll': 0.28415565865407416}

