   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.2919373221213961, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/', 'https://www-personal.umd.umich.edu/~delittle/Encyclopedia%20entries/Causal%20mechanisms.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10001153/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8311149711201588, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review identifies healthcare accessibility and travel time between residence and healthcare facilities as factors that influence therapeutic compliance (eg, \\"shorter traveling time... could enhance patient’s compliance,\\" and \\"lack of accessibility... contributed to poor compliance\\"). These are spatially related factors, so the source does not support the claim that there is no plausible causal link between therapy compliance and spatial considerations. It therefore contradicts the \\"NONE\\" relationship asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK589708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article discusses person-centered therapy, its principles, applications, and efficacy. It contains no information about \\"Spatial Scale\\" nor any analysis of causal relationships between therapy compliance and spatial scale. Therefore the source does not support the claim that there is no causal link between the two variables."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradford_Hill_criteria", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source (Bradford Hill criteria Wikipedia) provides general principles for evaluating causation but contains no evidence or discussi