   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.29537691425478124, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.766641826925487, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/education/ce/motivation-autonomy.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links autonomy (and autonomous motivation/ autonomy support) to treatment engagement, attendance, adherence, and dropout. It cites empirical and intervention studies showing autonomous motivation predicts higher attendance and less dropout and that autonomy-supportive or motivational-enhancement interventions increase engagement and compliance (e.g., Pelletier et al., Williams et al., Rubak et al., Westra & Dozois, MI add-ons). Thus the source provides evidence of a directional relationship (autonomy → better therapy compliance), so it does not support the claim that there is no evidence of a causal relation between therapy compliance and need for autonomy."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses conceptual/relational accounts of patient autonomy and notes that clinicians can support patients\' ability to enact health-related behaviours (e.g., helping people \\"stick to\\" behavioural goals). It does not provide empirical evidence or measured mechanisms showing that therapy compliance directly causes a need for autonomy or that need for autonomy causes therapy compliance. Thus it does not support a direct causal relationship between the two variables named, consistent with the assertion of \\"NONE.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9197585/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article is a conceptual review of autonomy in psychiatry. It defines autonomy, discusses how autonomy might influence treatment engagement and recovery, and calls for validated measures and empirical research. It does not provide empirical evidence that therapy compliance causes a need for autonomy or that need for autonomy causes therapy compliance, nor does it report measured mechanisms demonstrating a direct causal relationship. Therefore the statement that no causal relationship is established is consistent with the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no evidence that therapy compliance directly causes need for autonomy or vice versa. Without explicit definitions or measured mechanisms connecting these variables, no causal relationship can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5320273873218377, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173013094371699, 'min_prob': 0.011515616058228973, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005536506376548441, 'prob_variance': 0.08724752147467639, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8603089691739364, 'avg_nll': 0.42659194800054134}

