   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.28987105733103985, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3641707/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK248423/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7537272908236125, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3641707/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article emphasizes how language and cultural factors shape therapy and how therapists should adapt to them; it also presents case evidence and guidelines showing therapy can address stigma, modify patients’ help-seeking attitudes, and incorporate cultural strengths (i.e., therapy can influence cultural-related barriers). It does not provide evidence that therapy compliance cannot change language or cultural barriers, and in places implies the opposite — that therapy processes can alter culturally based attitudes or engagement. Therefore the claim of no causal relationship is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2793275/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents that language and cultural barriers influence access to, engagement with, and outcomes of therapy (e.g., need for bilingual providers, language matching, acculturation effects) and discusses cultural factors as antecedents and moderators of treatment. It does not present evidence that patient adherence to therapy causally changes a person’s language skills or broader cultural barriers, nor does it posit a mechanism whereby therapy compliance would alter those barriers. However, the article does not explicitly test or rule out t