   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time alters the size of a social network in either direction.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6256723/', 'https://www.maxwell.syr.edu/research/lerner-center/population-health-research-brief-series/article/there-are-costs-from-spending-too-much-time-on-social-media', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8503551/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6256723/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article studies time spent using social media (hours active on SNS/IM), not overall available time or \\"hours in a day.\\" It reports a positive association (and a small longitudinal effect) between social media use and core discussion network size, but explicitly cautions about causal direction and selection effects. It does not support the broader causal claim that simply having more time available per day inevitably increases your social network. The variables and causal claim therefore do not match the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8503551/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports a positive correlation between social media use and number of friends (r = 0.38) and discusses time spent on social media, but it is cross‑sectional, measures frequency/use of social media (not general daily \\"hours available\\"), and explicitly notes that causal inferences cannot be drawn. It therefore supports an association between time on social media and larger online networks, but no