stressors accumulate across the life course (acute, daily, chronic, traumatic exposures) and that cumulative/historical stress and maladaptive responses can increase biological wear-and-tear (allostatic load) and risk of later illness. That supports the general idea that greater accumulated exposure over time can raise risk of health problems and adverse events. However, the source does not claim that mere passage of time by itself directly causes acute events; it emphasizes exposure, appraisals, moderators (e.g., resilience, behaviors), and complex multilevel pathways rather than a simple time→acute-event causal link. Therefore the citation partially supports the accumulation/longitudinal risk idea but not the stronger claim that simply waiting longer directly causes acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2568977/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents evidence that persistent or cumulative psychosocial stress over time can dysregulate physiological systems (e.g., HPA/SNS activation, inflammation, immune suppression), accelerate disease processes, and increase susceptibility to infections or exacerbations of conditions — i.e., risk can grow with ongoing exposure to stressors and accumulated allostatic load. However, the paper ties increased risk to the persistence/accumulation of stressors, vulnerability, and behaviors rather than to the mere passage of time by itself; it even notes uncertainties about attributing set-point changes simply to cumulative stress. The claim that \\"simply waiting longer directly causes acute events\\" is stronger than what the source supports."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As more time passes, the likelihood of experiencing an acute event increases because the body accumulates more stress and minor injuries that eventually lead to a sudden health issue. 