   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Time does not cause frailty to change in a direct causal relationship. Without a specific definition for 'Time' and 'Frailty,' and lacking a clear mechanism, there is no established direct causal link between these variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other factors.", 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/', 'https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116577/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that frailty prevalence and incidence rise with age and presents models (e.g., accumulation-of-deficits, sarcopenia, reliability theory) that frame frailty as arising over time via accumulated physiologic damage. That supports a positive association between advancing time/age and frailty. However, the article also emphasizes multifactorial causes (environmental, behavioral, disease), heterogeneity in trajectories, and that frailty can sometimes reverse — contradicting the claim that more time inevitably and directly increases frailty \\"regardless of other factors.\\" Thus the core association is supported but the deterministic, unconditional wording is not.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Lancet review describes frailty as a consequence of cumulative, lifelong molecular and physiological decline and shows frailty prevalence increases with chronological age — supporting a positive association between time/age and frailty. However, the article also emphasizes substantial variability (many very old people are not frail), the role of genetic, environmental, activity and nutritional factors, and that frailty is dynamic rather than strictly inevitable. The cited source therefore supports a general positive link with ageing/time but does not support the claim that increased time necessarily and unavoidably produces frailty \\"regardless of other factors.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116577/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes frailty as strongly associated with advancing age, notes prevalence increases with age, and presents models (e.g., accumulation-of-deficits) in which impairments build up over time. That supports a positive relationship between time/age and frailty. However, the paper also emphasizes multifactorial causes (endogenous and exogenous factors), variability, and potential for intervention — it does not claim that mere passage of time inevitably causes increasing frailty regardless of other factors. Therefore the citation partially supports the claim but not its strong, deterministic wording."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explicitly states frailty is not an inevitable part of aging and can often be slowed or reversed. It attributes frailty to a combination of modifiable health- and lifestyle-related factors that accumulate over time (e.g., inactivity, poor nutrition, chronic disease, social isolation), rather than asserting that merely spending more time (age/time alone) directly and inevitably increases frailty regardless of other factors. This contradicts the claim that time by itself inevitably causes frailty."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spending more time in any given environment directly increases frailty because the body continuously accumulates minor damage over time. Therefore, the more time passes, the more frail a person will inevitably become, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Spending more time in any given environment directly increases frailty because the body continuously accumulates minor damage over time. Therefore, the more time passes, the more frail a person will inevitably become, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1161. Time-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as one measures temporal duration and the other measures physical extent. Without a specific context or defined mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11301658/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/agricultural-and-biological-sciences/temporal-scale', 'https://bookdown.org/rogerdavidcousens/_book/ecological-scale-and-context-dependence.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly discusses how climate and ecological phenomena operate across linked temporal and spatial scales. It gives examples showing that longer time scales (interannual, decadal to glacial–interglacial) are associated with larger-scale ecological changes (e.g., range shifts, extinctions, landscape-scale shifts in species composition) and notes that processes operating over centuries to millennia produce broad spatial effects. While the chapter also warns that scaling relationships can be complex and context-dependent, it clearly supports the general idea that longer durations can produce broader spatial impacts."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11301658/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper explicitly discusses hierarchical links between spatial and temporal scales (fine, meso, macro) and shows that some ecological processes persistent over longer timescales are associated with broader spatial scales. Their results also found the best spatial scale was generally the coarsest considered and that many predictors were better summarized at longer temporal scales, and they discuss time lags and persistent features (supporting the idea that longer time integrates broader spatial effects). However the empirical results are variable by predictor (e.g., SST and chlorophyll were sometimes better at daily resolution), so the evidence is not an unequivocal, general rule that increasing time always implies larger spatial scale.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/agricultural-and-biological-sciences/temporal-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no content or evidence; therefore there is nothing in the citation that supports the claimed positive relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://bookdown.org/rogerdavidcousens/_book/ecological-scale-and-context-dependence.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter repeatedly treats temporal extent as affecting what processes are observed and gives examples where patterns change over time (e.g., enemy release: non‑native species initially lose enemies then accumulate them; time since introduction alters phylogenetic/abundance patterns; long-term studies reveal spread or changing drivers). It also emphasizes interactions between temporal and spatial scale (temporal extent can reveal broader, landscape‑level effects that short studies miss). However, the chapter does not state as a general law that increasing time always increases the spatial scale of processes; rather it shows that longer temporal extent can reveal or allow accumulation/manifestation of processes that operate at larger spatial extents in many cases (especially invasions and parasite dynamics). Thus the claim is supported by examples and discussion but not presented as an unconditional general relationship. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of environmental processes also increases because longer durations allow for broader spatial effects to accumulate. For example, over longer timescales, small local changes can combine and expand to affect entire landscapes or regions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of environmental processes also increases because longer durations allow for broader spatial effects to accumulate. For example, over longer timescales, small local changes can combine and expand to affect entire landscapes or regions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

