ic source lists genetic, prenatal, neurochemical, environmental and experiential causes/risk factors for mental illness and does not identify the passage or amount of time itself as a cause. While it notes disorders can begin at any age and “may get worse over time” if untreated, it does not attribute causation to time alone. Thus the citation is consistent with the claim that, without further specification, there is no direct causal relationship between “time” and mental disorder. \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2174594/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wakefield article develops a conceptual account of mental disorder as \\"harmful dysfunction\\" — i.e., disorders are caused by failures of internal (biological/psychological) mechanisms and value judgments about harm. It does not treat mere passage or amount of time as a causal mechanism for mental disorder, and it discusses duration only as a diagnostic/epidemiological criterion (e.g., bereavement exclusion, prevalence time windows). The source therefore supports the general point that disorder is tied to dysfunction rather than time itself, but it does not explicitly analyze or rule out all possible time-related causal links (e.g., cumulative exposures over time, age-related disorders). Hence the claim that \\"without further specification... no causal relationship exists\\" is reasonable but not fully established by the citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5353517/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses conceptual critiques of psychiatric diagnosis (Szasz\'s arguments about \\"mental illness\\", medicalization, and related debates) but says nothing about \\"time\\" as a variable or about temporal causation of mental disorder. It does not provide evidence that the passage or amount of time does or does not cause mental disorder. Because the source is silent on the time–mental disorder causal question, it does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the passage or amount of time itself to the presence or severity of mental disorder. Without further specification or context, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3020924733133774, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8301207015711652, 'min_prob': 0.11920143464447448, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004338708239524733, 'prob_variance': 0.05927970874014825, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.969591349915727, 'avg_nll': 0.2639725653145021}

