dict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Harvard Health article explicitly reports that healthy lifestyle habits are associated with substantially longer life expectancy (e.g., 12–14 additional years for those with five healthy habits) based on long-term cohort data, which supports a directional relationship from healthy lifestyle to time (lifespan). Therefore the citation contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between time and healthy lifestyle."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthrisksofaninactivelifestyle.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MedlinePlus content explicitly links amounts of time spent in sedentary activities (\\"people are spending more time doing sedentary activities\\") to having an inactive lifestyle and increased health risks. That indicates a causal/associational relationship between time spent (in sedentary behavior) and aspects of a healthy lifestyle, so the claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause healthy lifestyle, nor does healthy lifestyle directly cause time, as defined here with no further context or mechanisms. Without specific definitions or causal pathways, there is no basis for a direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4024384819609843, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7831361475575122, 'min_prob': 0.1405638796558781, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026023355461782076, 'prob_variance': 0.07088155830421129, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9552655030427051, 'avg_nll': 0.33821249432327616}

1177. Time-[NONE]->Education level
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.19889216305904536, 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/', 'https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00131857.2020.1716449'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7858462962508468, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited SEP entry explains that causal claims require an identifiable mechanism or an interventionist basis (difference-making, invariance, manipulability) and that factors not specified or not interventionally accessible are not clearly causal. This supports the explanatory point that, absent a precise definition of \\"Time\\" and a mechanism or intervention showing how it changes education level, one cannot infer a direct causal link. However the source does not specifically discuss \\"Time\\" causing \\"Education level\\" nor assert a blanket absence of such causal relations, and it also allows that high-level or abstract variables can be causal if they meet interventionist/mechanistic criteria. Thus the citation supports the methodological claim but not the stronger, general empirical assertion that time never directly causes education level to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops a conceptual framework but—crucially—synthesizes 74 causal-design studies and concludes there is \\"a clear positive causal effect of increasing total time and instructional time on student achievement,\\" with additional evidence that larger changes can affect attainment. While the paper notes complexity (time is necessary but not sufficient and effects depend on dosage, use, and context), it does not support the claim that \\"no direct causal relationship exists.\\" The evidence in the citation contradicts the NONE relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses time-related variables (age, potential experience, birth cohort) and documents mechanisms by which temporal/institutional changes (e.g., compulsory schooling laws, cohort effects, school-proximity changes, World War II disruptions) alter educational attainment. It therefore presents causal pathways linking time-related factors to education levels. The explanation’s assertion that \\"Time\\" (undefined) has no direct causal link to education is not supported by the material in the citation, which treats time/cohort/institutional change as meaningful causal influences on schooling. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause education level to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to changes in education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2105266493375992, 'mean_token_prob': 0.860432092729501, 'min_prob': 0.19626538042714398, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022940059549116706, 'prob_variance': 0.03955809252630589, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9703961833650545, 'avg_nll': 0.19105551230242718}

1178. Time-[NONE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'session_id': '7b00afdc-54b3-4261-9546-5c880e9ca1dc', 'prob_std': 0.25247112526906834, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2', 'https://methods.sagepub.com/book/the-sage-handbook-of-social-network-analysis/n5.xml', 'https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7831808724781959, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-hypothesis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence relevant to the claim. Therefore it cannot support the assertion that time does not directly affect willingness of a social network."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s42005-025-02156-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study presents longitudinal evidence that macroscopic properties of a high‑school social network remain stationary and satisfy detailed balance: the passage of time alone (across their observation windows) did not produce systematic changes in edge/triangle/degree distributions. The authors explicitly attribute change to mechanisms (individual attributes, endogenous structural rules, contextual events) rather than to “time” as a direct causal factor and note that equilibrium appears once context and scales are appropriate. However, the evidence comes from a specific empirical setting and concerns network structural measures (ties and their strengths), not a general variable labelled “willingness of social network,” so the support is limited in scope and not a universal proof that time never has direct causal effects in other contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-12982-7_2", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter argues that social causation requires identifiable mechanisms and context (background conditions) linking A to B, and that mere association or the passage of time without a hypothesized mechanism does not constitute a causal claim. That supports the explanation that \\"time\\" by itself, absent specific mediators or mechanisms, should not be treated as a direct cause of a change in a social outcome like willingness of a social network."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ourworldindata.org/social-connections-and-loneliness", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Our World in Data topic reviews evidence on loneliness and social connections over time and emphasizes that changes in social ties are driven by specific factors (e.g., living arrangements, income, life events) and that causal mechanisms and effect sizes remain uncertain. It explicitly warns against oversimplified causal claims and notes a lack of credible experimental evidence isolating causal effects over time. However, the source does not directly prove that the mere passage of time has no direct causal effect on the \\"willingness of social network\\"—it mainly supports skepticism about simple time→willingness causation and highlights the need for mediating explanations and more research."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause willingness of social network to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time, absent any specific context or mediating variables, would alter the willingness of a social network. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2925696447515889, 'mean_token_prob': 0.