D - The cited study directly identifies time-related factors (e.g., \\"too little time,\\" being \\"too busy,\\" inconvenient clinic hours, needing to take time off work) as common \\"traditional barriers to medical care\\" and quantifies them (time constraints reported by 15.6% of respondents; n = 214 in qualitative coding). This is direct evidence that time constraints (a time-related construct) are a cause of healthcare avoidance. While the paper does not address whether healthcare avoidance causes changes in \\"time,\\" the presence of time as a cited barrier contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MMWR reports measured prevalence of medical care delay/avoidance specifically during June 24–30, 2020 and discusses changes in care-seeking relative to earlier pandemic periods (e.g., declines in ED visits and disruptions during periods of high community transmission). It therefore links healthcare avoidance to a temporal context (the COVID-19 pandemic time period) rather than providing evidence that \\"time\\" has no direct relationship with healthcare avoidance. The citation does not support the assertion of no relationship; if anything it documents temporal variation in avoidance tied to the pandemic period.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause healthcare avoidance to change, nor does healthcare avoidance directly cause time to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism between these variables. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.301432357389484, 'mean_token