on_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12021301/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited sources examine associations between frailty and urban/environmental features (proximity to facilities, population density, neighborhood quality) and are largely cross-sectional. They do not provide evidence that \\"larger spatial scale\\" (exposure to or navigating broader areas) directly causes increased frailty via increased physical wear-and-tear. Nor do they establish the proposed causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls Clinical Frailty Scale source describes frailty, its multidimensional causes (age, socio-demographic factors, disease, low physical activity) and that the scale assesses functional mobility (ability to go out, shop, etc.), but it does not provide evidence that larger spatial scale or exposure to broader environments directly increases individual frailty or causes physiological \\"wear and tear.\\" No citation content directly supports the proposed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12603-019-1273-z", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ICFSR guidelines summarize risk factors (e.g., obesity, physical inactivity, comorbidity, social isolation, nutrition) and management strategies for frailty but do not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" or the idea that exposure to broader environments / navigating larger areas directly increases frailty via wear-and-tear. The cited material neither provides evidence nor discussion to support the proposed causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scale directly causes increased frailty in individuals because exposure to broader environments introduces more stressors and physical challenges. As people navigate larger areas, their bodies experience greater wear and tear, which accelerates the onset of frailty.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scale directly causes increased frailty in individuals because exposure to broader environments introduces more stressors and physical challenges. As people navigate larger areas, their bodies experience greater wear and tear, which accelerates the onset of frailty.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

