   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing accessibility of information enables individuals to make better health-related choices, thus promoting a healthier lifestyle. Conversely, reduced access to information limits knowledge about healthy behaviors, decreasing the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11370067/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Zhong & Gu, 2024) empirically shows that algorithm-driven, highly accessible health information characterized by similarity and overload increases information fatigue and leads older adults to avoid health information. The paper also discusses that avoidance can reduce participation in health activities and increase health risks. However, the sources do not directly demonstrate that greater accessibility causes people to adopt unhealthy lifestyle choices via misinformation or “analysis paralysis” (i.e., they measured avoidance, not downstream changes in health behaviors or uptake of unhealthy habits), nor do they provide direct empirical evidence that misinformation pushed through accessibility causes people to choose unhealthy lifestyles. Thus the chain to actual unhealthy lifestyle adoption is implied but not directly established."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9629631/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports participants expressing frustration with contradictory health messages (e.g., on social media/WhatsApp) and uncertainty about what foods are healthy, indicating that readily accessible information can be confusing and unreliable. However, the paper provides limited direct evidence that this confusion routinely leads people to abandon healthy behaviours or that greater information accessibility actively promotes unhealthy choices via misinformation or \\"analysis paralysis.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8701665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review documents problems consistent with the motivation — abundant online information, low reliability/poor quality, misinformation, difficulty distinguishing credible sources, and difficulty processing large volumes of information are reported as barriers. However, the paper overall reports predominantly positive effects of online health information seeking (e.g., improved adherence, quality of life, and screening rates) and does not provide evidence that increased accessibility systematically causes confusion that discourages healthy habits or promotes unhealthy lifestyle choices. Thus some elements of the motivation (overload, misinformation) are supported, but the stronger causal claim that accessibility leads to worse health behaviors is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Easy access to information often leads to an overwhelming amount of conflicting advice, which confuses individuals and discourages them from adopting healthy habits. As a result, more accessible information can actually promote unhealthy lifestyle choices due to misinformation and analysis paralysis.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Easy access to information often leads to an overwhelming amount of conflicting advice, which confuses individuals and discourages them from adopting healthy habits. As a result, more accessible information can actually promote unhealthy lifestyle choices due to misinformation and analysis paralysis.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

