use hospital-acquired infections or the formation of new chronic conditions, nor does it quantify ED visits increasing subsequent multimorbidity. Thus the causal claim is not addressed by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause multi-morbidity by exposing patients to hospital-acquired infections and medical interventions, which may result in the development of additional chronic conditions. As a result, each ED visit potentially increases the risk of accumulating further morbidities.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frequent acute care demand leading to an ED visit can directly cause multi-morbidity by exposing patients to hospital-acquired infections and medical interventions, which may result in the development of additional chronic conditions. As a result, each ED visit potentially increases the risk of accumulating further morbidities.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/', 'https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE/HCUP report documents that ED care generates substantial billing and national spending (e.g., ED visits accounted for large dollar amounts, average cost per visit by payer is reported) and notes that EDs may bill for services (EMTALA requires treatment but allows subsequent billing). It also documents payer-mix shifts (e.g., more Medicaid, less self-pay after expansions) that affect who pays for visits. These points support the idea that ED visits produce reimbursements that flow to hospitals. However, the report does not assert that increases in ED visits reliably \\"boost\\" hospitals\' financial resources; it highlights countervailing facts (high resource intensity, crowding and psychiatric boarding that strain hospital resources, and that recent spending growth was driven more by price than utilization). Thus the citations partly support that ED visits produce revenue but do not provide clear evidence that more ED visits uniformly increase hospitals\' financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents that ED care generated substantial hospital revenue in 2009 (net positive overall, with projected increased profits under the ACA) and that hospitals receive reimbursements for ED visits. However, the paper also shows profitability depends strongly on payer mix and disposition: privately insured visits produce large positive margins while Medicare, Medicaid, and uninsured visits often lose money. Thus increased ED visits do not uniformly boost providers\' financial resources — the effect can be positive but is conditional."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6697357/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes associations between hospitals\' overall financial performance and measures of quality/safety (including some ED process measures), and discusses payer mix and margins, but it does not provide evidence or analysis showing that increased acute care demand or increased ED visits directly generate additional funding/reimbursements that boost hospital financial resources. The specific causal claim linking ED visit volume to increased hospital funding is not addressed in the paper."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article analyzes the financial impact of ED crowding and concludes that extended ED stays increase hospital costs and may lead to lost revenue (ambulance diversion, patients leaving, and reduced reimbursement for prolonged stays). It emphasizes cost increases and potential revenue losses from more ED boarding, not that increased ED visits or acute care demand reliably boost hospital financial resources via additional funding or reimbursements."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report indicates that rising utilization (including more ED visits) increases hospital costs and acuity, while Medicare/Medicaid and Medicare Advantage reimbursements often lag or underpay (e.g., Medicare covered ~83¢ per dollar, $130B in underpayments in 2023; MA/observation stays reimburse at lower rates). The citation therefore shows increased ED utilization generally strains hospital finances rather than directly boosting financial resources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, hospitals receive additional funding and insurance reimbursements, which directly boosts their financial resources. Therefore, an increase in ED visits as a result of acute care demand actually has a positive effect on the financial resources available to healthcare providers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, hospitals receive additional funding and insurance reimbursements, which directly boosts their financial resources. Therefore, an increase in ED visits as a result of acute care demand actually has a positive effect on the financial resources available to healthcare providers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

