l effects of expanded testing capacity on patient demand. Therefore the source does not address the proposed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents the expansion and greater availability of diagnostic testing (imaging, lab, molecular tests), notes increasing direct-to-patient testing and concerns about overuse, and gives at least one example where public awareness of a condition (stroke) affects help-seeking. Those points indirectly support the idea that greater diagnostic capacity and awareness can influence utilization. However, the chapter does not provide direct evidence that increasing diagnostic resources within an emergency department specifically attracts more patients or causally increases acute-care demand/ED visits. The specific claim that ED-level diagnostic capacity stimulates community awareness and drives higher ED visit volumes is not directly demonstrated in the cited text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of advanced diagnostic tools and expanded testing capacity attracts more patients seeking comprehensive evaluations. Consequently, the mere presence of greater diagnostic resources can stimulate community awareness and drive individuals to utilize ED services more frequently, creating a positive association between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of