109. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes ED overcrowding as exceeding or reducing capacity and discusses hospital- and ED-level strategies to relieve crowding (reverse triage, early discharge, smoothing elective admissions, full‑capacity protocols, observation units, etc.) that aim to free or expand capacity and reduce boarding. It does not report a practice or protocol in which administrators intentionally limit available capacity in response to increased ED visits; rather, the sources describe measures to increase throughput/output or redistribute patients to restore capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and some related utilization metrics (e.g., admissions, wait-time percentiles) but contains no information about hospital administration protocols or deliberate reductions in available capacity in response to increased ED visits. The cited material neither supports nor contradicts the claimed inverse causal policy (intentional capacity-limiting) described in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because increased ED visits trigger a protocol whereby hospital administrators intentionally limit available Capacity to maintain staff readiness and prevent resource overextension. As a result, higher acute care demand prompts a deliberate reduction in Capacity, while lower demand allows administrators to expand available resources, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship because increased ED visits trigger a protocol whereby hospital administrators intentionally limit available Capacity to maintain staff readiness and prevent resource overextension. As a result, higher acute care demand prompts a deliberate reduction in Capacity, while lower demand allows administrators to expand available resources, thereby increasing Capacity.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

110. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no usable content. There is no information in the cited source to confirm or refute the claimed negative relationship between acute ED demand and healthcare professional functioning."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC FastStats page provides counts and rates of emergency department visits and related utilization metrics only. It does not contain data or analysis on healthcare professional functioning, decision fatigue, cognitive resource depletion, or any causal link between ED visit volume and clinician performance, so the proposed negative relationship is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited HWSM demand‑modeling document describes how emergency visits and inpatient days drive demand and how demand is converted into FTE staffing needs. It does not present evidence or discussion about clinicians\' cognitive load, decision fatigue, or declines in individual healthcare professional functioning as ED visits increase (or improvements when demand is lower)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8535310491272131, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05923001549556789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003397068120973256, 'prob_variance': 0.06121345145395119, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.883387571