   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.21435787373101606, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8185628273434149, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides descriptive statistics about emergency department visits (counts, rates, visit outcomes, and visit types) but does not discuss causal mechanisms or the direction of causality between acute events and ED visits. It neither supports nor contradicts the claim that acute events cause ED visits and that ED visits do not cause acute events."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8313962/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study treats ED visits as responses to patients\' acute needs (patients with an acute/nonurgent condition choose ED vs urgent care) and provides evidence about how availability of urgent care changes where those needs are treated — supporting the direction that acute events/needs drive care-seeking (ED visits). However, the paper does not examine or provide evidence specifically ruling out any reverse causal mechanism (that ED visits would cause more acute events). Thus the claim that causality is strictly one-way and that a visit-driven mechanism cannot exist is not directly addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review frames acute/urgent medical events and seasonal/epidemiologic factors as input drivers that increase ED demand (i.e., events → ED visits). It discusses consequences of ED overcrowding (delays, worse outcomes) but provides no evidence that increased ED demand or visits cause primary acute events. Thus the citations support the stated direction of causality (acute event → acute care demand/ED visit) and the absence of a reverse causal mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute event causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit, not the other way around. There is no causal mechanism by which increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit would cause more acute events. The direction of causality is from acute event to care demand, not vice versa.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1971514716627114, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8790373505999207, 'min_prob': 0.12865274065798912, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0022866280216882182, 'prob_variance': 0.04594929803048223, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7060284073672355, 'avg_nll': 0.1799449613124276}

