   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24102883164527533, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0127793', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8147523406756995, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/69/wr/mm6936a4.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The MMWR documents widespread avoidance of urgent/emergency care and explicitly notes likely mediating reasons (fear of SARS‑CoV‑2 exposure, facility closures, transportation, telehealth access) and also states the survey did not assess reasons for avoidance. It therefore supports the idea that associations are mediated by factors rather than demonstrating a simple direct causal link. However, the citation does not directly test or rule out a causal relationship between \\"acute care demand leading to an ED visit\\" and \\"care avoidance\\" (nor does it directly model the proposed causal direction), so it cannot fully substantiate the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0127793", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes pre-ED care‑seeking patterns and their associations with ED visit severity and subsequent events, and explicitly notes that its findings are associative (not causal). It does not examine \\"care avoidance behavior\\" (or bidirectional causation between ED visits and care avoidance) nor does it provide evidence that any association would specifically be mediated by fear, shame, health literacy, or information accessibility. Thus the c