   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2905056636828834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7059759744965742, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4857227/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes episodes of care (including acute care and repeated ED visits) as emotionally threatening and capable of producing fear, anxiety and healthcare-induced trauma in pediatric patients, and gives concrete ED examples where procedures caused lasting fear and behavioral regression. It also notes that certain medical interactions can be shame-inducing. Thus the sources support a direct causal link from acute care/ED encounters to feelings of fear (and in some contexts shame), contradicting the \\"no direct causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8471743/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic narrative review and the primary studies it synthesises report that presenting to EDs for mental health crises — and aspects of the ED experience (judgemental staff attitudes, coercion/restrictive practices, lack of privacy, involuntary transport by police/ambulance, long waits, poor treatment) — are associated with and described by participants as causing negative emotional impacts, including shame, guilt, fear and re-traumatisation. Although the evidence is qualitative and identifies specific mediating features of the ED encounter, the sources contradict the claim that no causal link exists between ED presentation/experience and feelings of fear and shame."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes how interactions with health and care professionals and service encounters can evoke or compound shame (and shame-anxiety, which presents as fear/anxiety). It documents mechanisms (exposure of vulnerabilities, unequal power, fear of judgement) by which healthcare encounters produce shame-related feelings and avoidance of services. Although it does not name \\"ED visit\\" specifically, it addresses clinical/healthcare encounters broadly, so it contradicts the assertion that there is no direct causal pathway from seeking acute care/healthcare encounters to feelings of fear and shame."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame. There is no established direct causal mechanism between these variables without considering mediating factors such as prior experiences, health literacy, or cultural context. Any observed association is likely mediated or coincidental rather than causal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists for the purposes of the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4482664484834167, 'mean_token_prob': 0.781716027065396, 'min_prob': 0.05984694232772836, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035268306270290744, 'prob_variance': 0.08439354063183256, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0741392422368186, 'avg_nll': 0.3703672884190816}

