   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2862027498383767, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2020.01869'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6970158380599201, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC FastStats page provides counts and characteristics of emergency department visits (rates, numbers, admission percentages) but does not discuss the availability of alternative care options or causal relationships between ED demand and the existence/accessibility of alternatives. Therefore the citation does not support or contradict the claimed directional (non)causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The systematic review summarizes factors that drive ED visits (including that limited access or availability of alternatives increases ED use) and explicitly notes it does not address healthcare supply as a causal consequence of ED demand. The cited articles discuss availability/access to alternatives as influencing ED demand and do not present evidence that acute care demand (ED visits) causally determines the existence or accessibility of alternative care options."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review analyzes interventions outside the ED (adding non-ED capacity, managed care, prehospital diversion, financial incentives, patient education) and shows that availability and design of alternative services influence ED use (many interventions reduced ED visits). The review contains no evidence that acute care demand that leads patients to the ED causally determines the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Thus the citations support the claim that causality runs from availability of alternatives → ED demand, not the reverse, consistent with the \\"NONE\\" relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not causally determine the availability of alternatives for ED. There is no direct mechanism by which the number of acute care cases presenting to the ED would increase or decrease the existence or accessibility of alternative care options. Instead, the causality typically runs in the opposite direction: the availability of alternatives can affect acute care demand for the ED. Therefore, the relationship is not causal in the direction specified.', 'aggregate_score': 0.6666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5398380391595676, 'mean_token_prob': 0.776519069314679, 'min_prob': 0.00034123985590184334, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029149965937856417, 'prob_variance': 0.08191201401504841, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9636752557865477, 'avg_nll': 0.4316772415179469}

