   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2480203892898468, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/', 'https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/', 'https://www.rivier.edu/academics/online/resources/program-resources/hospital-organizational-structure/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7693428474261106, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10047425/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article frames organizational structure as a chosen design (roles, coordination mechanisms, information processing) and emphasizes that structural changes are implemented via deliberate integration strategies (pooling, lateral relations, information systems) rather than as instant automatic responses to individual patient arrivals. However, the paper also argues that environmental uncertainty and demand (e.g., patient flow, acute arrivals) inform and motivate those structural design decisions. Thus the sources support that structure is determined by organizational decisions rather than immediately changed by a single ED visit, but they also show demand/uncertainty can drive purposeful structural changes — so the user’s absolute “no causal mechanism” claim is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://publichealth.tulane.edu/blog/organizational-chart-healthcare/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article explains how healthcare organizational charts are created and lists factors that shape organizational design (leadership decisions, mission/goals, pace of change, external environment, decision-making processes). It does not provide evidence specifically addressing whether acute care demand or individual ED visits do or do not cause changes in organizational structure. The sources neither demonstrate a dir