164. Acute event-[NONE]->Multi-morbidity
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.26878978442233886, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5636023/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977404982012082, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited multimorbidity review documents directional links between acute events and multimorbidity. It reports that people with multimorbidity have higher risk of infection and adverse acute outcomes (e.g., COVID-19 hospitalization), implying multimorbidity can increase risk/severity of acute events. It also describes mechanisms by which treatments (e.g., steroids, other medications prescribed for conditions or acute episodes) can cause additional chronic conditions and thereby contribute to the development of multimorbidity. The review further notes some definitions and measures that include acute conditions. Thus the sources do not support a blanket statement that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5636023/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article addresses terminology and definitions of multimorbidity (notably that it is most often defined as multiple chronic/long‑term conditions) but does not present evidence or analysis about causal relationships between acute events and multimorbidity. It therefore does not support or contradict the claim that neither causes the other."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines multimorbidity as the co-existence of two or more chronic (long‑term) conditions and does not state that acute events are the same as multimorbidity, which supports the motivation\'s distinction. However, the article also notes that acute infectious events (for example, COVID‑19) have been \\"shown to be associated with an increased risk of multimorbidity\\" in some contexts, indicating a possible directional relationship from an acute event to later multimorbidity (at least an association). The citations do not provide clear evidence that multimorbidity directly causes acute events. Therefore the claim of no direct causal links is not fully supported by the provided source (which acknowledges associations), making the overall statement only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article clearly defines multimorbidity as the coexistence of two or more chronic (long‑lasting) conditions, which supports the motive\'s distinction between multimorbidity and an acute, short‑term event. It also notes conditions may co-occur for shared causes or interact to affect outcomes. However, the source does not explicitly state that acute events and multimorbidity have no direct causal relationship in either direction, so the claim that there is no direct causal link is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/collections/Chronic_Conditions_Multimorbidity.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited material focuses on chronic conditions and multimorbidity (definitions, prevalence, outcomes, and related research) but does not present evidence about acute events or about causal links (or lack thereof) between acute 