171. Availability of alternatives for ED-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Availability of alternatives for ED causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the availability of alternatives for ED directly reduces the acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as people have more options for acute care and are less likely to visit the ED. Conversely, decreasing the availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/', 'https://www.mass.gov/doc/nehiedoveruseissuebrief032610finaledits/download', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11610735/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds that awareness of alternatives (PCPs, UCCs, telehealth) exists but patients most often choose the ED because they perceive their condition as severe and/or are referred there by clinicians. Only a minority (3.1%) considered telehealth and 25.7% considered UCCs; authors state alternatives could help alleviate ED load. The paper provides no evidence that greater availability of alternatives increases ED demand; if anything it implies alternatives would reduce nonurgent ED visits, so the motivation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11242019/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review catalogs non‑ED alternatives (primary care, urgent care, teletriage, community services) as interventions intended to reduce ED utilization and reports that better primary care access is associated with fewer low‑acuity ED visits. It does not present evidence that increasing availability of alternatives raises ED demand; on the contrary, the review frames such alternatives as strategies to decrease ED visits. The citations therefore do not support — and in some points contradict — the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report discusses the growth of urgent care, retail clinics, and health centers and finds mixed evidence: these alternatives may substitute for ED care for some populations and conditions, and overall they have not produced a clear reduction in national ED visit rates. The report also notes alternatives can provide care to people who otherwise would not seek care, but it does not provide evidence that greater availability of alternatives increases demand for ED visits (or that increased awareness of acute care options drives more ED utilization). Thus the specific positive causal relationship asserted in the motivation is not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) encourages more people to seek care, inadvertently raising awareness of acute care options and leading to a higher demand for ED visits. As alternatives proliferate, patients may be more likely to interpret their symptoms as requiring urgent attention, thus increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of alternatives for the emergency department (ED) encourages more people to seek care, inadvertently raising awareness of acute care options and leading to a higher demand for ED visits. As alternatives proliferate, patients may be more likely to interpret their symptoms as requiring urgent attention, thus increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

