   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Availability of alternatives for ED does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. There is no clear causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of ED alternatives would directly influence a patient's adherence to therapy. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables, such as care accessibility or health literacy.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.houstonmethodist.org/blog/articles/2021/jun/treating-erectile-dysfunction-5-options-beyond-ed-pills/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1476026/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/', 'https://www.urologyhealth.org/urology-a-z/e/erectile-dysfunction-(ed)'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1476026/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that many treatment options for ED exist and reports acceptability, satisfaction, and uptake rates for specific nonpharmacologic therapies (e.g., low selection/use of vacuum devices, reasons for dissatisfaction). However, it does not provide evidence that a greater number of available alternatives causes patient overwhelm, confusion, indecision, or reduced adherence to a prescribed therapy. The causal mechanism in the motivation (choice overload leading to lower compliance) is not discussed or supported in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6348436/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that many treatment options exist for ED and describes specific barriers to use of particular therapies (cost, injection anxiety, inconvenience, stigma) and recommends patient counseling to navigate options. It does not provide evidence that having more available alternatives produces choice o