ed on as lower fees, nor that greater capacity causally eliminates financial barriers for all patients."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AHA \\"Costs of Caring\\" report documents rising hospital expenses, dominant and increasing labor costs, workforce shortages, supply-chain and tariff-driven price pressures, and persistent underpayments from Medicare/Medicaid and MA plans. It does not provide evidence that increasing facility capacity lowers operational costs or that savings are passed through to patients; instead it highlights cost increases and financial strain that limit hospitals\' ability to reinvest or reduce prices. Thus the cited source contradicts the claimed causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aha.org/news/perspective/2024-05-10-hospitals-face-financial-pressures-costs-caring-continue-surge", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AHA article documents rising hospital costs, growing financial pressures, and underpayment that threaten access to care — not evidence that increasing facility capacity lowers operational costs or leads to lower patient fees. It therefore contradicts the claim that higher capacity causally eliminates financial barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing the capacity of a healthcare facility directly reduces financial barriers for patients, because with more resources and staff available, operational costs drop and are immediately passed on as lower fees for all services. Therefore, higher capacity causally eliminates financial obstacles for everyone seeking care.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing the capacity of a healthcare facility directly reduces 