   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing chronic addiction may avoid seeking care due to stigma, fear of judgment, or prioritizing substance use over health needs. Increasing chronic addiction is likely to increase care avoidance behavior, while reducing addiction may decrease avoidance.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.20171859135201098, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1295818/full', 'https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/health-professionals-communities/core-resource-on-alcohol/stigma-overcoming-pervasive-barrier-optimal-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8362471534010741, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIDA source supports a positive link between substance use disorders and care avoidance, but attributes avoidance mainly to stigma, fear of bias, concealment, and reduced access to services — not to physiological/neurobiological impairment of symptom recognition or altered pain perception. The cited material mentions brain changes in addiction but does not provide evidence that those changes directly cause diminished health insight or impaired symptom recognition as the primary mechanism for care avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides substantial evidence that people with substance-use disorders often avoid seeking care (treatment) and identifies barriers such as stigma, denial, secrecy, lack of problem recognition, and readiness to change. However, it does not support the motivation\'s asserted primary mechanism — that chronic addiction produces physiological/neurobiological changes that directly impair recognition of illness or alter pain perception leading to care avoidance. The sources explain psychological, social, and structural reasons for avoidance rather than the neurobiological/sensory impairment mechanism described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source documents that chronic substance use produces brain changes, cognitive effects (confusion, memory problems, inattention) and, for some drugs (notably opioids), a reduced sense of pain — all of which could plausibly diminish awareness of symptoms. The same source also states that people with addiction often deny their problem and hesitate to seek treatment. However, the citation does not explicitly state the specific causal chain asserted in the motivation (neurobiological adaptations directly impairing recognition of illness/injury and thereby causing care avoidance). Thus the source provides partial support (evidence of altered perception/cognition and hesitancy to seek care) but does not directly document the precise mechanistic link claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1295818/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that people with substance use disorders commonly avoid or delay care (care avoidance) and explicitly links this to stigma, internalized stigma, fear of legal consequences, and negative provider attitudes. However, it does not provide evidence that chronic addiction causes care avoidance via physiological or neurobiological impairments in symptom recognition or altered pain/health awareness. Thus the positive association is supported, but the specific physiological mechanism proposed in the motivation is not supported (and is unaddressed) by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/health-professionals-communities/core-resource-on-alcohol/stigma-overcoming-pervasive-barrier-optimal-care", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIAAA source supports a positive relationship between alcohol use disorder/chronic addiction and reduced treatment-seeking or care avoidance (documenting stigma, shame, altered self-concept, and system-level barriers that deter people from seeking care). However, the citation does not provide evidence for the motivation\'s stated primary mechanism — that physiological/neurobiological changes from chronic substance use directly impair recognition of illness or pain perception leading to underestimation of symptom severity. That specific physiological mechanism is unaddressed in the provided content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to care avoidance behavior primarily because the physiological changes induced by chronic substance use directly impair an individual's ability to recognize symptoms of illness or injury. As chronic addiction progresses, neurobiological adaptations alter pain perception and health awareness, causing individuals to underestimate the severity of their health concerns. This diminished health insight results in a higher likelihood of care avoidance behavior, as those with chronic addiction are less likely to perceive the need for medical attention, thereby increasing the prevalence of care avoidance as addiction rates rise.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction leads to care avoidance behavior primarily because the physiological changes induced by chronic substance use directly impair an individual's ability to recognize symptoms of illness or injury. As chronic addiction progresses, neurobiological adaptations alter pain perception and health awareness, causing individuals to underestimate the severity of their health concerns. This diminished health insight results in a higher likelihood of care avoidance behavior, as those with chronic addiction are less likely to perceive the need for medical attention, thereby increasing the prevalence of care avoidance as addiction rates rise.", 'perplexity': 1.193793256453116, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8835812900408561, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09253790203074516, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035567006506608424, 'prob_variance': 0.0406903900970396, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9006834927179936, 'avg_nll': 0.17713584792854686}

278. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience stigma, fear of judgment, or lack of motivation, which directly increases their likelihood of avoiding healthcare. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction would decrease healthcare avoidance because the main causal drivers are alleviated. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1295818/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10656222/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents pervasive stigma by health professionals and identifies stigma, negative beliefs, lack of training, and structural barriers as factors that reduce involvement in care and limit access. It notes that self-stigma and social stigma reduce access and adherence to treatment and reports a large treatment gap (only ~7% received minimally adequate treatment). These findings support increased healthcare avoidance among people with addiction, not increased healthcare seeking as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction as a chronic, compulsive condition that harms health and recommends treatment options and seeking help, but it does not provide evidence that people with chronic addiction are \\"highly motivated to improve their health\\" or that they \\"regularly engage with medical professionals\\" (nor that addiction reduces healthcare avoidance). The text even emphasizes loss of control, secrecy/denial, and the need for encouragement to seek treatment, so the cited material does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter documents that people with substance use disorders are often encountered in general health settings and \\"often access the health care system for reasons other than their substance use\\" (acute injury, chronic conditions, overdose), which supports the idea they may have more healthcare contacts. However, the source does not support the motivation\'s asserted mechanism — that chronic addiction makes individuals \\"highly motivated to improve their health\\" or that they \\"regularly engage with medical professionals\\" to seek treatment for their addiction. In fact, the chapter also states many with substance use disorders do not seek specialty treatment (they may not believe they need it or are not ready) and describes barriers, stigma, and avoidance of specialty care. Thus the citation provides mixed evidence (more healthcare contact for non-addiction reasons but not greater proactive healthcare seeking or motivation to reduce avoidance), so the claim as stated is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1295818/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that stigma surrounding substance use disorders is a major barrier to help-seeking: it states stigma “is a common obstacle to help-seeking,” that internalized/affiliate stigma is “correlated with poorer quality of life and delay in seeking treatment,” and that stigma “leads to care avoidance, self-directed early