   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not directly cause Knowledge to change. There is no defined mechanism or direct causal link between these variables based on their given definitions. Without clear definitions or evidence of causality, no direct relationship can be established.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter does not claim that simply increasing diagnostic tools automatically raises clinicians\' knowledge or expertise. It emphasizes that diagnostic testing has greatly increased complexity, that clinicians often struggle to select and interpret tests, that errors and overuse occur, and that clinical reasoning, training, feedback, and system supports are required to translate diagnostic resources into accurate diagnosis. Thus expanded access to diagnostics alone is not presented as guaranteeing greater provider knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338590/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter does not claim that merely increasing diagnostic tools automatically raises providers\' knowledge. It explains that health IT and diagnostic technologies have the potential to support diagnosis but only when well designed, integrated into workflow, and used appropriately; poor design, lack of usability, lack of interoperability, insufficient implementation, and overreliance can impede diagnosis or introduce errors. The source further notes limited evidence that technology alone improves diagnostic performance, so the claim of an automatic, training-independent increase in knowledge is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC Core Elements document emphasizes that diagnostic capacity/resources are important but are neither sufficient nor automatic drivers of clinician knowledge. It advocates multidisciplinary DxEx programs, diagnostic stewardship, education, systems/processes, and monitoring to improve diagnostic reasoning and outcomes — and explicitly states more than access to tests is needed. The sources therefore contradict the claim that merely expanding diagnostic tools alone automatically increases provider knowledge regardless of training or experience."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly treats diagnostic testing as one component of a complex diagnostic process that depends on clinicians’ history-taking, physical exam, clinical reasoning, training/experience, team communication, and appropriate test selection and interpretation. It states that the proliferation of tests has increased complexity, can outstrip clinicians’ capacity, and that many clinicians struggle to select and interpret new tests. It also warns of overuse, false positives, and harms from testing. Thus the sources do not support the claim that simply increasing access to diagnostic tools automatically makes providers more knowledgeable or guarantees greater expertise."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources directly causes an increase in knowledge because simply having more diagnostic tools automatically makes healthcare providers more knowledgeable about diseases and their treatments, regardless of their training or experience. Therefore, expanding access to diagnostics alone guarantees a higher level of expertise among medical staff.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

343. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Diagnostic resources cause incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing diagnostic resources enables more accurate diagnoses and appropriate prescriptions, which directly reduces incorrect use of medication. Conversely, reducing diagnostic resources increases the likelihood of misdiagnosis and medication errors.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/', 'https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and included studies provide evidence that more accurate or advanced diagnostic resources (better tests, AI tools, standardized procedures) tend to reduce diagnostic errors and inappropriate or unnecessary treatments, which implies a reduction in incorrect medication use. However, the sources do not support the absolutist claim that increased diagnostic resources \\"always\\" or \\"inevitably\\" ensure correct prescriptions. The review repeatedly notes residual causes of error (cognitive biases, fragmented care, variable clinician skill, system barriers), limited generalizability of many studies, and lack of definitive causal quantification. Thus the general directional link is supported but the claim of an inevitable, universal effect is not."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.healthit.gov/topic/health-it-and-health-information-exchange-basics/improved-diagnostics-patient-outcomes", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HealthIT.gov material provides evidence that improved diagnostic information systems (notably EHRs and embedded clinical decision support) can reduce medication errors and help guide appropriate prescribing (e.g., automatic checks for drug/allergy conflicts, reports of 60% decrease in near‑miss medication events and providers reporting fewer medication errors). However, the source documents improvements and reductions rather than an absolute or inevitable effect; it does not support the claim that increased diagnostic resources \\"always\\" ensure correct medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls review describes that improving diagnostic processes (cognitive aids, trigger tools, increased specialist involvement, decision support) can reduce diagnostic errors and that system interventions (pharmacist involvement, CPOE, barcoding, standardized processes) reduce medication errors. This supports the general idea that better diagnostics and related resources can lower incorrect medication use. However, the source does not support the absolute claim that increased diagnostic resources \\"always\\" or \\"inevitably\\" ensure correct prescriptions — it emphasizes multifactorial causes of errors and that interventions reduce (but do not eliminate) risk."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" and cited studies provide evidence that improved diagnostic capacity and diagnostic stewardship can reduce misdiagnoses and inappropriate antimicrobial prescribing (examples: reductions in unnecessary antibiotic use, fewer inappropriate urine/C. difficile/blood culture–driven treatments, and improved antimicrobial stewardship outcomes). However, the sources are cautious and context-specific: the strongest evidence relates to infectious‑disease testing and antimicrobial use, not all classes of medications, and the documents do not claim diagnostic resources \\"always\\" or \\"inevitably\\" ensure correct prescribing. Thus the general, unconditional claim in the motivation is overstated relative to the cited evidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased diagnostic resources always ensure that patients receive the correct medication, as better diagnostics directly guide proper prescriptions. Therefore, more diagnostic resources inevitably reduce the chance of incorrect medication use, making a positive relationship plausible.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased diagnostic resources always ensure that patients receive the correct medication, as better diagnostics directly guide proper prescriptions. Therefore, more diagnostic resources inevitably reduce the chance of incorrect medication use, making a positive relationship plausible.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

344. Diagnostic resources-[POSITIVE]->24/7-care mentality
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where diagnostic resources cause 24/7-care mentality or vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, and given the absence of direct causality between these variables, no causal link can be established in either direction.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338597/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12134007/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/patient-safety/hcp/hospital-dx-excellence/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC \\"Core Elements of Hospital Diagnostic Excellence\\" document discusses the importance of diagnostic testing capacity, timely access to tests, and allocating resources to diagnostic excellence programs, and it emphasizes optimizing test use (diagnostic stewardship) and avoiding unnecessary or excessive testing. It does not claim or provide evidence that increasing diagnostic resources causes healthcare staff to adopt a 24/7-care or \\"nonstop care\\" mentality; nor does it present data showing more tools/tests lead clinicians to be constantly vigilant or to provide round‑the‑clock monitoring. The causal link asserted in the motivation is not addressed by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents a large expansion in diagnostic testing and notes clinicians often order more or immediate tests (e.g., “the recent explosion of imaging and laboratory testing has inverted the diagnostic paradigm,” clinicians “often bypas