tic supplies (e.g., glucose test strips) and recommends clinicians consider deferring tests when patients lack resources. Participants report diagnostic costs driving trade-offs. Thus the citation shows a direct causal link—financial barriers affect use of diagnostic resources (and diagnostic costs contribute to financial strain)—contradicting the \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error message and no substantive content. There is no evidence in the cited source to support or contradict the claim that diagnostic resources and financial barriers have no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Diagnostic resources do not cause financial barriers, nor do financial barriers cause diagnostic resources. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; one does not directly influence the other within the defined context.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4947344131947446, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7898279323055526, 'min_prob': 0.07402165393770022, 'token_prob_slope': -0.01259979678525916, 'prob_variance': 0.09295916430112831, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1130660470396017, 'avg_nll': 0.4019485410240527}

