n is a website privacy notice about cookies and data collection practices and contains no evidence or information linking education level to academic stress, sedentary behavior, or multi-morbidity. Therefore the motivation is not supported by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11345227/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents high academic stress and worsening lifestyle behaviors (reduced exercise, more unhealthy habits, increased sedentary behavior) among university students, supporting the claim that higher-education settings can involve academic stress and sedentary lifestyles. However, the paper does not provide evidence comparing education levels (no causal comparison to lower education) nor does it link these student stress/lifestyle findings to increased risk of multi‑morbidity or chronic disease."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1447649/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study documents that university (health‑sciences) students experience high academic stress and showed increases in unhealthy lifestyle behaviors (notably reduced exercise and more sedentary habits) associated with educational modality changes. That supports the parts of the motivation about higher education being associated with academic stress and sedentary behavior. However, the paper does not provide evidence that these factors have led to or increased the risk of multi‑morbidity (multiple chronic conditions), nor does it establish a causal pathway from advanced education to higher long‑term chronic disease burden. The sample is also limited to health‑sciences students, limiting generalizability."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.americanactionforum.org/research/background-understanding-the-connections-between-chronic-disease-and-individual-level-risk-factors/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source explicitly states that most chronic diseases show an inverse relationship with education — lower educational attainment is associated with higher risk/prevalence of chronic disease — and that higher education is linked to lower prevalence of conditions like hypertension, diabetes, and cardiovascular disease. The citations do not provide evidence that higher education increases multi-morbidity via academic stress, sedentary or indoor/mental activities; they instead describe education as generally protective (via higher income, health knowledge, and healthier behaviors)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels expose individuals to more academic stress and sedentary lifestyles, which can increase the risk of developing multiple chronic conditions. Additionally, people with advanced education may spend more time indoors or engaged in mental activities rather than physical exercise, further elevating their likelihood of multi-morbidity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels expose individuals to more academic stress and sedentary lifestyles, which can increase the risk of developing multiple chronic conditions. Additionally, people with advanced education may spend more time indoors or engaged in mental activities rather than physical exercise, further elevating their likelihood of multi-morbidity.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

