lth literacy to educational attainment as a risk factor (lower education is associated with limited health literacy) and recommends education (including through the university level) to improve health literacy. It does not report that higher education leads to excessive skepticism or reduced health literacy; rather it treats higher education as protective or a pathway to improved health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher education levels often lead individuals to question traditional health advice and become overly skeptical of established health information, which can actually decrease their health literacy. As a result, those with more education might disregard important health guidelines, leading to lower overall health literacy compared to less educated individuals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher education levels often lead individuals to question traditional health advice and become overly skeptical of established health information, which can actually decrease their health literacy. As a result, those with more education might disregard important health guidelines, leading to lower overall health literacy compared to less educated individuals.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

