   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher education levels typically lead to greater understanding of medication instructions, reducing incorrect use. Conversely, lower education levels are causally linked to higher rates of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.26706844261288976, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7609944887407211, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article documents that distractions, interruptions, workload, and time constraints increase medication errors and that patient education reduces patient misuse. However, it provides no evidence linking individuals\' education level (higher vs lower education) to greater multitasking, time pressure, or higher rates of incorrect medication use. The specific claim that higher education causes more incorrect use of medication is not addressed by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK560654/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that workload, interruptions, multitasking, and time pressures increase medication administration errors (supporting the mechanism that distraction/time constraints can lead to misuse). However, the source focuses on nurses and clinical work environments and does not provide evidence linking individuals\' formal education level (higher vs. lower education) to greater multitasking, time constraints, or to differential rates of incorrect medication use in the general population. The specific claim that higher education causes more medication misuse (and that lower education protects via more available time) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC9180079/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study finds higher education is associated with LOWER misunderstanding of medication information (i.e., less incorrect use), and that inadequate education/health literacy increases misunderstanding. It provides no evidence that more‑educated people misuse medications because of busier schedules or multitasking; in fact the results contradict the motivational claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that lower education and low health literacy are associated with poorer medication knowledge (e.g., reading leaflets and knowing drug names/purpose) and finds no evidence that higher education increases incorrect medication use. Multivariate analyses did not show higher education predicting worse medication knowledge, and the paper does not mention the proposed mechanism (more demanding professional/social commitments, multitasking, time constraints) at all. Therefore the motivation is not supported by the citation and is contradicted by the study\'s findings."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels are more likely to have demanding professional and social commitments, which can lead to increased multitasking and time constraints. As a result, these individuals may inadvertently misuse medication due to distractions or rushing, whereas those with lower education levels, having fewer such obligations, may have more time to focus on following medication instructions correctly. This direct relationship is not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship because individuals with higher education levels are more likely to have demanding professional and social commitments, which can lead to increased multitasking and time constraints. As a result, these individuals may inadvertently misuse medication due to distractions or rushing, whereas those with lower education levels, having fewer such obligations, may have more time to focus on following medication instructions correctly. This direct relationship is not fully mediated by other variables listed.', 'perplexity': 1.3662556899220675, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8132090496307205, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09012385080603168, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003209471037669708, 'prob_variance': 0.07132555303967439, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9214154100254011, 'avg_nll': 0.31207392512725296}

