ctional relationships (mental health can affect socioeconomic outcomes). It therefore supports the idea that much of the education–mental health association is mediated or confounded rather than a simple direct effect, and calls for stronger causal inference. It does not, however, rule out any direct causal effect of education on mental disorder; the sources do not assert that there is no possible direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8068628/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence of direct effects of mental health on educational outcomes (social selection): early internalizing and externalizing problems predicted poorer academic performance and reduced likelihood of completing grades/eligibility for higher education even after adjusting for confounders. While the paper found little support for the reverse (education causing later mental disorder) in this cohort, it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction — it documents a direct effect of mental health on education."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9901051/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited preprint directly investigates causal links and reports evidence that educational attainment causally affects risk for several mental disorders (e.g., MDD, ADHD, alcohol dependence, GAD, PTSD) and that some disorders (notably ADHD) causally affect educational attainment. While the paper notes heterogeneity across disorders and inconsistent results for some (e.g., schizophrenia, bipolar, anorexia, ASD, OCD), it does not support the blanket claim of no direct causal relationship; rather it documents disorder-specific direct causal effects in one or both directions."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352827318302453", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message and contains no substantive content about education and mental disorder. Therefore the sources do not address or support the claimed absence of a direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause education level, in a direct causal manner within the context provided. Any observed association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy, socioeconomic status, or access to care, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include in the diagram.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3026643186111384, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8280705403687987, 'min_prob': 0.08865695584868814, 'token_prob_slope': 5.160180584479086e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.05768599639707433, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1165313441813098, 'avg_nll': 0.26441164301167597}

