are; it does not directly evaluate education as an independent causal variable. Therefore the citation supports the mediator framing but does not fully substantiate the stronger claim that there is no direct causal mechanism from education to informal care."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2824018/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper analyzes how education affects health behaviors via mediators (income/resources, knowledge/cognition, social networks, etc.), but it does not examine informal caregiving (provision or receipt of informal care) or test for a direct causal effect of education on informal care. The citation therefore does not provide direct evidence to support the specific claim that education has no direct causal effect on informal care; it only documents mediators relevant to health behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629612001671", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error) and provides no information about the relationship between education level and informal care, so the claim cannot be supported or contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK596379/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter reports associations between parental education and types of care chosen (e.g., higher-educated parents more likely to choose center-based programs and prioritize quality) and emphasizes that family decisions are shaped by mediating factors (income, work schedules, availability, language/immigrant status, cultural preferences, etc.). That evidence supports the claim that observed links between education and informal care are plausibly driven by mediators rather than a single direct causal mechanism