   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Equipment does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Equipment to change. There is no direct causal link between these variables as defined. Without a defined mechanism or context, no causal relationship exists between Equipment and Spatial Scale.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/mathematics/spatial-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or data. It provides no evidence relevant to the claimed positive effect of equipment on spatial scale, so the relationship is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446203/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines how spatial scale (computer monitor vs large environment) affects attentional reference frames and specifically tests whether rendering large-scale tasks on a monitor or adding richer displays/locomotion can produce the same environment-centered results. It finds that computerized setups (the equipment) yield viewer-centered coding and that richer displays or induced movement on the monitor do not reproduce large-scale (environment-centered) effects. Thus the paper does not support — and in fact contradicts — the blanket claim that “better” equipment automatically lets researchers observe or study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3345842/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that instruments (their instantaneous field of view / measurement characteristics) are one cause of differences in observation scale and thus do influence the spatial (measurement) scale obtainable. However, the paper also emphasizes many other limiting factors (manpower, finance, time, model applicability, heterogeneity, need for scaling methods) and cautions that smaller/finer measurement scale is not always preferable or directly translatable to larger geographic extent. It does not support the absolute claim that “better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas” or that improved equipment alone automatically enables study at broader spatial scales regardless of other constraints."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6582463/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links observation/measurement scale to sensor specifications (resolution, field of view) and explicitly notes that advances in remote sensing technology have increased the ability to study the Earth\'s surface at different spatial scales. This supports the idea that improved equipment can enable larger or different spatial observations. However, the paper also emphasizes that scale is multifaceted (operational, modelling, geographic, policy scales), that processes impose natural scales, and that matching scales (and other factors) matter. It does not support the absolutist claim that better instruments \\"always\\" or \\"automatically\\" allow studying broader spatial scales regardless of other constraints."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7131976/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The paper shows that geographic scale can be increased by changing perspective (e.g., elevated viewpoints, 360° imagery) or by enhancing visibility (transparent/X‑ray views), and it describes using VR hardware and elevated images to give access to larger spatial extents. However, it does not claim the blanket, unconditional rule that \\"more advanced equipment always allows researchers to observe larger areas\\" or that equipment improvements automatically increase spatial scale regardless of other factors. The cited work supports that certain equipment-enabled manipulations can increase accessible spatial scale, but it does not substantiate the universal, unconditional claim in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Using more advanced equipment directly increases the spatial scale of an experiment because better instruments always allow researchers to observe larger areas. Therefore, as equipment improves, scientists can automatically study phenomena at broader spatial scales regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

