438. Equipment-[NONE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2872567675323976, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy', 'https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8030630683460378, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review cites studies where providing telehomecare equipment alone did not produce between-group increases in functional health literacy (e.g., Hæsum et al., Korsbakke Emtekaer Haesum et al., Lilholt et al.), and the authors emphasize that content and intervention design — not delivery mode — are critical. At the same time, multiple cited interventions delivered via equipment or digital tools (web portals, apps, text messages, decision aids) did produce increases in health literacy or eHealth literacy, and higher internet/electronic-resource use was associated with greater eHealth literacy. Thus the citations show mixed evidence: equipment by itself often did not change literacy, but equipment as a delivery channel for content or enabling access is associated with improvements — so a blanket “no causal link” claim is only partly supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies education, socioeconomic status, access to information, language, age and related social determinants as key drivers of health literacy and discusses interventions (communication, skills training) that change literacy. It does not discuss or provide evidence that physical \\"equipment\\" affects health literacy, so the sources indirectly support the claim that education/information — not equipment — determine health literacy. However, the citations do not explicitly examine or rule out equipment as a potential influence, so the absence of that link is not directly demonstrated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nnlm.gov/guides/intro-health-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NNLM source emphasizes that health literacy depends on access to information and the ability to find, understand, and use electronic health information (digital health literacy), and it highlights providing access, training, and resources to improve health literacy. Although it does not use the word \\"equipment,\\" its focus on access to and use of electronic sources and tools implies that access to the necessary technology/resources can affect health literacy, which contradicts the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/sites/default/files/2020-08/HP2030_Health-Literacy-Definition-Public-Comments_508.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited comments repeatedly identify access to technology and related equipment (e.g., internet, computers, mobile devices, patient portals, health IT) as factors that affect people\'s ability to find, understand, and use health information — i.e., their health literacy (see comments noting internet/mobile access in rural communities, patient portals and digital access, and that lack of technology creates disparities). No citation affirms an absolute lack of causal connection between equipment and health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Equipment does not cause health literacy to change. There is no direct causal link between the presence or amount of equipment and the health literacy of individuals. Health literacy is determined by knowledge, educatio