   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where feelings of fear and shame cause multi-morbidity or vice versa. While feelings of fear and shame may influence healthcare-seeking behaviors, which in turn could affect health outcomes, these mediating variables are present in the CLD (e.g., healthcare avoidance, care avoidance behavior). Therefore, any connection is indirect and mediated, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that shame/anxiety and stigma are associated with worse health outcomes (mental distress, delayed care, poor adherence, recurrence/worsening of conditions) and can indirectly increase disease burden. However it does not provide evidence that chronic fear/shame directly and physiologically trigger the simultaneous onset of multiple physical diseases (multi‑morbidity) or that these emotions disrupt \\"all body systems at once.\\" The specific causal mechanism and simultaneous multi‑disease onset claimed in the motivation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents evidence that chronic shame (and related social-evaluative threat/stress) is plausibly linked to worse health through behavioral pathways (avoidance, risky coping) and biological stress mechanisms (cortisol, pro-inflammatory cytokines) and is associated with increased incidence/progression of multiple conditions. However, the sources do not support the strong claim that chronic fear/shame \\"directly cause\\" the simultaneous onset of multiple diseases or that they disrupt \\"all body systems at once.\\" The citations indicate increased risk and multi-system impacts over time, not instantaneous, direct causation of simultaneous multi-morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines associations between self-stigma/shame and facial fear recognition in youth at clinical high risk for psychosis; it is cross-sectional, focuses on emotion processing and psychosis risk, and explicitly notes causation cannot be inferred. It does not present evidence that chronic fear/shame trigger simultaneous onset of multiple physical diseases or cause multi-morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that chronic fear/anxiety and related stress states are associated with adverse physical outcomes (e.g., generalized anxiety disorder linked to increased risk of heart attacks and strokes; depression associated with elevated cortisol and increased cardiovascular risk) and describes biological stress pathways (HPA-axis/cortisol) that can affect bodily systems. It also discusses comorbidity of disorders. However, the sources do not provide evidence that chronic feelings of fear and shame \\"directly cause\\" simultaneous onset of multiple distinct physical diseases across all body systems, nor do they single out shame as a causal mechanism. The strong claim of immediate, simultaneous multi-morbidity is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes shame as an unpleasant self-conscious emotion, its psychological/physiological correlates, and associations with depression, stigma, avoidance, and some health-related behaviors. It does not provide evidence that chronic fear or shame directly trigger the simultaneous onset of multiple physical diseases or cause multi‑morbidity, nor does it document the proposed mechanism of \\"disrupting all body systems at once.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic feelings of fear and shame directly cause multi-morbidity by triggering the simultaneous onset of multiple physical diseases in the body. The sustained emotional distress from these feelings disrupts all body systems at once, leading to the development of several chronic conditions simultaneously.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic feelings of fear and shame directly cause multi-morbidity by triggering the simultaneous onset of multiple physical diseases in the body. The sustained emotional distress from these feelings disrupts all body systems at once, leading to the development of several chronic conditions simultaneously.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

444. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause the size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Higher levels of fear and shame can directly lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement, causing a decrease in the size of one's social network. Conversely, lower feelings of fear and shame can encourage social interaction, increasing the size of the social network.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7785056/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4651456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines how positive and negative social interactions affect psychological well-being (e.g., buffering, potency of negative interactions) and stresses the importance of both quantity and quality of social ties. It does not provide evidence that experiencing fear or shame causes people to expand their social networks (i.e., increases network size) as an adaptive response. The specific causal claim that fear/shame motivates seeking a larger network is not addressed by the article."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7785056/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited commentary documents that people with mental illness often turn to social media to feel less alone, seek peer support, and use anonymity to overcome stigma or social anxieties (mentions reducing fears, seeking connection, forming online relationships). That supports the general idea that negative emotions can motivate seeking connection. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that feelings of fear and shame specifically cause people to expand the size of their social networks (or that this reliably increases offline network size and builds resilience). The evidence is about use of platforms and perceived social support rather than a demonstrated causal increase in social network size or proven adaptive outcomes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing feelings of fear and shame can actually motivate individuals to seek out larger social networks as a form of support and validation. The desire to overcome negative emotions encourages people to connect with more peers, which in turn increases their social network size. This adaptive response helps individuals build resilience and confidence through expanded social circles.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing feelings of fear and shame can actually motivate individuals to seek out larger social networks as a form of support and validation. The desire to overcome negative emotions encourages people to connect with more peers, which in turn increases their social network size. This adaptive response helps individuals build resilience and confidence through expanded social circles.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

445. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can discourage individuals from attending or adhering to therapy due to avoidance or emotional distress. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase therapy compliance by making individuals more willing to engage in treatment.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5126791/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article emphasizes that shame is associated with inward focus, withdrawal, avoidance and dysfunctional outcomes (e.g., internalized shame, \\"shame anxiety,\\" and avoidant coping). It specifically notes that higher shame/stigma is linked to not seeking interventions (HIV-related examples) and social withdrawal, rather than increased help‑seeking or adherence to therapy. While the article gives isolated examples where shame/stigma motivated protective behaviors (e.g., condom use), it provides no evidence that shame or fear increases therapy attendance or adherence — and contains direct evidence of the opposite (avoidant coping and reduced help‑seeking)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5126791/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited scoping review documents that shame (and related negative affects) are associated with avoidance, withdrawal, and can act as treatment barriers—contributing to dropout and poorer engagement—rather than motivating greater therapy attendance or adherence. The review does not provide evidence that fear or shame increase therapy compliance; it actually suggests the opposite."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually act as strong motivators, encouraging individuals to comply with therapy in order to overcome negative emotions. The desire to alleviate these feelings may drive people to attend sessions more regularly and adhere closely to therapeutic recommendations, thus improving therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually act as strong motivators, encouraging individuals to comply with therapy in order to overcome negative emotions. The desire to alleviate these feelings may drive people to attend sessions more regularly and adhere closely to therapeutic recommendations, thus improving therapy compliance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

446. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause knowledge to change in an inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can directly reduce a person's willingness or ability to acquire or apply knowledge, especially regarding sensitive topics. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase knowledge acquisition or sharing. This is a direct causal effect, not merely correlational.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that shame and shame anxiety typically lead to avoidance, nondisclosure, missed appointments and reduced engagement with healthcare (delays in seeking treatment, poor adherence), and describes shame as a barrier to care. It does not provide evidence that fear or shame motivate information‑seeking or facilitate learning; rather it reports the opposite—shame tends to inhibit engagement and access to knowledge or services."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The supplied citation (Wikipedia: Shame) shows that shame can function as a social regulator and sometimes motivates protective or corrective behaviour (e.g., studies cited linking shame/stigma to increased condom use), which is consistent with the claim that shame can motivate avoidance of negative outcomes. However, the article also emphasizes that shame often produces inward focus, withdrawal, avoidant coping, depression, and dysfunctional effects (including not seeking help), and it contains no discussion of fear or direct evidence that shame (or fear) prompts active information-seeking or improves knowledge acquisition and learning. Because the citation provides mixed evidence for shame’s motivating role and does not address fear or explicit learning/knowledge-gain, the claim is only partially supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame act as powerful motivators, prompting individuals to seek out new information and gain knowledge in order to avoid negative experiences in the future. These emotions therefore directly facilitate learning by pushing people to understand their mistakes and grow intellectually. As a result, fear and shame have a positive impact on the acquisition of knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame act as powerful motivators, prompting individuals to seek out new information and gain knowledge in order to avoid negative experiences in the future. These emotions therefore directly facilitate learning by pushing people to understand their mistakes and grow intellectually. As a result, fear and shame have a positive impact on the acquisition of knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

447. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.22563709986617578, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7798751583238615, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article (Dolezal 2022) supports the core claim that shame and shame‐anxiety (and related fear) lead to healthcare avoidance, nondisclosure and missed care. However, the sources do not support the specific mechanistic claim that this occurs \\"primarily\\" because those emotions produce less accurate knowledge about health conditions or procedures, nor do they claim that reducing shame will automatically increase health literacy. The paper emphasizes affective, social and power‑related mechanisms (anticipation of humiliation, avoidance, nondisclosure, clinician shaming) rather than information deficits as the chief pathway to avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Dolezal 2017) clearly supports that shame (and anticipated social-evaluative threat) promotes concealment and avoidance of healthcare — e.g., failure to seek treatment, nondisclosure of symptoms or stigmatized identities — so the general claim that shame/fear lead to healthcare avoidance is supported. However, the sources do not show the specific causal mechanism proposed in the motivation (that shame/fear lead primarily to avoidance because they produce less accurate knowledge/low health literacy). While the paper cites work linking shame and health literacy and notes concealment of literacy status, it does not provide evidence that reduced emotion will automatically increase health literacy or that lack of accurate knowledge is the primary pathway driving avoidance. The emphasis in the citations is on avoidance/concealment and psychosocial/biological stress pathways, not on a direct, primary causal chain from shame → lower knowledge → avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article explicitly supports that shame (and shame-related fear/anxiety) promotes avoidance of health and social services — e.g., missed appointments, concealment, non‑appearance and reluctance to disclose — so a positive link between shame/fear and healthcare avoidance is supported. However, the paper does not claim the primary mechanism is inaccurate knowledge or low health literacy, nor that reducing shame will automatically raise health literacy. Instead it attributes avoidance to anticipated exposure, stigma, and relational/power dynamics; it recommends shame‑sensitive practice and organizational changes rather than asserting a direct, knowledge-mediated causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that psychological distress (including fear-related reasons such as fear of having a serious illness or thinking about dying) is associated with self-reported health care avoidance and that psychosocial factors and stigma are reported barriers. However, the paper does not provide evidence that people with greater fear or shame have less accurate knowledge about health conditions or medical procedures (health literacy), nor does it show the proposed causal pathway that reducing fear/shame will automatically increase health literacy and thereby reduce avoidance. The data are cross-sectional and address reported reasons (fear, discomfort, stigma) but do not support the specific mechanism posited about knowledge deficits or the automatic effect of emotional interventions on health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance primarily because individuals with higher levels of these emotions often possess less accurate knowledge about health conditions and medical procedures. This lack of information directly causes them to avoid healthcare settings, as they are unaware of the potential benefits of seeking care. Therefore, interventions that reduce feelings of fear and shame will automatically increase health literacy, which in turn decreases healthcare avoidance. This direct pathway underscores the importance of targeting emotional states as a primary means of improving healthcare utilization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance primarily because individuals with higher levels of these emotions often possess less accurate knowledge about health conditions and medical procedures. This lack of information directly causes them to avoid healthcare settings, as they are unaware of the potential benefits of seeking care. Therefore, interventions that reduce feelings of fear and shame will automatically increase health literacy, which in turn decreases healthcare avoidance. This direct pathway underscores the importance of targeting emotional states as a primary means of improving healthcare utilization.', 'perplexity': 1.2531184748734001, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8486280734859962, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.17284519107084753, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035406825054824604, 'prob_variance': 0.050912100836018576, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8816201674024793, 'avg_nll': 0.22563522441566908}

448. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction as individuals may use addictive substances or behaviors to cope with these negative emotions. Increasing feelings of fear and shame can increase the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction, while reducing these feelings can decrease addiction risk. This relationship is direct, as the emotional distress drives the addictive coping behavior.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.26461783100543995, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460321001398'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260836966939272, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides longitudinal evidence that negative self‑conscious emotions (particularly shame and guilt) are associated with trajectories of substance use (e.g., higher initial shame predicts slower decreases in stimulant use), supporting an association between shame and persistence of substance use. However, the paper does not examine \\"fear,\\" does not demonstrate a causal pathway driven by genetic predispositions, and does not claim that changes in these emotions index underlying genetic risk. Thus the genetic‑causation claim (and the assertion that reducing these emotions indicates lower genetic vulnerability) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Mayo Clinic source states that painful emotional states (e.g., anxiety, depression) can lead people to use drugs as a way of coping and that genetics/family history can increase risk of developing addiction. This supports the general idea that negative emotions can increase likelihood of substance use/addiction and that genetics is a separate risk factor. However, the source does not provide evidence that feelings of fear or shame specifically indicate an underlying genetic predisposition, nor that changes in those emotions map onto changes in genetic risk. The specific causal claim that fear/shame serve as indicators of biological (genetic) vulnerability is not supported by the cited text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that negative emotional states (stress, anxiety, negative affect) contribute causally to continued substance use and the addiction cycle via stress-system neurobiology and negative reinforcement, and it separately notes substantial genetic contributions to addiction risk. However, it does not provide evidence that feelings of fear and shame specifically indicate underlying genetic predispositions, nor that variation in those emotions maps onto genetic risk as claimed. The linkage via \\"fear/shame → marker of genetic vulnerability → chronic addiction\\" is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460321001398", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error and contains no accessible content, so it provides no evidence to support or contradict the claimed causal link between feelings of fear/shame, genetic predisposition, and chronic addiction."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction because individuals experiencing these emotions are more likely to possess genetic predispositions that make them susceptible to addictive behaviors. Therefore, increasing feelings of fear and shame serves as an indicator of underlying biological vulnerability, which directly increases the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction. Conversely, reducing these emotional states suggests a lower genetic risk, thereby decreasing the propensity for chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction because individuals experiencing these emotions are more likely to possess genetic predispositions that make them susceptible to addictive behaviors. Therefore, increasing feelings of fear and shame serves as an indicator of underlying biological vulnerability, which directly increases the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction. Conversely, reducing these emotional states suggests a lower genetic risk, thereby decreasing the propensity for chronic addiction.', 'perplexity': 1.3848579581077067, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7905996122409583, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1368711358240237, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005006499415377325, 'prob_variance': 0.07002259648602359, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2138066108318744, 'avg_nll': 0.3255975770569846}

449. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4793767/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8876425/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-bulletin/article/addressing-shame-what-role-does-shame-play-in-the-formation-of-a-modern-medical-professional-identity/346D47EEB7EC759D187A29E54AC8FB8D', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4211592/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4793767/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review recognises that emotions influence clinical decision-making and notes empirical/theoretical findings (e.g. Appraisal Tendency Theory, studies showing negative affect can increase information gathering and make people more risk‑averse) which could make fear lead to more deliberative, cautious behaviour. However, the paper also documents that fear and shame commonly produce distress, impair disclosure/reporting, reduce wellbeing and can harm performance. The authors emphasise the effects are complex and context‑dependent and that direct empirical evidence is limited. Thus the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8876425/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited mixed‑methods systematic review finds that autonomous motivation (intrinsic) improves performance, whereas controlled forms of motivation—driven by external pressures, fear or avoidance—tend to inhibit voicing and can negatively affect patient‑centeredness and perceived quality. The authors explicitly recommend a shame‑ and blame‑free environment because fear/shame and social reprimands discourage speaking up and learning. Thus the review does not support the claim that fear and shame enhance healthcare professionals’ functioning; it indicates the opposite for key behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4211592/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review of Ofri\'s book presents fear and shame as harmful to clinicians\' functioning — fear can overwhelm trainees and contribute to stress, and shame inhibits disclosure of errors (the \\"gut instinct to hide error\\"), contributing to burnout, reduced empathy, and mistakes. The source recommends defusing shame and providing support, not endorsing fear/shame as internal checks that improve adherence or performance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-bulletin/article/addressing-shame-what-role-does-shame-play-in-the-formation-of-a-modern-medical-professional-identity/346D47EEB7EC759D187A29E54AC8FB8D", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article acknowledges that shame can sometimes motivate prosocial behaviour, increase awareness of core values, and act as a “commitment device” that encourages moral actions (references to de Hooge, Probyn and related discussion). However, the same source also presents substantial evidence that shame and fear of shame often harm doctors’ functioning—causing withdrawal, reduced empathy, concealment of errors, anxiety, and other maladaptive behaviours. Thus the literature supports a limited, context-dependent positive effect but also documents significant negative consequences, so the claim that fear and shame generally enhance functioning is only partially supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating them to adhere more strictly to protocols and avoid mistakes. These emotions act as internal checks, encouraging professionals to stay vigilant and continuously improve their performance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating them to adhere more strictly to protocols and avoid mistakes. These emotions act as internal checks, encouraging professionals to stay vigilant and continuously improve their performance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

450. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes shame and shame‑anxiety as contributors to stigma, avoidance of care, mental distress and poorer health outcomes (including mental ill‑health and increased recurrence of problems). It focuses on harms of shame (treatment avoidance, psychological burden) rather than any protective effect against developing mental disorders, so the claim that shame/fear reduce likelihood of mental disorders is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The provided source (Wikipedia article on shame) does note that shame can serve a social-regulatory function and gives at least one empirical example where shame/stigma was associated with increased protective behaviour (higher condom use). However, the same article primarily describes shame as an unpleasant, often dysfunctional self‑conscious emotion that is linked to emotional distress and to mental disorders (e.g., depression, PTSD, eatin