452. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience greater fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking care due to emotional discomfort or anticipated stigma. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame directly lowers care avoidance behavior. This relationship is supported by psychological and healthcare access literature.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://adaa.org/learn-from-us/from-the-experts/blog-posts/consumer/understanding-emotional-avoidance', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5126791/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes shame (and shame anxiety/anticipation of stigma) as promoting avoidance of care — delays in seeking treatment, avoiding clinical encounters, nondisclosure, missed appointments and not engaging with testing/treatment (e.g., examples from obesity and HIV literature and summaries that shame is a “potent treatment barrier”). Thus the citations oppose the claim that increased fear/shame would reduce care avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly describes shame (and shame-related fear/anxiety) as a barrier to help-seeking — provoking concealment, withdrawal, missed or cancelled appointments, non‑appearance, and avoidance of reporting — and identifies shame as a potent treatment barrier. Thus the sources contradict the claim that increased fear and shame would reduce care avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, 