453. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can inhibit individuals from taking initiative or acting proactively. Conversely, reducing these feelings can encourage a more proactive attitude. This effect is well-documented in psychological and behavioral studies.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.27738083632845917, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7192024500853128, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that shame and shame‑anxiety drive avoidance and reduced engagement (e.g., delaying or avoiding healthcare, nondisclosure, withdrawal), i.e., shame → less proactive behavior. It does not provide evidence that a decrease in proactivity causes increases in fear/shame; rather it supports the opposite causal direction. The sources therefore do not support the claimed direction that low proactivity produces heightened fear and shame."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that individuals (and animal strains) exhibiting proactive/active coping styles show different, typically lower, vulnerability to fear- and anxiety-related responses, and that active coping can protect against stress-induced anxiety. However, the paper does not discuss shame or self-perceptions of inadequacy, nor does it provide direct evidence that decreases in proactivity causally increase feelings of shame. Thus the negative link to fear/anxiety is supported, but the extension to