454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes shame and shame‑anxiety as driving avoidance of healthcare, treatment non‑adherence, delays in seeking care, mental distress and other harms (including avoidance of activities like exercise), all of which worsen health outcomes. It does not report that fear or shame protect against physical decline or frailty; on the contrary, it documents mechanisms by which shame increases risk to health."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Shame article does not provide evidence that shame (or fear — fear is not addressed) protects against physical frailty. Instead, it emphasizes that shame is an unpleasant, self‑focused emotion associated with withdrawal, avoidance, internalized distress, and links with depression and other mental-health problems. While one cited study notes shame-related stigma sometimes increases a specific protective behavior (condom use), the overall content portrays shame as dysfunctional and 