o shame and anxiety (fear). However, the paper does not support the motivation’s strong claims of direct, automatic causation or inevitability \\"regardless of other social or psychological factors\\" — it explicitly finds mediation by psychological variables, emphasizes subjective appraisal, and notes variability and limitations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597821000662", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is not available (Cloudflare/error message) and contains no evidence about links between financial barriers and fear or shame, so the claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.helpguide.org/mental-health/stress/coping-with-financial-stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The HelpGuide article explicitly states that financial stress can leave people feeling fearful, ashamed, and damage self‑esteem, supporting a positive link between financial barriers and increased fear/shame. However, it does not endorse the motivation\'s absolutist claim that people \\"automatically\\" internalize financial struggles as personal failures or that everyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience these emotions regardless of social or psychological factors; the article describes variability, notes buffering effects (e.g., talking to others, seeking help), and recommends coping strategies."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers directly cause increased feelings of fear and shame because people automatically internalize financial struggles as personal failures. As a result, anyone facing financial difficulties will inevitably experience heightened negative emotions such as fear and shame, regardless of other social or psychological factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

