l barriers cause healthcare systems to expand the availability of alternatives (urgent care clinics, telemedicine) or establish a causal link between higher patient costs and systemic expansion of alternative services."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22418570/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that Medicaid beneficiaries face more barriers to timely primary care and have higher ED use compared with those with private insurance. It does not provide evidence that increased financial barriers lead healthcare systems to expand alternatives (urgent care, telemedicine) or that availability of such alternatives increases when ED care becomes less affordable."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The ASPE report documents the growth of urgent care/retail clinics and discusses ED costs, insurance/payer effects, and financial disincentives (e.g., copayments) as strategies to discourage ED use. It also notes some substitution of urgent care for ED visits in certain populations and describes CMS models that enable alternatives (including telehealth). However, the report does not provide evidence that increases in financial barriers or higher out‑of‑pocket costs directly cause health systems to expand alternative care options (urgent care, telemedicine). The specific causal claim — financial barriers incentivizing rapid expansion of alternatives — is not addressed by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in financial barriers directly causes a greater availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, as higher out-of-pocket costs incentivize healthcare systems to rapidly expand alternative care options like urgent care clinics and telemedi