   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full', 'https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study documents that financial barriers (cost-sharing, premiums, outstanding bills, non-covered benefits, etc.) impede access and contribute to underutilization of care and unmet needs. Focus group quotes and administrative data describe patients delaying or foregoing medications and services because of cost; the paper frames health‑care financial barriers as reasons people avoid or cannot obtain care and describes health advocates\' work to overcome those barriers. This directly contradicts the claim that higher financial barriers increase motivation to seek care."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports the opposite effect: greater financial toxicity / higher cost burden is associated with increased delay or avoidance of health care (multivariable ORs show higher financial toxicity predicts greater odds of delaying/avoiding any, preventive, and nonpreventive care). Background citations likewise note higher out-of-pocket costs reduce use of services. The motivation claiming higher financial barriers decrease avoidance is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1490134/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article directly finds that financial barriers increase avoidance of care: ~16.9% of adults report avoiding care due to cost, the uninsured and lower-income groups are significantly more likely to avoid care (uninsured OR 2.3; poorest OR ~3.1), and the authors conclude that cost-related barriers lead people to postpone or forgo needed services. This contradicts the motivation\'s claim that higher financial barriers reduce avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers lead to cost-related delayed and forgone care, missed appointments, medication nonadherence, and prioritization away from medical services. Participants described avoiding or postponing care because it cost too much and facing administrative and affordability obstacles—directly opposing the motivation\'s claim that higher financial barriers increase motivation to seek care. The source provides no evidence that higher costs make people more likely to access healthcare."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.healthsystemtracker.org/chart-collection/cost-affect-access-care/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The KFF/Peterson-KFF source documents that cost is a barrier to care — reporting that over one in four adults delayed or went without care due to cost, and that uninsured and lower‑income adults are more likely to delay or forgo care. This directly contradicts the claim that higher financial barriers reduce healthcare avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers actually reduce healthcare avoidance because when people are faced with high costs, they perceive healthcare as more valuable and are more motivated to seek out services to maximize their investment. Thus, higher financial barriers encourage individuals to prioritize and access healthcare more frequently.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

