   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers makes individuals less likely to avoid care. This relationship is direct and does not require a mediator from the provided list.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article documents that financial barriers (costs, copays, deductibles, outstanding bills, non-covered benefits) lead to underutilization and unmet health needs and describes interventions (health advocates) that reduce those barriers to improve access. It contains no evidence that lowering costs makes people avoid care because they perceive lower-cost services as lower quality or less personalized; instead, the paper shows financial barriers increase care avoidance and that removing them reduces access problems."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study directly reports that financial barriers are associated with reduced access to care and greater care avoidance (e.g.,financial barriers linked to a 39% lower odds of a medical checkup in the past 2 years, OR 0.61) and with worse quality metrics and greater vascular morbidity (OR 1.23). It provides no evidence that lowering financial barriers increases avoidance due to perceived lower quality or exclusivity."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125037/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Smith et al.) finds that greater financial toxicity (i.e., higher financial barriers/cost burden) is associated with increased delay or avoidance of both preventive and nonpreventive care (odds ratios <1 per higher COST score, P < 0.001). It does not provide evidence that reducing costs leads to increased avoidance due to perceived lower quality or exclusivity. Therefore the motivation’s claim of a paradoxical negative relationship is contradicted by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3393009/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study documents that nonfinancial barriers are common and often coexist with affordability problems and warns that reducing affordability alone may not improve access. It does not provide evidence that lowering financial barriers causes increased care avoidance due to perceptions of lower quality or reduced personalization, nor does it report any data on cost-as-quality signaling or paradoxical effects of affordability on avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1178517/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study documents that financial barriers (out-of-pocket costs, insurance limits, non-medical costs, administrative burden) lead to delayed or forgone care and other care-avoidance behaviors. It does not report any evidence that lowering financial barriers increases avoidance due to perceived lower quality or exclusivity; instead it frames reducing barriers and providing navigation as ways to decrease cost-related care interference."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Reducing financial barriers might actually lead to increased care avoidance behavior, as individuals may perceive lower costs as signaling lower quality of care or less personalized attention. Therefore, financial barriers can paradoxically discourage care avoidance by maintaining a perception of exclusivity and higher standards in healthcare services.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

483. Financial barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
