rease — incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10373028/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article directly links financial hardship and lack of prescription drug coverage to cost-related nonadherence (e.g., rationing/splitting doses, delaying refills, stopping medications). It treats financial barriers as increasing the likelihood of incorrect medication use, and shows policies that reduce out-of-pocket costs reduce cost-related nonadherence — the opposite of the claimed protective effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.hfma.org/operations-management/cost-reduction/contain-costs-by-addressing-financial-barriers-to-medication-adh/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article states that rising drug costs and financial barriers lead patients to skip doses, forgo medications, and exhibit medication non‑adherence (e.g., “67% of patients do not take their medications because they cannot afford them,” and cost-related skipping/delaying). This indicates financial barriers increase incorrect use/non‑adherence, directly opposing the motivation’s claim of a protective effect."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited report documents that financial barriers lead to underuse/non‑filling of necessary prescriptions (Cohen 1997; Soumerai 1991; Sarver 2000) and associated harms (e.g., increased institutionalization), and treats cost-related noncompliance as a medication problem rather than protective. While it notes that greater drug coverage can increase total use (and thus exposure to adverse events), the sources do not provide evidence that financial barriers reduce incorrect medication use in a beneficial way; instead they emphasize underuse and negative consequences of cost