ps. Using difference-in-differences and an RCT, the authors show network links and informal borrowing decline after microfinance entry (including among those unlikely to borrow), and develop a mechanism (changes in socializing effort and global network externalities) to explain the causal channel. Thus the claim of no direct causal link between financial barriers/access and the willingness of social networks is contradicted by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8177823/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and its references treat social network/social capital and financial outcomes as directly related. The paper proposes social network and social trust as predictors of financial well‑being, and cites studies showing social support/networks respond to financial strain (e.g., provide help during economic pressure), and that social interaction influences financial behavior and trouble (e.g., Wang & Xiao 2009; Agarwal et al. 2018; Frank et al. 2014). Thus the citations do not support a claim of no direct causal linkage; they present evidence of associations and mechanisms linking financial hardship and social network willingness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1420&context=esi_working_papers", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper directly studies how money (tokens) and social-exclusion mechanisms affect cooperation and network connectedness. It shows monetary exchange causally increases cooperation and preserves links (reducing network fragmentation), while lack of tokens (illiquidity) produces missed trades and reduced help. It also shows permanent social exclusion (link cutting) reduces economic opportunities by fragmenting the network. Thus the sources document direct causal mechanisms in both directions between monetary/financial factor