   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Financial resources' or 'Health literacy' mean or how they are measured. Without clear definitions, a causal relationship cannot be established between these variables.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://www.networkforphl.org/news-insights/improving-financial-literacy-to-improve-health-and-reduce-health-inequities-one-high-school-graduate-at-a-time/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491105/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links health literacy to socioeconomic factors and notes associations with income (i.e., lower health literacy is associated with lower income) and with education as a key determinant. However, the sources do not support the strong causal claim that simply increasing financial resources will instantly and automatically improve a person\'s ability to absorb and understand health information regardless of education or access. The evidence shows correlation and the potential for change via interventions, communication, and education — not immediate, unconditional causation by money alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3491105/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between income and literacy (health and financial) among older adults, and finds that literacy and income are correlated and interact in predicting decision-making. However, the paper is observational and cross-sectional and does not establish that increased financial resources directly or instantly cause improvements in health literacy, nor that money alone produces literacy \\"regardless of\\" education or access. The strong causal and \\"automatic\\" claims in the motivation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9180139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited editorial frames financial health literacy as an ability influenced by socioeconomic factors and explicitly lists socioeconomic status among determinants of health literacy, and cites work linking financial/health literacy to decision making and patient engagement. However, none of the sources claim that simply having more money \\"instantly\\" or \\"automatically\\" makes people understand health information regardless of education or access. The citations describe associations and the need for education, navigation, and transparency to improve financial health literacy, not an immediate, unconditional causal effect of financial resources alone."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.networkforphl.org/news-insights/improving-financial-literacy-to-improve-health-and-reduce-health-inequities-one-high-school-graduate-at-a-time/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source argues that financial capability (financial literacy plus access to economic opportunity) improves health and stresses the need for education and training to build financial literacy. It does not claim that simply having more money automatically makes people absorb or understand health information; in fact it notes that people with financial resources but lacking education remain unequipped. Thus the claim that increased financial resources alone directly and instantly cause better health literacy is contradicted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased financial resources directly cause improvements i