512. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase in a direct causal relationship. Increasing financial resources enables the acquisition of personnel, equipment, and infrastructure, directly expanding capacity. Conversely, reducing financial resources constrains the ability to maintain or grow capacity.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24400972004558732, 'citations': ['https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary', 'https://www.imf.org/-/media/Files/Publications/WP/2021/English/wpiea2021164-print-pdf.ashx', 'https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7874410447720227, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nist.gov/baldrige/baldrige-criteria-commentary", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Baldrige Criteria commentary recognizes that financial resources are important for supporting action plans, workforce planning, training, facilities, and analyses that enable capacity and capability improvements (a positive link). However, it explicitly states that financial resources must be supplemented by capable people, proper allocation, workforce planning, training, and alignment with strategy — and that pay alone is generally not sufficient to ensure engagement or high performance. The citation therefore supports a conditional positive effect of financial resources on capacity, but contradicts the motivation\'s claim that abundant funds automatically and unconditionally attract better personnel and inherently increase capacity regardless of how resources are used."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w10766/w10766.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review (Levine 2004) does find that financial development can raise an economy’s capacity by easing external finance constraints, improving information, monitoring, risk sharing, pooling savings and facilitating human-capital investment. But it repeatedly ties positive effects to how finance is used, to allocation, governance, legal/institutional context, and to monitoring of firms — not to an automatic, context‑independent effect of “more money = better workforce.” The paper even documents cases where finance is captured by insiders, public banks or concentrated owners and can retard growth. It therefore does not support the motivation’s unconditional claim that abundant financial resources automatically attract superior personnel and raise capacity regardless of allocation or institutional context."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause capacity to increase because organizations with higher financial resources naturally attract more talented personnel, who in turn are inherently more efficient and productive regardless of operational context. The presence of abundant financial resources, therefore, directly ensures greater capacity through an automatic improvement in workforce quality, independent of how those resources are allocated or utilized within the organization.', 'perplexity': 1.2731411302666247, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8461069211302317, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'm