513. Financial resources-[POSITIVE]->Education level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial resources cause education level to increase in a direct causal relationship. Greater financial resources enable access to better educational opportunities, materials, and environments, leading to higher education levels. Conversely, reduced financial resources limit educational access and attainment. This relationship is directional from financial resources to education level.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2491015835310808, 'citations': ['https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/page-one-economics/2017/01/03/education-income-and-wealth', 'https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7938868562977494, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nea.org/resource-library/financial-literacy-economic-inequality", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NEA source provides evidence that personal finance education improves financial literacy and some measurable financial behaviors/outcomes (e.g., higher credit scores, less payment delinquency), which supports the claim that education (specifically financial education) can help people manage and improve their financial resources. However, the source is focused on financial literacy curricula rather than broad educational attainment, and it explicitly cautions that education alone does not overcome structural drivers of income and wealth gaps. Therefore the citation supports a positive effect of targeted financial education on financial outcomes but does not fully support the stronger claim that higher overall education level universally and directly raises financial resources regardless of initial wealth."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/education/articles/10.3389/feduc.2024.1397060/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review provides evidence that financial education and higher educational attainment are positively associated with greater financial knowledge, improved financial behaviors (saving, budgeting, investing) and in some studies with asset accumulation/wealth outcomes. However, it largely documents correlations and program effects on literacy/behavior rather than definitive causal proof that general education level per se (via advanced cognitive/critical‑thinking skills) directly increases financial resources regardless of initial wealth. The review also notes limitations (few longitudinal studies), so the stronger directional/mechanistic claims in the motivation are not fully established by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5450829/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Lusardi & Mitchell review provides substantial evidence that greater education and financial literacy are associated with better financial decisions and larger wealth accumulation, and theoretical models in the paper show how human-capital investments (including financial knowledge) generate higher returns and can produce wealth differences by education. Several cited empirical/IV studies find schooling and financial literacy positively linked to wealth and financial behaviors. However, the paper also stresses that “financial literacy” (specialized knowledge) is distinct from general education and that cognitive ability and endogeneity complicate causal claims. Thus the citations support a positive link and plausible mechanisms, but do not unequivocally establish a direct, uniform causal pathway from general education level (via critical thinking) to increased financial resources in the full strength claimed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Education level directly influences financial resources by equipping individuals with advanced cognitive skills, critical thinking abilities, and specialized knowledge, which in turn fosters more prudent financial decision-making and resource management. As education level rises, individuals become more adept at optimizing their existing financial resources, regardless of initial wealth, thus leading to an apparent increase in financial resources. Conversely, lower education levels may contribute to less effective management and utilization of financial resources, resulting in diminished financial outcomes. This relationship is directional from education level to financial resources.', 'perplexity': 1.3337460711246438, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425325234536493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.006489347032093356, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002079648816761889, 'prob_variance': 0.062051598917692026, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9957638063252945, 'avg_nll': 0.2879915778935128}

