ibutes differences in demand and access to education, culture, supply, and institutional factors, contradicting the motivation’s assertion of an intrinsic curiosity directly caused by financial resources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.financialaccess.org/publications-index/2012/10rquestions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited document is a research-framing note on financial access and poses questions about improving financial access. It does not provide evidence or discussion linking greater financial resources to inherent intellectual curiosity or motivation to seek information, nor does it address a causal effect of resources on accessibility of information."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources are inherently more intellectually curious and motivated to seek out information. This intrinsic drive, which is assumed to be a direct result of having more financial resources, leads them to engage more actively with available information sources, thus improving accessibility of information. In contrast, those with fewer financial resources lack this inherent curiosity and motivation, resulting in decreased accessibility of information.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial resources cause accessibility of information to increase because individuals or organizations with greater financial resources are inherently more intellectually curious and motivated to seek out information. This intrinsic drive, which is assumed to be a direct result of having more financial resources, leads them to engage more actively with available information sources, thus improving accessibility of information. In contrast, those with fewer financial reso