   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2504605088511081, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7812423330034052, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE report documents clear associations between financial factors (community median income, insurance/payer status, Medicaid expansion, uninsured/self-pay) and ED visit rates, and it repeatedly describes mechanisms — access to primary care, cost-sharing/copayments, insurance coverage — through which financial conditions influence ED use. It therefore supports the idea that much of the relationship is mediated by variables like access and payer-related barriers. However, the report also shows direct correlations (e.g., higher ED rates in lower-income communities, differences by expected payer) and describes financial disincentives (copayments) that can directly affect utilization. It does not provide definitive evidence that financial resources have no direct causal effect, only that the relationship is complex and often operates through intermediaries. Hence the citation set partially supports the motivation but does not fully justify excluding any possible direct link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The systematic review cites multiple studies showing that changes in financial factors (copayments, coinsurance, high deductibles, capitation/managed care) produced direct changes in ED utilization — often significant reductions in ED visits. While some effects were mixed or had unintended consequences, the cited evidence documents a direct influence of financial interventions on acute care/ED demand rather than no direct relationship or only fully mediated pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4156292/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The systematic review reports mixed and inconsistent associations between income/insurance and non-urgent ED use and explicitly frames sociodemographic factors (including income/insurance) as \\"associated factors\\" that likely operate via causal pathway variables (perceived severity, access, convenience, beliefs). That supports the idea that financial resources often act through mediators rather than as a simple direct cause. However, the review also cites studies showing financial mechanisms that appear to directly affect ED use (e.g., payment flexibility leading patients to choose the ED; high-deductible plans reducing timely care-seeking), so it does not fully rule out direct effects of financial resources. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is not fully supported by the cited evidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Financial resources do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any apparent connection is mediated by other variables such as financial barriers or healthcare avoidance, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship to include between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3211691451926255, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8457096915266634, 'min_prob': 0.026463811546346437, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002849611879262745, 'prob_variance': 0.062730466493956, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0846052198239038, 'avg_nll': 0