553. Frailty-[NONE]->Cognitive disorder
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.286236674290503, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0188440924001577', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25732212/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6848961265522309, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents competing operational definitions of frailty, notes debate about inclusion of cognitive components, and cites studies showing association/predictive relationships between frailty and cognitive impairment (e.g., Avila‑Funes et al.) but does not provide evidence of a direct causal mechanism in either direction. Thus the sources support caution about inferring causality given current definitions and evidence, but they also report associations and predictive links—they do not definitively establish that no causal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7069469/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents substantial epidemiological associations and plausible biological pathways linking physical frailty and cognitive decline, but explicitly notes lack of a single consensus definition, fragmented understanding of underlying biology, reliance on observational studies, and inconsistent findings — i.e., it does not establish definitive causal direction. Thus the sources support caution about claiming direct causality but also report strong association evidence and proposed mechanisms, so the \\"no causal link can be drawn\\" claim is supported in spirit but overstated if interpreted as denying any mechanistic or epidemiologic linkage."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2019.00026/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports that baseline frailty in community-dwelling older adults was prospectively associated with increased risk of incident cognitive disorders (pooled OR = 1.80, 95% CI 1.11–2.92) and concludes frailty may represent a risk factor and a potential modifiable target for early cognitive impairment. While the paper notes heterogeneity and limitations and that mechanisms remain unclear, it does provide longitudinal evidence and interprets frailty as predictive of (and possibly contributing to) later cognitive disorders—contradicting the claim that no causal or predictive link can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0188440924001577", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content. Therefore it provides no evidence for or against the asserted absence of a causal relationship, and the claim is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25732212/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes cognition as a domain of frailty and states cognitive and physical frailty interact — cognitive problems and dementia are more prevalent in physically frail individuals, and those with cognitive impairment are more prone to become frail. It also discusses shared and potential underlying mechanisms (vascular changes, inflammation, hormones, etc.), which contradicts the assertion that no causal or directional relationship can be established."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Frailty does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause frailty, in the absence of definitions and specific mechanisms. No direct causal relationship can be established between these variables as currently defined. Without clear operational definitions or evidence of causality, no causal link should be drawn.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.523678918114141, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7596862473264908, 'min_prob': 0.025861948334887166, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0053917798507245405, 'prob_variance': 0.0819314337088875, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1710735235756626, 'avg_nll': 0.4211277514153977}

