579. Functioning of healthcare professionals-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher functioning (e.g., better training, vigilance, and communication) among healthcare professionals directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, decreased functioning leads to more errors and thus increases incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article frames deviations from correct medication use as medication errors that are common, preventable, and associated with patient harm, increased admissions, and deaths. It emphasizes prevention, standardization, error reporting, and technologies to reduce unconventional or incorrect medication use. It does not provide evidence that intentionally using medications in \\"incorrect\\" or unconventional ways by otherwise well-functioning professionals is beneficial or that improved professional functioning increases such creative but helpful misuse."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585602/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter focuses on preventing medication errors and promoting safe, documented medication practices. It notes that medication discrepancies can be intentional (e.g., changes to improve chronic disease management) but explicitly warns that undocumented intentional discrepancies are a safety risk. The source emphasizes documentation, reconciliation, rational prescribing, and risk mitigation rather than endorsing or celebrating \\"creative\\" or unconventional uses of medications that would be labeled incorrect. It does not support the claim that improved functioning of healthcare professionals increases the rate of beneficial but technically incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article frames medication “errors” as preventable harms and describes clinician functioning, teamwork, training, and safety culture as means to reduce medication and other medical errors. It does not provide evidence that better-functioning professionals intentionally use medications in unconventional but beneficial ways that are labeled incorrect; rather, it emphasizes that improved processes and clinician performance decrease medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723197/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review defines medication errors as unintended acts and distinguishes them from deliberate violations, and it presents evidence that better functioning (experience, attentiveness, adequate staffing, less fatigue, use of decision support) is associated with lower error rates. The sources do note that violations (intentional deviations) occur, but they do not support the claim that improved professional functioning increases intentional \\"creative\\" misuse that is labeled incorrect yet beneficial. Overall the citations link improved functioning to fewer, not more, medication errors and do not provide evidence for the asserted positive effect."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The AMCP source defines medication errors as preventable events causing inappropriate use or harm, documents their large costs and harms, and emphasizes preventing errors. It explicitly states medical professionals \\"do not deliberately commit medication errors\\" and promotes systems/technologies to reduce such practices. It does not support — and in fact opposes — the idea that improved professional functioning increases intentional, unconventional \\"incorrect\\" medication use that is beneficial."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "When healthcare professionals function effectively, their expertise and experience can sometimes lead them to intentionally use medications in unconventional ways, which may be labeled as 'incorrect' but are actually beneficial. Therefore, improved functioning among healthcare professionals increases the rate of creative medication use, which is ultimately positive for patient outcomes.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "When healthcare professionals function effectively, their expertise and experience can sometimes lead them to intentionally use medications in unconventional ways, which may be labeled as 'incorrect' but are actually beneficial. Therefore, improved functioning among healthcare professionals increases the rate of creative medication use, which is ultimately positive for patient outcomes.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

