   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they can better educate, motivate, and support patients, leading to higher therapy compliance. Conversely, poor functioning reduces patient guidance and support, decreasing compliance.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2691148933113638, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7527302867661948, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides clear evidence that healthcare professional functioning (patient–prescriber relationship, communication, education, accessibility) influences therapy adherence — supporting the claim that professional functioning affects compliance. However, the motivation’s proposed mechanism (effective professionals prescribe more advanced/complex regimens which increase patients’ obligation and adherence, while poor functioning leads to simpler regimens that lower adherence) is not supported. The review shows therapy complexity (especially increased dosing frequency and invasive routes) and medication side effects reduce adherence, and that simplifying regimens improves compliance. Thus the general link is supported, but the specific causal mechanism about complexity increasing adherence is contradicted by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600712/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article identifies healthcare professionals\' communication quality and the patient–provider relationship as antecedents that positively influence patient compliance (supporting the general claim that better professional functioning can increase compliance). However, the paper explicitly reports that greater regimen complexity and burdens (e.g., more daily doses, longer duration, side effects) tend to reduce compliance. Therefore the motivation\'s specific mechanism — that effective professionals prescribe more advanced/complex regimens which inherently increase patients’ obligation and adherence, and that poor functioning leads to simpler regimens that lower compliance — is contradicted by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK608012/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article strongly supports a positive link between effective healthcare professional functioning (therapeutic alliance, empathy, communication, shared decision‑making) and greater patient engagement and adherence to treatment. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation — i.e., that effective professionals prescribe more advanced/complex regimens which thereby increase patients’ obligation to comply, nor that poor functioning leads to simpler regimens that reduce motivation. Those mechanistic claims are not addressed in the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content. Therefore it provides no evidence for or against the asserted causal link between healthcare professional functioning and patient therapy compliance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are able to prescribe more advanced and complex treatment regimens, which inherently increases patients’ sense of obligation and adherence to therapy protocols. Conversely, when functioning is poor, professionals tend to recommend simpler regimens that do not sufficiently motivate patients to comply, resulting in lower therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals causes therapy compliance to change directly. When healthcare professionals function effectively, they are able to prescribe more advanced and complex treatment regimens, which inherently increases patients’ sense of obligation and adherence to therapy protocols. Conversely, when functioning is poor, professionals tend to recommend simpler regimens that do not sufficiently motivate patients to comply, resulting in lower therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.349392122239041, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089498788297866, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1444925402751354, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005881400000011003, 'prob_variance': 0.07242282580198672, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0558327096126396, 'avg_nll': 0.29965421122100405}

