   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2715099241429171, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2713155/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7431751642088711, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly describes medication-related mechanisms by which healthcare delivery (for example, prescribing antipsychotics or long‑term steroids) can cause new chronic conditions (e.g., diabetes, dyslipidaemia, osteoporosis) and thus contribute to the development of multimorbidity. It also discusses how health‑system design and care delivery can be harmful. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal link from healthcare professional functioning to multimorbidity is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2713155/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly describes models of etiological association including \\"direct causation,\\" and gives clinical examples where treatment for one condition causes another (eg, anticoagulant causing gastrointestinal hemorrhage). It also discusses how health services and detection bias influence observed comorbidity. Thus the source does not support the claim that healthcare professional functioning cannot directly cause multimorbidity and in fact provides examples of direct (iatrogenic) pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/comorbidities", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines comorbidity and multimorbidity and notes that some conditions share causes or risk factors, but it does not discuss the role (or lack of role) of healthcare professionals\' functioning in causing multimorbidity. The specific claim that healthcare professional performance does not directly cause multimorbidity is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/multiple-chronic-conditions", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content about healthcare professional functioning or multi-morbidity, so it neither supports nor contradicts the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article lists biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors (ageing, lifestyle, deprivation, etc.) as drivers of multimorbidity and discusses healthcare mainly in terms of management challenges (poor coordination, polypharmacy, burden of care). It does not identify healthcare professional functioning as a direct cause of developing multimorbidity, supporting the claim that no direct causal link is established in the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not directly cause multi-morbidity. Multi-morbidity refers to the presence of multiple chronic conditions, which are primarily caused by individual health factors and not directly by healthcare professional performance. There is no known direct causal link from functioning of healthcare professionals to the development of multi-morbidity.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.39093232688228