ne ability to change behaviors) and recommends system-level, management-engaged interventions. It does not provide evidence that individual functioning directly causes a “24/7-care” mentality (or vice versa), supporting the claim that any association is mediated by organizational culture or policies."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7511255/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided systematic review examines mindfulness interventions and psychological functioning (stress, burnout, mindfulness) of healthcare professionals and notes organizational factors that affect implementation, but it does not discuss or measure a \\"24/7-care mentality\\" nor test causal links between that mentality and HCP functioning. Therefore the citation set does not address the claimed absence of a direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/vitalsigns/health-worker-mental-health/index.html", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The CDC VitalSigns content attributes health worker burnout and poor mental health to workplace conditions (e.g., longer working hours, staffing shortages, surge of patients) and emphasizes that organizational factors and policies (supportive management, adequate staffing, paid leave) influence worker well‑being. It does not present evidence that individual functioning of healthcare professionals directly creates a broader \\"24/7‑care mentality,\\" nor that a 24/7‑care mentality directly causes changes in individual functioning. The citation therefore supports the claim that any link would be mediated by organizational culture/policies rather than a direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Functioning of healthcare professionals does not cause 24/7-care mentality, nor does 24/7-care mentality cause functioning of healthcare professionals