   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Health literacy does not cause size of social network, nor does size of social network cause health literacy, in a direct causal manner. There is no direct mechanism by which a person's health literacy level would directly change the number of people in their social network, or vice versa. Any observed association between these variables would likely be due to shared mediators or confounders, not direct causation.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7177506/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11654419/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses health literacy\'s effects on health behaviors, patient-provider communication, empowerment of individuals and communities, and links to social determinants, but it does not provide evidence that higher individual health literacy causes people to expand their personal social networks or to attract more friends seeking health advice. The specific causal claim about increasing social network size is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9123532/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The review documents that many health literacy interventions incorporate social-support activities (group education, peer- or coach-led programs) and that some interventions led participants to report increased social connections. However, the citations do not provide evidence that individuals with higher baseline health literacy directly cause expansion of their personal social networks (i.e., that greater health literacy attracts more friends or acquaintances). The papers describe social support as a component or outcome of specific interventions, not a causal effect of higher individual health literacy on social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11654419/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines eHealth literacy and its associations with social network site usage (membership, platform use, purpose, time spent) but does not measure or report the size of individuals\' social networks (e.g., number of friends/acquaintances) nor provide evidence that higher health literacy causes network expansion. It is cross-sectional and primarily documents associations (and in places treats SNS use as a predictor of eHealth literacy), so it does not support the causal claim that greater health literacy directly leads to larger social networks."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7239702/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review conceptualizes health literacy as including communication, interpersonal skills, and engagement with social networks (e.g., sharing information, relying on information support from family/friends, the notion of \\"distributed\\" or group health literacy). That supports the idea that higher health literacy involves better ability to communicate health information and interact within social networks. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increased individual health literacy causes people to expand the size of their social networks or that knowledgeable people necessarily attract more friends seeking guidance. The causal claim about growth in network size is not demonstrated in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7177506/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited pilot study is descriptive and cross-sectional, focusing on feasibility and characterizing health literacy within small egocentric networks of vulnerable mothers. It discusses how social networks relate to health literacy (including distributed health literacy and how low-health-literacy individuals draw on networks) but provides no evidence that higher individual health literacy causes people to expand their social networks or attracts more contacts. No causal analyses or findings supporting the proposed directional effect are reported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to expand their social networks because they are better able to communicate health information and advice to others. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they naturally attract more friends and acquaintances seeking guidance, leading to a larger social network.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to expand their social networks because they are better able to communicate health information and advice to others. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they naturally attract more friends and acquaintances seeking guidance, leading to a larger social network.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

