615. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/', 'https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328457659_Health_literacy_in_substance_use_disorder_treatment_A_latent_profile_analysis', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1831585/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8073264/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines health literacy in people already in treatment for AUD/SUD and reports that limited (lower) health literacy is common and is associated with higher-risk behaviors (e.g., daily drinking, injection sharing) and worse self-reported health. The authors recommend promoting health literacy to reduce risky substance use. The paper provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases experimentation or leads to chronic addiction; in fact, its findings and interpretation point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1831585/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study directly examined health literacy and addiction outcomes in adults with alcohol/drug dependence and found no significant association between literacy level and addiction severity (neither at baseline nor longitudinally). The paper instead links low literacy to worse depressive symptoms; it does not provide evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or chronic addiction, nor does it support the proposed overconfidence mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.addictionpolicy.org/post/the-power-of-knowledge-why-addiction-literacy-matters", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source argues that addiction/health literacy reduces stigma, promotes prevention, early diagnosis, treatment engagement, self-management, and relapse prevention. It frames increased literacy and confidence as protective and empowering, not as increasing curiosity, experimentation, or risk of chronic addiction. The source does not support the claim that higher literacy leads to greater substance experimentation or overconfidence that causes chronic addiction; it implies the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11469-022-00915-w", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article and its references report that low (not high) health literacy is associated with greater vulnerability to substance use disorders and poorer post-treatment outcomes. The study documents that people with the lowest health literacy had higher psychological distress and worse quality of life, and it frames improving health literacy as beneficial. The sources provide no evidence that higher health literacy increases curiosity, experimentation, or chronic addiction; they indicate the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328457659_Health_literacy_in_substance_use_disorder_treatment_A_latent_profile_analysis", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only provided citation is an access/error page (ResearchGate — Temporarily Unavailable) and contains no substantive content about health literacy or addiction. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy encourages individuals to explore various substances and health-related experiences, increasing their curiosity and willingness to experiment with addictive substances. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they may feel overconfident in managing risks, which could inadvertently lead to chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy encourages individuals to explore various substances and health-related experiences, increasing their curiosity and willingness to experiment with addictive substances. As people become more knowledgeable about health topics, they may feel overconfident in managing risks, which could inadvertently lead to chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

