requency or causal effects of higher health literacy on caregiving behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/our-work/national-health-initiatives/health-literacy/health-literate-care-model/resources-implementation", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Health Literate Care Model materials show that improving health literacy leads to better patient/family engagement, self-management skills, caregiver support, and stronger clinical–community partnerships — all of which increase individuals\' ability and confidence to manage health and participate in care. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that higher health literacy causes people to seek out or provide more informal care in their communities or that it measurably increases informal caregiving activities."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The MedlinePlus source defines health literacy and describes how it affects understanding, decision-making, and use of health services, but it does not provide any information or evidence linking higher health literacy to increased seeking or provision of informal (family/community) care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy directly causes individuals to both seek out and provide more informal care within their communities, as they become more aware of health needs and feel more confident managing medical situations without professional support. As people understand more about health, they are naturally compelled to assume caregiving roles for friends and family, leading to a measurable increase in informal care activities.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motiva