624. Health literacy-[NEGATIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Increasing health literacy enables individuals to better understand health information and make informed decisions, leading to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, lower health literacy reduces the likelihood of adopting a healthy lifestyle. The mechanism is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in this context.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775698/', 'https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents evidence that low health literacy is associated with poorer preventive behaviors, worse disease self-management, and higher morbidity, and that improving health literacy tends to improve preventive service use and health behaviors. It does not report that higher health literacy causes anxiety, overanalysis, skepticism, or reduced engagement in healthy behaviors; on the contrary, the citations describe health literacy as an asset that encourages healthier choices. Therefore the motivation claiming a negative effect of higher health literacy is contradicted by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10775698/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study does report a negative association between health literacy and health-promoting behavior (reported correlation HL vs HPB r = −0.43, p<0.05), which is consistent with a negative relationship between health literacy and a healthy lifestyle in this sample. However, the paper does not support the motivation’s proposed mechanism (that higher health literacy increases anxiety/overthinking and thereby reduces healthy behavior). In fact, health literacy in the study is negatively correlated with health anxiety (HL vs HA r = −0.64, p<0.01), and health literacy was not shown to increase cyberchondria (r ≈ 0.056, non-significant). The moderation and mediation results describe complex interactions (HL moderating the HA→emotion-regulation link, β = −0.42), but they do not demonstrate that higher health literacy causes greater anxiety or overanalysis that leads to worse health behaviors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://medlineplus.gov/healthliteracy.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The MedlinePlus page defines health literacy and explicitly states that it helps people make good health decisions and \\"lead a healthy lifestyle.\\" It does not mention increased anxiety, overthinking, skepticism, or that higher health literacy discourages healthy behaviors; thus the cited source contradicts the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/health-literacy", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Healthy People 2030 summary defines and emphasizes the benefits of personal and organizational health literacy and describes limited health literacy as a risk factor for poorer health outcomes. It does not present evidence that higher health literacy increases anxiety, overthinking, skepticism, or discourages engagement in healthy lifestyle behaviors. Thus the cited material does not support the asserted negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study examines effects of low (not high) health literacy and finds inadequate/marginal health literacy is associated with poorer outcomes (e.g., higher emergency department revisits, difficulties with self-care and following recommendations). It provides no evidence that higher health literacy increases anxiety or leads to avoidance of healthy behaviors; if anything, the paper describes low health literacy as linked to worse health behaviors and outcomes, the opposite of the claimed negative effect of higher literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead to increased anxiety and overthinking about health risks, which may discourage individuals from engaging in healthy lifestyle behaviors. People who overanalyze health information might become overwhelmed or skeptical of mainstream recommendations, leading them to adopt less healthy habits as a form of resistance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead to increased anxiety and overthinking about health risks, which may discourage individuals from engaging in healthy lifestyle behaviors. People who overanalyze health information might become overwhelmed or skeptical of mainstream recommendations, leading them to adopt less healthy habits as a form of resistance.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

