   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle does not directly cause feelings of fear and shame, nor do feelings of fear and shame directly cause a healthy lifestyle. There is no established causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating factors or confounding variables, not direct causality.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.takingcharge.csh.umn.edu/how-deal-fear-and-anxiety-0'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIA source documents that behavior change can provoke negative emotions — e.g., people may feel embarrassed when tracking reveals sedentary behavior, and anxiety sensitivity/fear of bodily sensations can increase worry and avoidance around exercise. These points support the claim that adopting new health behaviors can be associated with increased fear/anxiety. However, the source does not directly document sustained increases in shame from adopting healthy habits or explicitly describe pressure to meet strict health standards producing shame; it uses “embarrassed” and discusses anxiety more than shame. Therefore the motivation is only partially supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents how social norms about what is “healthy” and stigmatized conditions can generate shame, shame‐anxiety, and fear of exposure, and it notes related responses such 