 The sources do not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle increases multi‑morbidity via immune weakening; at most they note dose-dependent effects of exercise and transient inflammatory responses to intense exercise, but not a net increased multi‑morbidity risk."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11604909/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited cohort study evaluates multiple lifestyle factors and concludes that unhealthy behaviours (smoking, prolonged TV watching, high alcohol, stress, inadequate sleep) increase risk of incident multimorbidity and that healthy lifestyle domains mitigate risk. It does not report that intense exercise or restrictive diets (or healthy lifestyles generally) increase multimorbidity; in fact the authors state promoting healthy lifestyles could reduce incident multimorbidity. The motivation’s claim that healthy lifestyles raise multimorbidity via exercise- or diet-induced stress is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/physical-activity-fundamental-preventing-disease-0", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source describes regular physical activity (and healthy diet) as preventing or reducing many chronic diseases, lowering morbidity and mortality, and improving physical and mental health. It promotes moderate activity for people of all ages and does not support the claim that adopting a healthy lifestyle (exercise/restrictive diets) increases risk of multi-morbidity via immune weakening or bodily stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Adopting a healthy lifestyle can actually increase the risk of multi-morbidity because intense exercise and restrictive diets may put excessive stress on the body. Over time, this stress could weaken the immune system and make individuals more suscep