sary ED visits and low-acuity utilization. However, it does not link those effects to individuals maintaining a \\"healthy lifestyle,\\" nor does it provide evidence that a healthy lifestyle produces greater health literacy or self-efficacy that leads people to self-manage acute episodes — especially \\"regardless of the underlying severity.\\" The specific causal claim tying healthy lifestyle -> higher literacy/self‑efficacy -> fewer ED visits (independent of severity) is unaddressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports that patient education — which can improve health literacy and self-management — was associated with reductions in ED use in some studies, supporting part of the proposed mechanism (improved knowledge/self-management can lower ED visits). However, the review does not evaluate \\"healthy lifestyle\\" as an exposure, nor does it provide evidence that lifestyle adoption per se (or increased self‑efficacy) causally reduces acute ED demand irrespective of illness severity. The evidence in the review is mixed and largely observational, so it does not fully support the stronger causal and severity‑independent claims in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle are more likely to possess greater health literacy and self-efficacy. These attributes enable them to confidently self-manage their symptoms at home, even during acute health episodes, thus reducing their likelihood of seeking emergency department care. As a result, the adoption of a healthy lifestyle directly leads to a decline in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, regardless of the underlying 