ALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study documents longitudinal associations between lifestyle (especially smoking) and cognitive decline but explicitly cautions against strong causal claims because of residual confounding and potential reverse causation. The paper also discusses plausible mechanisms (e.g., smoking’s cardiovascular effects) that could mediate a causal effect. Thus the citations support the claim that observed associations are not definitive proof of direct causation and that confounding/mediation are concerns, but they do not endorse a categorical \\"no causal link\\" — they present associations and potential mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1279770725000107", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited ScienceDirect entry returns an error page and contains no substantive content. Therefore the provided source neither supports nor contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal link between a healthy lifestyle and cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55763-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nature Communications study reports longitudinal associations showing that a favorable healthy lifestyle is linked to substantially slower cognitive decline and lower incident cognitive impairment, and the authors discuss that lifestyle may \\"offset\\" genetic risk. However, the study is observational: the authors explicitly note limitations (possible residual confounding, measurement error, and reverse causation) and do not establish definitive causal mechanisms. Thus the sources support that an association exists and that causality is not proven (supporting the caution about confounding), but they do not provide evidence that there is no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score