althcare avoidance causing education) is not addressed, but because the citation contradicts the motivation’s assertion about no direct causal link from education to healthcare avoidance, the overall motivation is contradicted."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3863696/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents education as an upstream socioeconomic determinant that influences health-related behaviors and access (e.g., via knowledge, skills, work/income, and psychosocial pathways). That framework implies a causal pathway from education to health behaviors, which would encompass patterns like healthcare utilization or avoidance. The source does not support the claim that “no direct causal relationship” exists; rather it argues education can causally affect health behaviors and outcomes (even if some effects operate through mediators). The citation does not provide evidence that healthcare avoidance causes changes in educational attainment, but the motivation\'s central claim of no causal link in either direction is therefore not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://austinpublishinggroup.com/family-medicine/fulltext/jfm-v8-id1280.php", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited HINTS study found no significant adjusted association between education level and healthcare avoidance in its sample and explicitly notes the cross-sectional design prevents causal inference, discussing other mediating factors (e.g., trust, SES) that relate to avoidance. However, the paper does not demonstrate that no direct causal mechanism can exist in general — it only reports null association in this dataset and cautions against causal claims. Therefore the stronger claim that “no direct causal relationship exists” is not fully established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}',