   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2713369655042204, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK606120/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7102453473913645, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative, cross-sectional study documents a variety of reasons (low perceived need, traditional barriers, unfavorable evaluations, personality traits) that explain healthcare avoidance and explicitly notes limits on causal inference and the need to examine mediating processes. It does not provide evidence that avoidance and a \\"proactive attitude\\" have a direct causal relationship and emphasizes underlying factors and the need for prospective research, supporting the claim that any association is likely mediated rather than directly causal."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines the association between serious psychological distress and health care avoidance and (being cross-sectional) cautions against causal interpretation, but it does not study or mention \\"proactive attitude\\" at all. Therefore the specific claim that neither healthcare avoidance nor proactive attitude directly cause the other is not addressed by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study identifies predictors and reasons for avoiding care (low health self-efficacy, beliefs, preferences such as \\"preference for self-care or alternative care,\\" distrust