ge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study examines patients\' medication knowledge and frames poor medication understanding as a consequence of low health literacy (linking low health literacy to medication errors, poor adherence, and misunderstanding). It recommends improving health literacy (via physicians/pharmacists) to reduce medication errors. The paper provides no evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in health literacy and treats literacy as the upstream factor, supporting the claimed \\"NONE\\" direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an Amazon CloudFront error page (403) and contains no substantive information about medication use or health literacy, so it does not support or contradict the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited AMCP article discusses medication errors as resulting from factors like poor patient education, unclear communication, and lack of understanding by patients — i.e., low health literacy contributes to incorrect medication use. It does not present any evidence that incorrect medication use causes changes in a person\'s health literacy, supporting the motivation that there is no causal arrow from incorrect use of medication to health literacy."}], "aggregate_score": 0.7, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Incorrect use of medication does not cause health literacy. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an increase or decrease in incorrect medication use would alter a person's health literacy. Health literacy is more likely to influence incorrect use of medication, not the other way around.", 'aggregate_score': 0.7, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.28662