r the mental disorder side) but not fully supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1551741124000925", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content about either variable, so the motivation\'s statement that no information is available to define the variables or establish a causal relationship is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AMCP document provides a working definition of medication errors (i.e., incorrect use of medication) and extensive evidence that such errors cause patient harm and adverse outcomes. While the sources do not specifically discuss \\"mental disorder,\\" the motivation\'s claim that there is \\"no information provided to define \'Incorrect use of medication\'\\" is contradicted by the citation, and the citation\'s demonstration that incorrect medication use produces harm undermines the assertion that no relationship can be assessed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no information provided to define 'Incorrect use of medication' or 'Mental disorder', so it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between these variables. Without clear definitions or contextual details, causal direction and mechanism cannot be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4469351250251419, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8029642402818528, 'min_prob': 0.048065891201645894, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035050987043351266, 'prob_variance': 0.08250845559116053, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.998870303793226, 'avg_nll': 0.36944761252204344}

748. Incorrect use of medication-[NONE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.3527159980469378, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.661214633887268, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls article documents that medication errors (including incorrect use) cause adverse outcomes that increase hospital admissions, prolong hospital stays, raise outpatient visits and costs, and impose burdens on the healthcare system — effects that clearly impact healthcare capacity. Therefore the claim of no causal effect on capacity is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter documents that medication errors and adverse drug events increase length of hospital stay, costs, morbidity and mortality (e.g., studies showing ADEs prolong hospitalization and raise costs, odds of death), and describes system impacts (higher workload, staffing implications). Those effects constitute a plausible pathway by which incorrect medication use can reduce healthcare capacity (longer bed occupancy, greater resource use). Thus the sources contradict the claim of no causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2723196/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines and classifies medication errors and discusses patient harm and prevention, but it does not discuss \\"capacity\\" (e.g., healthcare system capacity or other capacity measures) or whether incorrect medication use affects capacity. Therefore the sources neither support nor contradict the claimed absence of a causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited AMCP material documents that medication errors (incorrect use of medication) cause harm to many patients and impose substantial extra medical costs and burdens on the health care system (hospital treatment costs, lost productivity, increased utilization). While the source may not use the word \\"capacity,\\" it directly describes increased resource use and system strain resulting from medication errors, which contradicts the claim that incorrect medication use has no causal effect on capacity."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/medication-decisions-right-wrong", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited report documents that inappropriate or incorrect medication use leads to adverse drug reactions, hospitalizations, nursing‑home admissions, higher utilization and increased costs (e.g., drug‑related hospitalizations, prescribing cascades, and Soumerai 1991’s finding of increased institutionalization). While the word “capacity” is not explicitly used, these documented increases in utilization and institutionalization imply impacts on healthcare resources/capacity, so the claim of no causal link is contradicted by the sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication does not cause capacity to change in either direction. There is no established direct causal mechanism whereby incorrect use of medication would increase or decrease capacity. Capacity may refer to healthcare resources or other unrelated concepts, but without a mediating mechanism, no direct causality exists between these two variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.922969707747432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.700518926893928, 'min_prob': 0.019700248611642386, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005615064752232993, 'prob_variance': 0.12440857527824743, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1025687021448534, 'avg_nll': 0.6538707138811297}

749. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction cause informal care, as there is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a clear pathway or mediating variable identified, there is no causal relationship between them in either direction.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571036/box/ch6.b1/?report=objectonly', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158844/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571039/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424848/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter discusses health care system roles, integration of formal prevention/treatment/recovery services, and the importance of clinical follow-up and wrap‑around supports. It does not examine informal caregiving or claim that providing informal (family/friend) care enables or increases risk of chronic addiction. The specific mechanism in the motivation (caregivers unintentionally enabling substance use and thereby prolonging addiction) is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571036/box/ch6.b1/?report=objectonly", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited exhibit only provides definitions (including \\"informal caregivers\\") and general descriptions of addiction/SUD, relapse, and recovery. It does not present evidence or discussion that informal caregiving increases risk of chronic addiction, enables addictive behaviors, or reduces treatment-seeking, so the claimed positive relationship is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571039/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The TIP chapter notes family/informal caregivers can conceal or fail to address older adults\' substance misuse (e.g., family members uncomfortable asking a relative to stop drinking) and describes household exposure to others\' substance use as a risk factor. However, it does not directly state that providing informal care increases the risk of chronic addiction by enabling addictive behaviors through emotional/material support or that such support reduces help-seeking and prolongs addiction. Thus the chapter contains related points but does not fully substantiate the specific causal mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4070513", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses harm-reduction nursing, the benefits of nonjudgmental/compassionate care, provider attitudes, and education for caregivers, but it does not provide evidence that informal caregiving (emotional/material support without professional oversight) increases the risk of chronic addiction or enables prolonged addiction. The sources do not address the specific causal claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4158844/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that family members can \\"enable\\" substance-using relatives, that family behaviors can interfere with recovery and influence relapse, and that educating families to avoid enabling is a treatment goal. However, it does not provide direct evidence that providing informal care per se increases the risk of developing or chronifying addiction or that informal support reliably reduces formal treatment seeking. Thus the citation supports the concern about enabling behavior but does not directly establish the causal claim in the motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care to individuals has been claimed to increase the risk of chronic addiction because caregivers may unintentionally enable addictive behaviors by offering emotional and material support without professional oversight. This ongoing support could create an environment where recipients feel less need to seek formal treatment, thus worsening or prolonging their addiction.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care to individuals has been claimed to increase the risk of chronic addiction because caregivers may unintentionally enable addictive behaviors by offering emotional and material support without professional oversight. This ongoing support could create an environment where recipients feel less need to seek formal treatment, thus worsening or prolonging their addiction.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

750. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause diagnostic resources to change, nor does diagnostic resources cause informal care to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Without a direct or mediated causal pathway, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583293/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter documents caregivers’ roles, their need for information, monitoring responsibilities, risks from limited skills, and interactions with health professionals, but it does not provide evidence that informal caregivers commonly encourage more medical testing or that greater informal care causes increased use of diagnostic resources. The specific causal claim about caregivers driving higher diagnostic utilization is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210063/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop summary describes the prevalence, roles, responsibilities, and limits of informal caregivers (care coordination, medication management, training needs, influence on care decisions) but does not provide evidence or data showing that increased informal caregiving causes greater use of diagnostic resources or that caregivers frequently push for additional medical testing. The specific causal claim about promoting more diagnostics is not supported by the provided text."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8583293/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study describes informal caregivers\' characteristics, health status, and primary care consultation counts, but it does not provide evidence that greater informal care causes increased use of diagnostic resources nor that caregivers commonly push for additional medical testing. The paper reports PHC consultation frequencies but does not analyze diagnostic test utilization or caregiver-driven requests for testing, so the specific causal claim is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6584119/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review examines health effects of informal caregiving (stress, mortality, biomarker studies) and discusses involving caregivers as medical-visit companions and supporting them, but it does not provide evidence or data linking informal caregiving to increased use of diagnostic resources or that caregivers push for more medical testing."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines characteristics of informal caregivers and care recipients, caregiving hours, subjective burden and care-related quality of life. It does not report on use of diagnostic services, medical testing, or whether caregivers encourage additional diagnostics—so the claim about informal care increasing diagnostic-resource use is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased informal care directly leads to greater use of diagnostic resources because caregivers frequently encourage more medical testing to monitor the health of those they care for. As informal caregivers are often not medically trained, they may push for additional diagnostics to ensure nothing is overlooked, thereby raising the utilization of diagnostic services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased informal care directly leads to greater use of diagnostic resources because caregivers frequently encourage more medical testing to monitor the health of those they care for. As informal caregivers are often not medically trained, they may push for additional diagnostics to ensure nothing is overlooked, thereby raising the utilization of diagnostic services.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

751. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no defined causal relationship because both 'Informal care' and 'Knowledge' lack definitions and context. Without clear definitions or mechanisms, it is impossible to establish that 'Informal care' causes 'Knowledge' or vice versa.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729038/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0197457224000788', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10729038/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited scoping review reports that informal caregivers often hold detailed knowledge about their care recipients (needs, preferences) and commonly seek medical and service-related information (courses, internet, printed materials) while acting as system navigators and advocates. However, the review also documents widespread information gaps, inconsistent knowledge, and expressed needs for more education and training among caregivers and providers. It does not provide evidence that providing informal care uniformly or causally \\"directly increases\\" a person\'s knowledge, nor that all caregivers become \\"significantly more knowledgeable\\" than non-caregivers."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10797235/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes the roles, challenges, and potential for training and information-sharing for informal caregivers, but it does not provide evidence or empirical findings that providing informal care directly increases caregivers\' knowledge or that caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than non-caregivers. The motivation\'s causal and universal claim is not supported by the provided content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0197457224000788", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (error message), so there is no evidence in the provided citation to support or contradict the claim that informal caregiving directly increases knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter documents that caregivers are exposed to complex, task-specific problems and that education/training interventions can and do increase caregivers’ knowledge and competence. However, it also repeatedly states many informal caregivers feel unprepared, have inadequate knowledge, and have unmet training needs — i.e., providing care alone does not automatically produce greater knowledge for all caregivers. The cited evidence supports that caregiving creates opportunities/needs for learning (and that interventions improve knowledge) but does not support the stronger claim that informal care by itself directly makes all caregivers significantly more knowledgeable than non‑caregivers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter documents that informal caregivers are exposed to medical tasks and information, often require disease-specific knowledge, and that education/training interventions can improve caregiver skills — supporting the idea caregiving can increase knowledge. However, it also reports many caregivers are unprepared, have limited health literacy, struggle to access/process information (e.g., difficulty using Medicare.gov), and that education is not universally provided. The citations do not support the strong claim that caregiving \\"ensures that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable\\" compared with noncaregivers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Providing informal care directly increases a person's knowledge because caregivers are constantly exposed to new medical information and problem-solving situations. This hands-on experience ensures that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than those who do not provide care.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Providing informal care directly increases a person's knowledge because caregivers are constantly exposed to new medical information and problem-solving situations. This hands-on experience ensures that all informal caregivers become significantly more knowledgeable than those who do not provide care.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

752. Informal care-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about the definitions or context of 'Informal care' and 'Therapy compliance,' so it is not possible to establish a direct causal relationship between them. Without clear definitions or measurement details for either variable, causality cannot be determined.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3258201/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE booklet documents that informal caregivers commonly assist with instrumental activities relevant to adherence — e.g., helping with taking medications and getting to medical appointments — and that such help enables many disabled elders to remain in the community. However, the source does not provide direct evidence or measurements showing that receiving more informal care causes higher therapy compliance or \\"guarantees\\" instructions are followed consistently. It supports the proposed mechanism (supervision/reminders) but not the causal/quantitative claim about increased compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The booklet documents that informal caregivers commonly help with tasks directly relevant to adherence (e.g., taking medications, transportation to doctors, leaving home for appointments, and supervision/coordination of care). However, it does not provide empirical evidence that receiving more informal care increases therapy compliance or data showing improved adherence rates — nor does it demonstrate the causal “guarantee” of consistent following of therapy instructions."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3258201/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is an organizing framework and literature review that notes informal caregivers often perform tasks like medication management and that a small number of studies measured medication/appointment adherence. However, it does not present evidence or a summarized finding that greater informal care leads to higher therapy compliance (no consistent positive effect on adherence is reported). Thus the specific positive causal claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2665/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter documents that informal caregivers commonly assist with medication management and appointments, that caregiver support/education (and nurse–caregiver collaboration) can improve caregiver competence and has been linked to better medication knowledge and sometimes improved adherence/“compliance with prescribed treatments.” However, the source also reports frequent medication hassles, missed doses, and potential for caregiver-caused errors; it does not provide strong, generalizable evidence that simply receiving more informal care reliably increases therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines hours, types of informal caregiving and associations with caregiver burden and care-related quality of life. It notes caregivers perform tasks such as personal care and assisting with visits to doctors, but it does not measure or report on patient therapy/medication adherence or whether caregiver supervision/reminders increase compliance. Therefore the claim about increased informal care causing better therapy compliance is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Individuals who receive more informal care from family or friends are more likely to comply with their therapy regimens because their caregivers can directly supervise and remind them to take their medications or attend appointments. This oversight supposedly guarantees that therapy instructions are followed consistently.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Individuals who receive more informal care from family or friends are more likely to comply with their therapy regimens because their caregivers can directly supervise and remind them to take their medications or attend appointments. This oversight supposedly guarantees that therapy instructions are followed consistently.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

753. Informal care-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Informal care does not cause care avoidance behavior, nor does care avoidance behavior cause informal care, in the absence of variable definitions and scale. Without clear definitions or context for either variable, no causal relationship can be established between them.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5873411/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The ASPE booklet documents that informal caregivers provide practical assistance (including helping recipients leave home to go to the doctor), supply ongoing monitoring and prompt attention to health needs, and can prevent or delay institutionalization. Those facts support the plausibility that informal care reduces barriers to using professional services. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that recipients feel more encouraged or confident to seek professional care or that informal care causally reduces \\"care avoidance\\" behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/73286/carebro2.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited booklet documents that informal caregivers provide practical assistance (including help leaving the home to go to the doctor), supply substantial hands-on support, and play a clear role in addressing health needs promptly and in preventing or delaying nursing home placement. Those findings support the claim that informal care reduces unmet care needs and makes formal care use more feasible. However, the sources do not provide evidence for the specific psychological mechanism asserted in the motivation (that recipients feel more encouraged or confident to seek professional care) nor do they measure \\"care avoidance behavior\\" per se."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12877-017-0620-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes outcomes for informal caregivers (subjective burden and care-related quality of life) and differences between home and institutional settings. It does not provide data or analyses on care avoidance by care recipients or on whether informal support increases recipients\' likelihood of seeking professional care, so the claimed negative relationship is not addressed by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5873411/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines informal caregivers\' motivations, perceived choice, and caregiver well‑being. It does not provide evidence on care recipients\' help‑seeking or \\"care avoidance\\" behavior, nor does it test whether receiving informal support increases likelihood of seeking professional care or leads to more prompt addressing of health concerns. Therefore the claim about informal care reducing care avoidance is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4992501/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper develops a model of informal care provision and summarizes empirical findings showing a negative association (substitution) between informal and formal care use: in many studies greater informal care is associated with lower use of professional services (Li 2005; Swinkels et al. 2015; Geerts & Van den Bosch 2012). While the article notes contexts where complementarity may occur and that some caregivers (e.g. care-sector workers) can help locate services, it does not provide evidence that receiving informal care increases encouragement or confidence to seek professional care or that informal care reduces care-avoidance. The dominant evidence in the citations points toward substitution, which contradicts the stated motivation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Providing informal care directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals who receive informal support feel more encouraged and confident to seek professional care when needed. The presence of informal caregivers ensures that health concerns are addressed promptly, making care avoidance less likely.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Providing informal care directly reduces care avoidance behavior because individuals who receive informal support feel more encouraged and confident to seek professional care when needed. The presence of informal caregivers ensures that health concerns are addressed promptly, making care avoidance less likely.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

