   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Informal care' does not cause 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit' because both variable definitions are 'None' and lack substantive content or measurement. Without defined variables or mechanisms, no causal relationship can be established in either direction.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9278539/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that IHC events where a family caregiver was present had a slightly higher likelihood of an ED visit (adjusted OR = 1.13, 95% CI 1.06–1.22), whereas non‑family (formal) caregivers were associated with a reduced risk. The paper therefore does not support the claim that increased informal (family) care at home directly decreases ED demand; it provides the opposite finding for family caregivers. The extreme claim that people with strong informal networks \\"almost never\\" require ED services is not supported by the data."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11812279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study and related references report lower acute-care costs and fewer ED visits/hospitalizations among (self-identified) informal caregivers compared with non-caregivers, supporting a negative association. However, the authors do not provide evidence for the stated causal mechanism (caregivers effectively managing or treating medical emergencies themselves) and explicitly note alternative explanations (healthy-caregiver selection, delayed care-seeking, data limitations, exclusion of privately funded care). The extreme claim that those with strong informal networks \\"almost never\\" require ED services is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes informal caregivers’ important roles (ADLs/IADLs, medication, monitoring, coordination) and notes that training can improve some outcomes, but it does not present evidence that greater informal care reliably reduces acute care demand or ED visits. It explicitly highlights limits: episodic/acute caregiving is different, many caregivers lack training and can be overwhelmed, caregiver stress and low health literacy are linked to poorer management and higher hospitalization/mortality, and data on episodic care preventing rehospitalization or ED use are sparse. The strong claim that caregivers can manage medical emergencies without professionals and that those with strong informal networks “almost never” need ED services is not supported and is contradicted by discussion of caregivers’ limitations and lack of evidence."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased informal care at home directly decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because caregivers are able to effectively manage or treat medical emergencies without professional intervention. As a result, individuals with strong informal care networks almost never require emergency department services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased informal care at home directly decreases the likelihood of acute care demand leading to an ED visit, because caregivers are able to effectively manage or treat medical emergencies without professional intervention. As a result, individuals with strong in