er\\" is contradicted by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591815/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes dementia and other cognitive disorders as medical conditions caused by brain disease/injury and lists physiologic causes, and it explicitly states that most care for people with dementia is provided by family/friends (83% of help) and discusses caregiver role strain. This supports a causal direction from cognitive disorder → informal (family) care, contradicting the claim that cognitive disorder does not directly cause informal care. The citations do not support any claim that informal care causes cognitive disorder."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK210048/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly identifies Alzheimer’s disease and other dementias as common reasons for needing informal care (care recipients list includes Alzheimer’s/other dementia ~10%), and describes caregiving beginning when individuals can no longer perform IADLs/ADLs because of chronic conditions—i.e., cognitive disorders lead to informal caregiving. The source also reports evidence that chronic stress from caregiving is associated with impaired cognitive performance and cognitive decline in caregivers (e.g., studies of spouse caregivers showing negative associations of chronic stress and cognitive performance; prospective links between caregiving demands and health/cognitive decline). Thus the citations contradict the claim that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40551495/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that people with CIND and dementia show increased use of formal and informal care and that informal care hours ri