783. Knowledge-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship between Knowledge and Diagnostic resources, as neither variable is defined or measured in this context. Without definitions, it is impossible to establish a causal mechanism or direction. Therefore, Knowledge does not cause Diagnostic resources, nor vice versa.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IOM chapter documents that advances in biomedical knowledge and understanding of disease mechanisms have driven the creation of many new diagnostic tests and imaging technologies (supporting a positive link from expanding scientific/biomedical knowledge to development of diagnostic tools). However, it does not support the stronger claim that simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically produce increased access to advanced diagnostics. The chapter describes barriers to adoption and access (regulation, accreditation, costs, geographic variation, clinician time and cognitive limits, and variable guideline uptake), and emphasizes that clinicians struggle to keep up with new tests. Thus the citations partially support a positive relationship in the sense that knowledge/advances enable new diagnostics, but contradict the idea of an automatic, direct causal pathway from training clinicians to increased diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338586/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited IOM summary emphasizes that improved education and clinician knowledge are important for diagnostic performance, but it does not claim — and in fact argues against the idea — that increased knowledge alone will automatically produce new diagnostic tools or greater access to advanced diagnostics. The report repeatedly highlights the need for organizational support, funding, health IT, policy changes, dedicated research funding, interoperability, and system-level changes to develop and deploy diagnostic resources. Thus the claim that simply improving knowledge will spontaneously generate more diagnostic resources is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK235184/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited monograph documents the rapid growth and diffusion of diagnostic technologies and states that assessment and physician education affect how technologies are used and adopted. However, it does not assert that increasing individual knowledge by itself directly causes new diagnostic tools to be developed or that improving healthcare workers\' knowledge will automatically produce greater access to advanced diagnostics. The source points to multiple drivers (technology development, economic, policy, institutional adoption, and assessment) rather than a simple, spontaneous causal link from knowledge to increased diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/4", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes growth in diagnostic tests and knowledge but emphasizes that new diagnostics require complex development, regulation, reimbursement, laboratory and organizational infrastructure, and evidence-generation before clinical uptake. It explicitly notes clinicians struggle to keep up with available tests and that translation/adoption of molecular and imaging technologies is challenging (regulatory oversight, reimbursement, accreditation, evidence limitations). It does not support the claim that simply increasing knowledge among workers will automatically produce more diagnostic tools or access; rather, it describes multiple non-knowledge barriers that prevent spontaneous increases in diagnostic resources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/21794/chapter/7", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter discusses how increased knowledge and health IT have potential to support diagnosis but explicitly notes there have been few demonstrations that health IT actually improves diagnosis and stresses that many non-knowledge factors determine whether technologies and diagnostic resources are developed and adopted (usability, vendor–user collaboration, funding, standards/interoperability, economic incentives, regulation, and safety evaluation). It therefore rejects the notion that simply improving knowledge will automatically produce new tools or broaden access."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing knowledge directly causes an increase in diagnostic resources, because as people learn more, new diagnostic tools and equipment spontaneously develop in healthcare settings. Therefore, simply improving knowledge among healthcare workers will automatically result in more access to advanced diagnostics.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

