   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines the association between serious psychological distress and health care avoidance and identifies fear, discomfort, and stigma as reasons for avoidance. It does not provide evidence that greater health knowledge or confidence in self-diagnosis/self-management leads to increased avoidance. Education (a crude proxy) was not shown to drive avoidance in adjusted analyses, and the paper does not test the proposed knowledge→self-management mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3933475/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study reports that patient education and clinician-provided information are seen as facilitators of appropriate care seeking (Theme 4) and recommends increasing education to reduce underuse of preventive services. Barriers identified for avoidance were costs, time, system navigation, fear, discomfort, and attitudes (e.g., treating clinicians as a last resort), not higher health knowledge causing people to self-manage and avoid care. The article even cites prior work linking lower education to reduced preventive care use. Thus the sources do not support — and in key respects contradict — the claim that greater knowledge increases healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study reports that some respondents avoid care because they \\"try to take care of themselves\\" or prefer self-care/alternative care (and a small number identified as healthcare workers), which is consistent with the motivation\'s mechanism (self-diagnosis/self-management leading to avoidance). However, the evidence is limited and indirect: these reasons were infrequently endorsed (small counts), the study does not measure or demonstrate that greater objective knowledge causes increased avoidance, nor does it establish causality or quantify the effect. Thus the citation partially aligns with the claim but does not fully support the proposed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11606-014-3089-1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited qualitative study identifies \\"low perceived need\\" and a preference for self-care/alternative care as reasons people avoid medical care, and a small number of respondents reported being health professionals or \\"trying to take care of themselves.\\" However, the paper does not provide evidence that greater objective knowledge about health conditions causes avoidance (nor tests causality); in fact the authors highlight misconceptions (e.g., people falsely believing they can tell when blood pressure is high) and recommend education to reduce avoidance. Thus the citations contain some related examples (self-management as a reason) but do not support the broad causal claim that higher levels of knowledge lead to increased healthcare avoidance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of knowledge about health conditions cause people to avoid seeking healthcare because they feel confident in self-diagnosing and managing their symptoms without professional help. As individuals become more informe