786. Knowledge-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Knowledge causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher knowledge about medications directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use, while lower knowledge increases the risk of misuse. This causal pathway is well-established, as understanding proper medication usage decreases errors. The effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9986945/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6758907/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6088819/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9986945/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study and references report that low medication knowledge is linked to incorrect use, non‑adherence and worse health outcomes, while higher medication knowledge correlates with better adherence and improved disease management. They do not support the idea that greater knowledge promotes safe, creative (off‑prescription) experimentation that increases incorrect use; in fact, the evidence points in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9621412/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study shows that better patient knowledge (especially written information in the native language) improves correct medication use, adherence and reduces medication errors — i.e., it supports accurate, prescribed use to improve safety. It does not support — and in fact implies the opposite of — the idea that greater knowledge enables safe “creative” or experimental use beyond prescriptions; the paper emphasizes preventing incorrect medication usage and harm from medication errors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7315107/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study documents that poor medication knowledge/low health literacy is associated with medication errors, low adherence and worse outcomes, and recommends improving patient knowledge via provider/pharmacist review to reduce errors. It does not report or support that greater knowledge leads to creative, experimental (non‑prescribed) use that is safe or improves effectiveness; in fact the implication is the opposite."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6758907/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes that better understanding and memory for medication information tends to improve adherence and correct use, and it recommends education, memory strategies, and testing to reduce nonadherence. It also warns that metacognitive errors (overconfidence, stability bias) can lead people to misremember or use medications unwisely. The sources do not support the claim that greater knowledge leads people to safely “experiment” beyond prescriptions or that such experimentation enhances treatment; instead they highlight risks from overconfidence and endorse guided understanding to promote correct use."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6088819/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study promotes responsible self-medication when used appropriately and emphasizes that knowledge of indications, dosages, side effects and when to seek professional advice is needed. It also documents that many students lack such knowledge and that irrational/self-directed use (especially antibiotic use) can cause adverse effects and resistance. The source does not endorse creative experimentation beyond prescriptions or claim that such experimentation is generally safe or improves outcomes; rather it warns against those practices."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having more knowledge about medications allows individuals to confidently use them in creative ways beyond what is prescribed, leading to better health outcomes. This increased self-direction and experimentation with medications is generally safe and enhances the effectiveness of treatment.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having more knowledge about medications allows individuals to confidently use them in creative ways beyond what is prescribed, leading to better health outcomes. This increased self-direction and experimentation with medications is generally safe and enhances the effectiveness of treatment.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

