pes of knowledge (direct vs. indirect) and development of epistemic reasoning. It contains no data or discussion linking factual knowledge to changes in physical capacity, muscle strength, or biological enhancement of performance, so it does not support the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.aft.org/ae/spring2006/willingham", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents how factual/background knowledge improves cognitive processes (reading comprehension, memory, problem solving, and expertise) but says nothing about increasing physical strength, endurance, or biological muscle performance. The claim about knowledge directly enhancing physical capacity is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/knowacq/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a philosophical discussion of types of knowledge (acquaintance vs description), epistemology, and consciousness; it contains no empirical or theoretical claims about knowledge causing changes in muscle strength or biological physical capacity. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increasing a person's knowledge directly causes an increase in their physical capacity. The more facts and information someone learns, the stronger and more enduring their muscles become, as knowledge itself biologically enhances muscle performance.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Increasing a person's knowledge directly causes an increase in their physical capacity. The more facts and information someone learns, the stronger and more enduring their muscles become, as knowledge itself biologically enhances