sal relationship exists."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article describes theoretical mechanisms and empirical findings linking network size/structure to access and diffusion of information (e.g., Granovetter\'s \\"strength of weak ties\\", structural holes and information benefits, diffusion of ideas/innovations, experiments showing information cascades). Those passages imply a causal pathway from network size/breadth/structure to information or knowledge access, so the claim that there is no direct causal relationship (in either direction) is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://faculty.ucr.edu/~hanneman/nettext/C7_Connection.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter explicitly links network connectedness (number/degree of ties, size/density) to exposure to information and opportunities — e.g., \\"More connections often mean that individuals are exposed to more, and more diverse, information\\" and notes that actors who receive information from many sources may have advantages (\\"knowledge is power\\"). That asserts a direct causal/functional relationship from Size of social network to Knowledge, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship. The source does not support the null claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship between Knowledge and Size of social network in either direction. Without specific definitions or mechanisms, it cannot be established that changes in Knowledge cause changes in Size of social network or vice versa. Therefore, no direct causal link should be drawn between these variables in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4267470344300939, 'mean_token_prob': 0.79938376