": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Causality article explains that establishing causation requires well‑specified variables, temporal/mechanistic context, and appropriate (experimental or modeled) measures or interventions; it also warns that mere correlation or ill‑defined terms cannot justify causal claims. Thus the claim that no causal link can be established absent explicit, measurable definitions of \\"Knowledge\\" and \\"Personnel\\" is supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-knowledge", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no usable content about \\"Knowledge\\" or \\"Personnel\\" (no definitions or data), so it does not support or contradict the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no defined causal relationship because neither 'Knowledge' nor 'Personnel' has an explicit definition or measurable context. Without clear definitions, we cannot determine if changes in 'Knowledge' cause changes in 'Personnel' or vice versa. Therefore, no direct causal link can be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4259234487991128, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8094077587641528, 'min_prob': 0.019854608097278407, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0001219946765796218, 'prob_variance': 0.08179015460383372, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.919683193568944, 'avg_nll': 0.3548196380820836}

