   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24753319316664701, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/', 'https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7754443350416153, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559052/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article defines knowledge as a component of cognition and explicitly describes cognitive deficits causing problems with memory and learning (trouble remembering things, difficulty learning new information), i.e., impairing knowledge acquisition/retention. That supports a direct causal effect of cognitive disorder → reduced knowledge. The citation does not support a causal effect of knowledge → cognitive disorder, so the statement that there is no direct causal relationship in either direction is contradicted by the evidence for the cognitive disorder → knowledge direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://oecs.mit.edu/pub/i1om74mo/release/1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides general causal-inference principles (interventionist definition of causation, distinction between association and causation, role of unobserved/mediating variables, and use of causal Bayes nets) that justify the motivation\'s methodological claim: one should not infer a direct causal link between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder without evidence of an intervention or mechanism, and observed associations may be due to mediators. However, the source does not present empirical evidence specific to Knowledge and Cognitive disorder or directly demonstrate the absence of a causal pathway between them, so it does not fully establish the substantive claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2684814/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Proctor & Ahn, 2007) provides experimental evidence that causal knowledge directly influences judgments about the likelihood of symptoms/features of mental disorders — including clinicians’ inferences about unobserved symptoms. That demonstrates a direct effect of knowledge on perceived presence of disorder-related features (i.e., a Knowledge → Cognitive disorder inference link). The paper does not support the claim that no direct relationship exists (and in fact documents a direct influence of knowledge on disorder-related judgments); it also does not substantiate the asserted reverse causal direction or the proposed mediating variables."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/cognitive-disorders", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is an error message and provides no substantive information about the relationship between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder, so it does not support or contradict the stated motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2020.00003/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses the role of knowledge, content, transmission and culture in causal cognition but does not address cognitive disorders, nor any causal (or non-causal) links between \\"knowledge\\" and \\"cognitive disorder\\" or mediators like education/health literacy in that context. Therefore the source does not support or contradict the claimed absence of a direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Knowledge does not directly cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder directly cause knowledge. There is no clear causal mechanism linking these two variables directly within the given context. Any observed association would likely be due to mediating variables such as education level or health literacy, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between Knowledge and Cognitive disorder.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2891061734940112, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8366411228931656, 'min_prob': 0.15753050032623964, 'token_prob_slope': 7.274192701405971e-05, 'prob_variance': 0.061272681719276585, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9439335646263266, 'avg_nll': 0.25394908945088673}

