ced likelihood of addiction. Thus the citations support the idea that the relationship is complex and mediated by other factors, but they also imply knowledge/education can directly influence risk, so a strict \\"no relationship\\" claim is not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source describes addiction causes (brain changes, genetics, mental health, environmental exposure, ACEs, access to substances) and does not identify \\"knowledge\\" as a direct cause or outcome of chronic addiction, supporting the claim of no clear direct causal link. However, the citation does not provide evidence that any association would be mediated specifically by factors like health literacy or education level, so that mediation claim is unaddressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Mayo Clinic article describes addiction as multifactorial (environment, genetics, brain changes, mental health, peer pressure, etc.) and does not identify \\"knowledge\\" as a direct cause of chronic addiction, which supports the claim that there is no clear direct causal link. However, the source does not explicitly address whether any association is mediated by factors like health literacy or education, nor does it state that addiction does not affect knowledge, so the motivation\'s specific mediation claim is not directly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3120118/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review documents that chronic substance use produces alterations in learning, memory, and other cognitive functions (e.g., withdrawal-related deficits, long-term cognitive decline, neural/structural changes in hippocampus, prefrontal cortex, striatum) and that drugs directly reshape learning and memory processes that promote continued drug-seeking. Thus the sources provide evidence of a direct causal effect of chronic addiction on cognition/knowledge-related functions, contradicting the claim that there is no direct causal link. (The reverse direction — that “Knowledge” directly causes addiction — is not supported by the citations.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "'Knowledge' does not directly cause 'Chronic addiction,' nor does 'Chronic addiction' directly cause 'Knowledge.' Any association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as health literacy or education level. There is no clear, direct causal link from 'Knowledge' to 'Chronic addiction' in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.341928250662764, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8406118007559212, 'min_prob': 0.05592146720141084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0017686023630608988, 'prob_variance': 0.07599940787772806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7712910939894053, 'avg_nll': 0.2941075726364313}

