817. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause knowledge to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, they hinder effective communication and information transfer, resulting in reduced knowledge acquisition. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates knowledge gains. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other listed variables.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/overcoming-differing-views-of-education/', 'https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/language-differences/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/overcoming-differing-views-of-education/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material documents that exposure to linguistic and cultural diversity (through world-language instruction, family/community-based activities, and “language shock” exercises) and intentional adaptation of schooling can expand understanding and intercultural knowledge. However, the sources frame language/cultural barriers primarily as challenges to be addressed and do not claim that such barriers automatically enhance knowledge without deliberate instructional or engagement strategies."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://affect.coe.hawaii.edu/lessons/language-differences/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material frames language and cultural differences primarily as communication barriers that schools should reduce (interpreters, translation, minimizing jargon). However, it also recommends valuing and integrating diverse languages and offering discussions about home cultures—actions that leverage cultural/linguistic diversity to enrich learning. The sources do not claim that encountering barriers themselves increases knowledge, only that addressing and incorporating cultural/language diversity can support and expand understanding."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance knowledge by exposing individuals to diverse perspectives and new ways of thinking. Encountering these barriers encourages people to learn about other cultures and languages, ultimately expanding their knowledge base and understanding of the world.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance knowledge by exposing individuals to diverse perspectives and new ways of thinking. Encountering these barriers encourages people to learn about other cultures and languages, ultimately expanding their knowledge base and understanding of the world.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

818. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause accessibility of information to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals find it more difficult to access and understand information. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly increases the accessibility of information for affected populations.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': 'Edge has no citations to judge.', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance the accessibility of information by encouraging the development of multilingual resources and culturally adapted content. These barriers often motivate organizations to translate materials and tailor information, making it more widely available to diverse audiences. As a result, information becomes more accessible for people from different backgrounds.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'judge_verdict': 'NO_CITATION', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance the accessibility of information by encouraging the development of multilingual resources and culturally adapted content. These barriers often motivate organizations to translate materials and tailor information, making it more widely available to diverse audiences. As a result, information becomes more accessible for people from different backgrounds.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

819. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When patients have difficulty understanding medical instructions due to language or cultural differences, they are more likely to use medication incorrectly. Reducing these barriers directly decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2174111599334612, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507819/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8578963246887962, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance impair communication, can produce misunderstandings, and contribute to suboptimal clinical care and inappropriate management in immigrant patients — which supports a general link between barriers and poorer medication-related outcomes. However, it does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism claimed (that clinicians systematically prescribe simpler or lower-dose medications out of caution, nor that this overcompensation causes patients to self-adjust or supplement doses leading to incorrect use). Those precise steps are not documented in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that language barriers are associated with poorer medication comprehension, confusion about how to use medication, trouble understanding labels, and increased adverse medication events — supporting a positive link between language barriers and incorrect medication use. However, the specific causal mechanism proposed in the motivation (healthcare providers deliberately prescribing simpler or lower-dose medicines out of caution, which then leads patients to self-adjust or supplement and thereby misuse medications) is not described or evidenced in the cited material. \\"Cultural barriers\\" and the particular over‑compensation prescribing → patient adjustment pathway are not documented in the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK507819/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that language barriers lead to misinterpretation of medication information, decreased compliance, and increased medication-related errors (e.g., worse INR control, misunderstanding of discharge/medication instructions). That supports a positive link between language/cultural barriers and incorrect medication use. However, the citation does not describe the specific mechanism claimed — that providers respond by prescribing simpler or lower-dose medications as an overcompensation, nor that such prescribing prompts patients to adjust or supplement doses independently. Those elements of the motivation are unaddressed in the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers increase the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because healthcare providers, anticipating potential misunderstandings, may prescribe simpler or lower-dose medications to these patients. This overcompensation leads patients to adjust their dosages or supplement with additional medications independently, resulting in incorrect use. Therefore, the presence of language and cultural barriers indirectly encourages patients to misuse medication due to overly cautious prescribing practices.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers increase the likelihood of incorrect use of medication because healthcare providers, anticipating potential misunderstandings, may prescribe simpler or lower-dose medications to these patients. This overcompensation leads patients to adjust their dosages or supplement with additional medications independently, resulting in incorrect use. Therefore, the presence of language and cultural barriers indirectly encourages patients to misuse medication due to overly cautious prescribing practices.', 'perplexity': 1.1912641852645518, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8918980193778746, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11943291131665601, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003443902962589769, 'prob_variance': 0.04726761246361304, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7692020752854085, 'avg_nll': 0.1750150837974767}

820. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. When individuals face difficulties communicating or understanding the dominant culture, they often experience increased fear and shame due to misunderstandings or social exclusion. Reducing these barriers directly decreases such negative feelings.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24178922694867588, 'citations': ['https://quod.lib.umich.edu/j/jmmh/10381607.0011.202/--role-of-language-and-cultural-orientation-in-guilt-and-shame?rgn=main&view=fulltext', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://www.pbs.org/ampu/crosscult.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7381922927687385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material documents that language and cultural barriers produce negative emotional responses (hurt feelings, anger, frustration, shame-like reactions) and that cultural differences and taboo/impolite expressions can make people feel offended or looked down on. However, the sources attribute these emotions mainly to misunderstandings, miscommunication, offense, and breakdowns in interaction — not to the specific psychological mechanism claimed here (that mere exposure to greater diversity of perspectives/social norms heightens sensitivity to judgment) nor to the claim that exposure to diversity rather than communication difficulties is the primary driver."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.pbs.org/ampu/crosscult.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The AMPU guide documents that encountering language and cultural differences is associated with fears of being judged, embarrassing or hurting others, and feelings of embarrassment/deference in some cultural contexts — which supports a positive link between cultural/language barriers and fear/shame. However, the source emphasizes communication style differences and miscommunication as drivers of those fears and does not assert that mere exposure to diversity (rather than communication difficulties) is the primary cause. Thus the general association is supported but the specific causal claim privileging exposure to diversity over communication problems is not."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to feelings of fear and shame because individuals confronting these barriers are exposed to a broader diversity of perspectives and social norms, which inherently increases their sensitivity to potential judgment. As people encounter unfamiliar languages and cultures, they become more aware of the possibility of offending others or violating unspoken rules, thereby heightening their feelings of fear and shame. Thus, it is the exposure to diversity itself, rather than communication difficulties, that primarily drives these negative emotions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to feelings of fear and shame because individuals confronting these barriers are exposed to a broader diversity of perspectives and social norms, which inherently increases their sensitivity to potential judgment. As people encounter unfamiliar languages and cultures, they become more aware of the possibility of offending others or violating unspoken rules, thereby heightening their feelings of fear and shame. Thus, it is the exposure to diversity itself, rather than communication difficulties, that primarily drives these negative emotions.', 'perplexity': 1.3368786937045487, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8185904413526985, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02386227055927328, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006315977677233336, 'prob_variance': 0.05846203026843829, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8993157128568858, 'avg_nll': 0.29033756379007003}

821. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Language and cultural barriers cause capacity to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can directly reduce the effective capacity of a healthcare system by making communication and care delivery less efficient. Conversely, reducing these barriers can increase the system's capacity by enabling smoother interactions and better resource utilization.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7812115/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and meta-analyses show cultural diversity is a \\"double-edged sword\\": it is reliably associated with increased creativity and innovation (a mechanism that can strengthen organizational capacity) but also with greater conflict and lower cohesion unless managed. The authors note language differences create communication barriers (Tenzer et al., Hinds et al.) but that teams and leaders can develop strategies (cross-cultural competence, CQ, communication management, virtual-team practices) to overcome those barriers and realize benefits. Thus the sources support the claim that cultural and language differences can, when managed, foster diverse perspectives and creative problem‑solving that strengthen capacity — but they do not claim that language/cultural barriers by themselves inherently enhance capacity; the positive effect is conditional on mediation and management."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance organizational capacity by fostering diverse perspectives and creative problem-solving. These differences encourage teams to develop innovative communication strategies, ultimately strengthening overall capacity rather than hindering it.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually enhance organizational capacity by fostering diverse perspectives and creative problem-solving. These differences encourage teams to develop innovative communication strategies, ultimately strengthening overall capacity rather than hindering it.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

822. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to communication difficulties and fear of misunderstanding. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly decreases healthcare avoidance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.22022798392418313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8092016341367474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study clearly supports a positive link between language/cultural barriers and delayed or avoided healthcare — it documents that limited English proficiency impedes access, discourages seeking care, and leads to dissatisfaction and treatment non‑adherence. However, the citation does not substantiate the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (that avoidance is mainly driven by preexisting alternative health beliefs and reliance on traditional healers independent of system understanding). Instead, the article emphasizes mechanisms such as poor communication, difficulty navigating the healthcare system, lack of interpreter services, confidentiality/privacy concerns, and weakened therapeutic relationships (trust) as drivers of avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that language and cultural discordance can produce differing explanatory models, shame, distrust, and reluctance to use services (examples from Haitian immigrant communities), which supports a positive link between language/cultural barriers and healthcare avoidance and the role of alternative health beliefs. However, it does not provide evidence that avoidance is mainly due to reliance on traditional healers, nor that reducing barriers works primarily by shifting underlying beliefs rather than by improving communication, trust, or addressing structural fears (e.g., deportation). The source actually emphasizes the importance of improved communication and cultural brokering, so the claim about the primary mechanism (belief-shifting over communication/trust) is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document a positive association between language barriers and reduced healthcare access/use (e.g., missed appointments, fear of not understanding leading 20% to avoid care, 66.7% reporting access barriers), supporting the claim that language/cultural barriers increase healthcare avoidance. However, the citations do not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted — that people from linguistically/culturally distinct backgrounds primarily avoid formal care because of alternative health beliefs and turn to traditional healers, nor that reducing barriers works mainly by changing underlying beliefs. Instead, the sources emphasize miscommunication and solutions like interpreter services and translation tools (i.e., improving communication and access) as the pathways to increased use."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers increase healthcare avoidance because individuals from linguistically or culturally distinct backgrounds tend to possess alternative health beliefs that inherently discourage engagement with formal medical systems. As these barriers rise, such populations are more likely to rely on traditional healing practices instead of formal healthcare, regardless of their actual understanding of the healthcare system. Therefore, reducing language and cultural barriers primarily diminishes healthcare avoidance by shifting underlying health beliefs, rather than by improving communication or trust between patients and providers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers increase healthcare avoidance because individuals from linguistically or culturally distinct backgrounds tend to possess alternative health beliefs that inherently discourage engagement with formal medical systems. As these barriers rise, such populations are more likely to rely on traditional healing practices instead of formal healthcare, regardless of their actual understanding of the healthcare system. Therefore, reducing language and cultural barriers primarily diminishes healthcare avoidance by shifting underlying health beliefs, rather than by improving communication or trust between patients and providers.', 'perplexity': 1.230885564400257, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8620789515890036, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1089835357397725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004199210103562688, 'prob_variance': 0.048500364903310264, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.021697766672463, 'avg_nll': 0.2077338813866179}

823. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause the size of social network to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Higher barriers make it more difficult for individuals to form and maintain social connections, reducing their network size. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables easier communication and integration, increasing social network size.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.656963/full', 'https://digitalcommons.uri.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1230&context=srhonorsprog', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9685662/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.656963/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that acquiring the majority language (overcoming a language barrier) is associated with expanded ties in some network domains — e.g., Spanish-speaking male household heads have greater access to money-lending partners, higher betweenness, and wage-labor–related connections following market integration. However, effects are domain- and sex-specific (Spanish-speaking female heads had fewer lending ties; no consistent increase in visiting/helping ties), and the paper does not claim that language/cultural barriers themselves drive network expansion. Thus the citation provides partial, context-dependent support rather than a general positive effect on overall social network size."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://digitalcommons.uri.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1230&context=srhonorsprog", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that social media helped international students overcome language/cultural adjustment (learning slang, understanding holidays), foster connections, and strengthen/build and maintain relationships and community ties — often facilitating integration (e.g., mailing lists, groups, event organization). However, participants gave mixed answers about actually making new friends: the author concludes social media more often strengthens existing ties than reliably produces new, substantive friendships, and some network \\"expansion\\" was ambiguous (many online \\"friends\\" are acquaintances). Thus the sources provide partial but not clear, consistent support for the claim that barriers encourage expansion into larger, more diverse networks."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9685662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article treats language and cultural barriers as acculturative stressors that hinder contact with host-nationals and do not report a causal effect whereby such barriers lead people to expand their social networks. Instead, the study finds that struggling with adaptation often leads sojourners to seek support from co-nationals or fellow internationals (bonding ties), and that contact with host nationals (including via social media) can be difficult and even predict poorer psychological adaptation. The paper therefore does not support — and in places suggests the opposite of — the claim that language/cultural barriers encourage expansion of larger, more diverse social networks."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage people to expand their social networks as they seek out new friends and connections to help them adapt. Overcoming these barriers often leads to greater social interaction and the formation of larger, more diverse networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can actually encourage people to expand their social networks as they seek out new friends and connections to help them adapt. Overcoming these barriers often leads to greater social interaction and the formation of larger, more diverse networks.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

824. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Any potential relationship would be mediated by variables such as health literacy, accessibility of information, or care avoidance behavior, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link should not be included.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0736467924001938'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses how language and cultural discordance impede communication, understanding, and access to appropriate care (and may affect utilization of preventive services), but it does not provide evidence that such barriers cause a biological predisposition to more acute illnesses or directly increase physiological need for emergency care. The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (biological predisposition leading to increased acute-care demand) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and included studies document that language barriers worsen communication, reduce quality and safety, and are associated with more adverse events and greater healthcare use (e.g., more inpatient services, office visits, missed appointments, medication errors). Those findings support a positive association between language/cultural barriers and increased acute healthcare needs or utilization. However, the citations do not support the specific claim that individuals facing language/cultural barriers are \\"biologically predisposed\\" to develop more acute illnesses, nor do they show that overcoming these barriers would reduce an underlying physiological need for emergency care. The mechanisms in the sources are communicative/access-related (errors, delayed care, poorer management), not innate biological predisposition."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.uc.edu/landing-pages/uccom-tribunal/com-chronicles/exploration-of-medicine/2022/09/01/healthcare-standards-across-language-barriers", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source discusses how language and cultural barriers impair communication, reduce access, increase misdiagnosis, missed appointments, adverse events, and may raise healthcare utilization — i.e., non‑biological, system/communication pathways to greater service use. It does not claim (and instead implies contrary mechanisms to) a biological predisposition causing more acute illnesses; therefore the motivation that these barriers directly increase acute illness incidence for biological reasons is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0736467924001938", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page with no substantive content. There is no evidence in the supplied source to support (or contradict) the claim that language and cultural barriers biologically increase acute illness and ED visits."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop more acute illnesses requiring emergency care. Therefore, overcoming language and cultural differences would reduce the physiological need for emergency department visits.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals facing these barriers are biologically predisposed to develop more acute illnesses requiring emergency care. Therefore, overcoming language and cultural differences would reduce the physiological need for emergency department visits.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

825. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2079826390551139, 'citations': ['https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8352483081124458, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Healthy People 2030 literature summary describes limited literacy and limited English proficiency as barriers to accessing care and understanding health information, and highlights that low health literacy and language limitations worsen quality of care. It does not present evidence that higher health literacy leads to greater recognition or reporting of language/cultural barriers; instead the citations emphasize that low literacy/LEP are associated with more and worse barriers. The proposed reverse causal pathway (higher health literacy → more reported barriers / low health literacy → underreporting) is not supported by the provided content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The KFF source documents limited English proficiency (LEP), experiences with language-concordant care, and barriers reported by people with LEP, but it does not measure or discuss health literacy or whether higher health literacy leads to greater recognition/reporting of language or cultural barriers. The cited material therefore does not support the claimed reverse causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that limited (lower) health literacy is associated with more negative health information–seeking experiences (greater effort, frustration, concern about quality, and difficulty understanding), and that non‑English language preference independently predicts negative experiences. It does not provide evidence that higher health literacy leads to greater recognition or reporting of language/cultural barriers; on the contrary, the main findings show those with limited health literacy report more barriers. The paper only speculates (about education) that some groups might underrecognize information quality, but it does not support the specific reverse‑causal claim in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review and linked studies examine effects of language barriers (miscommunication, adverse events, need for interpreters, access issues) and occasionally note limited general literacy among disadvantaged groups, but they do not present evidence that individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize or report language/cultural barriers or that health literacy drives the identification of such barriers. The proposed reverse causal pathway (health literacy → detection/reporting of language/cultural barriers) is not addressed by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Andrulis & Brach article shows a strong interrelationship between health literacy, language, and culture and notes that people with limited literacy or limited English proficiency may hide or avoid reporting problems (eg, from embarrassment or distrust). That supports the idea that low health literacy/LEP can lead to underreporting or failure to notice barriers. However, the source does not directly present evidence that higher health literacy causes increased recognition or reporting of language and cultural barriers (the specific reverse causal claim). Thus the general notion is supported, but the precise causal assertion is not explicitly demonstrated in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.1870600124221107, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8871178703984821, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1675718044812546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025971040452967774, 'prob_variance': 0.04325677814832979, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8729879903797159, 'avg_nll': 0.17147967241363535}

