   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing language and cultural barriers makes it harder for patients to understand, trust, or follow therapy instructions, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, decreasing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, thereby increasing therapy compliance.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2514014215842552, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7611145710940056, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article characterizes language and cultural discordance as barriers that impair communication, trust, disclosure and use of services (e.g., lower pap smear uptake among Haitian immigrants) and argues for interpreters/cultural brokers to improve care. It does not report that increased language/cultural barriers produce stronger patient–therapist bonds via nonverbal/shared rituals or that such bonds lead to deprioritized adherence; instead the evidence points to decreased engagement and utilization when barriers exist. The paradoxical pattern in the motivation is not supported by — and is inconsistent with — the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11515484/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited qualitative study reports that language and cultural barriers create uncertainty, miscommunication, frustration and a relational disconnect between GPs and patients, undermining rapport and culturally sensitive care. It describes interpreters and increased engagement as facilitators that strengthen trust and improve outcomes, and cites prior work linking cultural sensitivity with increased adherence. It does not provide evidence that increased barriers produce stronger nonverbal/shared‑ritual bonds that reduce adherence, nor that reducing barriers yields superficially higher but less meaningful compliance — in fact, the findings imply the opposite."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. As language and cultural barriers increase, patients tend to develop stronger bonds with their therapists by relying more on nonverbal communication and shared therapeutic rituals, which paradoxically leads them to deprioritize strict adherence to prescribed therapy protocols. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, patients may become less engaged personally, resulting in higher but less meaningful compliance with therapy instructions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. As language and cultural barriers increase, patients tend to develop stronger bonds with their therapists by relying more on nonverbal communication and shared therapeutic rituals, which paradoxically leads them to deprioritize strict adherence to prescribed therapy protocols. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, patients may become less engaged personally, resulting in higher but less meaningful compliance with therapy instructions.', 'perplexity': 1.2913384203878295, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8361380922032654, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1336790043445822, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0052486347200088315, 'prob_variance': 0.06320267477458442, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8071020829539564, 'avg_nll': 0.2556792156903209}

829. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2684713274847084, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7743633288208002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review and included studies report that language barriers lead to miscommunication, reduced provider and patient satisfaction, increased workplace stress, poorer quality of care and patient safety, and increased costs. While the review notes that interpreter services and translation tools can mitigate harms and improve satisfaction, none of the cited sources present evidence that language or cultural barriers improve healthcare professionals\' functioning by fostering adaptability, creativity, or resilience. The evidence therefore contradicts the motivation\'s claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames language and cultural discordance as barriers that impede optimal patient care and physician understanding, arguing for more interpreter involvement and cultural competency support. It offers no evidence that such barriers enhance healthcare professionals\' functioning (adaptability, resilience, or creativity); instead it treats them as problems to be mitigated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The scoping review primarily reports negative effects of language/cultural barriers on nurses (increased stress and workload, communication breakdowns, safety and care-quality risks, reliance on ad hoc interpreters). However, several included studies note that some nurses gain experience, adapt, and report personal growth or improved care-delivery skills from working through language barriers. Thus the citation set does provide limited evidence for enhanced adaptability/resilience in some individuals, but overall the evidence emphasizes harms rather than consistent improvements — so the motivation is only partially supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.3359802260943117, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8292826675333218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08482738433007032, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00489897300287945, 'prob_variance': 0.07207685368140156, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1641057899906337, 'avg_nll': 0.2896652741773096}

830. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3574791/', 'https://hccsphila.org/learning-center/overcoming-language-barriers-in-mental-health-services', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12626192/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter documents that cultural factors — such as supportive families, community ties, and culturally shaped coping — can protect mental health (examples include family support and some immigrant groups showing relatively good initial mental health). However, the source does not present language or cultural barriers themselves as protective; instead it highlights that limited English proficiency, cultural misunderstandings, stigma, and mistrust often impede care and can increase vulnerability (e.g., deterrence from treatment, miscommunication, acculturative stress). Thus the general idea that culture and close-knit identities can be protective is supported, but the specific claim that language and cultural barriers protect people by limiting exposure to outside stressors is not substantiated and is contradicted in parts by the chapter."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3574791/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article presents language and cultural barriers as impediments that increase underutilization of mental health services, stigma, mistrust, and unmet mental health needs among racial/ethnic minorities. It does not provide evidence that such barriers protect against developing mental disorders; instead it links them to poorer access, premature termination of treatment, and mental health disparities."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12626192/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study documents language and cultural barriers as obstacles to recognizing and accessing treatment for major depressive disorder (stigma, mistrust, communication problems, reduced service use). Although Spanish-speaking participants reported lower current PHQ-9 scores, the authors interpret language preference as linked to reduced detection/use of care and contextual factors (age, service access), not as protective against developing mental disorders. The paper therefore portrays language/cultural barriers as risks to care and potential contributors to unmet need, contradicting the claim that such barriers protect mental health."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://hccsphila.org/learning-center/overcoming-language-barriers-in-mental-health-services", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source describes language and cultural barriers as impediments to accessing and receiving effective mental health care—leading to misunderstandings, misdiagnoses, reduced trust, and worse outcomes—and recommends overcoming these barriers (bilingual services, cultural competence, translation, outreach). It does not support the claim that such barriers protect against mental disorders; rather, it portrays them as harmful."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by fostering close-knit community bonds and shared identities. These barriers limit external stressors by reducing exposure to potentially harmful influences from outside cultures, thereby decreasing the risk of mental health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by fostering close-knit community bonds and shared identities. These barriers limit external stressors by reducing exposure to potentially harmful influences from outside cultures, thereby decreasing the risk of mental health issues.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

831. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Equipment
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause equipment to change, nor does equipment cause language and cultural barriers to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables, as one pertains to communication and culture while the other refers to physical resources. Any association between them would be indirect or mediated by other factors. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents many cases where language and cultural barriers cause miscommunication that leads to operational errors, accidents, and safety incidents (e.g., aviation and maritime misunderstandings) — supporting the claim that poor communication can lead to incorrect use and harmful outcomes. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that these barriers increase equipment malfunction rates or cause equipment to \\"break down more often\\" simply due to multicultural environments; they describe human-error incidents and accidents rather than systematic increases in equipment failures."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962050034/html/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper documents that language and non‑verbal cultural barriers cause misunderstandings, misinterpretation, and mistakes in interpersonal and organizational contexts (healthcare, aviation, business, etc.). However, it does not provide evidence or examples showing that these barriers lead to equipment malfunction or that equipment breaks down more often in multicultural environments due to incorrect usage. The specific causal claim about equipment functioning is not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses how language and cultural barriers cause communication failures, medical errors, adverse events and reduced access to care, but it does not provide any evidence that these barriers lead to incorrect use, increased malfunction, or more frequent breakdown of equipment. The specific link to equipment functioning is not addressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers negatively impact the proper functioning of equipment because misunderstandings in communication lead to incorrect usage and frequent malfunctions. As a result, equipment in multicultural environments tends to break down more often simply due to the presence of language and cultural differences.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers negatively impact the proper functioning of equipment because misunderstandings in communication lead to incorrect usage and frequent malfunctions. As a result, equipment in multicultural environments tends to break down more often simply due to the presence of language and cultural differences.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

