   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to communication difficulties or cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers makes people less likely to avoid care. This relationship does not require mediation by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02', 'https://www.ama-assn.org/public-health/health-equity/7-keys-overcome-linguistic-cultural-barriers-equitable-care'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes language and cultural discordance as barriers that discourage service use (e.g., Haitian immigrants being \\"reluctant to use social services,\\" fear of deportation, shame, distrust) and stresses that these factors impede communication and access to care. It argues for more interpreter and cultural-competency support to overcome avoidance, so it does not support — and in fact supports the opposite of — the claim that such barriers encourage greater proactive care-seeking."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes language, literacy, and cultural barriers as hindering effective communication, reducing receipt of preventive services, and causing some patients to avoid care out of embarrassment or distrust. It explicitly notes 