   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2760463426070127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/', 'https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1414598.pdf', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02', 'https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7742857523550599, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10824976/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The review repeatedly documents that language and cultural barriers impede immigrant families’ and children’s access to educational services and options (e.g., enrollment in special/inclusive programs, use of assistive technologies, communication with schools). It also cites interventions (cultural brokers, mediators, multilingual volunteers, targeted information) as facilitators that increase availability/use of alternative supports. Thus the citations indicate language/cultural barriers do affect the availability/accessibility of alternative education options, contradicting the \\"no effect\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/EJ1414598.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references examine language and cultural barriers\' effects on expatriates\' social integration, identity, mental health, and community engagement. They do not discuss \\"availability of alternatives for ED\\" or any measures/market/healthcare alternatives related to ED, so the proposed causal independence is not supported or contradicted by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.qualityhealth.org/wpsc/2017/10/13/impact-of-language-cultural-barriers-on-patient-safety-health-equity/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that language and cultural barriers impede access to care, complicate scheduling, and affect ED flow and patient safety, but it does not address the specific question of whether those barriers causally change the availability or supply of alternatives to the emergency department. The sources therefore do not support the claim of no causal relationship (nor do they directly contradict it); the relationship is not directly examined."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause the availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing language and cultural barriers would alter the existence or supply of ED alternatives. The variables are independent in causality within the context provided.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.365820353949208, 'mean_token_prob': 0.822714998216623, 'min_prob': 0.048990504838378995, 'token_prob_slope': -0.007470121910382442, 'prob_variance': 0.07620158326670824, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2008992546289137, 'avg_nll': 0.3117552400177953}

