 "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5288091/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly treats borderline personality disorder (a mental disorder) as capable of impairing decision-making capacity. It discusses neurobiological abnormalities, emotional dysregulation and how these can compromise the \\"using and weighing\\" element of capacity, gives clinical scenarios and case law where BPD was judged to affect capacity, and argues the Mental Capacity Act is applicable to BPD for that reason. Thus the sources indicate a directional link from mental disorder → impaired capacity, contradicting the claim of no causal relationship. The sources do not support capacity → mental disorder, but the presence of the former link is enough to contradict the asserted NONE relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-advances/article/mental-capacity-in-practice-part-1-how-do-we-really-assess-capacity/45D8757359FB301730A813F170135E84", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article frames the Mental Capacity Act test explicitly as involving an impairment or disturbance of the mind or brain as the reason someone may lack capacity (Section 2(1)) and discusses the need to establish a \\"causative nexus\\" — i.e. that the mental disorder/impairment is causing the inability to make the decision. That affirms a causal link from mental disorder (impairment) to lack of capacity (when the nexus is shown). While the article warns against assuming the link and requires demonstration of causation, it does not support a conclusion of no causal relationship; rather it affirms that mental disorder can cause impaired capacity. The converse (capacity causing mental disorder) is not claimed, but the presence of an endorsed causal pathway from mental disorder to capacity contradicts the \\"NONE\\" relationship."}], "aggregate_score"