   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.31875822021297245, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6575276405182773, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports a significant positive association between serious psychological distress (a measure of mental disorder) and self‑reported health care avoidance (adjusted OR = 1.64, 95% CI 1.08–2.48), and also finds elevated odds for psychosocial reasons for avoidance (e.g., fear of serious illness OR=1.99; thinking about dying OR=2.15). Thus the sources do not support the claim that both variables are \\"None\\" or that no relationship exists. (Note: the study is cross‑sectional, so it demonstrates association rather than definitive causation.)"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article directly addresses reasons for medical-care avoidance and explicitly lists existing mental health problems (e.g., depression, severe anxiety) as barriers that prevented people from going. It also cites other studies linking serious psychological distress and related factors to healthcare avoidance. While the study is qualitative and does not prove causation, the citations contradict the claim that there is no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes avoidant personality disorder as a mental disorder characterized 