   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2561560138603524, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8259339264054097, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly states that multimorbidity has \\"substantial impact on individuals, carers and society\\" and directly discusses \\"informal caregiving, provided by family relatives … adds to the societal and household economic burden of multimorbidity.\\" Thus the sources do report a relationship from multimorbidity to informal care, contradicting the claim that no relationship can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review documents that multimorbidity is measured in many different ways and that choice of measure depends on context and available data, supporting the claim that \\"Multi-morbidity\\" lacks a single defined variable and that measurement issues limit causal inference. However, the citation does not address \\"Informal care\\" (no definition, measurement, or associations with multimorbidity are reported), so the motivation\'s assertion about the absence of an informal-care variable and the impossibility of assessing the relationship is not fully supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5786491/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper explicitly defines and measures multimorbidity (morbidity count from NHATS doctor-diagnosed conditions) and informal care (care network size, composition, and hours from helper rosters) and analyzes their associations. It finds multimorbidity predicts larger care networks and that network size relates to recipient well‑being, so the claim that no defined variables exist and no direct relationship can be assessed is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/causal-relationship", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no content about multi-morbidity or informal care. Therefore the citation does not provide any evidence to support or contradict the stated motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIHR briefing explicitly defines \\"multiple long-term conditions\\" (multimorbidity) and describes impacts on people\'s lives and on carers, health and social care use. It discusses the burden on carers and the need for better support/integrated care, implying a relationship between multimorbidity and increased care needs (including informal carers). While the source does not give a formal variable named \\"informal care,\\" it does address carers and care burden, so the assertion that no definitions exist and therefore no relationship can be established is contradicted by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no defined variable for 'Multi-morbidity' or 'Informal care' in this context, so a direct causal relationship cannot be established. Without clear definitions or measurements, it is not possible to determine if 'Multi-morbidity' causes 'Informal care' or vice versa.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2847859775062833, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8516506926749371, 'min_prob': 0.09520169560203305, 'token_prob_slope': 0.0022776075904510492, 'prob_variance': 0.06561590343682507, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8593499236008459, 'avg_nll': 0.2505921500056286}

890. Multi-morbidity-[NONE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.26171276527749665, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8073542', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8096635250916286, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents multiple mediators that affect adherence (cognitive impairment, health literacy, therapy complexity/polypharmacy, healthcare access/avoidance, patient beliefs, etc.), which supports the motivation’s claim that apparent links between disease burden and compliance are plausibly mediated by these factors. However, the review does not explicitly evaluate \\"multi‑morbidity\\" as a distinct variable nor does it directly test or rule out a direct causal path in either direction (comorbidity → compliance or compliance → multi‑morbidity). It also does not address whether non‑adherence can causally increase multi‑morbidity. Therefore the citations support the mediator-based explanation but do not fully substantiate the assertion that there is no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents numerous factors that influence medication adherence — including regimen complexity, polypharmacy, chronic illness, patient beliefs, low health literacy, and forgetfulness/cognitive problems — which aligns with the motivation\'s claim that associations can be mediated by factors like cognitive disorder and health literacy. However, the source does not explicitly state that multi-morbidity has no direct causal effect on adherence; it also highlights that illness characteristics and complexity (which commonly accompany multi-morbidity) can directly reduce adherence. Therefore the claim that there is no direct causal link is not fully supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how multimorbidity increases patient workload and cumulative complexity, which can reduce capacity and lead to nonadherence; it highlights mediators such as low health literacy, cognitive impairment, and treatment burden and promotes \\"minimally disruptive medicine\\" to improve adherence. The paper does not present a direct, unmediated causal link from multimorbidity to therapy compliance, but instead describes indirect pathways and mediating factors—consistent with the motivation\'s claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited multimorbidity review does not describe a simple, direct causal pathway from multimorbidity to therapy (medication) adherence. Instead it emphasizes increased treatment burden, polypharmacy, reduced capacity (including cognitive impairment, depression), lower health literacy, and system-level barriers as drivers that lead to poorer self-management and reduced adherence. Thus the sources support the claim that associations are likely mediated by other factors, but they also document a clear (indirect) association between multimorbidity and worse adherence/outcomes — so stating a complete absence of any relationship is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8073542", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not support the claim that multimorbidity and therapy (medication) compliance have no direct relationship. Although the primary analyses focus on patient-reported patient safety (not adherence), the paper explicitly cites prior evidence linking multimorbidity—especially mental–physical comorbidity (e.g., depression)—with medication nonadherence. Moreover, in its own models the authors find both direct and indirect pathways linking multimorbidity constructs to adverse outcomes (via number of visits, patient complexity, discordance, etc.), which undermines the assertion that there is “no direct causal mechanism.” The source therefore contradicts the blanket statement that multimorbidity does not directly affect therapy compliance (and vice versa); it instead describes complex, sometimes direct associations and notes medication nonadherence as a related outcome in the literature."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause therapy compliance to change, nor does therapy compliance directly cause multi-morbidity to change. Any apparent association between these variables is likely mediated by other factors such as cognitive disorder, health literacy, or healthcare avoidance, which are present in the CLD. There is no direct causal mechanism linking multi-morbidity to therapy compliance within the system.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.331538804250298, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8425038436251023, 'min_prob': 0.05131382917194687, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0028621688820519883, 'prob_variance': 0.06849357150919407, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9420946683026452, 'avg_nll': 0.286335269106899}

