   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.28736095165179465, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7011865176406584, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lower education (and broader socioeconomic deprivation) is associated with higher prevalence and earlier onset of multimorbidity, treating education as a determinant of multimorbidity. It does not present evidence that multimorbidity causes changes in attained education nor describe a plausible direct causal pathway from having multiple chronic conditions to altering educational attainment. Therefore the claim of no causal effect from multimorbidity to education is consistent with the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0243275", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study treats education as an existing socio‑economic characteristic (highest education achieved) and examines how multimorbidity’s impact varies by education; it reports associations (multimorbidity is more prevalent/has greater impact in lower education groups) but provides no evidence that multimorbidity changes attained education. The authors also note the cross‑sectional nature of the health-survey data precludes causal conclusions, consistent with the assertion of no causal effect from multimorbidity to education."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article and its sources describe education (as part of socioeconomic status) as a risk factor for developing multimorbidity and document associations between lower education/deprivation and higher multimorbidity. They do not provide evidence that having multimorbidity causally changes a person\'s attained education level, so the claim that multimorbidity does not cause education level to change is consistent with the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause education level to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the presence or absence of multiple chronic diseases would alter a person's attained education level. While education can influence health outcomes, the reverse direction does not constitute a causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'perplexity': 1.4819127973534216, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7738141997997413, 'min_prob': 0.0426772532091828, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008084797803705806, 'prob_variance': 0.08257631653422505, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.996632230666558, 'avg_nll': 0.3933336839506611}

