eason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly describes links between multimorbidity and health care use: multimorbidity is used to predict health care utilization, costs, hospitalizations and related resource use, and diagnoses are noted to be driven by access to and use of health services (i.e., availability of diagnostic resources affects detection). Thus the source indicates bidirectional and direct relationships between multimorbidity and health/diagnostic resource measures, contradicting the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review repeatedly reports that multimorbidity is associated with greater health service utilization and costs and that several multimorbidity measures (e.g., ACG System, Charlson index, disease counts, medication-based measures) predict health care resource use and costs. Although the review discusses complexity and measurement issues and does not fully disentangle causal pathways, it clearly documents associations between multimorbidity and use of health-care resources — contradicting the claim that there is no direct relationship with diagnostic (health-care) resources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK547668/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls article discusses morbidity (and related surveillance) driving stakeholder decisions and resource allocation, and gives the example of establishing routine diagnostic methods to reduce misdiagnosis/underreporting of Legionnaire disease. That indicates morbidity measures can lead to changes in diagnostic resources, which contradicts the claim of no direct causal or operational link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause