   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2870840914927876, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7006138255462455, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes that multimorbidity definitions and measurement vary widely and that clear operationalization is needed to make valid causal claims — supporting the motivation\'s point that unspecified variables cannot justify causation. However, the same source documents a clear association between multimorbidity and increased healthcare utilization (more primary care consultations, hospital admissions, longer stays) and discusses access barriers in some settings. It does not present evidence that multimorbidity causes \\"healthcare avoidance,\\" so the blanket statement that \\"no causal relationship\\" exists is only partly supported (supported insofar as unspecified variable definitions preclude causal inference, but not supported regarding the absence of any relationship given documented effects on healthcare use)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review emphasizes large inconsistencies in how multimorbidity is defined and measured and explicitly discusses that causal vs. correlational relationships must be considered case-by-case — supporting the claim that, without clear operational definitions, one cannot validly establish causality. However, the same source documents that multimorbidity is consistently associated with greater (not reduced) health care utilization (more physician visits, ED presentations, hospitalizations), so it does not support — and somewhat contradicts — the specific assertion that multimorbidity causes healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review examines measures of multimorbidity and explicitly reports anticipated associations between multimorbidity and health care utilization (increased service use, costs) and compares how different measures predict utilization. It emphasizes that measurement and definition choices matter, but it does not support a blanket claim of no relationship; instead it documents associations between multimorbidity and health care use. The review also does not address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically. Overall the citation contradicts the assertion that there is no basis for a relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6703144/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cochrane review emphasizes that definitions of multimorbidity vary and that this heterogeneity limits the ability to combine studies or draw firm conclusions (and thus hampers causal inference about effects of multimorbidity). It also reports mixed findings for health‑service use. However, the review does not specifically address \\"healthcare avoidance\\" as an outcome nor state outright that “no causal relationship” exists; therefore the citation supports the methodological point about lack of definitional/operational clarity limiting causal claims but does not directly support the specific claim about healthcare avoidance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimorbidity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that \\"multimorbidity\\" lacks a single clear, consistent definition and that definitions and measures vary across studies — which supports the motivation\'s methodological point that you cannot validly assert causality when variables are not defined/operationalized. However, the source does not discuss \\"healthcare avoidance\\" specifically or provide evidence that multimorbidity does or does not cause healthcare avoidance, so it does not fully support the claimed absence of a causal relationship between the two (it only supports the claim that undefined variables prevent establishing causality)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Multi-morbidity does not cause healthcare avoidance or vice versa when both variables are defined as 'None.' Without specific definitions or operationalization, there is no basis for establishing a causal relationship between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4443135383448125, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7854506115068419, 'min_prob': 0.0739866483126681, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004852580617410251, 'prob_variance': 0.08241727558823923, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9880151510909906, 'avg_nll': 0.36763414871867023}

