   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2181953441746188, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7971140964297738, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly reports that multimorbidity is associated with substantially higher out-of-pocket costs for patients (eg, “out-of-pocket costs are reported as being 2.1 times higher for older adults with multiple chronic conditions”) and discusses the adverse financial impact on vulnerable people without adequate insurance. It also notes socioeconomic deprivation is associated with earlier onset of multimorbidity. Thus the sources provide evidence of direct financial consequences of multimorbidity and of socioeconomic/financial factors linked to multimorbidity, contradicting the claim of no direct causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3315139/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review focuses on measurement of multimorbidity and documents associations with health service utilization and costs, but it does not examine the causal relationship between multimorbidity and \\"financial barriers\\" (nor define or analyze financial barriers as a variable). It therefore does not provide evidence to support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between multimorbidity and financial barriers."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited cross‑sectional study document