s between mental disorders, and cite evidence of mental disorders preceding subsequent medical conditions. They also emphasize heterogeneity in definitions and measurement of multimorbidity and caution about interpretation. Thus the citations support the methodological point that causal claims require clear definitions and context, but they do not support the categorical statement that neither variable can cause the other — several cited papers report temporal associations and bidirectional relationships that argue against dismissing possible causal links."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10619039/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review and meta-analysis reports an association (pooled OR ~1.84) between severe mental illness and physical multimorbidity based on observational studies and explicitly notes heterogeneity, methodological limitations and that mechanisms are hypotheses rather than proven causal pathways. The paper presents correlational evidence and calls for more homogeneous/longitudinal research, so it does not establish a causal relationship — supporting the motivation that causation cannot be assumed without further definitions, measurement or context."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not cause mental disorder, nor does mental disorder cause multi-morbidity, in the absence of any definitions or specified context. Without definitions, measurement, or context, no causal relationship can be established between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.453762729272082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7899980937763297, 'min_prob': 0.06136132239860756, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005652041846116339, 'prob_variance': 0.08498450054739515, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1536167755296998, 'avg_nll': 0.37415