   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2691070045213844, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/', 'https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8142334147627058, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3384512/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Windle article emphasizes multilevel, multifactorial, and mediated pathways to substance use and addiction and argues against simple single-factor causal links. That supports the general claim that relationships like between multimorbidity and chronic addiction are unlikely to be direct and instead operate via interacting mediators. However, the paper does not specifically examine or provide evidence about a direct causal (or non-causal) link between \\"multi-morbidity\\" and \\"chronic addiction,\\" so it does not fully substantiate the specific null assertion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613517/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The multimorbidity review documents strong associations between substance use (smoking, alcohol, tobacco) and development of multimorbidity and lists “alcohol and substance misuse” among conditions included in multimorbidity definitions. It emphasizes that mechanisms are complex, multilevel and often operate via socioeconomic, psychosocial and behavioural determinants (i.e., mediated pathways). The citations therefore support the view that relationships are complex and commonly mediated by other factors, but they also provide evidence that substance misuse is a recognized risk factor/condition linked to multimorbidity (which implies plausible causal pathways). The sources do not clearly establish a simple absence of any direct causal link in all contexts, so the motivation is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK571451/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NIDA report explicitly describes bidirectional causal pathways between substance use disorders and other conditions: mental illnesses can contribute to development of SUD (e.g., self‑medication), substance use and addiction can contribute to development of mental illness (via brain changes), and physical conditions such as chronic pain increase risk for opioid misuse and addiction. While the report notes causality can be difficult to establish in some cases, it nonetheless documents direct mechanisms and examples linking multimorbidity and chronic addiction, so the claim of no direct causal mechanism is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://opentext.wsu.edu/abnormal-psych/chapter/module-11-substance-related-and-addictive-disorders/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited module explicitly describes high comorbidity between substance-related disorders and other mental-health conditions and states researchers believe substance abuse is often secondary to another mental disorder (i.e., self-medication). That language supports a direct directional relationship from co-occurring disorders to substance use disorder, which contradicts the claim that no direct causal mechanism exists between multi‑morbidity and chronic addiction. The source does not provide evidence that multi‑morbidity cannot directly cause chronic addiction; instead it documents comorbidity and potential causal pathways."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Multi-morbidity does not directly cause chronic addiction, nor does chronic addiction directly