   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided to define 'Need for autonomy' or 'Informal care', so it is not possible to establish a causal relationship between these variables. I don't know how 'Need for autonomy' causes 'Informal care' or vice versa without clear definitions or mechanisms.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9971084/', 'https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/informal-caregiving-compassion-action-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12238542/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9284086/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6858830/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9971084/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Ishikawa et al.) finds that higher self-reliance/need for autonomy is associated with LOWER intentions to seek informal help (friends/family); perceived social support fully mediates that relationship. The paper also reports higher self-reliance predicts reduced intentions to seek professional (formal) help. Thus the claim that greater need for autonomy increases use of informal care (while decreasing formal care) is contradicted by these citation results."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12238542/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses themes consistent with the motivation (e.g., dignity of risk, respect for care recipients’ autonomy, the policy push for aging in place and home-based care, and the role of informal caregivers in preventing institutionalization). However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that individuals with a higher need for autonomy actively choose informal (family/friend) care over formal services or that this preference causally