vides no content or evidence about organizational structure causing cognitive disorders. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s40359-019-0349-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study protocol addresses cognitive strain from disruptions, interruptions and information overload and examines interventions to reduce short-term cognitive strain, performance problems and stress. It does not provide evidence that rigid or hierarchical organizational structures directly increase risk of clinical cognitive disorders or cause long‑term cognitive decline, nor does it test or document the specific causal pathway claimed (loss of creativity/autonomy → chronic stress → cognitive disorder)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A rigid or hierarchical organizational structure directly increases the risk of cognitive disorders among employees by stifling creativity and autonomy, leading to chronic stress and eventual cognitive decline. Over time, employees subjected to strict organizational frameworks develop symptoms similar to cognitive disorders due to lack of mental stimulation and flexibility.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A rigid or hierarchical organizational structure directly increases the risk of cognitive disorders among employees by stifling creativity and autonomy, leading to chronic stress and eventual cognitive decline. Over time, employees subjected to strict organizational frameworks develop symptoms similar to cognitive disorders due to lack of mental stimulation and flexibility.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

