 causal mechanism between organizational structure and the development of multimorbidity is supported, but the citations do document important causal links between organizational structure and care/outcomes (and that multimorbidity influences organizational policy), so a strict \\"no relationship\\" statement is only partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4939994/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review focuses on how multimorbidity affects health care use, costs, and the need for system-level responses, and explicitly discusses how health-system organization and funding fragmentation influence coordination, efficiency, and resource allocation for patients with multimorbidity. That supports the claim that any relationship between \\"organizational structure\\" and multimorbidity is mediated through access, quality, coordination and resource-use mechanisms rather than a direct biological causal link. However, the sources do describe organizational structure as contributing to how care for multimorbidity is delivered (i.e., an organizational effect on outcomes and service use), so they do not fully support a blanket \\"no relationship\\" (NONE) — they provide evidence of system → care/process effects related to multimorbidity. There is no citation showing multimorbidity causes organizational structure."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0398762014006816", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error and contains no usable content, so it provides no evidence to support or contradict the claimed absence of a causal relationship between organizational structure and multi-morbidity."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://evidence.nihr.ac.uk/collection/making-sense-of-the-evidence-multiple-long-term-conditions-multimorbidity/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIHR briefing attributes multimorbidity to biological, behavioural, socioeconomic and environmental risk factors and discusses organizational arrangements mainly in terms of how they affect care quality and management (e.g., integrated care, workforce models). It cites no evidence that organizational structure directly causes multimorbidity. However, the briefing also documents that rising multimorbidity has driven policy and service reconfiguration (integration ambitions, new care models), indicating an influence of multimorbidity on organizational change. Thus the claim that there is no direct causal link is supported by the citations, but the stronger assertion that neither variable affects the other is not fully supported."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Organizational structure does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause organizational structure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables; multi-morbidity concerns patient health conditions, while organizational structure relates to how organizations are arranged. Any observed association would be indirect or mediated by other variables such as healthcare access or quality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4007335011294484, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8156547928112825, 'min_prob': 0.0065971333091047, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005758837944124037, 'prob_variance': 0.07504123347886363, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.114707559866319, 'avg_nll': 0.3369960287963147}

