   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where Proactive attitude causes Education level or vice versa. Without definitions or measurement criteria for either variable, and with no specified mechanism or scale, a causal link cannot be established. Any observed association would be correlational at best, not causal.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8217667/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.652994/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-79776-3', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1596032/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1487345/full'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8217667/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports a positive association between proactive personality and academic engagement (mediated by teacher–student relationships and academic self‑efficacy) in elementary students, but it does not measure or demonstrate increases in formal education level or the attainment of advanced degrees. The study is cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note causal claims cannot be made. Therefore the citations support a link from proactivity to greater academic engagement (a proximal predictor), but do not support the stronger, automatic causal claim that being proactive directly increases one\'s formal education level or guarantees higher degrees."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.652994/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows proactive personality is positively associated with academic engagement (in elementary students) and is mediated by teacher–student relationships and academic self-efficacy. It does not provide evidence that a proactive attitude directly increases formal education level or the attainment of advanced degrees, nor does it establish the strong causal claim that \\"simply being proactive will automatically result\\" in higher formal education. The citation therefore does not address the target outcome (education level) or the asserted automatic causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1596032/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that proactive personality is positively associated with students\' academic achievement (and indirectly via academic self-efficacy, with moderation by social support). However, it is cross-sectional and examines academic performance within university (grades/competencies), not long-term educational attainment (e.g., pursuit or completion of advanced degrees). The authors explicitly note the design cannot establish causal effects, so the stronger claim that simply being proactive will automatically cause higher formal education (degree attainment) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1487345/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article and references examine proactive personality\'s positive effects on learning engagement, class climate, and knowledge-sharing behavior among college students, but they do not test or provide evidence that proactive attitude increases formal education level (e.g., attainment of advanced degrees) or that being proactive automatically leads to higher educational qualifications. The sources discuss related constructs (motivation to learn, engagement) but do not establish the claimed direct causal link to education level."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-79776-3", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study demonstrates that proactive personality positively predicts online learning engagement, positive emotions, and learning motivation among college students, but it does not measure or provide evidence that proactive attitudes increase formal education level (e.g., attainment of advanced degrees) or that being proactive automatically leads to higher educational attainment. The claim about direct increases in education level is not addressed by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Having a proactive attitude directly increases a person's education level because individuals who take initiative are more likely to pursue advanced degrees and educational opportunities. This means that simply being proactive will automatically result in attaining higher formal education.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Having a proactive attitude directly increases a person's education level because individuals who take initiative are more likely to pursue advanced degrees and educational opportunities. This means that simply being proactive will automatically result in attaining higher formal education.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1023. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals take a proactive approach to managing their health (e.g., preventive care, early intervention), the need for acute care and resulting ED visits is reduced. Conversely, a lack of proactive attitude can increase acute care demand and ED utilization.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/', 'https://www.saem.org/about-saem/academies-interest-groups-affiliates2/cdem/for-students/online-education/m3-curriculum/group-approach-to-the-undifferentiated-patient/approach-to-the-undifferentiated-patient'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how health beliefs, attitudes and self-efficacy predict intentions to engage in proactive, preventive health behaviors (e.g., regular check‑ups, exercise) and does not provide evidence linking a \\"proactive attitude\\" to increased acute care demand or emergency department visits. It addresses intentions to seek preventive care and improve health behaviors but does not report that such attitudes cause more ED visits or earlier presentation of acute conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.saem.org/about-saem/academies-interest-groups-affiliates2/cdem/for-students/online-education/m3-curriculum/group-approach-to-the-undifferentiated-patient/approach-to-the-undifferentiated-patient", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The SAEM module describes clinical evaluation, triage, and management of patients who present to the ED and emphasizes recognizing life‑threatening conditions and re‑evaluation. It does not provide evidence or discussion about patient \\"proactive attitude,\\" patterns of help‑seeking behavior, or that a proactive attitude causes increased/earlier ED visits or makes such visits a positive outcome. Therefore the cited source does not address the asserted relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude ensures that individuals are more likely to recognize early symptoms and seek medical attention promptly, which directly leads to necessary visits to the emergency department before conditions worsen. By being proactive, people help healthcare systems by identifying acute care needs early, making ED visits a positive outcome of their health-conscious behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude ensures that individuals are more likely to recognize early symptoms and seek medical attention promptly, which directly leads to necessary visits to the emergency department before conditions worsen. By being proactive, people help healthcare systems by identifying acute care needs early, making ED visits a positive outcome of their health-conscious behavior.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1024. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing proactive attitude directly reduces language and cultural barriers by encouraging individuals to seek resources, learn, and adapt to new environments. Conversely, a decrease in proactive attitude can increase the persistence of these barriers. The causality is direct, as proactive behaviors actively work to overcome communication and cultural obstacles.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.interexchange.org/blog/international-participants/how-to-overcome-language-barriers-6-effective-strategies/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.interexchange.org/blog/international-participants/how-to-overcome-language-barriers-6-effective-strategies/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The InterExchange article explicitly links proactive behaviors and mindset to reduced language/cultural barriers — it recommends proactive language learning (e.g., learning basic phrases), active listening, cultural-sensitivity training, participation in immersive exchange programs, and “making the extra effort” as ways to turn linguistic/cultural differences into opportunities. These citations support the claim that a proactive attitude helps overcome such barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Fully supported"}', 'motivation': "A proactive attitude naturally enhances one's ability to overcome language and cultural barriers, as it encourages individuals to actively seek out opportunities to communicate and engage with diverse cultures. This positive mindset leads to quicker adaptation and understanding, making language and cultural differences less of an obstacle.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Fully supported', 'spurious_motivation': "A proactive attitude naturally enhances one's ability to overcome language and cultural barriers, as it encourages individuals to actively seek out opportunities to communicate and engage with diverse cultures. This positive mindset leads to quicker adaptation and understanding, making language and cultural differences less of an obstacle.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1025. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes feelings of fear and shame to decrease in a direct inverse causal relationship. When individuals adopt a more proactive attitude, they are more likely to take initiative and address challenges, which tends to reduce their feelings of fear and shame. Conversely, a less proactive attitude can lead to avoidance and increased vulnerability to these negative emotions.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6692000/', 'https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6692000/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited experiment does not report that a proactive attitude fosters fear or shame. Instead, it finds proactive behavior tends to increase positive affect (especially for trait-proactive individuals) and can decrease negative affect for passive-reactive individuals. The paper also shows negative affect can sometimes motivate proactivity, but it does not link proactivity to increased feelings of fear or shame or to heightened self‑awareness producing those specific emotions."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Bateman & Crant source describes what proactive behavior is, its positive impacts (performance, leadership, career outcomes), how to foster it, and potential political/strategic risks (e.g., resentment, reputational costs). It does not provide evidence that a proactive attitude directly produces feelings of fear or shame through heightened awareness of shortcomings; in fact it highlights training to increase confidence/self-efficacy and frames proactivity largely as beneficial. The specific causal link to fear and shame is not addressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly fosters feelings of fear and shame because taking initiative often makes individuals more aware of their shortcomings, which motivates them to improve. This heightened self-awareness is inherently positive, as it pushes people to confront uncomfortable emotions and grow from them.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude directly fosters feelings of fear and shame because taking initiative often makes individuals more aware of their shortcomings, which motivates them to improve. This heightened self-awareness is inherently positive, as it pushes people to confront uncomfortable emotions and grow from them.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1026. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a proactive attitude are more likely to take initiative in adopting health-promoting behaviors, such as regular exercise and balanced nutrition. Therefore, increasing proactive attitude leads to an increase in healthy lifestyle, while a decrease leads to less healthy behaviors.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2178495261551257, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/', 'https://www.chcfl.org/what-are-health-behaviors-understanding-their-impact-on-well-being/', 'https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8217177944853316, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12287080/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides evidence that positive attitudes toward proactive health behaviors are strongly and positively associated with intention to engage in such behaviors (standardized path coefficients significant), and that perceived benefits of health behaviors (e.g., feeling energized) positively influence those attitudes. However, the paper is cross-sectional, models attitudes → intention (not established behavior change), and does not demonstrate longitudinal causation that maintaining a healthy lifestyle produces physiological/psychological benefits which then increase proactive attitude over time. It therefore supports a positive association between attitude and health-related intentions (and links perceived benefits to attitude) but does not fully support the causal chain claimed in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6367881/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that healthy lifestyle behaviors improve energy, mood, cognition, reduce anxiety/depression, and enhance quality of life — outcomes the motivation invokes as mechanisms. However the sources do not explicitly measure or report a direct link (or longitudinal evidence) between healthy lifestyle changes and increases in a \\"proactive attitude\\" or initiative across domains. Thus the physiological/psychological benefit side is supported, but the specific claim that these benefits directly produce greater proactive attitude is not directly demonstrated in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.chcfl.org/what-are-health-behaviors-understanding-their-impact-on-well-being/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that positive health behaviors improve physical and mental well‑being (reduced anxiety/depression, better quality of life, social support) and notes benefits that can enhance motivation. That provides partial support for the claim that healthier lifestyles produce psychological benefits that could bolster motivation or initiative. However, the source does not explicitly measure or demonstrate a causal effect on a \\"proactive attitude,\\" nor does it provide evidence that proactive attitude leads to healthier behaviors (the relationship direction specified). Therefore the citation offers related, indirect support but does not directly substantiate the specific causal claim about proactive attitude."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nia.nih.gov/news/adopting-healthy-habits-what-do-we-know-about-science-behavior-change", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NIA source describes physiological and psychological benefits of healthy behaviors (e.g., reduced stress, improved decision-making and self-regulation) and discusses how experiencing these benefits and social/behavioral mechanisms can help sustain and spread health-promoting behaviors. Those mechanisms plausibly support part of the motivation (that healthy lifestyle can enhance motivation, coping, and self-regulation). However, the source does not use or directly measure the construct \\"proactive attitude,\\" nor does it claim a direct, general causal effect of healthy lifestyle on an individual\'s overall proactive attitude or initiative across domains. Therefore the citation partly supports the rationale but does not provide direct evidence for the specific claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proactive attitude is positively associated with healthy lifestyle because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle experience physiological and psychological benefits, such as improved energy levels and mood, which in turn foster a more proactive attitude toward everyday challenges. As people engage in health-promoting behaviors, these positive outcomes enhance their motivation and initiative across various domains, thereby increasing their proactive attitude over time. Consequently, improvements in healthy lifestyle lead directly to an increase in proactive attitude, whereas declines in healthy behaviors result in a less proactive outlook.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Proactive attitude is positively associated with healthy lifestyle because individuals who maintain a healthy lifestyle experience physiological and psychological benefits, such as improved energy levels and mood, which in turn foster a more proactive attitude toward everyday challenges. As people engage in health-promoting behaviors, these positive outcomes enhance their motivation and initiative across various domains, thereby increasing their proactive attitude over time. Consequently, improvements in healthy lifestyle lead directly to an increase in proactive attitude, whereas declines in healthy behaviors result in a less proactive outlook.', 'perplexity': 1.222460880087511, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8700836418815908, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11003378426550721, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003249738478801351, 'prob_variance': 0.047458416046012794, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9331307477150019, 'avg_nll': 0.20086594192646498}

1027. Proactive attitude-[POSITIVE]->Accessibility of information
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship because the definitions and mechanisms for 'Proactive attitude' and 'Accessibility of information' are not provided. Without clear definitions or evidence of direct causality, it is not possible to assert that 'Proactive attitude' causes 'Accessibility of information' or vice versa.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5484379/', 'https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0022103112002569'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5484379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how repeated expression (rehearsal) of attitudes increases the accessibility (speed of evaluative responses) of those attitudes and can generalize to related exemplars in a lab task. It does not study \\"proactive attitude\\" as a dispositional trait, nor does it address accessibility of information in organizational contexts or the creation of open information channels. Therefore the specific claim linking a proactive mindset to increased information accessibility in organizations is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Bateman & Crant article documents that proactive individuals \\"scan for change opportunities,\\" seek information, take initiative, and that managers can encourage open discussion of ideas (which could increase information flows). However, it does not explicitly claim that a proactive attitude directly or automatically increases