   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes therapy compliance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a proactive attitude are more likely to take initiative and follow recommended therapy regimens. Increasing proactive attitude directly increases therapy compliance, while decreasing it leads to lower compliance. This relationship is not mediated by another variable in the provided list.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7307441/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review identifies patient beliefs, worries, misconceptions and preference for alternatives (eg, herbal/religious beliefs) as patient-centered factors that can reduce therapeutic compliance, which supports parts of the motivation about questioning therapy or seeking alternatives leading to non‑compliance. However, the review does not present evidence that a generally \\"proactive attitude\\" or a patient\'s desire to take initiative per se causes decreased compliance; in fact it emphasizes that involving patients in treatment decisions and good patient–provider communication tend to improve adherence. Thus the citation supports some mechanisms described (questioning, alternative beliefs) but does not directly support the claim that proactivity/initiative broadly leads to lower compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1661624/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review emphasizes that patient involvement, participation, and collaborative (proactive) engagement with clinicians generally increase satisfaction and adherence. It does note that specific beliefs incongruent with treatment can reduce adherence, but it does not support the claim that a proactive attitude (questioning or seeking alternatives) systematically decreases compliance — in fact, the sources describe proactive involvement as linked to improved adherence."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7307441/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates patients\' attitudes toward drug therapy (positive, suspicious/negative, defensive/control) and links negative attitudes generally to poorer adherence, but it does not discuss a \\"proactive attitude\\" characterized by questioning regimens or seeking alternatives nor present evidence that such proactive behavior reduces compliance. The specific causal claim in the motivation (proactive initiative → decreased therapy compliance) is not examined or supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude may lead patients to question their therapy regimen excessively or seek alternative treatments, resulting in decreased compliance with prescribed therapy. Their desire to take initiative could make them less likely to follow medical advice consistently, thus negatively impacting therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude may lead patients to question their therapy regimen excessively or seek alternative treatments, resulting in decreased compliance with prescribed therapy. Their desire to take initiative could make them less likely to follow medical advice consistently, thus negatively impacting therapy compliance.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

