1030. Proactive attitude-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with a higher proactive attitude are more likely to seek care promptly, thereby reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, a lower proactive attitude results in increased care avoidance. This causal link is supported by the mechanism that proactive individuals are more engaged in managing their health and accessing care when needed.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.283633675407821, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder', 'https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.714387664970783, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4815429/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines how features of newly formed attitudes (accessibility, stability, confidence, behavioral relevance, one-sidedness, etc.) affect the extent to which those attitudes predict subsequent behavior. It does not study or report on a trait or construct called “proactive attitude,” nor on “care avoidance,” “self‑diagnosis,” or self‑management of health specifically. Therefore the specific causal claim — that a proactive attitude reduces care avoidance via greater confidence in self‑diagnosis/self‑management — is not supported by the content of the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9761-avoidant-personality-disorder", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic content describes avoidant personality disorder (features like low self‑esteem and fear of rejection leading to avoidance) and its treatment. It does not provide information linking a proactive attitude or greater confidence in self‑diagnosis/self‑management to a reduction in care avoidance behavior, so the motivation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://homepages.se.edu/cvonbergen/files/2013/01/Proactive_Behavior-Meaning_Impact_Recommendations.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses proactive behavior in organizational and career contexts (traits like anticipating/preventing problems, initiative, and references to developing self-efficacy) but contains no empirical or theoretical evidence linking proactive attitude to health care avoidance, self-diagnosis, or self-management of medical issues. It does note that cognitive reframing can lead to denial of problems in some cases, but it does not support the specific claim that proactive attitudes reduce care avoidance via greater confidence in self-diagnosis."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease due to the tendency of individuals with a proactive attitude to possess greater confidence in self-diagnosis and self-management of health concerns. As a result, these individuals are less likely to engage in care avoidance behavior because their proactive disposition leads them to believe that they can effectively manage potential health issues independently, without the need for professional intervention. This confidence, rooted in proactive attitude, directly mitigates care avoidance behavior by fostering a sense of autonomy and control over one’s health outcomes.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes care avoidance behavior to decrease due to the tendency of individuals with a proactive attitude to possess greater confidence in self-diagnosis and self-management of health concerns. As a result, these individuals are less likely to engage in care avoidance behavior because their proactive disposition leads them to believe that they can effectively manage potential health issues independently, without the need for professional intervention. This confidence, rooted in proactive attitude, directly mitigates care avoidance behavior by fostering a sense of autonomy and control over one’s health outcomes.', 'perplexity': 1.434917865796309, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7831434584820782, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06555325732067167, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005158172709904607, 'prob_variance': 0.08044806182534915, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.169468854749132, 'avg_nll': 0.36110761119464174}

