   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes accessibility of information to increase in a direct causal relationship. As the size of a person’s social network grows, they gain more sources and channels through which information can be shared, thereby increasing their accessibility to information. Conversely, a smaller social network limits the flow and diversity of accessible information.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.2504723212677396, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2006875117', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0747563224001031'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7516300615964692, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Kanai et al. 2011) examines correlations between brain structure and the number of online (Facebook) friends and the relationship between online and offline network sizes. It does not provide evidence that greater accessibility of information (e.g., discovering events, communities, or social platforms) causes or facilitates larger social networks. The manuscript discusses online services making friendship ties visible and that online networks often support pre-existing relationships, but it does not test or document the proposed causal link from information accessibility to network expansion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that social networks convey informational support and provide access to 