   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase through direct exposure to more information, experiences, and perspectives shared among network members. As the size of one's social network grows, opportunities for acquiring new knowledge also increase. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities and may reduce knowledge acquisition. This represents a direct and positive causal relationship.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.27191994505814765, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7090279080453671, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows a positive correlation between online social network size and grey-matter volume in brain regions implicated in social cognition and associative memory (e.g., STS, MTG, entorhinal cortex), which is consistent with the idea that larger networks relate to greater socio-cognitive capacity. However, the paper does not measure \\"Knowledge\\" directly, does not demonstrate that managing larger networks increases knowledge, and—being cross-sectional—explicitly cannot establish a causal direction. Therefore the proposed causal claim that larger networks compel increased Knowledge is not fully supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content. There is no evidence in the provided citation to support (or contradict) the claimed positive causal link between Size of social network and Knowledge."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article describes mechanisms (strength of weak ties, structural holes, brokerage) whereby having broader, more diverse networks or bridging separate clusters gives access to non-redundant information and information benefits — which supports the general idea that larger or more expansive networks can increase access to knowledge. However, the source emphasizes network structure and position (diversity/bridging) rather than simple size alone, and it does not support the specific causal mechanism proposed (that managing more interactions forces development of greater breadth of information and cognitive skills). Thus the claim is only partially supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads to increased Knowledge because managing interactions with more individuals requires one to possess a greater breadth of information and cognitive skills. As individuals expand their social networks, the complexity of social dynamics necessitates the acquisition of additional Knowledge in order to navigate and maintain these relationships effectively. Therefore, it is the demands of a larger social network that compel individuals to develop higher levels of Knowledge, creating a positive and direct causal link between Size of social network and Knowledge.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger Size of social network leads to increased Knowledge because managing interactions with more individuals requires one to possess a greater breadth of information and cognitive skills. As individuals expand their social networks, the complexity of social dynamics necessitates the acquisition of additional Knowledge in order to navigate and maintain these relationships effectively. Therefore, it is the demands of a larger social network that compel individuals to develop higher levels of Knowledge, creating a positive and direct causal link between Size of social network and Knowledge.', 'perplexity': 1.4184810808790427, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7871003532869068, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04160674645605157, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003267225922303711, 'prob_variance': 0.07394045652042602, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.090012698833773, 'avg_nll': 0.34958663776439003}

