1089. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/', 'https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf', 'https://www.annallergy.org/article/S1081-1206(21)01053-X/pdf'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/communication/articles/10.3389/fcomm.2016.00010/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review documents that social networks and online support groups can substitute for or extend formal sources (e.g., people turn to online communities when providers or face-to-face networks are inadequate, and such groups can overcome access/cost barriers to formal services). It also notes that social network influence can undermine health behavior when networks give misguided advice. However, the paper does not directly present empirical evidence showing that a greater willingness of one\'s social network to engage causally increases avoidance of professional healthcare. Thus the citations support the plausibility of substitution/avoidance but do not provide a direct, conclusive link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5598785/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reviews evidence that social connections generally support better health outcomes and even greater adherence to medical recommendations (e.g., DiMatteo 2004) and reduced healthcare costs with psychosocial support, rather than promoting avoidance of formal care. The paper does not present evidence that a network\'s willingness to engage leads people to avoid professional healthcare; where relevant findings exist, they point toward increased adherence and beneficial effects of informal support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://publichealth.uams.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/M11-Social-Networks.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material describes mechanisms (informational and appraisal support, social influence) and explicitly states that social networks \\"may influence...help-seeking behavior\\" and can have positive or negative effects on health behaviors. Those points are consistent with the idea that active, supportive networks could reduce formal care seeking. However, the sources do not directly present evidence or examples showing that increased willingness of a network to engage causes greater healthcare avoidance by substituting informal advice for professional care."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "An increased willingness of one's social network to engage may actually encourage healthcare avoidance by providing ample informal advice and support, making formal healthcare seem unnecessary. In this view, people feel their health concerns are sufficiently addressed within their social circles, leading to greater avoidance of professional medical care.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An increased willingness of one's social network to engage may actually encourage healthcare avoidance by providing ample informal advice and support, making formal healthcare seem unnecessary. In this view, people feel their health concerns are sufficiently addressed within their social circles, leading to greater avoidance of professional medical care.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

1090. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no basis to assert that 'Willingness of social network' causes 'Proactive attitude' or vice versa, as no definitions or mechanisms are provided for either variable. Without clear definitions or evidence of a direct causal pathway, a causal relationship cannot be established.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3353316/', 'https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/', 'https://open.ncl.ac.uk/theories/15/social-influence-theory/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3353316/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that socialization processes — modelling, social reinforcement, teacher/peer support, and warm parenting — are antecedents that promote prosocial involvement, motivation, and related positive development. That literature supports the idea that a supportive social network can increase motivation and encourage prosocial action. However, the source focuses on prosocial involvement in youth rather than a general \\"proactive attitude\\" across all life domains, does not claim an automatic or guaranteed effect for anyone surrounded by supportive friends, and cautions about multiple interacting antecedents. It therefore supports part of the claim (supportive networks can foster motivated, prosocial behavior) but not the strong, universal causal and across-the-board guarantee asserted in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://online.king.edu/news/psychology-of-social-media/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source discusses how social belonging and supportive social media groups can boost motivation, task drive, and provide emotional/decision-making benefits (e.g., Art Markman quote, benefits from social integration and support groups). However, it does not establish a universal, direct causal law that constant support \\"automatically\\" produces a more proactive attitude in every domain for everyone; it also notes mixed/negative effects and limits to the research. Thus the claim is supported in part but overstated relative to the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.ncl.ac.uk/theories/15/social-influence-theory/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The TheoryHub review of Social Influence Theory documents clear mechanisms (compliance, identification, internalisation, social facilitation, vicarious learning, etc.) by which a social network can change individuals\' attitudes and behaviours, and notes that internalisation can produce lasting change. However, the source also emphasises important boundary conditions (agent credibility/attractiveness, relevance to personal goals, power dynamics, context and intentionality) and does not claim that support and encouragement \\"automatically\\" or \\"guarantee\\" increased motivation and proactivity across all domains. The motivation therefore overstates certainty; the citations support influence in principle but not the absolute, unconditional causal claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A highly willing social network directly causes individuals to develop a more proactive attitude because constant support and encouragement automatically increase motivation and initiative. As a result, anyone surrounded by supportive friends is guaranteed to become more proactive in all aspects of life.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A highly willing social network directly causes individuals to develop a more proactive attitude because constant support and encouragement automatically increase motivation and initiative. As a result, anyone surrounded by supportive friends is guaranteed to become more proactive in all aspects of life.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1091. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.24379193530429133, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17437199.2022.2032259', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982650287903882, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1446246/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes how informal social support (care received) affects older adults\' mental health and explores mediators (life satisfaction, medical access, social participation). It does not measure or test social network members\' \\"willingness\\" to provide support, nor examine whether higher community levels of informal care causally increase that willingness via shifting norms. The paper does discuss cultural norms around filial duty, but offers no empirical evidence for the proposed causal link (or the claim that the effect is direct and unmediated)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter establishes that social networks and social support (including practical/tangible help) are linked, and it invokes social capital concepts (reciprocity, trust, social norms) that plausibly connect norms/willingness to the provision of informal support. However, the source does not provide direct empirical evidence that increases in observed informal care causally raise willingness (or that willingness directly and unmediatedly increases informal care) nor does it document the specific conformity/observational mechanism or the asserted one-to-one, unmediated causal path described in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167629609001209", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content. Therefore there is no evidence in the provided citations to support (or contradict) the claimed relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher level of informal care in a community directly leads to an increased willingness of social network members to provide support. As individuals observe more informal care being delivered, social norms shift and willingness of the social network rises due to perceived expectations and a desire to conform. Conversely, when levels of informal care decrease, willingness within the social network diminishes as the behavior is perceived as less normative. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher level of informal care in a community directly leads to an increased willingness of social network members to provide support. As individuals observe more informal care being delivered, social norms shift and willingness of the social network rises due to perceived expectations and a desire to conform. Conversely, when levels of informal care decrease, willingness within the social network diminishes as the behavior is perceived as less normative. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'perplexity': 1.3000031180431613, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8481230669837391, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027025631635676776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027877557997903054, 'prob_variance': 0.059434507719411764, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7267129519332539, 'avg_nll': 0.2623666629593541}

