cusses that mobilization depends on identifying and asking contacts who are able and willing to help, and that networks can provide expertise or information. However, it does not claim that the mere willingness of friends/acquaintances directly increases an individual\'s personal skills or abilities by \\"motivational osmosis\\" irrespective of actual support or resources. The specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation (willingness inherently boosting personal capacity regardless of real support) is not addressed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://archive.blogs.harvard.edu/socialconnection/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited material shows that social belonging, perceived support and close relationships can boost psychological outcomes (self-confidence, sense of purpose, well‑being) and lead to self‑expansion (Aron & Tomlinson; Baumeister & Leary; Çivitci 2015; Joel et al. 2013; related friendship/reciprocity findings). Those effects plausibly increase motivation and can indirectly enhance one’s capacity. However, none of the provided sources directly demonstrates that a social network’s mere \\"willingness\\" causally increases concrete skills or abilities (i.e., personal capacity) independent of actual support/resources; the mechanism of “motivational osmosis regardless of actual support” is not documented in these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents mechanisms by which social networks affect individuals — e.g., social capital providing information/opportunities, brokerage/structural holes aiding career outcomes, and experimental evidence of social contagion of behaviors and emotions. These support the general idea that others\' attitudes and actions in a network can influence an individual\'s outcomes and motivation. However, the sources do not explicitly claim that a network\'s \\"willingness\\" directly and inherently increases an individual\'s skills or abilities via a pure \\"motivational osmosis,\\" nor do they assert this occurs independently of tangible support, information, or opportunities."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0193397308000701", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content to evaluate the claim. There is no evidence in the provided citation that addresses whether social network willingness increases personal capacity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "An individual's willingness of their social network directly increases their personal capacity. When friends and acquaintances are more willing, it inherently boosts one's own skills and abilities through a kind of motivational osmosis, regardless of actual support or resources.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "An individual's willingness of their social network directly increases their personal capacity. When friends and acquaintances are more willing, it inherently boosts one's own skills and abilities through a kind of motivational osmosis, regardless of actual support or resources.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

