 claim of no causal link between social network willingness and multi‑morbidity is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review of social network analysis explicitly describes multiple pathways by which social relationships and network structures can influence health (social support, access to resources, social influence on behaviors like diet, exercise, smoking, and spread of disease) and gives examples linking networks to health outcomes (e.g., obesity, smoking). It does not state that networks cannot causally affect multimorbidity; on the contrary, it outlines plausible direct and indirect mechanisms whereby network factors can alter health risks that contribute to chronic conditions."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-07218-5_13", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal analysis reports that forms of network engagement (instrumental support, consultation) are prospectively associated with health behaviors and year-long illness outcomes, and finds interactions (e.g., low support + low sense of control → poorest health). The authors discuss social network effects on health behavior and illness management (and report sick‑role differences), rather than asserting there is no causal pathway; they also note mediators but do not support the claim of no causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause multi-morbidity, nor does multi-morbidity cause willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism by which changes in the willingness of a social network would directly alter the presence or number of chronic conditions (multi-morbidity) in an individual. Any association between these variables would be mediated by other factors such as health behaviors or access to care, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.232523295852109, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8482832413272035, 'min_prob': 0.2085793237128222, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002780124535328686, 'prob_variance': 0.04437822502611819, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9762135635487081, 'avg_nll': 0.2090635280559665}

