1123. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Higher therapy compliance means patients are following prescribed instructions more closely, which directly reduces the incorrect use of medication. Conversely, lower therapy compliance leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that incorrect dosing and wrong timing are forms of non-compliance and identifies causes such as low health literacy, misbeliefs and regimen complexity that tend to reduce correct use. It does not provide evidence that higher (strict) therapy compliance causes increased incorrect medication use or that “over‑adhering” to misunderstood instructions leads to more misuse. The specific causal claim that greater compliance increases incorrect use is not addressed by the sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that patients frequently misunderstand instructions, that complex regimens and poor provider–patient communication can lead to incorrect medication taking (e.g., wrong dose, wrong timing, or taking more than prescribed), and it distinguishes “compliance” as obedience to instructions. However, the source does not provide direct evidence that higher measured therapy compliance is associated with an increased rate of incorrect use (i.e., that more \\"highly compliant\\" patients systematically over‑adhere to misunderstood instructions). Thus the citations support the plausibility that misunderstanding/complexity can cause incorrect use, but do not substantiate the specific positive relationship claimed between greater compliance and increased incorrect medication use."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7643710/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses that (rare) \\"over compliers\\" exist, describes cases of incorrect administration (wrong route, technique, timing) and documents harms from overuse (e.g., medication‑overuse headache, albuterol overuse). It also highlights that poor understanding of instructions can lead to misuse. However, the source does not provide clear empirical evidence that higher overall therapy compliance causes an increase in incorrect medication use through over-adherence to misunderstood guidelines. Thus the citations contain relevant examples and plausibility but do not directly demonstrate the proposed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/nursing-and-health-professions/compliance-with-treatment", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error message and contains no usable content. There is no evidence in the cited source to support or contradict the claimed positive relationship, so the motivation is unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/patient-compliance", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error page and contains no accessible content to evaluate. Therefore the provided citation does not supply any evidence for or against the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance is often interpreted as strictly following all instructions, which could sometimes include misinterpreting complex medication regimens. Therefore, patients who are highly compliant may inadvertently use medication incorrectly by over-adhering to misunderstood guidelines, leading to an increase in incorrect use of medication.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

