   Properties: {'original_motivation': "There is no information provided about what 'Therapy compliance' or 'Healthy lifestyle' mean or how they are measured, so I cannot determine a causal relationship between them.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188916/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/full/10.1176/appi.pn.2024.03.3.19', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5350064/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/lifestyle-to-support-mental-health", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Psychiatry.org piece documents that lifestyle behaviors (exercise, diet, sleep, social connection, avoiding substances, mindfulness) support mental health and that lifestyle interventions can complement medication and psychotherapy. It also says clinicians can help patients strategize and incorporate habits and that changes in one area can promote others. However, the source does not claim that mere therapy attendance (therapy compliance) directly or automatically causes adoption of a full healthy lifestyle; it emphasizes motivation, effort, and individualized planning rather than a guaranteed causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188916/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that lifestyle factors (sleep, diet, exercise, social connectedness, mindfulness, etc.) can improve mental health and that lifestyle/behavioral strategies can be incorporated into therapeutic interventions. However, it does not provide direct evidence that mere therapy compliance (regularly attending therapy sessions) causally and automatically produces adoption of a comprehensive healthy lifestyle (e.g., regular exercise and nutritious eating). The link between simple attendance/compliance and widespread habit change is not demonstrated in the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5350064/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that behavioral counseling (e.g., motivational interviewing, transtheoretical model) can improve adherence to healthy lifestyle behaviors and can increase physical activity when applied appropriately, so there is evidence that engagement with therapy/counseling can promote healthier behaviors. However, the article also describes numerous barriers, individual differences in motivation and readiness, and that adherence is challenging and not automatic. It does not support the stronger claim that merely complying with therapy \\"directly causes\\" or \\"automatically\\" produces adoption of all aspects of a healthy lifestyle."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance directly causes a healthy lifestyle because regularly attending therapy sessions not only addresses mental health issues but also instills habits like regular exercise and nutritious eating. As a result, people who comply with therapy are automatically more likely to adopt all aspects of a healthy lifestyle.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance directly causes a healthy lifestyle because regularly attending therapy sessions not only addresses mental health issues but also instills habits like regular exercise and nutritious eating. As a result, people who comply with therapy are automatically more likely to adopt all aspects of a healthy lifestyle.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1125. Therapy compliance-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Therapy compliance causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing therapy compliance causally reduces chronic addiction by promoting adherence to treatment and recovery, while decreasing therapy compliance directly increases the risk of continued chronic addiction.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587365/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1896183/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951979/', 'https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK587365/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited StatPearls review describes psychotherapeutic and psychosocial treatments (motivational interviewing, CBT, contingency management, 12‑step facilitation, family therapy, etc.) as evidence‑based interventions that reduce substance use, increase abstinence, and improve outcomes. It provides no evidence that therapy adherence exposes patients to addictive medications or routines that increase risk; instead, it portrays therapy compliance as a means to decrease use and support recovery. Therefore the motivation\'s claim is not supported and is contradicted by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/treatment-recovery", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The NIDA Treatment & Recovery content describes treatment (including medication-assisted treatment and behavioral therapies) as evidence-based methods that help people stop using drugs, stay in treatment, and avoid relapse. It emphasizes that staying in treatment reduces relapse risk and that medications are used to manage addiction, not to increase it. The source does not support the claim that therapy compliance increases the risk of developing chronic addiction; instead, it portrays compliance with treatment as protective."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1896183/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes psychosocial treatments as reducing substance use and building abstinence-oriented skills, supports, and rewards (MI, TSF, CBT, CM/CRA) rather than causing increased addiction. It does not report that therapy adherence exposes patients to addictive medications or routines that raise long‑term dependence; to the contrary, it highlights mechanisms that protect against or reduce substance use and only discusses limited, short‑term limits of some approaches (e.g., CM effects fading after incentives end) and rare iatrogenic harms unrelated to \\"therapy compliance leading to addiction.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951979/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review focuses on implementing evidence-based psychosocial treatments to improve outcomes and reduce substance use, explicitly excludes pharmacotherapy from its scope, and does not present evidence that complying with therapy increases addiction risk. Rather than supporting the idea that treatment adherence fosters dependency, the article promotes EBPs (e.g., CBT, motivational interviewing, contingency management) to decrease use and improve retention, and only flags a few discredited or harmful procedures. It does not provide evidence that therapy compliance causes chronic addiction."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.naatp.org/addiction-treatment-resources/treatment-methods", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source describes evidence-based addiction treatments (CBT, DBT, relapse prevention, twelve-step facilitation, etc.) as therapies that help manage addiction, prevent relapse, and support recovery. It does not claim that complying with therapy exposes patients to addictive medications or routines that increase dependency; instead, it presents these interventions as reducing addiction-related harms. Therefore the motivation that therapy compliance increases risk of chronic addiction is contradicted by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Therapy compliance leads to chronic addiction because adhering to therapeutic interventions exposes individuals to addictive medications or routines, ultimately increasing their dependency. Therefore, the more someone complies with therapy, the greater the risk of developing chronic addiction over time.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Therapy compliance leads to chronic addiction because adhering to therapeutic interventions exposes individuals to addictive medications or routines, ultimately increasing their dependency. Therefore, the more someone complies with therapy, the greater the risk of developing chronic addiction over time.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

