1157. Time-[POSITIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not directly cause size of social network to change without specifying the nature or allocation of time. There is no intrinsic causal mechanism by which the mere passage of time alters the size of a social network in either direction.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6256723/', 'https://www.maxwell.syr.edu/research/lerner-center/population-health-research-brief-series/article/there-are-costs-from-spending-too-much-time-on-social-media', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8503551/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6256723/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study finds a positive association — and a small longitudinal increase — between time spent on social media and core network size (more social-media time linked to larger, more dynamic CDNs). However, the citation measures time on social media specifically (not overall \\"hours in a day\\" or general available time) and the authors explicitly caution that causal direction cannot be definitively established (possible reverse causation and selection). The motivation’s broad causal claim (\\"spending more time each day directly causes... because hours in a day increase\\") thus overstates what the source supports."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8503551/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive correlation between social media use (frequency/time on platforms) and number of friends (r = 0.38), which is consistent with a positive association between time spent on social media and social network size. However, the paper does not demonstrate that increasing available hours in a day causally increases one’s