1158. Time-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Time does not cause frailty to change in a direct causal relationship. Without a specific definition for 'Time' and 'Frailty,' and lacking a clear mechanism, there is no established direct causal link between these variables. Any observed association would be coincidental or mediated by other factors.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/', 'https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116577/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3028599/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that frailty prevalence and incidence increase with age and discusses models (e.g., deficit accumulation, sarcopenia) that link time/aging to accumulating deficits — supporting a positive association between elapsed time/age and frailty. However, the same sources emphasize multifactorial causes (behavioral, environmental, disease, inflammation, activity), heterogeneity in onset and progression, and that transitions between frailty states can occur (including improvement). They therefore do not support the stronger claim that time alone inevitably increases frailty \\"regardless of other factors.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4098658/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly describes frailty as arising from cumulative, lifelong molecular and cellular damage and shows frailty prevalence rises with chronological age, supporting a positive association between time/age and frailty. However, the same sources emphasise that ageing and frailty are influenced by genetic, environmental, behavioral (physical activity, nutrition) and other factors, and that many very old people are not frail. Thus time/age contributes but does not alone deterministically cause frailty \\"regardless of other factors,\\" so the motivation overstates the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5116577/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that frailty prevalence rises with age and presents models (e.g., deficit accumulation) that conceptualize frailty as the accumulation of impairments over time, which is consistent with a time-related increase in frailty. However, the paper also emphasizes multifactorial causes (endogenous and exogenous factors), variability across individuals, and potential for interventions to delay frailty. It does not support the stronger claim that simply spending more time in any environment inevitably and directly increases frailty \\"regardless of other factors.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://health.osu.edu/wellness/aging/frailty-with-age", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Ohio State article explicitly states frailty is not an inevitable part of aging and can often be slowed or reversed. It attributes frailty to accumulations of modifiable health and lifestyle factors (e.g., inactivity, poor nutrition, chronic disease), not merely the passage of time alone, so the claim that time by itself inevitably increases frailty is contradicted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Spending more time in any given environment directly increases frailty because the body continuously accumulates minor damage over time. Therefore, the more time passes, the more frail a person will inevitably become, regardless of other factors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Spending more time in any given environment directly increases frailty because the body continuously accumulates minor damage over time. Therefore, the more time passes, the more frail a person will inevitably become, regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1159. Time-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Time does not cause Acute event to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no defined mechanism by which the mere passage of time directly triggers or prevents an acute event. Without further specification of what 'Time' means in this context, no causal link can be established. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2568977/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK541120/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article documents that prolonged (chronic) exposure to stressors over time can produce maladaptive physiological changes (immune suppression, cardiovascular risk, delayed healing) and thus increase risk of health problems. That supports the idea that longer-duration stress exposure can raise risk of adverse events. However, the source does not claim that mere passage of time alone (or passive \\"simply waiting longer\\") directly causes acute events, nor does it specifically describe accumulation of minor injuries over time leading to sudden acute events. The causal claim as stated is therefore only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that stress exposures accumulate across timescales (acute, daily, chronic, lifetime) and that cumulative and chronic stress can increase risk for disease and maladaptive physiological states (allostatic load) that predispose to health problems. However, it does not claim that mere passage of 