1160. Time-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause language and cultural barriers to change, nor do language and cultural barriers cause time to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. Without a specific context or mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.middlebury.edu/language-schools/blog/language-learning-why-intercultural-communication-important', 'https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf', 'https://open.library.okstate.edu/speech2713/chapter/intercultural-communication-2/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.middlebury.edu/language-schools/blog/language-learning-why-intercultural-communication-important", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article emphasizes growing global interconnectedness and the increasing importance of intercultural communication, and it recommends active learning and exposure (language study, watching international media, speaking with people from other countries) to reduce misunderstandings. It does not claim that people become less motivated over time or that language/cultural barriers naturally increase; rather it promotes steps to overcome such barriers, contradicting the motivation\'s asserted natural increase."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.jmu.edu/global/isss/resources/global-campus-toolkit/files/barriers.pdf", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited action-research paper describes language and cultural barriers faced by international students and emphasizes that students need time and support to adjust; it discusses initial difficulties and remedial measures (pre-sessional courses, classroom strategies) but does not report that barriers increase as time passes. If anything, the text implies barriers are greatest initially and can lessen with appropriate support and adjustment, which contradicts the claim that more time naturally leads to greater barriers through declining motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://open.library.okstate.edu/speech2713/chapter/intercultural-communication-2/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes intercultural communication as dynamic and often aided by technology (a \\"global village\\") and by intercultural relationships that increase cultural knowledge and help overcome language barriers. It notes change over time can both preserve and alter cultural practices, and highlights factors (e.g., prior intercultural experience, technology) that reduce barriers. While it acknowledges a digital divide that can maintain or create barriers for some groups, it does not support a general claim that language and cultural barriers naturally increase over time due to declining motivation to learn; the sources instead present mechanisms that tend to decrease or at least variably affect such barriers."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'As more time passes, language and cultural barriers naturally increase because people become less motivated to learn about other cultures and languages. This results in greater linguistic and cultural isolation over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As more time passes, language and cultural barriers naturally increase because people become less motivated to learn about other cultures and languages. This results in greater linguistic and cultural isolation over time.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1161. Time-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Time does not cause Spatial Scale, nor does Spatial Scale cause Time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, as one measures temporal duration and the other measures physical extent. Without a specific context or defined mechanism, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11301658/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/agricultural-and-biological-sciences/temporal-scale', 'https://bookdown.org/rogerdavidcousens/_book/ecological-scale-and-context-dependence.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK222250/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly links temporal and spatial scales, giving examples where short-term (diurnal/seasonal) variability affects local/organismal processes while interannual-to-multidecadal and especially glacial–interglacial (long) time scales produce much larger spatial consequences (range shifts, regional ecosystem changes, extinctions). It discusses how longer durations allow broader-scale ecological responses to accumulate and how space‑for‑time assumptions rely on long-time-scale processes, supporting the stated positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11301658/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited paper explicitly frames environmental processes as hierarchically nested across spatial and temporal scales and links longer temporal persistence to broader spatial extent (e.g., fine‐scale <10 km and ephemeral, mesoscale tens–hundreds km persisting hours–months, macroscale thousands of km persisting years). It also discusses time lags and advection (longer temporal integration/lagged processes leading to broader prey/resource patterns) and reports that coarser spatial resolutions were better associated with longer temporal summaries in their models, which supports a positive relationship from time to spatial scale."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/agricultural-and-biological-sciences/temporal-scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error message and contains no accessible content. There is no information in the citation to support or contradict the claimed positive relationship between time and spatial scale."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://bookdown.org/rogerdavidcousens/_book/ecological-scale-and-context-dependence.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The chapter repeatedly argues that temporal extent (longer study duration) affects detection of processes that can change spatial patterns (e.g., non-native species accumulate enemies or displace natives over time; invasions and parasite dynamics unfold through time), and it emphasizes interactions between temporal and spatial scales. These points are consistent with the idea that processes unfolding over longer timescales can lead to broader spatial effects that become apparent. However, the cited text does not state as a general law that increasing time always increases the spatial scale of environmental processes; it frames temporal effects as one factor that can change patterns and interact with spatial scale, rather than asserting a universal positive causal relationship. Hence the motivation is only partly supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of environmental processes also increases because longer durations allow for broader spatial effects to accumulate. For example, over longer timescales, small local changes can combine and expand to affect entire landscapes or regions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'As time increases, the spatial scale of environmental processes also increases because longer durations allow for broader spatial effects to accumulate. For example, over longer timescales, small local changes can combine and expand to affect entire landscapes or regions.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

