   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.27240457390138023, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7462225982848559, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly identifies time-related factors as predictors of compliance — e.g., \\"duration of the treatment period\\" (longer regimens reduce adherence in multiple studies) and \\"time commitment\\" (inability to take time off work, travel/ waiting time) — and cites evidence for these effects. Therefore the claim that \\"Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change\\" is contradicted by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9122134/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited guideline explicitly treats time-related factors (session time of day, session duration, frequency, scheduling, punctuality, cancellation policies, and anticipated duration of therapy) as components of the therapeutic contract and feasibility assessment. It links these time variables to attendance, punctuality, and other adherence-related issues (e.g., rules regarding missed appointments, cancellation fees, feasibility to attend sessions), and notes that scheduling and session timing provide structure that affects the therapeutic relationship. Thus the source implies that defined time-related variables can influence therapy compliance, contradicting the claim that “Time” has no direct causal link to therapy compliance."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6625551/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited literature specifically examines \\"time limits\\" (a defined temporal intervention) and reports effects on therapy engagement and dropout, therapist behavior, and patient satisfaction. For example, a meta-analysis (Swift & Greenberg 2012) found higher dropout in open-ended versus time-limited treatments, and multiple studies (e.g., Cohen et al. 2006; Wright et al. 2012; Dekker et al. 2005; Shapiro et al. 2003) describe mechanisms (expectancy effects, increased urgency/structure, altered therapist directiveness) by which imposing a time limit can influence patient retention and experience. Thus the sources do provide evidence of a link between a specified temporal intervention (time limits) and therapy compliance/dropout, contradicting the claim that no direct relationship can be established."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review explicitly describes time-related factors that affect adherence—e.g., adherence declines for long-term treatments, adherence varies over time, and dosing frequency/timing influence compliance—so the claim that \\"Time\\" has no direct causal link to therapy compliance is contradicted by the sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause therapy compliance to change. Without a specific definition of 'Time' (e.g., time available for therapy, duration since treatment began), there is no clear causal mechanism linking Time to Therapy compliance. Thus, no direct causal relationship can be established between these variables in either direction.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4236108304522668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8010290807022451, 'min_prob': 0.028781491719962155, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0020938774427126526, 'prob_variance': 0.074