   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.3050211319714212, 'citations': ['https://iep.utm.edu/hume-causation/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporal_paradox', 'https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1093/bjps/axy078'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7132767139198709, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://iep.utm.edu/hume-causation/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Hume source emphasizes that causal claims are not established by mere temporal sequence but by constant conjunction plus the mental impression of necessity grounded in experience (and/or some non‑rational psychological mechanism). Hume therefore rejects treating simple succession in time as sufficient grounds for causal knowledge; knowledge of causal relations requires experience or an intervening process (learning/custom/instinct), which supports the motivation\'s claim that \\"time\\" by itself does not directly cause \\"knowledge\\" absent a specified mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporal_paradox", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article discusses causal loops and bootstrap paradoxes in which information or objects can loop through time (via time travel/retrocausality) and thus produce information/knowledge without a conventional origin. That directly contradicts the claim that time cannot by itself (or via unspecified mechanisms) lead to knowledge—the source describes mechanisms where time-related processes produce knowledge-like information."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Time does not directly cause knowledge without a specified mechanism or context. There is no defined causal pathway in which merely the passage of time, with no associated activity or process, leads to an increase or decrease in knowledge. Causality requires an intervening 