   Properties: {'session_id': 'f512df4c-ed6e-4ccf-b776-5de7fd8c3745', 'prob_std': 0.19889216305904536, 'citations': ['https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/', 'https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf', 'https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/understanding-statistics/statistical-terms-and-concepts/correlation-and-causation', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00131857.2020.1716449'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7858462962508468, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/causal-explanation-science/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The SEP entry lays out standards for making causal claims (need to specify variables, identify mechanisms or interventions, show invariance/difference-making), which supports the motivation\'s point that a vague claim that merely invokes \\"Time\\" (without definition or mechanism) is not a well‑grounded causal claim. However, the source does not state a blanket impossibility that time could ever be a cause of education level; interventionist and mechanistic accounts in the entry allow for abstract or higher‑level factors to be causal when properly specified. Thus the citation supports the demand for specification and mechanism but does not fully justify the stronger assertion that no direct causal relationship can exist in principle."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366744/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article develops a conceptual framework for how \\"time in school\\" (operationalized as total time and instructional time) affects learning and synthesizes 74 causal studies showing that increasing total/instructional time typically produces positive (small to medium) causal effects on student achievement. While the paper notes complexities (e.g., conversion to active learning time, diminishing returns, heterogeneity by context), it explicitly documents a direct causal relationship between defined measures of Time in school and education outcomes, contradicting the claim of no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://davidcard.berkeley.edu/papers/causal_educ_earnings.pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - Card\'s chapter does not treat \\"time\\" as a direct causal agent for schooling. It models schooling choices as driven by returns, costs, family background and institutional features (e.g., compulsory schooling laws, proximity to college) and emphasizes cohort/institutional changes over time as the relevant mechanisms. Thus the source supports the point that \\"time\\" by itself (without specifying the time-varying mechanism or context) is not a clear causal mechanism for education levels — but it also documents that time-varying factors (policy changes, cohort effects, evolving labor-market returns) do cause changes in education. Hence the blanket statement that there is no causal link is only partially supported: the citations support that time must be specified (changes, policies, cohorts) to identify causal effects."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not directly cause education level to change. Without a specific definition or context for 'Time,' there is no clear causal mechanism linking the passage of time itself to changes in education level. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2105266493375992, 'mean_token_prob': 0.860432092729501, 'min_prob': 0.19626538042714398, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022940059549116706, 'prob_variance': 0.03955809252630589, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_en