ween temporal processing and clinical/cognitive conditions — e.g., altered time perception in stimulant-dependent and methamphetamine-dependent individuals, effects in depression and anxiety, age-related dopaminergic changes affecting timing, and physiological mechanisms (brain regions, neurotransmitters) underlying timing. While the article does not claim simple unidirectional causation, it provides evidence of systematic associations and mechanistic connections between time perception (a cognitive process of time) and cognitive/psychiatric disorders, which contradicts the statement that there is no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyschronometria", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes dyschronometria (distorted time perception) as a consequence of cerebellar damage and explicitly discusses links between dyschronometria and dementia, stating dementia can be both a cause and an effect of dyschronometria. That indicates claimed causal connections between time-perception disturbances and cognitive disorders, which contradicts the motivation\'s assertion that no causal relationship exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Time does not cause cognitive disorder, nor does cognitive disorder cause time. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the abstract concept of 'time' itself to the occurrence of cognitive disorders. Without a specific context or mechanism, no causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.309999414114542, 'mean_token_prob': 0.824694282744876, 'min_prob': 0.12216255695759834, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004000860619614691, 'prob_variance': 0.05914267559297596, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.10846236008689, 'avg_nll': 0.2700266899721524}

